const PRELOADED_QUESTIONS=[{"id":"mpre-model-001","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":1,"topic":"Model Rule 1.1","question":"An attorney practices in a state that does not require lawyers to participate in continuing legal education courses. After the attorney forms a partnership with several other lawyers, the majority decides that the firm will not pay for continuing legal education courses since they are not required by the state. Consequently, the attorney tells the firm he will not attend any continuing legal education courses because they all cost money. However, he decides to study legal issues on his own to keep up to date, since he has no malpractice insurance. Is it proper for the attorney to refuse to attend any continuing legal education courses?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the state does not offer free continuing legal education courses.","B":"Yes, because the attorney will independently undertake continuing study and education in the law.","C":"No, because the attorney cannot maintain competence without attending continuing legal education courses.","D":"No, because the attorney does not have malpractice insurance."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer must provide competent representation to clients. Model Rule 1.1. Competent representation requires having the legal knowledge and skill necessary for the representation. To maintain the required level of knowledge and skill, a lawyer should keep abreast of changes in the law through continuing study and education, as well as comply with any continuing education requirements to which the lawyer is subject. Model Rule 1.1. Independent study is just as valid as continuing legal education courses in maintaining the attorney’s competence, although failure to attend continuing legal education courses would be improper if the state required him to do so. A is incorrect. The attorney must maintain competence without regard to the cost of doing so. Additionally, this answer choice implies that continuing legal education courses are the only way for the attorney to maintain his competence. C is incorrect. As stated above, continuing legal education courses are not the only way the attorney can maintain his competence. D is incorrect. Being insured against malpractice has nothing to do with a lawyer’s duty to maintain the legal knowledge and skills needed to provide competent representation.","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer must provide competent representation to clients. Model Rule 1.1. Competent representation requires having the legal knowledge and skill necessary for the representation. To maintain the required level of knowledge and skill, a lawyer should keep abreast of changes in the law through continuing study and education, as well as comply with any continuing education requirements to which the lawyer is subject. Model Rule 1.1. Independent study is just as valid as continuing legal education courses in maintaining the attorney’s competence, although failure to attend continuing legal education courses would be improper if the state required him to do so. A is incorrect. The attorney must maintain competence without regard to the cost of doing so. Additionally, this answer choice implies that continuing legal education courses are the only way for the attorney to maintain his competence. C is incorrect. As stated above, continuing legal education courses are not the only way the attorney can maintain his competence. D is incorrect. Being insured against malpractice has nothing to do with a lawyer’s duty to maintain the legal knowledge and skills needed to provide competent representation.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the attorney to refuse to attend any continuing legal education courses?","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer must provide competent representation to clients. Model Rule 1.1. Competent representation requires having the legal knowledge and skill necessary for the representation. To maintain the required level of knowledge and skill, a lawyer should keep abreast of changes in the law through continuing study and education, as well as comply with any continuing education requirements to which the lawyer is subject. Model Rule 1.1. Independent study is just as valid as continuing legal education courses in maintaining the attorney’s competence, although failure to attend continuing legal education courses would be improper if the state required him to do so. A is incorrect. The attorney must maintain competence without regard to the cost of doing so. Additionally, this answer choice implies that continuing legal education courses are the only way for the attorney to maintain his competence. C is incorrect. As stated above, continuing legal education courses are not the only way the attorney can maintain his competence. D is incorrect. Being insured against malpractice has nothing to do with a lawyer’s duty to maintain the legal knowledge and skills needed to provide competent representation.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer must provide competent representation to clients. Model Rule 1.1. Competent representation requires having the legal knowledge and skill necessary for the representation. To maintain the required level of knowledge and skill, a lawyer should keep abreast of changes in the law through continuing study and education, as well as comply with any continuing education requirements to which the lawyer is subject. Model Rule 1.1. Independent study is just as valid as continuing legal education courses in maintaining the attorney’s competence, although failure to attend continuing legal education courses would be improper if the state required him to do so. A is incorrect. The attorney must maintain competence without regard to the cost of doing so. Additionally, this answer choice implies that continuing legal education courses are the only way for the attorney to maintain his competence. C is incorrect. As stated above, continuing legal education courses are not the only way the attorney can maintain his competence. D is incorrect. Being insured against malpractice has nothing to do with a lawyer’s duty to maintain the legal knowledge and skills needed to provide competent representation.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[48],"sourceAnswerPages":[86],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师在某州执业，该州并不要求律师参加继续法律教育课程。该律师与另外几名律师成立合伙制律师事务所后，多数合伙人决定，既然州法律并无此项要求，事务所就不为继续法律教育课程支付费用。因此，该律师告诉事务所，由于所有此类课程都要收费，他不会参加任何继续法律教育课程。不过，由于自己没有律师执业过失保险，他决定自行研究法律问题，以掌握最新发展。该律师拒绝参加任何继续法律教育课程是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该州没有提供免费的继续法律教育课程。","B":"是，因为该律师将自行持续学习并接受法律教育。","C":"否，因为该律师不参加继续法律教育课程就无法保持胜任能力。","D":"否，因为该律师没有律师执业过失保险。"},"explanationZh":"B项正确。律师必须向委托人提供胜任的代理。《职业行为示范规则》第1.1条。胜任的代理要求律师具备该项代理所需的法律知识与技能。为了保持所要求的知识和技能水平，律师应当通过持续学习和教育了解法律变化，并遵守其所适用的任何继续教育要求。《职业行为示范规则》第1.1条。就维持律师的胜任能力而言，自主学习与参加继续法律教育课程同样有效；但如果该州要求律师参加此类课程而他拒绝参加，则其行为将属不当。\n\nA项错误。律师必须保持胜任能力，而不能以成本为由不这样做。此外，该选项暗示继续法律教育课程是律师维持胜任能力的唯一方式，这并不正确。\n\nC项错误。如上所述，继续法律教育课程并不是律师保持胜任能力的唯一方式。\n\nD项错误。律师是否购买执业过失保险，与其维持提供胜任代理所需法律知识和技能的义务无关。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-002","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":2,"topic":"Model Rule 1.1 / Model Rule 1.16(b)(6)","question":"An attorney is a newly admitted member of the bar. A partner in his firm was recognized as an expert in securities regulation law. A corporation retained the firm to qualify the corporation’s stock for public sale. After accepting the matter, the partner preferred to spend his time on cases with larger fee potential, so he assigned the corporation matter to the attorney. The attorney told the partner he knew nothing about securities regulation law and that he had too little time to prepare himself for the corporation’s sale without substantial help from the partner. The partner refused to help and directed the attorney to proceed. Was the partner’s conduct proper in this matter?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because as a member of the bar, the attorney is licensed to handle any legal matter.","B":"Yes, because the partner may withdraw from a case if work on it would cause him unreasonable financial hardship.","C":"No, because the partner knew the attorney was not competent to handle the matter, and the partner failed to provide supervision adequate to protect the client’s interest.","D":"No, because the corporation had not given the partner permission to assign the attorney to work on the matter."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.1, an attorney must have the legal knowledge and skill necessary for the representation. In determining whether an attorney has the requisite knowledge and skill in a particular matter, relevant factors include the relative complexity and specialized nature of the matter, the attorney’s general experience, the attorney’s training and experience in the field in question, the preparation and study the attorney is able to give the matter, and whether it is feasible to refer the matter to, or associate or consult with, an attorney of established competence in the field in question. The standard of competence is usually that of a general practitioner, unless the attorney claims to be an expert in a particular area of law. Here, it appears that the corporation retained the firm because the partner held himself out as an expert in securities regulation law, a complex and specialized area of the law, and the attorney, a newly minted attorney, specifically stated he did not have the time to prepare himself to handle the matter competently. Consequently, the attorney would be in violation of the Rule if he continues to represent the corporation without the required level of competence, and the partner would be in violation of the Rule for directing him to do so and failing to give the attorney proper supervision. A is incorrect. Although the attorney is a member of the bar and therefore licensed to practice law, he is admittedly incompetent to handle this particular representation without assistance from an attorney who is competent in this area. Consequently, the partner is in violation for directing the attorney to represent the corporation without any assistance. B is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.16(b)(6), an attorney may withdraw from a case if working on it would cause unreasonable financial hardship on the attorney. However, the partner willingly accepted the corporation as a client, and the mere fact the partner “prefers” to work on cases with larger fee potential would not meet the standard for withdrawal. D is incorrect. If the assignment is appropriate, a client generally does not need to give permission for an attorney to assign that representation to an associate. However, because a client’s consent does not excuse incompetent representation, the representation would be improper even if the corporation had given permission to assign the matter to the attorney.","rule":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.1, an attorney must have the legal knowledge and skill necessary for the representation. In determining whether an attorney has the requisite knowledge and skill in a particular matter, relevant factors include the relative complexity and specialized nature of the matter, the attorney’s general experience, the attorney’s training and experience in the field in question, the preparation and study the attorney is able to give the matter, and whether it is feasible to refer the matter to, or associate or consult with, an attorney of established competence in the field in question. The standard of competence is usually that of a general practitioner, unless the attorney claims to be an expert in a particular area of law. Here, it appears that the corporation retained the firm because the partner held himself out as an expert in securities regulation law, a complex and specialized area of the law, and the attorney, a newly minted attorney, specifically stated he did not have the time to prepare himself to handle the matter competently. Consequently, the attorney would be in violation of the Rule if he continues to represent the corporation without the required level of competence, and the partner would be in violation of the Rule for directing him to do so and failing to give the attorney proper supervision. A is incorrect. Although the attorney is a member of the bar and therefore licensed to practice law, he is admittedly incompetent to handle this particular representation without assistance from an attorney who is competent in this area. Consequently, the partner is in violation for directing the attorney to represent the corporation without any assistance. B is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.16(b)(6), an attorney may withdraw from a case if working on it would cause unreasonable financial hardship on the attorney. However, the partner willingly accepted the corporation as a client, and the mere fact the partner “prefers” to work on cases with larger fee potential would not meet the standard for withdrawal. D is incorrect. If the assignment is appropriate, a client generally does not need to give permission for an attorney to assign that representation to an associate. However, because a client’s consent does not excuse incompetent representation, the representation would be improper even if the corporation had given permission to assign the matter to the attorney.","irac":{"issue":"Was the partner’s conduct proper in this matter?","rule":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.1, an attorney must have the legal knowledge and skill necessary for the representation. In determining whether an attorney has the requisite knowledge and skill in a particular matter, relevant factors include the relative complexity and specialized nature of the matter, the attorney’s general experience, the attorney’s training and experience in the field in question, the preparation and study the attorney is able to give the matter, and whether it is feasible to refer the matter to, or associate or consult with, an attorney of established competence in the field in question. The standard of competence is usually that of a general practitioner, unless the attorney claims to be an expert in a particular area of law. Here, it appears that the corporation retained the firm because the partner held himself out as an expert in securities regulation law, a complex and specialized area of the law, and the attorney, a newly minted attorney, specifically stated he did not have the time to prepare himself to handle the matter competently. Consequently, the attorney would be in violation of the Rule if he continues to represent the corporation without the required level of competence, and the partner would be in violation of the Rule for directing him to do so and failing to give the attorney proper supervision. A is incorrect. Although the attorney is a member of the bar and therefore licensed to practice law, he is admittedly incompetent to handle this particular representation without assistance from an attorney who is competent in this area. Consequently, the partner is in violation for directing the attorney to represent the corporation without any assistance. B is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.16(b)(6), an attorney may withdraw from a case if working on it would cause unreasonable financial hardship on the attorney. However, the partner willingly accepted the corporation as a client, and the mere fact the partner “prefers” to work on cases with larger fee potential would not meet the standard for withdrawal. D is incorrect. If the assignment is appropriate, a client generally does not need to give permission for an attorney to assign that representation to an associate. However, because a client’s consent does not excuse incompetent representation, the representation would be improper even if the corporation had given permission to assign the matter to the attorney.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.1, an attorney must have the legal knowledge and skill necessary for the representation. In determining whether an attorney has the requisite knowledge and skill in a particular matter, relevant factors include the relative complexity and specialized nature of the matter, the attorney’s general experience, the attorney’s training and experience in the field in question, the preparation and study the attorney is able to give the matter, and whether it is feasible to refer the matter to, or associate or consult with, an attorney of established competence in the field in question. The standard of competence is usually that of a general practitioner, unless the attorney claims to be an expert in a particular area of law. Here, it appears that the corporation retained the firm because the partner held himself out as an expert in securities regulation law, a complex and specialized area of the law, and the attorney, a newly minted attorney, specifically stated he did not have the time to prepare himself to handle the matter competently. Consequently, the attorney would be in violation of the Rule if he continues to represent the corporation without the required level of competence, and the partner would be in violation of the Rule for directing him to do so and failing to give the attorney proper supervision. A is incorrect. Although the attorney is a member of the bar and therefore licensed to practice law, he is admittedly incompetent to handle this particular representation without assistance from an attorney who is competent in this area. Consequently, the partner is in violation for directing the attorney to represent the corporation without any assistance. B is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.16(b)(6), an attorney may withdraw from a case if working on it would cause unreasonable financial hardship on the attorney. However, the partner willingly accepted the corporation as a client, and the mere fact the partner “prefers” to work on cases with larger fee potential would not meet the standard for withdrawal. D is incorrect. If the assignment is appropriate, a client generally does not need to give permission for an attorney to assign that representation to an associate. However, because a client’s consent does not excuse incompetent representation, the representation would be improper even if the corporation had given permission to assign the matter to the attorney.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.1","Model Rule 1.16(b)(6)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[48,49],"sourceAnswerPages":[86,87],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师刚刚取得律师执业资格。他所在事务所的一名合伙人被公认为证券监管法专家。一家公司聘请该事务所办理其股票公开发行资格事宜。接受委托后，该合伙人更愿意把时间花在潜在律师费更高的案件上，于是把这家公司的事务交给该律师办理。该律师告诉合伙人，自己对证券监管法一无所知，而且如果没有合伙人的大量帮助，他没有足够时间在该公司公开发行股票前做好准备。合伙人拒绝提供帮助，并指示该律师继续办理。该合伙人在此事中的行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该律师既然已经取得律师资格，就有执照处理任何法律事务。","B":"是，因为如果办理某案会给合伙人造成不合理的经济困难，合伙人可以退出代理。","C":"否，因为合伙人知道该律师没有能力胜任此事，却未提供足以保护委托人利益的监督。","D":"否，因为该公司没有允许合伙人把该事务交由该律师办理。"},"explanationZh":"C项正确。根据《职业行为示范规则》第1.1条，律师必须具备该项代理所需的法律知识和技能。判断一名律师是否具备处理特定事项所需的知识与技能时，相关因素包括：事项的相对复杂程度和专业性；律师的一般执业经验；律师在有关领域所受的训练和积累的经验；律师能够为该事项投入的准备与研究；以及是否能够把该事项转交给该领域中具备公认胜任能力的律师，或者与该律师合作、向其咨询。通常采用一般执业律师的胜任标准；但如果律师宣称自己是某一法律领域的专家，则标准会有所不同。\n\n本题中，该公司显然是因为该合伙人对外宣称自己是证券监管法专家而聘请该事务所的，而证券监管法又是复杂且高度专业化的法律领域。该律师刚取得执业资格，并明确表示自己没有足够时间做好准备以胜任该事项。因此，如果他在不具备所需胜任能力的情况下继续代理该公司，就会违反规则；合伙人指示他这样做且未给予适当监督，同样违反规则。\n\nA项错误。尽管该律师已经取得执业资格，依法可以从事法律业务，但他已经承认，如果得不到一名熟悉该领域的律师协助，自己就不能胜任这一特定代理。因此，合伙人在不提供任何协助的情况下指示他代理该公司，构成违规。\n\nB项错误。根据《职业行为示范规则》第1.16(b)(6)条，如果继续办理案件会给律师造成不合理的经济负担，律师可以退出代理。但是，该合伙人自愿接受了公司的委托；他仅仅“更愿意”办理潜在律师费更高的案件，并不符合退出代理的标准。\n\nD项错误。如果内部工作分配本身适当，通常无需取得委托人的同意，律师即可把有关代理工作分配给事务所的受雇律师。但是，委托人的同意并不能使不胜任的代理变得正当；因此，即使公司允许把此事交给该律师办理，该代理仍属不当。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-003","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":3,"topic":"Model Rule 8.4 / Model Rule 8.4(c) / Model Rule 8.4(b)","question":"An attorney is a member of the bar in one state and is also licensed as a stockbroker in another. In his application for renewal of his stockbroker’s license the attorney knowingly filed a false financial statement. Soon thereafter, the attorney is convicted in the second state for knowingly filing a false financial statement. Is the attorney subject to discipline as a member of the bar of the first state?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because his actions involve dishonesty or misrepresentation.","B":"Yes, because he was first convicted of a criminal offense in the second state.","C":"No, because his action was not done in his capacity as an attorney.","D":"No, because his action was not in the first state."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.4 defines the acts that constitute misconduct by a lawyer. Model Rule 8.4(c) describes as professional misconduct any conduct “involving dishonesty, fraud, deceit or misrepresentation.” The definition is not limited to misconduct in connection with the practice of law but, under Comment [2], extends to all offenses that “indicate lack of those characteristics relevant to law practice,” including violence, dishonesty, and breach of trust. Also, misconduct is misconduct wherever it occurs—whether in the state in which the attorney is licensed to practice or elsewhere. Here, the attorney has knowingly filed a false financial statement in connection with a professional license application, a clear violation of the Rule. B is incorrect. The definition of misconduct extends far beyond conviction for a criminal offense. Comment [2] to Model Rule 8.4 states, “Many kinds of illegal conduct reflect adversely on fitness to practice law, such as offenses involving fraud and the offense of willful failure to file an income tax return.” C is incorrect. A lawyer is judged not only by his or her conduct in his or her capacity as lawyer, but by all conduct that measures his or her honesty, trustworthiness, and fitness as a lawyer. See Model Rule 8.4(b). D is incorrect. Geography has nothing to do with the concept of misconduct. Misconduct is misconduct wherever it occurs.","rule":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.4 defines the acts that constitute misconduct by a lawyer. Model Rule 8.4(c) describes as professional misconduct any conduct “involving dishonesty, fraud, deceit or misrepresentation.” The definition is not limited to misconduct in connection with the practice of law but, under Comment [2], extends to all offenses that “indicate lack of those characteristics relevant to law practice,” including violence, dishonesty, and breach of trust. Also, misconduct is misconduct wherever it occurs—whether in the state in which the attorney is licensed to practice or elsewhere. Here, the attorney has knowingly filed a false financial statement in connection with a professional license application, a clear violation of the Rule. B is incorrect. The definition of misconduct extends far beyond conviction for a criminal offense. Comment [2] to Model Rule 8.4 states, “Many kinds of illegal conduct reflect adversely on fitness to practice law, such as offenses involving fraud and the offense of willful failure to file an income tax return.” C is incorrect. A lawyer is judged not only by his or her conduct in his or her capacity as lawyer, but by all conduct that measures his or her honesty, trustworthiness, and fitness as a lawyer. See Model Rule 8.4(b). D is incorrect. Geography has nothing to do with the concept of misconduct. Misconduct is misconduct wherever it occurs.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline as a member of the bar of the first state?","rule":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.4 defines the acts that constitute misconduct by a lawyer. Model Rule 8.4(c) describes as professional misconduct any conduct “involving dishonesty, fraud, deceit or misrepresentation.” The definition is not limited to misconduct in connection with the practice of law but, under Comment [2], extends to all offenses that “indicate lack of those characteristics relevant to law practice,” including violence, dishonesty, and breach of trust. Also, misconduct is misconduct wherever it occurs—whether in the state in which the attorney is licensed to practice or elsewhere. Here, the attorney has knowingly filed a false financial statement in connection with a professional license application, a clear violation of the Rule. B is incorrect. The definition of misconduct extends far beyond conviction for a criminal offense. Comment [2] to Model Rule 8.4 states, “Many kinds of illegal conduct reflect adversely on fitness to practice law, such as offenses involving fraud and the offense of willful failure to file an income tax return.” C is incorrect. A lawyer is judged not only by his or her conduct in his or her capacity as lawyer, but by all conduct that measures his or her honesty, trustworthiness, and fitness as a lawyer. See Model Rule 8.4(b). D is incorrect. Geography has nothing to do with the concept of misconduct. Misconduct is misconduct wherever it occurs.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.4 defines the acts that constitute misconduct by a lawyer. Model Rule 8.4(c) describes as professional misconduct any conduct “involving dishonesty, fraud, deceit or misrepresentation.” The definition is not limited to misconduct in connection with the practice of law but, under Comment [2], extends to all offenses that “indicate lack of those characteristics relevant to law practice,” including violence, dishonesty, and breach of trust. Also, misconduct is misconduct wherever it occurs—whether in the state in which the attorney is licensed to practice or elsewhere. Here, the attorney has knowingly filed a false financial statement in connection with a professional license application, a clear violation of the Rule. B is incorrect. The definition of misconduct extends far beyond conviction for a criminal offense. Comment [2] to Model Rule 8.4 states, “Many kinds of illegal conduct reflect adversely on fitness to practice law, such as offenses involving fraud and the offense of willful failure to file an income tax return.” C is incorrect. A lawyer is judged not only by his or her conduct in his or her capacity as lawyer, but by all conduct that measures his or her honesty, trustworthiness, and fitness as a lawyer. See Model Rule 8.4(b). D is incorrect. Geography has nothing to do with the concept of misconduct. Misconduct is misconduct wherever it occurs.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 8.4","Model Rule 8.4(c)","Model Rule 8.4(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[49],"sourceAnswerPages":[87,88],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师是第一州的执业律师，同时还在第二州取得了证券经纪人执照。该律师在申请续期证券经纪人执照时，明知内容虚假仍提交了一份虚假财务报表。不久后，他在第二州因明知而提交虚假财务报表被定罪。作为第一州的执业律师，他是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为他的行为涉及不诚实或虚假陈述。","B":"是，因为他先在第二州被判犯有刑事罪。","C":"否，因为该行为并非以律师身份实施。","D":"否，因为该行为并非发生在第一州。"},"explanationZh":"A项正确。《职业行为示范规则》第8.4条界定了构成律师不当行为的行为。第8.4(c)条把任何“涉及不诚实、欺诈、欺骗或虚假陈述”的行为规定为职业不当行为。这一定义并不限于与法律执业有关的行为；根据评注[2]，它还延伸至一切“表明行为人缺乏法律执业所需相关品格”的违法行为，包括暴力、不诚实和背弃信任。此外，不当行为无论发生在哪里都仍是不当行为——无论是在律师获准执业的州，还是其他地方。本题中，该律师在申请一项职业执照时明知而提交虚假财务报表，显然违反该规则。\n\nB项错误。不当行为的范围远远超出因刑事犯罪被定罪的情形。《职业行为示范规则》第8.4条评注[2]指出：“许多种类的违法行为会对从事法律业务的适格性产生不利影响，例如涉及欺诈的犯罪以及故意不申报所得税的犯罪。”\n\nC项错误。评价律师时，不仅要看其以律师身份实施的行为，还要考察所有能够反映其诚实性、可信赖性和从事律师职业适格性的行为。参见《职业行为示范规则》第8.4(b)条。\n\nD项错误。行为发生的地域与是否构成不当行为无关。不当行为无论发生在哪里，都仍属不当行为。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-004","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":4,"topic":"Model Rule 7.3","question":"An attorney is a sole practitioner whose practice is largely in the areas of tax, wills, estates, and trusts. The attorney learned of a new Internal Revenue Service (IRS) regulation that probably affects the trust provisions in a will she prepared for a client two years ago. The attorney has not represented the client since she drew up the will, and believes another lawyer now represents her. She also has no interest in drawing up a new one for the client. Is the attorney subject to discipline if she calls the former client and advises her of the new IRS ruling and the need to revise the will?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney has reason to believe that the former client has another lawyer.","B":"Yes, because the attorney would be soliciting legal business from a person who is not a current client.","C":"No, because the attorney does not plan on preparing a new will for the former client.","D":"No, because the client is a former client of the attorney."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Lawyers are prohibited from soliciting business from prospective clients by in-person contact or live telephone contact. Model Rule 7.3. The key word is “prospective”—that is, “future” clients. The prohibition against solicitation does not extend to persons with whom the lawyer has had a prior personal or professional relationship. Consequently, the prohibition does not extend to contacts with former clients, personal friends, or family members. Here, the attorney is dealing with a former client whose interests may be adversely affected if the attorney does not advise her of the new IRS regulation. A is incorrect. The attorney is permitted to contact the woman as a former client whether or not she has reason to believe her former client has another attorney. The attorney has no way of knowing whether her former client has another attorney or if the other attorney is a specialist in tax law or is even aware of the new regulation. The Rule does not require the attorney to inquire into these facts. B is incorrect. The test is not whether the person is a current client but whether the person is a former client. For purposes of Model Rule 7.3, once a client always a client. C is incorrect. The attorney is free to offer and supply her services to her client in any way her former client may be willing to utilize them. The issue here is solicitation, not limits on the scope of representation.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Lawyers are prohibited from soliciting business from prospective clients by in-person contact or live telephone contact. Model Rule 7.3. The key word is “prospective”—that is, “future” clients. The prohibition against solicitation does not extend to persons with whom the lawyer has had a prior personal or professional relationship. Consequently, the prohibition does not extend to contacts with former clients, personal friends, or family members. Here, the attorney is dealing with a former client whose interests may be adversely affected if the attorney does not advise her of the new IRS regulation. A is incorrect. The attorney is permitted to contact the woman as a former client whether or not she has reason to believe her former client has another attorney. The attorney has no way of knowing whether her former client has another attorney or if the other attorney is a specialist in tax law or is even aware of the new regulation. The Rule does not require the attorney to inquire into these facts. B is incorrect. The test is not whether the person is a current client but whether the person is a former client. For purposes of Model Rule 7.3, once a client always a client. C is incorrect. The attorney is free to offer and supply her services to her client in any way her former client may be willing to utilize them. The issue here is solicitation, not limits on the scope of representation.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline if she calls the former client and advises her of the new IRS ruling and the need to revise the will?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Lawyers are prohibited from soliciting business from prospective clients by in-person contact or live telephone contact. Model Rule 7.3. The key word is “prospective”—that is, “future” clients. The prohibition against solicitation does not extend to persons with whom the lawyer has had a prior personal or professional relationship. Consequently, the prohibition does not extend to contacts with former clients, personal friends, or family members. Here, the attorney is dealing with a former client whose interests may be adversely affected if the attorney does not advise her of the new IRS regulation. A is incorrect. The attorney is permitted to contact the woman as a former client whether or not she has reason to believe her former client has another attorney. The attorney has no way of knowing whether her former client has another attorney or if the other attorney is a specialist in tax law or is even aware of the new regulation. The Rule does not require the attorney to inquire into these facts. B is incorrect. The test is not whether the person is a current client but whether the person is a former client. For purposes of Model Rule 7.3, once a client always a client. C is incorrect. The attorney is free to offer and supply her services to her client in any way her former client may be willing to utilize them. The issue here is solicitation, not limits on the scope of representation.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Lawyers are prohibited from soliciting business from prospective clients by in-person contact or live telephone contact. Model Rule 7.3. The key word is “prospective”—that is, “future” clients. The prohibition against solicitation does not extend to persons with whom the lawyer has had a prior personal or professional relationship. Consequently, the prohibition does not extend to contacts with former clients, personal friends, or family members. Here, the attorney is dealing with a former client whose interests may be adversely affected if the attorney does not advise her of the new IRS regulation. A is incorrect. The attorney is permitted to contact the woman as a former client whether or not she has reason to believe her former client has another attorney. The attorney has no way of knowing whether her former client has another attorney or if the other attorney is a specialist in tax law or is even aware of the new regulation. The Rule does not require the attorney to inquire into these facts. B is incorrect. The test is not whether the person is a current client but whether the person is a former client. For purposes of Model Rule 7.3, once a client always a client. C is incorrect. The attorney is free to offer and supply her services to her client in any way her former client may be willing to utilize them. The issue here is solicitation, not limits on the scope of representation.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[49,50],"sourceAnswerPages":[88],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名独立执业律师的业务主要涉及税法、遗嘱、遗产和信托。她得知美国国税局（IRS）发布了一项新规定，该规定很可能影响她两年前为一名委托人起草的遗嘱中的信托条款。自起草该遗嘱后，她没有再代理该委托人，并且相信该委托人目前由另一名律师代理。她本人也没有兴趣为该委托人重新起草一份遗嘱。如果她给这名前委托人打电话，告知新的国税局规定以及修改遗嘱的必要性，她是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该律师有理由相信这名前委托人已经另有律师。","B":"是，因为该律师是在向一名现非其委托人的人招揽法律业务。","C":"否，因为该律师不打算为这名前委托人起草新遗嘱。","D":"否，因为该人是该律师的前委托人。"},"explanationZh":"D项正确。律师不得通过当面接触或实时电话联系向潜在委托人招揽业务。《职业行为示范规则》第7.3条。关键在于“潜在”一词，也就是“未来的”委托人。禁止招揽的规定并不延伸至与律师先前存在个人关系或职业关系的人。因此，该禁令不适用于前委托人、私人朋友或家庭成员。本题中，律师联系的是一名前委托人；如果律师不把新的国税局规定告知她，她的利益可能受到不利影响。\n\nA项错误。无论律师是否有理由相信前委托人已经聘请另一名律师，她都可以联系这名前委托人。她无法确定前委托人是否确实另有律师，也无法确定另一名律师是否是税法专家或者是否知道这项新规定。规则并不要求她调查这些事实。\n\nB项错误。判断标准不是该人目前是否仍是委托人，而是该人是否曾经是律师的委托人。就《职业行为示范规则》第7.3条而言，一旦建立过委托关系，即属于规则所认可的既有关系。\n\nC项错误。只要前委托人愿意接受，律师可以提出并以该前委托人愿意采用的任何方式提供服务。本题所涉及的是招揽问题，而不是代理范围的限制。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-005","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":5,"topic":"Model Rule 1.15(e)","question":"An attorney was retained to appeal the client’s criminal conviction and to seek bail pending appeal. The agreed fee for the appearance on the bail hearing was $50 per hour. The attorney received $800 from the client, of which $300 was a deposit to secure the attorney’s fee, and $500 was for bail costs in the event that bail was obtained. The attorney maintained two office bank accounts: a “Fee Account,” in which all fees were deposited and from which all office expenses were paid, and a “Clients’ Fund Account.” The attorney deposited the $800 in the “Clients’ Fund Account” the week before the bail hearing. The attorney expended six hours of time on the bail hearing. The effort to obtain bail was unsuccessful. Dissatisfied, the client immediately demanded return of the $800. It is now proper for the attorney to:","choices":{"A":"transfer the $800 to the “Fee Account.”","B":"transfer $300 to the “Fee Account” and leave $500 in the “Clients’ Fund Account” until the attorney’s fee for the final appeal is determined.","C":"transfer $300 to the “Fee Account” and send the client a $500 check on the “Clients’ Fund Account.”","D":"send the client a $500 check and leave $300 in the “Clients’ Fund Account” until the matter is resolved."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. The answer recognizes the three basic principles a lawyer must observe when dealing with client funds: (1) client funds must always be kept separate from the lawyer’s funds in a designated clients’ escrow account; (2) as soon as the client’s interest in the funds is fixed, the client’s money must be delivered to the client; and (3) if both the client and the attorney claim any interest in any part of the funds—for example, if a dispute arises as to the lawyer’s fees—the funds must remain in the separate client account until there is an accounting and their respective interests can be severed. Model 1.15. Here, the attorney’s maximum claim was for fees totaling 6 hours × $50 = $300. When the bail hearing was completed, the client’s interest in the advance was $500. The attorney had a duty to return this promptly. Because the client had demanded $800, the lawyer’s fee of $300 was in dispute. Consequently, the money representing the fee had to be retained in the Clients’ Fund Account until the dispute was resolved. A is incorrect. The funds in dispute totaled only $300. The balance of $500 was not in dispute and belonged to the client when it was not applied to the bail costs. The attorney was under a duty to return $500 to the client because the money was not used for the purpose intended. B is incorrect. Because the fee of $300 was in dispute, the attorney had no right to transfer that sum to his fee account. He was required to retain the funds in his Clients’ Fund Account until the dispute was resolved. Further, the attorney had no right to retain the balance of $500 in either account pending the appeal. The money was deposited with the attorney only in connection with the bail application, not the appeal itself. C is incorrect. The attorney was right to send the $500 to the client, but wrong in transferring $300 to his fee account. So long as his fee was in dispute, the $300 had to be retained in the Clients’ Fund Account. Model Rule 1.15(e).","rule":"D is the correct answer. The answer recognizes the three basic principles a lawyer must observe when dealing with client funds: (1) client funds must always be kept separate from the lawyer’s funds in a designated clients’ escrow account; (2) as soon as the client’s interest in the funds is fixed, the client’s money must be delivered to the client; and (3) if both the client and the attorney claim any interest in any part of the funds—for example, if a dispute arises as to the lawyer’s fees—the funds must remain in the separate client account until there is an accounting and their respective interests can be severed. Model 1.15. Here, the attorney’s maximum claim was for fees totaling 6 hours × $50 = $300. When the bail hearing was completed, the client’s interest in the advance was $500. The attorney had a duty to return this promptly. Because the client had demanded $800, the lawyer’s fee of $300 was in dispute. Consequently, the money representing the fee had to be retained in the Clients’ Fund Account until the dispute was resolved. A is incorrect. The funds in dispute totaled only $300. The balance of $500 was not in dispute and belonged to the client when it was not applied to the bail costs. The attorney was under a duty to return $500 to the client because the money was not used for the purpose intended. B is incorrect. Because the fee of $300 was in dispute, the attorney had no right to transfer that sum to his fee account. He was required to retain the funds in his Clients’ Fund Account until the dispute was resolved. Further, the attorney had no right to retain the balance of $500 in either account pending the appeal. The money was deposited with the attorney only in connection with the bail application, not the appeal itself. C is incorrect. The attorney was right to send the $500 to the client, but wrong in transferring $300 to his fee account. So long as his fee was in dispute, the $300 had to be retained in the Clients’ Fund Account. Model Rule 1.15(e).","irac":{"issue":"An attorney was retained to appeal the client’s criminal conviction and to seek bail pending appeal. The agreed fee for the appearance on the bail hearing was $50 per hour. The attorney received $800 from the client, of which $300 was a deposit to secure the attorney’s fee, and $500 was for bail costs in the event that bail was obtained. The attorney maintained two office bank accounts: a “Fee Account,” in which all fees were deposited and from which all office expenses were paid, and a “Clients’ Fund Account.” The attorney deposited the $800 in the “Clients’ Fund Account” the week before the bail hearing. The attorney expended six hours of time on the bail hearing. The effort to obtain bail was unsuccessful. Dissatisfied, the client immediately demanded return of the $800. It is now proper for the attorney to:","rule":"D is the correct answer. The answer recognizes the three basic principles a lawyer must observe when dealing with client funds: (1) client funds must always be kept separate from the lawyer’s funds in a designated clients’ escrow account; (2) as soon as the client’s interest in the funds is fixed, the client’s money must be delivered to the client; and (3) if both the client and the attorney claim any interest in any part of the funds—for example, if a dispute arises as to the lawyer’s fees—the funds must remain in the separate client account until there is an accounting and their respective interests can be severed. Model 1.15. Here, the attorney’s maximum claim was for fees totaling 6 hours × $50 = $300. When the bail hearing was completed, the client’s interest in the advance was $500. The attorney had a duty to return this promptly. Because the client had demanded $800, the lawyer’s fee of $300 was in dispute. Consequently, the money representing the fee had to be retained in the Clients’ Fund Account until the dispute was resolved. A is incorrect. The funds in dispute totaled only $300. The balance of $500 was not in dispute and belonged to the client when it was not applied to the bail costs. The attorney was under a duty to return $500 to the client because the money was not used for the purpose intended. B is incorrect. Because the fee of $300 was in dispute, the attorney had no right to transfer that sum to his fee account. He was required to retain the funds in his Clients’ Fund Account until the dispute was resolved. Further, the attorney had no right to retain the balance of $500 in either account pending the appeal. The money was deposited with the attorney only in connection with the bail application, not the appeal itself. C is incorrect. The attorney was right to send the $500 to the client, but wrong in transferring $300 to his fee account. So long as his fee was in dispute, the $300 had to be retained in the Clients’ Fund Account. Model Rule 1.15(e).","analysis":"D is the correct answer. The answer recognizes the three basic principles a lawyer must observe when dealing with client funds: (1) client funds must always be kept separate from the lawyer’s funds in a designated clients’ escrow account; (2) as soon as the client’s interest in the funds is fixed, the client’s money must be delivered to the client; and (3) if both the client and the attorney claim any interest in any part of the funds—for example, if a dispute arises as to the lawyer’s fees—the funds must remain in the separate client account until there is an accounting and their respective interests can be severed. Model 1.15. Here, the attorney’s maximum claim was for fees totaling 6 hours × $50 = $300. When the bail hearing was completed, the client’s interest in the advance was $500. The attorney had a duty to return this promptly. Because the client had demanded $800, the lawyer’s fee of $300 was in dispute. Consequently, the money representing the fee had to be retained in the Clients’ Fund Account until the dispute was resolved. A is incorrect. The funds in dispute totaled only $300. The balance of $500 was not in dispute and belonged to the client when it was not applied to the bail costs. The attorney was under a duty to return $500 to the client because the money was not used for the purpose intended. B is incorrect. Because the fee of $300 was in dispute, the attorney had no right to transfer that sum to his fee account. He was required to retain the funds in his Clients’ Fund Account until the dispute was resolved. Further, the attorney had no right to retain the balance of $500 in either account pending the appeal. The money was deposited with the attorney only in connection with the bail application, not the appeal itself. C is incorrect. The attorney was right to send the $500 to the client, but wrong in transferring $300 to his fee account. So long as his fee was in dispute, the $300 had to be retained in the Clients’ Fund Account. Model Rule 1.15(e).","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.15(e)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[50],"sourceAnswerPages":[88,89],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师受聘就委托人的刑事定罪提出上诉，并申请在上诉期间保释。双方约定，该律师出席保释听证的收费标准为每小时50美元。律师从委托人处收到800美元，其中300美元是为担保律师费而支付的预付款，500美元则用于在获准保释时支付保释相关费用。律师设有两个事务所银行账户：一个是存入全部律师费并支付全部办公支出的“律师费账户”，另一个是“客户资金账户”。律师在保释听证前一周把800美元全部存入“客户资金账户”。他为保释听证投入了六小时，但申请保释未获成功。委托人对此不满，立即要求返还全部800美元。此时，律师适当的做法是：","choicesZh":{"A":"把800美元全部转入“律师费账户”。","B":"把300美元转入“律师费账户”，并把500美元留在“客户资金账户”中，直至确定最终上诉的律师费。","C":"把300美元转入“律师费账户”，并从“客户资金账户”开具一张500美元的支票交给委托人。","D":"向委托人开具一张500美元的支票，并把300美元留在“客户资金账户”中，直至争议解决。"},"explanationZh":"D项正确。该答案体现了律师处理委托人资金时必须遵守的三项基本原则：（1）委托人的资金必须始终与律师自己的资金分开，存放在指定的客户托管账户中；（2）一旦委托人对资金享有的权益已经确定，就必须把属于委托人的款项交付给委托人；（3）如果委托人与律师都对某部分资金主张权益——例如双方就律师费发生争议——该笔资金必须继续留在独立的客户账户中，直至完成核算并能够分清双方各自的权益。《职业行为示范规则》第1.15条。\n\n本题中，律师最多可以主张的律师费为6小时×每小时50美元，即300美元。保释听证结束后，委托人对预付款中500美元的权益已经确定，律师有义务及时退还。由于委托人要求返还全部800美元，律师对300美元律师费的权利存在争议。因此，代表该律师费的300美元必须继续留在“客户资金账户”中，直至争议解决。\n\nA项错误。存在争议的资金只有300美元。其余500美元并无争议；该款未用于保释费用后即属于委托人。由于这500美元没有用于原定目的，律师有义务将其返还给委托人。\n\nB项错误。由于300美元律师费存在争议，律师无权把该款转入自己的律师费账户；在争议解决前，他必须把该款留在“客户资金账户”中。此外，律师无权在上诉结束前把剩余500美元留在任何账户中。委托人把这笔钱交给律师，只是为了办理保释申请，而不是为了上诉本身。\n\nC项错误。律师把500美元退还委托人是正确的，但把300美元转入自己的律师费账户是错误的。只要律师费仍存在争议，这300美元就必须留在“客户资金账户”中。《职业行为示范规则》第1.15(e)条。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-006","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":6,"topic":"Rule 2.2","question":"A judge has been assigned to try a criminal prosecution by the state against a defendant. Ten years previously, the judge, while serving as a deputy attorney general in the state, led an investigation of the defendant for suspected criminal conduct. The investigation did not establish any basis for prosecution. None of the matters previously investigated is involved in or affects the present prosecution. The judge had completely forgotten the earlier investigation, and had no prejudice toward the defendant. Is it proper for the judge to try the case?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because none of the matters previously investigated is involved in or affects the present case.","B":"Yes, because there is no indication the judge might be prejudiced against the defendant because of the prior investigation.","C":"No, because the judge had substantial responsibility in initiating the previous investigation of defendant.","D":"No, because the judge had substantial responsibility in determining that the previous investigation did not establish any basis for prosecution."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. A judge is expected to perform his or her duties fairly, impartially, and without prejudice against any litigant. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.2. The test is the judge’s mindset as perceived by a reasonable observer. If the judge has a personal bias toward a party or his or her lawyer, or personal knowledge of disputed evidentiary facts concerning the proceeding, he or she must disqualify himself or herself. Also, if he or she or a former law partner has served either side as a lawyer concerning the matter, or if he or she has served as a material witness in the matter, he or she must disqualify himself or herself. B correctly states the issue facing the judge. However, if there is any chance that his prior investigation of the defendant will prejudice his judgment, he must disqualify himself. A is incorrect because it misstates the test for disqualification. It doesn’t matter that the prior investigation is unrelated to the present case. The relevant question is whether the judge is adversely influenced by his participation in the investigation. C is incorrect. Although it states a basis for suspecting prejudice by the judge against the defendant, it is not as good an answer as B, which correctly frames the issue as one of potential prejudice. D is incorrect. This option does not revolve around the judge’s state of mind. It is just as reasonable to conclude that the judge was not prejudiced by the prior investigation as that he was prejudiced. The issue is whether he was and is actually prejudiced against the defendant. B is the best formula for deciding that issue.","rule":"B is the correct answer. A judge is expected to perform his or her duties fairly, impartially, and without prejudice against any litigant. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.2. The test is the judge’s mindset as perceived by a reasonable observer. If the judge has a personal bias toward a party or his or her lawyer, or personal knowledge of disputed evidentiary facts concerning the proceeding, he or she must disqualify himself or herself. Also, if he or she or a former law partner has served either side as a lawyer concerning the matter, or if he or she has served as a material witness in the matter, he or she must disqualify himself or herself. B correctly states the issue facing the judge. However, if there is any chance that his prior investigation of the defendant will prejudice his judgment, he must disqualify himself. A is incorrect because it misstates the test for disqualification. It doesn’t matter that the prior investigation is unrelated to the present case. The relevant question is whether the judge is adversely influenced by his participation in the investigation. C is incorrect. Although it states a basis for suspecting prejudice by the judge against the defendant, it is not as good an answer as B, which correctly frames the issue as one of potential prejudice. D is incorrect. This option does not revolve around the judge’s state of mind. It is just as reasonable to conclude that the judge was not prejudiced by the prior investigation as that he was prejudiced. The issue is whether he was and is actually prejudiced against the defendant. B is the best formula for deciding that issue.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the judge to try the case?","rule":"B is the correct answer. A judge is expected to perform his or her duties fairly, impartially, and without prejudice against any litigant. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.2. The test is the judge’s mindset as perceived by a reasonable observer. If the judge has a personal bias toward a party or his or her lawyer, or personal knowledge of disputed evidentiary facts concerning the proceeding, he or she must disqualify himself or herself. Also, if he or she or a former law partner has served either side as a lawyer concerning the matter, or if he or she has served as a material witness in the matter, he or she must disqualify himself or herself. B correctly states the issue facing the judge. However, if there is any chance that his prior investigation of the defendant will prejudice his judgment, he must disqualify himself. A is incorrect because it misstates the test for disqualification. It doesn’t matter that the prior investigation is unrelated to the present case. The relevant question is whether the judge is adversely influenced by his participation in the investigation. C is incorrect. Although it states a basis for suspecting prejudice by the judge against the defendant, it is not as good an answer as B, which correctly frames the issue as one of potential prejudice. D is incorrect. This option does not revolve around the judge’s state of mind. It is just as reasonable to conclude that the judge was not prejudiced by the prior investigation as that he was prejudiced. The issue is whether he was and is actually prejudiced against the defendant. B is the best formula for deciding that issue.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. A judge is expected to perform his or her duties fairly, impartially, and without prejudice against any litigant. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.2. The test is the judge’s mindset as perceived by a reasonable observer. If the judge has a personal bias toward a party or his or her lawyer, or personal knowledge of disputed evidentiary facts concerning the proceeding, he or she must disqualify himself or herself. Also, if he or she or a former law partner has served either side as a lawyer concerning the matter, or if he or she has served as a material witness in the matter, he or she must disqualify himself or herself. B correctly states the issue facing the judge. However, if there is any chance that his prior investigation of the defendant will prejudice his judgment, he must disqualify himself. A is incorrect because it misstates the test for disqualification. It doesn’t matter that the prior investigation is unrelated to the present case. The relevant question is whether the judge is adversely influenced by his participation in the investigation. C is incorrect. Although it states a basis for suspecting prejudice by the judge against the defendant, it is not as good an answer as B, which correctly frames the issue as one of potential prejudice. D is incorrect. This option does not revolve around the judge’s state of mind. It is just as reasonable to conclude that the judge was not prejudiced by the prior investigation as that he was prejudiced. The issue is whether he was and is actually prejudiced against the defendant. B is the best formula for deciding that issue.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 2.2"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[50,51],"sourceAnswerPages":[89,90],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名法官被指派审理州政府对一名被告提起的刑事案件。十年前，该法官担任本州副总检察长期间，曾负责调查这名被告涉嫌实施的犯罪行为。该调查没有发现足以起诉的依据。先前调查的事项均不涉及也不影响目前的刑事案件。法官已经完全忘记了早先的调查，对被告也不存在任何偏见。由该法官审理此案是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为先前调查的事项均不涉及也不影响本案。","B":"是，因为没有迹象表明法官会因先前的调查而对被告产生偏见。","C":"否，因为法官在启动先前针对被告的调查时承担了重要责任。","D":"否，因为法官在认定先前调查没有形成起诉依据时承担了重要责任。"},"explanationZh":"B项正确。法官应当公平、公正地履行职责，不得对任何诉讼参与方抱有偏见。《司法行为示范准则》第2.2条。判断标准是：一名合理观察者会如何看待法官的心态。如果法官对某一方或其律师存在个人偏见，或者对该程序中有争议的证据事实具有个人了解，法官必须回避。如果法官本人或其以前的法律合伙人曾就同一事项担任任何一方的律师，或者法官本人曾就该事项担任重要证人，也必须回避。\n\nB项正确指出了法官所面对的核心问题。不过，如果法官先前调查被告的经历有任何可能影响其判断，他就必须回避。\n\nA项错误，因为它错误表述了回避标准。先前调查与本案无关并非决定性因素；相关问题是，法官是否会因参与先前调查而受到不利影响。\n\nC项错误。该事实确实可能使人怀疑法官对被告存在偏见，但该答案不如B项准确，因为B项正确地把问题表述为是否存在潜在偏见。\n\nD项错误。该选项没有围绕法官的实际心态展开。既可以合理推断先前调查没有使法官产生偏见，也可以推断其产生了偏见。真正的问题是法官过去和现在是否实际对被告抱有偏见；B项是判断该问题的最佳表述。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-007","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":7,"topic":"Model Rule 2.1 / Model Rule 1.5 / Model Rule 1.3","question":"An attorney represented a landlord in a variety of matters over several years. The attorney’s engagement letter did not specifically state that he would provide anything other than legal advice. An elderly widow living on public assistance filed suit against the landlord alleging that the landlord withheld without justification the security deposit on a rental unit that the widow vacated three years ago. She brought the action for herself, without counsel, in small claims court. The attorney investigated the claim and learned that it was legally barred by the applicable statute of limitations, although the widow’s underlying claim was meritorious. The attorney told the landlord of the legal defense, but emphasized that the widow’s claim was just and that, in all fairness, the security deposit should be returned to her. The attorney told the landlord: “I strongly recommend that you pay the widow the full amount with interest. It is against your long-term business interests to be known in the community as a landlord who routinely withholds security deposits even though the tenant leaves the apartment in good condition. Paying the claim now will prevent future headaches for you.” Ultimately, the landlord paid the widow’s claim. Was the attorney’s conduct proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the landlord did not object to the attorney’s advice and paid the widow’s claim.","B":"Yes, because the attorney may refer to both legal and nonlegal considerations in advising a client.","C":"No, because the attorney’s engagement letter did not inform the landlord that the attorney’s advice on the matter would include both legal and nonlegal considerations.","D":"No, because in advising the landlord to pay the full claim, the attorney failed to represent zealously the landlord’s legal interests."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. When a lawyer gives a client advice, he or she may rely on considerations other than those involved in giving purely technical legal advice. Model Rule 2.1. Consequently, it is proper for a lawyer to rely on moral, economic, and social factors in doing so, which is what the attorney did here. A is incorrect because the issue isn’t whether the landlord accepted the attorney’s advice—and, in fact, a lawyer should not be deterred from giving candid advice simply because the client might not be happy about it—but whether the attorney acted properly in going beyond the technical legal aspect of the case in advising the landlord. C is incorrect. An initial, or engagement, letter to the client is concerned with fees. Its purpose is to make clear what services the lawyer will provide and what he or she will charge for them, not in what form he or she will render advice. Model Rule 1.5. D is incorrect. It raises the issue of a lawyer’s responsibility to “act with . . . zeal in advocacy.” Model Rule 1.3, Comment [1]. But that isn’t really the issue here. The issue is what is the proper scope for the advice a lawyer gives a client. Not only is it proper for a lawyer to refer to considerations other than the law in advising a client, he or she also has a responsibility to exercise independent professional judgment in advising a client and to advise the client in a straightforward, honest manner, in the best interests of the client. Here, the attorney has done exactly that.","rule":"B is the correct answer. When a lawyer gives a client advice, he or she may rely on considerations other than those involved in giving purely technical legal advice. Model Rule 2.1. Consequently, it is proper for a lawyer to rely on moral, economic, and social factors in doing so, which is what the attorney did here. A is incorrect because the issue isn’t whether the landlord accepted the attorney’s advice—and, in fact, a lawyer should not be deterred from giving candid advice simply because the client might not be happy about it—but whether the attorney acted properly in going beyond the technical legal aspect of the case in advising the landlord. C is incorrect. An initial, or engagement, letter to the client is concerned with fees. Its purpose is to make clear what services the lawyer will provide and what he or she will charge for them, not in what form he or she will render advice. Model Rule 1.5. D is incorrect. It raises the issue of a lawyer’s responsibility to “act with . . . zeal in advocacy.” Model Rule 1.3, Comment [1]. But that isn’t really the issue here. The issue is what is the proper scope for the advice a lawyer gives a client. Not only is it proper for a lawyer to refer to considerations other than the law in advising a client, he or she also has a responsibility to exercise independent professional judgment in advising a client and to advise the client in a straightforward, honest manner, in the best interests of the client. Here, the attorney has done exactly that.","irac":{"issue":"Was the attorney’s conduct proper?","rule":"B is the correct answer. When a lawyer gives a client advice, he or she may rely on considerations other than those involved in giving purely technical legal advice. Model Rule 2.1. Consequently, it is proper for a lawyer to rely on moral, economic, and social factors in doing so, which is what the attorney did here. A is incorrect because the issue isn’t whether the landlord accepted the attorney’s advice—and, in fact, a lawyer should not be deterred from giving candid advice simply because the client might not be happy about it—but whether the attorney acted properly in going beyond the technical legal aspect of the case in advising the landlord. C is incorrect. An initial, or engagement, letter to the client is concerned with fees. Its purpose is to make clear what services the lawyer will provide and what he or she will charge for them, not in what form he or she will render advice. Model Rule 1.5. D is incorrect. It raises the issue of a lawyer’s responsibility to “act with . . . zeal in advocacy.” Model Rule 1.3, Comment [1]. But that isn’t really the issue here. The issue is what is the proper scope for the advice a lawyer gives a client. Not only is it proper for a lawyer to refer to considerations other than the law in advising a client, he or she also has a responsibility to exercise independent professional judgment in advising a client and to advise the client in a straightforward, honest manner, in the best interests of the client. Here, the attorney has done exactly that.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. When a lawyer gives a client advice, he or she may rely on considerations other than those involved in giving purely technical legal advice. Model Rule 2.1. Consequently, it is proper for a lawyer to rely on moral, economic, and social factors in doing so, which is what the attorney did here. A is incorrect because the issue isn’t whether the landlord accepted the attorney’s advice—and, in fact, a lawyer should not be deterred from giving candid advice simply because the client might not be happy about it—but whether the attorney acted properly in going beyond the technical legal aspect of the case in advising the landlord. C is incorrect. An initial, or engagement, letter to the client is concerned with fees. Its purpose is to make clear what services the lawyer will provide and what he or she will charge for them, not in what form he or she will render advice. Model Rule 1.5. D is incorrect. It raises the issue of a lawyer’s responsibility to “act with . . . zeal in advocacy.” Model Rule 1.3, Comment [1]. But that isn’t really the issue here. The issue is what is the proper scope for the advice a lawyer gives a client. Not only is it proper for a lawyer to refer to considerations other than the law in advising a client, he or she also has a responsibility to exercise independent professional judgment in advising a client and to advise the client in a straightforward, honest manner, in the best interests of the client. Here, the attorney has done exactly that.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 2.1","Model Rule 1.5","Model Rule 1.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[51,52],"sourceAnswerPages":[90],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师多年来在各种事务中代理一名房东。律师的聘用函并未明确说明他会提供法律意见以外的其他建议。一名依靠公共救济生活的老年寡妇起诉该房东，称她三年前搬出租赁房屋时，房东无正当理由扣留了她的押金。她没有聘请律师，而是自行在小额索赔法院提起诉讼。该律师调查后发现，虽然寡妇的实体请求有理由，但该请求已受适用诉讼时效的阻却。律师把这一法律抗辩告知房东，同时强调寡妇的请求是正当的，从公平角度看，应当把押金退还给她。\n\n律师对房东说：“我强烈建议你把全部金额连同利息支付给这名寡妇。如果你在社区中被认为是一个即使租户保持公寓状况良好也经常扣留押金的房东，这不符合你的长期商业利益。现在支付这项请求，可以避免你今后遇到更多麻烦。”最终，房东支付了寡妇所请求的款项。该律师的行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为房东没有反对律师的建议，而且支付了寡妇所请求的款项。","B":"是，因为律师向委托人提供建议时，可以同时考虑法律因素和非法律因素。","C":"否，因为律师的聘用函没有告知房东，律师就此事提出的建议会同时包括法律因素和非法律因素。","D":"否，因为律师建议房东全额付款，没有热忱维护房东的法律利益。"},"explanationZh":"B项正确。律师向委托人提供建议时，可以依据纯技术性法律意见之外的其他考量。《职业行为示范规则》第2.1条。因此，律师在提供建议时考虑道德、经济和社会因素是适当的，本题律师正是这样做的。\n\nA项错误。问题不在于房东是否接受了律师的建议。事实上，律师不应仅仅因为委托人可能不高兴，就不敢提出坦率意见。真正的问题是，律师在向房东提供建议时超出案件纯技术性的法律层面，是否适当。\n\nC项错误。最初向委托人发出的聘用函主要涉及律师费，其目的在于明确律师将提供哪些服务以及如何收费，而不是规定律师应以何种形式提出建议。《职业行为示范规则》第1.5条。\n\nD项错误。该选项提出了律师应当“热忱地进行代理”的责任问题。《职业行为示范规则》第1.3条评注[1]。但这并非本题真正的争点。本题所问的是律师向委托人提供建议的适当范围。律师不仅可以在建议中考虑法律以外的因素，还有责任运用独立专业判断，以直接、诚实并符合委托人最佳利益的方式提出建议。本题律师所做的正是如此。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-008","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":8,"topic":"Model Rule 5.5(a) / Model Rule 4.3","question":"An attorney is a member of the bar and a salaried employee of a bank’s trust department. As part of his duties, he prepares a monthly newsletter concerning wills, trusts, estates, and taxes, which the bank sends to all of its customers. The newsletter contains a recommendation to the customer to review his or her will in light of the information contained and, if the customer has any questions, to bring the will to the bank, where the attorney will review the customer’s will and answer the customer’s legal questions. The bank provides the attorney’s services to its customers for no charge. Is the attorney subject to discipline for the foregoing?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because by sending out the newsletter the attorney is giving legal advice to persons who are not his clients.","B":"Yes, because the attorney is assisting the bank in the unauthorized practice of law.","C":"No, because no charge is made for the attorney’s advice.","D":"No, because the attorney is a member of the bar."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. Basic to the integrity of the legal profession is the principle that only persons admitted to the practice of law may provide legal services to others. Consequently, any person who is not a lawyer but who gives legal advice is said to engage in the unauthorized practice of law, and any lawyer who helps a nonlawyer to engage in the unauthorized practice of law violates Model Rule 5.5(a). Here, the bank is not authorized to practice law, but it is providing legal services through its salaried employee, the attorney. Thus, the attorney is assisting the bank in the unauthorized practice of law. A is incorrect. A newsletter does not provide legal “advice” to any particular person, but only supplies generalized legal information to the general public. Even so, a lawyer is not prevented from giving advice to a nonclient. However, if the lawyer is representing a client whose interests may be affected by the nonclient’s actions, the lawyer is required to avoid stating or implying to the nonclient that the lawyer is disinterested. In that case, if the lawyer knows or reasonably should know that the unrepresented person misunderstands the lawyer’s role in the matter, he or she must make reasonable efforts to correct the misunderstanding. Model Rule 4.3. C is incorrect. Whether the attorney charges a fee or not, he’s still assisting the bank in the unauthorized practice of law. D is incorrect. Only a lawyer is subject to discipline under the Model Rules. Consequently, the attorney is subject to discipline because he is a member of the bar.","rule":"B is the correct answer. Basic to the integrity of the legal profession is the principle that only persons admitted to the practice of law may provide legal services to others. Consequently, any person who is not a lawyer but who gives legal advice is said to engage in the unauthorized practice of law, and any lawyer who helps a nonlawyer to engage in the unauthorized practice of law violates Model Rule 5.5(a). Here, the bank is not authorized to practice law, but it is providing legal services through its salaried employee, the attorney. Thus, the attorney is assisting the bank in the unauthorized practice of law. A is incorrect. A newsletter does not provide legal “advice” to any particular person, but only supplies generalized legal information to the general public. Even so, a lawyer is not prevented from giving advice to a nonclient. However, if the lawyer is representing a client whose interests may be affected by the nonclient’s actions, the lawyer is required to avoid stating or implying to the nonclient that the lawyer is disinterested. In that case, if the lawyer knows or reasonably should know that the unrepresented person misunderstands the lawyer’s role in the matter, he or she must make reasonable efforts to correct the misunderstanding. Model Rule 4.3. C is incorrect. Whether the attorney charges a fee or not, he’s still assisting the bank in the unauthorized practice of law. D is incorrect. Only a lawyer is subject to discipline under the Model Rules. Consequently, the attorney is subject to discipline because he is a member of the bar.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for the foregoing?","rule":"B is the correct answer. Basic to the integrity of the legal profession is the principle that only persons admitted to the practice of law may provide legal services to others. Consequently, any person who is not a lawyer but who gives legal advice is said to engage in the unauthorized practice of law, and any lawyer who helps a nonlawyer to engage in the unauthorized practice of law violates Model Rule 5.5(a). Here, the bank is not authorized to practice law, but it is providing legal services through its salaried employee, the attorney. Thus, the attorney is assisting the bank in the unauthorized practice of law. A is incorrect. A newsletter does not provide legal “advice” to any particular person, but only supplies generalized legal information to the general public. Even so, a lawyer is not prevented from giving advice to a nonclient. However, if the lawyer is representing a client whose interests may be affected by the nonclient’s actions, the lawyer is required to avoid stating or implying to the nonclient that the lawyer is disinterested. In that case, if the lawyer knows or reasonably should know that the unrepresented person misunderstands the lawyer’s role in the matter, he or she must make reasonable efforts to correct the misunderstanding. Model Rule 4.3. C is incorrect. Whether the attorney charges a fee or not, he’s still assisting the bank in the unauthorized practice of law. D is incorrect. Only a lawyer is subject to discipline under the Model Rules. Consequently, the attorney is subject to discipline because he is a member of the bar.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. Basic to the integrity of the legal profession is the principle that only persons admitted to the practice of law may provide legal services to others. Consequently, any person who is not a lawyer but who gives legal advice is said to engage in the unauthorized practice of law, and any lawyer who helps a nonlawyer to engage in the unauthorized practice of law violates Model Rule 5.5(a). Here, the bank is not authorized to practice law, but it is providing legal services through its salaried employee, the attorney. Thus, the attorney is assisting the bank in the unauthorized practice of law. A is incorrect. A newsletter does not provide legal “advice” to any particular person, but only supplies generalized legal information to the general public. Even so, a lawyer is not prevented from giving advice to a nonclient. However, if the lawyer is representing a client whose interests may be affected by the nonclient’s actions, the lawyer is required to avoid stating or implying to the nonclient that the lawyer is disinterested. In that case, if the lawyer knows or reasonably should know that the unrepresented person misunderstands the lawyer’s role in the matter, he or she must make reasonable efforts to correct the misunderstanding. Model Rule 4.3. C is incorrect. Whether the attorney charges a fee or not, he’s still assisting the bank in the unauthorized practice of law. D is incorrect. Only a lawyer is subject to discipline under the Model Rules. Consequently, the attorney is subject to discipline because he is a member of the bar.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.5(a)","Model Rule 4.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[52],"sourceAnswerPages":[91],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师具有律师资格，并受雇于一家银行的信托部门，领取固定薪资。作为工作职责的一部分，他每月编写一份有关遗嘱、信托、遗产和税务的通讯，由银行发送给所有客户。通讯建议客户根据其中的信息重新审查自己的遗嘱；如果有任何疑问，可以把遗嘱带到银行，由该律师审查并回答客户的法律问题。银行免费向客户提供该律师的服务。该律师是否会因上述行为而受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师发送该通讯，就是向并非其委托人的人提供法律意见。","B":"是，因为律师在协助银行未经授权从事法律业务。","C":"否，因为律师提供意见不收取费用。","D":"否，因为该人具有律师资格。"},"explanationZh":"B项正确。法律职业诚信的一项基本原则是，只有获准从事法律业务的人才能向他人提供法律服务。因此，非律师提供法律意见即属于未经授权从事法律业务；律师帮助非律师从事此类业务，则违反《职业行为示范规则》第5.5(a)条。本题中，银行没有从事法律业务的资格，却通过领取银行薪资的律师雇员向客户提供法律服务。因此，该律师是在协助银行未经授权从事法律业务。\n\nA项错误。通讯并非针对某个特定个人提供法律“意见”，而只是向公众提供一般性法律信息。即使如此，规则也不禁止律师向非委托人提供意见。但是，如果律师正在代理一名委托人，而非委托人的行为可能影响该委托人的利益，律师不得向非委托人陈述或暗示自己在该事项中保持中立。如果律师知道或理应知道无律师代理的人误解了律师在该事项中的角色，律师必须作出合理努力纠正误解。《职业行为示范规则》第4.3条。\n\nC项错误。无论该律师是否收费，他仍在协助银行未经授权从事法律业务。\n\nD项错误。只有律师才受《职业行为示范规则》的纪律管辖。因此，该人正因为具有律师资格，才可能因上述行为受到纪律处分。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-009","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":9,"topic":"Model Rule 1.1","question":"An attorney was recently admitted to practice and was hired as a new associate at a large firm employing over 100 lawyers. The attorney was working late one night when he received a telephone call from his cousin. His cousin said that he was calling from the police station where he had just been arrested for possession of cocaine with intent to distribute. He was permitted to make only one phone call, and the attorney was the only lawyer he knew. The attorney responded that he had no criminal law experience and that his firm did not handle criminal cases. Nevertheless, his cousin pleaded with the attorney to come to the police station and see what he could do to get him out on bail. The attorney replied that he would do what he could. The attorney went to the police station and using what information he recalled from his criminal law and procedure courses attempted to get his cousin released on bail. However, as a result of his inexperience, the attorney was unable to secure his cousin’s release that night. The next morning, the attorney found an experienced criminal lawyer for his cousin, who obtained his cousin’s release within one hour. Was the attorney’s conduct proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because neither referral nor consultation was practical under the circumstances.","B":"Yes, because the attorney was a close relative.","C":"No, because the attorney had no special training or experience in criminal cases.","D":"No, because the attorney did not have the requisite level of competence to accept representation in the case."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. Although the attorney does not have the “requisite knowledge and skill” to competently represent his cousin and knows he doesn’t, this scenario would fall under the “emergency” situation discussed in Model Rule 1.1, Comment [3]. When his cousin called, it would have been impractical for the attorney to refer his cousin to another lawyer or to consult with another lawyer, so he gave reasonably necessary assistance to get his cousin out on bail. However, for any additional work, the attorney would have to become competent or find another lawyer to take on the matter, which the attorney did in this case. B is incorrect. The fact that the attorney and his cousin are related has nothing to do with whether the attorney can represent his cousin competently. The attorney must decide whether he has the requisite knowledge and skill to provide his cousin with competent representation regardless of their relationship. C is incorrect. It is precisely because of the attorney’s inexperience that he made the decision that he did. He acted appropriately in giving assistance in an “emergency” situation, which included finding an experienced criminal lawyer for his cousin. D is incorrect. The attorney did not accept representation.","rule":"A is the correct answer. Although the attorney does not have the “requisite knowledge and skill” to competently represent his cousin and knows he doesn’t, this scenario would fall under the “emergency” situation discussed in Model Rule 1.1, Comment [3]. When his cousin called, it would have been impractical for the attorney to refer his cousin to another lawyer or to consult with another lawyer, so he gave reasonably necessary assistance to get his cousin out on bail. However, for any additional work, the attorney would have to become competent or find another lawyer to take on the matter, which the attorney did in this case. B is incorrect. The fact that the attorney and his cousin are related has nothing to do with whether the attorney can represent his cousin competently. The attorney must decide whether he has the requisite knowledge and skill to provide his cousin with competent representation regardless of their relationship. C is incorrect. It is precisely because of the attorney’s inexperience that he made the decision that he did. He acted appropriately in giving assistance in an “emergency” situation, which included finding an experienced criminal lawyer for his cousin. D is incorrect. The attorney did not accept representation.","irac":{"issue":"Was the attorney’s conduct proper?","rule":"A is the correct answer. Although the attorney does not have the “requisite knowledge and skill” to competently represent his cousin and knows he doesn’t, this scenario would fall under the “emergency” situation discussed in Model Rule 1.1, Comment [3]. When his cousin called, it would have been impractical for the attorney to refer his cousin to another lawyer or to consult with another lawyer, so he gave reasonably necessary assistance to get his cousin out on bail. However, for any additional work, the attorney would have to become competent or find another lawyer to take on the matter, which the attorney did in this case. B is incorrect. The fact that the attorney and his cousin are related has nothing to do with whether the attorney can represent his cousin competently. The attorney must decide whether he has the requisite knowledge and skill to provide his cousin with competent representation regardless of their relationship. C is incorrect. It is precisely because of the attorney’s inexperience that he made the decision that he did. He acted appropriately in giving assistance in an “emergency” situation, which included finding an experienced criminal lawyer for his cousin. D is incorrect. The attorney did not accept representation.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. Although the attorney does not have the “requisite knowledge and skill” to competently represent his cousin and knows he doesn’t, this scenario would fall under the “emergency” situation discussed in Model Rule 1.1, Comment [3]. When his cousin called, it would have been impractical for the attorney to refer his cousin to another lawyer or to consult with another lawyer, so he gave reasonably necessary assistance to get his cousin out on bail. However, for any additional work, the attorney would have to become competent or find another lawyer to take on the matter, which the attorney did in this case. B is incorrect. The fact that the attorney and his cousin are related has nothing to do with whether the attorney can represent his cousin competently. The attorney must decide whether he has the requisite knowledge and skill to provide his cousin with competent representation regardless of their relationship. C is incorrect. It is precisely because of the attorney’s inexperience that he made the decision that he did. He acted appropriately in giving assistance in an “emergency” situation, which included finding an experienced criminal lawyer for his cousin. D is incorrect. The attorney did not accept representation.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[52,53],"sourceAnswerPages":[91,92],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师最近刚取得执业资格，并被一家拥有一百多名律师的大型事务所聘为新的受雇律师。一天深夜，他正在加班时接到表兄打来的电话。表兄说，自己因持有可卡因且意图贩卖刚刚被捕，目前正在警察局。他只被允许打一个电话，而这名律师是他认识的唯一律师。该律师回答说，自己没有刑法执业经验，所在事务所也不办理刑事案件。尽管如此，表兄仍恳求律师到警察局看看能否设法让他保释出去。律师回答说会尽力而为。\n\n律师到达警察局，凭借自己从刑法和刑事诉讼课程中记得的知识，尝试帮助表兄获得保释。但由于缺乏经验，他当晚未能使表兄获释。第二天早晨，他为表兄找到了一名经验丰富的刑事律师；该律师在一小时内使表兄获得释放。该律师的行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为在当时情况下，转介给其他律师或者向其他律师咨询都不可行。","B":"是，因为该律师与当事人是近亲属。","C":"否，因为该律师没有办理刑事案件的专门训练或经验。","D":"否，因为该律师不具备接受本案代理所需的胜任能力。"},"explanationZh":"A项正确。虽然该律师不具备胜任代理表兄所需的“必要知识和技能”，而且自己也知道这一点，但本题属于《职业行为示范规则》第1.1条评注[3]所讨论的“紧急情况”。表兄打来电话时，把他转介给其他律师或者向其他律师咨询并不现实，因此该律师提供了在当时合理必要的协助，试图使表兄获得保释。但是，对于后续任何工作，该律师都必须通过学习取得胜任能力，或者找到另一名律师接手；本题中他确实找到了另一名律师。\n\nB项错误。律师与表兄之间的亲属关系，与律师能否胜任代理无关。不论双方有什么关系，律师都必须判断自己是否具备提供胜任代理所需的知识和技能。\n\nC项错误。恰恰是因为缺乏经验，该律师才作出了题中所述决定。他在“紧急情况”下提供协助，并随后为表兄找到经验丰富的刑事律师，处理是适当的。\n\nD项错误。该律师并没有接受对本案的持续代理；他只是在紧急情况下提供合理必要的临时协助。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-010","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":10,"topic":"Model Rule 5.5(a) / Model Rule 5.4(a)","question":"A business attorney entered into a partnership with a certified public accountant. The partnership provided legal and other assistance to clients in connection with business and tax planning, tax filings, and other personal and corporate business matters. The accountant performed only work that she was authorized to perform as a certified public accountant. The attorney made reasonable efforts to ensure that the accountant did not interfere with the attorney’s compliance with his professional obligations as a lawyer. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because some of the activities of the partnership consisted of the practice of law.","B":"Yes, because lawyers may not form partnerships with nonlawyers.","C":"No, because the accountant performed only work that she was authorized to perform as a certified public accountant.","D":"No, because the attorney made reasonable efforts to ensure that the accountant did not interfere with the attorney’s compliance with his professional obligations as a lawyer."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not form a partnership with a nonlawyer if any of the activities of the partnership consist of the “practice of law.” A lawyer who does so is assisting another in the unauthorized practice of law, and is subject to discipline. Model Rule 5.5(a). Here, the important point is that the partnership is delivering the legal services, which choice A states explicitly. B is incorrect. There is no absolute ban on lawyer and nonlawyer partnerships. As stated above, the issue is whether the partnership delivers legal services. For example, the attorney would not be subject to discipline if this partnership provided only accounting services. C is incorrect. Again, the important fact is that the partnership is providing legal services. However, as a partner, the accountant is receiving a portion of the entity’s profits and has control over the legal aspects of the business. Under Model Rule 5.4(a), a lawyer may not share legal fees with a nonlawyer, which would clearly happen here under the partnership agreement. This Rule is the major deterrent to partnerships like this, and even splitting fees with a charity is not allowed. The only exceptions to this Rule involve paying the estates of deceased attorneys or law firm retirement plans, neither of which apply here. Consequently, what the accountant actually does in terms of work is not important, because, as a partner in this partnership, she is still engaged in the unauthorized practice of law. D is incorrect. As stated above, the main issues are the creation of a partnership that provides legal services and the sharing of fees with a nonlawyer. Simply keeping the accountant from interfering with the attorney’s professional obligations is not enough.","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not form a partnership with a nonlawyer if any of the activities of the partnership consist of the “practice of law.” A lawyer who does so is assisting another in the unauthorized practice of law, and is subject to discipline. Model Rule 5.5(a). Here, the important point is that the partnership is delivering the legal services, which choice A states explicitly. B is incorrect. There is no absolute ban on lawyer and nonlawyer partnerships. As stated above, the issue is whether the partnership delivers legal services. For example, the attorney would not be subject to discipline if this partnership provided only accounting services. C is incorrect. Again, the important fact is that the partnership is providing legal services. However, as a partner, the accountant is receiving a portion of the entity’s profits and has control over the legal aspects of the business. Under Model Rule 5.4(a), a lawyer may not share legal fees with a nonlawyer, which would clearly happen here under the partnership agreement. This Rule is the major deterrent to partnerships like this, and even splitting fees with a charity is not allowed. The only exceptions to this Rule involve paying the estates of deceased attorneys or law firm retirement plans, neither of which apply here. Consequently, what the accountant actually does in terms of work is not important, because, as a partner in this partnership, she is still engaged in the unauthorized practice of law. D is incorrect. As stated above, the main issues are the creation of a partnership that provides legal services and the sharing of fees with a nonlawyer. Simply keeping the accountant from interfering with the attorney’s professional obligations is not enough.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not form a partnership with a nonlawyer if any of the activities of the partnership consist of the “practice of law.” A lawyer who does so is assisting another in the unauthorized practice of law, and is subject to discipline. Model Rule 5.5(a). Here, the important point is that the partnership is delivering the legal services, which choice A states explicitly. B is incorrect. There is no absolute ban on lawyer and nonlawyer partnerships. As stated above, the issue is whether the partnership delivers legal services. For example, the attorney would not be subject to discipline if this partnership provided only accounting services. C is incorrect. Again, the important fact is that the partnership is providing legal services. However, as a partner, the accountant is receiving a portion of the entity’s profits and has control over the legal aspects of the business. Under Model Rule 5.4(a), a lawyer may not share legal fees with a nonlawyer, which would clearly happen here under the partnership agreement. This Rule is the major deterrent to partnerships like this, and even splitting fees with a charity is not allowed. The only exceptions to this Rule involve paying the estates of deceased attorneys or law firm retirement plans, neither of which apply here. Consequently, what the accountant actually does in terms of work is not important, because, as a partner in this partnership, she is still engaged in the unauthorized practice of law. D is incorrect. As stated above, the main issues are the creation of a partnership that provides legal services and the sharing of fees with a nonlawyer. Simply keeping the accountant from interfering with the attorney’s professional obligations is not enough.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not form a partnership with a nonlawyer if any of the activities of the partnership consist of the “practice of law.” A lawyer who does so is assisting another in the unauthorized practice of law, and is subject to discipline. Model Rule 5.5(a). Here, the important point is that the partnership is delivering the legal services, which choice A states explicitly. B is incorrect. There is no absolute ban on lawyer and nonlawyer partnerships. As stated above, the issue is whether the partnership delivers legal services. For example, the attorney would not be subject to discipline if this partnership provided only accounting services. C is incorrect. Again, the important fact is that the partnership is providing legal services. However, as a partner, the accountant is receiving a portion of the entity’s profits and has control over the legal aspects of the business. Under Model Rule 5.4(a), a lawyer may not share legal fees with a nonlawyer, which would clearly happen here under the partnership agreement. This Rule is the major deterrent to partnerships like this, and even splitting fees with a charity is not allowed. The only exceptions to this Rule involve paying the estates of deceased attorneys or law firm retirement plans, neither of which apply here. Consequently, what the accountant actually does in terms of work is not important, because, as a partner in this partnership, she is still engaged in the unauthorized practice of law. D is incorrect. As stated above, the main issues are the creation of a partnership that provides legal services and the sharing of fees with a nonlawyer. Simply keeping the accountant from interfering with the attorney’s professional obligations is not enough.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.5(a)","Model Rule 5.4(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[53,54],"sourceAnswerPages":[92],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名商事律师与一名注册会计师成立了合伙组织。该合伙组织在商业和税务规划、税务申报以及其他个人和公司商业事务方面，向客户提供法律及其他协助。会计师仅从事注册会计师依法有权从事的工作。律师作出了合理努力，确保会计师不会妨碍自己遵守律师的职业义务。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该合伙组织的一部分业务构成法律执业。","B":"是，因为律师不得与非律师成立合伙组织。","C":"否，因为会计师只从事其作为注册会计师有权从事的工作。","D":"否，因为律师已作出合理努力，确保会计师不会妨碍其履行律师职业义务。"},"explanationZh":"A项正确。如果一个合伙组织的任何业务构成“法律执业”，律师就不得与非律师成立该合伙组织。律师这样做即是在协助他人未经授权从事法律业务，并应受纪律处分。《职业行为示范规则》第5.5(a)条。本题的关键是法律服务由该合伙组织本身提供，A项对此作出了明确表述。\n\nB项错误。律师与非律师成立合伙组织并非一概受到禁止。如上所述，关键在于该合伙组织是否提供法律服务。例如，如果该组织仅提供会计服务，律师就不会因此受到纪律处分。\n\nC项错误。关键事实仍然是该合伙组织正在提供法律服务。此外，会计师作为合伙人，有权取得该实体的一部分利润，并对业务中的法律事项具有控制权。根据《职业行为示范规则》第5.4(a)条，律师不得与非律师分割律师费，而该合伙协议显然会造成这种结果。该规则是阻止此类合伙关系的主要规定，甚至不允许与慈善机构分割律师费。其例外仅涉及向已故律师的遗产付款或律师事务所退休计划，本题均不适用。因此，会计师实际从事什么具体工作并不重要；她作为该合伙组织的合伙人，仍参与了未经授权的法律业务。\n\nD项错误。如上所述，主要问题是成立一个提供法律服务的合伙组织，以及与非律师分割律师费。仅仅防止会计师干预律师的职业义务，并不足以使该安排合法。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-011","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":11,"topic":"Model Rule 1.2(c)","question":"An attorney is widely regarded as an exceptionally competent practitioner in the field of criminal law. A client of the attorney became the subject of a grand jury investigation in a matter that could result in a felony indictment. The client lacked sufficient funds to pay for the attorney’s services beyond the grand jury stage. He asked the attorney to provide limited representation for a flat fee. Under the arrangement he proposed, the attorney would advise the client concerning the grand jury investigation, but the representation would end when an indictment was returned or the grand jury decided not to indict. The attorney fully advised the client of the practical and legal aspects of the client’s proposal. Is it proper for the attorney to accept this limited representation?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the client and not the attorney suggested the arrangement.","B":"Yes, because the attorney and the client may agree to limit the scope of the representation so long as the limitation is reasonable under the circumstances.","C":"No, because the attorney should not limit the scope of the representation based on the client’s ability to pay.","D":"No, because the scope of the representation may not be limited in a criminal case."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. As long as the client is fully informed, a lawyer and client may agree to limit the scope of the lawyer’s representation so long as the limitation is reasonable under the circumstances. Model Rule 1.2(c). Although clients usually retain a lawyer to take care of all aspects of a particular matter, if the client affirmatively consents after consultation, the client can limit the scope of the representation to only one part of the claim. Consequently, it is clearly permissible under the Rules for the client and the lawyer to agree to a representation that only covers the grand jury proceeding. A is incorrect. The important issues are whether the client is informed, agrees, and the limitation is reasonable. Whose idea it was is completely irrelevant. While a client will usually be the one to suggest a limit to the scope of representation, if it was the attorney’s suggestion, the analysis would still be the same. C is incorrect. Lawyers are not obligated to provide free legal services, and the limited representation here puts the client in a better position than if he were represented by no lawyer at all. When an attorney and a client agree to limit the scope of a representation, the client’s ability to pay is most likely the cause. D is incorrect. The fact a case is a criminal or civil matter is also irrelevant.","rule":"B is the correct answer. As long as the client is fully informed, a lawyer and client may agree to limit the scope of the lawyer’s representation so long as the limitation is reasonable under the circumstances. Model Rule 1.2(c). Although clients usually retain a lawyer to take care of all aspects of a particular matter, if the client affirmatively consents after consultation, the client can limit the scope of the representation to only one part of the claim. Consequently, it is clearly permissible under the Rules for the client and the lawyer to agree to a representation that only covers the grand jury proceeding. A is incorrect. The important issues are whether the client is informed, agrees, and the limitation is reasonable. Whose idea it was is completely irrelevant. While a client will usually be the one to suggest a limit to the scope of representation, if it was the attorney’s suggestion, the analysis would still be the same. C is incorrect. Lawyers are not obligated to provide free legal services, and the limited representation here puts the client in a better position than if he were represented by no lawyer at all. When an attorney and a client agree to limit the scope of a representation, the client’s ability to pay is most likely the cause. D is incorrect. The fact a case is a criminal or civil matter is also irrelevant.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the attorney to accept this limited representation?","rule":"B is the correct answer. As long as the client is fully informed, a lawyer and client may agree to limit the scope of the lawyer’s representation so long as the limitation is reasonable under the circumstances. Model Rule 1.2(c). Although clients usually retain a lawyer to take care of all aspects of a particular matter, if the client affirmatively consents after consultation, the client can limit the scope of the representation to only one part of the claim. Consequently, it is clearly permissible under the Rules for the client and the lawyer to agree to a representation that only covers the grand jury proceeding. A is incorrect. The important issues are whether the client is informed, agrees, and the limitation is reasonable. Whose idea it was is completely irrelevant. While a client will usually be the one to suggest a limit to the scope of representation, if it was the attorney’s suggestion, the analysis would still be the same. C is incorrect. Lawyers are not obligated to provide free legal services, and the limited representation here puts the client in a better position than if he were represented by no lawyer at all. When an attorney and a client agree to limit the scope of a representation, the client’s ability to pay is most likely the cause. D is incorrect. The fact a case is a criminal or civil matter is also irrelevant.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. As long as the client is fully informed, a lawyer and client may agree to limit the scope of the lawyer’s representation so long as the limitation is reasonable under the circumstances. Model Rule 1.2(c). Although clients usually retain a lawyer to take care of all aspects of a particular matter, if the client affirmatively consents after consultation, the client can limit the scope of the representation to only one part of the claim. Consequently, it is clearly permissible under the Rules for the client and the lawyer to agree to a representation that only covers the grand jury proceeding. A is incorrect. The important issues are whether the client is informed, agrees, and the limitation is reasonable. Whose idea it was is completely irrelevant. While a client will usually be the one to suggest a limit to the scope of representation, if it was the attorney’s suggestion, the analysis would still be the same. C is incorrect. Lawyers are not obligated to provide free legal services, and the limited representation here puts the client in a better position than if he were represented by no lawyer at all. When an attorney and a client agree to limit the scope of a representation, the client’s ability to pay is most likely the cause. D is incorrect. The fact a case is a criminal or civil matter is also irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.2(c)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[54],"sourceAnswerPages":[93],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师被广泛认为是刑法领域能力极为出色的执业者。他的一名委托人成为大陪审团调查对象，该调查可能导致重罪起诉。该委托人的资金不足，无法支付大陪审团阶段之后的律师服务费用，因此请求律师按固定费用提供有限范围代理。按照委托人提出的安排，律师将就大陪审团调查向其提供建议，但在大陪审团提出起诉书或决定不起诉时，代理即告终止。律师已向委托人充分说明这一方案在实际和法律方面的影响。律师接受这项有限范围代理是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该安排是由委托人而非律师提出的。","B":"是，因为只要限制在当时情况下合理，律师与委托人可以约定限制代理范围。","C":"否，因为律师不应根据委托人的支付能力限制代理范围。","D":"否，因为刑事案件的代理范围不得受到限制。"},"explanationZh":"B项正确。只要委托人获得充分信息，律师与委托人就可以约定限制律师的代理范围，但该限制必须在当时情况下合理。《职业行为示范规则》第1.2(c)条。虽然委托人通常会聘请律师处理某一事项的全部方面，但如果委托人在咨询后明确同意，也可以把代理范围限制在请求中的某一个部分。因此，委托人与律师约定只代理大陪审团程序，显然为规则所允许。\n\nA项错误。重要问题是委托人是否获得充分信息、是否同意，以及限制是否合理。该方案最初由谁提出完全无关。虽然通常由委托人提出限制代理范围，但即使建议来自律师，分析也完全相同。\n\nC项错误。律师没有义务免费提供法律服务；本题的有限范围代理，仍使委托人处于比完全没有律师代理更有利的地位。律师与委托人约定限制代理范围时，委托人的支付能力往往正是产生该限制的原因。\n\nD项错误。案件属于刑事还是民事，同样与是否可以合理限制代理范围无关。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-012","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":12,"topic":"Model Rule 1.6(a) / Model Rule 1.6","question":"An attorney represents a client who is under indictment for homicide. In the course of the representation, the client told the attorney that she had previously killed two other people. These murders are completely unrelated to the murder indictment for which the attorney is providing representation. With the client’s consent, the attorney made a tape recording of the client’s confession regarding the unrelated homicides. At the attorney’s request, the client also drew a map of the remote locations of the victims’ graves from the unrelated killings. Those bodies have not been found by the police, and the client is not a suspect in either crime, both of which remain unsolved. Is the attorney subject to discipline if he fails to voluntarily disclose to the authorities his knowledge of the two prior murders and the locations of the victim’s bodies?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because as an officer of the court, the attorney must disclose any knowledge that he has, whether privileged or not, concerning the commission of the prior crimes by his client.","B":"Yes, because the attorney is impeding the state’s access to significant evidence.","C":"No, because the attorney did not represent or advise his client with respect to the prior crimes.","D":"No, because the information was obtained by the attorney in the course of the representation."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not reveal any information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent. Model Rule 1.6(a). This is probably the most important Rule in the Model Rules of Professional Conduct. It is intended to promote candid communications between the client and attorney, so all the aspects of a particular legal problem become known and the lawyer can provide the most competent legal advice. Importantly, if a client knew the attorney could share his or her confidences to third parties, competent representation would become much more difficult, if not impossible, as the client would likely leave the lawyer in the dark on many important facts. Here, although the prior murders are not part of the present case, this information was obtained during the course of the attorney’s representation, and consequently “relates” to that representation. Additionally, the prior murders would likely affect the attorney’s strategy in the present case, specifically whether his client should plead guilty or testify at trial. A is incorrect. Although there are several exceptions to the Model Rule 1.6’s confidentiality requirement, none of the exceptions requires an attorney to disclose a client’s past crimes of violence. Under the major exceptions to the Rule, a lawyer may reveal confidences to the extent necessary to prevent the client from committing a crime causing imminent death or substantial bodily injury (including a client’s potential suicide), to the extent necessary to establish a claim or defense in a controversy between the lawyer and the client, or to the extent necessary to prevent or rectify financial harm caused by a crime or fraud involving the lawyer’s services. Often, when testing this particular issue, the MPRE uses “as an officer of the court” in its “distracter” answer choices. B is incorrect. Despite the fact the attorney is impeding the state’s access to significant evidence, he still has an obligation to keep the client’s information confidential. This answer option is trying to prey on your sympathies to the victims’ families. Don’t fall for the “sympathy” choice. C is incorrect. As stated above, although the attorney did not advise the client with respect to the prior crimes, he discovered that information during his representation of the client during this matter. However, if the attorney had advised the client regarding how to commit the prior crimes or how to hide the bodies, he would be subject to discipline and criminal prosecution.","rule":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not reveal any information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent. Model Rule 1.6(a). This is probably the most important Rule in the Model Rules of Professional Conduct. It is intended to promote candid communications between the client and attorney, so all the aspects of a particular legal problem become known and the lawyer can provide the most competent legal advice. Importantly, if a client knew the attorney could share his or her confidences to third parties, competent representation would become much more difficult, if not impossible, as the client would likely leave the lawyer in the dark on many important facts. Here, although the prior murders are not part of the present case, this information was obtained during the course of the attorney’s representation, and consequently “relates” to that representation. Additionally, the prior murders would likely affect the attorney’s strategy in the present case, specifically whether his client should plead guilty or testify at trial. A is incorrect. Although there are several exceptions to the Model Rule 1.6’s confidentiality requirement, none of the exceptions requires an attorney to disclose a client’s past crimes of violence. Under the major exceptions to the Rule, a lawyer may reveal confidences to the extent necessary to prevent the client from committing a crime causing imminent death or substantial bodily injury (including a client’s potential suicide), to the extent necessary to establish a claim or defense in a controversy between the lawyer and the client, or to the extent necessary to prevent or rectify financial harm caused by a crime or fraud involving the lawyer’s services. Often, when testing this particular issue, the MPRE uses “as an officer of the court” in its “distracter” answer choices. B is incorrect. Despite the fact the attorney is impeding the state’s access to significant evidence, he still has an obligation to keep the client’s information confidential. This answer option is trying to prey on your sympathies to the victims’ families. Don’t fall for the “sympathy” choice. C is incorrect. As stated above, although the attorney did not advise the client with respect to the prior crimes, he discovered that information during his representation of the client during this matter. However, if the attorney had advised the client regarding how to commit the prior crimes or how to hide the bodies, he would be subject to discipline and criminal prosecution.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline if he fails to voluntarily disclose to the authorities his knowledge of the two prior murders and the locations of the victim’s bodies?","rule":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not reveal any information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent. Model Rule 1.6(a). This is probably the most important Rule in the Model Rules of Professional Conduct. It is intended to promote candid communications between the client and attorney, so all the aspects of a particular legal problem become known and the lawyer can provide the most competent legal advice. Importantly, if a client knew the attorney could share his or her confidences to third parties, competent representation would become much more difficult, if not impossible, as the client would likely leave the lawyer in the dark on many important facts. Here, although the prior murders are not part of the present case, this information was obtained during the course of the attorney’s representation, and consequently “relates” to that representation. Additionally, the prior murders would likely affect the attorney’s strategy in the present case, specifically whether his client should plead guilty or testify at trial. A is incorrect. Although there are several exceptions to the Model Rule 1.6’s confidentiality requirement, none of the exceptions requires an attorney to disclose a client’s past crimes of violence. Under the major exceptions to the Rule, a lawyer may reveal confidences to the extent necessary to prevent the client from committing a crime causing imminent death or substantial bodily injury (including a client’s potential suicide), to the extent necessary to establish a claim or defense in a controversy between the lawyer and the client, or to the extent necessary to prevent or rectify financial harm caused by a crime or fraud involving the lawyer’s services. Often, when testing this particular issue, the MPRE uses “as an officer of the court” in its “distracter” answer choices. B is incorrect. Despite the fact the attorney is impeding the state’s access to significant evidence, he still has an obligation to keep the client’s information confidential. This answer option is trying to prey on your sympathies to the victims’ families. Don’t fall for the “sympathy” choice. C is incorrect. As stated above, although the attorney did not advise the client with respect to the prior crimes, he discovered that information during his representation of the client during this matter. However, if the attorney had advised the client regarding how to commit the prior crimes or how to hide the bodies, he would be subject to discipline and criminal prosecution.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not reveal any information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent. Model Rule 1.6(a). This is probably the most important Rule in the Model Rules of Professional Conduct. It is intended to promote candid communications between the client and attorney, so all the aspects of a particular legal problem become known and the lawyer can provide the most competent legal advice. Importantly, if a client knew the attorney could share his or her confidences to third parties, competent representation would become much more difficult, if not impossible, as the client would likely leave the lawyer in the dark on many important facts. Here, although the prior murders are not part of the present case, this information was obtained during the course of the attorney’s representation, and consequently “relates” to that representation. Additionally, the prior murders would likely affect the attorney’s strategy in the present case, specifically whether his client should plead guilty or testify at trial. A is incorrect. Although there are several exceptions to the Model Rule 1.6’s confidentiality requirement, none of the exceptions requires an attorney to disclose a client’s past crimes of violence. Under the major exceptions to the Rule, a lawyer may reveal confidences to the extent necessary to prevent the client from committing a crime causing imminent death or substantial bodily injury (including a client’s potential suicide), to the extent necessary to establish a claim or defense in a controversy between the lawyer and the client, or to the extent necessary to prevent or rectify financial harm caused by a crime or fraud involving the lawyer’s services. Often, when testing this particular issue, the MPRE uses “as an officer of the court” in its “distracter” answer choices. B is incorrect. Despite the fact the attorney is impeding the state’s access to significant evidence, he still has an obligation to keep the client’s information confidential. This answer option is trying to prey on your sympathies to the victims’ families. Don’t fall for the “sympathy” choice. C is incorrect. As stated above, although the attorney did not advise the client with respect to the prior crimes, he discovered that information during his representation of the client during this matter. However, if the attorney had advised the client regarding how to commit the prior crimes or how to hide the bodies, he would be subject to discipline and criminal prosecution.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.6(a)","Model Rule 1.6"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[54,55],"sourceAnswerPages":[93,94],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理一名因杀人罪被起诉的委托人。在代理过程中，委托人告诉律师，她以前还杀害过另外两个人。这两起杀人行为与律师目前代理的杀人罪起诉完全无关。经委托人同意，律师把委托人关于两起无关杀人案的供述录了音。应律师要求，委托人还画了一张地图，标明两起案件中被害人坟墓所在的偏远地点。警方尚未找到这些尸体；委托人也不是两案的嫌疑人，两案至今均未侦破。如果律师没有主动向有关机关披露自己对先前两起杀人案以及被害人遗体位置的了解，他是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师作为法庭人员，必须披露其掌握的有关委托人实施先前犯罪的一切信息，无论该信息是否受特权保护。","B":"是，因为律师妨碍了州政府取得重要证据。","C":"否，因为律师没有就先前的犯罪代理委托人或向其提供建议。","D":"否，因为这些信息是律师在代理过程中获得的。"},"explanationZh":"D项正确。除非委托人给予知情同意，律师不得披露与代理委托人有关的任何信息。《职业行为示范规则》第1.6(a)条。这很可能是《职业行为示范规则》中最重要的一条。其目的在于促进委托人与律师之间的坦率沟通，使律师能够了解某一法律问题的全部情况，从而提供最胜任的法律意见。尤其重要的是，如果委托人知道律师可能把其秘密告诉第三人，胜任的代理就会变得非常困难，甚至不可能，因为委托人很可能隐瞒许多重要事实。\n\n本题中，虽然先前两起杀人案不属于目前案件的一部分，但这些信息是在律师代理过程中获得的，因而与该代理“有关”。此外，先前的杀人行为很可能影响律师在本案中的策略，特别是委托人是否应当认罪或者在庭审中作证。\n\nA项错误。尽管第1.6条的保密义务存在若干例外，但没有任何例外要求律师披露委托人过去实施的暴力犯罪。该规则的主要例外允许律师在必要范围内披露保密信息，以防止委托人实施可能造成迫近死亡或重大身体伤害的犯罪（包括委托人可能自杀的情形）；在律师与委托人的争议中确立律师的请求或抗辩；或者防止或纠正利用律师服务实施犯罪或欺诈所造成的经济损害。MPRE考查这一问题时，经常在干扰选项中使用“作为法庭人员”这一说法。\n\nB项错误。尽管律师确实妨碍了州政府取得重要证据，他仍负有对委托人信息保密的义务。该选项试图利用考生对被害人家属的同情；不要选择这种“同情型”答案。\n\nC项错误。如上所述，虽然律师没有就先前犯罪向委托人提供建议，但他是在本案代理期间得知这些信息的。不过，如果律师曾建议委托人如何实施先前的犯罪或如何藏匿尸体，他将可能同时受到纪律处分和刑事追诉。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-013","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":13,"topic":"Model Rule 1.11(a)(2) / Model Rule 1.11(b)","question":"A client has asked an attorney to represent her in obtaining compensation for a tract of land that is being condemned by the state department of transportation to build a new highway. Two years ago, the attorney had been employed by the department and had been assigned to search title on several tracts of land, including the one owned by the client. The attorney remembers a department engineer had drafted a confidential memorandum advising against running a new highway across the client’s land because of a potential adverse environmental impact. Because of this information, the attorney believes it is possible to prevent the condemnation of the client’s land or to increase the settlement amount. What is the proper action for the attorney to take?","choices":{"A":"Represent the client on the issue of damages only and not disclose the information that might prevent the condemnation.","B":"Represent the client and attempt to prevent the condemnation by using the information about the adverse environmental impact.","C":"Refuse to represent the client but disclose to her the information about the adverse environmental impact.","D":"Refuse to represent the client and not disclose the information about the adverse environmental impact."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer who was formerly employed by a government agency may not later represent a client “in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially” as a public employee, unless the government agency consents in writing. Model Rule 1.11(a)(2). If the lawyer is practicing in a firm, this disqualification would cover any other lawyer in the firm unless the firm puts together a screening arrangement and gives written notice to the agency. Model Rule 1.11(b). Here, the attorney participated personally and substantially by conducting a title search on the client’s property when he was employed by the government agency. Furthermore, the attorney may not disclose the confidential information he learned while working at the department because a lawyer who was formerly employed by a government agency must not reveal confidential information relating to a former representation. Model Rules 1.11(a)(1) and 1.9(c). A is incorrect. Because the attorney participated personally and substantially in the matter as a public employee, he cannot now represent the client unless the government agency consents in writing, whether or not he plans to use the confidential information he learned while working there. Importantly, even if he only represents the client on damages, he will still be representing her “in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially.” B is incorrect. As stated above, he can neither represent the client nor disclose the confidential information. C is incorrect. Whether or not he represents the client, he still cannot disclose the government’s confidential information. Model Rules 1.11(a)(1) and 1.9(c).","rule":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer who was formerly employed by a government agency may not later represent a client “in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially” as a public employee, unless the government agency consents in writing. Model Rule 1.11(a)(2). If the lawyer is practicing in a firm, this disqualification would cover any other lawyer in the firm unless the firm puts together a screening arrangement and gives written notice to the agency. Model Rule 1.11(b). Here, the attorney participated personally and substantially by conducting a title search on the client’s property when he was employed by the government agency. Furthermore, the attorney may not disclose the confidential information he learned while working at the department because a lawyer who was formerly employed by a government agency must not reveal confidential information relating to a former representation. Model Rules 1.11(a)(1) and 1.9(c). A is incorrect. Because the attorney participated personally and substantially in the matter as a public employee, he cannot now represent the client unless the government agency consents in writing, whether or not he plans to use the confidential information he learned while working there. Importantly, even if he only represents the client on damages, he will still be representing her “in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially.” B is incorrect. As stated above, he can neither represent the client nor disclose the confidential information. C is incorrect. Whether or not he represents the client, he still cannot disclose the government’s confidential information. Model Rules 1.11(a)(1) and 1.9(c).","irac":{"issue":"What is the proper action for the attorney to take?","rule":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer who was formerly employed by a government agency may not later represent a client “in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially” as a public employee, unless the government agency consents in writing. Model Rule 1.11(a)(2). If the lawyer is practicing in a firm, this disqualification would cover any other lawyer in the firm unless the firm puts together a screening arrangement and gives written notice to the agency. Model Rule 1.11(b). Here, the attorney participated personally and substantially by conducting a title search on the client’s property when he was employed by the government agency. Furthermore, the attorney may not disclose the confidential information he learned while working at the department because a lawyer who was formerly employed by a government agency must not reveal confidential information relating to a former representation. Model Rules 1.11(a)(1) and 1.9(c). A is incorrect. Because the attorney participated personally and substantially in the matter as a public employee, he cannot now represent the client unless the government agency consents in writing, whether or not he plans to use the confidential information he learned while working there. Importantly, even if he only represents the client on damages, he will still be representing her “in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially.” B is incorrect. As stated above, he can neither represent the client nor disclose the confidential information. C is incorrect. Whether or not he represents the client, he still cannot disclose the government’s confidential information. Model Rules 1.11(a)(1) and 1.9(c).","analysis":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer who was formerly employed by a government agency may not later represent a client “in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially” as a public employee, unless the government agency consents in writing. Model Rule 1.11(a)(2). If the lawyer is practicing in a firm, this disqualification would cover any other lawyer in the firm unless the firm puts together a screening arrangement and gives written notice to the agency. Model Rule 1.11(b). Here, the attorney participated personally and substantially by conducting a title search on the client’s property when he was employed by the government agency. Furthermore, the attorney may not disclose the confidential information he learned while working at the department because a lawyer who was formerly employed by a government agency must not reveal confidential information relating to a former representation. Model Rules 1.11(a)(1) and 1.9(c). A is incorrect. Because the attorney participated personally and substantially in the matter as a public employee, he cannot now represent the client unless the government agency consents in writing, whether or not he plans to use the confidential information he learned while working there. Importantly, even if he only represents the client on damages, he will still be representing her “in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially.” B is incorrect. As stated above, he can neither represent the client nor disclose the confidential information. C is incorrect. Whether or not he represents the client, he still cannot disclose the government’s confidential information. Model Rules 1.11(a)(1) and 1.9(c).","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.11(a)(2)","Model Rule 1.11(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[55],"sourceAnswerPages":[94,95],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名委托人请求律师代理她，就州交通部门为修建新公路而征收其一块土地一事取得补偿。两年前，该律师曾受雇于该部门，并被指派调查若干地块的产权，其中包括该委托人所有的土地。律师记得，部门的一名工程师曾起草一份保密备忘录，建议不要让新公路穿过委托人的土地，因为这样做可能产生不利环境影响。律师根据该信息认为，有可能阻止征收，或者提高和解金额。该律师应当采取什么适当行动？","choicesZh":{"A":"仅就损害赔偿问题代理委托人，不披露可能阻止征收的信息。","B":"代理委托人，并利用有关不利环境影响的信息尝试阻止征收。","C":"拒绝代理委托人，但向她披露有关不利环境影响的信息。","D":"拒绝代理委托人，并且不披露有关不利环境影响的信息。"},"explanationZh":"D项正确。曾受雇于政府机构的律师，之后不得代理委托人处理其作为公职人员曾“亲自且实质性参与”的事项，除非该政府机构书面同意。《职业行为示范规则》第1.11(a)(2)条。如果该律师在律师事务所执业，这一取消代理资格通常也会延伸至事务所的其他律师，除非事务所建立隔离措施并书面通知有关政府机构。《职业行为示范规则》第1.11(b)条。\n\n本题中，律师受雇于政府机构期间曾对委托人的土地进行产权调查，因而亲自且实质性地参与了该事项。此外，他不得披露在该部门工作期间获悉的保密信息，因为曾受雇于政府机构的律师不得泄露与先前代理有关的保密信息。《职业行为示范规则》第1.11(a)(1)条和第1.9(c)条。\n\nA项错误。律师作为公职人员曾亲自且实质性地参与该事项，因此，除非政府机构书面同意，否则他现在不能代理该委托人；他是否打算使用任职期间获得的保密信息并不重要。尤其是，即使他只代理损害赔偿部分，仍然是在“与其曾亲自且实质性参与的事项有关”的范围内代理委托人。\n\nB项错误。如上所述，他既不能代理委托人，也不能披露该保密信息。\n\nC项错误。无论是否代理委托人，他都不能披露政府的保密信息。《职业行为示范规则》第1.11(a)(1)条和第1.9(c)条。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-014","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":14,"topic":"Model Rule 1.9 / Model Rule 1.9(a) / Model Rule 1.10(a)","question":"An assistant district attorney was in charge of the presentation before a grand jury of evidence that led to an indictment charging 32 defendants with conspiracy to sell controlled drugs. Shortly after the grand jury returned the indictments, the attorney resigned as assistant district attorney and became an associate in the law office of an attorney for one of the indicted codefendants. The former district attorney did not reveal any confidence or secret learned while he was an assistant district attorney, but he also did not expressly state he would not participate in the case. Is it proper for that attorney to continue to represent the codefendant?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the former assistant district attorney did not reveal to the attorney any confidence or secret learned while an assistant district attorney.","B":"Yes, because a public prosecutor must make timely disclosure to the defense attorney of any exculpatory evidence.","C":"No, because the former assistant district attorney did not agree to not participate in the representation of the codefendant.","D":"No, because the former assistant district attorney had substantial responsibility for the indictment of the codefendant."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. These facts invoke the concepts of conflicts of interest and imputed disqualification. Model Rule 1.9 deals with the conflict faced by a lawyer who has represented one client, terminates the representation, and then is asked to represent a new client with interests adverse to those of the lawyer’s former client. If you simplify these facts, it’s clear that the former assistant district attorney could not himself represent the codefendant after he participated in the investigation of the codefendant because the interests of the codefendant are adverse to the former assistant district attorney’s interests. Model Rule 1.9(a). But the question asks about the attorney’s representation of the codefendant, not the former assistant district attorney’s. Model Rule 1.10(a) extends the former assistant district attorney’s disqualification to the attorney under the imputed disqualification rule: “While lawyers are associated in a firm, none of them shall knowingly represent a client when any one of them practicing alone would be prohibited from doing so by Model Rules 1.7. or 1.9.” A is incorrect. Model Rule 1.9 doesn’t give the lawyer with the prohibited conflict the discretion to decide that he or she can protect the interests of the new client by protecting confidences. Under these circumstances, the lawyer may not represent the new client if (1) the lawyer himself or herself formerly represented a client in a substantially related matter with interests adverse to the new client’s interests (Model Rule 1.9 (a)) or (2) the lawyer was associated with a firm representing a client with interests adverse to his or her present client, about whom the lawyer acquired information protected by the confidentiality rules that is material to the new client’s matter (Model Rule 1.9(b)(2)). B is incorrect. It states correctly a basic rule that prosecutors must follow, but it is not responsive to the question. The facts do not suggest that there was any exculpatory evidence, or that the former assistant district attorney knew of or withheld any. Also, the statement in B is irrelevant to the facts because the former assistant district attorney is no longer a prosecutor. C is incorrect. The initial prohibition is directed at the former assistant district attorney because he’s the lawyer with the conflict between the former and present clients. But the prohibition extends to the attorney under the imputed disqualification rule, whether or not the former assistant district attorney participates. Model Rule 1.10.","rule":"D is the correct answer. These facts invoke the concepts of conflicts of interest and imputed disqualification. Model Rule 1.9 deals with the conflict faced by a lawyer who has represented one client, terminates the representation, and then is asked to represent a new client with interests adverse to those of the lawyer’s former client. If you simplify these facts, it’s clear that the former assistant district attorney could not himself represent the codefendant after he participated in the investigation of the codefendant because the interests of the codefendant are adverse to the former assistant district attorney’s interests. Model Rule 1.9(a). But the question asks about the attorney’s representation of the codefendant, not the former assistant district attorney’s. Model Rule 1.10(a) extends the former assistant district attorney’s disqualification to the attorney under the imputed disqualification rule: “While lawyers are associated in a firm, none of them shall knowingly represent a client when any one of them practicing alone would be prohibited from doing so by Model Rules 1.7. or 1.9.” A is incorrect. Model Rule 1.9 doesn’t give the lawyer with the prohibited conflict the discretion to decide that he or she can protect the interests of the new client by protecting confidences. Under these circumstances, the lawyer may not represent the new client if (1) the lawyer himself or herself formerly represented a client in a substantially related matter with interests adverse to the new client’s interests (Model Rule 1.9 (a)) or (2) the lawyer was associated with a firm representing a client with interests adverse to his or her present client, about whom the lawyer acquired information protected by the confidentiality rules that is material to the new client’s matter (Model Rule 1.9(b)(2)). B is incorrect. It states correctly a basic rule that prosecutors must follow, but it is not responsive to the question. The facts do not suggest that there was any exculpatory evidence, or that the former assistant district attorney knew of or withheld any. Also, the statement in B is irrelevant to the facts because the former assistant district attorney is no longer a prosecutor. C is incorrect. The initial prohibition is directed at the former assistant district attorney because he’s the lawyer with the conflict between the former and present clients. But the prohibition extends to the attorney under the imputed disqualification rule, whether or not the former assistant district attorney participates. Model Rule 1.10.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for that attorney to continue to represent the codefendant?","rule":"D is the correct answer. These facts invoke the concepts of conflicts of interest and imputed disqualification. Model Rule 1.9 deals with the conflict faced by a lawyer who has represented one client, terminates the representation, and then is asked to represent a new client with interests adverse to those of the lawyer’s former client. If you simplify these facts, it’s clear that the former assistant district attorney could not himself represent the codefendant after he participated in the investigation of the codefendant because the interests of the codefendant are adverse to the former assistant district attorney’s interests. Model Rule 1.9(a). But the question asks about the attorney’s representation of the codefendant, not the former assistant district attorney’s. Model Rule 1.10(a) extends the former assistant district attorney’s disqualification to the attorney under the imputed disqualification rule: “While lawyers are associated in a firm, none of them shall knowingly represent a client when any one of them practicing alone would be prohibited from doing so by Model Rules 1.7. or 1.9.” A is incorrect. Model Rule 1.9 doesn’t give the lawyer with the prohibited conflict the discretion to decide that he or she can protect the interests of the new client by protecting confidences. Under these circumstances, the lawyer may not represent the new client if (1) the lawyer himself or herself formerly represented a client in a substantially related matter with interests adverse to the new client’s interests (Model Rule 1.9 (a)) or (2) the lawyer was associated with a firm representing a client with interests adverse to his or her present client, about whom the lawyer acquired information protected by the confidentiality rules that is material to the new client’s matter (Model Rule 1.9(b)(2)). B is incorrect. It states correctly a basic rule that prosecutors must follow, but it is not responsive to the question. The facts do not suggest that there was any exculpatory evidence, or that the former assistant district attorney knew of or withheld any. Also, the statement in B is irrelevant to the facts because the former assistant district attorney is no longer a prosecutor. C is incorrect. The initial prohibition is directed at the former assistant district attorney because he’s the lawyer with the conflict between the former and present clients. But the prohibition extends to the attorney under the imputed disqualification rule, whether or not the former assistant district attorney participates. Model Rule 1.10.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. These facts invoke the concepts of conflicts of interest and imputed disqualification. Model Rule 1.9 deals with the conflict faced by a lawyer who has represented one client, terminates the representation, and then is asked to represent a new client with interests adverse to those of the lawyer’s former client. If you simplify these facts, it’s clear that the former assistant district attorney could not himself represent the codefendant after he participated in the investigation of the codefendant because the interests of the codefendant are adverse to the former assistant district attorney’s interests. Model Rule 1.9(a). But the question asks about the attorney’s representation of the codefendant, not the former assistant district attorney’s. Model Rule 1.10(a) extends the former assistant district attorney’s disqualification to the attorney under the imputed disqualification rule: “While lawyers are associated in a firm, none of them shall knowingly represent a client when any one of them practicing alone would be prohibited from doing so by Model Rules 1.7. or 1.9.” A is incorrect. Model Rule 1.9 doesn’t give the lawyer with the prohibited conflict the discretion to decide that he or she can protect the interests of the new client by protecting confidences. Under these circumstances, the lawyer may not represent the new client if (1) the lawyer himself or herself formerly represented a client in a substantially related matter with interests adverse to the new client’s interests (Model Rule 1.9 (a)) or (2) the lawyer was associated with a firm representing a client with interests adverse to his or her present client, about whom the lawyer acquired information protected by the confidentiality rules that is material to the new client’s matter (Model Rule 1.9(b)(2)). B is incorrect. It states correctly a basic rule that prosecutors must follow, but it is not responsive to the question. The facts do not suggest that there was any exculpatory evidence, or that the former assistant district attorney knew of or withheld any. Also, the statement in B is irrelevant to the facts because the former assistant district attorney is no longer a prosecutor. C is incorrect. The initial prohibition is directed at the former assistant district attorney because he’s the lawyer with the conflict between the former and present clients. But the prohibition extends to the attorney under the imputed disqualification rule, whether or not the former assistant district attorney participates. Model Rule 1.10.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.9","Model Rule 1.9(a)","Model Rule 1.10(a)","Model Rule 1.9(b)(2)","Model Rule 1.10"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[55,56],"sourceAnswerPages":[95,96],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名助理地区检察官负责向大陪审团提交证据，该证据促使大陪审团起诉32名被告共谋销售管制药物。大陪审团提出起诉书后不久，该律师辞去助理地区检察官职务，成为其中一名被起诉共同被告的辩护律师事务所中的受雇律师。这名前助理地区检察官没有透露其任职期间获悉的任何保密信息或秘密，但也没有明确表示自己不会参与该案。原先代理该共同被告的律师继续代理是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为这名前助理地区检察官没有向该律师透露其任职期间获悉的任何保密信息或秘密。","B":"是，因为公诉人必须及时向辩护律师披露任何有利于被告的证据。","C":"否，因为这名前助理地区检察官没有同意不参与对该共同被告的代理。","D":"否，因为这名前助理地区检察官对起诉该共同被告承担了重要责任。"},"explanationZh":"D项正确。本题涉及利益冲突和利益冲突归责。《职业行为示范规则》第1.9条处理以下冲突：律师曾代理一名委托人，代理终止后，又被要求代理一名利益与前委托人相对立的新委托人。把本题事实简化后可以看出，这名前助理地区检察官参加过对共同被告的调查，因此不能亲自代理该共同被告，因为共同被告的利益与其前政府委托人的利益相对立。《职业行为示范规则》第1.9(a)条。\n\n但是，本题询问的是该共同被告原律师的代理，而不是前助理地区检察官本人的代理。第1.10(a)条依据利益冲突归责规则，把前助理地区检察官的取消代理资格延伸至同一事务所的该律师：“律师在同一事务所执业期间，如果其中任何一名律师单独执业时会因第1.7条或第1.9条而被禁止代理，其他律师均不得明知而代理该委托人。”\n\nA项错误。第1.9条并未赋予存在禁止性冲突的律师自行判断其是否能够通过保护保密信息来维护新委托人利益的权力。在下列情况下，律师不得代理新委托人：（1）律师本人先前在实质相关事项中代理的委托人与新委托人利益相对立（第1.9(a)条）；或者（2）律师先前所在事务所代理过与其现委托人利益相对立的一方，而律师取得了受保密规则保护且对新事项重大的信息（第1.9(b)(2)条）。\n\nB项错误。该选项正确陈述了检察官必须遵守的一项基本规则，但没有回答本题。事实没有表明存在任何有利于被告的证据，也没有表明前助理地区检察官知道或隐瞒了此类证据。此外，他现在已经不再是检察官，该说法与本题事实无关。\n\nC项错误。最初的禁止针对前助理地区检察官，因为他在前委托人与现委托人之间存在冲突；但无论他是否实际参与该案，该禁止都会根据利益冲突归责规则延伸至同一事务所的律师。《职业行为示范规则》第1.10条。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-015","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":15,"topic":"Model Rule 1.6(a) / Model Rule 1.6(b)","question":"An attorney filed an action on behalf of his client for breach of contract. In fact, the client had no legal basis for suit, but wanted to harass the defendant. In order to induce the attorney to file the action, the client made certain false statements of material fact to the attorney, which the attorney included in the complaint filed against the defendant. At the trial of the case, the client took the stand and testified as set forth in the complaint. The trial court ordered judgment for the client. After entry of judgment, the client wrote the attorney a letter marked “Confidential,” in which the client admitted that she had lied to the attorney and had testified falsely in the case. Upon complaint of the defendant, who claimed the attorney had knowingly used false testimony in the case, disciplinary proceedings were instituted against the attorney. Is it proper for the attorney to use the client’s letter in the attorney’s defense in the disciplinary proceedings?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because it is necessary to do so in order to protect the attorney’s rights.","B":"Yes, because the client had committed a fraud on the court in which the case was tried.","C":"No, because the attorney learned the facts from the client in confidence.","D":"No, because disclosure by the attorney could result in the client’s prosecution for perjury."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. The question deals with the general obligation of a lawyer not to reveal any information relating to representation of a client without the client’s consent after consultation. The obligation is defined in Model Rule 1.6(a). Model Rule 1.6(b) creates three exceptions to the general rule. One of the exceptions permits a lawyer to reveal a client confidence to defend him- or herself in a controversy with the client or against a criminal charge or civil claim for conduct involving the client, or to respond to allegations in any proceeding concerning the representation. Because the defendant has made a serious charge that would have grave consequences for the attorney if accepted as true, the attorney has a right to reveal the contents of the client’s letter. B is incorrect. Although the client has indeed committed a fraud on the court, the attorney would be obligated not to reveal the fraud except for the need to protect himself against the false accusation of complicity. General client fraud does not fall within one of the exceptions to the general duty of confidentiality. C is incorrect. Whether or not the attorney learned the facts in confidences, he has a right to disclose them to protect himself in the disciplinary proceeding. D is incorrect. When a lawyer is accused of a disciplinary infraction, he or she has a right to disclose a client’s confidences to defend him- or herself. In the balance between the lawyer’s right to defend himself or herself and the consequences to the client of revealing the client’s confidence, the Rules have tipped the scales in favor of the lawyer, whose livelihood and reputation are at stake. Otherwise, the lawyer would be unable to defend him- or herself.","rule":"A is the correct answer. The question deals with the general obligation of a lawyer not to reveal any information relating to representation of a client without the client’s consent after consultation. The obligation is defined in Model Rule 1.6(a). Model Rule 1.6(b) creates three exceptions to the general rule. One of the exceptions permits a lawyer to reveal a client confidence to defend him- or herself in a controversy with the client or against a criminal charge or civil claim for conduct involving the client, or to respond to allegations in any proceeding concerning the representation. Because the defendant has made a serious charge that would have grave consequences for the attorney if accepted as true, the attorney has a right to reveal the contents of the client’s letter. B is incorrect. Although the client has indeed committed a fraud on the court, the attorney would be obligated not to reveal the fraud except for the need to protect himself against the false accusation of complicity. General client fraud does not fall within one of the exceptions to the general duty of confidentiality. C is incorrect. Whether or not the attorney learned the facts in confidences, he has a right to disclose them to protect himself in the disciplinary proceeding. D is incorrect. When a lawyer is accused of a disciplinary infraction, he or she has a right to disclose a client’s confidences to defend him- or herself. In the balance between the lawyer’s right to defend himself or herself and the consequences to the client of revealing the client’s confidence, the Rules have tipped the scales in favor of the lawyer, whose livelihood and reputation are at stake. Otherwise, the lawyer would be unable to defend him- or herself.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the attorney to use the client’s letter in the attorney’s defense in the disciplinary proceedings?","rule":"A is the correct answer. The question deals with the general obligation of a lawyer not to reveal any information relating to representation of a client without the client’s consent after consultation. The obligation is defined in Model Rule 1.6(a). Model Rule 1.6(b) creates three exceptions to the general rule. One of the exceptions permits a lawyer to reveal a client confidence to defend him- or herself in a controversy with the client or against a criminal charge or civil claim for conduct involving the client, or to respond to allegations in any proceeding concerning the representation. Because the defendant has made a serious charge that would have grave consequences for the attorney if accepted as true, the attorney has a right to reveal the contents of the client’s letter. B is incorrect. Although the client has indeed committed a fraud on the court, the attorney would be obligated not to reveal the fraud except for the need to protect himself against the false accusation of complicity. General client fraud does not fall within one of the exceptions to the general duty of confidentiality. C is incorrect. Whether or not the attorney learned the facts in confidences, he has a right to disclose them to protect himself in the disciplinary proceeding. D is incorrect. When a lawyer is accused of a disciplinary infraction, he or she has a right to disclose a client’s confidences to defend him- or herself. In the balance between the lawyer’s right to defend himself or herself and the consequences to the client of revealing the client’s confidence, the Rules have tipped the scales in favor of the lawyer, whose livelihood and reputation are at stake. Otherwise, the lawyer would be unable to defend him- or herself.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. The question deals with the general obligation of a lawyer not to reveal any information relating to representation of a client without the client’s consent after consultation. The obligation is defined in Model Rule 1.6(a). Model Rule 1.6(b) creates three exceptions to the general rule. One of the exceptions permits a lawyer to reveal a client confidence to defend him- or herself in a controversy with the client or against a criminal charge or civil claim for conduct involving the client, or to respond to allegations in any proceeding concerning the representation. Because the defendant has made a serious charge that would have grave consequences for the attorney if accepted as true, the attorney has a right to reveal the contents of the client’s letter. B is incorrect. Although the client has indeed committed a fraud on the court, the attorney would be obligated not to reveal the fraud except for the need to protect himself against the false accusation of complicity. General client fraud does not fall within one of the exceptions to the general duty of confidentiality. C is incorrect. Whether or not the attorney learned the facts in confidences, he has a right to disclose them to protect himself in the disciplinary proceeding. D is incorrect. When a lawyer is accused of a disciplinary infraction, he or she has a right to disclose a client’s confidences to defend him- or herself. In the balance between the lawyer’s right to defend himself or herself and the consequences to the client of revealing the client’s confidence, the Rules have tipped the scales in favor of the lawyer, whose livelihood and reputation are at stake. Otherwise, the lawyer would be unable to defend him- or herself.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.6(a)","Model Rule 1.6(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[56,57],"sourceAnswerPages":[96,97],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代表委托人提起违约之诉。事实上，委托人的起诉没有任何法律依据，她只是想骚扰被告。为了诱使律师提起诉讼，委托人向律师作出若干关于重大事实的虚假陈述，律师把这些陈述写入针对被告提交的起诉状。在庭审中，委托人出庭并按照起诉状中的内容作证。法院判决委托人胜诉。判决登记后，委托人给律师写了一封标注“保密”的信，承认自己曾欺骗律师，并在案件中作了虚假证言。\n\n随后，被告投诉称律师在案件中明知而使用虚假证言，纪律机构因此对该律师启动纪律程序。在纪律程序中，律师使用委托人的信为自己辩护是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为为了保护律师自身权利，有必要这样做。","B":"是，因为委托人对审理该案的法院实施了欺诈。","C":"否，因为律师是在保密情况下从委托人处得知这些事实。","D":"否，因为律师披露该信可能导致委托人因伪证罪被追诉。"},"explanationZh":"A项正确。本题涉及律师未经委托人咨询后同意，不得披露与代理有关信息的一般义务，该义务规定于《职业行为示范规则》第1.6(a)条。第1.6(b)条对一般规则规定了若干例外。其中一项允许律师为在与委托人的争议中为自己辩护、应对涉及委托人的行为而产生的刑事指控或民事请求，或者回应任何程序中有关其代理行为的指控，而披露委托人的保密信息。由于被告提出的指控十分严重，一旦被认定属实将给律师造成重大后果，因此律师有权披露委托人信件的内容。\n\nB项错误。虽然委托人确实对法院实施了欺诈，但如果不是为了保护律师免受“共同参与欺诈”这一虚假指控，律师仍负有不披露该欺诈的义务。一般性的委托人欺诈并不属于保密义务的一般例外。\n\nC项错误。无论律师是否在保密情况下得知这些事实，他都有权为在纪律程序中保护自己而披露。\n\nD项错误。当律师被指控违反纪律规则时，有权披露委托人的保密信息以进行自我辩护。在律师自我辩护的权利与披露保密信息对委托人造成的后果之间，规则倾向保护其生计和声誉面临风险的律师；否则，律师将无法为自己辩护。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-016","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":16,"topic":"Model Rule 1.18 / Model Rule 7.4","question":"An attorney who is a sole practitioner limits his practice to personal injury cases. He regularly places advertisements in local newspapers that state that his practice is limited to personal injury cases, including medical malpractice. After seeing one of the attorney’s ads, a man approached the attorney for representation in a medical malpractice case. After a 30-minute interview, the attorney told the man that he was too busy to take his case because it appeared quite complicated. He further offered to refer the man to another lawyer who regularly practiced in the field. He reminded the man that he should see another lawyer promptly before the statute of limitations expired and he lost his right to sue. Although the attorney did not charge the man for the interview, the man was upset at wasting 30 minutes of his time. The man did not contact another lawyer until eight months later, when he learned that the statute of limitations on his claim had expired six months after his interview with the attorney. In fact, the man had a meritorious medical malpractice claim. Is the attorney subject to civil liability?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney falsely advertised his availability for medical malpractice cases.","B":"Yes, because the attorney did not advise the man as to the date the statute of limitations would expire.","C":"No, because the attorney did not violate any duty owed to the man.","D":"No, because the attorney offered to refer the man to another medical malpractice lawyer."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.18, a prospective client is a person who discusses with the lawyer the possibility of forming a client-lawyer relationship with respect to a certain matter. The Comment to the Rule recognizes that prospective clients should receive some but not all of the protection afforded clients. Here, the attorney talked to the prospective client, declined the representation, gave the man another lawyer’s name, and urged him to contact the other lawyer in a timely manner. Under the Rule, the attorney did not owe the prospective client any other duty, except that he must not mislead the prospective client into believing the attorney will represent him, and he must also protect any confidential information revealed by the prospective client. Model Rules 3.3 and 1.6. A is incorrect because the attorney did not advertise falsely. The advertisement did not state, either expressly or impliedly, that he would take every case presented to him. Importantly, advertising in and of itself does not create an attorney-client relationship. False or misleading advertising is improper, but a lawyer may communicate that he or she practices certain types of law. Model Rules 7.1 and 7.4. However, with certain historical exceptions (such as “patent attorney”), a lawyer may not state or imply he or she is a specialist in a particular field of law unless the lawyer has been certified as a specialist by an organization that has been approved by an appropriate state authority or the American Bar Association. Model Rule 7.4. The name of any certifying organization must be clearly identified in the communication. B is incorrect. Under these circumstances, the attorney was not required to give the man the exact date the statute of limitations would run. However, if that date had been within a very short time, such as one to two days instead of the six months here, the attorney may have had a duty to warn him. D is incorrect. This answer implies that the reason the attorney is not subject to civil liability is because he offered to give the man a referral. However, as stated above, the reason the attorney is not subject to liability is because he did everything that was required under Model Rule 1.18. Always pick the answer that addresses the exact requirements and concerns of the particular Rule being tested.","rule":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.18, a prospective client is a person who discusses with the lawyer the possibility of forming a client-lawyer relationship with respect to a certain matter. The Comment to the Rule recognizes that prospective clients should receive some but not all of the protection afforded clients. Here, the attorney talked to the prospective client, declined the representation, gave the man another lawyer’s name, and urged him to contact the other lawyer in a timely manner. Under the Rule, the attorney did not owe the prospective client any other duty, except that he must not mislead the prospective client into believing the attorney will represent him, and he must also protect any confidential information revealed by the prospective client. Model Rules 3.3 and 1.6. A is incorrect because the attorney did not advertise falsely. The advertisement did not state, either expressly or impliedly, that he would take every case presented to him. Importantly, advertising in and of itself does not create an attorney-client relationship. False or misleading advertising is improper, but a lawyer may communicate that he or she practices certain types of law. Model Rules 7.1 and 7.4. However, with certain historical exceptions (such as “patent attorney”), a lawyer may not state or imply he or she is a specialist in a particular field of law unless the lawyer has been certified as a specialist by an organization that has been approved by an appropriate state authority or the American Bar Association. Model Rule 7.4. The name of any certifying organization must be clearly identified in the communication. B is incorrect. Under these circumstances, the attorney was not required to give the man the exact date the statute of limitations would run. However, if that date had been within a very short time, such as one to two days instead of the six months here, the attorney may have had a duty to warn him. D is incorrect. This answer implies that the reason the attorney is not subject to civil liability is because he offered to give the man a referral. However, as stated above, the reason the attorney is not subject to liability is because he did everything that was required under Model Rule 1.18. Always pick the answer that addresses the exact requirements and concerns of the particular Rule being tested.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to civil liability?","rule":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.18, a prospective client is a person who discusses with the lawyer the possibility of forming a client-lawyer relationship with respect to a certain matter. The Comment to the Rule recognizes that prospective clients should receive some but not all of the protection afforded clients. Here, the attorney talked to the prospective client, declined the representation, gave the man another lawyer’s name, and urged him to contact the other lawyer in a timely manner. Under the Rule, the attorney did not owe the prospective client any other duty, except that he must not mislead the prospective client into believing the attorney will represent him, and he must also protect any confidential information revealed by the prospective client. Model Rules 3.3 and 1.6. A is incorrect because the attorney did not advertise falsely. The advertisement did not state, either expressly or impliedly, that he would take every case presented to him. Importantly, advertising in and of itself does not create an attorney-client relationship. False or misleading advertising is improper, but a lawyer may communicate that he or she practices certain types of law. Model Rules 7.1 and 7.4. However, with certain historical exceptions (such as “patent attorney”), a lawyer may not state or imply he or she is a specialist in a particular field of law unless the lawyer has been certified as a specialist by an organization that has been approved by an appropriate state authority or the American Bar Association. Model Rule 7.4. The name of any certifying organization must be clearly identified in the communication. B is incorrect. Under these circumstances, the attorney was not required to give the man the exact date the statute of limitations would run. However, if that date had been within a very short time, such as one to two days instead of the six months here, the attorney may have had a duty to warn him. D is incorrect. This answer implies that the reason the attorney is not subject to civil liability is because he offered to give the man a referral. However, as stated above, the reason the attorney is not subject to liability is because he did everything that was required under Model Rule 1.18. Always pick the answer that addresses the exact requirements and concerns of the particular Rule being tested.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.18, a prospective client is a person who discusses with the lawyer the possibility of forming a client-lawyer relationship with respect to a certain matter. The Comment to the Rule recognizes that prospective clients should receive some but not all of the protection afforded clients. Here, the attorney talked to the prospective client, declined the representation, gave the man another lawyer’s name, and urged him to contact the other lawyer in a timely manner. Under the Rule, the attorney did not owe the prospective client any other duty, except that he must not mislead the prospective client into believing the attorney will represent him, and he must also protect any confidential information revealed by the prospective client. Model Rules 3.3 and 1.6. A is incorrect because the attorney did not advertise falsely. The advertisement did not state, either expressly or impliedly, that he would take every case presented to him. Importantly, advertising in and of itself does not create an attorney-client relationship. False or misleading advertising is improper, but a lawyer may communicate that he or she practices certain types of law. Model Rules 7.1 and 7.4. However, with certain historical exceptions (such as “patent attorney”), a lawyer may not state or imply he or she is a specialist in a particular field of law unless the lawyer has been certified as a specialist by an organization that has been approved by an appropriate state authority or the American Bar Association. Model Rule 7.4. The name of any certifying organization must be clearly identified in the communication. B is incorrect. Under these circumstances, the attorney was not required to give the man the exact date the statute of limitations would run. However, if that date had been within a very short time, such as one to two days instead of the six months here, the attorney may have had a duty to warn him. D is incorrect. This answer implies that the reason the attorney is not subject to civil liability is because he offered to give the man a referral. However, as stated above, the reason the attorney is not subject to liability is because he did everything that was required under Model Rule 1.18. Always pick the answer that addresses the exact requirements and concerns of the particular Rule being tested.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.18","Model Rule 7.4"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[57],"sourceAnswerPages":[97],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名独立执业律师只办理人身伤害案件。他经常在当地报纸刊登广告，说明其业务限于人身伤害案件，包括医疗过失案件。一名男子看到广告后，来找该律师代理一宗医疗过失案件。面谈30分钟后，律师告诉男子，该案看起来相当复杂，而自己太忙，无法承办；律师还提出把男子转介给另一名经常办理此类案件的律师，并提醒他应当在诉讼时效届满、丧失起诉权之前尽快去见另一名律师。律师没有收取面谈费用，但男子对浪费了30分钟感到不满。八个月后，男子才联系另一名律师，此时他得知，其请求的诉讼时效早在与该律师面谈六个月后就已经届满。事实上，该男子的医疗过失请求具有法律依据。该律师是否应承担民事责任？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该律师虚假宣传自己可以承办医疗过失案件。","B":"是，因为该律师没有告知男子诉讼时效届满的具体日期。","C":"否，因为该律师没有违反其对该男子负有的任何义务。","D":"否，因为该律师曾提出把男子转介给另一名医疗过失律师。"},"explanationZh":"C项正确。根据《职业行为示范规则》第1.18条，潜在委托人是指就某一事项与律师讨论建立律师—委托人关系可能性的人。该条评注承认，潜在委托人应获得给予正式委托人的部分、但并非全部保护。本题中，律师与潜在委托人交谈后拒绝代理，向男子提供了另一名律师的信息，并敦促他及时联系另一名律师。依据规则，律师不再对其负有其他义务，但不得误导潜在委托人，使其相信律师将会代理，也必须保护潜在委托人披露的保密信息。参见《职业行为示范规则》第3.3条和第1.6条。\n\nA项错误，因为律师没有发布虚假广告。广告既未明示也未暗示律师会接受向其提出的每一个案件。尤其要注意，刊登广告本身并不会建立律师—委托人关系。虚假或误导性广告属于不当行为，但律师可以说明自己从事某些种类的法律业务。《职业行为示范规则》第7.1条和第7.4条。不过，除少数历史形成的称谓（例如“专利律师”）外，律师不得声称或暗示自己是某一法律领域的专家，除非其已经获得有关州主管机关或美国律师协会认可机构的专家认证；宣传中还必须清楚标明认证机构名称。《职业行为示范规则》第7.4条。\n\nB项错误。在本题情况下，律师无须告知男子诉讼时效届满的确切日期。不过，如果时效只剩极短时间，例如一两天而不是本题的六个月，律师可能负有警告义务。\n\nD项错误。该选项暗示律师不承担民事责任的原因，是他提出了转介。但如上所述，真正原因是律师已经履行第1.18条所要求的一切义务。应当选择准确对应所考规则具体要求与关注点的答案。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-017","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":17,"topic":"Model Rule 1.2(d)","question":"An attorney is employed by a client who is a fugitive from justice under indictment for armed robbery. The attorney, after thorough legal research and investigation of the facts furnished by the client, reasonably believes the indictment is fatally defective and should be dismissed as a matter of law. The attorney advised the client of his opinion and urged the client to surrender. The client told the attorney that she would not surrender. The attorney informed the district attorney that he represented the client and that he had counseled her to surrender but that she refused to follow his advice. The attorney has not advised his client on how to avoid arrest and prosecution and does not know where she is hiding. Is the attorney subject to discipline if he continues to represent the client?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the client is engaging in continuing illegal conduct.","B":"Yes, because the client refused to accept the attorney’s advice and surrender.","C":"No, because the attorney is not counseling the client to avoid arrest and prosecution.","D":"No, because the attorney reasonably believes the indictment is defective."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may not knowingly counsel or assist a client in conduct the lawyer knows is criminal or fraudulent. Model Rule 1.2(d). When the client’s course of action has already begun and is continuing, the lawyer is required to avoid assisting the client in the criminal act, such as by suggesting how the wrongdoing can be concealed. Here, although the client is engaged in continuing illegal conduct by remaining a fugitive, the attorney is not actually counseling or assisting the client in doing so (in fact, he encouraged the client to surrender). The important point in the Rule is “counsel or assist” in the criminal act. Consequently, the attorney is acting correctly under the Rules. A is incorrect. While the client is engaging in continuing illegal conduct, the client is nevertheless entitled to the advice of a lawyer, and the attorney is not helping her to remain in hiding. Importantly, the attorney may discuss the legal consequences of any proposed course of conduct and may counsel or assist the client in making a good faith effort to determine the validity, scope, meaning, or application of the law. Model Rule 1.2(d). B is incorrect. If a lawyer gives appropriate advice, he or she cannot be disciplined simply because his or her client refuses to listen. D is incorrect. Whether or not the indictment is defective, the attorney is still acting appropriately here.","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may not knowingly counsel or assist a client in conduct the lawyer knows is criminal or fraudulent. Model Rule 1.2(d). When the client’s course of action has already begun and is continuing, the lawyer is required to avoid assisting the client in the criminal act, such as by suggesting how the wrongdoing can be concealed. Here, although the client is engaged in continuing illegal conduct by remaining a fugitive, the attorney is not actually counseling or assisting the client in doing so (in fact, he encouraged the client to surrender). The important point in the Rule is “counsel or assist” in the criminal act. Consequently, the attorney is acting correctly under the Rules. A is incorrect. While the client is engaging in continuing illegal conduct, the client is nevertheless entitled to the advice of a lawyer, and the attorney is not helping her to remain in hiding. Importantly, the attorney may discuss the legal consequences of any proposed course of conduct and may counsel or assist the client in making a good faith effort to determine the validity, scope, meaning, or application of the law. Model Rule 1.2(d). B is incorrect. If a lawyer gives appropriate advice, he or she cannot be disciplined simply because his or her client refuses to listen. D is incorrect. Whether or not the indictment is defective, the attorney is still acting appropriately here.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline if he continues to represent the client?","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may not knowingly counsel or assist a client in conduct the lawyer knows is criminal or fraudulent. Model Rule 1.2(d). When the client’s course of action has already begun and is continuing, the lawyer is required to avoid assisting the client in the criminal act, such as by suggesting how the wrongdoing can be concealed. Here, although the client is engaged in continuing illegal conduct by remaining a fugitive, the attorney is not actually counseling or assisting the client in doing so (in fact, he encouraged the client to surrender). The important point in the Rule is “counsel or assist” in the criminal act. Consequently, the attorney is acting correctly under the Rules. A is incorrect. While the client is engaging in continuing illegal conduct, the client is nevertheless entitled to the advice of a lawyer, and the attorney is not helping her to remain in hiding. Importantly, the attorney may discuss the legal consequences of any proposed course of conduct and may counsel or assist the client in making a good faith effort to determine the validity, scope, meaning, or application of the law. Model Rule 1.2(d). B is incorrect. If a lawyer gives appropriate advice, he or she cannot be disciplined simply because his or her client refuses to listen. D is incorrect. Whether or not the indictment is defective, the attorney is still acting appropriately here.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may not knowingly counsel or assist a client in conduct the lawyer knows is criminal or fraudulent. Model Rule 1.2(d). When the client’s course of action has already begun and is continuing, the lawyer is required to avoid assisting the client in the criminal act, such as by suggesting how the wrongdoing can be concealed. Here, although the client is engaged in continuing illegal conduct by remaining a fugitive, the attorney is not actually counseling or assisting the client in doing so (in fact, he encouraged the client to surrender). The important point in the Rule is “counsel or assist” in the criminal act. Consequently, the attorney is acting correctly under the Rules. A is incorrect. While the client is engaging in continuing illegal conduct, the client is nevertheless entitled to the advice of a lawyer, and the attorney is not helping her to remain in hiding. Importantly, the attorney may discuss the legal consequences of any proposed course of conduct and may counsel or assist the client in making a good faith effort to determine the validity, scope, meaning, or application of the law. Model Rule 1.2(d). B is incorrect. If a lawyer gives appropriate advice, he or she cannot be disciplined simply because his or her client refuses to listen. D is incorrect. Whether or not the indictment is defective, the attorney is still acting appropriately here.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.2(d)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[57,58],"sourceAnswerPages":[98],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师受雇代理一名因持械抢劫被起诉、目前在逃的委托人。律师对委托人提供的事实进行充分调查和法律研究后，合理相信起诉书存在致命缺陷，依法应予撤销。律师把自己的意见告知委托人并敦促她自首，但委托人表示不会自首。律师通知地区检察官，自己代理该委托人，已劝其自首但她拒绝听从。律师没有建议委托人如何逃避逮捕和追诉，也不知道她藏在哪里。如果律师继续代理该委托人，他是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为委托人正在持续实施违法行为。","B":"是，因为委托人拒绝接受律师建议并自首。","C":"否，因为律师没有建议或帮助委托人逃避逮捕和追诉。","D":"否，因为律师合理相信起诉书存在缺陷。"},"explanationZh":"C项正确。律师不得明知而建议或协助委托人实施律师明知属于犯罪或欺诈的行为。《职业行为示范规则》第1.2(d)条。当委托人的行为已经开始并仍在持续时，律师必须避免协助该犯罪行为，例如不得建议如何掩盖不法行为。本题中，委托人继续在逃确属持续违法行为，但律师并未实际建议或协助她这样做；相反，他还敦促她自首。规则的重点是是否“建议或协助”实施犯罪。因此，该律师的行为符合规则。\n\nA项错误。尽管委托人正在持续实施违法行为，她仍有权获得律师的法律意见，而且该律师没有帮助她继续藏匿。特别要注意，律师可以讨论任何拟议行为的法律后果，也可以建议或协助委托人善意地确定法律的效力、范围、含义或适用。《职业行为示范规则》第1.2(d)条。\n\nB项错误。律师提出适当建议后，不能仅仅因为委托人拒绝听从而受到纪律处分。\n\nD项错误。无论起诉书是否存在缺陷，该律师在本题中的行为都同样适当。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-018","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":18,"topic":"Model Rule 4.2 / Rule 4.2","question":"An attorney represented a real estate developer who was trying to buy several properties. The attorney arranged a meeting with an owner of two large parcels of land, hoping to arrange a sale to the developer. When the attorney scheduled this meeting, he neither knew nor asked whether the owner was represented by counsel in the matter. Shortly after the meeting began, the owner disclosed that he had retained counsel to assist in the sale of the two parcels of land, but that his lawyer could not be present that day. He further stated that he would be meeting with his lawyer the next day. The attorney asked the owner if they could talk anyway, and stated that he wouldn’t ask the owner to sign anything until his lawyer had a chance to look over anything they discussed. The owner, an experienced businessman and negotiator, agreed to continue as suggested, and a tentative agreement was soon worked out. Was the attorney’s conduct proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the owner knowingly agreed to continue the discussions without his own lawyer being present.","B":"Yes, because the attorney did not present the owner with any documents to sign during the meeting.","C":"No, because the attorney negotiated with the owner after learning that the owner was represented by a lawyer in the matter.","D":"No, because the attorney failed to ascertain whether the owner was represented by a lawyer before beginning the negotiation session."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. “In representing a client, a lawyer shall not communicate about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows to be represented by another lawyer in the matter, unless the lawyer has the consent of the other lawyer or is authorized to do so by law or a court order.” Model Rule 4.2. This Rule applies even though the represented person initiates or consents to the communication. A is incorrect. The represented person’s lawyer is the one who must consent, not the represented person. Consequently, as stated above, Rule 4.2 applies even if the represented person initiates or consents to the communication. B is incorrect. The prohibition is on any communication, and is not based on the signing of any documents. D is incorrect. Under Rule 4.2, communication is prohibited only when the lawyer knows that the person is represented by another lawyer. This means that the lawyer must have actual knowledge of the representation, but such knowledge may be inferred from the circumstances. Thus, although the Rule imposes no duty on the lawyer to investigate, the lawyer cannot evade the Rule by ignoring the obvious.","rule":"C is the correct answer. “In representing a client, a lawyer shall not communicate about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows to be represented by another lawyer in the matter, unless the lawyer has the consent of the other lawyer or is authorized to do so by law or a court order.” Model Rule 4.2. This Rule applies even though the represented person initiates or consents to the communication. A is incorrect. The represented person’s lawyer is the one who must consent, not the represented person. Consequently, as stated above, Rule 4.2 applies even if the represented person initiates or consents to the communication. B is incorrect. The prohibition is on any communication, and is not based on the signing of any documents. D is incorrect. Under Rule 4.2, communication is prohibited only when the lawyer knows that the person is represented by another lawyer. This means that the lawyer must have actual knowledge of the representation, but such knowledge may be inferred from the circumstances. Thus, although the Rule imposes no duty on the lawyer to investigate, the lawyer cannot evade the Rule by ignoring the obvious.","irac":{"issue":"Was the attorney’s conduct proper?","rule":"C is the correct answer. “In representing a client, a lawyer shall not communicate about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows to be represented by another lawyer in the matter, unless the lawyer has the consent of the other lawyer or is authorized to do so by law or a court order.” Model Rule 4.2. This Rule applies even though the represented person initiates or consents to the communication. A is incorrect. The represented person’s lawyer is the one who must consent, not the represented person. Consequently, as stated above, Rule 4.2 applies even if the represented person initiates or consents to the communication. B is incorrect. The prohibition is on any communication, and is not based on the signing of any documents. D is incorrect. Under Rule 4.2, communication is prohibited only when the lawyer knows that the person is represented by another lawyer. This means that the lawyer must have actual knowledge of the representation, but such knowledge may be inferred from the circumstances. Thus, although the Rule imposes no duty on the lawyer to investigate, the lawyer cannot evade the Rule by ignoring the obvious.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. “In representing a client, a lawyer shall not communicate about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows to be represented by another lawyer in the matter, unless the lawyer has the consent of the other lawyer or is authorized to do so by law or a court order.” Model Rule 4.2. This Rule applies even though the represented person initiates or consents to the communication. A is incorrect. The represented person’s lawyer is the one who must consent, not the represented person. Consequently, as stated above, Rule 4.2 applies even if the represented person initiates or consents to the communication. B is incorrect. The prohibition is on any communication, and is not based on the signing of any documents. D is incorrect. Under Rule 4.2, communication is prohibited only when the lawyer knows that the person is represented by another lawyer. This means that the lawyer must have actual knowledge of the representation, but such knowledge may be inferred from the circumstances. Thus, although the Rule imposes no duty on the lawyer to investigate, the lawyer cannot evade the Rule by ignoring the obvious.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 4.2","Rule 4.2"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[58,59],"sourceAnswerPages":[98,99],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理一名试图购买多处不动产的房地产开发商。律师安排与两大块土地的所有人会面，希望促成其向开发商出售土地。安排会面时，律师既不知道也没有询问该土地所有人在此事中是否有律师代理。会面开始不久，土地所有人说明，他已经聘请律师协助出售两块土地，但其律师当天无法到场；他还说第二天将与自己的律师见面。该律师询问双方是否仍可继续交谈，并表示在土地所有人的律师有机会审阅双方讨论的任何内容之前，不会要求土地所有人签署文件。该土地所有人是一名经验丰富的商人和谈判者，同意按此建议继续，双方很快形成了一项初步协议。该律师的行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为土地所有人明知自己的律师不在场，仍同意继续讨论。","B":"是，因为该律师在会面期间没有要求土地所有人签署任何文件。","C":"否，因为律师得知土地所有人在此事中已有律师代理后，仍继续与其谈判。","D":"否，因为律师在开始谈判前没有查明土地所有人是否已有律师代理。"},"explanationZh":"C项正确。“律师在代理委托人时，不得就代理事项与律师知道在该事项中由另一名律师代理的人进行沟通，除非取得对方律师的同意，或者法律或法院命令授权这样做。”《职业行为示范规则》第4.2条。即使有律师代理的人主动发起或同意沟通，该规则仍然适用。\n\nA项错误。必须同意沟通的是该人的律师，而不是被代理人本人。因此，如上所述，即使被代理人主动发起或同意沟通，第4.2条仍然适用。\n\nB项错误。规则禁止的是任何有关代理事项的沟通，并不以是否签署文件为条件。\n\nD项错误。根据第4.2条，只有在律师知道对方由另一名律师代理时，沟通才被禁止。这要求律师具有实际认识，但该认识可以根据周围情形推断。规则并未规定律师负有主动调查义务，但律师不能通过故意无视明显事实来规避规则。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-019","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":19,"topic":"Model Rule 1.7 / Model Rule 1.7(a) / Model Rule 1.7(a)(2)","question":"An attorney prepared a will for a client and acted as one of the subscribing witnesses to the client’s execution of the will. The will left all of the client’s estate to his son. Later, at the client’s request, the attorney prepared a second will for the client and acted as one of the subscribing witnesses to the client’s execution of the second will. The second will left one-half of the client’s estate to his son and the other one-half to his housekeeper. The client died and the housekeeper has offered the second will for probate. If the son requests that the attorney represent him in opposing probate of the second will on the grounds of fraud and undue influence, is it proper for the attorney to do so?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because after the client’s death the attorney may represent his son.","B":"Yes, because the client’s son is a beneficiary under both wills.","C":"No, because an attorney guarantees the validity of a will that he or she prepares.","D":"No, because the attorney would be taking a position adverse to a will she prepared and witnessed."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. The guiding Rule is Model Rule 1.7, which deals with conflicts of interest. Unless the son is able to prove fraud and undue influence, you must assume that the client intended to cut his son’s bequest in half. Thus, his son’s position is directly adverse to the client’s. If the client were still alive, Model Rule 1.7(a) would preclude representation of his son by the attorney because the interests of two clients would be directly adverse to each other. Now that the client is dead, the attorney is still constrained by Model Rule 1.7(a)(2), which also directs a lawyer not to represent a client if representation of that client may be materially limited by the lawyer’s responsibilities to another client or to a third person. (A deceased client is still a client for purpose of this Rule.) Note also that the attorney will necessarily be called as witness to execution of the will and the behavior and circumstances of the deceased client. Because a lawyer may not act as advocate at a trial in which the lawyer is likely to be a necessary witness on a disputed issue (Model Rule 3.7), the attorney should not agree to represent the son in this will contest. A is incorrect. The client’s death does not trigger a release of the attorney from her duty of loyalty to the client. The second will is assumed to express the client’s intent. The loyalty that the attorney owed to the client is transferred to the client’s will, which the attorney prepared and witnessed. B is incorrect. Although it correctly states one fact, that fact is immaterial to the issue raised by the entire fact pattern. The issue is the ability of a lawyer to represent two clients with directly adverse interests at the same time. C is incorrect. First of all, it is incorrect on the law. A lawyer is not a guarantor of the validity of a will that he or she prepares. His or her job is to restate in legally sufficient form the intent of the testator and, if asked to do so, to supervise the proper execution of the will. Also, the statement is irrelevant to the issue raised by the facts.","rule":"D is the correct answer. The guiding Rule is Model Rule 1.7, which deals with conflicts of interest. Unless the son is able to prove fraud and undue influence, you must assume that the client intended to cut his son’s bequest in half. Thus, his son’s position is directly adverse to the client’s. If the client were still alive, Model Rule 1.7(a) would preclude representation of his son by the attorney because the interests of two clients would be directly adverse to each other. Now that the client is dead, the attorney is still constrained by Model Rule 1.7(a)(2), which also directs a lawyer not to represent a client if representation of that client may be materially limited by the lawyer’s responsibilities to another client or to a third person. (A deceased client is still a client for purpose of this Rule.) Note also that the attorney will necessarily be called as witness to execution of the will and the behavior and circumstances of the deceased client. Because a lawyer may not act as advocate at a trial in which the lawyer is likely to be a necessary witness on a disputed issue (Model Rule 3.7), the attorney should not agree to represent the son in this will contest. A is incorrect. The client’s death does not trigger a release of the attorney from her duty of loyalty to the client. The second will is assumed to express the client’s intent. The loyalty that the attorney owed to the client is transferred to the client’s will, which the attorney prepared and witnessed. B is incorrect. Although it correctly states one fact, that fact is immaterial to the issue raised by the entire fact pattern. The issue is the ability of a lawyer to represent two clients with directly adverse interests at the same time. C is incorrect. First of all, it is incorrect on the law. A lawyer is not a guarantor of the validity of a will that he or she prepares. His or her job is to restate in legally sufficient form the intent of the testator and, if asked to do so, to supervise the proper execution of the will. Also, the statement is irrelevant to the issue raised by the facts.","irac":{"issue":"If the son requests that the attorney represent him in opposing probate of the second will on the grounds of fraud and undue influence, is it proper for the attorney to do so?","rule":"D is the correct answer. The guiding Rule is Model Rule 1.7, which deals with conflicts of interest. Unless the son is able to prove fraud and undue influence, you must assume that the client intended to cut his son’s bequest in half. Thus, his son’s position is directly adverse to the client’s. If the client were still alive, Model Rule 1.7(a) would preclude representation of his son by the attorney because the interests of two clients would be directly adverse to each other. Now that the client is dead, the attorney is still constrained by Model Rule 1.7(a)(2), which also directs a lawyer not to represent a client if representation of that client may be materially limited by the lawyer’s responsibilities to another client or to a third person. (A deceased client is still a client for purpose of this Rule.) Note also that the attorney will necessarily be called as witness to execution of the will and the behavior and circumstances of the deceased client. Because a lawyer may not act as advocate at a trial in which the lawyer is likely to be a necessary witness on a disputed issue (Model Rule 3.7), the attorney should not agree to represent the son in this will contest. A is incorrect. The client’s death does not trigger a release of the attorney from her duty of loyalty to the client. The second will is assumed to express the client’s intent. The loyalty that the attorney owed to the client is transferred to the client’s will, which the attorney prepared and witnessed. B is incorrect. Although it correctly states one fact, that fact is immaterial to the issue raised by the entire fact pattern. The issue is the ability of a lawyer to represent two clients with directly adverse interests at the same time. C is incorrect. First of all, it is incorrect on the law. A lawyer is not a guarantor of the validity of a will that he or she prepares. His or her job is to restate in legally sufficient form the intent of the testator and, if asked to do so, to supervise the proper execution of the will. Also, the statement is irrelevant to the issue raised by the facts.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. The guiding Rule is Model Rule 1.7, which deals with conflicts of interest. Unless the son is able to prove fraud and undue influence, you must assume that the client intended to cut his son’s bequest in half. Thus, his son’s position is directly adverse to the client’s. If the client were still alive, Model Rule 1.7(a) would preclude representation of his son by the attorney because the interests of two clients would be directly adverse to each other. Now that the client is dead, the attorney is still constrained by Model Rule 1.7(a)(2), which also directs a lawyer not to represent a client if representation of that client may be materially limited by the lawyer’s responsibilities to another client or to a third person. (A deceased client is still a client for purpose of this Rule.) Note also that the attorney will necessarily be called as witness to execution of the will and the behavior and circumstances of the deceased client. Because a lawyer may not act as advocate at a trial in which the lawyer is likely to be a necessary witness on a disputed issue (Model Rule 3.7), the attorney should not agree to represent the son in this will contest. A is incorrect. The client’s death does not trigger a release of the attorney from her duty of loyalty to the client. The second will is assumed to express the client’s intent. The loyalty that the attorney owed to the client is transferred to the client’s will, which the attorney prepared and witnessed. B is incorrect. Although it correctly states one fact, that fact is immaterial to the issue raised by the entire fact pattern. The issue is the ability of a lawyer to represent two clients with directly adverse interests at the same time. C is incorrect. First of all, it is incorrect on the law. A lawyer is not a guarantor of the validity of a will that he or she prepares. His or her job is to restate in legally sufficient form the intent of the testator and, if asked to do so, to supervise the proper execution of the will. Also, the statement is irrelevant to the issue raised by the facts.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.7","Model Rule 1.7(a)","Model Rule 1.7(a)(2)","Model Rule 3.7"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[59],"sourceAnswerPages":[99],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师为委托人起草了一份遗嘱，并作为见证人之一见证委托人签署遗嘱。该遗嘱把委托人的全部遗产留给其儿子。后来，应委托人要求，律师又为其起草第二份遗嘱，并再次作为见证人之一见证签署。第二份遗嘱把一半遗产留给儿子，另一半留给管家。委托人去世后，管家把第二份遗嘱提交遗嘱认证。如果儿子请求该律师代理自己，以欺诈和不当影响为由反对认证第二份遗嘱，律师接受代理是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为委托人去世后，律师可以代理其儿子。","B":"是，因为委托人的儿子在两份遗嘱中都是受益人。","C":"否，因为律师对自己起草的遗嘱的有效性负保证责任。","D":"否，因为律师将采取不利于自己起草并见证的遗嘱的立场。"},"explanationZh":"D项正确。指导本题的是关于利益冲突的《职业行为示范规则》第1.7条。除非儿子能够证明欺诈和不当影响，否则必须假定委托人有意把给儿子的遗赠减少一半。因此，儿子的立场与委托人的立场直接对立。如果委托人仍然在世，第1.7(a)条会因为两名委托人的利益直接对立而禁止该律师代理儿子。\n\n委托人现已去世，律师仍受第1.7(a)(2)条约束；该条还规定，如果代理某一委托人可能因律师对另一委托人或第三人所负责任而受到实质性限制，律师不得进行该代理。（就本规则而言，已故委托人仍被视为委托人。）还应注意，该律师必然会被传唤，就遗嘱签署过程以及已故委托人的行为和当时情况作证。律师不得在自己可能成为争议问题必要证人的庭审中同时担任辩护人或代理人（第3.7条），因此不应同意代理儿子进行遗嘱争议诉讼。\n\nA项错误。委托人死亡并不解除律师对委托人负有的忠诚义务。第二份遗嘱被推定为表达委托人的真实意愿。律师对委托人的忠诚义务延续至由其起草并见证的委托人遗嘱。\n\nB项错误。该项陈述的事实虽然正确，但与整个事实情境提出的问题无关。问题是律师能否同时代表利益直接对立的委托人。\n\nC项错误。首先，该陈述在法律上不正确。律师并不保证自己起草的遗嘱一定有效；其职责是以法律上充分的形式表达立遗嘱人的意愿，并在受托时监督遗嘱被适当签署。此外，该陈述与题中利益冲突问题无关。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-020","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":20,"topic":"Model Rule 3.5(b)","question":"A judge was trying a case without a jury. After both parties had completed the presentation of evidence and arguments, the judge took the case under advisement. After the case was under advisement for several weeks, the attorney heard rumors that the judge was having difficulty determining the issue of factual causation and was uncertain about the applicable law. Immediately after hearing these rumors, the attorney telephoned the judge, told the judge of the rumors the attorney had heard, and asked if the judge would like to reopen the case for additional evidence and briefing from both parties. Thereafter, the judge reopened the case for further testimony and requested supplementary briefs from both parties. Was it proper for the attorney to communicate with the judge?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because both parties were given full opportunity to present their views on the issues in the case.","B":"Yes, because the attorney did not make any suggestion as to how the judge should decide the matter.","C":"No, because the attorney communicated with the judge on a pending matter without advising opposing counsel.","D":"No, because the attorney caused the judge to reopen a case that had been taken under advisement."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. The essence of our judicial system is the open and public development of facts and issues before jury and judge. Consequently, neither lawyer may communicate with the judge or any juror who is not simultaneously or equally available to the other lawyer. The intent is to prevent undue influence by either side. Further, Model Rule 3.5(b) says specifically, “A lawyer shall not . . . communicate ex parte with such a person [judge, juror, prospective juror or other official] during the proceeding unless authorized to do so by law or court order.” Here, however noble his intent, the attorney called the judge without advising his adversary that he would do. This was a direct violation of the Rules. A is incorrect. Ex parte contact with a judge is not permitted even if it ultimately leads to an argument by both sides. Obviously, an ex parte conversation between a lawyer and the judge is not monitored for content. Although it may lead to open argument, it’s always possible that some nuance or some ingredient in the original conversation has already worked its influence on the judge. B is incorrect for the same basic reason as A. Ex parte contacts with judge or jury are prohibited, period. D is incorrect. Whether or not the ex parte contact leads to a better result, it is prohibited. If it were not, one could never be certain that the record of a trial correctly reflected the testimony or the arguments that influenced the outcome.","rule":"C is the correct answer. The essence of our judicial system is the open and public development of facts and issues before jury and judge. Consequently, neither lawyer may communicate with the judge or any juror who is not simultaneously or equally available to the other lawyer. The intent is to prevent undue influence by either side. Further, Model Rule 3.5(b) says specifically, “A lawyer shall not . . . communicate ex parte with such a person [judge, juror, prospective juror or other official] during the proceeding unless authorized to do so by law or court order.” Here, however noble his intent, the attorney called the judge without advising his adversary that he would do. This was a direct violation of the Rules. A is incorrect. Ex parte contact with a judge is not permitted even if it ultimately leads to an argument by both sides. Obviously, an ex parte conversation between a lawyer and the judge is not monitored for content. Although it may lead to open argument, it’s always possible that some nuance or some ingredient in the original conversation has already worked its influence on the judge. B is incorrect for the same basic reason as A. Ex parte contacts with judge or jury are prohibited, period. D is incorrect. Whether or not the ex parte contact leads to a better result, it is prohibited. If it were not, one could never be certain that the record of a trial correctly reflected the testimony or the arguments that influenced the outcome.","irac":{"issue":"Was it proper for the attorney to communicate with the judge?","rule":"C is the correct answer. The essence of our judicial system is the open and public development of facts and issues before jury and judge. Consequently, neither lawyer may communicate with the judge or any juror who is not simultaneously or equally available to the other lawyer. The intent is to prevent undue influence by either side. Further, Model Rule 3.5(b) says specifically, “A lawyer shall not . . . communicate ex parte with such a person [judge, juror, prospective juror or other official] during the proceeding unless authorized to do so by law or court order.” Here, however noble his intent, the attorney called the judge without advising his adversary that he would do. This was a direct violation of the Rules. A is incorrect. Ex parte contact with a judge is not permitted even if it ultimately leads to an argument by both sides. Obviously, an ex parte conversation between a lawyer and the judge is not monitored for content. Although it may lead to open argument, it’s always possible that some nuance or some ingredient in the original conversation has already worked its influence on the judge. B is incorrect for the same basic reason as A. Ex parte contacts with judge or jury are prohibited, period. D is incorrect. Whether or not the ex parte contact leads to a better result, it is prohibited. If it were not, one could never be certain that the record of a trial correctly reflected the testimony or the arguments that influenced the outcome.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. The essence of our judicial system is the open and public development of facts and issues before jury and judge. Consequently, neither lawyer may communicate with the judge or any juror who is not simultaneously or equally available to the other lawyer. The intent is to prevent undue influence by either side. Further, Model Rule 3.5(b) says specifically, “A lawyer shall not . . . communicate ex parte with such a person [judge, juror, prospective juror or other official] during the proceeding unless authorized to do so by law or court order.” Here, however noble his intent, the attorney called the judge without advising his adversary that he would do. This was a direct violation of the Rules. A is incorrect. Ex parte contact with a judge is not permitted even if it ultimately leads to an argument by both sides. Obviously, an ex parte conversation between a lawyer and the judge is not monitored for content. Although it may lead to open argument, it’s always possible that some nuance or some ingredient in the original conversation has already worked its influence on the judge. B is incorrect for the same basic reason as A. Ex parte contacts with judge or jury are prohibited, period. D is incorrect. Whether or not the ex parte contact leads to a better result, it is prohibited. If it were not, one could never be certain that the record of a trial correctly reflected the testimony or the arguments that influenced the outcome.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.5(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[59,60],"sourceAnswerPages":[100],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名法官正在审理一宗无陪审团案件。双方当事人提交完证据并完成辩论后，法官把案件留待裁决。案件待决数周后，一名律师听到传闻，称法官在判断事实因果关系方面遇到困难，并且不能确定应适用的法律。律师听到传闻后立即打电话给法官，把所听传闻告知法官，并询问法官是否愿意重新开庭，让双方补充证据和书面意见。此后，法官重新开庭听取进一步证言，并要求双方提交补充书面意见。该律师与法官进行上述沟通是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为双方都有充分机会就案件争点提出自己的观点。","B":"是，因为律师没有就法官应当如何裁决提出任何建议。","C":"否，因为律师在没有通知对方律师的情况下，就待决事项与法官进行了沟通。","D":"否，因为律师促使法官重新审理已经留待裁决的案件。"},"explanationZh":"C项正确。司法制度的核心，是在陪审团和法官面前公开形成并审理事实和争点。因此，任何一方律师都不得在另一方律师无法同时或同等参与的情况下，与法官或陪审员沟通；其目的是防止任何一方施加不当影响。《职业行为示范规则》第3.5(b)条还明确规定：“除非法律或法院命令授权，律师在程序进行期间不得与此类人员［法官、陪审员、潜在陪审员或其他官员］进行单方沟通。”\n\n本题中，无论律师的动机多么善意，他都在没有通知对方律师的情况下给法官打电话，直接违反了规则。\n\nA项错误。即使单方接触最终促成双方都参加的辩论，也不得与法官进行单方沟通。显然，律师与法官之间的单方谈话无法由对方监督其内容。即使随后进行了公开辩论，最初谈话中的某种语气或信息仍可能已经影响法官。\n\nB项错误，理由与A项相同。与法官或陪审团进行单方接触受到绝对禁止。\n\nD项错误。无论单方接触是否产生更好的结果，该行为都受到禁止。否则，人们将无法确定庭审记录是否真实反映了影响裁判结果的证言或论点。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-021","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":21,"topic":"Model Rule 4.1","question":"An attorney represented a seller in negotiating the sale of his ice cream parlor. The seller told the attorney in confidence that, although the business had once been very profitable, recent profits had been stable but modest. As the negotiations proceeded, the buyer appeared to be losing interest in the deal. Hoping to restore the buyer’s interest, the attorney stated, “The ice cream business is every American’s dream: happy kids, steady profits, and a clear conscience.” The buyer bought the ice cream parlor but was disappointed when his own profits proved to be modest. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney made a false statement of fact to the buyer.","B":"Yes, because the attorney exaggerated the profitability of the business.","C":"No, because the attorney represented the seller, not the buyer.","D":"No, because the attorney’s statement constitutes acceptable puffing in negotiations."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from knowingly making a false statement of material fact or law to a third person in the course of representing a client. Model Rule 4.1. Omissions that rise to the level of an affirmative false statement are also improper. Importantly, the Rule applies only to statements of material fact. Under generally accepted standards of negotiation, certain types of statements ordinarily are not taken as statements of material fact. Model Rule 4.1, Comment [2]. Estimates of price, value, or settlement maximums are ordinarily placed in this category, and are considered mere “puffing” on the part of the attorney. Here, the attorney’s comments as to “steady profits” amounted to mere “puffing.” A is incorrect. Here, although the lawyer may have exaggerated the value of his client’s business, this exaggeration would not be considered a statement of material fact for the reasons stated above. B is incorrect. This answer choice is basically the same choice as A, except choice A is stating a suggested legal reason for discipline, while choice B is stating the factual way the attorney might be violating that legal reason (that is, the attorney is making a false statement of fact by exaggerating the profitability of the business). Because only one answer choice can be correct, recognizing the similarity between the two choices cancels out both answer choices. C is incorrect. Under Model Rule 4.1, it is irrelevant which party the lawyer represents.","rule":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from knowingly making a false statement of material fact or law to a third person in the course of representing a client. Model Rule 4.1. Omissions that rise to the level of an affirmative false statement are also improper. Importantly, the Rule applies only to statements of material fact. Under generally accepted standards of negotiation, certain types of statements ordinarily are not taken as statements of material fact. Model Rule 4.1, Comment [2]. Estimates of price, value, or settlement maximums are ordinarily placed in this category, and are considered mere “puffing” on the part of the attorney. Here, the attorney’s comments as to “steady profits” amounted to mere “puffing.” A is incorrect. Here, although the lawyer may have exaggerated the value of his client’s business, this exaggeration would not be considered a statement of material fact for the reasons stated above. B is incorrect. This answer choice is basically the same choice as A, except choice A is stating a suggested legal reason for discipline, while choice B is stating the factual way the attorney might be violating that legal reason (that is, the attorney is making a false statement of fact by exaggerating the profitability of the business). Because only one answer choice can be correct, recognizing the similarity between the two choices cancels out both answer choices. C is incorrect. Under Model Rule 4.1, it is irrelevant which party the lawyer represents.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from knowingly making a false statement of material fact or law to a third person in the course of representing a client. Model Rule 4.1. Omissions that rise to the level of an affirmative false statement are also improper. Importantly, the Rule applies only to statements of material fact. Under generally accepted standards of negotiation, certain types of statements ordinarily are not taken as statements of material fact. Model Rule 4.1, Comment [2]. Estimates of price, value, or settlement maximums are ordinarily placed in this category, and are considered mere “puffing” on the part of the attorney. Here, the attorney’s comments as to “steady profits” amounted to mere “puffing.” A is incorrect. Here, although the lawyer may have exaggerated the value of his client’s business, this exaggeration would not be considered a statement of material fact for the reasons stated above. B is incorrect. This answer choice is basically the same choice as A, except choice A is stating a suggested legal reason for discipline, while choice B is stating the factual way the attorney might be violating that legal reason (that is, the attorney is making a false statement of fact by exaggerating the profitability of the business). Because only one answer choice can be correct, recognizing the similarity between the two choices cancels out both answer choices. C is incorrect. Under Model Rule 4.1, it is irrelevant which party the lawyer represents.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from knowingly making a false statement of material fact or law to a third person in the course of representing a client. Model Rule 4.1. Omissions that rise to the level of an affirmative false statement are also improper. Importantly, the Rule applies only to statements of material fact. Under generally accepted standards of negotiation, certain types of statements ordinarily are not taken as statements of material fact. Model Rule 4.1, Comment [2]. Estimates of price, value, or settlement maximums are ordinarily placed in this category, and are considered mere “puffing” on the part of the attorney. Here, the attorney’s comments as to “steady profits” amounted to mere “puffing.” A is incorrect. Here, although the lawyer may have exaggerated the value of his client’s business, this exaggeration would not be considered a statement of material fact for the reasons stated above. B is incorrect. This answer choice is basically the same choice as A, except choice A is stating a suggested legal reason for discipline, while choice B is stating the factual way the attorney might be violating that legal reason (that is, the attorney is making a false statement of fact by exaggerating the profitability of the business). Because only one answer choice can be correct, recognizing the similarity between the two choices cancels out both answer choices. C is incorrect. Under Model Rule 4.1, it is irrelevant which party the lawyer represents.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 4.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[60],"sourceAnswerPages":[100,101],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理卖方谈判出售其冰淇淋店。卖方秘密告诉律师，虽然该店过去利润很高，但近期利润一直稳定却不多。随着谈判推进，买方似乎逐渐失去兴趣。为了重新引起买方兴趣，律师说：“冰淇淋生意是每个美国人的梦想：孩子们开心、利润稳定、问心无愧。”买方随后购买了冰淇淋店，但发现自己获得的利润也很少，因而感到失望。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师向买方作出了虚假的事实陈述。","B":"是，因为律师夸大了该企业的盈利能力。","C":"否，因为律师代理的是卖方而不是买方。","D":"否，因为律师的陈述属于谈判中可以接受的夸张宣传。"},"explanationZh":"D项正确。律师在代理委托人过程中，不得明知而向第三人作出关于重大事实或法律的虚假陈述。《职业行为示范规则》第4.1条。达到积极虚假陈述程度的隐瞒同样不当。重要的是，该规则只适用于关于重大事实的陈述。按照一般公认的谈判惯例，某些种类的陈述通常不被视为重大事实陈述。《职业行为示范规则》第4.1条评注[2]。对价格、价值或最高和解金额的估计通常属于这一类别，并被视为律师的单纯“夸张宣传”。本题中，律师关于“利润稳定”的说法属于这种夸张宣传。\n\nA项错误。虽然律师可能夸大了委托人企业的价值，但基于上述理由，这种夸大不被视为重大事实陈述。\n\nB项错误。该项与A项基本相同，只是A项提出可能受处分的法律理由，而B项描述律师可能违反该法律理由的事实方式，即通过夸大盈利能力作出虚假事实陈述。由于只能有一个正确选项，两个选项的高度相似反而说明二者都不正确。\n\nC项错误。根据第4.1条，律师代理哪一方与该规则是否适用无关。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-022","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":22,"topic":"Model Rule 7.2 / Model Rule 7.1 / Model Rule 7.3","question":"An attorney has a radio commercial that states: “Do you have a legal problem? Are you being sued? Call me, a licensed attorney at law. Initial conference charge is $25 for one hour. Act now and protect your interests. Call at 1234 Main Street; telephone area code (101) 123-4567.” All of the statements were true, and the broadcast could not be heard outside of the state in which the attorney was licensed. Is the attorney subject to discipline for the commercial?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney’s qualifications are not stated.","B":"Yes, because the radio broadcast may encourage litigation.","C":"No, because all the statements in the radio broadcast are true.","D":"No, because the radio broadcast is not heard outside the state in which the attorney is licensed."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. It is clear that a lawyer may advertise his or her services. Under Model Rule 7.2, a lawyer may advertise by any public medium or through written or recorded communication. The right is subject, however, to Model Rules 7.1 and 7.3. Model Rule 7.1 imposes the requirement that the advertisement not be false or misleading. Generally, so long as the statements in the advertisement are truthful, the ad passes muster. Model Rule 7.3 forbids direct solicitation either in person or by telephone. Here, no part of the attorney’s ad would seem to be anything more than a statement of simple facts about the attorney. A is incorrect. The advertisement need not state the lawyer’s qualifications. On the contrary, a lawyer who ventures to state his or her qualifications risks the possibility that he or she will overstate them. Comment [2] to Model Rule 7.2 lists some of the matters a lawyer may list with impunity: name, address, telephone number, range of services, basis for fees, foreign language ability, references and, with their consent, names of clients regularly represented. B is incorrect. Nothing in this ad can reasonably be construed as encouraging litigation. D is incorrect. Many radio and television stations can be heard across state lines. As long as a lawyer licensed in one state does not state that he or she is able to practice in a state in which he or she is not licensed, it will be assumed that his or her ad is directed only at listeners and viewers in the state in which he or she is licensed.","rule":"C is the correct answer. It is clear that a lawyer may advertise his or her services. Under Model Rule 7.2, a lawyer may advertise by any public medium or through written or recorded communication. The right is subject, however, to Model Rules 7.1 and 7.3. Model Rule 7.1 imposes the requirement that the advertisement not be false or misleading. Generally, so long as the statements in the advertisement are truthful, the ad passes muster. Model Rule 7.3 forbids direct solicitation either in person or by telephone. Here, no part of the attorney’s ad would seem to be anything more than a statement of simple facts about the attorney. A is incorrect. The advertisement need not state the lawyer’s qualifications. On the contrary, a lawyer who ventures to state his or her qualifications risks the possibility that he or she will overstate them. Comment [2] to Model Rule 7.2 lists some of the matters a lawyer may list with impunity: name, address, telephone number, range of services, basis for fees, foreign language ability, references and, with their consent, names of clients regularly represented. B is incorrect. Nothing in this ad can reasonably be construed as encouraging litigation. D is incorrect. Many radio and television stations can be heard across state lines. As long as a lawyer licensed in one state does not state that he or she is able to practice in a state in which he or she is not licensed, it will be assumed that his or her ad is directed only at listeners and viewers in the state in which he or she is licensed.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for the commercial?","rule":"C is the correct answer. It is clear that a lawyer may advertise his or her services. Under Model Rule 7.2, a lawyer may advertise by any public medium or through written or recorded communication. The right is subject, however, to Model Rules 7.1 and 7.3. Model Rule 7.1 imposes the requirement that the advertisement not be false or misleading. Generally, so long as the statements in the advertisement are truthful, the ad passes muster. Model Rule 7.3 forbids direct solicitation either in person or by telephone. Here, no part of the attorney’s ad would seem to be anything more than a statement of simple facts about the attorney. A is incorrect. The advertisement need not state the lawyer’s qualifications. On the contrary, a lawyer who ventures to state his or her qualifications risks the possibility that he or she will overstate them. Comment [2] to Model Rule 7.2 lists some of the matters a lawyer may list with impunity: name, address, telephone number, range of services, basis for fees, foreign language ability, references and, with their consent, names of clients regularly represented. B is incorrect. Nothing in this ad can reasonably be construed as encouraging litigation. D is incorrect. Many radio and television stations can be heard across state lines. As long as a lawyer licensed in one state does not state that he or she is able to practice in a state in which he or she is not licensed, it will be assumed that his or her ad is directed only at listeners and viewers in the state in which he or she is licensed.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. It is clear that a lawyer may advertise his or her services. Under Model Rule 7.2, a lawyer may advertise by any public medium or through written or recorded communication. The right is subject, however, to Model Rules 7.1 and 7.3. Model Rule 7.1 imposes the requirement that the advertisement not be false or misleading. Generally, so long as the statements in the advertisement are truthful, the ad passes muster. Model Rule 7.3 forbids direct solicitation either in person or by telephone. Here, no part of the attorney’s ad would seem to be anything more than a statement of simple facts about the attorney. A is incorrect. The advertisement need not state the lawyer’s qualifications. On the contrary, a lawyer who ventures to state his or her qualifications risks the possibility that he or she will overstate them. Comment [2] to Model Rule 7.2 lists some of the matters a lawyer may list with impunity: name, address, telephone number, range of services, basis for fees, foreign language ability, references and, with their consent, names of clients regularly represented. B is incorrect. Nothing in this ad can reasonably be construed as encouraging litigation. D is incorrect. Many radio and television stations can be heard across state lines. As long as a lawyer licensed in one state does not state that he or she is able to practice in a state in which he or she is not licensed, it will be assumed that his or her ad is directed only at listeners and viewers in the state in which he or she is licensed.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.2","Model Rule 7.1","Model Rule 7.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[60,61],"sourceAnswerPages":[101],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师在广播中播放如下广告：“你是否遇到法律问题？是否正在被起诉？请致电本人，我是一名持照律师。首次咨询一小时收费25美元。立即行动，保护你的权益。地址：Main Street 1234号；电话：(101) 123-4567。”广告中的全部陈述均属实，而且广播信号在该律师获准执业的州以外无法收听。该律师是否会因这则广告受到纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为广告没有说明律师的资历。","B":"是，因为广播广告可能鼓励诉讼。","C":"否，因为广播广告中的全部陈述都是真实的。","D":"否，因为该广播在律师获准执业的州以外无法收听。"},"explanationZh":"C项正确。律师显然可以宣传自己的服务。根据《职业行为示范规则》第7.2条，律师可以通过任何公共媒介，或者书面、录音等通信方式发布广告。不过，该权利受第7.1条和第7.3条约束。第7.1条要求广告不得虚假或具有误导性；一般而言，只要广告中的陈述真实，广告就符合要求。第7.3条禁止通过当面接触或电话进行直接招揽。本题广告中的内容均只是关于律师的简单事实陈述。\n\nA项错误。广告不必说明律师的资历。相反，律师一旦选择陈述自己的资历，就可能面临夸大资历的风险。第7.2条评注[2]列出律师可以安全刊载的一些事项，包括姓名、地址、电话号码、服务范围、收费依据、外语能力、推荐资料，以及在取得同意后经常代理的委托人姓名。\n\nB项错误。这则广告中的任何内容都不能被合理理解为鼓励诉讼。\n\nD项错误。许多广播电台和电视台的信号可以跨越州界。只要在一州取得执业资格的律师没有声称自己能够在未取得资格的州执业，就会被认为其广告仅面向其获准执业州内的听众或观众。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-023","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":23,"topic":"Model Rule 7.2 / Model Rule 7.1 / Model Rule 7.3","question":"An attorney, recently admitted to practice, opened an office near a residential neighborhood and published the following advertisement in the local newspaper. COUPON Get Acquainted With Your Neighborhood Lawyer A. Attorney Suite 2—1100 Magnolia Avenue Sunshine City, State 01000 Telephone: (555) 555-5555 In order to acquaint you with our services, we are offering a one-hour consultation to review your estate plans, including your wills, trusts, and similar documents, all at the nominal cost of $25 to anyone presenting this coupon. Call now for an appointment. Everything in the advertisement was true. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney is soliciting business from persons with whom the attorney had no prior relationship.","B":"Yes, because the attorney requires the use of a coupon.","C":"No, because the attorney provides the services described for the fee stated.","D":"No, because the attorney is not seeking business from persons who are already represented by a lawyer."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2, lawyers are permitted to advertise, subject to Model Rule 7.1, which prohibits a lawyer from making false or misleading statements about his or her services, and Model Rule 7.3, which limits direct contact with prospective clients. So long as there are no false or misleading statements in the attorney’s advertisement, he would not be subject to discipline for placing this ad. A is incorrect. Option A refers to Model Rule 7.3(a), which prohibits a lawyer from making an in-person or live telephone or real-time electronic contact with a prospective client unless the lawyer has had a prior relationship with the prospective client. This question deals with written advertising. B is incorrect. There is no reason the attorney shouldn’t use a coupon in his advertisement. D is incorrect. The issue here is whether the advertisement meets the requirements of the Rules. The attorney is placing a general written advertisement and has no way of knowing whether a person is currently represented by another lawyer.","rule":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2, lawyers are permitted to advertise, subject to Model Rule 7.1, which prohibits a lawyer from making false or misleading statements about his or her services, and Model Rule 7.3, which limits direct contact with prospective clients. So long as there are no false or misleading statements in the attorney’s advertisement, he would not be subject to discipline for placing this ad. A is incorrect. Option A refers to Model Rule 7.3(a), which prohibits a lawyer from making an in-person or live telephone or real-time electronic contact with a prospective client unless the lawyer has had a prior relationship with the prospective client. This question deals with written advertising. B is incorrect. There is no reason the attorney shouldn’t use a coupon in his advertisement. D is incorrect. The issue here is whether the advertisement meets the requirements of the Rules. The attorney is placing a general written advertisement and has no way of knowing whether a person is currently represented by another lawyer.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2, lawyers are permitted to advertise, subject to Model Rule 7.1, which prohibits a lawyer from making false or misleading statements about his or her services, and Model Rule 7.3, which limits direct contact with prospective clients. So long as there are no false or misleading statements in the attorney’s advertisement, he would not be subject to discipline for placing this ad. A is incorrect. Option A refers to Model Rule 7.3(a), which prohibits a lawyer from making an in-person or live telephone or real-time electronic contact with a prospective client unless the lawyer has had a prior relationship with the prospective client. This question deals with written advertising. B is incorrect. There is no reason the attorney shouldn’t use a coupon in his advertisement. D is incorrect. The issue here is whether the advertisement meets the requirements of the Rules. The attorney is placing a general written advertisement and has no way of knowing whether a person is currently represented by another lawyer.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2, lawyers are permitted to advertise, subject to Model Rule 7.1, which prohibits a lawyer from making false or misleading statements about his or her services, and Model Rule 7.3, which limits direct contact with prospective clients. So long as there are no false or misleading statements in the attorney’s advertisement, he would not be subject to discipline for placing this ad. A is incorrect. Option A refers to Model Rule 7.3(a), which prohibits a lawyer from making an in-person or live telephone or real-time electronic contact with a prospective client unless the lawyer has had a prior relationship with the prospective client. This question deals with written advertising. B is incorrect. There is no reason the attorney shouldn’t use a coupon in his advertisement. D is incorrect. The issue here is whether the advertisement meets the requirements of the Rules. The attorney is placing a general written advertisement and has no way of knowing whether a person is currently represented by another lawyer.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.2","Model Rule 7.1","Model Rule 7.3","Model Rule 7.3(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[61,62],"sourceAnswerPages":[102],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名刚取得执业资格的律师在住宅区附近开设办公室，并在当地报纸刊登以下广告：\n\n“优惠券——认识你的社区律师  \nA. Attorney 律师  \nMagnolia Avenue 1100号2室  \nSunshine City，State 01000  \n电话：(555) 555-5555  \n为了让你了解我们的服务，任何出示本优惠券的人均可仅支付25美元，获得一小时咨询，由我们审查你的遗产规划，包括遗嘱、信托及类似文件。请立即致电预约。”\n\n广告中的全部内容均属实。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师在向与自己没有既有关系的人招揽业务。","B":"是，因为律师要求使用优惠券。","C":"否，因为律师会按广告所述费用提供广告所述服务。","D":"否，因为律师并非向已经有律师代理的人寻求业务。"},"explanationZh":"C项正确。根据《职业行为示范规则》第7.2条，律师可以刊登广告，但须遵守第7.1条关于不得对服务作出虚假或误导性陈述的规定，以及第7.3条对直接接触潜在委托人的限制。只要广告中没有虚假或误导性陈述，律师就不会因刊登该广告受到纪律处分。\n\nA项错误。该项涉及第7.3(a)条：除非律师与潜在委托人已有关系，否则不得通过当面、实时电话或实时电子方式与其接触。本题涉及的是书面广告。\n\nB项错误。没有任何规则禁止律师在广告中使用优惠券。\n\nD项错误。争点是广告是否符合规则要求。律师发布的是面向一般公众的书面广告，无从知道看到广告的人目前是否已有其他律师代理。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-024","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":24,"topic":"Model Rule 8.3 / Model Rule 1.6","question":"An attorney was retained by a client to incorporate client’s business, which previously had been operated as a sole proprietorship. The attorney noticed in the client’s file copies of some correspondence from the client to another attorney concerning the possibility of that attorney incorporating the client’s business. The attorney questioned the client to make certain that any attorney-client relationship between the other attorney and the client had been terminated. The client told the attorney, “It certainly has been terminated. When I discussed the matter with that guy six months ago, he asked for a retainer of $1,000, which I paid him. He did absolutely nothing after he got the money, even though I called him weekly, and finally, last week when I again complained, he returned the retainer. But don’t say anything about it because he is an old friend of my family, and I was just happy I got the money back.” The attorney believed the other attorney was guilty of professional misconduct, although the attorney did not think the other attorney usually acted in this manner. The client just wanted to drop the matter. Is the attorney subject to discipline if she does not report her knowledge of the other attorney’s conduct to the appropriate authority?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney believes the other attorney was clearly guilty of professional misconduct.","B":"Yes, because the attorney did not believe the other attorney usually neglected matters entrusted to him.","C":"No, because the client was satisfied by the other attorney’s return of the retainer.","D":"No, because the client has not agreed that the attorney may report the information."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Ordinarily, a lawyer is duty bound to report another lawyer’s violation of the Model Rules of Professional Conduct if the violation raises a substantial question as to that lawyer’s honesty, trustworthiness, or fitness as a lawyer. Model Rule 8.3. However, when the information about the violation is protected under the client-confidentiality rules of Model Rule 1.6, this duty becomes discretionary. Even so Comment [2] to Model Rule 8.3 advises: “However, a lawyer should encourage a client to consent to disclosure where prosecution would not substantially prejudice the client’s interests.” On these facts, which show that the client would be reluctant to report the other attorney because he is an old friend, the attorney would probably be wasting her breath to encourage the disclosure of the other attorney’s neglect of the client’s interests. Ultimately, because the information about the violation is protected under client-confidentiality rules (because the client told the attorney about the violation during the course of her representation of him), the attorney will not be subject to discipline if she chooses not to report the other attorney without the client’s consent. A is incorrect. Whether or not the attorney believes the other attorney was guilty of professional misconduct, she will not be subject to discipline if she chooses not to report the other attorney without the client’s consent. B is incorrect. If there is no Model Rule 1.6 confidentiality issue, the attorney’s belief as to the other attorney’s usual conduct would be immaterial. The duty to report another lawyer’s misconduct of which a lawyer has knowledge is absolute, not discretionary. Once an act of misconduct is known, it becomes a matter for the “appropriate professional authority,” not for the lawyer. Only when the client tells the lawyer in confidence and does not consent to disclosure can the lawyer choose not to report another attorney. C is incorrect. The trigger that converts the attorney’s duty from an absolute duty to a discretionary option is the lack of the client’s consent, not the level of the client’s satisfaction with the other attorney or the fact that the client was not out of money.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Ordinarily, a lawyer is duty bound to report another lawyer’s violation of the Model Rules of Professional Conduct if the violation raises a substantial question as to that lawyer’s honesty, trustworthiness, or fitness as a lawyer. Model Rule 8.3. However, when the information about the violation is protected under the client-confidentiality rules of Model Rule 1.6, this duty becomes discretionary. Even so Comment [2] to Model Rule 8.3 advises: “However, a lawyer should encourage a client to consent to disclosure where prosecution would not substantially prejudice the client’s interests.” On these facts, which show that the client would be reluctant to report the other attorney because he is an old friend, the attorney would probably be wasting her breath to encourage the disclosure of the other attorney’s neglect of the client’s interests. Ultimately, because the information about the violation is protected under client-confidentiality rules (because the client told the attorney about the violation during the course of her representation of him), the attorney will not be subject to discipline if she chooses not to report the other attorney without the client’s consent. A is incorrect. Whether or not the attorney believes the other attorney was guilty of professional misconduct, she will not be subject to discipline if she chooses not to report the other attorney without the client’s consent. B is incorrect. If there is no Model Rule 1.6 confidentiality issue, the attorney’s belief as to the other attorney’s usual conduct would be immaterial. The duty to report another lawyer’s misconduct of which a lawyer has knowledge is absolute, not discretionary. Once an act of misconduct is known, it becomes a matter for the “appropriate professional authority,” not for the lawyer. Only when the client tells the lawyer in confidence and does not consent to disclosure can the lawyer choose not to report another attorney. C is incorrect. The trigger that converts the attorney’s duty from an absolute duty to a discretionary option is the lack of the client’s consent, not the level of the client’s satisfaction with the other attorney or the fact that the client was not out of money.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline if she does not report her knowledge of the other attorney’s conduct to the appropriate authority?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Ordinarily, a lawyer is duty bound to report another lawyer’s violation of the Model Rules of Professional Conduct if the violation raises a substantial question as to that lawyer’s honesty, trustworthiness, or fitness as a lawyer. Model Rule 8.3. However, when the information about the violation is protected under the client-confidentiality rules of Model Rule 1.6, this duty becomes discretionary. Even so Comment [2] to Model Rule 8.3 advises: “However, a lawyer should encourage a client to consent to disclosure where prosecution would not substantially prejudice the client’s interests.” On these facts, which show that the client would be reluctant to report the other attorney because he is an old friend, the attorney would probably be wasting her breath to encourage the disclosure of the other attorney’s neglect of the client’s interests. Ultimately, because the information about the violation is protected under client-confidentiality rules (because the client told the attorney about the violation during the course of her representation of him), the attorney will not be subject to discipline if she chooses not to report the other attorney without the client’s consent. A is incorrect. Whether or not the attorney believes the other attorney was guilty of professional misconduct, she will not be subject to discipline if she chooses not to report the other attorney without the client’s consent. B is incorrect. If there is no Model Rule 1.6 confidentiality issue, the attorney’s belief as to the other attorney’s usual conduct would be immaterial. The duty to report another lawyer’s misconduct of which a lawyer has knowledge is absolute, not discretionary. Once an act of misconduct is known, it becomes a matter for the “appropriate professional authority,” not for the lawyer. Only when the client tells the lawyer in confidence and does not consent to disclosure can the lawyer choose not to report another attorney. C is incorrect. The trigger that converts the attorney’s duty from an absolute duty to a discretionary option is the lack of the client’s consent, not the level of the client’s satisfaction with the other attorney or the fact that the client was not out of money.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Ordinarily, a lawyer is duty bound to report another lawyer’s violation of the Model Rules of Professional Conduct if the violation raises a substantial question as to that lawyer’s honesty, trustworthiness, or fitness as a lawyer. Model Rule 8.3. However, when the information about the violation is protected under the client-confidentiality rules of Model Rule 1.6, this duty becomes discretionary. Even so Comment [2] to Model Rule 8.3 advises: “However, a lawyer should encourage a client to consent to disclosure where prosecution would not substantially prejudice the client’s interests.” On these facts, which show that the client would be reluctant to report the other attorney because he is an old friend, the attorney would probably be wasting her breath to encourage the disclosure of the other attorney’s neglect of the client’s interests. Ultimately, because the information about the violation is protected under client-confidentiality rules (because the client told the attorney about the violation during the course of her representation of him), the attorney will not be subject to discipline if she chooses not to report the other attorney without the client’s consent. A is incorrect. Whether or not the attorney believes the other attorney was guilty of professional misconduct, she will not be subject to discipline if she chooses not to report the other attorney without the client’s consent. B is incorrect. If there is no Model Rule 1.6 confidentiality issue, the attorney’s belief as to the other attorney’s usual conduct would be immaterial. The duty to report another lawyer’s misconduct of which a lawyer has knowledge is absolute, not discretionary. Once an act of misconduct is known, it becomes a matter for the “appropriate professional authority,” not for the lawyer. Only when the client tells the lawyer in confidence and does not consent to disclosure can the lawyer choose not to report another attorney. C is incorrect. The trigger that converts the attorney’s duty from an absolute duty to a discretionary option is the lack of the client’s consent, not the level of the client’s satisfaction with the other attorney or the fact that the client was not out of money.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 8.3","Model Rule 1.6"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[62],"sourceAnswerPages":[102,103],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名委托人过去以个人独资形式经营企业，现聘请一名律师为其办理公司化手续。律师在委托人的档案中看到若干通信副本，是委托人与另一名律师就由后者办理公司化的可能性所进行的往来。律师询问委托人，以确认其与另一名律师之间的律师—委托人关系已经终止。委托人说：\n\n“当然已经终止了。六个月前我和那个人谈这件事时，他要求我支付1,000美元预付律师费，我也付了。他拿到钱后什么都没做，尽管我每周都给他打电话。最后，上周我再次投诉时，他退还了预付款。不过不要对此说什么，因为他是我家的老朋友，我拿回钱就已经很满意了。”\n\n该律师认为另一名律师明显实施了职业不当行为，但不认为对方通常都会这样行事。委托人只想让此事就此结束。如果律师不向适当主管机关报告自己所知的另一名律师行为，她是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师认为另一名律师明显实施了职业不当行为。","B":"是，因为律师不认为另一名律师通常都会疏忽受托事项。","C":"否，因为另一名律师退还预付款后，委托人已经满意。","D":"否，因为委托人没有同意律师报告该信息。"},"explanationZh":"D项正确。通常，如果另一名律师违反《职业行为示范规则》的行为对其诚实性、可信赖性或作为律师的适格性提出重大疑问，律师有义务报告该违规行为。《职业行为示范规则》第8.3条。但是，如果有关违规的信息受到第1.6条委托人保密规则的保护，这项报告义务就变为可以酌情履行。即便如此，第8.3条评注[2]仍建议：“如果追究该行为不会对委托人利益造成实质性损害，律师应鼓励委托人同意披露。”\n\n根据本题事实，委托人因为另一名律师是家庭老朋友而不愿报告；律师即使鼓励其披露对方疏忽委托人利益的行为，可能也不会奏效。最终，由于委托人在现律师代理过程中告知有关违规行为，该信息受委托人保密规则保护；在未取得委托人同意的情况下，律师选择不报告另一名律师，不会受到纪律处分。\n\nA项错误。无论律师是否认为另一名律师构成职业不当行为，在没有委托人同意的情况下，她选择不报告都不会因此受处分。\n\nB项错误。如果不存在第1.6条的保密问题，律师对另一名律师通常行为的看法并不重要。律师一旦知道另一名律师实施了不当行为，报告义务是绝对的而非酌情的；此后应由“适当的职业主管机关”处理，而不是由知情律师自行决定。只有在委托人秘密告知且不同意披露时，律师才可选择不报告。\n\nC项错误。使绝对报告义务变成酌情选择的因素，是委托人没有同意，而不是委托人对另一名律师是否满意，也不是委托人是否最终没有金钱损失。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-025","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":25,"topic":"Model Rule 1.15(d) / Model Rule 2.1 / Rule 1.15(d)","question":"An attorney represented a client as a plaintiff in a personal injury matter under a standard contingent fee contract. The client agreed to settle the case for $1,000,000, from which funds the attorney would receive $250,000. The client informed the attorney that she planned to take $25,000 of the settlement funds and spend the money purchasing lottery tickets. The attorney told the client that he disagreed with this plan and encouraged the client to take some classes on investing money. The client agreed to take the classes, but still insisted on playing the lottery. The attorney received the check for $1,000,000 three days before the client was to attend the investing classes. The attorney held the check for one week, giving the client at least a few days of classes. The attorney then informed the client of the receipt of the funds, disbursed the funds according to the agreement, and also furnished the client with an accounting. The attorney told the client that he had delayed notice to allow time for the client to come to her senses. The client laughed and said, “I guess your plan worked, because these classes have convinced me to invest my money in the stock market instead of playing the lottery.” Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney had a duty to promptly notify the client of the receipt of the $1,000,000.","B":"Yes, because the attorney gave unsolicited advice about nonlegal matters.","C":"No, because the client did not object to the withholding of the notice and funds.","D":"No, because the attorney acted in the client’s best interest."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer must promptly notify the client on receiving funds, securities, or other properties in which the client has an interest. Model Rule 1.15(d). In addition, except as otherwise permitted by law or by agreement with the client, a lawyer shall promptly deliver to the client any funds or property the client is entitled to receive and, on request by the client, promptly render a full accounting regarding such property. Here, the attorney deliberately delayed notifying the client for a week so the client could take investing classes. The fact that the attorney was arguably doing so in the client’s best interest is irrelevant. B is incorrect. The lawyer was permitted to give this unsolicited advice. Under Model Rule 2.1, a lawyer who is advising a client “shall exercise independent professional judgment and render candid advice.” In doing so, the lawyer may “refer not only to law but to other considerations such as moral, economic, social, and political factors that may be relevant to the client’s situation.” C is incorrect. The lawyer’s failure to promptly notify the client of the lawyer’s receipt of the client funds is not excused by the client’s later failure to object. Many times, the MPRE will try to conceal a wrong answer by having the client later consent to the attorney’s violation of the Rules. D is incorrect. Under Rule 1.15(d), as stated above, there is no exception when the lawyer thinks it would be in the client’s best interest to delay notification.","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer must promptly notify the client on receiving funds, securities, or other properties in which the client has an interest. Model Rule 1.15(d). In addition, except as otherwise permitted by law or by agreement with the client, a lawyer shall promptly deliver to the client any funds or property the client is entitled to receive and, on request by the client, promptly render a full accounting regarding such property. Here, the attorney deliberately delayed notifying the client for a week so the client could take investing classes. The fact that the attorney was arguably doing so in the client’s best interest is irrelevant. B is incorrect. The lawyer was permitted to give this unsolicited advice. Under Model Rule 2.1, a lawyer who is advising a client “shall exercise independent professional judgment and render candid advice.” In doing so, the lawyer may “refer not only to law but to other considerations such as moral, economic, social, and political factors that may be relevant to the client’s situation.” C is incorrect. The lawyer’s failure to promptly notify the client of the lawyer’s receipt of the client funds is not excused by the client’s later failure to object. Many times, the MPRE will try to conceal a wrong answer by having the client later consent to the attorney’s violation of the Rules. D is incorrect. Under Rule 1.15(d), as stated above, there is no exception when the lawyer thinks it would be in the client’s best interest to delay notification.","irac":{"issue":"” Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer must promptly notify the client on receiving funds, securities, or other properties in which the client has an interest. Model Rule 1.15(d). In addition, except as otherwise permitted by law or by agreement with the client, a lawyer shall promptly deliver to the client any funds or property the client is entitled to receive and, on request by the client, promptly render a full accounting regarding such property. Here, the attorney deliberately delayed notifying the client for a week so the client could take investing classes. The fact that the attorney was arguably doing so in the client’s best interest is irrelevant. B is incorrect. The lawyer was permitted to give this unsolicited advice. Under Model Rule 2.1, a lawyer who is advising a client “shall exercise independent professional judgment and render candid advice.” In doing so, the lawyer may “refer not only to law but to other considerations such as moral, economic, social, and political factors that may be relevant to the client’s situation.” C is incorrect. The lawyer’s failure to promptly notify the client of the lawyer’s receipt of the client funds is not excused by the client’s later failure to object. Many times, the MPRE will try to conceal a wrong answer by having the client later consent to the attorney’s violation of the Rules. D is incorrect. Under Rule 1.15(d), as stated above, there is no exception when the lawyer thinks it would be in the client’s best interest to delay notification.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer must promptly notify the client on receiving funds, securities, or other properties in which the client has an interest. Model Rule 1.15(d). In addition, except as otherwise permitted by law or by agreement with the client, a lawyer shall promptly deliver to the client any funds or property the client is entitled to receive and, on request by the client, promptly render a full accounting regarding such property. Here, the attorney deliberately delayed notifying the client for a week so the client could take investing classes. The fact that the attorney was arguably doing so in the client’s best interest is irrelevant. B is incorrect. The lawyer was permitted to give this unsolicited advice. Under Model Rule 2.1, a lawyer who is advising a client “shall exercise independent professional judgment and render candid advice.” In doing so, the lawyer may “refer not only to law but to other considerations such as moral, economic, social, and political factors that may be relevant to the client’s situation.” C is incorrect. The lawyer’s failure to promptly notify the client of the lawyer’s receipt of the client funds is not excused by the client’s later failure to object. Many times, the MPRE will try to conceal a wrong answer by having the client later consent to the attorney’s violation of the Rules. D is incorrect. Under Rule 1.15(d), as stated above, there is no exception when the lawyer thinks it would be in the client’s best interest to delay notification.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.15(d)","Model Rule 2.1","Rule 1.15(d)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[62,63],"sourceAnswerPages":[103,104],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师依据标准风险代理协议，在人身伤害案件中代理原告。委托人同意以1,000,000美元和解，其中律师将取得250,000美元。委托人告诉律师，她计划从和解款中拿出25,000美元购买彩票。律师表示反对，并鼓励她参加一些投资理财课程。委托人同意上课，但仍坚持购买彩票。律师在委托人开始上投资课前三天收到1,000,000美元支票。律师把支票扣留了一周，让委托人至少先上几天课。之后，律师才通知委托人已经收到款项，按照协议分配资金，并向委托人提供账目报告。律师告诉委托人，自己延迟通知是为了给她时间恢复理智。委托人笑着说：“看来你的计划奏效了，因为这些课程说服我把钱投资股市，而不是买彩票。”该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师有义务及时通知委托人已经收到1,000,000美元。","B":"是，因为律师主动就非法律事项提出了未经请求的建议。","C":"否，因为委托人没有反对律师延迟通知和交付资金。","D":"否，因为律师是为了委托人的最佳利益而行事。"},"explanationZh":"A项正确。律师收到委托人享有权益的资金、证券或其他财产后，必须及时通知委托人。《职业行为示范规则》第1.15(d)条。此外，除非法律另有许可或律师与委托人另有约定，律师必须及时向委托人交付其有权取得的资金或财产，并在委托人提出要求时，及时就该财产提供完整账目。本题中，律师故意延迟一周通知委托人，以便委托人先参加投资课程。即使律师可以主张自己这样做是为了委托人的最佳利益，也与是否违规无关。\n\nB项错误。律师可以主动提出这种建议。根据《职业行为示范规则》第2.1条，向委托人提供建议的律师“应当运用独立专业判断并提出坦率意见”。律师这样做时，“不仅可以考虑法律，还可以考虑与委托人处境相关的道德、经济、社会及政治因素”。\n\nC项错误。律师未及时通知已经收到委托人资金，不能因委托人后来没有反对而得到补救。MPRE经常通过安排委托人事后同意，试图掩饰律师此前违反规则的事实。\n\nD项错误。如上所述，第1.15(d)条并不存在“律师认为延迟通知符合委托人最佳利益”这一例外。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-026","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":26,"topic":"Model Rule 7.1 / Model Rule 7.3(a) / Model Rule 7.3","question":"An attorney served two four-year terms as the state’s governor immediately prior to reopening his law office in the state. The attorney printed and mailed an announcement of his return to private practice to members of the bar, previous clients, and personal friends whom he had never represented. The printed announcement stated that the attorney had reopened his law office, gave his address and telephone number, and added that he had been the state’s governor for the past eight years. Is the attorney subject to discipline for the announcement?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the mailing included persons who had not been his clients.","B":"Yes, because his service as governor is unrelated to his ability as a lawyer.","C":"No, because the information in the announcement was true.","D":"No, because all of the information was already in the public domain."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. Even though this is a somewhat limited communication, it is clearly an advertisement for the attorney’s new law office, and as such is covered by Model Rule 7.1. Model Rule 7.1 forbids only false or misleading communications, and this communication was neither false nor misleading. Here, all the communication did was state the simple facts regarding the reopening of the office, its location and phone number, and the fact that the attorney had been the state’s governor for eight years. A is incorrect. A lawyer is prohibited from making in-person, live telephone, or real-time electronic contact with prospective clients to solicit employment when the lawyer’s motive for doing so is the lawyer’s financial gain. Model Rule 7.3(a). This Rule seeks to address the fact that “the person, who may already feel overwhelmed by the circumstances giving rise to the need for legal services, may find it difficult to fully evaluate all available alternatives with reasoned judgment and appropriate self-interest in the face of the lawyer’s presence and insistence upon being retained immediately.” Model Rule 7.3, Comment [2]. However, there is an exception for contacts with former clients, friends, other lawyers, or relatives, which is what this answer choice is trying to get you to think of, since this communication is only going out to those people who fall within this exception. However, as the communication here is simply a printed announcement, it does not fall within this type of banned behavior, so the exception does not apply. B is incorrect. Model Rule 7.1 forbids only false or misleading communications in lawyer advertising. However, an advertisement can be misleading if it creates an unjustified expectation as to the results the lawyer may achieve. Here, a truthful statement about the attorney’s prior service as governor is unlikely to create any unjustified expectations as to what the attorney can achieve and is therefore not misleading. D is incorrect. Depending on how it is used in the advertisement, information in the public domain can still be either false or misleading.","rule":"C is the correct answer. Even though this is a somewhat limited communication, it is clearly an advertisement for the attorney’s new law office, and as such is covered by Model Rule 7.1. Model Rule 7.1 forbids only false or misleading communications, and this communication was neither false nor misleading. Here, all the communication did was state the simple facts regarding the reopening of the office, its location and phone number, and the fact that the attorney had been the state’s governor for eight years. A is incorrect. A lawyer is prohibited from making in-person, live telephone, or real-time electronic contact with prospective clients to solicit employment when the lawyer’s motive for doing so is the lawyer’s financial gain. Model Rule 7.3(a). This Rule seeks to address the fact that “the person, who may already feel overwhelmed by the circumstances giving rise to the need for legal services, may find it difficult to fully evaluate all available alternatives with reasoned judgment and appropriate self-interest in the face of the lawyer’s presence and insistence upon being retained immediately.” Model Rule 7.3, Comment [2]. However, there is an exception for contacts with former clients, friends, other lawyers, or relatives, which is what this answer choice is trying to get you to think of, since this communication is only going out to those people who fall within this exception. However, as the communication here is simply a printed announcement, it does not fall within this type of banned behavior, so the exception does not apply. B is incorrect. Model Rule 7.1 forbids only false or misleading communications in lawyer advertising. However, an advertisement can be misleading if it creates an unjustified expectation as to the results the lawyer may achieve. Here, a truthful statement about the attorney’s prior service as governor is unlikely to create any unjustified expectations as to what the attorney can achieve and is therefore not misleading. D is incorrect. Depending on how it is used in the advertisement, information in the public domain can still be either false or misleading.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for the announcement?","rule":"C is the correct answer. Even though this is a somewhat limited communication, it is clearly an advertisement for the attorney’s new law office, and as such is covered by Model Rule 7.1. Model Rule 7.1 forbids only false or misleading communications, and this communication was neither false nor misleading. Here, all the communication did was state the simple facts regarding the reopening of the office, its location and phone number, and the fact that the attorney had been the state’s governor for eight years. A is incorrect. A lawyer is prohibited from making in-person, live telephone, or real-time electronic contact with prospective clients to solicit employment when the lawyer’s motive for doing so is the lawyer’s financial gain. Model Rule 7.3(a). This Rule seeks to address the fact that “the person, who may already feel overwhelmed by the circumstances giving rise to the need for legal services, may find it difficult to fully evaluate all available alternatives with reasoned judgment and appropriate self-interest in the face of the lawyer’s presence and insistence upon being retained immediately.” Model Rule 7.3, Comment [2]. However, there is an exception for contacts with former clients, friends, other lawyers, or relatives, which is what this answer choice is trying to get you to think of, since this communication is only going out to those people who fall within this exception. However, as the communication here is simply a printed announcement, it does not fall within this type of banned behavior, so the exception does not apply. B is incorrect. Model Rule 7.1 forbids only false or misleading communications in lawyer advertising. However, an advertisement can be misleading if it creates an unjustified expectation as to the results the lawyer may achieve. Here, a truthful statement about the attorney’s prior service as governor is unlikely to create any unjustified expectations as to what the attorney can achieve and is therefore not misleading. D is incorrect. Depending on how it is used in the advertisement, information in the public domain can still be either false or misleading.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. Even though this is a somewhat limited communication, it is clearly an advertisement for the attorney’s new law office, and as such is covered by Model Rule 7.1. Model Rule 7.1 forbids only false or misleading communications, and this communication was neither false nor misleading. Here, all the communication did was state the simple facts regarding the reopening of the office, its location and phone number, and the fact that the attorney had been the state’s governor for eight years. A is incorrect. A lawyer is prohibited from making in-person, live telephone, or real-time electronic contact with prospective clients to solicit employment when the lawyer’s motive for doing so is the lawyer’s financial gain. Model Rule 7.3(a). This Rule seeks to address the fact that “the person, who may already feel overwhelmed by the circumstances giving rise to the need for legal services, may find it difficult to fully evaluate all available alternatives with reasoned judgment and appropriate self-interest in the face of the lawyer’s presence and insistence upon being retained immediately.” Model Rule 7.3, Comment [2]. However, there is an exception for contacts with former clients, friends, other lawyers, or relatives, which is what this answer choice is trying to get you to think of, since this communication is only going out to those people who fall within this exception. However, as the communication here is simply a printed announcement, it does not fall within this type of banned behavior, so the exception does not apply. B is incorrect. Model Rule 7.1 forbids only false or misleading communications in lawyer advertising. However, an advertisement can be misleading if it creates an unjustified expectation as to the results the lawyer may achieve. Here, a truthful statement about the attorney’s prior service as governor is unlikely to create any unjustified expectations as to what the attorney can achieve and is therefore not misleading. D is incorrect. Depending on how it is used in the advertisement, information in the public domain can still be either false or misleading.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.1","Model Rule 7.3(a)","Model Rule 7.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[63,64],"sourceAnswerPages":[104],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师在重新于本州开设律师事务所之前，连续担任了两届、每届四年的州长。他印制并邮寄了一份恢复私人执业的通知，收件人包括律师协会成员、以前的委托人，以及他从未代理过的私人朋友。通知说明该律师已经重新开设律师事务所，列明地址和电话号码，并补充说明他过去八年担任本州州长。该律师是否会因这份通知受到纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为邮寄对象包括从未成为其委托人的人。","B":"是，因为其州长任职经历与律师能力无关。","C":"否，因为通知中的信息真实。","D":"否，因为所有信息原本都已属于公开信息。"},"explanationZh":"C项正确。尽管这是一种对象范围较有限的通信，但显然是在为律师新开设的事务所做广告，因此受《职业行为示范规则》第7.1条约束。第7.1条仅禁止虚假或误导性通信，而本题通知既不虚假也不具有误导性。它只是陈述事务所重新开业、地址、电话号码，以及律师过去八年担任州长等简单事实。\n\nA项错误。如果律师为了自身经济利益而招揽业务，不得通过当面、实时电话或实时电子方式接触潜在委托人。《职业行为示范规则》第7.3(a)条。该规则考虑到，需要法律服务的人可能已经被有关处境压得不知所措；面对律师本人到场并坚持要求立即受聘，他们可能难以运用理性判断并按照自身利益充分评估所有可选方案。第7.3条评注[2]。该限制对前委托人、朋友、其他律师或亲属存在例外，而本题收件人恰好属于这些类别。不过，本题只是邮寄印刷通知，本来就不属于上述被禁止的实时接触，因此无须依赖该例外。\n\nB项错误。第7.1条只禁止律师广告中的虚假或误导性通信。如果广告使人对律师能够取得的结果产生没有正当根据的期待，也可能具有误导性。但如实陈述律师曾任州长，通常不会使人对其能取得的法律结果产生无正当根据的期待，因而并不具有误导性。\n\nD项错误。即使信息已属公开信息，根据它在广告中的使用方式，仍有可能构成虚假或误导性陈述。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-027","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":27,"topic":"Model Rule 3.5(a) / Model Rule 3.5 / Rule 4.1(A)(8)","question":"An attorney has tried many contested cases before a certain judge. The attorney believes the judge is lacking both in knowledge of the law and in good judgment and that another attorney would make an excellent judge. The attorney wishes to help defeat the judge and assist the other attorney in getting elected. The attorney intends to contribute an anonymous $5,000 donation to other attorney’s campaign. Is it proper for the attorney to do so?","choices":{"A":"Yes, the attorney may give $5,000 to the other attorney personally for his campaign.","B":"Yes, because the attorney’s contribution to the other attorney is made anonymously.","C":"No, because the attorney is practicing before the court to which the other attorney seeks election.","D":"No, because the attorney is not giving the $5,000 to a committee formed to further the other attorney’s election."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. In construing the propriety of a lawyer’s contribution in a judicial election campaign, the ABA Model Rules and the ABA Model Code of Judicial Conduct must be read in tandem. Model Rule 3.5(a) prohibits a lawyer from seeking to influence a judge or juror. Although the other attorney is not a judge yet, he soon could be. Comment [1] to Model Rule 3.5 says: “Many forms of improper influence upon a tribunal [ . . . ] are specified in the ABA Model Code of Judicial Conduct, with which an advocate should be familiar.” The cases and the disciplinary rulings in all states almost invariably construe a loan or gift to a judge by a lawyer who appears before him or her as an attempt to influence the judge. Thus, a contribution to the other attorney’s campaign may not be made by a gift to the other attorney personally under Model Rule 3.5. Further, Rule 4.1(A)(8) of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct states, a candidate shall not “personally solicit or accept campaign contributions other than through a campaign committee authorized by Rule 4.4.” D correctly recognizes that the attorney may make his contribution to the other attorney’s campaign committee. A is incorrect. The attorney may not give the contribution to the other attorney personally. The underlying reason for the rule prohibiting giving personal gifts directly to the candidate is that it will lead to excessive influence by the donor lawyer when he or she appears before that person if that person in fact becomes a judge. B is incorrect. The Rules do not require that the gift be made anonymously, only that it not be made to the person running for judge personally. A donation to the judge’s duly constituted campaign committee is perfectly proper. C is incorrect. A lawyer may contribute to a judge’s campaign committee whether or not he or she appears before the judge. This recognizes the fact that lawyers who appear before a judge are better able than others to determine whether the judge is really qualified.","rule":"D is the correct answer. In construing the propriety of a lawyer’s contribution in a judicial election campaign, the ABA Model Rules and the ABA Model Code of Judicial Conduct must be read in tandem. Model Rule 3.5(a) prohibits a lawyer from seeking to influence a judge or juror. Although the other attorney is not a judge yet, he soon could be. Comment [1] to Model Rule 3.5 says: “Many forms of improper influence upon a tribunal [ . . . ] are specified in the ABA Model Code of Judicial Conduct, with which an advocate should be familiar.” The cases and the disciplinary rulings in all states almost invariably construe a loan or gift to a judge by a lawyer who appears before him or her as an attempt to influence the judge. Thus, a contribution to the other attorney’s campaign may not be made by a gift to the other attorney personally under Model Rule 3.5. Further, Rule 4.1(A)(8) of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct states, a candidate shall not “personally solicit or accept campaign contributions other than through a campaign committee authorized by Rule 4.4.” D correctly recognizes that the attorney may make his contribution to the other attorney’s campaign committee. A is incorrect. The attorney may not give the contribution to the other attorney personally. The underlying reason for the rule prohibiting giving personal gifts directly to the candidate is that it will lead to excessive influence by the donor lawyer when he or she appears before that person if that person in fact becomes a judge. B is incorrect. The Rules do not require that the gift be made anonymously, only that it not be made to the person running for judge personally. A donation to the judge’s duly constituted campaign committee is perfectly proper. C is incorrect. A lawyer may contribute to a judge’s campaign committee whether or not he or she appears before the judge. This recognizes the fact that lawyers who appear before a judge are better able than others to determine whether the judge is really qualified.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the attorney to do so?","rule":"D is the correct answer. In construing the propriety of a lawyer’s contribution in a judicial election campaign, the ABA Model Rules and the ABA Model Code of Judicial Conduct must be read in tandem. Model Rule 3.5(a) prohibits a lawyer from seeking to influence a judge or juror. Although the other attorney is not a judge yet, he soon could be. Comment [1] to Model Rule 3.5 says: “Many forms of improper influence upon a tribunal [ . . . ] are specified in the ABA Model Code of Judicial Conduct, with which an advocate should be familiar.” The cases and the disciplinary rulings in all states almost invariably construe a loan or gift to a judge by a lawyer who appears before him or her as an attempt to influence the judge. Thus, a contribution to the other attorney’s campaign may not be made by a gift to the other attorney personally under Model Rule 3.5. Further, Rule 4.1(A)(8) of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct states, a candidate shall not “personally solicit or accept campaign contributions other than through a campaign committee authorized by Rule 4.4.” D correctly recognizes that the attorney may make his contribution to the other attorney’s campaign committee. A is incorrect. The attorney may not give the contribution to the other attorney personally. The underlying reason for the rule prohibiting giving personal gifts directly to the candidate is that it will lead to excessive influence by the donor lawyer when he or she appears before that person if that person in fact becomes a judge. B is incorrect. The Rules do not require that the gift be made anonymously, only that it not be made to the person running for judge personally. A donation to the judge’s duly constituted campaign committee is perfectly proper. C is incorrect. A lawyer may contribute to a judge’s campaign committee whether or not he or she appears before the judge. This recognizes the fact that lawyers who appear before a judge are better able than others to determine whether the judge is really qualified.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. In construing the propriety of a lawyer’s contribution in a judicial election campaign, the ABA Model Rules and the ABA Model Code of Judicial Conduct must be read in tandem. Model Rule 3.5(a) prohibits a lawyer from seeking to influence a judge or juror. Although the other attorney is not a judge yet, he soon could be. Comment [1] to Model Rule 3.5 says: “Many forms of improper influence upon a tribunal [ . . . ] are specified in the ABA Model Code of Judicial Conduct, with which an advocate should be familiar.” The cases and the disciplinary rulings in all states almost invariably construe a loan or gift to a judge by a lawyer who appears before him or her as an attempt to influence the judge. Thus, a contribution to the other attorney’s campaign may not be made by a gift to the other attorney personally under Model Rule 3.5. Further, Rule 4.1(A)(8) of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct states, a candidate shall not “personally solicit or accept campaign contributions other than through a campaign committee authorized by Rule 4.4.” D correctly recognizes that the attorney may make his contribution to the other attorney’s campaign committee. A is incorrect. The attorney may not give the contribution to the other attorney personally. The underlying reason for the rule prohibiting giving personal gifts directly to the candidate is that it will lead to excessive influence by the donor lawyer when he or she appears before that person if that person in fact becomes a judge. B is incorrect. The Rules do not require that the gift be made anonymously, only that it not be made to the person running for judge personally. A donation to the judge’s duly constituted campaign committee is perfectly proper. C is incorrect. A lawyer may contribute to a judge’s campaign committee whether or not he or she appears before the judge. This recognizes the fact that lawyers who appear before a judge are better able than others to determine whether the judge is really qualified.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.5(a)","Model Rule 3.5","Rule 4.1(A)(8)","Rule 4.4"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[64],"sourceAnswerPages":[104,105],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师曾在某位法官面前审理过许多有争议的案件。律师认为该法官既缺乏法律知识，也缺乏良好判断力，并认为另一名律师会成为一名优秀法官。律师希望帮助击败现任法官，并协助另一名律师当选。他打算匿名向另一名律师的竞选活动捐赠5,000美元。该律师这样做是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，该律师可以把5,000美元直接交给另一名律师本人用于竞选。","B":"是，因为该律师对另一名律师的捐款是匿名的。","C":"否，因为该律师正在另一名律师竞选的法院出庭执业。","D":"否，因为该律师没有把5,000美元交给为推动另一名律师当选而成立的竞选委员会。"},"explanationZh":"D项正确。判断律师在司法选举活动中捐款是否适当时，必须结合阅读ABA《职业行为示范规则》和ABA《司法行为示范准则》。《职业行为示范规则》第3.5(a)条禁止律师试图影响法官或陪审员。虽然另一名律师目前还不是法官，但可能很快成为法官。第3.5条评注[1]指出：“对审裁机构施加不当影响的许多形式……规定于ABA《司法行为示范准则》中，诉讼代理律师应当熟悉这些规定。”各州的判例和纪律裁定几乎一贯把经常在某法官面前出庭的律师向该法官提供贷款或礼物，视为试图影响法官。因此，根据第3.5条，不得以直接赠与候选人本人的方式向其竞选活动捐款。\n\n此外，《司法行为示范准则》第4.1(A)(8)条规定，候选人不得“亲自招揽或接受竞选捐款，但通过第4.4条授权的竞选委员会进行的除外”。D项正确认识到，该律师可以把捐款交给另一名律师的竞选委员会。\n\nA项错误。律师不得把捐款直接交给另一名律师本人。禁止直接向司法候选人提供个人礼物的理由，是如果候选人最终成为法官，捐赠律师日后在其面前出庭时，可能因此对法官产生过度影响。\n\nB项错误。规则并不要求捐款必须匿名，只要求不得直接交给竞选法官的人本人。向依法成立的竞选委员会捐款完全适当。\n\nC项错误。无论律师是否在有关法官面前出庭，都可以向其竞选委员会捐款。这也承认，经常在法官面前执业的律师往往比其他人更有能力判断该法官是否真正具备任职资格。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-028","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":28,"topic":"Model Rule 8.2(a) / Model Rule 8.2 / Model Rule 8.2(b)","question":"An attorney practices law in the same community as a lawyer who is running for election as a state judge. The attorney has frequently observed the judicial candidate’s courtroom demeanor in litigated cases. Based on those experiences, the attorney believes that the judicial candidate does not have a proper judicial temperament. A local news reporter asked the attorney how he would rate the candidate, and the attorney responded in good faith that he believed the candidate was unsuited for the bench and lacked the proper judicial temperament for a judge. A local newspaper with a wide circulation quoted the attorney’s remarks. Were the attorney’s remarks proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney was not seeking judicial office.","B":"Yes, because the attorney believed the candidate was unsuited for the bench.","C":"No, because the remarks serve to bring the judiciary into disrepute.","D":"No, because a lawyer should not publicly comment on candidates for judicial office."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not make a statement that the lawyer knows to be false or with reckless disregard as to its truth or falsity concerning the qualifications or integrity of a judge, adjudicatory officer, or public legal officer, or of a candidate for election or appointment to judicial or legal office.” Model Rule 8.2(a). However, lawyers’ assessments are necessarily relied on in determining the fitness of persons being considered for election or appointment to judicial offices. Model Rule 8.2, Comment [1]. Consequently, the attorney’s expression of good faith views about a judicial candidate’s qualifications was proper, because “[e]xpressing honest and candid opinions on such matters contributes to improving the administration of justice.” Model Rule 8.2, Comment [1]. The Rule targets false statements, not honest opinions. A is incorrect. Even if the attorney was seeking judicial office, it was still proper for him to provide good faith opinions about the judicial candidate’s qualifications because such opinions promote the administration of justice by assisting the voters in making informed electoral decisions. Model Rule 8.2, Comment [1]. If he were a candidate for public office, the attorney would be required to comply with the applicable provisions of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct. Model Rule 8.2(b). However, the Model Code does not forbid comments such as those made by the attorney. C is incorrect. Although false statements about judicial candidates can unfairly undermine public confidence in the administration of justice, a lawyer’s good faith expressions of opinion about a judicial candidate’s temperament, based on the lawyer’s experience, promotes the administration of justice by helping voters make informed decisions. Model Rule 8.2. Also, the lawyer running for election has not yet served in the judiciary, so any comments regarding his ability and temperament would say nothing regarding the judiciary. D is incorrect. As stated above, although false public statements about a judicial candidate’s qualifications are improper, lawyers are entitled to make truthful public statements about judicial candidates.","rule":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not make a statement that the lawyer knows to be false or with reckless disregard as to its truth or falsity concerning the qualifications or integrity of a judge, adjudicatory officer, or public legal officer, or of a candidate for election or appointment to judicial or legal office.” Model Rule 8.2(a). However, lawyers’ assessments are necessarily relied on in determining the fitness of persons being considered for election or appointment to judicial offices. Model Rule 8.2, Comment [1]. Consequently, the attorney’s expression of good faith views about a judicial candidate’s qualifications was proper, because “[e]xpressing honest and candid opinions on such matters contributes to improving the administration of justice.” Model Rule 8.2, Comment [1]. The Rule targets false statements, not honest opinions. A is incorrect. Even if the attorney was seeking judicial office, it was still proper for him to provide good faith opinions about the judicial candidate’s qualifications because such opinions promote the administration of justice by assisting the voters in making informed electoral decisions. Model Rule 8.2, Comment [1]. If he were a candidate for public office, the attorney would be required to comply with the applicable provisions of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct. Model Rule 8.2(b). However, the Model Code does not forbid comments such as those made by the attorney. C is incorrect. Although false statements about judicial candidates can unfairly undermine public confidence in the administration of justice, a lawyer’s good faith expressions of opinion about a judicial candidate’s temperament, based on the lawyer’s experience, promotes the administration of justice by helping voters make informed decisions. Model Rule 8.2. Also, the lawyer running for election has not yet served in the judiciary, so any comments regarding his ability and temperament would say nothing regarding the judiciary. D is incorrect. As stated above, although false public statements about a judicial candidate’s qualifications are improper, lawyers are entitled to make truthful public statements about judicial candidates.","irac":{"issue":"Were the attorney’s remarks proper?","rule":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not make a statement that the lawyer knows to be false or with reckless disregard as to its truth or falsity concerning the qualifications or integrity of a judge, adjudicatory officer, or public legal officer, or of a candidate for election or appointment to judicial or legal office.” Model Rule 8.2(a). However, lawyers’ assessments are necessarily relied on in determining the fitness of persons being considered for election or appointment to judicial offices. Model Rule 8.2, Comment [1]. Consequently, the attorney’s expression of good faith views about a judicial candidate’s qualifications was proper, because “[e]xpressing honest and candid opinions on such matters contributes to improving the administration of justice.” Model Rule 8.2, Comment [1]. The Rule targets false statements, not honest opinions. A is incorrect. Even if the attorney was seeking judicial office, it was still proper for him to provide good faith opinions about the judicial candidate’s qualifications because such opinions promote the administration of justice by assisting the voters in making informed electoral decisions. Model Rule 8.2, Comment [1]. If he were a candidate for public office, the attorney would be required to comply with the applicable provisions of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct. Model Rule 8.2(b). However, the Model Code does not forbid comments such as those made by the attorney. C is incorrect. Although false statements about judicial candidates can unfairly undermine public confidence in the administration of justice, a lawyer’s good faith expressions of opinion about a judicial candidate’s temperament, based on the lawyer’s experience, promotes the administration of justice by helping voters make informed decisions. Model Rule 8.2. Also, the lawyer running for election has not yet served in the judiciary, so any comments regarding his ability and temperament would say nothing regarding the judiciary. D is incorrect. As stated above, although false public statements about a judicial candidate’s qualifications are improper, lawyers are entitled to make truthful public statements about judicial candidates.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not make a statement that the lawyer knows to be false or with reckless disregard as to its truth or falsity concerning the qualifications or integrity of a judge, adjudicatory officer, or public legal officer, or of a candidate for election or appointment to judicial or legal office.” Model Rule 8.2(a). However, lawyers’ assessments are necessarily relied on in determining the fitness of persons being considered for election or appointment to judicial offices. Model Rule 8.2, Comment [1]. Consequently, the attorney’s expression of good faith views about a judicial candidate’s qualifications was proper, because “[e]xpressing honest and candid opinions on such matters contributes to improving the administration of justice.” Model Rule 8.2, Comment [1]. The Rule targets false statements, not honest opinions. A is incorrect. Even if the attorney was seeking judicial office, it was still proper for him to provide good faith opinions about the judicial candidate’s qualifications because such opinions promote the administration of justice by assisting the voters in making informed electoral decisions. Model Rule 8.2, Comment [1]. If he were a candidate for public office, the attorney would be required to comply with the applicable provisions of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct. Model Rule 8.2(b). However, the Model Code does not forbid comments such as those made by the attorney. C is incorrect. Although false statements about judicial candidates can unfairly undermine public confidence in the administration of justice, a lawyer’s good faith expressions of opinion about a judicial candidate’s temperament, based on the lawyer’s experience, promotes the administration of justice by helping voters make informed decisions. Model Rule 8.2. Also, the lawyer running for election has not yet served in the judiciary, so any comments regarding his ability and temperament would say nothing regarding the judiciary. D is incorrect. As stated above, although false public statements about a judicial candidate’s qualifications are improper, lawyers are entitled to make truthful public statements about judicial candidates.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 8.2(a)","Model Rule 8.2","Model Rule 8.2(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[64,65],"sourceAnswerPages":[105,106],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师与一名正在竞选州法官的律师在同一社区执业。该律师经常在诉讼案件中观察这名司法职位候选人的法庭表现。根据这些经历，律师认为该候选人不具备适当的司法气质。一名当地新闻记者询问律师如何评价该候选人，律师善意回答说，自己认为该候选人不适合担任法官，也缺乏法官应有的司法气质。一家发行量很大的地方报纸引用了律师的评论。该律师的评论是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该律师本人没有竞选司法职位。","B":"是，因为该律师确实认为该候选人不适合担任法官。","C":"否，因为这些评论会使司法机关蒙受恶名。","D":"否，因为律师不应公开评论司法职位候选人。"},"explanationZh":"B项正确。“律师不得就法官、裁判官员、公共法律官员，或者司法或法律职位的选举或任命候选人的资格或诚信，发表其明知虚假或者罔顾真伪的陈述。”《职业行为示范规则》第8.2(a)条。但是，人们在判断参加司法职位选举或任命者是否适任时，必然会依赖律师的评价。第8.2条评注[1]。因此，该律师善意表达对司法候选人资格的看法是适当的，因为“就此类事项表达诚实而坦率的意见，有助于改善司法行政”。该规则针对的是虚假陈述，而不是诚实意见。\n\nA项错误。即使该律师本人也在竞选司法职位，他仍可就另一名司法候选人的资格善意发表意见，因为这种意见可以帮助选民作出知情决定，从而促进司法行政。第8.2条评注[1]。如果律师是公职候选人，则还须遵守《司法行为示范准则》的适用规定。第8.2(b)条。但该准则并不禁止题中这种评论。\n\nC项错误。关于司法候选人的虚假陈述可能不公正地削弱公众对司法行政的信心；但律师依据自身经历，善意表达对候选人气质的意见，能够帮助选民作出知情决定，因而促进司法行政。第8.2条。此外，竞选律师尚未在司法机关任职，对其能力和气质的评论本身并不能说明司法机关的情况。\n\nD项错误。如上所述，关于司法候选人资格的虚假公开陈述不当，但律师有权就司法候选人作出真实的公开陈述。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-029","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":29,"topic":"Model Rule 1.3","question":"An attorney was retained by a passenger on a bus who was injured in a collision between the bus and a truck. Although he was busy, his time was not completely occupied with work for other clients. The passenger paid the attorney a retainer of $1,000, and agreed further that the attorney should have a fee of 25 percent of any recovery before filing suit, 30 percent of any recovery after suit was filed but before judgment, and 35 percent of any recovery after trial and judgment. The attorney promptly called the lawyer for the bus company and told him she was representing the passenger and would like to talk about a settlement. The attorney made an appointment to talk to the lawyer for the bus company, but did not keep the appointment. The attorney continued to put off talking to the lawyer for the bus company. Meanwhile, the passenger became concerned because she had heard nothing from her attorney. The passenger called the attorney’s office, but was told the attorney was not in and would not call back. The passenger was told not to worry because the attorney would look after her interests. After 10 months had passed, the passenger went to another attorney for advice. The other attorney advised the passenger that the statute of limitations would run in one week and, with the passenger’s consent, immediately filed suit for the passenger. Upon the passenger’s demand, the attorney refunded the $1,000 the passenger had paid. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney’s time was not completely occupied with work for other clients.","B":"Yes, because the attorney neglected the representation of the passenger.","C":"No, because the passenger’s suit was filed before the statute of limitations ran.","D":"No, because the attorney returned the $1,000 retainer to the passenger."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.3 states: “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Comment [2] states: “A lawyer’s work load must be controlled so that each matter can be handled competently.” Comment [3] states: “Perhaps no professional shortcoming is more widely resented than procrastination.” The facts here show the typical way a lawyer can end up subject to discipline: (1) make appointments and ignore them, (2) ignore the client’s phone calls and requests for information and progress reports, (3) fail to keep the client advised, (4) work on other matters to the exclusion or neglect of the client, and (5) ignore the passage of time and the inevitable running of the statute of limitations. The key word in Answer B is “neglect.” Here, the attorney has been guilty of professional neglect. After misuse of client funds, this is the most common basis for lawyer discipline. A is incorrect. Commitment to one or several clients to the neglect of another is exactly what Model Rule 1.3 was intended to prevent. C is incorrect. The attorney is not relieved of the consequences of her neglect because the passenger avoided the running of the statute. The passenger has already suffered in other ways. First, if the attorney had pursued her settlement negotiations, the passenger might have enjoyed the proceeds of settlement many months earlier. Second, the attitude of the bus company may have hardened in the interim and made settlement more difficult. Third, the passenger could have been spared the anguish and frustration caused by unanswered phone calls and worry about her claim. D is incorrect. The issue is not the retainer, but rather the attorney’s neglect of the passenger’s interests.","rule":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.3 states: “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Comment [2] states: “A lawyer’s work load must be controlled so that each matter can be handled competently.” Comment [3] states: “Perhaps no professional shortcoming is more widely resented than procrastination.” The facts here show the typical way a lawyer can end up subject to discipline: (1) make appointments and ignore them, (2) ignore the client’s phone calls and requests for information and progress reports, (3) fail to keep the client advised, (4) work on other matters to the exclusion or neglect of the client, and (5) ignore the passage of time and the inevitable running of the statute of limitations. The key word in Answer B is “neglect.” Here, the attorney has been guilty of professional neglect. After misuse of client funds, this is the most common basis for lawyer discipline. A is incorrect. Commitment to one or several clients to the neglect of another is exactly what Model Rule 1.3 was intended to prevent. C is incorrect. The attorney is not relieved of the consequences of her neglect because the passenger avoided the running of the statute. The passenger has already suffered in other ways. First, if the attorney had pursued her settlement negotiations, the passenger might have enjoyed the proceeds of settlement many months earlier. Second, the attitude of the bus company may have hardened in the interim and made settlement more difficult. Third, the passenger could have been spared the anguish and frustration caused by unanswered phone calls and worry about her claim. D is incorrect. The issue is not the retainer, but rather the attorney’s neglect of the passenger’s interests.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.3 states: “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Comment [2] states: “A lawyer’s work load must be controlled so that each matter can be handled competently.” Comment [3] states: “Perhaps no professional shortcoming is more widely resented than procrastination.” The facts here show the typical way a lawyer can end up subject to discipline: (1) make appointments and ignore them, (2) ignore the client’s phone calls and requests for information and progress reports, (3) fail to keep the client advised, (4) work on other matters to the exclusion or neglect of the client, and (5) ignore the passage of time and the inevitable running of the statute of limitations. The key word in Answer B is “neglect.” Here, the attorney has been guilty of professional neglect. After misuse of client funds, this is the most common basis for lawyer discipline. A is incorrect. Commitment to one or several clients to the neglect of another is exactly what Model Rule 1.3 was intended to prevent. C is incorrect. The attorney is not relieved of the consequences of her neglect because the passenger avoided the running of the statute. The passenger has already suffered in other ways. First, if the attorney had pursued her settlement negotiations, the passenger might have enjoyed the proceeds of settlement many months earlier. Second, the attitude of the bus company may have hardened in the interim and made settlement more difficult. Third, the passenger could have been spared the anguish and frustration caused by unanswered phone calls and worry about her claim. D is incorrect. The issue is not the retainer, but rather the attorney’s neglect of the passenger’s interests.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.3 states: “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Comment [2] states: “A lawyer’s work load must be controlled so that each matter can be handled competently.” Comment [3] states: “Perhaps no professional shortcoming is more widely resented than procrastination.” The facts here show the typical way a lawyer can end up subject to discipline: (1) make appointments and ignore them, (2) ignore the client’s phone calls and requests for information and progress reports, (3) fail to keep the client advised, (4) work on other matters to the exclusion or neglect of the client, and (5) ignore the passage of time and the inevitable running of the statute of limitations. The key word in Answer B is “neglect.” Here, the attorney has been guilty of professional neglect. After misuse of client funds, this is the most common basis for lawyer discipline. A is incorrect. Commitment to one or several clients to the neglect of another is exactly what Model Rule 1.3 was intended to prevent. C is incorrect. The attorney is not relieved of the consequences of her neglect because the passenger avoided the running of the statute. The passenger has already suffered in other ways. First, if the attorney had pursued her settlement negotiations, the passenger might have enjoyed the proceeds of settlement many months earlier. Second, the attitude of the bus company may have hardened in the interim and made settlement more difficult. Third, the passenger could have been spared the anguish and frustration caused by unanswered phone calls and worry about her claim. D is incorrect. The issue is not the retainer, but rather the attorney’s neglect of the passenger’s interests.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[65],"sourceAnswerPages":[106,107],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名公交车乘客在公交车与卡车相撞事故中受伤，聘请一名律师。该律师虽然很忙，但其全部时间并未都被其他委托人的工作占用。乘客向律师支付1,000美元预付律师费，并约定：起诉前取得的任何赔偿，律师收取25%；起诉后、判决前取得的赔偿，律师收取30%；庭审并判决后取得的赔偿，律师收取35%。律师很快给公交公司律师打电话，说明自己代理该乘客并希望讨论和解。律师约定与公交公司律师会面，却没有赴约，此后还不断拖延与对方律师交谈。\n\n与此同时，乘客因一直没有收到律师消息而开始担心。她给律师办公室打电话，却被告知律师不在，也不会回电；办公室只让她不要担心，称律师会照顾她的利益。十个月后，乘客向另一名律师咨询。另一名律师告诉她，诉讼时效将在一周后届满，并在取得乘客同意后立即为她起诉。应乘客要求，原律师退还了1,000美元预付律师费。原律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该律师的时间并未完全被其他委托人的工作占用。","B":"是，因为该律师疏忽了对乘客的代理。","C":"否，因为乘客在诉讼时效届满前已经提起诉讼。","D":"否，因为律师已向乘客退还1,000美元预付律师费。"},"explanationZh":"B项正确。《职业行为示范规则》第1.3条规定：“律师代理委托人时，应当以合理的勤勉和及时性行事。”评注[2]指出：“律师必须控制工作量，使每项事务都能得到胜任处理。”评注[3]指出：“在各种职业缺失中，拖延或许是最普遍招致怨恨的一种。”\n\n本题事实展示了律师受到纪律处分的典型过程：（1）作出会面安排却置之不理；（2）无视委托人的电话、信息请求和进度报告要求；（3）不及时向委托人通报情况；（4）专注其他事务而排斥或忽视该委托人的事项；以及（5）无视时间流逝和诉讼时效必然届满。B项的关键词是“疏忽”。该律师构成职业疏忽；除不当使用委托人资金外，这是律师纪律处分最常见的依据。\n\nA项错误。为一个或数个委托人投入精力，却因此疏忽另一名委托人，正是第1.3条旨在防止的行为。\n\nC项错误。乘客最终避免诉讼时效届满，并不能使律师免于承担疏忽的后果。乘客已经在其他方面受到损害。第一，如果律师及时推进和解谈判，乘客可能早数月取得和解款；第二，公交公司在拖延期间的态度可能变得更强硬，使和解更加困难；第三，乘客本可免受电话无人回应以及担心其请求的焦虑和挫折。\n\nD项错误。争点不是预付律师费，而是律师疏忽了乘客的利益。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-030","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":30,"topic":"Model Rule 3.2 / Model Rule 1.3 / Model Rule 3.4","question":"An attorney filed a personal injury suit on behalf of a plaintiff. The defendant was personally served with process. The attorney knows that the defendant is insured by an insurance company and that another attorney has been retained by the insurance company to represent the defendant. No responsive pleading has been filed on behalf of the defendant, and the time for filing expired over 10 days ago. Is the attorney subject to discipline if the attorney proceeds to have a default judgment entered?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney knows that another attorney had been retained by the insurance company to represent the defendant.","B":"Yes, because the attorney failed to extend professional courtesy to another lawyer.","C":"No, because the attorney is properly representing her client’s interests.","D":"No, because any judgment will be satisfied by the insurance company."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. Model Rule 3.2 directs a lawyer to make reasonable efforts to expedite litigation consistent with the interests of the client. Model Rule 1.3 directs a lawyer to act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client. Model Rule 3.4 covers fairness to opposing counsel, specifically issues such as falsifying evidence and inducing witnesses to give false testimony. Even though the attorney knows the insurance company is represented by another attorney, nothing in Model Rule 3.4 states that the attorney must warn the opposing attorney of a possible default judgment. Importantly, such a requirement could force a lawyer to violate Model Rules 3.2 and 1.3. A is incorrect. For the same reasons as above, the attorney’s knowledge that another attorney represented the insurance company did not give the attorney an obligation to inform the other attorney of the default judgment. B is incorrect. If the attorney knew the other attorney and had dealt with him in other matters, it might have been preferable to advise the other attorney before entering the default in order to avoid the other attorney’s anger, but nothing required the attorney to do so. D is incorrect. First, this answer choice is not responsive to the question. Second, each step in a litigation requires prompt action by a lawyer. Whether or not the insurance company will pay, the attorney must protect her client’s interests at every step.","rule":"C is the correct answer. Model Rule 3.2 directs a lawyer to make reasonable efforts to expedite litigation consistent with the interests of the client. Model Rule 1.3 directs a lawyer to act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client. Model Rule 3.4 covers fairness to opposing counsel, specifically issues such as falsifying evidence and inducing witnesses to give false testimony. Even though the attorney knows the insurance company is represented by another attorney, nothing in Model Rule 3.4 states that the attorney must warn the opposing attorney of a possible default judgment. Importantly, such a requirement could force a lawyer to violate Model Rules 3.2 and 1.3. A is incorrect. For the same reasons as above, the attorney’s knowledge that another attorney represented the insurance company did not give the attorney an obligation to inform the other attorney of the default judgment. B is incorrect. If the attorney knew the other attorney and had dealt with him in other matters, it might have been preferable to advise the other attorney before entering the default in order to avoid the other attorney’s anger, but nothing required the attorney to do so. D is incorrect. First, this answer choice is not responsive to the question. Second, each step in a litigation requires prompt action by a lawyer. Whether or not the insurance company will pay, the attorney must protect her client’s interests at every step.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline if the attorney proceeds to have a default judgment entered?","rule":"C is the correct answer. Model Rule 3.2 directs a lawyer to make reasonable efforts to expedite litigation consistent with the interests of the client. Model Rule 1.3 directs a lawyer to act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client. Model Rule 3.4 covers fairness to opposing counsel, specifically issues such as falsifying evidence and inducing witnesses to give false testimony. Even though the attorney knows the insurance company is represented by another attorney, nothing in Model Rule 3.4 states that the attorney must warn the opposing attorney of a possible default judgment. Importantly, such a requirement could force a lawyer to violate Model Rules 3.2 and 1.3. A is incorrect. For the same reasons as above, the attorney’s knowledge that another attorney represented the insurance company did not give the attorney an obligation to inform the other attorney of the default judgment. B is incorrect. If the attorney knew the other attorney and had dealt with him in other matters, it might have been preferable to advise the other attorney before entering the default in order to avoid the other attorney’s anger, but nothing required the attorney to do so. D is incorrect. First, this answer choice is not responsive to the question. Second, each step in a litigation requires prompt action by a lawyer. Whether or not the insurance company will pay, the attorney must protect her client’s interests at every step.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. Model Rule 3.2 directs a lawyer to make reasonable efforts to expedite litigation consistent with the interests of the client. Model Rule 1.3 directs a lawyer to act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client. Model Rule 3.4 covers fairness to opposing counsel, specifically issues such as falsifying evidence and inducing witnesses to give false testimony. Even though the attorney knows the insurance company is represented by another attorney, nothing in Model Rule 3.4 states that the attorney must warn the opposing attorney of a possible default judgment. Importantly, such a requirement could force a lawyer to violate Model Rules 3.2 and 1.3. A is incorrect. For the same reasons as above, the attorney’s knowledge that another attorney represented the insurance company did not give the attorney an obligation to inform the other attorney of the default judgment. B is incorrect. If the attorney knew the other attorney and had dealt with him in other matters, it might have been preferable to advise the other attorney before entering the default in order to avoid the other attorney’s anger, but nothing required the attorney to do so. D is incorrect. First, this answer choice is not responsive to the question. Second, each step in a litigation requires prompt action by a lawyer. Whether or not the insurance company will pay, the attorney must protect her client’s interests at every step.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.2","Model Rule 1.3","Model Rule 3.4"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[65,66],"sourceAnswerPages":[107,108],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代表原告提起人身伤害诉讼。被告已经被亲自送达诉讼文书。律师知道被告由一家保险公司承保，也知道保险公司已聘请另一名律师代理被告。被告一方尚未提交任何答辩文书，提交期限已超过十天。如果原告律师申请登记缺席判决，她是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师知道保险公司已经聘请另一名律师代理被告。","B":"是，因为律师没有向另一名律师提供职业上的礼让。","C":"否，因为律师是在适当维护其委托人的利益。","D":"否，因为任何判决都将由保险公司履行。"},"explanationZh":"C项正确。《职业行为示范规则》第3.2条要求律师作出与委托人利益一致的合理努力，加快诉讼进程。第1.3条要求律师代理委托人时以合理勤勉和及时性行事。第3.4条涉及对对方律师的公平，具体包括伪造证据和诱使证人作虚假证言等问题。尽管该律师知道保险公司由另一名律师代理，第3.4条并未规定她必须警告对方律师可能取得缺席判决。特别是，如果规定这种义务，反而可能迫使律师违反第3.2条和第1.3条。\n\nA项错误。基于上述相同理由，律师知道保险公司已有律师代理，并不会使她承担通知对方可能取得缺席判决的义务。\n\nB项错误。如果该律师认识对方律师并曾在其他事项中与其交往，为避免激怒对方，在申请缺席判决前通知对方可能是更好的做法；但没有任何规则要求她这样做。\n\nD项错误。首先，该答案没有回答本题。其次，诉讼的每一个阶段都要求律师及时行动。无论保险公司最终是否付款，律师都必须在每一阶段保护委托人的利益。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-031","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":31,"topic":"Rule 4.1(A)(11)","question":"An attorney is a candidate in a contested election for judicial office. Her opponent is the incumbent judge and has occupied the bench for many years. The director of the state commission on judicial conduct, upon inquiry by the attorney, erroneously told the attorney that the judge had been reprimanded by the commission for misconduct in office. The attorney, who had confidence in the director, believed him. In fact, the judge had not been reprimanded by the commission; the commission had conducted hearings on the judge’s alleged misconduct in office and, by a three-to-two vote, declined to reprimand the judge. Decisions of the commission, including reprimands, are not confidential. Is the attorney subject to discipline for publicly stating that the judge had been reprimanded for misconduct?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the official records of the commission would have disclosed the truth.","B":"Yes, because the judge had not been reprimanded.","C":"No, because the attorney reasonably relied on the director’s information.","D":"No, because the judge was a candidate in a contested election."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. Candidates for judicial office must observe the provisions of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct governing judicial campaigns and elections. Most of these provisions are contained in Canon 4. Rule 4.1(A)(11) provides that a candidate shall not “knowingly or with reckless disregard for the truth, make any false or misleading statement.” Importantly, a person’s knowledge may be inferred from the circumstances. Here, the attorney does not have actual knowledge that the judge was not reprimanded for misconduct in office. Nor was it unreasonable for her to rely on the statement of an official directly responsible for maintaining the information at issue. A is incorrect. It assumes that the attorney had a duty to inquire into the records themselves after getting a response to her inquiry from the director of the commission himself. This was not a reasonable requirement to impose on the attorney. B is incorrect. The test is not the truth of the statement but whether the attorney has knowledge of the truth. D is incorrect. The answer suggests that judges are held to a different standard in contested elections than in noncontested elections. However, even in a contested judicial election, there are constraints on the candidates that do not apply to candidates for nonjudicial offices. See Canon 4.","rule":"C is the correct answer. Candidates for judicial office must observe the provisions of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct governing judicial campaigns and elections. Most of these provisions are contained in Canon 4. Rule 4.1(A)(11) provides that a candidate shall not “knowingly or with reckless disregard for the truth, make any false or misleading statement.” Importantly, a person’s knowledge may be inferred from the circumstances. Here, the attorney does not have actual knowledge that the judge was not reprimanded for misconduct in office. Nor was it unreasonable for her to rely on the statement of an official directly responsible for maintaining the information at issue. A is incorrect. It assumes that the attorney had a duty to inquire into the records themselves after getting a response to her inquiry from the director of the commission himself. This was not a reasonable requirement to impose on the attorney. B is incorrect. The test is not the truth of the statement but whether the attorney has knowledge of the truth. D is incorrect. The answer suggests that judges are held to a different standard in contested elections than in noncontested elections. However, even in a contested judicial election, there are constraints on the candidates that do not apply to candidates for nonjudicial offices. See Canon 4.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for publicly stating that the judge had been reprimanded for misconduct?","rule":"C is the correct answer. Candidates for judicial office must observe the provisions of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct governing judicial campaigns and elections. Most of these provisions are contained in Canon 4. Rule 4.1(A)(11) provides that a candidate shall not “knowingly or with reckless disregard for the truth, make any false or misleading statement.” Importantly, a person’s knowledge may be inferred from the circumstances. Here, the attorney does not have actual knowledge that the judge was not reprimanded for misconduct in office. Nor was it unreasonable for her to rely on the statement of an official directly responsible for maintaining the information at issue. A is incorrect. It assumes that the attorney had a duty to inquire into the records themselves after getting a response to her inquiry from the director of the commission himself. This was not a reasonable requirement to impose on the attorney. B is incorrect. The test is not the truth of the statement but whether the attorney has knowledge of the truth. D is incorrect. The answer suggests that judges are held to a different standard in contested elections than in noncontested elections. However, even in a contested judicial election, there are constraints on the candidates that do not apply to candidates for nonjudicial offices. See Canon 4.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. Candidates for judicial office must observe the provisions of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct governing judicial campaigns and elections. Most of these provisions are contained in Canon 4. Rule 4.1(A)(11) provides that a candidate shall not “knowingly or with reckless disregard for the truth, make any false or misleading statement.” Importantly, a person’s knowledge may be inferred from the circumstances. Here, the attorney does not have actual knowledge that the judge was not reprimanded for misconduct in office. Nor was it unreasonable for her to rely on the statement of an official directly responsible for maintaining the information at issue. A is incorrect. It assumes that the attorney had a duty to inquire into the records themselves after getting a response to her inquiry from the director of the commission himself. This was not a reasonable requirement to impose on the attorney. B is incorrect. The test is not the truth of the statement but whether the attorney has knowledge of the truth. D is incorrect. The answer suggests that judges are held to a different standard in contested elections than in noncontested elections. However, even in a contested judicial election, there are constraints on the candidates that do not apply to candidates for nonjudicial offices. See Canon 4.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 4.1(A)(11)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[66],"sourceAnswerPages":[108],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师正在参加一场有竞争对手的法官职位选举。她的对手是现任法官，已任职多年。州司法行为委员会主任在该律师询问时，错误地告诉她，该法官曾因任职期间的不当行为受到委员会谴责。该律师信任主任，因而相信了这一说法。事实上，该法官并未受到委员会谴责；委员会曾就该法官涉嫌的任职不当行为举行听证，但以三票对两票决定不予谴责。委员会的决定（包括谴责决定）并不保密。该律师公开声称该法官曾因不当行为受到谴责，是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为委员会的正式记录本可揭示真相。","B":"是，因为该法官实际上并未受到谴责。","C":"否，因为该律师合理地信赖了主任提供的信息。","D":"否，因为该法官是有竞争对手的选举中的候选人。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。司法职位候选人必须遵守《司法行为示范准则》中有关司法竞选与选举的规定，其中大多数规定载于准则四。规则 4.1(A)(11) 规定，候选人不得“明知陈述虚假或具有误导性，或者罔顾其真假而作出虚假或具有误导性的陈述”。需要特别注意的是，可以根据具体情形推定一个人是否知情。本题中，该律师并不实际知道该法官未因任职不当行为受到谴责；而且，她信赖直接负责保管相关信息的官员所作的陈述，也并非不合理。A 不正确。该选项假定，在律师已经向委员会主任本人查询并获得答复之后，她仍负有亲自查阅委员会记录的义务。要求该律师这样做并不合理。B 不正确。判断标准并非该陈述客观上是否真实，而是该律师是否知道真相。D 不正确。该选项暗示，法官在有竞争对手的选举中适用的标准不同于无竞争对手的选举。然而，即便在有竞争对手的司法选举中，候选人仍受到一些并不适用于非司法职位候选人的限制。参见准则四。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-032","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":32,"topic":"Model Rule 8.2 / Model Rule 3.5","question":"An attorney is a well-known, highly skilled litigator. The attorney’s practice is in an area of law in which the trial proceedings are heard by the court without a jury. In an interview with a prospective client, the attorney said, “I make certain that I give the campaign committee of every candidate for elective judicial office more money than any other lawyer gives, whether it’s $500 or $5,000. Judges know who helps them get elected.” The prospective client did not retain the attorney. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney’s contributions are made without consideration of candidates’ merits.","B":"Yes, because the attorney implied that the attorney receives favored treatment by judges.","C":"No, because the attorney’s statements were true.","D":"No, because the prospective client did not retain the attorney."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. Lawyers are officers of the court and are charged with the responsibility not to make statements with reckless disregard for the truth concerning the qualifications or integrity of a judge. Model Rule 8.2. Comment [1] points out that false statements by a lawyer can undermine public confidence in the administration of justice. Here, the attorney has attacked the integrity not only of one judge, but of the entire judiciary. Especially because his work as a skilled litigator brings him before these very judges, who decide both fact and law, the inescapable inference is that he has special influence over them. A is incorrect. A lawyer may contribute to a judge’s campaign committee without accounting for his or her motivation in doing so. The Model Code of Judicial Conduct permits the campaign committees of judges to solicit funds from lawyers, but the funds may not be used for the private benefit of the candidates. C is incorrect. If the statements are indeed true, then the attorney is subject to discipline under Model Rule 3.5 for seeking “to influence a judge, juror, prospective juror or other official by means prohibited by law.” D is incorrect. It’s immaterial to the attorney’s misconduct that the prospective client did not retain him.","rule":"B is the correct answer. Lawyers are officers of the court and are charged with the responsibility not to make statements with reckless disregard for the truth concerning the qualifications or integrity of a judge. Model Rule 8.2. Comment [1] points out that false statements by a lawyer can undermine public confidence in the administration of justice. Here, the attorney has attacked the integrity not only of one judge, but of the entire judiciary. Especially because his work as a skilled litigator brings him before these very judges, who decide both fact and law, the inescapable inference is that he has special influence over them. A is incorrect. A lawyer may contribute to a judge’s campaign committee without accounting for his or her motivation in doing so. The Model Code of Judicial Conduct permits the campaign committees of judges to solicit funds from lawyers, but the funds may not be used for the private benefit of the candidates. C is incorrect. If the statements are indeed true, then the attorney is subject to discipline under Model Rule 3.5 for seeking “to influence a judge, juror, prospective juror or other official by means prohibited by law.” D is incorrect. It’s immaterial to the attorney’s misconduct that the prospective client did not retain him.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. Lawyers are officers of the court and are charged with the responsibility not to make statements with reckless disregard for the truth concerning the qualifications or integrity of a judge. Model Rule 8.2. Comment [1] points out that false statements by a lawyer can undermine public confidence in the administration of justice. Here, the attorney has attacked the integrity not only of one judge, but of the entire judiciary. Especially because his work as a skilled litigator brings him before these very judges, who decide both fact and law, the inescapable inference is that he has special influence over them. A is incorrect. A lawyer may contribute to a judge’s campaign committee without accounting for his or her motivation in doing so. The Model Code of Judicial Conduct permits the campaign committees of judges to solicit funds from lawyers, but the funds may not be used for the private benefit of the candidates. C is incorrect. If the statements are indeed true, then the attorney is subject to discipline under Model Rule 3.5 for seeking “to influence a judge, juror, prospective juror or other official by means prohibited by law.” D is incorrect. It’s immaterial to the attorney’s misconduct that the prospective client did not retain him.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. Lawyers are officers of the court and are charged with the responsibility not to make statements with reckless disregard for the truth concerning the qualifications or integrity of a judge. Model Rule 8.2. Comment [1] points out that false statements by a lawyer can undermine public confidence in the administration of justice. Here, the attorney has attacked the integrity not only of one judge, but of the entire judiciary. Especially because his work as a skilled litigator brings him before these very judges, who decide both fact and law, the inescapable inference is that he has special influence over them. A is incorrect. A lawyer may contribute to a judge’s campaign committee without accounting for his or her motivation in doing so. The Model Code of Judicial Conduct permits the campaign committees of judges to solicit funds from lawyers, but the funds may not be used for the private benefit of the candidates. C is incorrect. If the statements are indeed true, then the attorney is subject to discipline under Model Rule 3.5 for seeking “to influence a judge, juror, prospective juror or other official by means prohibited by law.” D is incorrect. It’s immaterial to the attorney’s misconduct that the prospective client did not retain him.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 8.2","Model Rule 3.5"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[67],"sourceAnswerPages":[108,109],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师是知名且业务精湛的诉讼律师。其执业领域中的案件均由法院进行无陪审团审判。在与一名潜在委托人面谈时，该律师说：“我会确保自己给每一位参加法官职位选举的候选人竞选委员会捐的钱都比其他任何律师多，不管金额是 500 美元还是 5,000 美元。法官都知道是谁帮助他们当选的。”该潜在委托人最终没有聘用这名律师。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该律师捐款时并未考虑候选人是否胜任。","B":"是，因为该律师暗示自己会受到法官的优待。","C":"否，因为该律师所说的话是真实的。","D":"否，因为该潜在委托人没有聘用该律师。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。律师是法院工作人员，并负有责任，不得在明知虚假或罔顾真假的情况下，就法官的资历或诚信作出陈述。《职业行为示范规则》8.2。评注 [1] 指出，律师作出的虚假陈述可能损害公众对司法运行的信心。本题中，该律师攻击的并非仅仅一名法官的诚信，而是整个司法体系的诚信。尤其是，因为他作为一名优秀诉讼律师，经常要在这些同时裁判事实与法律的法官面前出庭，其言论不可避免地会使人推断他对这些法官具有特殊影响力。A 不正确。律师可以向法官的竞选委员会捐款，而不必说明其捐款动机。《司法行为示范准则》允许法官的竞选委员会向律师募集资金，但这些资金不得用于候选人的私人利益。C 不正确。如果这些陈述确实属实，那么该律师会因试图“以法律禁止的方式影响法官、陪审员、候选陪审员或其他官员”而违反《职业行为示范规则》3.5，并因此应受纪律处分。D 不正确。潜在委托人最终是否聘用了该律师，与该律师的不当行为无关。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-033","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":33,"topic":"Rule 2.9(A)(2)","question":"A judge is presiding in a case that has, as its main issue, a complicated point of commercial law. The lawyers have not presented the case to the judge’s satisfaction, and the judge believes she needs additional legal advice. The judge’s former partner in law practice is an attorney and an expert in the field of law that is at issue. The attorney has no interest in the case. The judge believes the advice is necessary, so she contacts the attorney without telling any of the parties involved. Was it proper for the judge to consult the attorney?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney has no interest in the case.","B":"Yes, because the judge believes that the attorney’s advice is needed to serve the interests of justice.","C":"No, because all parties in the case did not first give their written consent to the judge’s consultation with the attorney.","D":"No, because the judge did not inform the parties of the attorney’s identity and the substance of the attorney’s advice and asks for responses."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Each side is entitled to know and to respond to every fact and every argument presented by the other side. If a judge could consult any legal source he or she wished without identifying it and giving each side the right to respond, the adversarial nature of the judicial system would collapse. At the same time, there are instances in which a judge will need to get advice on the law, especially when, as in these facts, the lawyers have not cleared up all the issues. To enable the judge to do this without subverting the adversarial process, Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(2) permits a judge to “obtain the written advice of a disinterested expert on the law applicable to a proceeding before the judge, if the judge gives advance notice to the parties of the person to be consulted and the subject matter of the advice to be solicited, and affords the parties a reasonable opportunity to object and respond to the notice and to the advice received.” A is incorrect. It’s not enough that the expert consulted by the judge be impartial. The parties are entitled to know what the expert has been asked and what he or she has advised. After all, the expert may be wrong or the judge may not have communicated the issues correctly. The parties may want to respond, and they must be given the opportunity to do so. B is incorrect. What’s at issue here is not the judge’s integrity, but the very process by which adversarial issues are determined under the judicial process. C is incorrect only because it doesn’t correspond with how the Model Code of Judicial Conduct resolves this issue. Under the Model Code, it is possible for the judge to ask for advice so long as he or she tells the parties what he or she is doing and gives them the opportunity to respond.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Each side is entitled to know and to respond to every fact and every argument presented by the other side. If a judge could consult any legal source he or she wished without identifying it and giving each side the right to respond, the adversarial nature of the judicial system would collapse. At the same time, there are instances in which a judge will need to get advice on the law, especially when, as in these facts, the lawyers have not cleared up all the issues. To enable the judge to do this without subverting the adversarial process, Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(2) permits a judge to “obtain the written advice of a disinterested expert on the law applicable to a proceeding before the judge, if the judge gives advance notice to the parties of the person to be consulted and the subject matter of the advice to be solicited, and affords the parties a reasonable opportunity to object and respond to the notice and to the advice received.” A is incorrect. It’s not enough that the expert consulted by the judge be impartial. The parties are entitled to know what the expert has been asked and what he or she has advised. After all, the expert may be wrong or the judge may not have communicated the issues correctly. The parties may want to respond, and they must be given the opportunity to do so. B is incorrect. What’s at issue here is not the judge’s integrity, but the very process by which adversarial issues are determined under the judicial process. C is incorrect only because it doesn’t correspond with how the Model Code of Judicial Conduct resolves this issue. Under the Model Code, it is possible for the judge to ask for advice so long as he or she tells the parties what he or she is doing and gives them the opportunity to respond.","irac":{"issue":"Was it proper for the judge to consult the attorney?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Each side is entitled to know and to respond to every fact and every argument presented by the other side. If a judge could consult any legal source he or she wished without identifying it and giving each side the right to respond, the adversarial nature of the judicial system would collapse. At the same time, there are instances in which a judge will need to get advice on the law, especially when, as in these facts, the lawyers have not cleared up all the issues. To enable the judge to do this without subverting the adversarial process, Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(2) permits a judge to “obtain the written advice of a disinterested expert on the law applicable to a proceeding before the judge, if the judge gives advance notice to the parties of the person to be consulted and the subject matter of the advice to be solicited, and affords the parties a reasonable opportunity to object and respond to the notice and to the advice received.” A is incorrect. It’s not enough that the expert consulted by the judge be impartial. The parties are entitled to know what the expert has been asked and what he or she has advised. After all, the expert may be wrong or the judge may not have communicated the issues correctly. The parties may want to respond, and they must be given the opportunity to do so. B is incorrect. What’s at issue here is not the judge’s integrity, but the very process by which adversarial issues are determined under the judicial process. C is incorrect only because it doesn’t correspond with how the Model Code of Judicial Conduct resolves this issue. Under the Model Code, it is possible for the judge to ask for advice so long as he or she tells the parties what he or she is doing and gives them the opportunity to respond.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Each side is entitled to know and to respond to every fact and every argument presented by the other side. If a judge could consult any legal source he or she wished without identifying it and giving each side the right to respond, the adversarial nature of the judicial system would collapse. At the same time, there are instances in which a judge will need to get advice on the law, especially when, as in these facts, the lawyers have not cleared up all the issues. To enable the judge to do this without subverting the adversarial process, Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(2) permits a judge to “obtain the written advice of a disinterested expert on the law applicable to a proceeding before the judge, if the judge gives advance notice to the parties of the person to be consulted and the subject matter of the advice to be solicited, and affords the parties a reasonable opportunity to object and respond to the notice and to the advice received.” A is incorrect. It’s not enough that the expert consulted by the judge be impartial. The parties are entitled to know what the expert has been asked and what he or she has advised. After all, the expert may be wrong or the judge may not have communicated the issues correctly. The parties may want to respond, and they must be given the opportunity to do so. B is incorrect. What’s at issue here is not the judge’s integrity, but the very process by which adversarial issues are determined under the judicial process. C is incorrect only because it doesn’t correspond with how the Model Code of Judicial Conduct resolves this issue. Under the Model Code, it is possible for the judge to ask for advice so long as he or she tells the parties what he or she is doing and gives them the opportunity to respond.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 2.9(A)(2)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[67,68],"sourceAnswerPages":[109,110],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名法官正在审理一宗案件，其核心争议是一项复杂的商法问题。双方律师对案件的陈述未能令法官满意，法官认为自己需要额外的法律意见。该法官以前在律师事务所的合伙人是一名律师，也是涉案法律领域的专家。该律师与本案没有任何利害关系。法官认为有必要获得其意见，于是在没有告知任何当事人的情况下联系了该律师。法官向该律师咨询是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为该律师与本案没有利害关系。","B":"适当，因为法官认为，为维护司法利益需要该律师的意见。","C":"不适当，因为法官在咨询该律师之前，没有先取得本案所有当事人的书面同意。","D":"不适当，因为法官没有向当事人告知该律师的身份以及该律师所提意见的内容，也没有征求当事人的回应。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。任何一方都有权知悉并回应另一方提出的每一项事实和每一个论点。如果法官可以任意咨询任何法律信息来源，却无需说明来源并给予各方回应的权利，那么司法制度的对抗性基础就会崩溃。与此同时，在某些情况下，法官确实需要就法律问题取得意见，尤其是像本题这样，双方律师未能澄清所有问题时。为使法官能够在不破坏对抗程序的前提下取得意见，《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.9(A)(2) 允许法官“就法官面前某一诉讼程序所适用的法律，取得一名无利害关系专家的书面意见，但法官须事先将拟咨询人员及拟征求意见的事项通知各方，并给予各方合理机会对该通知及所收到的意见提出异议和作出回应。”A 不正确。法官所咨询的专家保持中立即便必要，也还不够。当事人有权知道专家被问了什么，以及专家给出了什么意见。毕竟，专家可能出错，法官也可能没有准确说明争议问题。当事人可能希望回应，因此必须给予他们回应机会。B 不正确。本题所涉及的并非法官个人是否诚信，而是对抗性争议在司法程序中以何种过程获得裁判。C 不正确，仅仅是因为它与《司法行为示范准则》处理该问题的方式不符。根据该准则，只要法官将其所做之事告知各方，并给予各方回应机会，法官就可以征求意见，并不需要各方先行书面同意。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-034","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":34,"topic":"Rule 2.13(A)","question":"Under a state law, the court’s appointment of lawyers as special masters in certain proceedings is discretionary with the court. A judge decided to appoint special masters in all such proceedings, regardless of their nature and complexity, and decided that compensation for such appointees would be at a reasonable hourly rate. The judge believed that this practice would ensure competent and impartial handling of every proceeding. The judge further decided to use published law directories to compile a list of qualified prospective appointees. Is the judge’s proposed practice proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because it results in competent and impartial handling of the proceedings.","B":"Yes, because the appointees will be compensated at a reasonable hourly rate.","C":"No, because the practice may result in unnecessary appointments.","D":"No, because the judge cannot use law directories to compile a list of qualified prospective appointees."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. “In making administrative appointments, a judge: (1) shall exercise the power of appointment impartially and on the basis of merit; and (2) shall avoid nepotism, favoritism, and unnecessary appointments.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.13(A). Here, the judge is violating the Rule by unnecessarily appointing special masters regardless of the nature and complexity of a proceeding. Additionally, the underlying theme of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct is to avoid creating the appearance of impropriety. Here, the decision of the judge to appoint special masters without reference to need arguably creates such an appearance. A is incorrect. Achieving competence and impartiality does not justify making special master appointments in all cases before a judge. As stated above, the important point is that the judge must avoid making unnecessary appointments. B is incorrect. Even if a special master charges a reasonable rate, the judge is still violating the Rule by making unnecessary appointments. D is incorrect. Nothing in the Model Code prohibits a judge from using a law directory to make appointments. In fact, using general law directories in compiling the list is a logical way for the judge to avoid the appearance of either nepotism or favoritism.","rule":"C is the correct answer. “In making administrative appointments, a judge: (1) shall exercise the power of appointment impartially and on the basis of merit; and (2) shall avoid nepotism, favoritism, and unnecessary appointments.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.13(A). Here, the judge is violating the Rule by unnecessarily appointing special masters regardless of the nature and complexity of a proceeding. Additionally, the underlying theme of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct is to avoid creating the appearance of impropriety. Here, the decision of the judge to appoint special masters without reference to need arguably creates such an appearance. A is incorrect. Achieving competence and impartiality does not justify making special master appointments in all cases before a judge. As stated above, the important point is that the judge must avoid making unnecessary appointments. B is incorrect. Even if a special master charges a reasonable rate, the judge is still violating the Rule by making unnecessary appointments. D is incorrect. Nothing in the Model Code prohibits a judge from using a law directory to make appointments. In fact, using general law directories in compiling the list is a logical way for the judge to avoid the appearance of either nepotism or favoritism.","irac":{"issue":"Is the judge’s proposed practice proper?","rule":"C is the correct answer. “In making administrative appointments, a judge: (1) shall exercise the power of appointment impartially and on the basis of merit; and (2) shall avoid nepotism, favoritism, and unnecessary appointments.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.13(A). Here, the judge is violating the Rule by unnecessarily appointing special masters regardless of the nature and complexity of a proceeding. Additionally, the underlying theme of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct is to avoid creating the appearance of impropriety. Here, the decision of the judge to appoint special masters without reference to need arguably creates such an appearance. A is incorrect. Achieving competence and impartiality does not justify making special master appointments in all cases before a judge. As stated above, the important point is that the judge must avoid making unnecessary appointments. B is incorrect. Even if a special master charges a reasonable rate, the judge is still violating the Rule by making unnecessary appointments. D is incorrect. Nothing in the Model Code prohibits a judge from using a law directory to make appointments. In fact, using general law directories in compiling the list is a logical way for the judge to avoid the appearance of either nepotism or favoritism.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. “In making administrative appointments, a judge: (1) shall exercise the power of appointment impartially and on the basis of merit; and (2) shall avoid nepotism, favoritism, and unnecessary appointments.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.13(A). Here, the judge is violating the Rule by unnecessarily appointing special masters regardless of the nature and complexity of a proceeding. Additionally, the underlying theme of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct is to avoid creating the appearance of impropriety. Here, the decision of the judge to appoint special masters without reference to need arguably creates such an appearance. A is incorrect. Achieving competence and impartiality does not justify making special master appointments in all cases before a judge. As stated above, the important point is that the judge must avoid making unnecessary appointments. B is incorrect. Even if a special master charges a reasonable rate, the judge is still violating the Rule by making unnecessary appointments. D is incorrect. Nothing in the Model Code prohibits a judge from using a law directory to make appointments. In fact, using general law directories in compiling the list is a logical way for the judge to avoid the appearance of either nepotism or favoritism.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 2.13(A)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[68],"sourceAnswerPages":[110],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"根据某州法律，法院可以酌情决定是否在某些程序中指定律师担任特别监督官。一名法官决定，在所有此类程序中一律指定特别监督官，而不考虑程序的性质和复杂程度，并决定按合理的小时费率向受任者支付报酬。法官认为，这种做法可以确保每一项程序都得到称职且公正的处理。法官还决定利用公开出版的法律名录编制一份具备资格的潜在受任者名单。法官拟采取的做法是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为该做法能使有关程序得到称职且公正的处理。","B":"适当，因为受任者将按合理的小时费率获得报酬。","C":"不适当，因为该做法可能导致不必要的任命。","D":"不适当，因为法官不得利用法律名录编制具备资格的潜在受任者名单。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。“法官在作出行政任命时：(1) 应公正并以才干为依据行使任命权；以及 (2) 应避免任人唯亲、偏袒和不必要的任命。”《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.13(A)。本题中，法官不考虑程序的性质和复杂程度而一律指定特别监督官，由此作出了不必要的任命，违反了该规则。此外，《司法行为示范准则》的一个基本主题是避免造成行为不当的观感。本题中，法官不考虑是否确有需要就指定特别监督官的决定，也可以说造成了这种观感。A 不正确。实现称职与公正的目标，并不能正当化法官在所有案件中均指定特别监督官。如上所述，关键在于法官必须避免作出不必要的任命。B 不正确。即使特别监督官收取合理费用，法官作出不必要任命的行为仍然违反规则。D 不正确。《司法行为示范准则》没有任何规定禁止法官使用法律名录进行任命。事实上，法官在编制名单时使用一般法律名录，是避免造成任人唯亲或偏袒观感的一种合乎逻辑的方式。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-035","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":35,"topic":"Model Rule 5.5(a) / Model Rule 5.4(b) / Model Rule 5.4(a)","question":"A trust company entered into the following arrangement with an attorney who was newly admitted to the bar: The trust company would provide the attorney with free office space in the building in which the trust company had its offices. If a customer of the trust company contacted the trust company about a trust or will, an officer of the trust company, who would not be a lawyer, would advise the customer and help the customer work out the details of the trust or will. The customer would be informed that the necessary documents would be prepared by the trust company’s staff. The completed documents would be submitted by an officer of the trust company to the customer for execution. The attorney, in accordance with a memorandum from the trust company’s trust officer detailing the plan, would prepare the necessary documents. The attorney would never meet with the customer and would not charge the customer for these services. The attorney would be free to engage in private practice, subject only to the limitation that the attorney could not accept employment adverse to the trust company. Is the attorney subject to discipline for entering into the arrangement with the trust company?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney is restricting his right to practice.","B":"Yes, because the attorney is aiding the trust company in the practice of law.","C":"No, because the attorney is not charging the customer for his services.","D":"No, because the attorney is not giving advice to the trust company’s customers."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. Because the attorney is newly admitted, he may not realize that he’s become party to a scheme that enables the trust company to engage in the unauthorized practice of law. Model Rule 5.5(a) says quite simply, “A lawyer shall not practice law in a jurisdiction in violation of the regulation of the legal profession in that jurisdiction, or assist another in doing so.” The attorney is enabling the trust company to discuss and “work out” the details of trusts and wills, functions that clearly fall within the parameters of law practice. The attorney is also aiding the trust company and its officer to misrepresent to the customer the way in which the documents are being prepared. The arrangement between the attorney and the trust company is in the nature of a partnership, and Model Rule 5.4(b) prevents a lawyer from forming a partnership with a nonlawyer if the function of the partnership is the practice of law. Also, even if the attorney is not sharing in the trust company’s fees directly, he is in effect doing so by getting free office space. Importantly, Model Rule 5.4(a) prohibits fee splitting with nonlawyers. Ultimately, the attorney is subject to discipline for his actions. A is incorrect. Don’t be confused by the phrase “restricting the right to practice.” It may suggest to you the restrictions against a lawyer’s right to practice that are contained in Model Rule 5.6. But Model Rule 5.6 deals with restrictions imposed by restrictive employment agreements between a lawyer and his or her partners or law firm and by settlement agreements resolving controversies between private parties. Except for these provisions, which are intended to prevent economic pressure on a lawyer either to gain or keep employment or to settle a pending matter, there are no rules preventing a lawyer from restricting the range or extent of his or her practice. C is incorrect. A lawyer is not required to charge a client for his or her services. The problem here is not the failure to charge, but the fact that the attorney is helping the trust company engage in the unauthorized practice of law. D is incorrect. The important point is that the attorney is participating in an arrangement that enables persons who are not authorized to practice law to give legal advice.","rule":"B is the correct answer. Because the attorney is newly admitted, he may not realize that he’s become party to a scheme that enables the trust company to engage in the unauthorized practice of law. Model Rule 5.5(a) says quite simply, “A lawyer shall not practice law in a jurisdiction in violation of the regulation of the legal profession in that jurisdiction, or assist another in doing so.” The attorney is enabling the trust company to discuss and “work out” the details of trusts and wills, functions that clearly fall within the parameters of law practice. The attorney is also aiding the trust company and its officer to misrepresent to the customer the way in which the documents are being prepared. The arrangement between the attorney and the trust company is in the nature of a partnership, and Model Rule 5.4(b) prevents a lawyer from forming a partnership with a nonlawyer if the function of the partnership is the practice of law. Also, even if the attorney is not sharing in the trust company’s fees directly, he is in effect doing so by getting free office space. Importantly, Model Rule 5.4(a) prohibits fee splitting with nonlawyers. Ultimately, the attorney is subject to discipline for his actions. A is incorrect. Don’t be confused by the phrase “restricting the right to practice.” It may suggest to you the restrictions against a lawyer’s right to practice that are contained in Model Rule 5.6. But Model Rule 5.6 deals with restrictions imposed by restrictive employment agreements between a lawyer and his or her partners or law firm and by settlement agreements resolving controversies between private parties. Except for these provisions, which are intended to prevent economic pressure on a lawyer either to gain or keep employment or to settle a pending matter, there are no rules preventing a lawyer from restricting the range or extent of his or her practice. C is incorrect. A lawyer is not required to charge a client for his or her services. The problem here is not the failure to charge, but the fact that the attorney is helping the trust company engage in the unauthorized practice of law. D is incorrect. The important point is that the attorney is participating in an arrangement that enables persons who are not authorized to practice law to give legal advice.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for entering into the arrangement with the trust company?","rule":"B is the correct answer. Because the attorney is newly admitted, he may not realize that he’s become party to a scheme that enables the trust company to engage in the unauthorized practice of law. Model Rule 5.5(a) says quite simply, “A lawyer shall not practice law in a jurisdiction in violation of the regulation of the legal profession in that jurisdiction, or assist another in doing so.” The attorney is enabling the trust company to discuss and “work out” the details of trusts and wills, functions that clearly fall within the parameters of law practice. The attorney is also aiding the trust company and its officer to misrepresent to the customer the way in which the documents are being prepared. The arrangement between the attorney and the trust company is in the nature of a partnership, and Model Rule 5.4(b) prevents a lawyer from forming a partnership with a nonlawyer if the function of the partnership is the practice of law. Also, even if the attorney is not sharing in the trust company’s fees directly, he is in effect doing so by getting free office space. Importantly, Model Rule 5.4(a) prohibits fee splitting with nonlawyers. Ultimately, the attorney is subject to discipline for his actions. A is incorrect. Don’t be confused by the phrase “restricting the right to practice.” It may suggest to you the restrictions against a lawyer’s right to practice that are contained in Model Rule 5.6. But Model Rule 5.6 deals with restrictions imposed by restrictive employment agreements between a lawyer and his or her partners or law firm and by settlement agreements resolving controversies between private parties. Except for these provisions, which are intended to prevent economic pressure on a lawyer either to gain or keep employment or to settle a pending matter, there are no rules preventing a lawyer from restricting the range or extent of his or her practice. C is incorrect. A lawyer is not required to charge a client for his or her services. The problem here is not the failure to charge, but the fact that the attorney is helping the trust company engage in the unauthorized practice of law. D is incorrect. The important point is that the attorney is participating in an arrangement that enables persons who are not authorized to practice law to give legal advice.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. Because the attorney is newly admitted, he may not realize that he’s become party to a scheme that enables the trust company to engage in the unauthorized practice of law. Model Rule 5.5(a) says quite simply, “A lawyer shall not practice law in a jurisdiction in violation of the regulation of the legal profession in that jurisdiction, or assist another in doing so.” The attorney is enabling the trust company to discuss and “work out” the details of trusts and wills, functions that clearly fall within the parameters of law practice. The attorney is also aiding the trust company and its officer to misrepresent to the customer the way in which the documents are being prepared. The arrangement between the attorney and the trust company is in the nature of a partnership, and Model Rule 5.4(b) prevents a lawyer from forming a partnership with a nonlawyer if the function of the partnership is the practice of law. Also, even if the attorney is not sharing in the trust company’s fees directly, he is in effect doing so by getting free office space. Importantly, Model Rule 5.4(a) prohibits fee splitting with nonlawyers. Ultimately, the attorney is subject to discipline for his actions. A is incorrect. Don’t be confused by the phrase “restricting the right to practice.” It may suggest to you the restrictions against a lawyer’s right to practice that are contained in Model Rule 5.6. But Model Rule 5.6 deals with restrictions imposed by restrictive employment agreements between a lawyer and his or her partners or law firm and by settlement agreements resolving controversies between private parties. Except for these provisions, which are intended to prevent economic pressure on a lawyer either to gain or keep employment or to settle a pending matter, there are no rules preventing a lawyer from restricting the range or extent of his or her practice. C is incorrect. A lawyer is not required to charge a client for his or her services. The problem here is not the failure to charge, but the fact that the attorney is helping the trust company engage in the unauthorized practice of law. D is incorrect. The important point is that the attorney is participating in an arrangement that enables persons who are not authorized to practice law to give legal advice.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.5(a)","Model Rule 5.4(b)","Model Rule 5.4(a)","Model Rule 5.6"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[68,69],"sourceAnswerPages":[110,111],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家信托公司与一名刚取得律师执业资格的律师达成如下安排：信托公司在其办公楼内向该律师免费提供办公场所。如果信托公司的客户就信托或遗嘱事项联系公司，一名并非律师的公司职员会向客户提供建议，并帮助客户确定信托或遗嘱的具体内容。公司会告知客户，所需文件将由信托公司的工作人员制作。完成的文件将由信托公司职员交给客户签署。该律师将按照信托业务主管说明方案的备忘录制作所需文件。律师永远不会与客户会面，也不会就这些服务向客户收费。律师可以自由从事私人执业，唯一的限制是不得接受与信托公司利益相冲突的委托。该律师因与信托公司达成这一安排，是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该律师限制了自己的执业权。","B":"是，因为该律师协助信托公司从事法律执业活动。","C":"否，因为该律师没有就其服务向客户收费。","D":"否，因为该律师没有向信托公司的客户提供建议。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。由于该律师刚取得执业资格，他可能没有意识到，自己已经参与了一个使信托公司能够擅自从事法律执业活动的安排。《职业行为示范规则》5.5(a) 直截了当地规定：“律师不得在某司法管辖区内违反该管辖区对法律职业的监管规定从事法律执业活动，也不得协助他人这样做。”该律师使信托公司得以与客户讨论并“确定”信托和遗嘱的具体内容，而这些职能显然属于法律执业的范围。该律师还协助信托公司及其职员向客户错误说明文件的制作方式。律师与信托公司之间的安排具有合伙性质，而《职业行为示范规则》5.4(b) 禁止律师与非律师组成以从事法律执业活动为目的的合伙关系。此外，即使该律师没有直接分取信托公司的费用，他通过取得免费办公场所，实际上仍从中分享了利益。需要特别注意的是，《职业行为示范规则》5.4(a) 禁止律师与非律师分配法律服务费。归根结底，该律师会因其行为而应受纪律处分。A 不正确。不要被“限制执业权”这一表述误导。它可能使人想到《职业行为示范规则》5.6 中有关限制律师执业权的规定。但规则 5.6 所处理的是律师与其合伙人或律师事务所之间的限制性雇佣协议，以及解决私人当事人之间争议的和解协议所施加的限制。这些规定旨在防止以经济压力迫使律师为获得或保住工作，或者促使其解决待决事项；除此之外，并没有规则禁止律师自行限制其执业的领域或范围。C 不正确。律师并非必须就其服务向委托人收费。本题的问题不在于没有收费，而在于该律师正在帮助信托公司擅自从事法律执业活动。D 不正确。关键在于，该律师参与了一项使无权从事法律执业的人得以提供法律意见的安排。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-036","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":36,"topic":"Model Rule 1.9 / Model Rule 1.9(a) / Model Rule 1.9(c)(1)","question":"An attorney represented a husband and wife in the purchase of a business financed by contributions from their respective separate funds. The business was jointly operated by the husband and wife after acquisition. After several years, a dispute arose over the management of the business. The husband and wife sought the attorney’s advice, and the matter was settled on the basis of an agreement drawn by the attorney and signed by the husband and wife. Later, the wife asked the attorney to represent her in litigation against her husband based on the claim that her husband was guilty of fraud and misrepresentation in the negotiations for the prior settlement agreement. The husband has not retained counsel in the matter. Is it proper for the attorney to represent the wife in this matter?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because all information relevant to the litigation was received by the attorney in the presence of both the husband and wife.","B":"Yes, because there is reason to believe the husband misled both the wife and the attorney at the time of the prior agreement.","C":"No, because the attorney had previously acted for both parties in reaching the agreement now in dispute.","D":"No, because the husband is not now represented by independent counsel."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. Here, the attorney has represented both husband and wife in a business transaction, and then in resolving a business dispute between them. Each is a former client. As such, each is entitled to the protective umbrella of Model Rule 1.9. Model Rule 1.9(a) states that a lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client, unless the former client consents after consultation. Further, a lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter may not use information relating to the former representation to the disadvantage of the former client (with exceptions not relevant here). Model Rule 1.9(c)(1). Under these facts, the attorney may not represent the wife in litigation against her husband. A is incorrect. The important point is that the attorney represented both parties in the past, not that the husband may have lied to the attorney as well. B is incorrect. The rule against representing a current client in an action adverse to the interests of a former client protects all former clients, whatever the facts. D is incorrect. Again, Model Rule 1.9 is designed to protect a former client against an adverse action on behalf of a current or prospective client. That the former client is now represented by new counsel is irrelevant to the application of the Rule.","rule":"C is the correct answer. Here, the attorney has represented both husband and wife in a business transaction, and then in resolving a business dispute between them. Each is a former client. As such, each is entitled to the protective umbrella of Model Rule 1.9. Model Rule 1.9(a) states that a lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client, unless the former client consents after consultation. Further, a lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter may not use information relating to the former representation to the disadvantage of the former client (with exceptions not relevant here). Model Rule 1.9(c)(1). Under these facts, the attorney may not represent the wife in litigation against her husband. A is incorrect. The important point is that the attorney represented both parties in the past, not that the husband may have lied to the attorney as well. B is incorrect. The rule against representing a current client in an action adverse to the interests of a former client protects all former clients, whatever the facts. D is incorrect. Again, Model Rule 1.9 is designed to protect a former client against an adverse action on behalf of a current or prospective client. That the former client is now represented by new counsel is irrelevant to the application of the Rule.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the attorney to represent the wife in this matter?","rule":"C is the correct answer. Here, the attorney has represented both husband and wife in a business transaction, and then in resolving a business dispute between them. Each is a former client. As such, each is entitled to the protective umbrella of Model Rule 1.9. Model Rule 1.9(a) states that a lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client, unless the former client consents after consultation. Further, a lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter may not use information relating to the former representation to the disadvantage of the former client (with exceptions not relevant here). Model Rule 1.9(c)(1). Under these facts, the attorney may not represent the wife in litigation against her husband. A is incorrect. The important point is that the attorney represented both parties in the past, not that the husband may have lied to the attorney as well. B is incorrect. The rule against representing a current client in an action adverse to the interests of a former client protects all former clients, whatever the facts. D is incorrect. Again, Model Rule 1.9 is designed to protect a former client against an adverse action on behalf of a current or prospective client. That the former client is now represented by new counsel is irrelevant to the application of the Rule.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. Here, the attorney has represented both husband and wife in a business transaction, and then in resolving a business dispute between them. Each is a former client. As such, each is entitled to the protective umbrella of Model Rule 1.9. Model Rule 1.9(a) states that a lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client, unless the former client consents after consultation. Further, a lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter may not use information relating to the former representation to the disadvantage of the former client (with exceptions not relevant here). Model Rule 1.9(c)(1). Under these facts, the attorney may not represent the wife in litigation against her husband. A is incorrect. The important point is that the attorney represented both parties in the past, not that the husband may have lied to the attorney as well. B is incorrect. The rule against representing a current client in an action adverse to the interests of a former client protects all former clients, whatever the facts. D is incorrect. Again, Model Rule 1.9 is designed to protect a former client against an adverse action on behalf of a current or prospective client. That the former client is now represented by new counsel is irrelevant to the application of the Rule.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.9","Model Rule 1.9(a)","Model Rule 1.9(c)(1)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[69],"sourceAnswerPages":[111,112],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师曾代表一对夫妻购买一家企业，购买资金分别来自夫妻二人的个人财产。收购后，该企业由夫妻共同经营。数年后，双方就企业管理发生争议。夫妻二人向该律师寻求意见，最终依据律师起草并由二人签署的协议解决了争议。后来，妻子要求该律师代理她起诉丈夫，理由是丈夫在先前和解协议的谈判中实施了欺诈并作出虚假陈述。丈夫目前尚未就此事聘请律师。该律师代理妻子是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为与该诉讼有关的所有信息，都是该律师在夫妻二人同时在场时获知的。","B":"适当，因为有理由认为丈夫在达成先前协议时既误导了妻子，也误导了该律师。","C":"不适当，因为该律师先前曾同时代表双方达成现在受到争议的协议。","D":"不适当，因为丈夫目前没有独立律师代理。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。本题中，该律师先是在一项商业交易中同时代表丈夫和妻子，之后又在解决二人之间的商业争议时同时代表他们。因此，夫妻二人各自都是该律师的前委托人，并各自受到《职业行为示范规则》1.9 的保护。规则 1.9(a) 规定，律师先前曾就某一事项代表委托人的，此后不得在同一事项或与其实质相关的事项中，代表与该前委托人的利益存在重大不利关系的另一人，除非前委托人在协商后同意。此外，律师先前曾就某一事项代表委托人的，不得利用与该先前代理有关的信息损害前委托人，但本题不涉及的例外情形除外。规则 1.9(c)(1)。根据本题事实，该律师不得代理妻子起诉丈夫。A 不正确。关键在于该律师过去曾同时代表双方，而不在于丈夫是否也可能欺骗了律师。B 不正确。禁止律师代表现委托人采取不利于前委托人利益的行动这一规则，会保护所有前委托人，不因具体事实而异。D 不正确。同样，规则 1.9 旨在保护前委托人，使其免受律师代表现委托人或潜在委托人采取的不利行动。前委托人现在是否已有新律师代理，不影响该规则的适用。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-037","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":37,"topic":"Model Rule 1.2(a) / Model Rule 1.4(a)(2)","question":"A client retained an attorney to recover for a personal injury. In the retainer agreement signed by the client and the attorney, the client agreed to cooperate fully and pay the attorney a contingent fee computed as a percentage of the amount of recovery after expenses: 25 percent if settled before trial, 30 percent if settled before verdict, 35 percent after verdict, and 40 percent after appeal. The attorney’s representation of the client in the matter extended over a three-year period, during which the attorney advanced a large amount for litigation expenses. After trial, the client obtained a jury verdict for an amount larger than either the attorney or the client had anticipated. However, the defendant filed an appeal based on questions of evidence and the measure of damages. Meanwhile, the defendant made an offer of settlement for approximately the amount the attorney had originally projected as reasonable to expect. The client, who was hard-pressed financially, directed the attorney to accept the offer and settle. The attorney refused, because she was confident that there was no reversible error in the trial and that the appeal was without merit. The attorney reasonably believed that the appeal was filed solely to gain negotiating advantage in settlement negotiations. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney’s percentage under the fee contract increased after appeal.","B":"Yes, because the client directed the attorney to accept the settlement offer.","C":"No, because the decision whether to settle or defend an appeal is a tactical matter for the attorney to determine.","D":"No, because evaluation of the merits of an appeal requires the exercise of independent professional judgment."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. Subject to prior limitations on the scope of representation and the lawyer’s duty not to assist a client in criminal conduct, a lawyer “shall abide by a client’s decisions concerning the objectives of representation.” Model Rule 1.2(a). Specifically, a lawyer must abide by a client’s decision to settle a matter, or, in a criminal case, the client’s decision as to the “plea to be entered, whether to waive jury trial, and whether the client will testify.” Model Rule 1.2(a). However, the lawyer has implied authorization to decide what actions are required to achieve the client’s goals, as long as the lawyer consults with the client. Model Rule 1.4(a)(2). Here, once the client decided to settle, the attorney was required to accept the offer, whether or not the attorney reasonably believed the appeal was filed to gain an advantage in the settlement negotiations. A is incorrect. Although the percentage increase gives the attorney an incentive to favor going through the appeal, this incentive does not create an impermissible conflict of interest. The important point in this question is that the client directed the attorney to settle. C is incorrect. As stated above, the client makes any decision regarding settlement. A settlement is an objective of representation. Tactical matters are things such as the extent of discovery and what witnesses will be presented at trial. D is incorrect. Although it is true that evaluating the merits of an appeal requires the exercise of independent professional judgment, this does not change the fact that the client has the ultimate authority in deciding whether to settle a matter.","rule":"B is the correct answer. Subject to prior limitations on the scope of representation and the lawyer’s duty not to assist a client in criminal conduct, a lawyer “shall abide by a client’s decisions concerning the objectives of representation.” Model Rule 1.2(a). Specifically, a lawyer must abide by a client’s decision to settle a matter, or, in a criminal case, the client’s decision as to the “plea to be entered, whether to waive jury trial, and whether the client will testify.” Model Rule 1.2(a). However, the lawyer has implied authorization to decide what actions are required to achieve the client’s goals, as long as the lawyer consults with the client. Model Rule 1.4(a)(2). Here, once the client decided to settle, the attorney was required to accept the offer, whether or not the attorney reasonably believed the appeal was filed to gain an advantage in the settlement negotiations. A is incorrect. Although the percentage increase gives the attorney an incentive to favor going through the appeal, this incentive does not create an impermissible conflict of interest. The important point in this question is that the client directed the attorney to settle. C is incorrect. As stated above, the client makes any decision regarding settlement. A settlement is an objective of representation. Tactical matters are things such as the extent of discovery and what witnesses will be presented at trial. D is incorrect. Although it is true that evaluating the merits of an appeal requires the exercise of independent professional judgment, this does not change the fact that the client has the ultimate authority in deciding whether to settle a matter.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. Subject to prior limitations on the scope of representation and the lawyer’s duty not to assist a client in criminal conduct, a lawyer “shall abide by a client’s decisions concerning the objectives of representation.” Model Rule 1.2(a). Specifically, a lawyer must abide by a client’s decision to settle a matter, or, in a criminal case, the client’s decision as to the “plea to be entered, whether to waive jury trial, and whether the client will testify.” Model Rule 1.2(a). However, the lawyer has implied authorization to decide what actions are required to achieve the client’s goals, as long as the lawyer consults with the client. Model Rule 1.4(a)(2). Here, once the client decided to settle, the attorney was required to accept the offer, whether or not the attorney reasonably believed the appeal was filed to gain an advantage in the settlement negotiations. A is incorrect. Although the percentage increase gives the attorney an incentive to favor going through the appeal, this incentive does not create an impermissible conflict of interest. The important point in this question is that the client directed the attorney to settle. C is incorrect. As stated above, the client makes any decision regarding settlement. A settlement is an objective of representation. Tactical matters are things such as the extent of discovery and what witnesses will be presented at trial. D is incorrect. Although it is true that evaluating the merits of an appeal requires the exercise of independent professional judgment, this does not change the fact that the client has the ultimate authority in deciding whether to settle a matter.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. Subject to prior limitations on the scope of representation and the lawyer’s duty not to assist a client in criminal conduct, a lawyer “shall abide by a client’s decisions concerning the objectives of representation.” Model Rule 1.2(a). Specifically, a lawyer must abide by a client’s decision to settle a matter, or, in a criminal case, the client’s decision as to the “plea to be entered, whether to waive jury trial, and whether the client will testify.” Model Rule 1.2(a). However, the lawyer has implied authorization to decide what actions are required to achieve the client’s goals, as long as the lawyer consults with the client. Model Rule 1.4(a)(2). Here, once the client decided to settle, the attorney was required to accept the offer, whether or not the attorney reasonably believed the appeal was filed to gain an advantage in the settlement negotiations. A is incorrect. Although the percentage increase gives the attorney an incentive to favor going through the appeal, this incentive does not create an impermissible conflict of interest. The important point in this question is that the client directed the attorney to settle. C is incorrect. As stated above, the client makes any decision regarding settlement. A settlement is an objective of representation. Tactical matters are things such as the extent of discovery and what witnesses will be presented at trial. D is incorrect. Although it is true that evaluating the merits of an appeal requires the exercise of independent professional judgment, this does not change the fact that the client has the ultimate authority in deciding whether to settle a matter.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.2(a)","Model Rule 1.4(a)(2)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[69,70],"sourceAnswerPages":[112],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名委托人聘请律师为其人身伤害索赔。在委托人与律师签署的聘用协议中，委托人同意充分配合，并按照扣除诉讼费用后的赔偿金额向律师支付风险代理费：开庭前和解为 25%，裁决前和解为 30%，裁决后为 35%，上诉后为 40%。该律师代理此案历时三年，其间垫付了大笔诉讼费用。审判后，委托人获得的陪审团赔偿裁决高于律师和委托人原先的预期。然而，被告以证据问题及损害赔偿计算标准为由提起上诉。同时，被告提出和解，金额约等于该律师最初预测可合理期待获得的数额。委托人经济状况十分困难，指示律师接受该要约并达成和解。律师拒绝了，因为她确信审判中不存在足以导致撤销判决的错误，上诉也没有依据。律师合理地认为，被告提起上诉的唯一目的，是在和解谈判中取得优势。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为根据收费合同，律师在上诉后的收费比例提高了。","B":"是，因为委托人已指示律师接受和解要约。","C":"否，因为是否和解或者是否就上诉进行抗辩，属于应由律师决定的诉讼策略事项。","D":"否，因为评估上诉是否有依据，需要律师行使独立的专业判断。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。在代理范围事先受到限制以及律师不得协助委托人实施犯罪行为等约束下，律师“应遵从委托人就代理目标所作的决定”。《职业行为示范规则》1.2(a)。具体而言，律师必须遵从委托人是否就事项达成和解的决定；在刑事案件中，还必须遵从委托人关于“作何种答辩、是否放弃陪审团审判以及委托人是否作证”的决定。规则 1.2(a)。不过，只要律师与委托人协商，律师即获得默示授权，可以决定为实现委托人目标所需采取的行动。规则 1.4(a)(2)。本题中，委托人一旦决定和解，律师就必须接受该要约，无论律师是否合理地认为对方提起上诉是为了在和解谈判中取得优势。A 不正确。收费比例的提高虽然使律师有动机倾向于让案件进入上诉程序，但这种动机本身并未造成规则禁止的利益冲突。本题的关键是，委托人已指示律师达成和解。C 不正确。如上所述，任何有关和解的决定都由委托人作出。和解属于代理目标；诉讼策略事项则包括证据开示的范围以及在审判中提出哪些证人等。D 不正确。评估上诉是否有依据确实需要律师行使独立的专业判断，但这并不改变委托人对是否和解享有最终决定权这一事实。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-038","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":38,"topic":"Model Rule 7.2 / Model Rule 7.1","question":"An attorney advertises on the local television station. In the advertisements, a professional actor says: “Do you need a lawyer? Call 555-555-5555. Her fees might be lower than you think.” The attorney approved the prerecorded advertisement and is keeping in her office files a copy of the recording of the actual transmission and a record of when each transmission was made. For those responding to the advertisement, she charges for the initial consultation. Is the advertisement proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes.","B":"No, because it is not clear the attorney’s fees are lower than those generally charged in the area where she practices.","C":"No, because she used a professional actor for the television advertisement.","D":"No, because she makes a charge for the initial consultation."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may advertise his or her services in the same media as any other professional or merchant. Model Rule 7.2. There are, however, special limitations that apply only to lawyers. The most important is embodied in Model Rule 7.1. Model Rule 7.1 requires that the lawyer not make a misleading statement about himself or herself or his or her services. A statement is misleading if it contains a material misrepresentation of fact or law, omits a fact that will make the statement not misleading, is likely to create unjustified expectations about the results the lawyer is likely to achieve, or compares the lawyer’s services with other lawyers’ services without substantiation in fact. Here, the ad televised by the attorney meets all of these requirements on its face. Even the last sentence is not objectionable. It suggests only that the attorney may have a relatively low fee schedule, or that potential clients may have a mistaken notion about the level of legal fees in the attorney’s area. Also, the attorney is complying with the requirement to maintain copies of all of her ads. B is incorrect. The ad does not state that the attorney’s fees are lower than those generally charged in her area, only that the potential client may have a mistaken notion about legal fees in general. Further, a statement that the attorney’s fees were lower than those generally charged in her area would not be improper if the statement could be supported by the facts. C is incorrect. Nothing in the Rules prevents a lawyer from using professional actors to read a radio or TV ad. The actor is obviously not pretending to be the attorney; he is simply conveying her message in the same way as in other TV ads. If a lawyer did instruct an actor to pretend to be the lawyer when reading the ad, that, of course, would represent a material misrepresentation of fact and could lead to discipline. D is incorrect. Nothing in the Rules prohibits a lawyer from charging for an initial consultation and nothing in the ad suggests that the attorney will not charge for the consultation.","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may advertise his or her services in the same media as any other professional or merchant. Model Rule 7.2. There are, however, special limitations that apply only to lawyers. The most important is embodied in Model Rule 7.1. Model Rule 7.1 requires that the lawyer not make a misleading statement about himself or herself or his or her services. A statement is misleading if it contains a material misrepresentation of fact or law, omits a fact that will make the statement not misleading, is likely to create unjustified expectations about the results the lawyer is likely to achieve, or compares the lawyer’s services with other lawyers’ services without substantiation in fact. Here, the ad televised by the attorney meets all of these requirements on its face. Even the last sentence is not objectionable. It suggests only that the attorney may have a relatively low fee schedule, or that potential clients may have a mistaken notion about the level of legal fees in the attorney’s area. Also, the attorney is complying with the requirement to maintain copies of all of her ads. B is incorrect. The ad does not state that the attorney’s fees are lower than those generally charged in her area, only that the potential client may have a mistaken notion about legal fees in general. Further, a statement that the attorney’s fees were lower than those generally charged in her area would not be improper if the statement could be supported by the facts. C is incorrect. Nothing in the Rules prevents a lawyer from using professional actors to read a radio or TV ad. The actor is obviously not pretending to be the attorney; he is simply conveying her message in the same way as in other TV ads. If a lawyer did instruct an actor to pretend to be the lawyer when reading the ad, that, of course, would represent a material misrepresentation of fact and could lead to discipline. D is incorrect. Nothing in the Rules prohibits a lawyer from charging for an initial consultation and nothing in the ad suggests that the attorney will not charge for the consultation.","irac":{"issue":"Is the advertisement proper?","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may advertise his or her services in the same media as any other professional or merchant. Model Rule 7.2. There are, however, special limitations that apply only to lawyers. The most important is embodied in Model Rule 7.1. Model Rule 7.1 requires that the lawyer not make a misleading statement about himself or herself or his or her services. A statement is misleading if it contains a material misrepresentation of fact or law, omits a fact that will make the statement not misleading, is likely to create unjustified expectations about the results the lawyer is likely to achieve, or compares the lawyer’s services with other lawyers’ services without substantiation in fact. Here, the ad televised by the attorney meets all of these requirements on its face. Even the last sentence is not objectionable. It suggests only that the attorney may have a relatively low fee schedule, or that potential clients may have a mistaken notion about the level of legal fees in the attorney’s area. Also, the attorney is complying with the requirement to maintain copies of all of her ads. B is incorrect. The ad does not state that the attorney’s fees are lower than those generally charged in her area, only that the potential client may have a mistaken notion about legal fees in general. Further, a statement that the attorney’s fees were lower than those generally charged in her area would not be improper if the statement could be supported by the facts. C is incorrect. Nothing in the Rules prevents a lawyer from using professional actors to read a radio or TV ad. The actor is obviously not pretending to be the attorney; he is simply conveying her message in the same way as in other TV ads. If a lawyer did instruct an actor to pretend to be the lawyer when reading the ad, that, of course, would represent a material misrepresentation of fact and could lead to discipline. D is incorrect. Nothing in the Rules prohibits a lawyer from charging for an initial consultation and nothing in the ad suggests that the attorney will not charge for the consultation.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may advertise his or her services in the same media as any other professional or merchant. Model Rule 7.2. There are, however, special limitations that apply only to lawyers. The most important is embodied in Model Rule 7.1. Model Rule 7.1 requires that the lawyer not make a misleading statement about himself or herself or his or her services. A statement is misleading if it contains a material misrepresentation of fact or law, omits a fact that will make the statement not misleading, is likely to create unjustified expectations about the results the lawyer is likely to achieve, or compares the lawyer’s services with other lawyers’ services without substantiation in fact. Here, the ad televised by the attorney meets all of these requirements on its face. Even the last sentence is not objectionable. It suggests only that the attorney may have a relatively low fee schedule, or that potential clients may have a mistaken notion about the level of legal fees in the attorney’s area. Also, the attorney is complying with the requirement to maintain copies of all of her ads. B is incorrect. The ad does not state that the attorney’s fees are lower than those generally charged in her area, only that the potential client may have a mistaken notion about legal fees in general. Further, a statement that the attorney’s fees were lower than those generally charged in her area would not be improper if the statement could be supported by the facts. C is incorrect. Nothing in the Rules prevents a lawyer from using professional actors to read a radio or TV ad. The actor is obviously not pretending to be the attorney; he is simply conveying her message in the same way as in other TV ads. If a lawyer did instruct an actor to pretend to be the lawyer when reading the ad, that, of course, would represent a material misrepresentation of fact and could lead to discipline. D is incorrect. Nothing in the Rules prohibits a lawyer from charging for an initial consultation and nothing in the ad suggests that the attorney will not charge for the consultation.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.2","Model Rule 7.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[70,71],"sourceAnswerPages":[113],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师在当地电视台投放广告。在广告中，一名职业演员说道：“您需要律师吗？请拨打 555-555-5555。她的收费可能比您想象的低。”律师批准了这则预先录制的广告，并在办公室档案中保存了实际播出内容的录音副本以及每次播出的时间记录。对于因广告联系她的人，她会收取首次咨询费。该广告是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当。","B":"不适当，因为广告没有明确说明该律师的收费低于其执业地区通常收取的费用。","C":"不适当，因为她在电视广告中使用了职业演员。","D":"不适当，因为她会收取首次咨询费。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。律师可以像任何其他专业人士或商家一样，通过相同媒体为自己的服务做广告。《职业行为示范规则》7.2。不过，仍有一些只适用于律师的特殊限制，其中最重要的体现在规则 7.1。规则 7.1 要求律师不得对自己或其服务作出具有误导性的陈述。如果一项陈述对事实或法律作出重大虚假陈述，遗漏了为避免误导所必需的事实，可能使人对律师可能取得的结果产生没有正当依据的期待，或者在没有事实依据的情况下将该律师的服务与其他律师的服务进行比较，该陈述即具有误导性。本题中，该律师播出的广告从表面上看符合所有这些要求。甚至最后一句也没有问题。它只是暗示该律师的收费标准可能相对较低，或者潜在委托人对该律师所在地区的法律服务费水平可能存在错误认识。此外，该律师也遵守了保存其全部广告副本的要求。B 不正确。广告并未声称该律师的收费低于其所在地区通常收取的费用，而只是说潜在委托人可能对一般法律服务费存在错误认识。而且，如果事实能够支持“该律师的收费低于所在地区通常水平”这一陈述，那么作出该陈述也并非不当。C 不正确。规则没有禁止律师聘用职业演员播读广播或电视广告。该演员显然没有假装自己就是律师；他只是像其他电视广告中的演员一样传达律师的信息。当然，如果律师指示演员在播读广告时冒充律师本人，那就会构成对事实的重大虚假陈述，并可能导致纪律处分。D 不正确。规则没有禁止律师收取首次咨询费，广告也没有暗示该律师不会收取这项费用。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-039","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":39,"topic":"Model Rule 1.6 / Model Rule 1.6(b)(6) / Rule 1.6","question":"An attorney represented both the owner of an art gallery and a publisher. The gallery owner and the publisher each made a practice of paying the attorney’s fees in cash. The attorney received separate cash payments from the gallery owner and the publisher on the same day. Each payment consisted of 10 $100 bills, which the attorney immediately deposited in her bank account. One week later, the attorney was contacted by United States Treasury agents, who informed her that four of the bills had been identified as counterfeit. The agents did not accuse the attorney of knowingly passing the counterfeit money, but asked her who had given her the bills. The attorney was subpoenaed to testify before a grand jury and was asked who could have given her the counterfeit money. Is it proper for the attorney to provide the grand jury with the names of the gallery owner and the publisher?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because negotiation of a counterfeit bill is a criminal act.","B":"Yes, because under the circumstances neither client’s identity is privileged.","C":"No, because counterfeiting is not a crime that involves an imminent threat of death or serious bodily harm.","D":"No, because the attorney has no way of knowing which of the two clients gave her the counterfeit bills."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. Although Model Rule 1.6 protects a client’s confidential information under the attorney-client privilege, one of the exceptions is that “a lawyer may reveal information relating to the representation of a client to the extent the lawyer reasonably believes necessary . . . to comply with other law or a court order.” Model Rule 1.6(b)(6). In this case, the attorney has been subpoenaed to testify before a grand jury. Importantly, the clients’ names may be confidential under Rule 1.6, but they are not protected by the attorney-client privilege. The attorney-client privilege protects only confidential communications between a lawyer and a client. It does not protect clients’ identities unless disclosing their identities will implicitly reveal their confidential communications, which does not appear to be the case here. Thus, the attorney may provide the grand jury with the clients’ names. A is incorrect. Because the information isn’t protected by the attorney-client privilege, it is irrelevant that negotiating a counterfeit bill is a crime. C is incorrect. As stated above, the information is not confidential under Model Rule 1.6, so it is irrelevant whether it would fall under the exception that allows the disclosure of confidences to prevent imminent death or substantial bodily harm. D is incorrect. As stated above, the information in this case is not privileged. However, if the attorney’s information in this case were in fact privileged, it is true that the attorney could not disclose it under the “crime-fraud” exception, even though passing counterfeit money is a crime, because the attorney does not know which client paid with counterfeit bills, and also does not know whether the client who did so was aware the bills were counterfeit. Thus, if the attorney were to reveal confidential information, the attorney would be violating the Rule as to at least one innocent client who does not fall under the exception.","rule":"B is the correct answer. Although Model Rule 1.6 protects a client’s confidential information under the attorney-client privilege, one of the exceptions is that “a lawyer may reveal information relating to the representation of a client to the extent the lawyer reasonably believes necessary . . . to comply with other law or a court order.” Model Rule 1.6(b)(6). In this case, the attorney has been subpoenaed to testify before a grand jury. Importantly, the clients’ names may be confidential under Rule 1.6, but they are not protected by the attorney-client privilege. The attorney-client privilege protects only confidential communications between a lawyer and a client. It does not protect clients’ identities unless disclosing their identities will implicitly reveal their confidential communications, which does not appear to be the case here. Thus, the attorney may provide the grand jury with the clients’ names. A is incorrect. Because the information isn’t protected by the attorney-client privilege, it is irrelevant that negotiating a counterfeit bill is a crime. C is incorrect. As stated above, the information is not confidential under Model Rule 1.6, so it is irrelevant whether it would fall under the exception that allows the disclosure of confidences to prevent imminent death or substantial bodily harm. D is incorrect. As stated above, the information in this case is not privileged. However, if the attorney’s information in this case were in fact privileged, it is true that the attorney could not disclose it under the “crime-fraud” exception, even though passing counterfeit money is a crime, because the attorney does not know which client paid with counterfeit bills, and also does not know whether the client who did so was aware the bills were counterfeit. Thus, if the attorney were to reveal confidential information, the attorney would be violating the Rule as to at least one innocent client who does not fall under the exception.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the attorney to provide the grand jury with the names of the gallery owner and the publisher?","rule":"B is the correct answer. Although Model Rule 1.6 protects a client’s confidential information under the attorney-client privilege, one of the exceptions is that “a lawyer may reveal information relating to the representation of a client to the extent the lawyer reasonably believes necessary . . . to comply with other law or a court order.” Model Rule 1.6(b)(6). In this case, the attorney has been subpoenaed to testify before a grand jury. Importantly, the clients’ names may be confidential under Rule 1.6, but they are not protected by the attorney-client privilege. The attorney-client privilege protects only confidential communications between a lawyer and a client. It does not protect clients’ identities unless disclosing their identities will implicitly reveal their confidential communications, which does not appear to be the case here. Thus, the attorney may provide the grand jury with the clients’ names. A is incorrect. Because the information isn’t protected by the attorney-client privilege, it is irrelevant that negotiating a counterfeit bill is a crime. C is incorrect. As stated above, the information is not confidential under Model Rule 1.6, so it is irrelevant whether it would fall under the exception that allows the disclosure of confidences to prevent imminent death or substantial bodily harm. D is incorrect. As stated above, the information in this case is not privileged. However, if the attorney’s information in this case were in fact privileged, it is true that the attorney could not disclose it under the “crime-fraud” exception, even though passing counterfeit money is a crime, because the attorney does not know which client paid with counterfeit bills, and also does not know whether the client who did so was aware the bills were counterfeit. Thus, if the attorney were to reveal confidential information, the attorney would be violating the Rule as to at least one innocent client who does not fall under the exception.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. Although Model Rule 1.6 protects a client’s confidential information under the attorney-client privilege, one of the exceptions is that “a lawyer may reveal information relating to the representation of a client to the extent the lawyer reasonably believes necessary . . . to comply with other law or a court order.” Model Rule 1.6(b)(6). In this case, the attorney has been subpoenaed to testify before a grand jury. Importantly, the clients’ names may be confidential under Rule 1.6, but they are not protected by the attorney-client privilege. The attorney-client privilege protects only confidential communications between a lawyer and a client. It does not protect clients’ identities unless disclosing their identities will implicitly reveal their confidential communications, which does not appear to be the case here. Thus, the attorney may provide the grand jury with the clients’ names. A is incorrect. Because the information isn’t protected by the attorney-client privilege, it is irrelevant that negotiating a counterfeit bill is a crime. C is incorrect. As stated above, the information is not confidential under Model Rule 1.6, so it is irrelevant whether it would fall under the exception that allows the disclosure of confidences to prevent imminent death or substantial bodily harm. D is incorrect. As stated above, the information in this case is not privileged. However, if the attorney’s information in this case were in fact privileged, it is true that the attorney could not disclose it under the “crime-fraud” exception, even though passing counterfeit money is a crime, because the attorney does not know which client paid with counterfeit bills, and also does not know whether the client who did so was aware the bills were counterfeit. Thus, if the attorney were to reveal confidential information, the attorney would be violating the Rule as to at least one innocent client who does not fall under the exception.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.6","Model Rule 1.6(b)(6)","Rule 1.6"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[71],"sourceAnswerPages":[113,114],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师同时代理一家画廊的所有者和一名出版商。画廊所有者和出版商都习惯以现金支付律师费。某日，该律师分别收到画廊所有者和出版商支付的现金。每笔款项均由 10 张面值 100 美元的钞票组成，律师立即将其存入自己的银行账户。一周后，美国财政部的执法人员联系该律师，告知她其中有四张钞票已被认定为假钞。执法人员没有指控律师明知是假钞仍将其支付流通，而是询问这些钞票由谁交给她。该律师随后被传唤到大陪审团作证，并被问及谁可能把假钞交给了她。该律师向大陪审团提供画廊所有者和出版商的姓名是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为使用假钞进行支付属于犯罪行为。","B":"适当，因为在本题情形下，两名委托人的身份均不受特权保护。","C":"不适当，因为伪造货币并非涉及迫在眉睫的死亡或严重身体伤害威胁的犯罪。","D":"不适当，因为该律师无法知道两名委托人中究竟是谁把假钞交给了她。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。虽然《职业行为示范规则》1.6 保护处于律师—委托人特权范围内的委托人保密信息，但其中一项例外规定，“律师可以在其合理认为为……遵守其他法律或法院命令所必需的范围内，披露与代理有关的信息。”规则 1.6(b)(6)。本题中，该律师已被传唤到大陪审团作证。需要特别注意的是，委托人的姓名可能属于规则 1.6 所涵盖的保密信息，但并不受律师—委托人特权保护。律师—委托人特权只保护律师与委托人之间的保密沟通；除非披露委托人身份会同时默示披露其保密沟通，否则该特权不保护委托人身份，而本题显然不存在这种情形。因此，该律师可以向大陪审团提供两名委托人的姓名。A 不正确。由于该信息不受律师—委托人特权保护，使用假钞支付是否构成犯罪并不重要。C 不正确。如上所述，该信息不受规则 1.6 的保密义务限制，因此，它是否落入允许为防止迫在眉睫的死亡或重大身体伤害而披露秘密的例外，并不重要。D 不正确。如上所述，本题中的信息不受特权保护。不过，如果该律师掌握的信息事实上受特权保护，那么即使使用假钞属于犯罪，她确实也不能依据“犯罪—欺诈”例外披露信息，因为她不知道是哪一名委托人使用假钞付款，也不知道使用假钞的委托人当时是否知情。因此，如果律师披露的是保密信息，她至少会对一名无辜且不属于该例外范围的委托人违反规则。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-040","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":40,"topic":"Model Rule 1.7(a) / Model Rule 1.7(b) / Rule 1.6","question":"A corporation has applied to a bank for a $900,000 loan to be secured by a lien on the corporation’s inventory. The inventory, consisting of small items, constantly turns over. The security documents are complex and if improperly drawn they could result in an invalid lien. The bank has approved the loan on the condition that the corporation and the bank jointly retain an attorney to prepare the necessary security instruments and that the corporation pay the attorney’s fees. Both the corporation and the bank gave informed consent in writing to the attorney’s representation of both parties. This arrangement is customary in the city in which the attorney’s law office and the bank are located. It is obvious to the attorney that he can adequately represent the interests of both the corporation and the bank. Is it proper for the attorney to prepare the security documents under these circumstances?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because both the bank and the corporation have given their informed consent to the arrangement.","B":"Yes, because the arrangement is customary in the community.","C":"No, because the attorney’s fees are being paid by the corporation, not the bank.","D":"No, because the corporation and the bank have differing interests."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.7(a), a lawyer shall not represent a client if the representation of one client will be directly adverse to the representation of another client. Here, the attorney has a conflict of interest arising from the representation of two clients in the same transaction —the bank, which is the lender, and the corporation, which is the borrower. However, Model Rule 1.7(b) allows the attorney to represent both clients if he reasonably believes that he will be able to provide competent and diligent representation to each client, the representation is not prohibited by law, the representation does not involve the assertion of a claim by one client against the other, and each client gives informed consent in writing. Here, because the proposed representation meets all the requirements of Model Rule 1.7(b), the joint representation is proper. B is incorrect. Because the attorney has a conflict of interest under Model Rule 1.7(a), the fact that the arrangement is customary in the community is irrelevant. The important question is whether the proposed representation meets the requirements of Model Rule 1.7(b). C is incorrect. “A lawyer shall not accept compensation for representing a client from other than the client unless: (1) the client gives informed consent; (2) there is no interference with the lawyer’s independence of professional judgment or with the client-lawyer relationship; and (3) information relating to representation of a client is protected as required by Rule 1.6.” Model Rule 1.8(f). The Rule recognizes the fact that third-party payers frequently have interests that differ from the client, such as minimizing the amount spent on the representation. The classic example of an arrangement violating this Rule is when a defendant’s insurance company is responsible for the ultimate damage claim and is also paying the defendant’s legal defense expenses. Here, in a joint representation such as this one, there is no restriction against the payment of legal fees by one of the two clients with informed consent. Model Rule 1.8(f). D is incorrect. Although there is a conflict of interest, the attorney can represent clients with differing interests as long as the requirements of Model Rule 1.7(b) are met.","rule":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.7(a), a lawyer shall not represent a client if the representation of one client will be directly adverse to the representation of another client. Here, the attorney has a conflict of interest arising from the representation of two clients in the same transaction —the bank, which is the lender, and the corporation, which is the borrower. However, Model Rule 1.7(b) allows the attorney to represent both clients if he reasonably believes that he will be able to provide competent and diligent representation to each client, the representation is not prohibited by law, the representation does not involve the assertion of a claim by one client against the other, and each client gives informed consent in writing. Here, because the proposed representation meets all the requirements of Model Rule 1.7(b), the joint representation is proper. B is incorrect. Because the attorney has a conflict of interest under Model Rule 1.7(a), the fact that the arrangement is customary in the community is irrelevant. The important question is whether the proposed representation meets the requirements of Model Rule 1.7(b). C is incorrect. “A lawyer shall not accept compensation for representing a client from other than the client unless: (1) the client gives informed consent; (2) there is no interference with the lawyer’s independence of professional judgment or with the client-lawyer relationship; and (3) information relating to representation of a client is protected as required by Rule 1.6.” Model Rule 1.8(f). The Rule recognizes the fact that third-party payers frequently have interests that differ from the client, such as minimizing the amount spent on the representation. The classic example of an arrangement violating this Rule is when a defendant’s insurance company is responsible for the ultimate damage claim and is also paying the defendant’s legal defense expenses. Here, in a joint representation such as this one, there is no restriction against the payment of legal fees by one of the two clients with informed consent. Model Rule 1.8(f). D is incorrect. Although there is a conflict of interest, the attorney can represent clients with differing interests as long as the requirements of Model Rule 1.7(b) are met.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the attorney to prepare the security documents under these circumstances?","rule":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.7(a), a lawyer shall not represent a client if the representation of one client will be directly adverse to the representation of another client. Here, the attorney has a conflict of interest arising from the representation of two clients in the same transaction —the bank, which is the lender, and the corporation, which is the borrower. However, Model Rule 1.7(b) allows the attorney to represent both clients if he reasonably believes that he will be able to provide competent and diligent representation to each client, the representation is not prohibited by law, the representation does not involve the assertion of a claim by one client against the other, and each client gives informed consent in writing. Here, because the proposed representation meets all the requirements of Model Rule 1.7(b), the joint representation is proper. B is incorrect. Because the attorney has a conflict of interest under Model Rule 1.7(a), the fact that the arrangement is customary in the community is irrelevant. The important question is whether the proposed representation meets the requirements of Model Rule 1.7(b). C is incorrect. “A lawyer shall not accept compensation for representing a client from other than the client unless: (1) the client gives informed consent; (2) there is no interference with the lawyer’s independence of professional judgment or with the client-lawyer relationship; and (3) information relating to representation of a client is protected as required by Rule 1.6.” Model Rule 1.8(f). The Rule recognizes the fact that third-party payers frequently have interests that differ from the client, such as minimizing the amount spent on the representation. The classic example of an arrangement violating this Rule is when a defendant’s insurance company is responsible for the ultimate damage claim and is also paying the defendant’s legal defense expenses. Here, in a joint representation such as this one, there is no restriction against the payment of legal fees by one of the two clients with informed consent. Model Rule 1.8(f). D is incorrect. Although there is a conflict of interest, the attorney can represent clients with differing interests as long as the requirements of Model Rule 1.7(b) are met.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.7(a), a lawyer shall not represent a client if the representation of one client will be directly adverse to the representation of another client. Here, the attorney has a conflict of interest arising from the representation of two clients in the same transaction —the bank, which is the lender, and the corporation, which is the borrower. However, Model Rule 1.7(b) allows the attorney to represent both clients if he reasonably believes that he will be able to provide competent and diligent representation to each client, the representation is not prohibited by law, the representation does not involve the assertion of a claim by one client against the other, and each client gives informed consent in writing. Here, because the proposed representation meets all the requirements of Model Rule 1.7(b), the joint representation is proper. B is incorrect. Because the attorney has a conflict of interest under Model Rule 1.7(a), the fact that the arrangement is customary in the community is irrelevant. The important question is whether the proposed representation meets the requirements of Model Rule 1.7(b). C is incorrect. “A lawyer shall not accept compensation for representing a client from other than the client unless: (1) the client gives informed consent; (2) there is no interference with the lawyer’s independence of professional judgment or with the client-lawyer relationship; and (3) information relating to representation of a client is protected as required by Rule 1.6.” Model Rule 1.8(f). The Rule recognizes the fact that third-party payers frequently have interests that differ from the client, such as minimizing the amount spent on the representation. The classic example of an arrangement violating this Rule is when a defendant’s insurance company is responsible for the ultimate damage claim and is also paying the defendant’s legal defense expenses. Here, in a joint representation such as this one, there is no restriction against the payment of legal fees by one of the two clients with informed consent. Model Rule 1.8(f). D is incorrect. Although there is a conflict of interest, the attorney can represent clients with differing interests as long as the requirements of Model Rule 1.7(b) are met.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.7(a)","Model Rule 1.7(b)","Rule 1.6","Model Rule 1.8(f)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[71,72],"sourceAnswerPages":[114,115],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家公司向银行申请 900,000 美元贷款，并拟以公司存货上的担保权作为担保。这些存货由小件物品构成，周转频繁。担保文件十分复杂，如起草不当，可能导致担保权无效。银行批准了贷款，但条件是公司与银行共同聘请一名律师制作所需担保文书，并由公司支付律师费。公司和银行均以书面形式对律师同时代理双方作出知情同意。这种安排在律师事务所和银行所在地的城市十分常见。该律师显然能够充分代理公司和银行双方的利益。在这些情况下，由该律师制作担保文件是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为银行和公司均已对这一安排作出知情同意。","B":"适当，因为这一安排是当地惯例。","C":"不适当，因为律师费由公司而非银行支付。","D":"不适当，因为公司和银行的利益并不一致。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。根据《职业行为示范规则》1.7(a)，如果代理一名委托人会与另一名委托人的利益直接冲突，律师不得接受该代理。本题中，该律师同时代理同一交易中的两名委托人——作为贷款人的银行和作为借款人的公司，因而存在利益冲突。不过，规则 1.7(b) 允许律师在以下条件下同时代理双方：律师合理地相信其能够向每一名委托人提供称职且勤勉的代理；该代理不为法律所禁止；该代理不涉及一名委托人向另一名委托人主张权利；并且每一名委托人均以书面形式作出知情同意。本题拟议的代理满足规则 1.7(b) 的全部要求，因此共同代理是适当的。B 不正确。由于该律师在规则 1.7(a) 下存在利益冲突，这种安排是否属于当地惯例并不重要。关键问题是拟议代理是否符合规则 1.7(b) 的要求。C 不正确。“律师不得从委托人以外的人处接受代理报酬，除非：(1) 委托人作出知情同意；(2) 该付款不干扰律师的独立专业判断或律师—委托人关系；以及 (3) 与代理有关的信息按照规则 1.6 的要求受到保护。”规则 1.8(f)。该规则承认第三方付款人的利益经常与委托人不同，例如付款人可能希望尽量减少代理费用。违反该规则的经典例子是，被告的保险公司既负责最终的损害赔偿，又支付被告的法律抗辩费用。本题属于这种共同代理，在取得知情同意后，由两名委托人中的一方支付律师费并不受禁止。规则 1.8(f)。D 不正确。虽然确有利益冲突，但只要满足规则 1.7(b) 的要求，律师仍可代理利益不同的委托人。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-041","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":41,"topic":"Model Rule 1.2(d) / Model Rule 3.3(a)(3)","question":"An attorney agreed to represent a client who was being prosecuted for driving while intoxicated in a jurisdiction in which there is an increased penalty for a second offense. The client told the attorney that his driver’s license had been obtained under an assumed name because his prior license had been suspended for driving while under the influence of alcohol. Obtaining a license in this way was a felony. The client asked the attorney not to disclose his true name during the course of the representation and told the attorney that, if called as a witness, he would give his assumed name. The attorney informed the client that, in order to properly defend the case, the attorney must call the client as a witness. The attorney called the client as a witness and, in response to the attorney’s question “What is your name?” the client gave his assumed name. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney knowingly used false testimony.","B":"Yes, because the client committed a felony when he obtained the driver’s license under an assumed name.","C":"No, because the attorney’s knowledge of the client’s true name was obtained during the course of representation.","D":"No, because the client’s true name was not an issue in the proceeding."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may not counsel or assist a client in conduct that is criminal or fraudulent. Model Rule 1.2(d). Similarly, a lawyer has a duty under Model Rule 3.3(a)(3) not to use false evidence. When the attorney asked the client for his name, he knew that the response would be a falsehood that would mislead the court in its judgment of the client’s offense. B is incorrect. It’s immaterial to the attorney’s duty to the court whether the client committed a felony or a lesser crime. The point is the testimony would mislead the court into administering a lighter sentence than the law required. C is incorrect. While the information would ordinarily be protected because it was disclosed to the attorney as a confidence during the course of representation, it cannot be protected if it results in the presentation of testimony that the attorney knows is false. D is incorrect. The client’s name is an issue in the trial. If the client’s true name is used, he’s subject to the heavier penalty. If the court doesn’t learn his true name, the client gets the lighter penalty.","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may not counsel or assist a client in conduct that is criminal or fraudulent. Model Rule 1.2(d). Similarly, a lawyer has a duty under Model Rule 3.3(a)(3) not to use false evidence. When the attorney asked the client for his name, he knew that the response would be a falsehood that would mislead the court in its judgment of the client’s offense. B is incorrect. It’s immaterial to the attorney’s duty to the court whether the client committed a felony or a lesser crime. The point is the testimony would mislead the court into administering a lighter sentence than the law required. C is incorrect. While the information would ordinarily be protected because it was disclosed to the attorney as a confidence during the course of representation, it cannot be protected if it results in the presentation of testimony that the attorney knows is false. D is incorrect. The client’s name is an issue in the trial. If the client’s true name is used, he’s subject to the heavier penalty. If the court doesn’t learn his true name, the client gets the lighter penalty.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may not counsel or assist a client in conduct that is criminal or fraudulent. Model Rule 1.2(d). Similarly, a lawyer has a duty under Model Rule 3.3(a)(3) not to use false evidence. When the attorney asked the client for his name, he knew that the response would be a falsehood that would mislead the court in its judgment of the client’s offense. B is incorrect. It’s immaterial to the attorney’s duty to the court whether the client committed a felony or a lesser crime. The point is the testimony would mislead the court into administering a lighter sentence than the law required. C is incorrect. While the information would ordinarily be protected because it was disclosed to the attorney as a confidence during the course of representation, it cannot be protected if it results in the presentation of testimony that the attorney knows is false. D is incorrect. The client’s name is an issue in the trial. If the client’s true name is used, he’s subject to the heavier penalty. If the court doesn’t learn his true name, the client gets the lighter penalty.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may not counsel or assist a client in conduct that is criminal or fraudulent. Model Rule 1.2(d). Similarly, a lawyer has a duty under Model Rule 3.3(a)(3) not to use false evidence. When the attorney asked the client for his name, he knew that the response would be a falsehood that would mislead the court in its judgment of the client’s offense. B is incorrect. It’s immaterial to the attorney’s duty to the court whether the client committed a felony or a lesser crime. The point is the testimony would mislead the court into administering a lighter sentence than the law required. C is incorrect. While the information would ordinarily be protected because it was disclosed to the attorney as a confidence during the course of representation, it cannot be protected if it results in the presentation of testimony that the attorney knows is false. D is incorrect. The client’s name is an issue in the trial. If the client’s true name is used, he’s subject to the heavier penalty. If the court doesn’t learn his true name, the client gets the lighter penalty.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.2(d)","Model Rule 3.3(a)(3)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[72,73],"sourceAnswerPages":[115,116],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师同意代理一名因醉酒驾驶而被起诉的委托人。在该司法管辖区，第二次实施此类违法行为会受到加重处罚。委托人告诉律师，他是用假名取得驾驶执照的，因为他先前的执照已因酒后驾驶被吊销。以这种方式取得执照构成重罪。委托人要求律师在代理过程中不要披露他的真实姓名，并告诉律师，如果被传唤作证，他会说出自己的假名。律师告知委托人，为妥善进行抗辩，必须让委托人出庭作证。律师传唤委托人作证，并问道：“你叫什么名字？”委托人回答了自己的假名。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该律师明知证言虚假仍加以使用。","B":"是，因为委托人以假名取得驾驶执照时实施了重罪。","C":"否，因为律师是在代理过程中得知委托人真实姓名的。","D":"否，因为委托人的真实姓名不是该诉讼程序中的争议问题。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。律师不得建议或协助委托人实施犯罪或欺诈行为。《职业行为示范规则》1.2(d)。同样，根据规则 3.3(a)(3)，律师负有不得使用虚假证据的义务。律师询问委托人姓名时，明知其回答将是虚假的，并会在法院确定应对委托人的违法行为施加何种处罚时误导法院。B 不正确。就律师对法院承担的义务而言，委托人先前实施的是重罪还是较轻罪行并不重要。关键在于，该证言会误导法院，使法院施加比法律要求更轻的刑罚。C 不正确。该信息通常会因委托人在代理过程中将其作为秘密告知律师而受到保护，但如果保护该信息会导致律师提出其明知虚假的证言，就不得继续予以保护。D 不正确。委托人的姓名是审判中的争议问题。如果使用真实姓名，他会受到较重处罚；如果法院不知道其真实姓名，他就会受到较轻处罚。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-042","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":42,"topic":"Rule 2.11(A)(3) / Rule 2.11(C)","question":"A judge is one of three trustees of a trust for the educational benefit of her grandchildren. The trust owns 5,000 shares of stock in a large oil company. The stock has been selling for the past year at $10,000 a share. The oil company is suing a refining company for breach of an oil refining agreement, and the case is assigned to the judge for trial. The judge believes that she can be fair and impartial. Additionally, she does not personally own stock in either party to the litigation. Must the judge disqualify herself from the case?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the trust has more than a de minimis financial interest in the oil company.","B":"Yes, because the outcome of the lawsuit is likely to affect the value of the stock.","C":"No, because the judge does not personally own stock in either party to the litigation.","D":"No, because the judge believes she can remain impartial."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. If a judge personally or as a fiduciary (trustee of a trust) has more than a de minimis economic interest in the subject matter in controversy, disqualification is required. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(3). A financial interest disqualification can be remitted if the judge fully discloses the potential impartiality conflict on the record, all the parties and lawyers agree that the conflict is immaterial in a proceeding outside the presence of the judge, and the agreement is then incorporated into the record. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(C). However, considering the large size of the financial interest ($50,000,000), a remittance here is likely inappropriate. Also, the potential for remittance was not included among any of the answer choices, making A the best answer. B is incorrect. Even if the value of the stock is unlikely to be affected, disqualification is still necessary to prevent the appearance of judicial impropriety and the judge’s impartiality can reasonably be questioned under the circumstances. The overarching goal of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct is to make sure judges act in a manner that promotes public confidence in the integrity, fairness, and impartiality of the judiciary. Consequently, members of the judiciary must avoid creating even an appearance of bias. C is incorrect. Even if the judge does not personally own stock in either party to the litigation, she is a trustee who owns a legal interest in the stock while acting as a fiduciary for others with an equitable interest. Under these circumstances, the judge’s impartiality can still be reasonably questioned by others. Further adding to the appearance of impropriety, the trust is set up for the educational benefit of the judge’s grandchildren, and disqualification is similarly required in circumstances where a member of the judge’s family has an economic interest in the matter. D is incorrect. Although the judge believes she can be fair and impartial (and she might, in fact, be correct in this belief), that impartiality could be reasonably questioned by others. Consequently, she should disqualify herself for the same reasons as in B.","rule":"A is the correct answer. If a judge personally or as a fiduciary (trustee of a trust) has more than a de minimis economic interest in the subject matter in controversy, disqualification is required. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(3). A financial interest disqualification can be remitted if the judge fully discloses the potential impartiality conflict on the record, all the parties and lawyers agree that the conflict is immaterial in a proceeding outside the presence of the judge, and the agreement is then incorporated into the record. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(C). However, considering the large size of the financial interest ($50,000,000), a remittance here is likely inappropriate. Also, the potential for remittance was not included among any of the answer choices, making A the best answer. B is incorrect. Even if the value of the stock is unlikely to be affected, disqualification is still necessary to prevent the appearance of judicial impropriety and the judge’s impartiality can reasonably be questioned under the circumstances. The overarching goal of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct is to make sure judges act in a manner that promotes public confidence in the integrity, fairness, and impartiality of the judiciary. Consequently, members of the judiciary must avoid creating even an appearance of bias. C is incorrect. Even if the judge does not personally own stock in either party to the litigation, she is a trustee who owns a legal interest in the stock while acting as a fiduciary for others with an equitable interest. Under these circumstances, the judge’s impartiality can still be reasonably questioned by others. Further adding to the appearance of impropriety, the trust is set up for the educational benefit of the judge’s grandchildren, and disqualification is similarly required in circumstances where a member of the judge’s family has an economic interest in the matter. D is incorrect. Although the judge believes she can be fair and impartial (and she might, in fact, be correct in this belief), that impartiality could be reasonably questioned by others. Consequently, she should disqualify herself for the same reasons as in B.","irac":{"issue":"Must the judge disqualify herself from the case?","rule":"A is the correct answer. If a judge personally or as a fiduciary (trustee of a trust) has more than a de minimis economic interest in the subject matter in controversy, disqualification is required. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(3). A financial interest disqualification can be remitted if the judge fully discloses the potential impartiality conflict on the record, all the parties and lawyers agree that the conflict is immaterial in a proceeding outside the presence of the judge, and the agreement is then incorporated into the record. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(C). However, considering the large size of the financial interest ($50,000,000), a remittance here is likely inappropriate. Also, the potential for remittance was not included among any of the answer choices, making A the best answer. B is incorrect. Even if the value of the stock is unlikely to be affected, disqualification is still necessary to prevent the appearance of judicial impropriety and the judge’s impartiality can reasonably be questioned under the circumstances. The overarching goal of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct is to make sure judges act in a manner that promotes public confidence in the integrity, fairness, and impartiality of the judiciary. Consequently, members of the judiciary must avoid creating even an appearance of bias. C is incorrect. Even if the judge does not personally own stock in either party to the litigation, she is a trustee who owns a legal interest in the stock while acting as a fiduciary for others with an equitable interest. Under these circumstances, the judge’s impartiality can still be reasonably questioned by others. Further adding to the appearance of impropriety, the trust is set up for the educational benefit of the judge’s grandchildren, and disqualification is similarly required in circumstances where a member of the judge’s family has an economic interest in the matter. D is incorrect. Although the judge believes she can be fair and impartial (and she might, in fact, be correct in this belief), that impartiality could be reasonably questioned by others. Consequently, she should disqualify herself for the same reasons as in B.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. If a judge personally or as a fiduciary (trustee of a trust) has more than a de minimis economic interest in the subject matter in controversy, disqualification is required. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(3). A financial interest disqualification can be remitted if the judge fully discloses the potential impartiality conflict on the record, all the parties and lawyers agree that the conflict is immaterial in a proceeding outside the presence of the judge, and the agreement is then incorporated into the record. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(C). However, considering the large size of the financial interest ($50,000,000), a remittance here is likely inappropriate. Also, the potential for remittance was not included among any of the answer choices, making A the best answer. B is incorrect. Even if the value of the stock is unlikely to be affected, disqualification is still necessary to prevent the appearance of judicial impropriety and the judge’s impartiality can reasonably be questioned under the circumstances. The overarching goal of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct is to make sure judges act in a manner that promotes public confidence in the integrity, fairness, and impartiality of the judiciary. Consequently, members of the judiciary must avoid creating even an appearance of bias. C is incorrect. Even if the judge does not personally own stock in either party to the litigation, she is a trustee who owns a legal interest in the stock while acting as a fiduciary for others with an equitable interest. Under these circumstances, the judge’s impartiality can still be reasonably questioned by others. Further adding to the appearance of impropriety, the trust is set up for the educational benefit of the judge’s grandchildren, and disqualification is similarly required in circumstances where a member of the judge’s family has an economic interest in the matter. D is incorrect. Although the judge believes she can be fair and impartial (and she might, in fact, be correct in this belief), that impartiality could be reasonably questioned by others. Consequently, she should disqualify herself for the same reasons as in B.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 2.11(A)(3)","Rule 2.11(C)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[73],"sourceAnswerPages":[116],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名法官是某信托的三名受托人之一，该信托旨在资助其孙辈接受教育。信托持有一家大型石油公司 5,000 股股票；过去一年，该股票的交易价格为每股 10,000 美元。该石油公司正在起诉一家炼油公司违反石油精炼协议，案件被分配由该法官审理。法官相信自己能够保持公平公正。此外，她个人并未持有诉讼任一方的股票。该法官是否必须回避本案？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该信托在石油公司中拥有超过最低限度的经济利益。","B":"是，因为诉讼结果很可能影响股票价值。","C":"否，因为法官个人没有持有诉讼任一方的股票。","D":"否，因为法官相信自己能够保持公正。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。如果法官本人或者以受信人身份（即作为信托受托人）在争议事项中拥有超过最低限度的经济利益，就必须回避。《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.11(A)(3)。如果法官在记录中充分披露可能影响公正性的冲突，所有当事人和律师在法官不在场的程序中一致认为该冲突无关紧要，随后又将该协议记入记录，那么因经济利益而产生的回避事由可以得到豁免。规则 2.11(C)。不过，鉴于本题经济利益数额巨大（50,000,000 美元），豁免很可能并不适当。此外，各选项均未提出豁免的可能性，因此 A 是最佳答案。B 不正确。即便诉讼不太可能影响股票价值，仍有必要回避，以避免造成司法行为不当的观感；而且在本题情形下，其他人可以合理地质疑法官能否保持公正。《司法行为示范准则》的总体目标，是确保法官以能够提升公众对司法体系诚信、公平和公正之信心的方式行事。因此，司法人员甚至必须避免造成偏见的表象。C 不正确。即使法官个人并未持有诉讼任一方的股票，她仍是受托人，以受信人身份对股票拥有法律上的利益，而他人则拥有衡平法上的利益。在这种情况下，其他人仍可合理地质疑法官的公正性。此外，该信托是为了法官孙辈的教育利益设立的，这进一步造成不当观感；法官家属在事项中拥有经济利益时，同样需要回避。D 不正确。虽然法官相信自己能够公平公正，而且这种判断事实上也可能正确，但其他人仍可合理地质疑其公正性。因此，出于 B 项解析所述的相同理由，她应当回避。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-043","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":43,"topic":"Rule 3.7(A) / Rule 3.7(A)(6) / Rule 3.13","question":"A judge has served for many years as a director of a charitable organization that maintains a camp for disadvantaged children, although she has not received any compensation for her services. The organization has never been involved in litigation. The charity has decided to sponsor a public testimonial dinner in the judge’s honor. As part of the occasion, the local bar association intends to commission and present to the judge her portrait at a cost of $4,000. The money to pay for the portrait will come from a “public testimonial fund” that will be raised by the city’s bar association from contributions of lawyers who are members of the association and who practice in the city’s courts. Is it proper for the judge to accept the gift of the portrait?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the gift is incident to a public testimonial for the judge.","B":"Yes, because the judge did not receive compensation for her services to the charitable organization.","C":"No, because the cost of the gift exceeds $1,000.","D":"No, because the funds for the gift are contributed by lawyers who practice in the city’s courts."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. A judge’s extrajudicial activities are always subject to scrutiny to “minimize the risk of conflict with judicial obligations.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Canon 3. Rule 3.7(A) permits a judge to serve as an officer, director, trustee, or nonlegal advisor of “educational, religious, charitable, fraternal, or civic organizations not conducted for profit . . . .” (Note that the judge is precluded from giving any legal advice to the organization.) But the judge may not serve an organization that is engaged in a proceeding that is likely to come before the judge or that is engaged frequently in adversarial proceedings in the court of which the judge is a member. Rule 3.7(A)(6). Rule 3.13 permits a judge to accept gifts incident to a public testimonial. However, there are limitations on testimonials. Importantly, the gift must not be one that would “appear to a reasonable person to undermine the judge’s independence, integrity, or impartiality.” Rule 3.13(A). On these facts, the judge may accept the portrait. B is incorrect. The fact that the judge did not receive compensation is only one piece of the puzzle. Other pieces are also required to make the gift proper: (a) that it is a public testimonial, (b) by a charitable organization, (c) that does not engage in litigation before the judge’s court. (Note: a judge may receive compensation for activities permitted under Canon 3 if the source of payment does not give the appearance of influencing the judge in his or her judicial duties and if the compensation is reasonable and does not exceed what a nonjudge would receive for the same activity. Rule 3.12.) C is incorrect. There is no specific limit in the Model Code for the cost of a testimonial gift. The test is whether the value of the gift is so excessive as to suggest undue influence either in a specific matter or generally, and, also, whether the source of the gift is suspect. (On this issue, see the next paragraph.) D is incorrect. A testimonial gift from lawyers to a judge is not prohibited per se.","rule":"A is the correct answer. A judge’s extrajudicial activities are always subject to scrutiny to “minimize the risk of conflict with judicial obligations.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Canon 3. Rule 3.7(A) permits a judge to serve as an officer, director, trustee, or nonlegal advisor of “educational, religious, charitable, fraternal, or civic organizations not conducted for profit . . . .” (Note that the judge is precluded from giving any legal advice to the organization.) But the judge may not serve an organization that is engaged in a proceeding that is likely to come before the judge or that is engaged frequently in adversarial proceedings in the court of which the judge is a member. Rule 3.7(A)(6). Rule 3.13 permits a judge to accept gifts incident to a public testimonial. However, there are limitations on testimonials. Importantly, the gift must not be one that would “appear to a reasonable person to undermine the judge’s independence, integrity, or impartiality.” Rule 3.13(A). On these facts, the judge may accept the portrait. B is incorrect. The fact that the judge did not receive compensation is only one piece of the puzzle. Other pieces are also required to make the gift proper: (a) that it is a public testimonial, (b) by a charitable organization, (c) that does not engage in litigation before the judge’s court. (Note: a judge may receive compensation for activities permitted under Canon 3 if the source of payment does not give the appearance of influencing the judge in his or her judicial duties and if the compensation is reasonable and does not exceed what a nonjudge would receive for the same activity. Rule 3.12.) C is incorrect. There is no specific limit in the Model Code for the cost of a testimonial gift. The test is whether the value of the gift is so excessive as to suggest undue influence either in a specific matter or generally, and, also, whether the source of the gift is suspect. (On this issue, see the next paragraph.) D is incorrect. A testimonial gift from lawyers to a judge is not prohibited per se.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the judge to accept the gift of the portrait?","rule":"A is the correct answer. A judge’s extrajudicial activities are always subject to scrutiny to “minimize the risk of conflict with judicial obligations.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Canon 3. Rule 3.7(A) permits a judge to serve as an officer, director, trustee, or nonlegal advisor of “educational, religious, charitable, fraternal, or civic organizations not conducted for profit . . . .” (Note that the judge is precluded from giving any legal advice to the organization.) But the judge may not serve an organization that is engaged in a proceeding that is likely to come before the judge or that is engaged frequently in adversarial proceedings in the court of which the judge is a member. Rule 3.7(A)(6). Rule 3.13 permits a judge to accept gifts incident to a public testimonial. However, there are limitations on testimonials. Importantly, the gift must not be one that would “appear to a reasonable person to undermine the judge’s independence, integrity, or impartiality.” Rule 3.13(A). On these facts, the judge may accept the portrait. B is incorrect. The fact that the judge did not receive compensation is only one piece of the puzzle. Other pieces are also required to make the gift proper: (a) that it is a public testimonial, (b) by a charitable organization, (c) that does not engage in litigation before the judge’s court. (Note: a judge may receive compensation for activities permitted under Canon 3 if the source of payment does not give the appearance of influencing the judge in his or her judicial duties and if the compensation is reasonable and does not exceed what a nonjudge would receive for the same activity. Rule 3.12.) C is incorrect. There is no specific limit in the Model Code for the cost of a testimonial gift. The test is whether the value of the gift is so excessive as to suggest undue influence either in a specific matter or generally, and, also, whether the source of the gift is suspect. (On this issue, see the next paragraph.) D is incorrect. A testimonial gift from lawyers to a judge is not prohibited per se.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. A judge’s extrajudicial activities are always subject to scrutiny to “minimize the risk of conflict with judicial obligations.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Canon 3. Rule 3.7(A) permits a judge to serve as an officer, director, trustee, or nonlegal advisor of “educational, religious, charitable, fraternal, or civic organizations not conducted for profit . . . .” (Note that the judge is precluded from giving any legal advice to the organization.) But the judge may not serve an organization that is engaged in a proceeding that is likely to come before the judge or that is engaged frequently in adversarial proceedings in the court of which the judge is a member. Rule 3.7(A)(6). Rule 3.13 permits a judge to accept gifts incident to a public testimonial. However, there are limitations on testimonials. Importantly, the gift must not be one that would “appear to a reasonable person to undermine the judge’s independence, integrity, or impartiality.” Rule 3.13(A). On these facts, the judge may accept the portrait. B is incorrect. The fact that the judge did not receive compensation is only one piece of the puzzle. Other pieces are also required to make the gift proper: (a) that it is a public testimonial, (b) by a charitable organization, (c) that does not engage in litigation before the judge’s court. (Note: a judge may receive compensation for activities permitted under Canon 3 if the source of payment does not give the appearance of influencing the judge in his or her judicial duties and if the compensation is reasonable and does not exceed what a nonjudge would receive for the same activity. Rule 3.12.) C is incorrect. There is no specific limit in the Model Code for the cost of a testimonial gift. The test is whether the value of the gift is so excessive as to suggest undue influence either in a specific matter or generally, and, also, whether the source of the gift is suspect. (On this issue, see the next paragraph.) D is incorrect. A testimonial gift from lawyers to a judge is not prohibited per se.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 3.7(A)","Rule 3.7(A)(6)","Rule 3.13","Rule 3.13(A)","Rule 3.12"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[73,74],"sourceAnswerPages":[117],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名法官多年来一直担任某慈善组织的理事，该组织为处境不利的儿童运营一处营地；法官没有因这项服务获得任何报酬。该组织从未卷入诉讼。该慈善组织决定为法官举办一场公开表彰晚宴。作为活动的一部分，当地律师协会拟委托他人绘制一幅法官肖像并赠送给她，费用为 4,000 美元。肖像所需款项将来自一项“公开表彰基金”；该基金由本市律师协会向协会成员中在本市法院执业的律师募捐。法官接受这幅肖像作为礼物是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为该礼物是公开表彰活动附带的礼物。","B":"适当，因为法官没有因其为慈善组织提供的服务获得报酬。","C":"不适当，因为该礼物价值超过 1,000 美元。","D":"不适当，因为礼物所用资金由在本市法院执业的律师捐赠。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。法官的司法职务外活动始终应受到审视，以“尽量降低与司法义务发生冲突的风险”。《司法行为示范准则》准则三。规则 3.7(A) 允许法官在“并非以营利为目的的教育、宗教、慈善、联谊或公民组织……”中担任管理人员、理事、受托人或非法律顾问。（请注意，法官不得向该组织提供任何法律意见。）但如果某组织正在参与很可能提交该法官审理的程序，或者经常参加该法官所在法院的对抗性程序，法官就不得为该组织服务。规则 3.7(A)(6)。规则 3.13 允许法官接受公开表彰活动附带的礼物。不过，公开表彰仍受到限制。特别是，礼物不得属于“在一个理性人看来会削弱法官独立性、诚信或公正性”的礼物。规则 3.13(A)。根据本题事实，法官可以接受该肖像。B 不正确。法官没有获得报酬只是分析中的一个因素。还必须存在其他使该礼物适当的因素：(a) 它属于公开表彰活动；(b) 由慈善组织举行；以及 (c) 该组织不参与该法官所在法院的诉讼。（注意：法官可以就准则三允许的活动获得报酬，但付款来源不得造成其影响法官履行司法职责的观感，且报酬必须合理，不得超过非司法人员从事相同活动可获得的数额。规则 3.12。）C 不正确。《司法行为示范准则》没有对公开表彰礼物的价值设定具体上限。判断标准是，礼物价值是否高到足以暗示它会在某一具体事项中或一般性地产生不当影响，以及礼物来源是否可疑。（关于后一问题，参见下一段。）D 不正确。律师向法官赠送公开表彰礼物本身并不当然受到禁止。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-044","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":44,"topic":"Model Rule 3.7","question":"An attorney, who had represented a client for many years, prepared the client’s will and acted as one of the two subscribing witnesses to its execution. The will gave 10 percent of the client’s estate to the client’s housekeeper, 10 percent to the client’s son and sole heir, and the residue to charity. Upon the client’s death one year later, the executor of the will asked the attorney to represent him in probating the will and administering the estate. At that time, the executor informed the attorney that the client’s son had notified him that he would contest the probate of the will on the grounds that the client lacked the required mental capacity at the time the will was executed. The attorney believes that the client was fully competent at all times and will so testify, if called as a witness. The other subscribing witness to the client’s will predeceased the client. Is it proper for the attorney to represent the executor in the probate of the will?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney is the sole surviving witness to the execution of the will.","B":"Yes, because the attorney’s testimony will support the validity of the will.","C":"No, because the attorney will be called to testify on a contested issue of fact.","D":"No, because the attorney will be representing an interest adverse to the client’s heir at law."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer in litigation is expected to be a zealous advocate of his or her client’s position. If he or she is called to testify on a contested issue of fact, his or her role as advocate becomes confused. Also, his or her testimony may create an irreconcilable conflict of interest between the client and the attorney. For these reasons, a lawyer is prohibited from acting as advocate in a matter if he or she is likely to be called as a witness. Model Rule 3.7. However, there are three exceptions: (1) if the testimony relates to an uncontested issue, (2) if the testimony relates to the nature and value of the lawyer’s services rendered in the case, or (3) if the lawyer’s disqualification would work substantial hardship on the client. Model Rule 3.7. Because the facts here do not present an exception to the general rule, the attorney may not represent the executor. The executor may retain other counsel and the attorney will then be free to testify about the client’s competence. This question illustrates why a lawyer should never act as subscribing witness to a will he or she prepares. When a lawyer prepares a will and then acts as subscribing witness, he or she knows that he or she may later be called to appear as a witness in a contest of the will. A is incorrect. It restates the problem, not the solution. It’s exactly because the attorney is likely to be called as a witness that he may not represent the executor. True, there is some hardship on the executor in forcing him to find and retain other counsel to prove the will, but the problem is not insurmountable. On balance, the need to forestall participation by the lawyer as both advocate and witness is paramount. B is incorrect. The rule against the lawyer as witness assumes that the lawyer would testify in support of his or her client. “The problem can arise whether the lawyer is called as a witness on behalf of his client or is called by the opposing party.” Model Rule 3.7, Comment [6]. D is incorrect. It states correctly the underlying rationale for the Rule, but not the Rule itself. Whether or not the attorney’s testimony supports one side or another, he may not represent either side if he is likely to be called as a necessary witness.","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer in litigation is expected to be a zealous advocate of his or her client’s position. If he or she is called to testify on a contested issue of fact, his or her role as advocate becomes confused. Also, his or her testimony may create an irreconcilable conflict of interest between the client and the attorney. For these reasons, a lawyer is prohibited from acting as advocate in a matter if he or she is likely to be called as a witness. Model Rule 3.7. However, there are three exceptions: (1) if the testimony relates to an uncontested issue, (2) if the testimony relates to the nature and value of the lawyer’s services rendered in the case, or (3) if the lawyer’s disqualification would work substantial hardship on the client. Model Rule 3.7. Because the facts here do not present an exception to the general rule, the attorney may not represent the executor. The executor may retain other counsel and the attorney will then be free to testify about the client’s competence. This question illustrates why a lawyer should never act as subscribing witness to a will he or she prepares. When a lawyer prepares a will and then acts as subscribing witness, he or she knows that he or she may later be called to appear as a witness in a contest of the will. A is incorrect. It restates the problem, not the solution. It’s exactly because the attorney is likely to be called as a witness that he may not represent the executor. True, there is some hardship on the executor in forcing him to find and retain other counsel to prove the will, but the problem is not insurmountable. On balance, the need to forestall participation by the lawyer as both advocate and witness is paramount. B is incorrect. The rule against the lawyer as witness assumes that the lawyer would testify in support of his or her client. “The problem can arise whether the lawyer is called as a witness on behalf of his client or is called by the opposing party.” Model Rule 3.7, Comment [6]. D is incorrect. It states correctly the underlying rationale for the Rule, but not the Rule itself. Whether or not the attorney’s testimony supports one side or another, he may not represent either side if he is likely to be called as a necessary witness.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the attorney to represent the executor in the probate of the will?","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer in litigation is expected to be a zealous advocate of his or her client’s position. If he or she is called to testify on a contested issue of fact, his or her role as advocate becomes confused. Also, his or her testimony may create an irreconcilable conflict of interest between the client and the attorney. For these reasons, a lawyer is prohibited from acting as advocate in a matter if he or she is likely to be called as a witness. Model Rule 3.7. However, there are three exceptions: (1) if the testimony relates to an uncontested issue, (2) if the testimony relates to the nature and value of the lawyer’s services rendered in the case, or (3) if the lawyer’s disqualification would work substantial hardship on the client. Model Rule 3.7. Because the facts here do not present an exception to the general rule, the attorney may not represent the executor. The executor may retain other counsel and the attorney will then be free to testify about the client’s competence. This question illustrates why a lawyer should never act as subscribing witness to a will he or she prepares. When a lawyer prepares a will and then acts as subscribing witness, he or she knows that he or she may later be called to appear as a witness in a contest of the will. A is incorrect. It restates the problem, not the solution. It’s exactly because the attorney is likely to be called as a witness that he may not represent the executor. True, there is some hardship on the executor in forcing him to find and retain other counsel to prove the will, but the problem is not insurmountable. On balance, the need to forestall participation by the lawyer as both advocate and witness is paramount. B is incorrect. The rule against the lawyer as witness assumes that the lawyer would testify in support of his or her client. “The problem can arise whether the lawyer is called as a witness on behalf of his client or is called by the opposing party.” Model Rule 3.7, Comment [6]. D is incorrect. It states correctly the underlying rationale for the Rule, but not the Rule itself. Whether or not the attorney’s testimony supports one side or another, he may not represent either side if he is likely to be called as a necessary witness.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer in litigation is expected to be a zealous advocate of his or her client’s position. If he or she is called to testify on a contested issue of fact, his or her role as advocate becomes confused. Also, his or her testimony may create an irreconcilable conflict of interest between the client and the attorney. For these reasons, a lawyer is prohibited from acting as advocate in a matter if he or she is likely to be called as a witness. Model Rule 3.7. However, there are three exceptions: (1) if the testimony relates to an uncontested issue, (2) if the testimony relates to the nature and value of the lawyer’s services rendered in the case, or (3) if the lawyer’s disqualification would work substantial hardship on the client. Model Rule 3.7. Because the facts here do not present an exception to the general rule, the attorney may not represent the executor. The executor may retain other counsel and the attorney will then be free to testify about the client’s competence. This question illustrates why a lawyer should never act as subscribing witness to a will he or she prepares. When a lawyer prepares a will and then acts as subscribing witness, he or she knows that he or she may later be called to appear as a witness in a contest of the will. A is incorrect. It restates the problem, not the solution. It’s exactly because the attorney is likely to be called as a witness that he may not represent the executor. True, there is some hardship on the executor in forcing him to find and retain other counsel to prove the will, but the problem is not insurmountable. On balance, the need to forestall participation by the lawyer as both advocate and witness is paramount. B is incorrect. The rule against the lawyer as witness assumes that the lawyer would testify in support of his or her client. “The problem can arise whether the lawyer is called as a witness on behalf of his client or is called by the opposing party.” Model Rule 3.7, Comment [6]. D is incorrect. It states correctly the underlying rationale for the Rule, but not the Rule itself. Whether or not the attorney’s testimony supports one side or another, he may not represent either side if he is likely to be called as a necessary witness.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.7"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[74],"sourceAnswerPages":[117,118],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师为某委托人提供多年法律服务，曾为其起草遗嘱，并作为两名签署见证人之一见证遗嘱的签立。遗嘱将委托人遗产的 10% 留给其管家，10% 留给其儿子兼唯一法定继承人，其余财产留给慈善机构。一年后委托人去世，遗嘱执行人请求该律师代理遗嘱认证及遗产管理。此时，执行人告知律师，委托人的儿子已通知他，将以委托人签立遗嘱时不具备所需精神行为能力为由，对遗嘱认证提出异议。律师认为委托人一直具备完全行为能力，如被传唤作证，也会如此陈述。遗嘱的另一名签署见证人先于委托人去世。该律师代理执行人办理遗嘱认证是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为该律师是唯一仍在世的遗嘱签立见证人。","B":"适当，因为该律师的证言会支持遗嘱有效。","C":"不适当，因为该律师将被传唤就有争议的事实问题作证。","D":"不适当，因为该律师将代表与委托人的法定继承人利益相冲突的一方。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。在诉讼中，律师应当热忱主张委托人的立场。如果律师又被传唤就有争议的事实问题作证，其辩护人角色就会变得混乱，其证言还可能在委托人与律师之间造成无法调和的利益冲突。因此，如果一名律师很可能被传唤作为证人，规则通常禁止其在该事项中担任辩护人。《职业行为示范规则》3.7。不过，有三项例外：(1) 证言涉及没有争议的问题；(2) 证言涉及律师在本案中所提供服务的性质与价值；或者 (3) 取消该律师的代理资格会使委托人遭受重大困难。规则 3.7。由于本题事实不属于一般规则的任何例外，该律师不得代理遗嘱执行人。执行人可以另聘律师，届时该律师即可自由地就委托人的行为能力作证。本题说明了律师为什么绝不应担任自己起草之遗嘱的签署见证人。律师起草遗嘱后又担任签署见证人时，应当知道自己日后可能在遗嘱争议中被传唤作证。A 不正确。它只是重述了问题，而不是解决问题。恰恰因为该律师很可能被传唤作证，他才不能代理遗嘱执行人。强迫执行人寻找并聘请其他律师来证明遗嘱，确实会带来一些困难，但并非无法克服。权衡之下，避免律师同时以辩护人和证人身份参与案件具有更高的重要性。B 不正确。禁止律师兼任证人的规则本就预设了律师的证言可能支持其委托人。“无论律师是由自己的委托人一方传唤，还是由对方当事人传唤，该问题都可能出现。”规则 3.7 评注 [6]。D 不正确。它正确说明了该规则背后的理由，却没有准确表述规则本身。无论律师的证言支持哪一方，如果他很可能作为必要证人被传唤，就不得代理任何一方。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-045","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":45,"topic":"Model Rule 1.8(h)(2)","question":"An attorney represented a buyer in a real estate transaction. Due to the attorney’s negligence in drafting the purchase agreement, the buyer was required to pay for a survey that should have been paid by the seller. The attorney fully disclosed this negligence to the buyer, and the buyer suggested that he would be satisfied if the attorney simply reimbursed the buyer for the entire cost of the survey. Although the buyer might have recovered additional damages if a malpractice action were filed, the attorney reasonably believed that the proposed settlement was fair to the buyer. He sent the buyer a letter stating that the buyer should consider seeking independent representation before making a decision. Then, in order to forestall a malpractice action, the attorney readily agreed to make the reimbursement. The attorney drafted a settlement agreement, and it was executed by both the attorney and the buyer. Was the attorney’s conduct proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney advised the buyer in writing that the buyer should seek independent representation before deciding to enter into the settlement agreement.","B":"Yes, because the attorney reasonably believed that the proposed settlement was fair to the buyer.","C":"No, because the attorney settled a case involving liability for malpractice while the matter was still ongoing.","D":"No, because the buyer was not separately represented in negotiating and finalizing the settlement agreement."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. In settling a claim or potential claim for malpractice, a lawyer must advise an unrepresented client in writing of the desirability of seeking independent legal counsel. Model Rule 1.8(h)(2). B is incorrect. The attorney’s belief, or even whether the agreement is fair or unfair to the buyer, is not the issue. In this situation, under the Rules, the lawyer must advise the client in writing of the appropriateness of independent representation and must give the client ample time to find and consult with another lawyer. C is incorrect. There is no prohibition on settling a potential malpractice claim, whether or not a related matter is ongoing. D is incorrect. The Rules do not require that the buyer actually be separately represented, only that he be advised in writing of the desirability of seeking independent legal counsel and given time to consult with another lawyer.","rule":"A is the correct answer. In settling a claim or potential claim for malpractice, a lawyer must advise an unrepresented client in writing of the desirability of seeking independent legal counsel. Model Rule 1.8(h)(2). B is incorrect. The attorney’s belief, or even whether the agreement is fair or unfair to the buyer, is not the issue. In this situation, under the Rules, the lawyer must advise the client in writing of the appropriateness of independent representation and must give the client ample time to find and consult with another lawyer. C is incorrect. There is no prohibition on settling a potential malpractice claim, whether or not a related matter is ongoing. D is incorrect. The Rules do not require that the buyer actually be separately represented, only that he be advised in writing of the desirability of seeking independent legal counsel and given time to consult with another lawyer.","irac":{"issue":"Was the attorney’s conduct proper?","rule":"A is the correct answer. In settling a claim or potential claim for malpractice, a lawyer must advise an unrepresented client in writing of the desirability of seeking independent legal counsel. Model Rule 1.8(h)(2). B is incorrect. The attorney’s belief, or even whether the agreement is fair or unfair to the buyer, is not the issue. In this situation, under the Rules, the lawyer must advise the client in writing of the appropriateness of independent representation and must give the client ample time to find and consult with another lawyer. C is incorrect. There is no prohibition on settling a potential malpractice claim, whether or not a related matter is ongoing. D is incorrect. The Rules do not require that the buyer actually be separately represented, only that he be advised in writing of the desirability of seeking independent legal counsel and given time to consult with another lawyer.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. In settling a claim or potential claim for malpractice, a lawyer must advise an unrepresented client in writing of the desirability of seeking independent legal counsel. Model Rule 1.8(h)(2). B is incorrect. The attorney’s belief, or even whether the agreement is fair or unfair to the buyer, is not the issue. In this situation, under the Rules, the lawyer must advise the client in writing of the appropriateness of independent representation and must give the client ample time to find and consult with another lawyer. C is incorrect. There is no prohibition on settling a potential malpractice claim, whether or not a related matter is ongoing. D is incorrect. The Rules do not require that the buyer actually be separately represented, only that he be advised in writing of the desirability of seeking independent legal counsel and given time to consult with another lawyer.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.8(h)(2)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[74,75],"sourceAnswerPages":[118,119],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师在一项房地产交易中代理买方。由于律师在起草购买协议时存在疏忽，买方被迫支付了一笔本应由卖方承担的测量费。律师向买方充分披露了该疏忽，买方表示，只要律师全额偿还测量费，他就会感到满意。虽然买方若提起执业过失诉讼，可能获得额外损害赔偿，但律师合理地认为拟议和解对买方是公平的。他向买方发送一封信，说明买方在作出决定前应考虑寻求独立律师代理。随后，为避免执业过失诉讼，律师欣然同意偿还该费用。律师起草了一份和解协议，并由律师与买方双方签署。该律师的行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为律师以书面形式建议买方，在决定是否订立和解协议之前应寻求独立律师代理。","B":"适当，因为律师合理地认为拟议和解对买方公平。","C":"不适当，因为律师在相关事项仍在进行期间，和解了一项涉及执业过失责任的案件。","D":"不适当，因为买方在谈判并最终确定和解协议时没有由其他律师单独代理。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。在和解已经提出或可能提出的律师执业过失索赔时，律师必须以书面形式告知没有律师代理的委托人，寻求独立法律顾问是可取的。《职业行为示范规则》1.8(h)(2)。B 不正确。律师如何看待协议，甚至协议对买方究竟公平与否，都不是关键。在这种情况下，规则要求律师以书面形式告知委托人由独立律师代理是适当的，并给予委托人充分时间寻找和咨询另一名律师。C 不正确。无论相关事项是否仍在进行，规则均不禁止和解一项潜在的执业过失索赔。D 不正确。规则并不要求买方事实上必须由另一名律师代理；它只要求律师以书面形式告知买方寻求独立法律顾问是可取的，并给予他咨询另一名律师的时间。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-046","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":46,"topic":"Model Rule 3.4","question":"An attorney represented a plaintiff in a civil suit against a defendant who was represented by other counsel. In the course of developing the plaintiff’s case, the attorney discovered evidence that she reasonably believed showed that the defendant had committed a crime. The attorney felt that the defendant’s crime should be reported to local prosecutorial authorities. After full disclosure, the plaintiff consented to the attorney’s doing so. Without advising the defendant’s counsel, the attorney informed the local prosecutor of her findings, but she sought no advantage in the civil suit from her actions. The defendant was subsequently indicted, tried, and acquitted of the offense. Was the attorney’s disclosure to prosecutorial authorities proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney reasonably believed the defendant was guilty of a crime.","B":"Yes, because the attorney was required to report knowledge of criminal conduct when that knowledge was obtained through unprivileged sources.","C":"No, because the attorney did not advise the other counsel of her disclosure before making it.","D":"No, because the plaintiff’s civil suit against the defendant was still pending."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. If a lawyer identifies criminal behavior on the part of an opposing party, upon consent of the client, the lawyer may disclose such information to the prosecutor. Once the lawyer has the client’s consent, disclosure of the information would be permissive, not mandatory. B is incorrect. As stated above, the lawyer is not required to report an opposing party’s criminal activity to the prosecutor. Additionally, the decision to report the defendant to the criminal authorities does not depend on whether the information discovered is privileged or not. C is incorrect. There is no duty to inform the defendant’s lawyer before reporting the information. D is incorrect. It was permissible to report even though the civil suit was pending because the attorney was not trying to gain an advantage by making a complaint to the prosecutor. Had she been seeking an advantage, it might have been impermissible under Model Rule 3.4, which covers fairness to opposing party and counsel.","rule":"A is the correct answer. If a lawyer identifies criminal behavior on the part of an opposing party, upon consent of the client, the lawyer may disclose such information to the prosecutor. Once the lawyer has the client’s consent, disclosure of the information would be permissive, not mandatory. B is incorrect. As stated above, the lawyer is not required to report an opposing party’s criminal activity to the prosecutor. Additionally, the decision to report the defendant to the criminal authorities does not depend on whether the information discovered is privileged or not. C is incorrect. There is no duty to inform the defendant’s lawyer before reporting the information. D is incorrect. It was permissible to report even though the civil suit was pending because the attorney was not trying to gain an advantage by making a complaint to the prosecutor. Had she been seeking an advantage, it might have been impermissible under Model Rule 3.4, which covers fairness to opposing party and counsel.","irac":{"issue":"Was the attorney’s disclosure to prosecutorial authorities proper?","rule":"A is the correct answer. If a lawyer identifies criminal behavior on the part of an opposing party, upon consent of the client, the lawyer may disclose such information to the prosecutor. Once the lawyer has the client’s consent, disclosure of the information would be permissive, not mandatory. B is incorrect. As stated above, the lawyer is not required to report an opposing party’s criminal activity to the prosecutor. Additionally, the decision to report the defendant to the criminal authorities does not depend on whether the information discovered is privileged or not. C is incorrect. There is no duty to inform the defendant’s lawyer before reporting the information. D is incorrect. It was permissible to report even though the civil suit was pending because the attorney was not trying to gain an advantage by making a complaint to the prosecutor. Had she been seeking an advantage, it might have been impermissible under Model Rule 3.4, which covers fairness to opposing party and counsel.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. If a lawyer identifies criminal behavior on the part of an opposing party, upon consent of the client, the lawyer may disclose such information to the prosecutor. Once the lawyer has the client’s consent, disclosure of the information would be permissive, not mandatory. B is incorrect. As stated above, the lawyer is not required to report an opposing party’s criminal activity to the prosecutor. Additionally, the decision to report the defendant to the criminal authorities does not depend on whether the information discovered is privileged or not. C is incorrect. There is no duty to inform the defendant’s lawyer before reporting the information. D is incorrect. It was permissible to report even though the civil suit was pending because the attorney was not trying to gain an advantage by making a complaint to the prosecutor. Had she been seeking an advantage, it might have been impermissible under Model Rule 3.4, which covers fairness to opposing party and counsel.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.4"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[75,76],"sourceAnswerPages":[119],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师在一宗民事诉讼中代理原告，被告由另一名律师代理。在准备原告案件的过程中，该律师发现了一项证据，并合理地认为它表明被告实施了犯罪。律师认为应当向当地检察机关举报被告的犯罪。在充分披露相关情况后，原告同意律师这样做。律师没有通知被告律师，便将其发现告知当地检察官，但她并未试图借此在民事诉讼中取得任何优势。被告后来受到起诉、审判，最终被判无罪。该律师向检察机关作出披露是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为该律师合理地认为被告犯了罪。","B":"适当，因为律师如果从不受特权保护的来源获知犯罪行为，就有义务予以举报。","C":"不适当，因为该律师没有在披露前告知对方律师。","D":"不适当，因为原告针对被告的民事诉讼仍在进行。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。如果律师发现对方当事人有犯罪行为，在取得委托人同意后，可以将该信息披露给检察官。取得委托人同意后，披露该信息是允许的，但并非强制性的。B 不正确。如上所述，律师没有义务向检察官举报对方当事人的犯罪活动。此外，是否向刑事执法机关举报被告，并不取决于所发现的信息是否受特权保护。C 不正确。在举报该信息前，律师没有义务通知被告的律师。D 不正确。即使民事诉讼仍在进行，该举报仍属允许，因为律师并未试图通过向检察官举报而取得优势。如果她是为了取得优势，该行为可能违反《职业行为示范规则》3.4；该规则涉及对对方当事人及其律师的公平。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-047","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":47,"topic":"Model Rule 1.1 / Model Rule 1.2","question":"A witness was subpoenaed to appear and testify at a state legislative committee hearing. The witness retained an attorney to represent her at the hearing. During the hearing, the attorney, reasonably believing that it was in the witness’s best interest not to answer, advised the witness not to answer certain questions on the grounds that she had a constitutional right not to answer. The committee chairperson directed the witness to answer and cautioned her that refusal to answer was a misdemeanor and that criminal prosecution would be instituted if she did not answer. Upon the attorney’s advice, the witness persisted in her refusal to answer. The offense the witness committed did not involve moral turpitude, and the attorney reasonably believed the witness had a legal right to refuse to answer the questions. The witness was subsequently convicted for her refusal to answer. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because his advice to the witness was not legally sound.","B":"Yes, because the witness, in acting on the attorney’s advice, committed a crime.","C":"No, because the offense the witness committed did not involve moral turpitude.","D":"No, because the attorney reasonably believed the witness had a legal right to refuse to answer the questions."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. In making decisions and exercising options, a lawyer is required to act with competence. Model Rule 1.1. This means that he or she must employ “the legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness and preparation reasonably necessary for the representation.” In other words, the lawyer is not a guarantor of a successful outcome or even of a consequence-free result. He or she is required, however, to exercise his or her judgment in a manner that would be considered reasonable by other lawyers under all the circumstances. There are circumstances in which a lawyer may reasonably advise a client not to respond to questions by a legislative fact-finding committee, even when the client is faced by the risk of imprisonment. These facts do not tell us what the underlying facts were, but they do state clearly that the attorney “reasonably believed” that it was in the best interests of the witness not to answer. Ultimately, the attorney is not subject to discipline so long as he acted reasonably. A is incorrect. Whether or not the attorney ultimately made the right decision, he cannot be disciplined because he acted on a reasonable belief that he was protecting the witness. There is no suggestion in the facts that the attorney was not competent to represent the witness before the committee or to exercise his reasonable discretion to advise the witness not to answer. B is incorrect. The facts do not disclose the constitutional basis for the refusal by the witness to answer the committee’s questions. Obviously, however, the consequences to the witness in failing to exercise her constitutional rights were deemed by the attorney to be more serious and severe than the refusal to answer. Although the general rule is that an attorney may not counsel a client to engage, or assist a client, in conduct that the lawyer knows is criminal (Model Rule 1.2), most courts would support the witness’ right to refuse to answer if her constitutional rights were in jeopardy. C is incorrect. Model Rule 1.2 does not distinguish between crimes involving moral turpitude and other crimes. A lawyer may not assist a client in any crime, however characterized. However, the conflict between the need to protect a constitutional right not to answer and the obligation to observe a law requiring an answer is irreconcilable. The lawyer’s obligation under these circumstances is to advise the client of the consequences of each alternative.","rule":"D is the correct answer. In making decisions and exercising options, a lawyer is required to act with competence. Model Rule 1.1. This means that he or she must employ “the legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness and preparation reasonably necessary for the representation.” In other words, the lawyer is not a guarantor of a successful outcome or even of a consequence-free result. He or she is required, however, to exercise his or her judgment in a manner that would be considered reasonable by other lawyers under all the circumstances. There are circumstances in which a lawyer may reasonably advise a client not to respond to questions by a legislative fact-finding committee, even when the client is faced by the risk of imprisonment. These facts do not tell us what the underlying facts were, but they do state clearly that the attorney “reasonably believed” that it was in the best interests of the witness not to answer. Ultimately, the attorney is not subject to discipline so long as he acted reasonably. A is incorrect. Whether or not the attorney ultimately made the right decision, he cannot be disciplined because he acted on a reasonable belief that he was protecting the witness. There is no suggestion in the facts that the attorney was not competent to represent the witness before the committee or to exercise his reasonable discretion to advise the witness not to answer. B is incorrect. The facts do not disclose the constitutional basis for the refusal by the witness to answer the committee’s questions. Obviously, however, the consequences to the witness in failing to exercise her constitutional rights were deemed by the attorney to be more serious and severe than the refusal to answer. Although the general rule is that an attorney may not counsel a client to engage, or assist a client, in conduct that the lawyer knows is criminal (Model Rule 1.2), most courts would support the witness’ right to refuse to answer if her constitutional rights were in jeopardy. C is incorrect. Model Rule 1.2 does not distinguish between crimes involving moral turpitude and other crimes. A lawyer may not assist a client in any crime, however characterized. However, the conflict between the need to protect a constitutional right not to answer and the obligation to observe a law requiring an answer is irreconcilable. The lawyer’s obligation under these circumstances is to advise the client of the consequences of each alternative.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"D is the correct answer. In making decisions and exercising options, a lawyer is required to act with competence. Model Rule 1.1. This means that he or she must employ “the legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness and preparation reasonably necessary for the representation.” In other words, the lawyer is not a guarantor of a successful outcome or even of a consequence-free result. He or she is required, however, to exercise his or her judgment in a manner that would be considered reasonable by other lawyers under all the circumstances. There are circumstances in which a lawyer may reasonably advise a client not to respond to questions by a legislative fact-finding committee, even when the client is faced by the risk of imprisonment. These facts do not tell us what the underlying facts were, but they do state clearly that the attorney “reasonably believed” that it was in the best interests of the witness not to answer. Ultimately, the attorney is not subject to discipline so long as he acted reasonably. A is incorrect. Whether or not the attorney ultimately made the right decision, he cannot be disciplined because he acted on a reasonable belief that he was protecting the witness. There is no suggestion in the facts that the attorney was not competent to represent the witness before the committee or to exercise his reasonable discretion to advise the witness not to answer. B is incorrect. The facts do not disclose the constitutional basis for the refusal by the witness to answer the committee’s questions. Obviously, however, the consequences to the witness in failing to exercise her constitutional rights were deemed by the attorney to be more serious and severe than the refusal to answer. Although the general rule is that an attorney may not counsel a client to engage, or assist a client, in conduct that the lawyer knows is criminal (Model Rule 1.2), most courts would support the witness’ right to refuse to answer if her constitutional rights were in jeopardy. C is incorrect. Model Rule 1.2 does not distinguish between crimes involving moral turpitude and other crimes. A lawyer may not assist a client in any crime, however characterized. However, the conflict between the need to protect a constitutional right not to answer and the obligation to observe a law requiring an answer is irreconcilable. The lawyer’s obligation under these circumstances is to advise the client of the consequences of each alternative.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. In making decisions and exercising options, a lawyer is required to act with competence. Model Rule 1.1. This means that he or she must employ “the legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness and preparation reasonably necessary for the representation.” In other words, the lawyer is not a guarantor of a successful outcome or even of a consequence-free result. He or she is required, however, to exercise his or her judgment in a manner that would be considered reasonable by other lawyers under all the circumstances. There are circumstances in which a lawyer may reasonably advise a client not to respond to questions by a legislative fact-finding committee, even when the client is faced by the risk of imprisonment. These facts do not tell us what the underlying facts were, but they do state clearly that the attorney “reasonably believed” that it was in the best interests of the witness not to answer. Ultimately, the attorney is not subject to discipline so long as he acted reasonably. A is incorrect. Whether or not the attorney ultimately made the right decision, he cannot be disciplined because he acted on a reasonable belief that he was protecting the witness. There is no suggestion in the facts that the attorney was not competent to represent the witness before the committee or to exercise his reasonable discretion to advise the witness not to answer. B is incorrect. The facts do not disclose the constitutional basis for the refusal by the witness to answer the committee’s questions. Obviously, however, the consequences to the witness in failing to exercise her constitutional rights were deemed by the attorney to be more serious and severe than the refusal to answer. Although the general rule is that an attorney may not counsel a client to engage, or assist a client, in conduct that the lawyer knows is criminal (Model Rule 1.2), most courts would support the witness’ right to refuse to answer if her constitutional rights were in jeopardy. C is incorrect. Model Rule 1.2 does not distinguish between crimes involving moral turpitude and other crimes. A lawyer may not assist a client in any crime, however characterized. However, the conflict between the need to protect a constitutional right not to answer and the obligation to observe a law requiring an answer is irreconcilable. The lawyer’s obligation under these circumstances is to advise the client of the consequences of each alternative.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.1","Model Rule 1.2"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[76],"sourceAnswerPages":[119,120],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名证人收到传票，须在州议会委员会的听证会上出席并作证。证人聘请一名律师在听证会上代理她。听证期间，律师合理地认为不回答符合证人的最佳利益，因而以证人在宪法上有权拒绝回答为由，建议她不要回答某些问题。委员会主席指示证人作答，并警告她拒绝回答构成轻罪，如仍不回答，将受到刑事起诉。证人依律师建议坚持拒绝回答。证人实施的违法行为不涉及道德卑劣，而律师合理地认为证人在法律上有权拒绝回答这些问题。证人后来因拒绝回答而被定罪。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师向证人提供的建议在法律上并不正确。","B":"是，因为证人依照律师建议实施了犯罪。","C":"否，因为证人实施的违法行为不涉及道德卑劣。","D":"否，因为律师合理地认为证人在法律上有权拒绝回答这些问题。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。律师作出决定并行使选择权时，必须具备称职能力。《职业行为示范规则》1.1。这意味着律师必须运用“该代理合理需要的法律知识、技能、周全程度和准备”。换言之，律师并不保证一定取得成功结果，甚至也不保证其决定不会产生不利后果；但律师必须以在全部情形下会被其他律师认为合理的方式行使判断。在某些情况下，即使委托人面临监禁风险，律师仍可合理地建议委托人不要回答立法调查委员会的问题。本题没有说明相关基础事实，但明确指出律师“合理地认为”不回答符合证人的最佳利益。归根结底，只要律师行为合理，就不应受纪律处分。A 不正确。无论律师最终作出的决定是否正确，只要他是基于保护证人的合理信念行事，就不能因此受到纪律处分。事实没有表明该律师不能胜任在委员会前代理证人的工作，也没有表明他不能合理行使裁量权，建议证人不要回答。B 不正确。题目没有说明证人拒绝回答委员会问题所依据的具体宪法理由。不过，律师显然判断，证人不行使宪法权利所面临的后果，比拒绝回答的后果更为严重。一般规则确实是，律师不得建议委托人实施或协助委托人实施律师明知属于犯罪的行为（规则 1.2）；但如果证人的宪法权利受到威胁，多数法院会支持其拒绝回答的权利。C 不正确。规则 1.2 并不区分涉及道德卑劣的犯罪和其他犯罪。无论如何定性，律师都不得协助委托人实施任何犯罪。不过，保护拒绝回答的宪法权利与遵守要求作答的法律义务之间存在无法调和的冲突。在这种情况下，律师的义务是向委托人说明每一种选择的后果。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-048","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":48,"topic":"Model Rule 5.1 / Model Rule 1.1","question":"An attorney hired a recent law school graduate as an associate. For the first six months, the associate was assigned to draft legal documents that the attorney carefully reviewed and revised before filing. However, shortly after the associate was admitted to the bar, the attorney told the associate that he would be going on vacation the following week and was assigning her the representation of the landlord in a housing case that was going to trial while he was away. The associate had never conducted or observed a trial before and, because she had not previously worked on any housing cases, she was unfamiliar with the relevant law and procedure. She did not believe that she would have enough time to learn everything that she needed to know, but she was reluctant to decline the assignment. Before the trial began, she met with the landlord and disclosed that this would be her first trial, but the landlord did not object. Although the associate prepared diligently, the landlord lost the trial. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney did not ensure that the associate was competent to conduct the trial on her own.","B":"Yes, because the landlord lost at trial.","C":"No, because the attorney could reasonably assume that, having been admitted to the bar, the associate was capable of conducting the trial.","D":"No, because the landlord did not object to the associate’s representation."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. A newly admitted lawyer in a firm must have his or her work properly supervised by a more experienced lawyer to ensure that all lawyers in the firm conform to the Model Rules of Professional Conduct. Model Rule 5.1. Under Model Rule 1.1, a law firm owes a client a duty of competence, specifically the appropriate “legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness and preparation reasonably necessary for the representation.” Consequently, when a firm uses less-experienced lawyers to perform client work, the partners must supervise that work. In this case, the associate, who had never conducted or observed a trial and had not worked on landlord-tenant cases before, needed supervision. There was not enough time for the associate to become familiar with the relevant law and procedure, nor did she have the experience to competently handle the case. Importantly, the attorney did not take the proper precautions to make sure that the associate was adequately prepared to carry out the assignment. B is incorrect. The outcome of the trial is irrelevant to whether the associate was properly supervised. In particular, be wary of answer choices where a good outcome “fixes” the violation. C is incorrect. The associate’s admission to the bar did not relieve the attorney of the responsibility to properly supervise and train her. The question of a lawyer’s competence revolves around that lawyer’s training and experience, not the simple fact that the lawyer has passed a bar exam. D is incorrect. The consent of the landlord to this representation would not mitigate the fact that the attorney failed to properly supervise the associate. Also be wary of answer choices where a client consents to the Rule violation.","rule":"A is the correct answer. A newly admitted lawyer in a firm must have his or her work properly supervised by a more experienced lawyer to ensure that all lawyers in the firm conform to the Model Rules of Professional Conduct. Model Rule 5.1. Under Model Rule 1.1, a law firm owes a client a duty of competence, specifically the appropriate “legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness and preparation reasonably necessary for the representation.” Consequently, when a firm uses less-experienced lawyers to perform client work, the partners must supervise that work. In this case, the associate, who had never conducted or observed a trial and had not worked on landlord-tenant cases before, needed supervision. There was not enough time for the associate to become familiar with the relevant law and procedure, nor did she have the experience to competently handle the case. Importantly, the attorney did not take the proper precautions to make sure that the associate was adequately prepared to carry out the assignment. B is incorrect. The outcome of the trial is irrelevant to whether the associate was properly supervised. In particular, be wary of answer choices where a good outcome “fixes” the violation. C is incorrect. The associate’s admission to the bar did not relieve the attorney of the responsibility to properly supervise and train her. The question of a lawyer’s competence revolves around that lawyer’s training and experience, not the simple fact that the lawyer has passed a bar exam. D is incorrect. The consent of the landlord to this representation would not mitigate the fact that the attorney failed to properly supervise the associate. Also be wary of answer choices where a client consents to the Rule violation.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"A is the correct answer. A newly admitted lawyer in a firm must have his or her work properly supervised by a more experienced lawyer to ensure that all lawyers in the firm conform to the Model Rules of Professional Conduct. Model Rule 5.1. Under Model Rule 1.1, a law firm owes a client a duty of competence, specifically the appropriate “legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness and preparation reasonably necessary for the representation.” Consequently, when a firm uses less-experienced lawyers to perform client work, the partners must supervise that work. In this case, the associate, who had never conducted or observed a trial and had not worked on landlord-tenant cases before, needed supervision. There was not enough time for the associate to become familiar with the relevant law and procedure, nor did she have the experience to competently handle the case. Importantly, the attorney did not take the proper precautions to make sure that the associate was adequately prepared to carry out the assignment. B is incorrect. The outcome of the trial is irrelevant to whether the associate was properly supervised. In particular, be wary of answer choices where a good outcome “fixes” the violation. C is incorrect. The associate’s admission to the bar did not relieve the attorney of the responsibility to properly supervise and train her. The question of a lawyer’s competence revolves around that lawyer’s training and experience, not the simple fact that the lawyer has passed a bar exam. D is incorrect. The consent of the landlord to this representation would not mitigate the fact that the attorney failed to properly supervise the associate. Also be wary of answer choices where a client consents to the Rule violation.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. A newly admitted lawyer in a firm must have his or her work properly supervised by a more experienced lawyer to ensure that all lawyers in the firm conform to the Model Rules of Professional Conduct. Model Rule 5.1. Under Model Rule 1.1, a law firm owes a client a duty of competence, specifically the appropriate “legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness and preparation reasonably necessary for the representation.” Consequently, when a firm uses less-experienced lawyers to perform client work, the partners must supervise that work. In this case, the associate, who had never conducted or observed a trial and had not worked on landlord-tenant cases before, needed supervision. There was not enough time for the associate to become familiar with the relevant law and procedure, nor did she have the experience to competently handle the case. Importantly, the attorney did not take the proper precautions to make sure that the associate was adequately prepared to carry out the assignment. B is incorrect. The outcome of the trial is irrelevant to whether the associate was properly supervised. In particular, be wary of answer choices where a good outcome “fixes” the violation. C is incorrect. The associate’s admission to the bar did not relieve the attorney of the responsibility to properly supervise and train her. The question of a lawyer’s competence revolves around that lawyer’s training and experience, not the simple fact that the lawyer has passed a bar exam. D is incorrect. The consent of the landlord to this representation would not mitigate the fact that the attorney failed to properly supervise the associate. Also be wary of answer choices where a client consents to the Rule violation.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.1","Model Rule 1.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[76,77],"sourceAnswerPages":[120,121],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师聘用一位刚毕业的法学院学生担任助理律师。最初六个月，助理律师负责起草法律文件，由该律师在提交前仔细审阅和修改。然而，助理律师取得执业资格后不久，该律师告诉她，自己下周要休假，并将一宗届时开庭的住房案件中房东一方的代理工作交给她。助理律师此前从未亲自进行或旁听过审判，又从未办理过住房案件，因此不熟悉相关法律和程序。她认为自己没有足够时间掌握所有必要知识，却又不愿拒绝任务。审判开始前，她与房东会面，披露这是自己第一次出庭审判，但房东没有反对。尽管助理律师勤勉准备，房东仍在审判中败诉。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该律师没有确保助理律师具备独立进行审判的称职能力。","B":"是，因为房东在审判中败诉。","C":"否，因为助理律师既已取得执业资格，该律师可以合理地推定她能够进行审判。","D":"否，因为房东没有反对由助理律师代理。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。为确保律师事务所内所有律师均遵守《职业行为示范规则》，事务所中刚取得执业资格的律师必须由经验更丰富的律师对其工作进行适当监督。规则 5.1。根据规则 1.1，律师事务所对委托人负有称职代理义务，具体而言，必须具备该代理合理需要的“法律知识、技能、周全程度和准备”。因此，律师事务所安排经验较少的律师处理委托人事务时，合伙人必须监督其工作。本题中，该助理律师从未亲自进行或旁听过审判，也从未办理过房东—承租人案件，因而需要监督。她没有足够时间熟悉相关法律和程序，也缺少称职办理本案所需的经验。需要特别注意的是，该律师没有采取适当预防措施，以确保助理律师已经充分准备好完成任务。B 不正确。审判结果与助理律师是否受到适当监督无关。尤其要警惕那些暗示良好结果可以“补救”违反规则行为的选项。C 不正确。助理律师取得执业资格，并不能免除该律师适当监督和培训她的责任。判断律师是否称职，要看其培训和经验，而不能只看其是否通过律师资格考试。D 不正确。房东同意这项代理，并不能减轻该律师未适当监督助理律师这一事实。也要警惕那些暗示委托人可以同意违反规则行为的选项。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-049","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":49,"topic":"Model Rule 1.7","question":"An attorney currently represents a builder who is the plaintiff in a suit to recover for breach of a contract to build a house. The builder also has pending before the zoning commission a petition to rezone property the builder owns. However, the builder is represented by another attorney in the zoning matter. A neighbor who owns property adjoining that of the builder has asked the attorney to represent him in opposing the builder’s petition for rezoning. The neighbor knows that the attorney represents the builder in the contract action. Is it proper for the attorney to represent the neighbor in the zoning matter?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because there is no common issue of law or fact between the two matters.","B":"Yes, because one matter is a judicial proceeding and the other is an administrative proceeding.","C":"No, because the attorney is currently representing the builder in the contract action.","D":"No, because there is a possibility that both matters will be appealed to the same court."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. There is definitely a conflict of interest involved if the attorney represents the builder and the neighbor. A lawyer may not represent one client if it will be directly adverse to another client without the client’s informed consent. Model Rule 1.7. This is true even if the lawyer would be acting as advocate against the client in a matter unrelated to the one in which the lawyer represents the client. Model Rule 1.7, Comment [6]. It is possible the attorney could undertake the representation of the neighbor with informed written consent from the builder, but since that option is not offered, C remains the best answer. A is incorrect. The conflict of interest rule speaks to a lawyer’s loyalty to a client, so it doesn’t matter whether there are common issues in the two matters. If the attorney represents the neighbor in opposing the builder’s petition for rezoning, the builder would likely feel betrayed, which would likely damage the attorney’s and the builder’s lawyer-client relationship and interfere with the attorney’s representation of the builder in the contract suit. B is incorrect for the same reasons that A is incorrect. Loyalty to the client is the issue, and again, it doesn’t matter what types of proceedings are involved. D is incorrect. The issue is dealing with a concurrent conflict; what might or might not happen in the future has nothing to do with the attorney’s decision whether it is proper to undertake representation of the neighbor.","rule":"C is the correct answer. There is definitely a conflict of interest involved if the attorney represents the builder and the neighbor. A lawyer may not represent one client if it will be directly adverse to another client without the client’s informed consent. Model Rule 1.7. This is true even if the lawyer would be acting as advocate against the client in a matter unrelated to the one in which the lawyer represents the client. Model Rule 1.7, Comment [6]. It is possible the attorney could undertake the representation of the neighbor with informed written consent from the builder, but since that option is not offered, C remains the best answer. A is incorrect. The conflict of interest rule speaks to a lawyer’s loyalty to a client, so it doesn’t matter whether there are common issues in the two matters. If the attorney represents the neighbor in opposing the builder’s petition for rezoning, the builder would likely feel betrayed, which would likely damage the attorney’s and the builder’s lawyer-client relationship and interfere with the attorney’s representation of the builder in the contract suit. B is incorrect for the same reasons that A is incorrect. Loyalty to the client is the issue, and again, it doesn’t matter what types of proceedings are involved. D is incorrect. The issue is dealing with a concurrent conflict; what might or might not happen in the future has nothing to do with the attorney’s decision whether it is proper to undertake representation of the neighbor.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the attorney to represent the neighbor in the zoning matter?","rule":"C is the correct answer. There is definitely a conflict of interest involved if the attorney represents the builder and the neighbor. A lawyer may not represent one client if it will be directly adverse to another client without the client’s informed consent. Model Rule 1.7. This is true even if the lawyer would be acting as advocate against the client in a matter unrelated to the one in which the lawyer represents the client. Model Rule 1.7, Comment [6]. It is possible the attorney could undertake the representation of the neighbor with informed written consent from the builder, but since that option is not offered, C remains the best answer. A is incorrect. The conflict of interest rule speaks to a lawyer’s loyalty to a client, so it doesn’t matter whether there are common issues in the two matters. If the attorney represents the neighbor in opposing the builder’s petition for rezoning, the builder would likely feel betrayed, which would likely damage the attorney’s and the builder’s lawyer-client relationship and interfere with the attorney’s representation of the builder in the contract suit. B is incorrect for the same reasons that A is incorrect. Loyalty to the client is the issue, and again, it doesn’t matter what types of proceedings are involved. D is incorrect. The issue is dealing with a concurrent conflict; what might or might not happen in the future has nothing to do with the attorney’s decision whether it is proper to undertake representation of the neighbor.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. There is definitely a conflict of interest involved if the attorney represents the builder and the neighbor. A lawyer may not represent one client if it will be directly adverse to another client without the client’s informed consent. Model Rule 1.7. This is true even if the lawyer would be acting as advocate against the client in a matter unrelated to the one in which the lawyer represents the client. Model Rule 1.7, Comment [6]. It is possible the attorney could undertake the representation of the neighbor with informed written consent from the builder, but since that option is not offered, C remains the best answer. A is incorrect. The conflict of interest rule speaks to a lawyer’s loyalty to a client, so it doesn’t matter whether there are common issues in the two matters. If the attorney represents the neighbor in opposing the builder’s petition for rezoning, the builder would likely feel betrayed, which would likely damage the attorney’s and the builder’s lawyer-client relationship and interfere with the attorney’s representation of the builder in the contract suit. B is incorrect for the same reasons that A is incorrect. Loyalty to the client is the issue, and again, it doesn’t matter what types of proceedings are involved. D is incorrect. The issue is dealing with a concurrent conflict; what might or might not happen in the future has nothing to do with the attorney’s decision whether it is proper to undertake representation of the neighbor.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.7"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[77,78],"sourceAnswerPages":[121],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师目前代理一名建筑商，后者作为原告提起诉讼，要求就建造房屋合同遭违反获得赔偿。该建筑商另有一项申请正在分区委员会审理，请求变更其所有土地的分区用途。不过，在分区事项中，建筑商由另一名律师代理。拥有建筑商土地相邻物业的一名邻居，请求该律师代理他反对建筑商的分区变更申请。邻居知道该律师正在合同诉讼中代理建筑商。该律师在分区事项中代理邻居是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为两项事务之间没有共同的法律或事实问题。","B":"适当，因为一项是司法程序，另一项是行政程序。","C":"不适当，因为该律师目前正在合同诉讼中代理建筑商。","D":"不适当，因为两项事务都有可能上诉至同一法院。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。如果该律师同时代理建筑商和邻居，显然会产生利益冲突。未经委托人知情同意，律师不得代理一名委托人直接对抗另一名委托人。《职业行为示范规则》1.7。即使律师在与其代理现委托人之事项无关的另一事项中对该委托人采取对抗立场，这一规则仍然适用。规则 1.7 评注 [6]。该律师有可能在取得建筑商书面知情同意后代理邻居，但选项中没有这一条件，因此 C 仍是最佳答案。A 不正确。利益冲突规则所涉及的是律师对委托人的忠诚，因此两项事务是否具有共同问题并不重要。如果律师代理邻居反对建筑商的分区变更申请，建筑商很可能会感到遭到背叛，从而损害律师与建筑商之间的律师—委托人关系，并妨碍律师在合同诉讼中代理建筑商。B 不正确，理由与 A 相同。问题在于对委托人的忠诚，所涉及的程序属于何种类型同样不重要。D 不正确。本题处理的是同时发生的利益冲突；未来可能或不可能发生什么，与律师决定是否可以适当接受邻居的委托无关。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-050","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":50,"topic":"Model Rule 3.4 / Model Rule 3.8 / Model Rule 3.8(d)","question":"An elected prosecutor plans to run for reelection in six months. Last year, two teenage girls were kidnapped from a shopping center and sexually assaulted. The community was in an uproar about the crime and put pressure on the prosecutor to indict and convict the assailant. Four months ago, a suspect was arrested and charged with the crimes. The trial is scheduled to begin next week. The prosecutor met with the police chief last week to review the evidence in the case. At that time, the prosecutor first learned that, before they were interviewed by the detective in charge of sexual assault crimes, the two victims had been tape-recorded discussing the case between themselves in an interview room. Reviewing the tape, the prosecutor realized that the girls’ descriptions of the assailant differed significantly in terms of height, weight, and hair color. When officially interviewed, however, their descriptions matched almost perfectly. The suspect’s appointed counsel was busy handling a large caseload of indigent defendants and neglected to seek access to the prosecution’s investigative file. The prosecutor was virtually certain that suspect’s counsel was unaware of the tape recording. Given the other evidence in the case, the prosecutor reasonably believed that the girls accurately identified the suspect as their assailant. The prosecutor did not reveal the existence of the tape to defense counsel. Is the prosecutor subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the tape raises a legitimate question about the victims’ eyewitness identification of the suspect as the assailant.","B":"No, because the prosecutor reasonably believed that the girls accurately identified the suspect as their assailant.","C":"No, because under the adversary system of criminal justice, it is expected that each party will marshal the evidence best supporting its own position.","D":"No, because the suspect’s counsel did not submit a request for all mitigating or exculpatory evidence before the start of trial."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. With regard to civil cases, the adversary system contemplates that the evidence in a case is to be marshaled competitively by the parties. Model Rule 3.4, Comment [1]. Likewise, a lawyer’s duty not to conceal evidence in a civil case does not carry with it a duty to report or volunteer all relevant information. Prosecutors, however, have special responsibilities. Model Rule 3.8. “A prosecutor has the responsibility of a minister of justice and not simply that of an advocate” (Model Rule 3.8, Comment [1]) and as such, must “make timely disclosure to the defense of all evidence or information known to the prosecutor that tends to negate the guilt of the accused . . . .” Model Rule 3.8(d). The fact that the girls’ descriptions varied significantly at one point, but later matched almost perfectly, raises a definite question as to the accuracy of their identification and “tends to negate the guilt” of the suspect. Here, the prosecutor has a duty to reveal the existence of the tape to the suspect’s counsel. B is incorrect. What the prosecutor believes is not the point. If there is evidence or information that would raise a question as to the guilt of the suspect, the prosecutor must disclose the existence of that information or evidence in a timely manner. C is incorrect. This is a true statement with regard to civil cases. See Model Rule 3.4. However, as discussed above, a prosecutor has special responsibilities in a criminal case. Model Rule 3.8. D is incorrect. The prosecutor’s responsibilities under Model Rule 3.8 exist independently of a request by defense counsel. The prosecutor’s responsibility “carries with it specific obligations to see . . . that guilt is decided upon the basis of sufficient evidence.” Model Rule 3.8, Comment [1].","rule":"A is the correct answer. With regard to civil cases, the adversary system contemplates that the evidence in a case is to be marshaled competitively by the parties. Model Rule 3.4, Comment [1]. Likewise, a lawyer’s duty not to conceal evidence in a civil case does not carry with it a duty to report or volunteer all relevant information. Prosecutors, however, have special responsibilities. Model Rule 3.8. “A prosecutor has the responsibility of a minister of justice and not simply that of an advocate” (Model Rule 3.8, Comment [1]) and as such, must “make timely disclosure to the defense of all evidence or information known to the prosecutor that tends to negate the guilt of the accused . . . .” Model Rule 3.8(d). The fact that the girls’ descriptions varied significantly at one point, but later matched almost perfectly, raises a definite question as to the accuracy of their identification and “tends to negate the guilt” of the suspect. Here, the prosecutor has a duty to reveal the existence of the tape to the suspect’s counsel. B is incorrect. What the prosecutor believes is not the point. If there is evidence or information that would raise a question as to the guilt of the suspect, the prosecutor must disclose the existence of that information or evidence in a timely manner. C is incorrect. This is a true statement with regard to civil cases. See Model Rule 3.4. However, as discussed above, a prosecutor has special responsibilities in a criminal case. Model Rule 3.8. D is incorrect. The prosecutor’s responsibilities under Model Rule 3.8 exist independently of a request by defense counsel. The prosecutor’s responsibility “carries with it specific obligations to see . . . that guilt is decided upon the basis of sufficient evidence.” Model Rule 3.8, Comment [1].","irac":{"issue":"Is the prosecutor subject to discipline?","rule":"A is the correct answer. With regard to civil cases, the adversary system contemplates that the evidence in a case is to be marshaled competitively by the parties. Model Rule 3.4, Comment [1]. Likewise, a lawyer’s duty not to conceal evidence in a civil case does not carry with it a duty to report or volunteer all relevant information. Prosecutors, however, have special responsibilities. Model Rule 3.8. “A prosecutor has the responsibility of a minister of justice and not simply that of an advocate” (Model Rule 3.8, Comment [1]) and as such, must “make timely disclosure to the defense of all evidence or information known to the prosecutor that tends to negate the guilt of the accused . . . .” Model Rule 3.8(d). The fact that the girls’ descriptions varied significantly at one point, but later matched almost perfectly, raises a definite question as to the accuracy of their identification and “tends to negate the guilt” of the suspect. Here, the prosecutor has a duty to reveal the existence of the tape to the suspect’s counsel. B is incorrect. What the prosecutor believes is not the point. If there is evidence or information that would raise a question as to the guilt of the suspect, the prosecutor must disclose the existence of that information or evidence in a timely manner. C is incorrect. This is a true statement with regard to civil cases. See Model Rule 3.4. However, as discussed above, a prosecutor has special responsibilities in a criminal case. Model Rule 3.8. D is incorrect. The prosecutor’s responsibilities under Model Rule 3.8 exist independently of a request by defense counsel. The prosecutor’s responsibility “carries with it specific obligations to see . . . that guilt is decided upon the basis of sufficient evidence.” Model Rule 3.8, Comment [1].","analysis":"A is the correct answer. With regard to civil cases, the adversary system contemplates that the evidence in a case is to be marshaled competitively by the parties. Model Rule 3.4, Comment [1]. Likewise, a lawyer’s duty not to conceal evidence in a civil case does not carry with it a duty to report or volunteer all relevant information. Prosecutors, however, have special responsibilities. Model Rule 3.8. “A prosecutor has the responsibility of a minister of justice and not simply that of an advocate” (Model Rule 3.8, Comment [1]) and as such, must “make timely disclosure to the defense of all evidence or information known to the prosecutor that tends to negate the guilt of the accused . . . .” Model Rule 3.8(d). The fact that the girls’ descriptions varied significantly at one point, but later matched almost perfectly, raises a definite question as to the accuracy of their identification and “tends to negate the guilt” of the suspect. Here, the prosecutor has a duty to reveal the existence of the tape to the suspect’s counsel. B is incorrect. What the prosecutor believes is not the point. If there is evidence or information that would raise a question as to the guilt of the suspect, the prosecutor must disclose the existence of that information or evidence in a timely manner. C is incorrect. This is a true statement with regard to civil cases. See Model Rule 3.4. However, as discussed above, a prosecutor has special responsibilities in a criminal case. Model Rule 3.8. D is incorrect. The prosecutor’s responsibilities under Model Rule 3.8 exist independently of a request by defense counsel. The prosecutor’s responsibility “carries with it specific obligations to see . . . that guilt is decided upon the basis of sufficient evidence.” Model Rule 3.8, Comment [1].","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.4","Model Rule 3.8","Model Rule 3.8(d)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[78,79],"sourceAnswerPages":[122],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名民选检察官计划在六个月后竞选连任。去年，两名少女在一家购物中心遭绑架和性侵。社区对这起犯罪群情激愤，并向检察官施压，要求起诉并定罪施害者。四个月前，一名嫌疑人被捕并受到相关犯罪指控，审判定于下周开始。上周，检察官与警察局长会面审查案件证据。检察官这时才第一次得知，两名受害人在接受负责性侵案件的警探正式询问之前，曾在一间询问室内互相讨论本案，谈话被录了下来。检察官审听录音后发现，两名女孩对施害者的身高、体重和发色描述存在重大差异。但在随后正式接受询问时，两人的描述几乎完全一致。嫌疑人的指定律师正在忙于处理大量无力支付律师费的被告案件，疏于申请查阅控方调查档案。检察官几乎可以确定，嫌疑人的律师并不知道这段录音。鉴于本案其他证据，检察官合理地相信，两名女孩准确指认了嫌疑人就是施害者。检察官没有向辩护律师披露录音的存在。该检察官是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该录音使人对受害人将嫌疑人目击指认为施害者的准确性产生合理疑问。","B":"否，因为检察官合理地相信，两名女孩准确指认了嫌疑人就是施害者。","C":"否，因为刑事司法采用对抗制，各方理应分别汇集最有利于自身立场的证据。","D":"否，因为嫌疑人的律师在审判开始前没有请求控方提供全部减轻责任或有利于被告的证据。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。就民事案件而言，对抗制预期由双方以竞争方式汇集案件证据。《职业行为示范规则》3.4 评注 [1]。同样，律师在民事案件中不得隐匿证据的义务，并不附带要求其报告或主动提供全部相关信息。检察官则负有特殊责任。规则 3.8。“检察官承担的是司法维护者的责任，而不仅仅是辩护人的责任”（规则 3.8 评注 [1]），因此必须“及时向辩方披露检察官所知悉的、倾向于否定被告有罪的全部证据或信息……”。规则 3.8(d)。两名女孩的描述曾在某一时点存在重大差异，随后却又几乎完全一致，这确实会使人质疑其指认的准确性，并且“倾向于否定嫌疑人有罪”。因此，检察官负有向嫌疑人律师披露录音存在的义务。B 不正确。检察官本人相信什么不是关键。如果存在会使人质疑嫌疑人是否有罪的证据或信息，检察官就必须及时披露该信息或证据的存在。C 不正确。这一说法对民事案件是正确的。参见规则 3.4。但如上所述，检察官在刑事案件中负有特殊责任。规则 3.8。D 不正确。检察官在规则 3.8 下的责任独立于辩护律师是否提出请求。检察官的责任“附带特定义务，确保……有罪判定以充分证据为基础”。规则 3.8 评注 [1]。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-051","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":51,"topic":"Model Rule 3.4(f)","question":"An attorney represented the plaintiff in an automobile accident case. Two weeks before the date set for trial, the attorney discovered that there was an eyewitness to the accident. The attorney interviewed the witness. Her version of the accident was contrary to that of the plaintiff and, if believed by the trier of fact, would establish that the plaintiff was at fault. The witness told the attorney that she had not been interviewed by defense counsel. The witness also told the attorney that she was uncomfortable with testifying and that she had been thinking about taking a vacation to Europe the following week. The attorney told the witness that, since no one had subpoenaed her yet, she had no obligation to appear. He told her that trials were very difficult for witnesses and suggested that she take the vacation so that she would be unavailable to testify. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney asked the witness to leave the jurisdiction.","B":"Yes, because the attorney did not subpoena the witness knowing she was an eyewitness.","C":"No, because the witness had not been subpoenaed by the defense.","D":"No, because the attorney did not offer the witness any inducement not to appear at the trial."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from asking a person other than the client to refrain from voluntarily giving relevant information to another party unless that person is a relative, employee, or agent of the client and the lawyer reasonably believes that the person’s interests will not be adversely affected by refraining to give that information. Model Rule 3.4(f). In that case, the attorney may instruct the person to refrain from giving information unless subpoenaed. Here, the eyewitness did not fall under any of the exceptions to the Rule. B is incorrect. A lawyer is under no obligation to subpoena a witness that the lawyer does not intend to call at trial. C is incorrect. Even if a witness has not yet been subpoenaed, Model Rule 3.4(f) prohibits an attorney from asking that witness to refrain from giving relevant information to another party, unless that witness falls within one of the three exceptions. D is incorrect. Even though the attorney did not offer the witness any inducement not to appear at trial, Model Rule 3.4(f) prohibits the attorney from making any “request” to refrain from voluntarily giving relevant information.","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from asking a person other than the client to refrain from voluntarily giving relevant information to another party unless that person is a relative, employee, or agent of the client and the lawyer reasonably believes that the person’s interests will not be adversely affected by refraining to give that information. Model Rule 3.4(f). In that case, the attorney may instruct the person to refrain from giving information unless subpoenaed. Here, the eyewitness did not fall under any of the exceptions to the Rule. B is incorrect. A lawyer is under no obligation to subpoena a witness that the lawyer does not intend to call at trial. C is incorrect. Even if a witness has not yet been subpoenaed, Model Rule 3.4(f) prohibits an attorney from asking that witness to refrain from giving relevant information to another party, unless that witness falls within one of the three exceptions. D is incorrect. Even though the attorney did not offer the witness any inducement not to appear at trial, Model Rule 3.4(f) prohibits the attorney from making any “request” to refrain from voluntarily giving relevant information.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from asking a person other than the client to refrain from voluntarily giving relevant information to another party unless that person is a relative, employee, or agent of the client and the lawyer reasonably believes that the person’s interests will not be adversely affected by refraining to give that information. Model Rule 3.4(f). In that case, the attorney may instruct the person to refrain from giving information unless subpoenaed. Here, the eyewitness did not fall under any of the exceptions to the Rule. B is incorrect. A lawyer is under no obligation to subpoena a witness that the lawyer does not intend to call at trial. C is incorrect. Even if a witness has not yet been subpoenaed, Model Rule 3.4(f) prohibits an attorney from asking that witness to refrain from giving relevant information to another party, unless that witness falls within one of the three exceptions. D is incorrect. Even though the attorney did not offer the witness any inducement not to appear at trial, Model Rule 3.4(f) prohibits the attorney from making any “request” to refrain from voluntarily giving relevant information.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from asking a person other than the client to refrain from voluntarily giving relevant information to another party unless that person is a relative, employee, or agent of the client and the lawyer reasonably believes that the person’s interests will not be adversely affected by refraining to give that information. Model Rule 3.4(f). In that case, the attorney may instruct the person to refrain from giving information unless subpoenaed. Here, the eyewitness did not fall under any of the exceptions to the Rule. B is incorrect. A lawyer is under no obligation to subpoena a witness that the lawyer does not intend to call at trial. C is incorrect. Even if a witness has not yet been subpoenaed, Model Rule 3.4(f) prohibits an attorney from asking that witness to refrain from giving relevant information to another party, unless that witness falls within one of the three exceptions. D is incorrect. Even though the attorney did not offer the witness any inducement not to appear at trial, Model Rule 3.4(f) prohibits the attorney from making any “request” to refrain from voluntarily giving relevant information.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.4(f)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[79],"sourceAnswerPages":[122,123],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师在一宗汽车事故案件中代理原告。距离预定审判日期还有两周时，律师发现事故有一名目击证人。律师询问了该证人。她对事故经过的说法与原告相反；如果事实裁判者相信她的证言，就会认定原告对事故负有过错。证人告诉律师，辩方律师尚未询问她。证人还说，她对出庭作证感到不安，并一直考虑在下一周前往欧洲度假。律师告诉证人，由于尚无人向她发出传票，她没有出庭义务。律师又说，审判对证人来说非常艰难，并建议她去度假，以便届时无法出庭作证。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该律师要求证人离开该司法管辖区。","B":"是，因为该律师明知她是目击证人，却没有传唤她作证。","C":"否，因为辩方尚未向证人发出传票。","D":"否，因为该律师没有向证人提供任何不出庭的利益诱因。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。除非相关人员是委托人的亲属、雇员或代理人，且律师合理地认为该人员因不提供信息不会受到不利影响，否则律师不得要求委托人以外的人不要自愿向另一方提供相关信息。《职业行为示范规则》3.4(f)。在符合该例外时，律师可以指示该人员在未收到传票前不要提供信息。本题中，该目击证人不属于规则规定的任何例外。B 不正确。律师没有义务传唤其不打算在审判中提出的证人。C 不正确。即使证人尚未收到传票，规则 3.4(f) 仍禁止律师要求该证人不要向另一方自愿提供相关信息，除非该证人属于三项例外之一。D 不正确。即使律师没有向证人提供不出庭的任何利益诱因，规则 3.4(f) 仍禁止律师作出任何要求其不要自愿提供相关信息的“请求”。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-052","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":52,"topic":"Model Rule 4.2 / Model Rule 8.4(a)","question":"An attorney represented the plaintiff in a personal injury matter. The attorney had heard that the defendant in the matter was anxious to settle the case and reasonably believed that the defendant’s lawyer had not informed the defendant about the attorney’s recent offer of settlement. The attorney instructed her nonlawyer investigator to tell the defendant about the settlement offer so that the attorney could be sure that the defendant’s lawyer did not force the case to trial merely to increase the defendant’s lawyer’s fee. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the defendant was represented by counsel.","B":"Yes, because the attorney was assisting the investigator in the unauthorized practice of law.","C":"No, because the investigator is not a lawyer.","D":"No, because the attorney reasonably believed that the defendant’s lawyer was not keeping the defendant informed."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from communicating about a matter with a person known to be represented in the matter unless the person’s lawyer consents to the communication or the communication is authorized by law. Model Rule 4.2. The important point is that it is the lawyer’s consent that is necessary, not the client’s. In addition, a lawyer may not violate the Rules directly or through the acts of another. Model Rule 8.4(a). Here, the attorney used a nonlawyer agent, the investigator, to violate the no-contact rule. B is incorrect. The investigator merely relayed a communication from the attorney to the defendant and did not thereby engage in the unauthorized practice of law. C is incorrect. As stated above, a lawyer is prohibited from communicating with a represented person whether the communication is performed by the attorney or an agent of the attorney. Here, the attorney cannot get around the Rule by using a nonlawyer in making the communication. D is incorrect. Here, the defendant’s lawyer did not consent to the communication, and the fact the defendant’s lawyer might not have relayed the settlement offer did not otherwise justify the communication.","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from communicating about a matter with a person known to be represented in the matter unless the person’s lawyer consents to the communication or the communication is authorized by law. Model Rule 4.2. The important point is that it is the lawyer’s consent that is necessary, not the client’s. In addition, a lawyer may not violate the Rules directly or through the acts of another. Model Rule 8.4(a). Here, the attorney used a nonlawyer agent, the investigator, to violate the no-contact rule. B is incorrect. The investigator merely relayed a communication from the attorney to the defendant and did not thereby engage in the unauthorized practice of law. C is incorrect. As stated above, a lawyer is prohibited from communicating with a represented person whether the communication is performed by the attorney or an agent of the attorney. Here, the attorney cannot get around the Rule by using a nonlawyer in making the communication. D is incorrect. Here, the defendant’s lawyer did not consent to the communication, and the fact the defendant’s lawyer might not have relayed the settlement offer did not otherwise justify the communication.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from communicating about a matter with a person known to be represented in the matter unless the person’s lawyer consents to the communication or the communication is authorized by law. Model Rule 4.2. The important point is that it is the lawyer’s consent that is necessary, not the client’s. In addition, a lawyer may not violate the Rules directly or through the acts of another. Model Rule 8.4(a). Here, the attorney used a nonlawyer agent, the investigator, to violate the no-contact rule. B is incorrect. The investigator merely relayed a communication from the attorney to the defendant and did not thereby engage in the unauthorized practice of law. C is incorrect. As stated above, a lawyer is prohibited from communicating with a represented person whether the communication is performed by the attorney or an agent of the attorney. Here, the attorney cannot get around the Rule by using a nonlawyer in making the communication. D is incorrect. Here, the defendant’s lawyer did not consent to the communication, and the fact the defendant’s lawyer might not have relayed the settlement offer did not otherwise justify the communication.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from communicating about a matter with a person known to be represented in the matter unless the person’s lawyer consents to the communication or the communication is authorized by law. Model Rule 4.2. The important point is that it is the lawyer’s consent that is necessary, not the client’s. In addition, a lawyer may not violate the Rules directly or through the acts of another. Model Rule 8.4(a). Here, the attorney used a nonlawyer agent, the investigator, to violate the no-contact rule. B is incorrect. The investigator merely relayed a communication from the attorney to the defendant and did not thereby engage in the unauthorized practice of law. C is incorrect. As stated above, a lawyer is prohibited from communicating with a represented person whether the communication is performed by the attorney or an agent of the attorney. Here, the attorney cannot get around the Rule by using a nonlawyer in making the communication. D is incorrect. Here, the defendant’s lawyer did not consent to the communication, and the fact the defendant’s lawyer might not have relayed the settlement offer did not otherwise justify the communication.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 4.2","Model Rule 8.4(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[79,80],"sourceAnswerPages":[123],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师在一宗人身伤害案件中代理原告。律师听说被告急于和解，并合理地认为被告律师没有将该律师最近提出的和解要约告知被告。律师指示自己的一名非律师调查员，把和解要约告知被告，以确保被告律师不会仅仅为了增加自己的律师费而迫使案件进入审判。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为被告已有律师代理。","B":"是，因为该律师正在协助调查员擅自从事法律执业活动。","C":"否，因为调查员不是律师。","D":"否，因为该律师合理地认为被告律师没有及时向被告通报情况。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。律师明知某人在一项事务中已有律师代理，除非该人的律师同意沟通，或者法律授权该沟通，否则不得就该事务与此人联系。《职业行为示范规则》4.2。关键在于，所需的是其律师的同意，而非委托人本人的同意。此外，律师不得亲自违反规则，也不得通过他人的行为违反规则。规则 8.4(a)。本题中，该律师利用其非律师代理人即调查员违反了禁止联系规则。B 不正确。调查员只是将律师的信息转达给被告，并未因此擅自从事法律执业活动。C 不正确。如上所述，无论是律师本人还是其代理人实施沟通，律师都不得与已有律师代理的人联系。本题中，律师不能通过使用非律师进行沟通来规避规则。D 不正确。本题中，被告律师没有同意该沟通；被告律师可能没有转达和解要约这一事实，也不能以其他方式正当化该沟通。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-053","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":53,"topic":"Model Rule 1.7","question":"An attorney was approached by a husband and a wife who had decided to dissolve their marriage. They had no children and had worked out a tentative mutual property settlement. They did not want to retain separate lawyers because they hoped to save money and believed that working with one attorney was more likely to result in a reasonably amicable dissolution. Before coming to the attorney, they had drafted and each had signed a written agreement not to run up the costs and increase the adversarial nature of the dissolution by retaining separate lawyers. The attorney believed that he was able to provide competent and diligent representation to both the husband and the wife. The attorney consulted with both independently concerning the implications of the common representation, including the advantages and risks involved and the effect on their respective attorney-client privileges. The attorney reduced the disclosures to writing in the form of a written retainer agreement and gave them each several days to consult independent legal counsel if they so desired. The husband and the wife each chose not to consult independent counsel. After six months of reasonably amicable negotiations, the wife announced that she had changed her mind about the representation and had decided to retain her own lawyer. However, after the husband and the attorney insisted that she was obligated to adhere to her prior written agreement, she reluctantly agreed to abide by it. The attorney was then able to draft a property settlement agreement satisfactory to both parties. Is the attorney subject to discipline for his conduct in the representation?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney should not have undertaken to represent both the husband and the wife in the first place.","B":"Yes, because the attorney insisted that the wife not hire another lawyer.","C":"No, because both the husband and the wife initially consented to all aspects of the representation.","D":"No, because the husband and the wife independently made the agreement that neither would retain separate counsel."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. A client who has given consent to a potential conflict of interest may revoke the consent and, like any other client, terminate the lawyer’s representation at any time. Model Rule 1.7, Comment [21]. Here, it was improper for the attorney to insist that the wife adhere to the prior agreement since she had an absolute right to terminate the joint representation. A is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.7, the initial representation was proper because it was reasonable for the attorney to believe that he was able to provide competent and diligent representation to both the husband and the wife, and both clients’ consent was informed and in writing. C is incorrect. Even though the clients initially consented to the joint representation, each had an absolute right at any time to revoke the consent and terminate the attorney’s representation. Here, it was improper for the attorney to insist that the wife gave up that right. However, in some cases, a client’s revocation of consent to a joint representation can effect whether the attorney can continue to represent the other client. Whether a client’s revoked consent precludes the lawyer from continuing to represent other clients depends on the circumstances, “including the nature of the conflict, whether the client revoked consent because of a material change in circumstances, the reasonable expectations of the other client, and whether material detriment to the other clients or the lawyer would result.” Model Rule 1.7, Comment [21]. D is incorrect. As stated above, whether the husband and wife independently made the agreement or not, they still had an absolute right to revoke their consent to the joint representation at any time.","rule":"B is the correct answer. A client who has given consent to a potential conflict of interest may revoke the consent and, like any other client, terminate the lawyer’s representation at any time. Model Rule 1.7, Comment [21]. Here, it was improper for the attorney to insist that the wife adhere to the prior agreement since she had an absolute right to terminate the joint representation. A is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.7, the initial representation was proper because it was reasonable for the attorney to believe that he was able to provide competent and diligent representation to both the husband and the wife, and both clients’ consent was informed and in writing. C is incorrect. Even though the clients initially consented to the joint representation, each had an absolute right at any time to revoke the consent and terminate the attorney’s representation. Here, it was improper for the attorney to insist that the wife gave up that right. However, in some cases, a client’s revocation of consent to a joint representation can effect whether the attorney can continue to represent the other client. Whether a client’s revoked consent precludes the lawyer from continuing to represent other clients depends on the circumstances, “including the nature of the conflict, whether the client revoked consent because of a material change in circumstances, the reasonable expectations of the other client, and whether material detriment to the other clients or the lawyer would result.” Model Rule 1.7, Comment [21]. D is incorrect. As stated above, whether the husband and wife independently made the agreement or not, they still had an absolute right to revoke their consent to the joint representation at any time.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for his conduct in the representation?","rule":"B is the correct answer. A client who has given consent to a potential conflict of interest may revoke the consent and, like any other client, terminate the lawyer’s representation at any time. Model Rule 1.7, Comment [21]. Here, it was improper for the attorney to insist that the wife adhere to the prior agreement since she had an absolute right to terminate the joint representation. A is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.7, the initial representation was proper because it was reasonable for the attorney to believe that he was able to provide competent and diligent representation to both the husband and the wife, and both clients’ consent was informed and in writing. C is incorrect. Even though the clients initially consented to the joint representation, each had an absolute right at any time to revoke the consent and terminate the attorney’s representation. Here, it was improper for the attorney to insist that the wife gave up that right. However, in some cases, a client’s revocation of consent to a joint representation can effect whether the attorney can continue to represent the other client. Whether a client’s revoked consent precludes the lawyer from continuing to represent other clients depends on the circumstances, “including the nature of the conflict, whether the client revoked consent because of a material change in circumstances, the reasonable expectations of the other client, and whether material detriment to the other clients or the lawyer would result.” Model Rule 1.7, Comment [21]. D is incorrect. As stated above, whether the husband and wife independently made the agreement or not, they still had an absolute right to revoke their consent to the joint representation at any time.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. A client who has given consent to a potential conflict of interest may revoke the consent and, like any other client, terminate the lawyer’s representation at any time. Model Rule 1.7, Comment [21]. Here, it was improper for the attorney to insist that the wife adhere to the prior agreement since she had an absolute right to terminate the joint representation. A is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.7, the initial representation was proper because it was reasonable for the attorney to believe that he was able to provide competent and diligent representation to both the husband and the wife, and both clients’ consent was informed and in writing. C is incorrect. Even though the clients initially consented to the joint representation, each had an absolute right at any time to revoke the consent and terminate the attorney’s representation. Here, it was improper for the attorney to insist that the wife gave up that right. However, in some cases, a client’s revocation of consent to a joint representation can effect whether the attorney can continue to represent the other client. Whether a client’s revoked consent precludes the lawyer from continuing to represent other clients depends on the circumstances, “including the nature of the conflict, whether the client revoked consent because of a material change in circumstances, the reasonable expectations of the other client, and whether material detriment to the other clients or the lawyer would result.” Model Rule 1.7, Comment [21]. D is incorrect. As stated above, whether the husband and wife independently made the agreement or not, they still had an absolute right to revoke their consent to the joint representation at any time.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.7"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[80,81],"sourceAnswerPages":[123,124],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一对决定解除婚姻关系的夫妻找到一名律师。他们没有子女，并已初步拟定一份双方均可接受的财产和解方案。他们不希望分别聘请律师，因为想节省费用，并认为共同聘用一名律师更有可能使婚姻关系以较为友好的方式解除。在找该律师之前，二人已经起草并各自签署一份书面协议，约定不分别聘请律师，以免增加费用及离婚程序的对抗性。律师相信自己能够向丈夫和妻子双方提供称职且勤勉的代理。他分别与二人协商了共同代理的影响，包括相关利益与风险，以及对各自律师—委托人特权的影响。律师将这些披露写入书面聘用协议，并给二人各自数天时间，使其可以在愿意时咨询独立法律顾问。丈夫和妻子均选择不咨询独立律师。经过六个月较为友好的谈判，妻子宣布她改变了对共同代理的想法，决定聘请自己的律师。但丈夫和该律师坚持认为她有义务遵守先前的书面协议，妻子因而勉强同意继续遵守。此后，该律师起草出一份双方均满意的财产和解协议。该律师是否因其代理行为而应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该律师一开始就不应当同时代理丈夫和妻子。","B":"是，因为该律师坚持不让妻子另聘律师。","C":"否，因为丈夫和妻子起初均同意了共同代理的各个方面。","D":"否，因为丈夫和妻子是独立作出双方均不另聘律师之约定的。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。委托人即使已经同意某一潜在利益冲突，仍可撤回同意，并且像任何其他委托人一样，随时终止律师的代理。《职业行为示范规则》1.7 评注 [21]。本题中，妻子享有终止共同代理的绝对权利，因此律师坚持要求她遵守先前协议是不适当的。A 不正确。根据规则 1.7，最初的共同代理是适当的，因为律师合理地相信自己能够向丈夫和妻子双方提供称职且勤勉的代理，而两名委托人的同意既属于知情同意，也采用了书面形式。C 不正确。即使二人最初同意共同代理，每一人仍有绝对权利随时撤回同意并终止该律师的代理。本题中，律师坚持认为妻子已经放弃这项权利，这是不适当的。不过，在某些案件中，一名委托人撤回对共同代理的同意，可能影响律师能否继续代理另一名委托人。撤回同意是否使律师不得继续代理其他委托人，取决于具体情形，“包括冲突的性质、委托人是否因情势发生重大变化而撤回同意、另一名委托人的合理预期，以及其他委托人或律师是否会因此受到重大损害。”规则 1.7 评注 [21]。D 不正确。如上所述，无论丈夫和妻子是否独立作出该协议，他们仍然享有随时撤回共同代理同意的绝对权利。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-054","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":54,"topic":"Model Rule 3.9","question":"An attorney is a well-known tax lawyer and author. During congressional hearings on tax reform, the attorney testified to her personal belief and expert opinion on the pending reform package. She failed to disclose in her testimony that she was being compensated by a private client for her appearance. In her testimony, the attorney took the position favored by her client, but the position was also one that the attorney believed was in the public interest. Was it proper for the attorney to present this testimony without identifying her private client?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney believed that the position she advocated was in the public interest.","B":"Yes, because Congress is interested in the content of the testimony and not who is paying the witness.","C":"No, because a lawyer may not accept a fee for trying to influence legislative action.","D":"No, because a lawyer who appears in a legislative hearing should identify the capacity in which the lawyer appears."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer appearing before a legislative body must disclose that the appearance is in a representative capacity. Model Rule 3.9. This Rule recognizes the fact that a legislative body will likely give great weight to a lawyer’s opinions on a question of law or legislative rule making. Requiring a lawyer to disclose the client relationship allows the legislative body to assess any potential bias of the lawyer. A is incorrect. Here, the fact that the attorney believes her client’s position is in the public interest does not cure the Rule violation. Always be wary of answer choices that imply a Rule was not violated because the attorney would have said or done the same thing despite the Rule violation. B is incorrect. As stated above, the congressional members at the hearing would be very interested in knowing whether a witness is possibly biased in his or her testimony; therefore, Model Rule 3.9 requires a lawyer appearing in a representative capacity to disclose that fact to the legislative body. C is incorrect. Lawyers frequently serve as paid lobbyists. This activity is permissible so long as the lawyer-lobbyist complies with applicable rules, such as the requirement to disclose his or her representative capacity when appearing before a legislative body.","rule":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer appearing before a legislative body must disclose that the appearance is in a representative capacity. Model Rule 3.9. This Rule recognizes the fact that a legislative body will likely give great weight to a lawyer’s opinions on a question of law or legislative rule making. Requiring a lawyer to disclose the client relationship allows the legislative body to assess any potential bias of the lawyer. A is incorrect. Here, the fact that the attorney believes her client’s position is in the public interest does not cure the Rule violation. Always be wary of answer choices that imply a Rule was not violated because the attorney would have said or done the same thing despite the Rule violation. B is incorrect. As stated above, the congressional members at the hearing would be very interested in knowing whether a witness is possibly biased in his or her testimony; therefore, Model Rule 3.9 requires a lawyer appearing in a representative capacity to disclose that fact to the legislative body. C is incorrect. Lawyers frequently serve as paid lobbyists. This activity is permissible so long as the lawyer-lobbyist complies with applicable rules, such as the requirement to disclose his or her representative capacity when appearing before a legislative body.","irac":{"issue":"Was it proper for the attorney to present this testimony without identifying her private client?","rule":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer appearing before a legislative body must disclose that the appearance is in a representative capacity. Model Rule 3.9. This Rule recognizes the fact that a legislative body will likely give great weight to a lawyer’s opinions on a question of law or legislative rule making. Requiring a lawyer to disclose the client relationship allows the legislative body to assess any potential bias of the lawyer. A is incorrect. Here, the fact that the attorney believes her client’s position is in the public interest does not cure the Rule violation. Always be wary of answer choices that imply a Rule was not violated because the attorney would have said or done the same thing despite the Rule violation. B is incorrect. As stated above, the congressional members at the hearing would be very interested in knowing whether a witness is possibly biased in his or her testimony; therefore, Model Rule 3.9 requires a lawyer appearing in a representative capacity to disclose that fact to the legislative body. C is incorrect. Lawyers frequently serve as paid lobbyists. This activity is permissible so long as the lawyer-lobbyist complies with applicable rules, such as the requirement to disclose his or her representative capacity when appearing before a legislative body.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer appearing before a legislative body must disclose that the appearance is in a representative capacity. Model Rule 3.9. This Rule recognizes the fact that a legislative body will likely give great weight to a lawyer’s opinions on a question of law or legislative rule making. Requiring a lawyer to disclose the client relationship allows the legislative body to assess any potential bias of the lawyer. A is incorrect. Here, the fact that the attorney believes her client’s position is in the public interest does not cure the Rule violation. Always be wary of answer choices that imply a Rule was not violated because the attorney would have said or done the same thing despite the Rule violation. B is incorrect. As stated above, the congressional members at the hearing would be very interested in knowing whether a witness is possibly biased in his or her testimony; therefore, Model Rule 3.9 requires a lawyer appearing in a representative capacity to disclose that fact to the legislative body. C is incorrect. Lawyers frequently serve as paid lobbyists. This activity is permissible so long as the lawyer-lobbyist complies with applicable rules, such as the requirement to disclose his or her representative capacity when appearing before a legislative body.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.9"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[81],"sourceAnswerPages":[124,125],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师是知名的税法律师和作者。在国会举行的税制改革听证会上，该律师就待审改革方案陈述了自己的个人信念与专家意见。她在证言中没有披露，有一名私人委托人正在向她支付此次出席的报酬。律师在证言中主张委托人支持的立场，而她本人也认为该立场符合公共利益。该律师在不说明私人委托人身份的情况下作证是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为该律师认为自己主张的立场符合公共利益。","B":"适当，因为国会关心的是证言内容，而非谁向证人支付报酬。","C":"不适当，因为律师不得为试图影响立法行动而收取费用。","D":"不适当，因为律师在立法听证会上出席时，应说明其以何种身份出席。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。律师在立法机关前出席时，必须披露其是以代表他人的身份出席。《职业行为示范规则》3.9。该规则承认，立法机关很可能高度重视律师就法律问题或立法规则制定提出的意见。要求律师披露委托关系，使立法机关能够评估律师可能存在的偏见。A 不正确。律师认为委托人的立场符合公共利益，并不能消除其违反规则的行为。对那些暗示即使违反规则，律师本来也会说出或做出同样事情，所以就不构成违规的选项，应始终保持警惕。B 不正确。如上所述，参加听证的国会议员会十分关心证人的证言是否可能存在偏见；因此，规则 3.9 要求以代表他人身份出席的律师向立法机关披露这一事实。C 不正确。律师经常担任领取报酬的游说者。只要律师兼游说者遵守适用规则，例如在立法机关前出席时披露其代表身份，这项活动就是允许的。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-055","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":55,"topic":"Professional Responsibility","question":"An attorney closed her law practice when she became a state senator. A bank, one of the senator’s former private clients, asked her as its senator to try to persuade a state agency to grant the bank a license to open a new branch bank. While the bank’s request was pending before the agency, the senator wrote a letter on her legislative letterhead to the agency’s chair, asserting that the branch would satisfy a local business need and urging that the bank’s application be granted. The senator neither sought nor received any compensation from the bank for her efforts. Eventually the agency granted the bank’s application, in part because of the senator’s efforts. Is the senator subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the senator used her public position to influence the agency on behalf of the bank.","B":"Yes, because the agency granted the bank’s application in part due to the senator’s efforts.","C":"No, because the senator’s letter to the agency’s chair did not express an opinion about the law.","D":"No, because the senator acted on behalf of the bank as a constituent and not as a client."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. A practicing lawyer who is also a legislator may not use his or her official authority for the benefit of a private client. However, a legislator who is a lawyer but who is no longer practicing law may assist a constituent in its dealings with a state agency. Here, the facts show that the senator is helping the bank as a constituent, not a client. Consequently, the senator’s action would not subject her to discipline. A is incorrect. Because the senator was assisting the bank as a constituent and not a client, she did not misuse her official position. B is incorrect. The senator’s efforts as a legislator on behalf of a constituent were proper because she was no longer practicing law and was not seeking to assist the bank as a private client. Therefore, it does not matter that the senator in fact influenced the agency in her former client’s favor. C is incorrect. The Rules do not prohibit a legislator who is also a lawyer from expressing opinions about the law.","rule":"D is the correct answer. A practicing lawyer who is also a legislator may not use his or her official authority for the benefit of a private client. However, a legislator who is a lawyer but who is no longer practicing law may assist a constituent in its dealings with a state agency. Here, the facts show that the senator is helping the bank as a constituent, not a client. Consequently, the senator’s action would not subject her to discipline. A is incorrect. Because the senator was assisting the bank as a constituent and not a client, she did not misuse her official position. B is incorrect. The senator’s efforts as a legislator on behalf of a constituent were proper because she was no longer practicing law and was not seeking to assist the bank as a private client. Therefore, it does not matter that the senator in fact influenced the agency in her former client’s favor. C is incorrect. The Rules do not prohibit a legislator who is also a lawyer from expressing opinions about the law.","irac":{"issue":"Is the senator subject to discipline?","rule":"D is the correct answer. A practicing lawyer who is also a legislator may not use his or her official authority for the benefit of a private client. However, a legislator who is a lawyer but who is no longer practicing law may assist a constituent in its dealings with a state agency. Here, the facts show that the senator is helping the bank as a constituent, not a client. Consequently, the senator’s action would not subject her to discipline. A is incorrect. Because the senator was assisting the bank as a constituent and not a client, she did not misuse her official position. B is incorrect. The senator’s efforts as a legislator on behalf of a constituent were proper because she was no longer practicing law and was not seeking to assist the bank as a private client. Therefore, it does not matter that the senator in fact influenced the agency in her former client’s favor. C is incorrect. The Rules do not prohibit a legislator who is also a lawyer from expressing opinions about the law.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. A practicing lawyer who is also a legislator may not use his or her official authority for the benefit of a private client. However, a legislator who is a lawyer but who is no longer practicing law may assist a constituent in its dealings with a state agency. Here, the facts show that the senator is helping the bank as a constituent, not a client. Consequently, the senator’s action would not subject her to discipline. A is incorrect. Because the senator was assisting the bank as a constituent and not a client, she did not misuse her official position. B is incorrect. The senator’s efforts as a legislator on behalf of a constituent were proper because she was no longer practicing law and was not seeking to assist the bank as a private client. Therefore, it does not matter that the senator in fact influenced the agency in her former client’s favor. C is incorrect. The Rules do not prohibit a legislator who is also a lawyer from expressing opinions about the law.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":[],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[81,82],"sourceAnswerPages":[125],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师在成为州参议员时关闭了自己的律师业务。一家银行曾是该参议员私人执业时的委托人；银行请求她以本区参议员身份，尝试说服州政府机构向银行颁发开设新支行的许可证。在银行申请由该机构审理期间，参议员使用立法机关信笺向机构主席致函，声称该支行能够满足当地商业需要，并敦促批准银行申请。参议员既未请求，也未从银行获得任何报酬。最终，该机构批准了银行申请，部分原因正是参议员的努力。该参议员是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该参议员利用其公职身份代表银行影响政府机构。","B":"是，因为该机构部分由于参议员的努力而批准了银行申请。","C":"否，因为参议员写给机构主席的信没有就法律问题发表意见。","D":"否，因为参议员是把银行作为本选区选民而非委托人予以协助。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。仍在执业且兼任立法人员的律师，不得利用公职权力为私人委托人谋利。不过，已经不再执业的律师兼立法人员，可以协助本选区选民与州政府机构交涉。本题事实表明，参议员是在把银行作为选民而非委托人提供帮助。因此，她的行为不会使其受到纪律处分。A 不正确。由于参议员是在协助作为选民的银行，而非协助委托人，她没有滥用自己的公职地位。B 不正确。参议员已经不再执业，也不是要把银行作为私人委托人予以协助，因此，她作为立法人员为选民所作的努力是适当的。所以，她事实上影响政府机构作出了有利于其前委托人的决定，并不重要。C 不正确。规则不禁止兼具律师身份的立法人员就法律问题发表意见。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-056","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":56,"topic":"Model Rule 1.2(d)","question":"A county law prohibits stores from selling alcoholic beverages before noon on Sundays. Failure to comply is a misdemeanor punishable by a fine of $150. An attorney was hired by a client who owns several liquor stores. The client asked the attorney whether any storeowners had been prosecuted for violating the law and whether the fine could be imposed for every sale on a Sunday before noon or only for every Sunday on which alcohol was sold before noon. The client also asked what he could do to minimize the risk that he would be detected. The attorney accurately told the client that the fine could only be imposed for each Sunday on which he sold alcoholic beverages before noon, not for each transaction, and that no one had been prosecuted under the law as yet. She also told him that she thought it would be improper to advise him about how to avoid detection. The client thanked the attorney for the information and hung up. Several weeks later, the attorney learned that the client had begun to open his store for business on Sundays at 9 a.m. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney reasonably should have known that the information she gave the client would encourage him to violate the law.","B":"Yes, because the attorney did not discourage her client from breaking the law.","C":"No, because the attorney merely gave the client her honest opinion about the consequences that were likely to result if he violated the law.","D":"No, because the lawyer and the client could have discussed the best way to avoid detection under the criminal law."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. Although a lawyer is not allowed to counsel or assist a client in criminal or fraudulent conduct, a lawyer may help a client determine the validity, scope, meaning, or application of the law to a client’s particular situation. Model Rule 1.2(d). Here, the attorney is not helping the client break the law; she is simply delineating the law’s boundaries. A is incorrect. As long as a lawyer is not helping a client to violate the law, he or she is not required to make a guess as to how that client is going to use the lawyer’s advice, nor is the attorney subject to discipline if the client decides to use the lawyer’s advice to break the law. Here, the attorney simply gave her opinion regarding the parameters of the law. B is incorrect. Here, the attorney specifically told the client that she would not tell him how to avoid detection. All she did was tell him about the law and its history. Although discouraging a client from violating the law might have been the best practice, failing to discourage him from violating the law is not cause for discipline. D is incorrect. As stated above, lawyers may not assist clients in violating the law, and this includes discussing the best way to avoid detection.","rule":"C is the correct answer. Although a lawyer is not allowed to counsel or assist a client in criminal or fraudulent conduct, a lawyer may help a client determine the validity, scope, meaning, or application of the law to a client’s particular situation. Model Rule 1.2(d). Here, the attorney is not helping the client break the law; she is simply delineating the law’s boundaries. A is incorrect. As long as a lawyer is not helping a client to violate the law, he or she is not required to make a guess as to how that client is going to use the lawyer’s advice, nor is the attorney subject to discipline if the client decides to use the lawyer’s advice to break the law. Here, the attorney simply gave her opinion regarding the parameters of the law. B is incorrect. Here, the attorney specifically told the client that she would not tell him how to avoid detection. All she did was tell him about the law and its history. Although discouraging a client from violating the law might have been the best practice, failing to discourage him from violating the law is not cause for discipline. D is incorrect. As stated above, lawyers may not assist clients in violating the law, and this includes discussing the best way to avoid detection.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"C is the correct answer. Although a lawyer is not allowed to counsel or assist a client in criminal or fraudulent conduct, a lawyer may help a client determine the validity, scope, meaning, or application of the law to a client’s particular situation. Model Rule 1.2(d). Here, the attorney is not helping the client break the law; she is simply delineating the law’s boundaries. A is incorrect. As long as a lawyer is not helping a client to violate the law, he or she is not required to make a guess as to how that client is going to use the lawyer’s advice, nor is the attorney subject to discipline if the client decides to use the lawyer’s advice to break the law. Here, the attorney simply gave her opinion regarding the parameters of the law. B is incorrect. Here, the attorney specifically told the client that she would not tell him how to avoid detection. All she did was tell him about the law and its history. Although discouraging a client from violating the law might have been the best practice, failing to discourage him from violating the law is not cause for discipline. D is incorrect. As stated above, lawyers may not assist clients in violating the law, and this includes discussing the best way to avoid detection.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. Although a lawyer is not allowed to counsel or assist a client in criminal or fraudulent conduct, a lawyer may help a client determine the validity, scope, meaning, or application of the law to a client’s particular situation. Model Rule 1.2(d). Here, the attorney is not helping the client break the law; she is simply delineating the law’s boundaries. A is incorrect. As long as a lawyer is not helping a client to violate the law, he or she is not required to make a guess as to how that client is going to use the lawyer’s advice, nor is the attorney subject to discipline if the client decides to use the lawyer’s advice to break the law. Here, the attorney simply gave her opinion regarding the parameters of the law. B is incorrect. Here, the attorney specifically told the client that she would not tell him how to avoid detection. All she did was tell him about the law and its history. Although discouraging a client from violating the law might have been the best practice, failing to discourage him from violating the law is not cause for discipline. D is incorrect. As stated above, lawyers may not assist clients in violating the law, and this includes discussing the best way to avoid detection.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.2(d)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[82,83],"sourceAnswerPages":[125,126],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"某县法律禁止商店在星期日中午之前销售酒精饮料。违反者构成轻罪，可处 150 美元罚款。一名拥有数家酒类商店的委托人聘请了一名律师。委托人询问律师，是否有店主曾因违反该法律受到起诉，以及罚款是可以针对星期日中午前的每一笔销售分别施加，还是只能针对每一个发生过此类销售的星期日施加一次。委托人还询问可以怎样尽量降低被发现的风险。律师准确地告诉委托人，罚款只能按每个发生过中午前销售酒精饮料行为的星期日施加，而不能按每笔交易施加，并且至今尚无人因该法律受到起诉。她还告诉委托人，向他提供如何逃避查获的建议是不适当的。委托人感谢律师提供信息后挂断了电话。数周后，律师得知委托人已开始在星期日上午 9 点开门营业。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该律师理应知道，她提供的信息会鼓励委托人违法。","B":"是，因为该律师没有劝阻委托人违法。","C":"否，因为该律师只是如实说明了委托人违法后可能产生的后果。","D":"否，因为律师和委托人本可讨论逃避刑法查获的最佳方法。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。律师虽然不得建议或协助委托人实施犯罪或欺诈行为，但可以帮助委托人判断某项法律对其具体情况而言是否有效、适用范围如何、含义为何以及如何适用。《职业行为示范规则》1.2(d)。本题中，律师并未帮助委托人违法；她只是说明法律界限。A 不正确。只要律师没有帮助委托人违法，就不必猜测委托人会如何利用律师意见；即使委托人决定利用该意见违法，律师也不会因此应受纪律处分。本题中，律师只是就法律规定的范围给出了意见。B 不正确。本题中，律师明确告诉委托人，她不会说明如何逃避查获。她所做的只是解释法律及其实施历史。劝阻委托人违法或许是最佳做法，但未作劝阻并不足以构成纪律处分事由。D 不正确。如上所述，律师不得协助委托人违法，这也包括讨论逃避查获的最佳方法。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-057","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":57,"topic":"Rule 2.9(A)(3) / Rule 2.9(A)(2)","question":"A judge has served on a trial court of general jurisdiction for almost three years. During that time, he was assigned criminal cases almost exclusively. Several months ago, however, the judge was assigned an interesting case involving a constitutional challenge to a statute recently passed by the state legislature. The statute permitted any local public school district with an overcrowding problem to purchase educational services for its students in any other public or private school within 15 miles. Although the briefs submitted by the parties were excellent, the judge was not confident that he had a good grasp of the issues in the case. Accordingly, he took one of his more experienced colleagues on the trial court out to lunch and discussed the case with her in great detail. The colleague was far more conservative than the judge, but he agreed with her and eventually ruled in accord with her views. The case is now on appeal. Is the judge subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the judge sought an ex parte communication on the merits of a case pending before him.","B":"Yes, because the judge initiated a discussion with a colleague that may have influenced his judgment in the case.","C":"No, because the judge is permitted to obtain the advice of a disinterested expert on the law.","D":"No, because the judge was permitted to consult about a pending case with another judge."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Judges are permitted to consult with other judges on the court about pending cases as long as any consultation does not affect their responsibilities to personally decide the matters and as long as they make “reasonable efforts to avoid receiving factual information that is not part of the record.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(3). A is incorrect. A conversation with another judge on the court about a pending matter does not count as an inappropriate ex parte communication. B is incorrect. As stated above, a judge may consult with another judge in the court about a case, as long as the judge does not thereby give up his or her responsibility to personally decide the matter. C is incorrect. Under the Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(2), a judge may obtain written advice from a disinterested expert provided that the judge gives advance notice to the parties about details of the consultation and the opportunity to object and respond to the advice. This Rule, however, is not applicable to a situation in which a judge consults with another judge in the court.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Judges are permitted to consult with other judges on the court about pending cases as long as any consultation does not affect their responsibilities to personally decide the matters and as long as they make “reasonable efforts to avoid receiving factual information that is not part of the record.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(3). A is incorrect. A conversation with another judge on the court about a pending matter does not count as an inappropriate ex parte communication. B is incorrect. As stated above, a judge may consult with another judge in the court about a case, as long as the judge does not thereby give up his or her responsibility to personally decide the matter. C is incorrect. Under the Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(2), a judge may obtain written advice from a disinterested expert provided that the judge gives advance notice to the parties about details of the consultation and the opportunity to object and respond to the advice. This Rule, however, is not applicable to a situation in which a judge consults with another judge in the court.","irac":{"issue":"Is the judge subject to discipline?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Judges are permitted to consult with other judges on the court about pending cases as long as any consultation does not affect their responsibilities to personally decide the matters and as long as they make “reasonable efforts to avoid receiving factual information that is not part of the record.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(3). A is incorrect. A conversation with another judge on the court about a pending matter does not count as an inappropriate ex parte communication. B is incorrect. As stated above, a judge may consult with another judge in the court about a case, as long as the judge does not thereby give up his or her responsibility to personally decide the matter. C is incorrect. Under the Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(2), a judge may obtain written advice from a disinterested expert provided that the judge gives advance notice to the parties about details of the consultation and the opportunity to object and respond to the advice. This Rule, however, is not applicable to a situation in which a judge consults with another judge in the court.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Judges are permitted to consult with other judges on the court about pending cases as long as any consultation does not affect their responsibilities to personally decide the matters and as long as they make “reasonable efforts to avoid receiving factual information that is not part of the record.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(3). A is incorrect. A conversation with another judge on the court about a pending matter does not count as an inappropriate ex parte communication. B is incorrect. As stated above, a judge may consult with another judge in the court about a case, as long as the judge does not thereby give up his or her responsibility to personally decide the matter. C is incorrect. Under the Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(2), a judge may obtain written advice from a disinterested expert provided that the judge gives advance notice to the parties about details of the consultation and the opportunity to object and respond to the advice. This Rule, however, is not applicable to a situation in which a judge consults with another judge in the court.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 2.9(A)(3)","Rule 2.9(A)(2)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[83],"sourceAnswerPages":[126],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名法官在一个具有一般管辖权的初审法院任职近三年。在此期间，他几乎只被分配审理刑事案件。不过，数月前，他被分配审理一宗很有意思的案件，涉及对州立法机关最近通过的一项法律提出宪法质疑。该法律允许任何面临学生过度拥挤问题的地方公立学区，在 15 英里范围内的任何其他公立或私立学校为其学生购买教育服务。尽管双方提交的书面陈述都十分出色，法官仍不确信自己已经充分理解案件中的问题。因此，他邀请初审法院一名经验更丰富的同事共进午餐，并与她非常详细地讨论了本案。该同事远比这名法官保守，但法官认同她的看法，最终也按照她的观点作出裁判。案件现正处于上诉阶段。该法官是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为法官就其正在审理之案件的实体问题寻求了单方沟通。","B":"是，因为法官主动与同事讨论，而该讨论可能影响了他对案件的判断。","C":"否，因为法官可以取得无利害关系的法律专家意见。","D":"否，因为法官可以就待决案件咨询另一名法官。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。法官可以就待决案件咨询同一法院的其他法官，但该咨询不得影响其亲自裁判事项的责任，并且法官须作出“合理努力，避免接收不属于案件记录的事实信息”。《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.9(A)(3)。A 不正确。与同一法院的另一名法官讨论待决事项，不属于不适当的单方沟通。B 不正确。如上所述，只要法官不因此放弃亲自裁判案件的责任，就可以就案件咨询同一法院的另一名法官。C 不正确。根据《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.9(A)(2)，法官可以向无利害关系的专家取得书面意见，但须事先将咨询详情通知各方，并给予各方提出异议和回应意见的机会。不过，该规则不适用于法官咨询同一法院另一名法官的情形。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-058","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":58,"topic":"Model Rule 5.4(a) / Model Rule 1.5(a)","question":"An attorney is a senior partner at a law firm in which there are 50 lawyers. The firm pays each of its lawyers a fixed annual salary. In addition, at year’s end, each lawyer receives a bonus from the profits of the firm in proportion to the annual salary of each and its relation to the total of the fixed annual salaries of all lawyers. The attorney plans to introduce a new management plan under which the firm’s nonlawyer office administrator would have general charge of all business matters but would not participate in any decisions involving legal judgment. The administrator would be paid a fixed annual salary and would be included as a participant in the firm’s bonus plan on the same basis as the lawyers in the firm. This would usually yield a bonus of approximately one-fourth to one-third of the administrator’s total annual compensation. The amount paid to the administrator will not exceed the compensation commonly paid to law office administrators within the local legal community. Is it proper for the attorney to institute such a plan?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the amount paid to the administrator does not exceed the compensation commonly paid to law office administrators within the local legal community.","B":"Yes, because an employee of the firm may be compensated based on the profits of the firm.","C":"No, because the administrator’s bonus is computed on the same basis as those of the lawyers in the firm.","D":"No, because the administrator’s compensation is derived from the legal fees of the firm’s lawyers."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. This plan for part of the administrator’s compensation to be based upon the net profits of the firm is expressly permitted under Model Rule 5.4(a) as long as the nonlawyer is an employee of the firm. Importantly, the other aspects of the arrangement do not give the administrator an inappropriate ownership interest in the firm, nor in any right to control the legal work done in the law firm, thus avoiding any violation of the Rule. A is incorrect. The plan is proper, but the reason why it is proper has nothing to do with the compensation paid to other law office administrators in the community. This answer wrongly refers to Model Rule 1.5(a)’s guidelines on reasonable fees for lawyers, which lists as one factor fees paid to other similarly situated lawyers in the community. Be wary of answer choices referring to standards in the local community, as these are often wrong. C is incorrect. The Rules do not address the basis on which net profits may be divided and distributed to nonlawyer employees. Such decisions are properly made by the firm. D is incorrect. Although lawyers may not share legal fees with nonlawyers, Model Rule 5.4(a) contains an exception for compensation plans that involve the sharing of net profits with nonlawyer employees.","rule":"B is the correct answer. This plan for part of the administrator’s compensation to be based upon the net profits of the firm is expressly permitted under Model Rule 5.4(a) as long as the nonlawyer is an employee of the firm. Importantly, the other aspects of the arrangement do not give the administrator an inappropriate ownership interest in the firm, nor in any right to control the legal work done in the law firm, thus avoiding any violation of the Rule. A is incorrect. The plan is proper, but the reason why it is proper has nothing to do with the compensation paid to other law office administrators in the community. This answer wrongly refers to Model Rule 1.5(a)’s guidelines on reasonable fees for lawyers, which lists as one factor fees paid to other similarly situated lawyers in the community. Be wary of answer choices referring to standards in the local community, as these are often wrong. C is incorrect. The Rules do not address the basis on which net profits may be divided and distributed to nonlawyer employees. Such decisions are properly made by the firm. D is incorrect. Although lawyers may not share legal fees with nonlawyers, Model Rule 5.4(a) contains an exception for compensation plans that involve the sharing of net profits with nonlawyer employees.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the attorney to institute such a plan?","rule":"B is the correct answer. This plan for part of the administrator’s compensation to be based upon the net profits of the firm is expressly permitted under Model Rule 5.4(a) as long as the nonlawyer is an employee of the firm. Importantly, the other aspects of the arrangement do not give the administrator an inappropriate ownership interest in the firm, nor in any right to control the legal work done in the law firm, thus avoiding any violation of the Rule. A is incorrect. The plan is proper, but the reason why it is proper has nothing to do with the compensation paid to other law office administrators in the community. This answer wrongly refers to Model Rule 1.5(a)’s guidelines on reasonable fees for lawyers, which lists as one factor fees paid to other similarly situated lawyers in the community. Be wary of answer choices referring to standards in the local community, as these are often wrong. C is incorrect. The Rules do not address the basis on which net profits may be divided and distributed to nonlawyer employees. Such decisions are properly made by the firm. D is incorrect. Although lawyers may not share legal fees with nonlawyers, Model Rule 5.4(a) contains an exception for compensation plans that involve the sharing of net profits with nonlawyer employees.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. This plan for part of the administrator’s compensation to be based upon the net profits of the firm is expressly permitted under Model Rule 5.4(a) as long as the nonlawyer is an employee of the firm. Importantly, the other aspects of the arrangement do not give the administrator an inappropriate ownership interest in the firm, nor in any right to control the legal work done in the law firm, thus avoiding any violation of the Rule. A is incorrect. The plan is proper, but the reason why it is proper has nothing to do with the compensation paid to other law office administrators in the community. This answer wrongly refers to Model Rule 1.5(a)’s guidelines on reasonable fees for lawyers, which lists as one factor fees paid to other similarly situated lawyers in the community. Be wary of answer choices referring to standards in the local community, as these are often wrong. C is incorrect. The Rules do not address the basis on which net profits may be divided and distributed to nonlawyer employees. Such decisions are properly made by the firm. D is incorrect. Although lawyers may not share legal fees with nonlawyers, Model Rule 5.4(a) contains an exception for compensation plans that involve the sharing of net profits with nonlawyer employees.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.4(a)","Model Rule 1.5(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[83,84],"sourceAnswerPages":[126,127],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师是一家拥有 50 名律师的律师事务所的高级合伙人。事务所向每名律师支付固定年薪。此外，在年末，每名律师都会从事务所利润中获得奖金；奖金按照该律师年薪占全体律师固定年薪总额的比例计算。该律师计划引入一项新的管理方案，由事务所的一名非律师办公室行政主管全面负责所有商业事务，但不参与任何涉及法律判断的决定。行政主管将获得固定年薪，并与事务所律师按相同计算方式参加事务所奖金计划。奖金通常约占行政主管全年总报酬的四分之一至三分之一。向行政主管支付的总额，不会超过当地法律界通常向律师事务所行政主管支付的报酬。该律师实施这一方案是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为向行政主管支付的金额没有超过当地法律界通常向律师事务所行政主管支付的报酬。","B":"适当，因为事务所可以根据事务所利润向其雇员支付报酬。","C":"不适当，因为行政主管的奖金与事务所律师的奖金采用相同计算方式。","D":"不适当，因为行政主管的报酬来源于事务所律师收取的法律服务费。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。只要非律师是事务所雇员，《职业行为示范规则》5.4(a) 就明确允许将事务所净利润作为该雇员部分报酬的计算依据。需要特别注意的是，这项安排的其他方面既没有赋予行政主管不当的事务所所有权，也没有给予其控制事务所法律工作的权利，因而避免了违反该规则。A 不正确。该方案确实适当，但其适当性与当地其他律师事务所行政主管获得的报酬无关。该选项错误地援引了规则 1.5(a) 关于律师合理收费的指引；该指引把当地处境相似的其他律师收取的费用列为考虑因素之一。应警惕援引当地标准的选项，因为它们经常是错误的。C 不正确。规则没有规定应当以何种方式划分并向非律师雇员分配净利润。这类决定应由事务所作出。D 不正确。虽然律师不得与非律师分享法律服务费，但规则 5.4(a) 对涉及与非律师雇员分享净利润的报酬计划规定了例外。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-059","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":59,"topic":"Model Rule 1.6(a) / Model Rule 3.3(b) / Model Rule 1.6","question":"An attorney represented a client in an action against the client’s former partner to recover damages for breach of contract. During the representation, the client presented the attorney with incontrovertible proof that the former partner had committed perjury in a prior action that was resolved in the partner’s favor. Neither the attorney nor the client was involved in any way in the prior action. The attorney believes that it would be detrimental to the client’s best interests to reveal the perjury because of the implication that might be drawn from the former close personal and business relationship between the client and the former partner. Would it be proper for the attorney to fail to disclose the perjury to the tribunal?","choices":{"A":"No, because the information is unprivileged.","B":"No, because the attorney has knowledge that the former partner perpetrated a fraud on the tribunal.","C":"Yes, because neither the client nor the attorney was involved in the prior action.","D":"Yes, because the attorney believes that the disclosure would be detrimental to the client’s best interests."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. The information regarding the perjury committed by the client’s former partner is “information relating to the representation” of the client, which generally may not be disclosed without client consent. Model Rule 1.6(a). Confidential information may be disclosed without client consent under Model Rule 1.6(a) when “the disclosure is impliedly authorized in order to carry out the representation,” but there would be no implied authorization where, as here, the disclosure would likely harm the client. A is incorrect. The lawyer’s duty to protect client information under Model Rule 1.6(a) covers more than just privileged information. It covers all “information relating to the representation of a client.” Thus, it doesn’t matter whether the information is privileged or not. B is incorrect. Under Model Rule 3.3(b), if a lawyer is representing a client and offers material evidence (such as a person’s testimony) that is later discovered to be false, that lawyer must “take reasonable remedial measures, including, if necessary, disclosure to the tribunal.” However, that duty does not apply here because the attorney did not represent the client in the proceeding where the client’s former partner committed the perjury. Therefore, the attorney has no duty to disclose the perjury under Model Rule 3.3(b). C is incorrect. The duties created by Model Rule 3.3(b) last only to the end of the case. Here, the case in which the former partner committed perjury is over. Consequently, even if the attorney had represented the client in that proceeding, the attorney would have no duty at this time to disclose the former partner’s perjury. Importantly, if a lawyer first learns of the false evidence after the case is over, the duty of confidentiality under Model Rule 1.6 prohibits disclosure.","rule":"D is the correct answer. The information regarding the perjury committed by the client’s former partner is “information relating to the representation” of the client, which generally may not be disclosed without client consent. Model Rule 1.6(a). Confidential information may be disclosed without client consent under Model Rule 1.6(a) when “the disclosure is impliedly authorized in order to carry out the representation,” but there would be no implied authorization where, as here, the disclosure would likely harm the client. A is incorrect. The lawyer’s duty to protect client information under Model Rule 1.6(a) covers more than just privileged information. It covers all “information relating to the representation of a client.” Thus, it doesn’t matter whether the information is privileged or not. B is incorrect. Under Model Rule 3.3(b), if a lawyer is representing a client and offers material evidence (such as a person’s testimony) that is later discovered to be false, that lawyer must “take reasonable remedial measures, including, if necessary, disclosure to the tribunal.” However, that duty does not apply here because the attorney did not represent the client in the proceeding where the client’s former partner committed the perjury. Therefore, the attorney has no duty to disclose the perjury under Model Rule 3.3(b). C is incorrect. The duties created by Model Rule 3.3(b) last only to the end of the case. Here, the case in which the former partner committed perjury is over. Consequently, even if the attorney had represented the client in that proceeding, the attorney would have no duty at this time to disclose the former partner’s perjury. Importantly, if a lawyer first learns of the false evidence after the case is over, the duty of confidentiality under Model Rule 1.6 prohibits disclosure.","irac":{"issue":"Would it be proper for the attorney to fail to disclose the perjury to the tribunal?","rule":"D is the correct answer. The information regarding the perjury committed by the client’s former partner is “information relating to the representation” of the client, which generally may not be disclosed without client consent. Model Rule 1.6(a). Confidential information may be disclosed without client consent under Model Rule 1.6(a) when “the disclosure is impliedly authorized in order to carry out the representation,” but there would be no implied authorization where, as here, the disclosure would likely harm the client. A is incorrect. The lawyer’s duty to protect client information under Model Rule 1.6(a) covers more than just privileged information. It covers all “information relating to the representation of a client.” Thus, it doesn’t matter whether the information is privileged or not. B is incorrect. Under Model Rule 3.3(b), if a lawyer is representing a client and offers material evidence (such as a person’s testimony) that is later discovered to be false, that lawyer must “take reasonable remedial measures, including, if necessary, disclosure to the tribunal.” However, that duty does not apply here because the attorney did not represent the client in the proceeding where the client’s former partner committed the perjury. Therefore, the attorney has no duty to disclose the perjury under Model Rule 3.3(b). C is incorrect. The duties created by Model Rule 3.3(b) last only to the end of the case. Here, the case in which the former partner committed perjury is over. Consequently, even if the attorney had represented the client in that proceeding, the attorney would have no duty at this time to disclose the former partner’s perjury. Importantly, if a lawyer first learns of the false evidence after the case is over, the duty of confidentiality under Model Rule 1.6 prohibits disclosure.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. The information regarding the perjury committed by the client’s former partner is “information relating to the representation” of the client, which generally may not be disclosed without client consent. Model Rule 1.6(a). Confidential information may be disclosed without client consent under Model Rule 1.6(a) when “the disclosure is impliedly authorized in order to carry out the representation,” but there would be no implied authorization where, as here, the disclosure would likely harm the client. A is incorrect. The lawyer’s duty to protect client information under Model Rule 1.6(a) covers more than just privileged information. It covers all “information relating to the representation of a client.” Thus, it doesn’t matter whether the information is privileged or not. B is incorrect. Under Model Rule 3.3(b), if a lawyer is representing a client and offers material evidence (such as a person’s testimony) that is later discovered to be false, that lawyer must “take reasonable remedial measures, including, if necessary, disclosure to the tribunal.” However, that duty does not apply here because the attorney did not represent the client in the proceeding where the client’s former partner committed the perjury. Therefore, the attorney has no duty to disclose the perjury under Model Rule 3.3(b). C is incorrect. The duties created by Model Rule 3.3(b) last only to the end of the case. Here, the case in which the former partner committed perjury is over. Consequently, even if the attorney had represented the client in that proceeding, the attorney would have no duty at this time to disclose the former partner’s perjury. Importantly, if a lawyer first learns of the false evidence after the case is over, the duty of confidentiality under Model Rule 1.6 prohibits disclosure.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.6(a)","Model Rule 3.3(b)","Model Rule 1.6"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[84,85],"sourceAnswerPages":[127,128],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理委托人起诉其前合伙人违反合同并请求损害赔偿。在代理过程中，委托人向律师提供了无可辩驳的证据，证明前合伙人在先前一宗最终由前合伙人胜诉的案件中作了伪证。该律师和委托人均未以任何方式参与先前案件。律师认为，披露伪证会损害委托人的最佳利益，因为他人可能会从委托人与前合伙人先前密切的私人及商业关系中作出不利推断。该律师不向审裁机构披露伪证是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"不适当，因为该信息不受特权保护。","B":"不适当，因为该律师知道前合伙人曾对审裁机构实施欺诈。","C":"适当，因为委托人和律师均未参与先前案件。","D":"适当，因为律师认为披露会损害委托人的最佳利益。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。有关委托人前合伙人作伪证的信息，属于与该委托人的“代理有关的信息”，未经委托人同意通常不得披露。《职业行为示范规则》1.6(a)。如果“为实施代理而默示授权披露”，规则 1.6(a) 允许未经委托人同意披露保密信息；但像本题这样，披露很可能损害委托人时，就不存在默示授权。A 不正确。律师根据规则 1.6(a) 保护委托人信息的义务，范围不止于受特权保护的信息，而是涵盖所有“与委托人代理有关的信息”。因此，该信息是否受特权保护并不重要。B 不正确。根据规则 3.3(b)，律师在代理委托人的过程中提出重大证据（例如某人的证言），后来发现证据虚假时，必须“采取合理补救措施，必要时包括向审裁机构披露”。不过，该义务不适用于本题，因为前合伙人作伪证的程序中，该律师并未代理这名委托人。因此，律师在规则 3.3(b) 下没有披露伪证的义务。C 不正确。规则 3.3(b) 规定的义务只持续至案件终结。本题中，前合伙人作伪证的案件已经结束。因此，即使该律师曾在该程序中代理委托人，此时也没有义务披露前合伙人的伪证。需要特别注意的是，如果律师直到案件结束后才首次得知虚假证据，规则 1.6 的保密义务会禁止其披露。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-model-060","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Model Questions","questionNumber":60,"topic":"Model Rule 1.8(g) / Model Rule 1.2(a)","question":"An attorney represented 10 plaintiffs in a case against a railroad in which the plaintiffs were injured when a train derailed. The railroad offered the attorney a $500,000 lump sum settlement for the 10 plaintiffs. The attorney allocated the $500,000 among the 10 plaintiffs with the amount paid each plaintiff dependent on the nature and extent of that plaintiff’s injuries. The attorney reasonably believed the division was fair to each plaintiff. The railroad would not settle any of the claims unless all were settled. The attorney told each plaintiff the total amount the railroad was prepared to pay, the amount that the individual would receive, and the basis on which that amount was calculated. The attorney did not tell any plaintiff the amount to be received by any other plaintiff. The attorney believed that if she revealed to each plaintiff the amount of each individual settlement, it might jeopardize the settlement. Each of the plaintiffs agreed to his or her settlement amount and was satisfied with that amount. Is the attorney subject to discipline for entering into this settlement?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney was aiding the lawyer for the railroad in making a lump sum settlement.","B":"Yes, because no individual plaintiff knew the amount to be received by any other plaintiff.","C":"No, because disclosing all settlements to each plaintiff might have jeopardized the entire settlement.","D":"No, because the attorney reasonably believed that the division was fair and each plaintiff agreed to his or her settlement and was satisfied with the amount."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. Because this is an aggregate settlement of all of the clients’ claims, the attorney must obtain the clients’ informed consent after disclosing “the existence and nature of all the claims” and “the participation of each person in the settlement.” Model Rule 1.8(g). The necessary disclosure must include information about “all the material terms of the settlement, including what the other clients will receive or pay if the settlement or plea offer is accepted.” Model Rule 1.8(g), Comment [13]. Even if the settlement could be jeopardized, Model Rule 1.2(a) protects each client’s right to have the final say in deciding whether to accept or reject a settlement. Ultimately, it is not the lawyer’s place to decide what is the best outcome for the clients. A is incorrect. A lawyer may participate in an aggregate settlement of civil claims as long as the lawyer complies with the requirement of informed consent by all clients. Model Rule 1.8(g). Here, by not giving the clients all the information regarding what each one of them would receive under the settlement, the attorney failed to do so. C is incorrect. As stated above, regardless of whether the settlement will be jeopardized, the clients need to know all the material terms of the settlement. D is incorrect. The lawyer must still make a full disclosure to the clients whether or not the lawyer believes the aggregate settlement offer is fair, or whether or not the clients are satisfied with the amount. Always be wary of such “no-harm, no-foul” answer options.","rule":"B is the correct answer. Because this is an aggregate settlement of all of the clients’ claims, the attorney must obtain the clients’ informed consent after disclosing “the existence and nature of all the claims” and “the participation of each person in the settlement.” Model Rule 1.8(g). The necessary disclosure must include information about “all the material terms of the settlement, including what the other clients will receive or pay if the settlement or plea offer is accepted.” Model Rule 1.8(g), Comment [13]. Even if the settlement could be jeopardized, Model Rule 1.2(a) protects each client’s right to have the final say in deciding whether to accept or reject a settlement. Ultimately, it is not the lawyer’s place to decide what is the best outcome for the clients. A is incorrect. A lawyer may participate in an aggregate settlement of civil claims as long as the lawyer complies with the requirement of informed consent by all clients. Model Rule 1.8(g). Here, by not giving the clients all the information regarding what each one of them would receive under the settlement, the attorney failed to do so. C is incorrect. As stated above, regardless of whether the settlement will be jeopardized, the clients need to know all the material terms of the settlement. D is incorrect. The lawyer must still make a full disclosure to the clients whether or not the lawyer believes the aggregate settlement offer is fair, or whether or not the clients are satisfied with the amount. Always be wary of such “no-harm, no-foul” answer options.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for entering into this settlement?","rule":"B is the correct answer. Because this is an aggregate settlement of all of the clients’ claims, the attorney must obtain the clients’ informed consent after disclosing “the existence and nature of all the claims” and “the participation of each person in the settlement.” Model Rule 1.8(g). The necessary disclosure must include information about “all the material terms of the settlement, including what the other clients will receive or pay if the settlement or plea offer is accepted.” Model Rule 1.8(g), Comment [13]. Even if the settlement could be jeopardized, Model Rule 1.2(a) protects each client’s right to have the final say in deciding whether to accept or reject a settlement. Ultimately, it is not the lawyer’s place to decide what is the best outcome for the clients. A is incorrect. A lawyer may participate in an aggregate settlement of civil claims as long as the lawyer complies with the requirement of informed consent by all clients. Model Rule 1.8(g). Here, by not giving the clients all the information regarding what each one of them would receive under the settlement, the attorney failed to do so. C is incorrect. As stated above, regardless of whether the settlement will be jeopardized, the clients need to know all the material terms of the settlement. D is incorrect. The lawyer must still make a full disclosure to the clients whether or not the lawyer believes the aggregate settlement offer is fair, or whether or not the clients are satisfied with the amount. Always be wary of such “no-harm, no-foul” answer options.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. Because this is an aggregate settlement of all of the clients’ claims, the attorney must obtain the clients’ informed consent after disclosing “the existence and nature of all the claims” and “the participation of each person in the settlement.” Model Rule 1.8(g). The necessary disclosure must include information about “all the material terms of the settlement, including what the other clients will receive or pay if the settlement or plea offer is accepted.” Model Rule 1.8(g), Comment [13]. Even if the settlement could be jeopardized, Model Rule 1.2(a) protects each client’s right to have the final say in deciding whether to accept or reject a settlement. Ultimately, it is not the lawyer’s place to decide what is the best outcome for the clients. A is incorrect. A lawyer may participate in an aggregate settlement of civil claims as long as the lawyer complies with the requirement of informed consent by all clients. Model Rule 1.8(g). Here, by not giving the clients all the information regarding what each one of them would receive under the settlement, the attorney failed to do so. C is incorrect. As stated above, regardless of whether the settlement will be jeopardized, the clients need to know all the material terms of the settlement. D is incorrect. The lawyer must still make a full disclosure to the clients whether or not the lawyer believes the aggregate settlement offer is fair, or whether or not the clients are satisfied with the amount. Always be wary of such “no-harm, no-foul” answer options.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.8(g)","Model Rule 1.2(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[85],"sourceAnswerPages":[128],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理 10 名原告起诉一家铁路公司；列车脱轨造成这些原告受伤。铁路公司向该律师提出一项总额 500,000 美元、涵盖全部 10 名原告的和解方案。律师根据每名原告所受伤害的性质与程度，在 10 名原告之间分配这 500,000 美元。律师合理地认为，这种分配对每名原告均属公平。除非所有索赔一并和解，铁路公司不会和解其中任何一项索赔。律师告诉每名原告，铁路公司愿意支付的总额、该原告个人将获得的金额，以及该金额的计算依据。律师没有告诉任何原告其他原告各自将获得多少。律师认为，如果向每名原告披露每一份个人和解的金额，可能会危及整个和解。每名原告均同意自己获得的和解金额，并对此感到满意。该律师达成这一和解，是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该律师协助铁路公司的律师达成一揽子和解。","B":"是，因为任何一名原告都不知道其他原告将获得的金额。","C":"否，因为向每名原告披露全部个人和解金额，可能危及整个和解。","D":"否，因为该律师合理地认为分配公平，且每名原告均同意并满意于自己获得的金额。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。由于这是对所有委托人索赔的合并和解，律师必须在披露“全部索赔的存在及性质”以及“每个人在和解中的参与份额”后，取得各委托人的知情同意。《职业行为示范规则》1.8(g)。必要披露必须包括“和解的全部重大条款，包括在接受和解或答辩交易要约时，其他委托人将收取或支付什么”。规则 1.8(g) 评注 [13]。即使披露可能危及和解，规则 1.2(a) 仍保护每名委托人对是否接受或拒绝和解拥有最终决定权。归根结底，应当由委托人而非律师决定什么结果对他们最好。A 不正确。只要律师遵守取得所有委托人知情同意的要求，就可以参与民事索赔的合并和解。规则 1.8(g)。本题中，律师没有向委托人提供每个人在和解下将取得金额的全部信息，因此未满足该要求。C 不正确。如上所述，无论和解是否会受到危及，委托人都必须知悉和解的全部重大条款。D 不正确。无论律师是否认为合并和解要约公平，也无论委托人是否满意其各自金额，律师仍必须向委托人作出充分披露。应始终警惕这种“没有损害，就没有违规”的选项。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-001","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":1,"topic":"Model Rule 7.2(b)","question":"An attorney and a restaurant owner entered into a reciprocal referral arrangement. The attorney agreed to prominently display ads for the restaurant in her office, and to mention the restaurant to all of her clients who requested a recommendation of a nearby place to eat. In return, the owner agreed to prominently display ads for the attorney’s firm in the restaurant and to recommend the attorney to any of his customers who indicated a need for the services provided by the attorney. The reciprocal referral agreement was not exclusive, and the clients and customers would be informed of the existence and nature of the agreement. Is the attorney subject to discipline for entering into this agreement?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because she asked the owner to place ads for the firm in the restaurant.","B":"Yes, because the agreement provided something of value to the restaurant owner in return for recommending the attorney’s services.","C":"No, because she did not pay the restaurant owner for the referrals.","D":"No, because the agreement is not exclusive, and the clients and customers will be informed of the existence and nature of the agreement."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2(b), an attorney “shall not give anything of value to a person for recommending the lawyer’s services.” However, there are exceptions for paying the costs of advertisements or communications, legal service plans or not-for-profit or qualified referral plans, or the purchase of a practice. Here, the agreement with the restaurant owner clearly violates this Rule, since the owner is receiving valuable advertising space and recommendations. For this Rule, keep in mind that the Rule says “anything of value,” not simply “money.” A is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney is giving the restaurant owner something of value for recommending the lawyer’s services, not that the attorney asked the owner to put up some ads. Keep in mind that the Rules are particularly concerned with advertising practices that could lead to information that is misleading or overreaching, and an arrangement like this could likely lead to the dissemination of problematic information. C is incorrect. As stated above, the Rule is concerned with “anything of value,” not simply “money.” D is incorrect. Keep in mind that many wrong answers on the MPRE will set up a “no-harm, no-foul” situation. This answer choice implies that a lawyer could pay for referrals as long as he or she let everyone in on the action, which clearly makes little sense ethically. An answer choice like this can be eliminated immediately.","rule":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2(b), an attorney “shall not give anything of value to a person for recommending the lawyer’s services.” However, there are exceptions for paying the costs of advertisements or communications, legal service plans or not-for-profit or qualified referral plans, or the purchase of a practice. Here, the agreement with the restaurant owner clearly violates this Rule, since the owner is receiving valuable advertising space and recommendations. For this Rule, keep in mind that the Rule says “anything of value,” not simply “money.” A is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney is giving the restaurant owner something of value for recommending the lawyer’s services, not that the attorney asked the owner to put up some ads. Keep in mind that the Rules are particularly concerned with advertising practices that could lead to information that is misleading or overreaching, and an arrangement like this could likely lead to the dissemination of problematic information. C is incorrect. As stated above, the Rule is concerned with “anything of value,” not simply “money.” D is incorrect. Keep in mind that many wrong answers on the MPRE will set up a “no-harm, no-foul” situation. This answer choice implies that a lawyer could pay for referrals as long as he or she let everyone in on the action, which clearly makes little sense ethically. An answer choice like this can be eliminated immediately.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for entering into this agreement?","rule":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2(b), an attorney “shall not give anything of value to a person for recommending the lawyer’s services.” However, there are exceptions for paying the costs of advertisements or communications, legal service plans or not-for-profit or qualified referral plans, or the purchase of a practice. Here, the agreement with the restaurant owner clearly violates this Rule, since the owner is receiving valuable advertising space and recommendations. For this Rule, keep in mind that the Rule says “anything of value,” not simply “money.” A is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney is giving the restaurant owner something of value for recommending the lawyer’s services, not that the attorney asked the owner to put up some ads. Keep in mind that the Rules are particularly concerned with advertising practices that could lead to information that is misleading or overreaching, and an arrangement like this could likely lead to the dissemination of problematic information. C is incorrect. As stated above, the Rule is concerned with “anything of value,” not simply “money.” D is incorrect. Keep in mind that many wrong answers on the MPRE will set up a “no-harm, no-foul” situation. This answer choice implies that a lawyer could pay for referrals as long as he or she let everyone in on the action, which clearly makes little sense ethically. An answer choice like this can be eliminated immediately.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2(b), an attorney “shall not give anything of value to a person for recommending the lawyer’s services.” However, there are exceptions for paying the costs of advertisements or communications, legal service plans or not-for-profit or qualified referral plans, or the purchase of a practice. Here, the agreement with the restaurant owner clearly violates this Rule, since the owner is receiving valuable advertising space and recommendations. For this Rule, keep in mind that the Rule says “anything of value,” not simply “money.” A is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney is giving the restaurant owner something of value for recommending the lawyer’s services, not that the attorney asked the owner to put up some ads. Keep in mind that the Rules are particularly concerned with advertising practices that could lead to information that is misleading or overreaching, and an arrangement like this could likely lead to the dissemination of problematic information. C is incorrect. As stated above, the Rule is concerned with “anything of value,” not simply “money.” D is incorrect. Keep in mind that many wrong answers on the MPRE will set up a “no-harm, no-foul” situation. This answer choice implies that a lawyer could pay for referrals as long as he or she let everyone in on the action, which clearly makes little sense ethically. An answer choice like this can be eliminated immediately.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.2(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[130],"sourceAnswerPages":[235,236],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师与一家餐厅的所有者达成相互推荐安排。律师同意在其办公室显眼位置展示餐厅广告，并向所有请求推荐附近用餐地点的委托人提及该餐厅。作为交换，餐厅所有者同意在餐厅显眼位置展示该律师事务所的广告，并向任何表示需要该律师所提供服务的顾客推荐该律师。该相互推荐协议并非排他性协议，而且委托人与顾客都会被告知协议的存在和性质。律师因达成这一协议是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为她要求餐厅所有者在餐厅放置事务所广告。","B":"是，因为该协议以向餐厅所有者提供有价值之物为条件，换取其推荐律师服务。","C":"否，因为她没有为推荐向餐厅所有者支付金钱。","D":"否，因为该协议并非排他性协议，而且委托人与顾客都会被告知协议的存在和性质。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。根据《职业行为示范规则》7.2(b)，律师“不得为他人推荐律师服务而向其给予任何有价值之物”。不过，支付广告或通信成本、法律服务计划或非营利或合格转介计划的费用，以及购买律师业务，均属于例外。本题中，与餐厅所有者达成的协议明显违反该规则，因为餐厅所有者获得了有价值的广告空间和推荐机会。对于该规则，应记住其措辞是“任何有价值之物”，而不只是“金钱”。A 不正确。本题问题在于律师为了让餐厅所有者推荐律师服务而给予其有价值之物，而不在于律师请求所有者张贴广告。规则特别关注可能导致误导或过度影响信息的广告做法，而这种安排很可能导致问题信息被传播。C 不正确。如上所述，规则关注的是“任何有价值之物”，而不只是“金钱”。D 不正确。MPRE 的许多错误选项会设计成“没有损害，就没有违规”的情形。该选项暗示，只要律师让所有人都知道安排，就可以为推荐支付报酬，这在职业伦理上显然不合理。此类选项可以立即排除。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-002","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":2,"topic":"Model Rule 7.5(d) / Model Rule 7.5","question":"Attorneys Alpha and Beta practiced law under the firm name of Alpha and Beta. When Beta died, Alpha did not change the firm name. Thereafter, Alpha entered into an arrangement with another attorney, Gamma. Gamma pays Alpha a certain sum each month for office space and use of Alpha’s law library and for secretarial services, but Alpha and Gamma each has his own clients, and neither participates in the representation of the other’s clients or shares in fees paid. On the entrance to the suite of offices shared by Alpha and Gamma are the words “Law Firm of Alpha, Beta, and Gamma.” Is Alpha subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because Beta was deceased when Alpha made the arrangement with Gamma.","B":"Yes, because Gamma is not a partner of Alpha.","C":"No, because Alpha and Beta were partners at the time of Beta’s death.","D":"No, because Gamma is paying a share of the rent and office expenses."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 7.5(d) states that “lawyers may state or imply that they practice in a partnership or other organization only when that is the fact.” As Comment [2] explains, “lawyers sharing office facilities, but who are not in fact associated with each other in a law firm, may not denominate themselves as, for example, ‘Smith and Jones,’ for that title suggests that they are practicing law together in a firm.” Model Rule 7.5, Comment [2]. The big issue here is that Alpha and Gamma are holding themselves out to the world as partners, when they clearly are not. Keep in mind the Rules’ concern with keeping the public informed. A is incorrect. A law firm can continue to use “the names of deceased members where there has been a continuing succession in the firm’s identity.” Model Rule 7.5, Comment [1]. Here, Alpha is still around, so he could use the name. The big issue is that the new “firm” name misleads the public into thinking Alpha and Gamma are somehow connected. C is incorrect. This answer choice should be discarded immediately, as it contains no mention of Gamma, who is clearly at issue here. D is incorrect. This answer choice should also be discarded immediately, as paying for rent and office expenses does not make one a partner with another individual, and the new name implies that there is some kind of partnership between Alpha and Gamma.","rule":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 7.5(d) states that “lawyers may state or imply that they practice in a partnership or other organization only when that is the fact.” As Comment [2] explains, “lawyers sharing office facilities, but who are not in fact associated with each other in a law firm, may not denominate themselves as, for example, ‘Smith and Jones,’ for that title suggests that they are practicing law together in a firm.” Model Rule 7.5, Comment [2]. The big issue here is that Alpha and Gamma are holding themselves out to the world as partners, when they clearly are not. Keep in mind the Rules’ concern with keeping the public informed. A is incorrect. A law firm can continue to use “the names of deceased members where there has been a continuing succession in the firm’s identity.” Model Rule 7.5, Comment [1]. Here, Alpha is still around, so he could use the name. The big issue is that the new “firm” name misleads the public into thinking Alpha and Gamma are somehow connected. C is incorrect. This answer choice should be discarded immediately, as it contains no mention of Gamma, who is clearly at issue here. D is incorrect. This answer choice should also be discarded immediately, as paying for rent and office expenses does not make one a partner with another individual, and the new name implies that there is some kind of partnership between Alpha and Gamma.","irac":{"issue":"” Is Alpha subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 7.5(d) states that “lawyers may state or imply that they practice in a partnership or other organization only when that is the fact.” As Comment [2] explains, “lawyers sharing office facilities, but who are not in fact associated with each other in a law firm, may not denominate themselves as, for example, ‘Smith and Jones,’ for that title suggests that they are practicing law together in a firm.” Model Rule 7.5, Comment [2]. The big issue here is that Alpha and Gamma are holding themselves out to the world as partners, when they clearly are not. Keep in mind the Rules’ concern with keeping the public informed. A is incorrect. A law firm can continue to use “the names of deceased members where there has been a continuing succession in the firm’s identity.” Model Rule 7.5, Comment [1]. Here, Alpha is still around, so he could use the name. The big issue is that the new “firm” name misleads the public into thinking Alpha and Gamma are somehow connected. C is incorrect. This answer choice should be discarded immediately, as it contains no mention of Gamma, who is clearly at issue here. D is incorrect. This answer choice should also be discarded immediately, as paying for rent and office expenses does not make one a partner with another individual, and the new name implies that there is some kind of partnership between Alpha and Gamma.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 7.5(d) states that “lawyers may state or imply that they practice in a partnership or other organization only when that is the fact.” As Comment [2] explains, “lawyers sharing office facilities, but who are not in fact associated with each other in a law firm, may not denominate themselves as, for example, ‘Smith and Jones,’ for that title suggests that they are practicing law together in a firm.” Model Rule 7.5, Comment [2]. The big issue here is that Alpha and Gamma are holding themselves out to the world as partners, when they clearly are not. Keep in mind the Rules’ concern with keeping the public informed. A is incorrect. A law firm can continue to use “the names of deceased members where there has been a continuing succession in the firm’s identity.” Model Rule 7.5, Comment [1]. Here, Alpha is still around, so he could use the name. The big issue is that the new “firm” name misleads the public into thinking Alpha and Gamma are somehow connected. C is incorrect. This answer choice should be discarded immediately, as it contains no mention of Gamma, who is clearly at issue here. D is incorrect. This answer choice should also be discarded immediately, as paying for rent and office expenses does not make one a partner with another individual, and the new name implies that there is some kind of partnership between Alpha and Gamma.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.5(d)","Model Rule 7.5"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[130,131],"sourceAnswerPages":[236],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"律师甲和律师乙以“甲乙律师事务所”的名称执业。乙去世后，甲没有变更事务所名称。此后，甲与另一名律师丙达成安排。丙每月向甲支付一定金额，以使用办公空间、甲的法律图书室及秘书服务；但甲和丙各自拥有自己的委托人，双方均不参与代理对方的委托人，也不分享对方收取的费用。甲与丙共用办公套间的入口处写着“甲、乙、丙律师事务所”。甲是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为甲与丙作出该安排时，乙已经去世。","B":"是，因为丙不是甲的合伙人。","C":"否，因为乙去世时，甲和乙是合伙人。","D":"否，因为丙正在分担租金和办公费用。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》7.5(d) 规定：“只有在事实确实如此时，律师才可以声称或暗示自己在合伙组织或其他组织中执业。”正如评注 [2] 所解释的：“共用办公设施但事实上并未在同一律师事务所相互联合的律师，不得将自己称为例如‘史密斯与琼斯’，因为该名称会暗示二人正在同一事务所共同执业。”规则 7.5 评注 [2]。本题的核心问题是，甲和丙向外界宣称自己是合伙人，而事实显然并非如此。应记住，规则重视确保公众获得准确信息。A 不正确。如果“事务所身份具有连续的承继关系”，律师事务所可以继续使用已故成员的姓名。规则 7.5 评注 [1]。本题中，甲仍在事务所，因此可以继续使用原名称。真正的问题是，新“事务所”名称会误导公众，使人认为甲和丙存在某种联合关系。C 不正确。该选项完全没有提及本题明显涉及的丙，应立即排除。D 不正确。该选项同样应立即排除，因为分担租金和办公费用不会使一个人成为另一个人的合伙人，而新名称却暗示甲和丙之间存在某种合伙关系。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-003","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":3,"topic":"Model Rule 1.11(a)(2) / Model Rule 1.11","question":"An attorney was employed as a lawyer by the state Environmental Control Commission (ECC) for 10 years. During the last two years of her employment, the attorney spent most of her time in the preparation, trial, and appeal of a case involving the discharge by a corporation of industrial effluent into a river in the state. The judgment in the case, which is now final, contained a finding of a continuing and knowing discharge of a dangerous substance into a major stream by the corporation and assessed a penalty of $25,000. The governing statute also provides for private actions for damages by persons injured by the discharge of the effluent. The attorney recently left the employment of ECC and went into private practice. Three landowners have brought private damage actions against the corporation. They claim their farms were contaminated because they irrigated them with water that contained dangerous chemicals discharged by the corporation. The corporation has asked the attorney to represent it in defense of the three pending actions. The judgment in the prior case was not determinative of the corporation’s liability, and all of the information acquired by the attorney when representing the ECC is now a matter of public record. Is the attorney subject to discipline if she represents the corporation in these actions?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the judgment in the prior case is not determinative of the corporation’s liability.","B":"Yes, because the attorney had substantial responsibility in the matter while employed by ECC.","C":"No, because the attorney has acquired special competence in the matter.","D":"No, because all information acquired by the attorney while representing ECC is now a matter of public record."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.11(a)(2), “a lawyer who has formerly served as a public officer or employee of the government . . . shall not otherwise represent a client in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee” unless authorized to do so by the government agency. Importantly, Comment [3] states that these provisions are “designed not only to protect the former client, but also to prevent a lawyer from exploiting public office for the advantage of another client.” Model Rule 1.11, Comment [3]. Here, although the corporation is asking the attorney for help, it does not change the analysis. The attorney still participated personally and substantially in the investigation of the corporation’s effluent, and the Rule does not limit itself only to suits against the government. As a side note, don’t shy away from an answer choice simply because there have been several in a row (here, the first three correct choices were B). A is incorrect. As stated above, the issue is that the attorney participated personally and substantially in the investigation of the corporation’s effluent and could exploit information used during his government service. C is incorrect. “Special competence” does not protect a lawyer from actions that have the appearance of impropriety or unfairness. D is incorrect. Even if the information is in the public domain, the Rule seeks to keep the attorney from exploiting his prior government service.","rule":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.11(a)(2), “a lawyer who has formerly served as a public officer or employee of the government . . . shall not otherwise represent a client in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee” unless authorized to do so by the government agency. Importantly, Comment [3] states that these provisions are “designed not only to protect the former client, but also to prevent a lawyer from exploiting public office for the advantage of another client.” Model Rule 1.11, Comment [3]. Here, although the corporation is asking the attorney for help, it does not change the analysis. The attorney still participated personally and substantially in the investigation of the corporation’s effluent, and the Rule does not limit itself only to suits against the government. As a side note, don’t shy away from an answer choice simply because there have been several in a row (here, the first three correct choices were B). A is incorrect. As stated above, the issue is that the attorney participated personally and substantially in the investigation of the corporation’s effluent and could exploit information used during his government service. C is incorrect. “Special competence” does not protect a lawyer from actions that have the appearance of impropriety or unfairness. D is incorrect. Even if the information is in the public domain, the Rule seeks to keep the attorney from exploiting his prior government service.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline if she represents the corporation in these actions?","rule":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.11(a)(2), “a lawyer who has formerly served as a public officer or employee of the government . . . shall not otherwise represent a client in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee” unless authorized to do so by the government agency. Importantly, Comment [3] states that these provisions are “designed not only to protect the former client, but also to prevent a lawyer from exploiting public office for the advantage of another client.” Model Rule 1.11, Comment [3]. Here, although the corporation is asking the attorney for help, it does not change the analysis. The attorney still participated personally and substantially in the investigation of the corporation’s effluent, and the Rule does not limit itself only to suits against the government. As a side note, don’t shy away from an answer choice simply because there have been several in a row (here, the first three correct choices were B). A is incorrect. As stated above, the issue is that the attorney participated personally and substantially in the investigation of the corporation’s effluent and could exploit information used during his government service. C is incorrect. “Special competence” does not protect a lawyer from actions that have the appearance of impropriety or unfairness. D is incorrect. Even if the information is in the public domain, the Rule seeks to keep the attorney from exploiting his prior government service.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.11(a)(2), “a lawyer who has formerly served as a public officer or employee of the government . . . shall not otherwise represent a client in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee” unless authorized to do so by the government agency. Importantly, Comment [3] states that these provisions are “designed not only to protect the former client, but also to prevent a lawyer from exploiting public office for the advantage of another client.” Model Rule 1.11, Comment [3]. Here, although the corporation is asking the attorney for help, it does not change the analysis. The attorney still participated personally and substantially in the investigation of the corporation’s effluent, and the Rule does not limit itself only to suits against the government. As a side note, don’t shy away from an answer choice simply because there have been several in a row (here, the first three correct choices were B). A is incorrect. As stated above, the issue is that the attorney participated personally and substantially in the investigation of the corporation’s effluent and could exploit information used during his government service. C is incorrect. “Special competence” does not protect a lawyer from actions that have the appearance of impropriety or unfairness. D is incorrect. Even if the information is in the public domain, the Rule seeks to keep the attorney from exploiting his prior government service.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.11(a)(2)","Model Rule 1.11"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[131,132],"sourceAnswerPages":[236,237],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师曾在州环境控制委员会（ECC）担任律师 10 年。任职最后两年，她将大部分时间用于准备、审判及上诉一宗案件，该案涉及某公司向州内一条河流排放工业废水。该案判决现已确定，其中认定该公司持续且明知地向一条主要河流排放危险物质，并处以 25,000 美元罚款。适用法律还规定，因废水排放受到伤害的人可以提起私人损害赔偿诉讼。该律师最近离开 ECC，开始私人执业。三名土地所有者已经对该公司提起私人损害赔偿诉讼。他们声称，因使用含有该公司所排危险化学物质的河水灌溉，自己的农场受到污染。该公司请求律师代理其抗辩这三宗待决诉讼。先前案件的判决并不能决定公司在这些案件中的责任；而律师代理 ECC 时取得的全部信息，现均已成为公开记录。如果律师在这些诉讼中代理该公司，是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为先前案件的判决不能决定公司的责任。","B":"是，因为该律师受雇于 ECC 时曾对该事项承担重大职责。","C":"否，因为该律师在该事项上已经获得特别专业能力。","D":"否，因为该律师代理 ECC 时取得的全部信息现在都属于公开记录。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。根据《职业行为示范规则》1.11(a)(2)，“先前曾担任政府公职人员或政府雇员的律师……不得以其他方式代理委托人处理其作为公职人员或雇员时曾亲自且实质性参与的事项”，除非取得相关政府机构授权。评注 [3] 特别指出，这些规定“不仅旨在保护前委托人，也旨在防止律师利用公职为另一名委托人牟利”。规则 1.11 评注 [3]。本题中，虽然是公司主动向律师寻求帮助，但这并不改变分析。该律师仍曾亲自且实质性地参与对公司废水排放的调查，而规则并不限于仅仅针对政府提起的诉讼。另请注意，不要仅仅因为连续数题的正确答案都是同一个字母就回避该选项（本套题前三题的正确答案恰好均为 B）。A 不正确。如上所述，问题在于该律师曾亲自且实质性地参与对公司废水排放的调查，并可能利用其政府任职期间所掌握的信息。C 不正确。“特别专业能力”不能使律师免受那些造成行为不当或不公平观感的规则约束。D 不正确。即使信息已进入公共领域，该规则仍旨在防止律师利用先前的政府任职经历。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-004","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":4,"topic":"Model Rule 1.1","question":"An attorney is skilled in trying personal injury cases. The attorney accepted the representation of a plaintiff in a personal injury case on a contingent fee basis. While preparing the case for trial, the attorney realized that the direct examination and cross-examination of the medical experts would involve medical issues with which the attorney was not familiar and, as a consequence, the attorney might not be able to represent the plaintiff competently. Without informing the plaintiff, the attorney consulted another attorney who is also a medical doctor and a recognized specialist in the care and treatment of injuries of the type sustained by the plaintiff. The two attorneys agreed that the other attorney would participate in the trial to the limited extent of conducting the direct examination and cross-examination of the medical experts and that the attorneys would divide the fee in proportion to the services performed and the responsibility assumed by each. Upon conclusion of the matter, the attorney provided the plaintiff with a written statement explaining the relevant fees. Was the arrangement between the two attorneys proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the fee to be paid by the plaintiff was not increased by reason of the other attorney’s association.","B":"Yes, because the fee would be divided in proportion to the services performed and the responsibility assumed by each.","C":"No, because the plaintiff was not advised of the association of the other attorney.","D":"No, because, upon conclusion of the matter, the attorney provided the plaintiff with a written statement setting forth the method of determining both the fee and the division of the fee with the other attorney."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. Although an attorney can retain or contract with other lawyers outside the lawyer’s own firm to assist in the provision of legal service, “the lawyer should ordinarily obtain informed consent from the client.” Model Rule 1.1, Comment [6]. Imagine being a client and suddenly finding out some strange lawyer you had no say in choosing was working on your case. While an attorney has the right to make decisions in many aspects of a representation, in the case of bringing in another attorney, the attorney must ask for client consent. A is incorrect. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice, which should be dismissed immediately. B is incorrect. Similar to answer choice A, this “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice should be dismissed immediately. D is incorrect. As in the other two choices, this choice implies that the attorney can do something major like retaining another attorney without consulting the client so long as the attorney makes up for it later. Here, simply telling the client how the fees will be split does not fix the problem of an unauthorized and unexpected attorney working on the case.","rule":"C is the correct answer. Although an attorney can retain or contract with other lawyers outside the lawyer’s own firm to assist in the provision of legal service, “the lawyer should ordinarily obtain informed consent from the client.” Model Rule 1.1, Comment [6]. Imagine being a client and suddenly finding out some strange lawyer you had no say in choosing was working on your case. While an attorney has the right to make decisions in many aspects of a representation, in the case of bringing in another attorney, the attorney must ask for client consent. A is incorrect. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice, which should be dismissed immediately. B is incorrect. Similar to answer choice A, this “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice should be dismissed immediately. D is incorrect. As in the other two choices, this choice implies that the attorney can do something major like retaining another attorney without consulting the client so long as the attorney makes up for it later. Here, simply telling the client how the fees will be split does not fix the problem of an unauthorized and unexpected attorney working on the case.","irac":{"issue":"Was the arrangement between the two attorneys proper?","rule":"C is the correct answer. Although an attorney can retain or contract with other lawyers outside the lawyer’s own firm to assist in the provision of legal service, “the lawyer should ordinarily obtain informed consent from the client.” Model Rule 1.1, Comment [6]. Imagine being a client and suddenly finding out some strange lawyer you had no say in choosing was working on your case. While an attorney has the right to make decisions in many aspects of a representation, in the case of bringing in another attorney, the attorney must ask for client consent. A is incorrect. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice, which should be dismissed immediately. B is incorrect. Similar to answer choice A, this “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice should be dismissed immediately. D is incorrect. As in the other two choices, this choice implies that the attorney can do something major like retaining another attorney without consulting the client so long as the attorney makes up for it later. Here, simply telling the client how the fees will be split does not fix the problem of an unauthorized and unexpected attorney working on the case.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. Although an attorney can retain or contract with other lawyers outside the lawyer’s own firm to assist in the provision of legal service, “the lawyer should ordinarily obtain informed consent from the client.” Model Rule 1.1, Comment [6]. Imagine being a client and suddenly finding out some strange lawyer you had no say in choosing was working on your case. While an attorney has the right to make decisions in many aspects of a representation, in the case of bringing in another attorney, the attorney must ask for client consent. A is incorrect. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice, which should be dismissed immediately. B is incorrect. Similar to answer choice A, this “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice should be dismissed immediately. D is incorrect. As in the other two choices, this choice implies that the attorney can do something major like retaining another attorney without consulting the client so long as the attorney makes up for it later. Here, simply telling the client how the fees will be split does not fix the problem of an unauthorized and unexpected attorney working on the case.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[132],"sourceAnswerPages":[237,238],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师擅长人身伤害案件审判。他以风险代理费方式接受了一名人身伤害案件原告的委托。在准备案件审判时，律师发现，对医学专家的直接询问和交叉询问将涉及自己不熟悉的医学问题，因而自己可能无法称职地代理原告。律师没有告知原告，而是咨询了另一名同时具备医生资格、并且是治疗原告所受同类伤害之公认专家的律师。二人约定，由另一名律师在审判中有限参与，负责对医学专家进行直接询问和交叉询问，并由两名律师按照各自提供的服务及承担的责任比例分配费用。事项结束后，律师向原告提供了一份说明相关费用的书面陈述。两名律师之间的安排是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为原告应支付的费用没有因另一名律师的参与而增加。","B":"适当，因为费用将按照两名律师各自提供的服务及承担的责任比例分配。","C":"不适当，因为没有告知原告另一名律师将参与代理。","D":"不适当，因为事项结束后，律师向原告提供了一份说明费用计算方式及两名律师之间费用分配方式的书面陈述。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。律师虽然可以聘请或约请本事务所以外的其他律师协助提供法律服务，但“通常应取得委托人的知情同意”。《职业行为示范规则》1.1 评注 [6]。可以想象，如果作为委托人突然发现一名完全陌生、自己从未参与选择的律师正在处理自己的案件，会有什么感受。律师虽然有权就代理的许多方面作出决定，但在引入另一名律师时，必须请求委托人同意。A 不正确。这又是“没有损害，就没有违规”型选项，应立即排除。B 不正确。与 A 类似，这也是一个“没有损害，就没有违规”型选项，应立即排除。D 不正确。与另外两个选项相同，该选项暗示律师可以在不咨询委托人的情况下做出聘请另一名律师这样重大的决定，只要事后予以弥补即可。本题中，仅仅告知委托人费用将如何分配，并不能补救一名未经授权、委托人也未预期的律师已经参与案件这一问题。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-005","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":5,"topic":"Model Rule 3.6(a) / Model Rule 3.6(b)(2)","question":"An attorney represents a famous politician in an action against a newspaper for libel. The case has attracted much publicity, and a jury trial has been demanded. After one of the pretrial hearings, as the attorney left the courthouse, news reporters interviewed the attorney. In responding to questions, the attorney revealed the identity of the reporter. Is the attorney subject to discipline for making this statement?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney identified a prospective witness in the case.","B":"Yes, because prospective jurors might learn of the attorney’s remarks.","C":"No, because the statement relates to a matter of public record.","D":"No, because the trial has not commenced."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. During representation, a lawyer “shall not make an extrajudicial statement that the lawyer knows or reasonably should know will be disseminated by means of public communication and will have a substantial likelihood of materially prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding in the matter.” Model Rule 3.6(a). However, there is an exception for “information contained in a public record.” Model Rule 3.6(b)(2). Here, since the politician has sued the newspaper for libel, the identity of the reporter is a matter of public record. A is incorrect. While the reporter may be a prospective witness, the important point is that lawyers may make statements regarding matters of public record. B is incorrect. While prospective jurors may learn of the attorney’s remarks, this does not automatically make any remarks subject to discipline. D is incorrect. Although the trial has not yet commenced, the Rule is concerned with “prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding.” Model Rule 3.6(a). Clearly, prejudice can be created before a trial has actually commenced.","rule":"C is the correct answer. During representation, a lawyer “shall not make an extrajudicial statement that the lawyer knows or reasonably should know will be disseminated by means of public communication and will have a substantial likelihood of materially prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding in the matter.” Model Rule 3.6(a). However, there is an exception for “information contained in a public record.” Model Rule 3.6(b)(2). Here, since the politician has sued the newspaper for libel, the identity of the reporter is a matter of public record. A is incorrect. While the reporter may be a prospective witness, the important point is that lawyers may make statements regarding matters of public record. B is incorrect. While prospective jurors may learn of the attorney’s remarks, this does not automatically make any remarks subject to discipline. D is incorrect. Although the trial has not yet commenced, the Rule is concerned with “prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding.” Model Rule 3.6(a). Clearly, prejudice can be created before a trial has actually commenced.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for making this statement?","rule":"C is the correct answer. During representation, a lawyer “shall not make an extrajudicial statement that the lawyer knows or reasonably should know will be disseminated by means of public communication and will have a substantial likelihood of materially prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding in the matter.” Model Rule 3.6(a). However, there is an exception for “information contained in a public record.” Model Rule 3.6(b)(2). Here, since the politician has sued the newspaper for libel, the identity of the reporter is a matter of public record. A is incorrect. While the reporter may be a prospective witness, the important point is that lawyers may make statements regarding matters of public record. B is incorrect. While prospective jurors may learn of the attorney’s remarks, this does not automatically make any remarks subject to discipline. D is incorrect. Although the trial has not yet commenced, the Rule is concerned with “prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding.” Model Rule 3.6(a). Clearly, prejudice can be created before a trial has actually commenced.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. During representation, a lawyer “shall not make an extrajudicial statement that the lawyer knows or reasonably should know will be disseminated by means of public communication and will have a substantial likelihood of materially prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding in the matter.” Model Rule 3.6(a). However, there is an exception for “information contained in a public record.” Model Rule 3.6(b)(2). Here, since the politician has sued the newspaper for libel, the identity of the reporter is a matter of public record. A is incorrect. While the reporter may be a prospective witness, the important point is that lawyers may make statements regarding matters of public record. B is incorrect. While prospective jurors may learn of the attorney’s remarks, this does not automatically make any remarks subject to discipline. D is incorrect. Although the trial has not yet commenced, the Rule is concerned with “prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding.” Model Rule 3.6(a). Clearly, prejudice can be created before a trial has actually commenced.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.6(a)","Model Rule 3.6(b)(2)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[132,133],"sourceAnswerPages":[238],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理一名知名政治人物起诉一家报社诽谤。案件受到广泛关注，并已要求采用陪审团审判。一次审前听证结束后，律师离开法院时接受新闻记者采访。律师在回答问题时披露了涉案记者的身份。该律师是否因作出这一陈述而应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该律师指出了一名案件潜在证人的身份。","B":"是，因为候选陪审员可能获知该律师的言论。","C":"否，因为该陈述涉及公开记录中的事项。","D":"否，因为审判尚未开始。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。在代理期间，律师“不得作出其知道或理应知道会通过公开传播方式扩散，并且极有可能对该事项的裁判程序造成重大不利影响的庭外陈述”。《职业行为示范规则》3.6(a)。不过，“公开记录中包含的信息”属于例外。规则 3.6(b)(2)。本题中，由于该政治人物已起诉报社诽谤，涉案记者的身份属于公开记录。A 不正确。虽然该记者可能是潜在证人，但关键在于，律师可以就公开记录中的事项作出陈述。B 不正确。候选陪审员虽然可能获知律师言论，但这不会使任何此类言论自动成为纪律处分事由。D 不正确。虽然审判尚未开始，该规则关注的却是“对裁判程序造成不利影响”。规则 3.6(a)。显然，在审判实际开始前也可能产生这种不利影响。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-006","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":6,"topic":"Model Rule 1.11(a)(2) / Model Rule 1.11","question":"An attorney was employed as an assistant prosecutor in the district attorney’s office during the time that an investigation of a suspect was being conducted by that office. The attorney took no part in the investigation and had no knowledge of the facts other than those disclosed in the press. Two months ago, the attorney left the district attorney’s office and formed a partnership with another attorney. Last week, the suspect was indicted for offenses allegedly disclosed by the prior investigation. The suspect asked the attorney to represent him. The attorney declined to do so, but suggested his new partner. The district attorney’s office was promptly notified and consented to the representation. The attorney did not participate in the representation or share in the fee. Is the new partner subject to discipline if he represents the suspect?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney was employed in the district attorney’s office while the investigation of the suspect was being conducted.","B":"Yes, because the district attorney’s office was promptly notified and consented to the representation.","C":"No, because the attorney did not participate in the representation or share in the fee.","D":"No, because the attorney had no responsibility for or knowledge of the facts of the investigation of the suspect."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.11(a)(2), “a lawyer who has formerly served as a public officer or employee of the government . . . shall not otherwise represent a client in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee” unless authorized to do so by the government agency. Here, the attorney had nothing to do with the investigation. Remember, Model Rule 1.11 is concerned with former government employees exploiting knowledge about a party unfairly. Here, there is no knowledge to exploit. A is incorrect. As stated above, for Model Rule 1.11 to apply, the lawyer must have “participated personally and substantially” in the matter. Although the attorney worked in the district attorney’s office, he had nothing to do with the earlier investigation. B is incorrect. Although the district attorney’s office consented, the big issue is that the attorney had nothing to do with the earlier investigation. Thus, the district attorney’s office’s consent was unnecessary. C is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.11, if a lawyer is disqualified from representation, no lawyer in that firm may undertake the representation unless the disqualified lawyer is screened, takes no fee, and written notice is given to the appropriate government agency. Here, since the attorney was not “personally and substantially” involved in the earlier investigation, such actions would be unnecessary.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.11(a)(2), “a lawyer who has formerly served as a public officer or employee of the government . . . shall not otherwise represent a client in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee” unless authorized to do so by the government agency. Here, the attorney had nothing to do with the investigation. Remember, Model Rule 1.11 is concerned with former government employees exploiting knowledge about a party unfairly. Here, there is no knowledge to exploit. A is incorrect. As stated above, for Model Rule 1.11 to apply, the lawyer must have “participated personally and substantially” in the matter. Although the attorney worked in the district attorney’s office, he had nothing to do with the earlier investigation. B is incorrect. Although the district attorney’s office consented, the big issue is that the attorney had nothing to do with the earlier investigation. Thus, the district attorney’s office’s consent was unnecessary. C is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.11, if a lawyer is disqualified from representation, no lawyer in that firm may undertake the representation unless the disqualified lawyer is screened, takes no fee, and written notice is given to the appropriate government agency. Here, since the attorney was not “personally and substantially” involved in the earlier investigation, such actions would be unnecessary.","irac":{"issue":"Is the new partner subject to discipline if he represents the suspect?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.11(a)(2), “a lawyer who has formerly served as a public officer or employee of the government . . . shall not otherwise represent a client in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee” unless authorized to do so by the government agency. Here, the attorney had nothing to do with the investigation. Remember, Model Rule 1.11 is concerned with former government employees exploiting knowledge about a party unfairly. Here, there is no knowledge to exploit. A is incorrect. As stated above, for Model Rule 1.11 to apply, the lawyer must have “participated personally and substantially” in the matter. Although the attorney worked in the district attorney’s office, he had nothing to do with the earlier investigation. B is incorrect. Although the district attorney’s office consented, the big issue is that the attorney had nothing to do with the earlier investigation. Thus, the district attorney’s office’s consent was unnecessary. C is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.11, if a lawyer is disqualified from representation, no lawyer in that firm may undertake the representation unless the disqualified lawyer is screened, takes no fee, and written notice is given to the appropriate government agency. Here, since the attorney was not “personally and substantially” involved in the earlier investigation, such actions would be unnecessary.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.11(a)(2), “a lawyer who has formerly served as a public officer or employee of the government . . . shall not otherwise represent a client in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee” unless authorized to do so by the government agency. Here, the attorney had nothing to do with the investigation. Remember, Model Rule 1.11 is concerned with former government employees exploiting knowledge about a party unfairly. Here, there is no knowledge to exploit. A is incorrect. As stated above, for Model Rule 1.11 to apply, the lawyer must have “participated personally and substantially” in the matter. Although the attorney worked in the district attorney’s office, he had nothing to do with the earlier investigation. B is incorrect. Although the district attorney’s office consented, the big issue is that the attorney had nothing to do with the earlier investigation. Thus, the district attorney’s office’s consent was unnecessary. C is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.11, if a lawyer is disqualified from representation, no lawyer in that firm may undertake the representation unless the disqualified lawyer is screened, takes no fee, and written notice is given to the appropriate government agency. Here, since the attorney was not “personally and substantially” involved in the earlier investigation, such actions would be unnecessary.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.11(a)(2)","Model Rule 1.11"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[133],"sourceAnswerPages":[238,239],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师曾在地方检察官办公室担任助理检察官，当时该办公室正在调查一名嫌疑人。该律师没有参与调查，对相关事实的了解也仅限于媒体报道。两个月前，律师离开地方检察官办公室，与另一名律师组成合伙事务所。上周，嫌疑人因先前调查涉嫌揭示的犯罪而受到起诉。嫌疑人请求该律师代理自己。律师拒绝，但推荐了自己的新合伙人。地方检察官办公室很快收到通知，并同意该代理。该律师既不参与代理，也不分享代理费用。如果新合伙人代理嫌疑人，他是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为调查嫌疑人期间，该律师受雇于地方检察官办公室。","B":"是，因为地方检察官办公室很快收到通知并同意了该代理。","C":"否，因为该律师不参与代理，也不分享代理费用。","D":"否，因为该律师对嫌疑人的调查既不负有职责，也不了解相关事实。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。根据《职业行为示范规则》1.11(a)(2)，“先前曾担任政府公职人员或政府雇员的律师……不得以其他方式代理委托人处理其作为公职人员或雇员时曾亲自且实质性参与的事项”，除非取得政府机构授权。本题中，该律师与调查毫无关系。应记住，规则 1.11 所关注的是前政府雇员不公平地利用有关某一方的知识。本题中根本没有可供利用的知识。A 不正确。如上所述，规则 1.11 要求律师曾“亲自且实质性参与”有关事项。该律师虽然在地方检察官办公室工作，但与先前调查毫无关系。B 不正确。地方检察官办公室虽然同意代理，但核心问题是该律师未参与先前调查。因此，没有必要取得地方检察官办公室同意。C 不正确。根据规则 1.11，如果一名律师被取消代理资格，同一事务所的其他律师也不得接受代理，除非将该律师进行有效隔离、该律师不分享费用，并向有关政府机构发出书面通知。本题中，该律师没有“亲自且实质性参与”先前调查，因此无须采取这些措施。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-007","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":7,"topic":"Model Rule 1.3","question":"An attorney represents a plaintiff in a civil action that was filed a year ago and is about to be set for trial. The plaintiff informed the attorney that he could be available at any time during the months of October, November, and December. In discussing possible trial dates with opposing counsel and the court clerk, the attorney was advised that a trial date on October 5 was available and that the next available trial date would be December 10. Without first consulting the plaintiff, the attorney requested the December 10 trial date because she was representing a defendant in a felony criminal trial that was set for October 20 and she wanted as much time as possible to prepare for that trial. She was not court-appointed counsel in the criminal case. The later trial date will not prejudice the client. Was it proper for the attorney to agree to the December trial date without obtaining the plaintiff’s consent?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the plaintiff will not be prejudiced by the delay.","B":"Yes, because a criminal trial takes precedence over a civil trial.","C":"No, because the attorney should manage her calendar so that her cases can be tried promptly.","D":"No, because the attorney was not court-appointed counsel in the criminal case."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer should act with reasonable diligence and promptness when representing a client. Model Rule 1.3. Here, since the civil action was filed a year ago, the client gave his availability through December, the “delay” is only a little over two months, and the client will not be prejudiced by the delay, it is absolutely reasonable for the attorney to take the December 10 trial date. B is incorrect. There is no special Rule stating that criminal trials are more important than civil matters. C is incorrect. As stated above, there is no indication that her actions are causing an unreasonable delay in the civil trial. D is incorrect. Even if the attorney was court-appointed, that fact would not create special issues concerning scheduling of the two trials in this situation.","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer should act with reasonable diligence and promptness when representing a client. Model Rule 1.3. Here, since the civil action was filed a year ago, the client gave his availability through December, the “delay” is only a little over two months, and the client will not be prejudiced by the delay, it is absolutely reasonable for the attorney to take the December 10 trial date. B is incorrect. There is no special Rule stating that criminal trials are more important than civil matters. C is incorrect. As stated above, there is no indication that her actions are causing an unreasonable delay in the civil trial. D is incorrect. Even if the attorney was court-appointed, that fact would not create special issues concerning scheduling of the two trials in this situation.","irac":{"issue":"Was it proper for the attorney to agree to the December trial date without obtaining the plaintiff’s consent?","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer should act with reasonable diligence and promptness when representing a client. Model Rule 1.3. Here, since the civil action was filed a year ago, the client gave his availability through December, the “delay” is only a little over two months, and the client will not be prejudiced by the delay, it is absolutely reasonable for the attorney to take the December 10 trial date. B is incorrect. There is no special Rule stating that criminal trials are more important than civil matters. C is incorrect. As stated above, there is no indication that her actions are causing an unreasonable delay in the civil trial. D is incorrect. Even if the attorney was court-appointed, that fact would not create special issues concerning scheduling of the two trials in this situation.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer should act with reasonable diligence and promptness when representing a client. Model Rule 1.3. Here, since the civil action was filed a year ago, the client gave his availability through December, the “delay” is only a little over two months, and the client will not be prejudiced by the delay, it is absolutely reasonable for the attorney to take the December 10 trial date. B is incorrect. There is no special Rule stating that criminal trials are more important than civil matters. C is incorrect. As stated above, there is no indication that her actions are causing an unreasonable delay in the civil trial. D is incorrect. Even if the attorney was court-appointed, that fact would not create special issues concerning scheduling of the two trials in this situation.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[133,134],"sourceAnswerPages":[239],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师在一宗一年前提起、即将确定审判日期的民事诉讼中代理原告。原告告知律师，自己在 10 月、11 月和 12 月的任何时间都可以出庭。律师与对方律师和法院书记员讨论可能的审判日期时获知，10 月 5 日可以安排审判，而再下一个可用日期是 12 月 10 日。律师没有先咨询原告，便请求将审判日期定为 12 月 10 日，因为她正在代理一名被告参加定于 10 月 20 日进行的重罪刑事审判，并希望尽可能多留出时间准备该案。她在刑事案件中并非由法院指定的律师。较晚的审判日期不会损害该民事委托人的利益。律师未经原告同意便接受 12 月的审判日期，是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为延期不会损害原告利益。","B":"适当，因为刑事审判优先于民事审判。","C":"不适当，因为律师应当妥善管理日程，使自己的案件能够及时审判。","D":"不适当，因为律师在刑事案件中并非由法院指定。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。律师代理委托人时，应以合理的勤勉和及时程度行事。《职业行为示范规则》1.3。本题中，民事诉讼虽已提起一年，但委托人表示自己在 12 月前均可出庭，所谓“延期”仅略多于两个月，而且延期不会损害委托人，因此律师选择 12 月 10 日作为审判日期完全合理。B 不正确。没有任何特殊规则规定刑事审判比民事事项更重要。C 不正确。如上所述，没有迹象表明她的行为使民事审判发生不合理延误。D 不正确。即使该律师是法院指定的律师，这一事实在本题情形下也不会使两宗案件的排期产生任何特殊问题。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-008","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":8,"topic":"Model Rule 1.7(a)(1) / Model Rule 1.7 / Model Rule 1.9(a)","question":"Three men were indicted for the armed robbery of the cashier of a grocery store. Together, two of them met with an attorney and asked the attorney to represent them. The attorney then interviewed the two of them separately. Each told the attorney that the robbery had been committed by the third man while they sat in the third man’s car outside the store. They also said that the third man had said he needed some cigarettes, and that neither knew of the man’s plan to rob the cashier. The attorney agreed to the joint representation. One week prior to the trial date, one of the men represented by the attorney told the attorney that he wanted to plea bargain and that he was prepared to turn state’s evidence against both of the other two suspects. It is proper for attorney to:","choices":{"A":"request court approval to withdraw as lawyer for both men.","B":"continue to represent the man who had not stated that he wanted to plea bargain and, with the other man’s consent and court approval, withdraw as his lawyer.","C":"continue to represent the man who wanted to plea bargain and, with the other man’s consent and court approval, withdraw as that man’s lawyer.","D":"continue to represent both men, but not call the man who wanted to plea bargain as a witness."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer cannot represent two clients if the representation of one client will be directly adverse to another. Model Rule 1.7(a)(1). Here, the one client’s plan to turn state’s evidence against the other client clearly violates this restriction. Importantly, since the common representation has failed (since one client is turning against another), the attorney must ordinarily withdraw from representing all of the clients. Model Rule 1.7, Comment [29]. B is incorrect. “A lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client unless the former client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing.” Model Rule 1.9(a). Importantly, the other man’s consent is only to the withdrawal, not to the continued representation. This is an instance showing the importance of careful reading. C is incorrect. Considering the basic problem, which client the attorney chooses to stay with and that particular client’s argument does not change the analysis. D is incorrect. Choosing not to call one client as a witness does not fix the situation, since one client’s interest is directly adverse to the other client’s.","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer cannot represent two clients if the representation of one client will be directly adverse to another. Model Rule 1.7(a)(1). Here, the one client’s plan to turn state’s evidence against the other client clearly violates this restriction. Importantly, since the common representation has failed (since one client is turning against another), the attorney must ordinarily withdraw from representing all of the clients. Model Rule 1.7, Comment [29]. B is incorrect. “A lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client unless the former client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing.” Model Rule 1.9(a). Importantly, the other man’s consent is only to the withdrawal, not to the continued representation. This is an instance showing the importance of careful reading. C is incorrect. Considering the basic problem, which client the attorney chooses to stay with and that particular client’s argument does not change the analysis. D is incorrect. Choosing not to call one client as a witness does not fix the situation, since one client’s interest is directly adverse to the other client’s.","irac":{"issue":"Three men were indicted for the armed robbery of the cashier of a grocery store. Together, two of them met with an attorney and asked the attorney to represent them. The attorney then interviewed the two of them separately. Each told the attorney that the robbery had been committed by the third man while they sat in the third man’s car outside the store. They also said that the third man had said he needed some cigarettes, and that neither knew of the man’s plan to rob the cashier. The attorney agreed to the joint representation. One week prior to the trial date, one of the men represented by the attorney told the attorney that he wanted to plea bargain and that he was prepared to turn state’s evidence against both of the other two suspects. It is proper for attorney to:","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer cannot represent two clients if the representation of one client will be directly adverse to another. Model Rule 1.7(a)(1). Here, the one client’s plan to turn state’s evidence against the other client clearly violates this restriction. Importantly, since the common representation has failed (since one client is turning against another), the attorney must ordinarily withdraw from representing all of the clients. Model Rule 1.7, Comment [29]. B is incorrect. “A lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client unless the former client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing.” Model Rule 1.9(a). Importantly, the other man’s consent is only to the withdrawal, not to the continued representation. This is an instance showing the importance of careful reading. C is incorrect. Considering the basic problem, which client the attorney chooses to stay with and that particular client’s argument does not change the analysis. D is incorrect. Choosing not to call one client as a witness does not fix the situation, since one client’s interest is directly adverse to the other client’s.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer cannot represent two clients if the representation of one client will be directly adverse to another. Model Rule 1.7(a)(1). Here, the one client’s plan to turn state’s evidence against the other client clearly violates this restriction. Importantly, since the common representation has failed (since one client is turning against another), the attorney must ordinarily withdraw from representing all of the clients. Model Rule 1.7, Comment [29]. B is incorrect. “A lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client unless the former client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing.” Model Rule 1.9(a). Importantly, the other man’s consent is only to the withdrawal, not to the continued representation. This is an instance showing the importance of careful reading. C is incorrect. Considering the basic problem, which client the attorney chooses to stay with and that particular client’s argument does not change the analysis. D is incorrect. Choosing not to call one client as a witness does not fix the situation, since one client’s interest is directly adverse to the other client’s.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.7(a)(1)","Model Rule 1.7","Model Rule 1.9(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[134,135],"sourceAnswerPages":[239,240],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"三名男子因持械抢劫一家杂货店收银员而受到起诉。其中两人一同会见一名律师，请求律师代理他们。律师随后分别询问了二人。二人各自告诉律师，抢劫由第三名男子实施，当时他们坐在商店外第三人的汽车中。他们还说，第三人曾说自己需要买香烟，而二人均不知道他打算抢劫收银员。律师同意共同代理二人。审判日期前一周，其中一名由该律师代理的男子告诉律师，他希望进行认罪协商，并准备转为控方证人，指证另外两名嫌疑人。该律师适宜采取的做法是：","choicesZh":{"A":"请求法院准许其退出对两名男子的代理。","B":"继续代理未表示希望进行认罪协商的男子，并在另一名男子同意且法院批准后退出对另一人的代理。","C":"继续代理希望进行认罪协商的男子，并在另一名男子同意且法院批准后退出对另一人的代理。","D":"继续代理两名男子，但不传唤希望进行认罪协商的男子作证。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。如果代理一名委托人会与另一名委托人的利益直接冲突，律师不得同时代理二人。《职业行为示范规则》1.7(a)(1)。本题中，一名委托人计划转为控方证人指证另一名委托人，显然触犯这项限制。需要特别注意的是，共同代理已经失败（一名委托人转而对抗另一人），律师通常必须退出对所有委托人的代理。规则 1.7 评注 [29]。B 不正确。“律师先前曾就某一事项代理委托人的，此后不得在同一事项或与其实质相关的事项中，代理其利益与前委托人存在重大不利关系的另一人，除非前委托人作出经书面确认的知情同意。”规则 1.9(a)。关键是，另一名男子的同意仅针对律师退出代理，并未同意律师继续代理对方。这说明仔细阅读选项十分重要。C 不正确。考虑到根本问题，无论律师选择继续代理哪一名委托人，也无论该委托人的主张是什么，都不会改变分析。D 不正确。不传唤其中一名委托人作证不能解决问题，因为一名委托人的利益已经与另一名委托人的利益直接冲突。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-009","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":9,"topic":"Rule 2.9(A)(2)","question":"While presiding over the trial of a highly publicized antitrust case, a judge received in the mail a lengthy letter from a local attorney. The letter discussed the law applicable to the case. The judge knew that the attorney did not represent either party or any client whose interests could be affected by the outcome. The judge read the letter and, without mentioning its receipt to the lawyers in the pending case, filed the letter in his general file on antitrust litigation. Later, after reading the trial briefs in the case, the judge concluded that the attorney’s letter better explained the law applicable to the case pending before him than either of the trial briefs. The judge followed the attorney’s reasoning in formulating his decision. Prior to rendering his decision, the judge did not tell the attorneys anything about the letter. Was it proper for the judge to consider the attorney’s letter?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the judge did not initiate the communication with the attorney.","B":"Yes, because the attorney did not represent any client whose interests could be affected by the outcome.","C":"No, because the judge, prior to rendering his decision, did not communicate its contents to all counsel and give them an opportunity to respond.","D":"No, because the attorney is not of record as counsel in the case."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. Each side is entitled to know and to respond to every fact and every argument presented by the other side. If a judge could consult any legal source he or she wished without identifying it and giving each side the right to respond, the adversarial nature of the judicial system would collapse. At the same time, there are instances in which a judge will need to get advice on the law, especially when, as in these facts, the lawyers have not stated the situation clearly. To enable the judge to do this without subverting the adversarial process, Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(2) permits a judge to “obtain the written advice of a disinterested expert on the law applicable to a proceeding before the judge, if the judge gives advance notice to the parties of the person to be consulted and the subject matter of the advice to be solicited, and affords the parties a reasonable opportunity to object and respond to the notice and to the advice received.” A is incorrect. Always be wary of this type of answer choice. The MPRE frequently uses the idea of an attorney or judge not initiating the initial contact as a wrong answer choice. Considering the concerns addressed by the Rule, who started the communication is clearly irrelevant. This type of answer choice should be discarded immediately. B is incorrect. It’s not enough that the expert consulted by the judge be impartial. The parties are entitled to know what the expert has been asked and what he or she has advised. After all, the expert may be wrong or the judge may not have communicated the issues correctly. The parties may want to respond, and they must be given the opportunity to do so. D is incorrect. This answer choice seems to imply that an expert’s impartiality could make the communication proper. However, as stated above, this is not the case.","rule":"C is the correct answer. Each side is entitled to know and to respond to every fact and every argument presented by the other side. If a judge could consult any legal source he or she wished without identifying it and giving each side the right to respond, the adversarial nature of the judicial system would collapse. At the same time, there are instances in which a judge will need to get advice on the law, especially when, as in these facts, the lawyers have not stated the situation clearly. To enable the judge to do this without subverting the adversarial process, Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(2) permits a judge to “obtain the written advice of a disinterested expert on the law applicable to a proceeding before the judge, if the judge gives advance notice to the parties of the person to be consulted and the subject matter of the advice to be solicited, and affords the parties a reasonable opportunity to object and respond to the notice and to the advice received.” A is incorrect. Always be wary of this type of answer choice. The MPRE frequently uses the idea of an attorney or judge not initiating the initial contact as a wrong answer choice. Considering the concerns addressed by the Rule, who started the communication is clearly irrelevant. This type of answer choice should be discarded immediately. B is incorrect. It’s not enough that the expert consulted by the judge be impartial. The parties are entitled to know what the expert has been asked and what he or she has advised. After all, the expert may be wrong or the judge may not have communicated the issues correctly. The parties may want to respond, and they must be given the opportunity to do so. D is incorrect. This answer choice seems to imply that an expert’s impartiality could make the communication proper. However, as stated above, this is not the case.","irac":{"issue":"Was it proper for the judge to consider the attorney’s letter?","rule":"C is the correct answer. Each side is entitled to know and to respond to every fact and every argument presented by the other side. If a judge could consult any legal source he or she wished without identifying it and giving each side the right to respond, the adversarial nature of the judicial system would collapse. At the same time, there are instances in which a judge will need to get advice on the law, especially when, as in these facts, the lawyers have not stated the situation clearly. To enable the judge to do this without subverting the adversarial process, Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(2) permits a judge to “obtain the written advice of a disinterested expert on the law applicable to a proceeding before the judge, if the judge gives advance notice to the parties of the person to be consulted and the subject matter of the advice to be solicited, and affords the parties a reasonable opportunity to object and respond to the notice and to the advice received.” A is incorrect. Always be wary of this type of answer choice. The MPRE frequently uses the idea of an attorney or judge not initiating the initial contact as a wrong answer choice. Considering the concerns addressed by the Rule, who started the communication is clearly irrelevant. This type of answer choice should be discarded immediately. B is incorrect. It’s not enough that the expert consulted by the judge be impartial. The parties are entitled to know what the expert has been asked and what he or she has advised. After all, the expert may be wrong or the judge may not have communicated the issues correctly. The parties may want to respond, and they must be given the opportunity to do so. D is incorrect. This answer choice seems to imply that an expert’s impartiality could make the communication proper. However, as stated above, this is not the case.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. Each side is entitled to know and to respond to every fact and every argument presented by the other side. If a judge could consult any legal source he or she wished without identifying it and giving each side the right to respond, the adversarial nature of the judicial system would collapse. At the same time, there are instances in which a judge will need to get advice on the law, especially when, as in these facts, the lawyers have not stated the situation clearly. To enable the judge to do this without subverting the adversarial process, Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(2) permits a judge to “obtain the written advice of a disinterested expert on the law applicable to a proceeding before the judge, if the judge gives advance notice to the parties of the person to be consulted and the subject matter of the advice to be solicited, and affords the parties a reasonable opportunity to object and respond to the notice and to the advice received.” A is incorrect. Always be wary of this type of answer choice. The MPRE frequently uses the idea of an attorney or judge not initiating the initial contact as a wrong answer choice. Considering the concerns addressed by the Rule, who started the communication is clearly irrelevant. This type of answer choice should be discarded immediately. B is incorrect. It’s not enough that the expert consulted by the judge be impartial. The parties are entitled to know what the expert has been asked and what he or she has advised. After all, the expert may be wrong or the judge may not have communicated the issues correctly. The parties may want to respond, and they must be given the opportunity to do so. D is incorrect. This answer choice seems to imply that an expert’s impartiality could make the communication proper. However, as stated above, this is not the case.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 2.9(A)(2)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[135],"sourceAnswerPages":[240],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名法官在审理一宗受到高度关注的反垄断案件时，收到当地一名律师邮寄的一封长信。信中讨论了本案适用的法律。法官知道，该律师既不代理案件任何一方，也不代理其利益可能受到案件结果影响的任何委托人。法官读完信后，没有向待决案件中的律师提及收信一事，而是将其归入自己关于反垄断诉讼的一般档案。后来，法官阅读本案审判书状后，认为该律师的信比双方书状更好地解释了待决案件适用的法律。法官在作出裁判时采纳了该律师的推理。在作出裁判前，法官没有向双方律师透露有关该信的任何事情。法官考虑该律师的信是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为不是法官主动与该律师联系。","B":"适当，因为该律师不代理任何利益可能受到案件结果影响的委托人。","C":"不适当，因为法官在作出裁判前，没有将信中内容告知所有律师并给予其回应机会。","D":"不适当，因为该律师并非本案登记在册的代理律师。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。任何一方都有权知悉并回应另一方提出的每一项事实和每一个论点。如果法官可以任意咨询任何法律信息来源，却无需说明来源并给予各方回应权，司法制度的对抗性基础就会崩溃。与此同时，在某些情况下，法官确实需要取得法律意见，尤其是像本题这样，双方律师没有清楚说明问题时。为使法官能够在不破坏对抗程序的前提下这样做，《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.9(A)(2) 允许法官“就法官面前某一诉讼程序所适用的法律，取得一名无利害关系专家的书面意见，但法官须事先将拟咨询人员及拟征求意见的事项通知各方，并给予各方合理机会对通知及所收到的意见提出异议和作出回应。”A 不正确。应始终警惕这种选项。MPRE 经常把律师或法官不是最先主动联系的一方当作错误答案的理由。考虑到该规则所保护的利益，由谁发起沟通显然无关，应立即排除此类选项。B 不正确。法官所咨询的专家保持中立即便必要，也还不够。当事人有权知道专家被问了什么以及给出了什么意见。毕竟，专家可能出错，法官也可能没有准确传达争议问题。当事人可能希望回应，因此必须给予他们回应机会。D 不正确。该选项似乎暗示，专家公正就可以使沟通适当。但如上所述，事实并非如此。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-010","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":10,"topic":"Model Rule 7.1","question":"An attorney’s recorded radio advertisement stated: “For a fee of $600 I will represent a party to a divorce that does not result in a court trial of a contested issue of fact.” The attorney had the advertisement prerecorded and approved by the appropriate bar agency for broadcast. The attorney retained a recording of the actual transmission in her office. A woman who had previously agreed with her husband to an uncontested dissolution of their marriage heard the broadcast and called on the attorney in the attorney’s office. She told the attorney that she had heard the broadcast and asked the attorney to represent her. The attorney agreed to the representation. Because of the nature of the parties’ property, the attorney spent more time on the tax aspects of the case than the attorney anticipated. The time expended by the attorney, if charged at a reasonable hourly rate, would have resulted in a fee of $2,000. After the decree was entered, the attorney billed his client for $2,000. The client paid the fee. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"No, because the attorney’s fee was a reasonable charge for the time expended.","B":"No, because the attorney, when the representation was accepted, did not anticipate the tax problems.","C":"Yes, because the client paid the fee without protest.","D":"Yes, because the attorney charged a fee in excess of the advertised fee."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not make a false or misleading communication about the lawyer or the lawyer’s services.” Model Rule 7.1. Here, the advertisement is misleading because it claims the lawyer will only charge $600 for representation that does not go to trial, when the reality is that the representation may cost more if the attorney spends more time on the matter. Importantly, this is the only answer choice that specifically mentions anything about the advertisement, which is clearly the primary issue. A is incorrect. Although the fee might have been reasonable, the question specifically deals with a misleading advertisement and the potential ethical problems caused by it. B is incorrect. Again, this answer choice does not address the issue of the misleading advertisement. C is incorrect. This is another example of a “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice, which should be immediately tossed out.","rule":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not make a false or misleading communication about the lawyer or the lawyer’s services.” Model Rule 7.1. Here, the advertisement is misleading because it claims the lawyer will only charge $600 for representation that does not go to trial, when the reality is that the representation may cost more if the attorney spends more time on the matter. Importantly, this is the only answer choice that specifically mentions anything about the advertisement, which is clearly the primary issue. A is incorrect. Although the fee might have been reasonable, the question specifically deals with a misleading advertisement and the potential ethical problems caused by it. B is incorrect. Again, this answer choice does not address the issue of the misleading advertisement. C is incorrect. This is another example of a “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice, which should be immediately tossed out.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not make a false or misleading communication about the lawyer or the lawyer’s services.” Model Rule 7.1. Here, the advertisement is misleading because it claims the lawyer will only charge $600 for representation that does not go to trial, when the reality is that the representation may cost more if the attorney spends more time on the matter. Importantly, this is the only answer choice that specifically mentions anything about the advertisement, which is clearly the primary issue. A is incorrect. Although the fee might have been reasonable, the question specifically deals with a misleading advertisement and the potential ethical problems caused by it. B is incorrect. Again, this answer choice does not address the issue of the misleading advertisement. C is incorrect. This is another example of a “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice, which should be immediately tossed out.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not make a false or misleading communication about the lawyer or the lawyer’s services.” Model Rule 7.1. Here, the advertisement is misleading because it claims the lawyer will only charge $600 for representation that does not go to trial, when the reality is that the representation may cost more if the attorney spends more time on the matter. Importantly, this is the only answer choice that specifically mentions anything about the advertisement, which is clearly the primary issue. A is incorrect. Although the fee might have been reasonable, the question specifically deals with a misleading advertisement and the potential ethical problems caused by it. B is incorrect. Again, this answer choice does not address the issue of the misleading advertisement. C is incorrect. This is another example of a “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice, which should be immediately tossed out.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[135,136],"sourceAnswerPages":[241],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师预先录制的广播广告称：“对于不需要法院审理有争议事实问题的离婚案件，我会以 600 美元代理一方当事人。”律师预先录制了广告，并取得适当律师协会机构批准后播出。律师在办公室保存了一份实际播出内容的录音。一名先前已经与丈夫同意无争议解除婚姻关系的女子听到广告，来到律师办公室。她告诉律师自己听过广告，并请求律师代理。律师同意代理。由于夫妻财产的性质，律师在案件税务问题上花费的时间超出预期。如果按照合理小时费率计算，律师所花时间对应的费用为 2,000 美元。离婚判决作出后，律师向委托人开具 2,000 美元账单。委托人支付了费用。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为该律师收费与所花时间相比属合理金额。","B":"否，因为律师接受委托时没有预见到税务问题。","C":"是，因为委托人没有提出异议便支付了费用。","D":"是，因为律师收取的费用超过广告所载金额。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。“律师不得就律师本人或律师服务作出虚假或具有误导性的沟通。”《职业行为示范规则》7.1。本题中，广告声称只要代理没有进入审判，律师就只收取 600 美元；但实际情况是，如果律师在事项上花费更多时间，代理费用可能更高，因此广告具有误导性。需要特别注意的是，只有这个选项明确提到广告，而广告显然是本题的核心问题。A 不正确。该费用虽然可能合理，但题目具体涉及具有误导性的广告及其可能引发的职业伦理问题。B 不正确。同样，该选项没有处理误导性广告这一问题。C 不正确。这又是一个“没有损害，就没有违规”的选项，应立即排除。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-011","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":11,"topic":"Model Rule 5.5(a) / Model Rule 5.4(a)","question":"A certified public accountant proposed to an attorney who is a recognized specialist in the field of tax law, that the accountant and the attorney form a partnership for the purpose of providing clients with tax-related legal and accounting services. Both the accountant and the attorney have deserved reputations of being competent, honest, and trustworthy. The accountant further proposes that the announcement of the proposed partnership, the firm stationery, and all public directory listings clearly state that the accountant is a certified public accountant and that the attorney is a lawyer. The attorney will be the only person in the partnership who gives legal advice. Is the attorney subject to discipline if he enters into the proposed partnership with the accountant?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because one of the activities of the partnership would be providing legal services to clients.","B":"Yes, because the attorney would be receiving fees paid for other than legal services.","C":"No, because the partnership will assure to the public high-quality services in the fields of tax law and accounting.","D":"No, because the attorney is the only person in the partnership who gives advice on legal matters."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not form a partnership with a nonlawyer if any of the activities of the partnership consist of the “practice of law.” A lawyer who does so is assisting another in the unauthorized practice of law, and is subject to discipline. Model Rule 5.5(a). Here, the important point is that the partnership is delivering the legal services, which choice A states explicitly. B is incorrect. Although the attorney would be receiving fees for other than legal services, the primary problem is that the partnership with the nonlawyer would be engaged in the practice of law. C is incorrect. The important fact is that the partnership is providing legal services. However, as a partner, the accountant is receiving a portion of the entity’s profits and has control over aspects of the business. Under Model Rule 5.4(a), a lawyer may not share legal fees with a nonlawyer, which would clearly happen here under the partnership agreement. This Rule is the major deterrent to partnerships like this, and even splitting fees with a charity is not allowed. The only exceptions to this Rule involve paying the estates of deceased attorneys or law firm employee compensation or retirement plans, neither of which apply here. Although the intention might be to provide high-quality services, this fact does not change the analysis. D is incorrect. As stated above, the main issues are the creation of a partnership that provides legal services and the sharing of fees with a nonlawyer. Simply keeping the accountant from interfering with the attorney’s legal advice is not enough.","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not form a partnership with a nonlawyer if any of the activities of the partnership consist of the “practice of law.” A lawyer who does so is assisting another in the unauthorized practice of law, and is subject to discipline. Model Rule 5.5(a). Here, the important point is that the partnership is delivering the legal services, which choice A states explicitly. B is incorrect. Although the attorney would be receiving fees for other than legal services, the primary problem is that the partnership with the nonlawyer would be engaged in the practice of law. C is incorrect. The important fact is that the partnership is providing legal services. However, as a partner, the accountant is receiving a portion of the entity’s profits and has control over aspects of the business. Under Model Rule 5.4(a), a lawyer may not share legal fees with a nonlawyer, which would clearly happen here under the partnership agreement. This Rule is the major deterrent to partnerships like this, and even splitting fees with a charity is not allowed. The only exceptions to this Rule involve paying the estates of deceased attorneys or law firm employee compensation or retirement plans, neither of which apply here. Although the intention might be to provide high-quality services, this fact does not change the analysis. D is incorrect. As stated above, the main issues are the creation of a partnership that provides legal services and the sharing of fees with a nonlawyer. Simply keeping the accountant from interfering with the attorney’s legal advice is not enough.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline if he enters into the proposed partnership with the accountant?","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not form a partnership with a nonlawyer if any of the activities of the partnership consist of the “practice of law.” A lawyer who does so is assisting another in the unauthorized practice of law, and is subject to discipline. Model Rule 5.5(a). Here, the important point is that the partnership is delivering the legal services, which choice A states explicitly. B is incorrect. Although the attorney would be receiving fees for other than legal services, the primary problem is that the partnership with the nonlawyer would be engaged in the practice of law. C is incorrect. The important fact is that the partnership is providing legal services. However, as a partner, the accountant is receiving a portion of the entity’s profits and has control over aspects of the business. Under Model Rule 5.4(a), a lawyer may not share legal fees with a nonlawyer, which would clearly happen here under the partnership agreement. This Rule is the major deterrent to partnerships like this, and even splitting fees with a charity is not allowed. The only exceptions to this Rule involve paying the estates of deceased attorneys or law firm employee compensation or retirement plans, neither of which apply here. Although the intention might be to provide high-quality services, this fact does not change the analysis. D is incorrect. As stated above, the main issues are the creation of a partnership that provides legal services and the sharing of fees with a nonlawyer. Simply keeping the accountant from interfering with the attorney’s legal advice is not enough.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not form a partnership with a nonlawyer if any of the activities of the partnership consist of the “practice of law.” A lawyer who does so is assisting another in the unauthorized practice of law, and is subject to discipline. Model Rule 5.5(a). Here, the important point is that the partnership is delivering the legal services, which choice A states explicitly. B is incorrect. Although the attorney would be receiving fees for other than legal services, the primary problem is that the partnership with the nonlawyer would be engaged in the practice of law. C is incorrect. The important fact is that the partnership is providing legal services. However, as a partner, the accountant is receiving a portion of the entity’s profits and has control over aspects of the business. Under Model Rule 5.4(a), a lawyer may not share legal fees with a nonlawyer, which would clearly happen here under the partnership agreement. This Rule is the major deterrent to partnerships like this, and even splitting fees with a charity is not allowed. The only exceptions to this Rule involve paying the estates of deceased attorneys or law firm employee compensation or retirement plans, neither of which apply here. Although the intention might be to provide high-quality services, this fact does not change the analysis. D is incorrect. As stated above, the main issues are the creation of a partnership that provides legal services and the sharing of fees with a nonlawyer. Simply keeping the accountant from interfering with the attorney’s legal advice is not enough.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.5(a)","Model Rule 5.4(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[136,137],"sourceAnswerPages":[241,242],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名注册会计师向一名公认的税法专家律师提议，二人组成合伙组织，为委托人提供与税务有关的法律及会计服务。会计师和律师都因称职、诚实和可信而享有当之无愧的声誉。会计师还提议，在拟议合伙组织的公告、事务所信笺和所有公开名录中明确说明会计师是注册会计师、律师是执业律师。该律师将是合伙组织中唯一提供法律意见的人。如果律师与会计师组成拟议的合伙组织，是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该合伙组织的活动之一将是向委托人提供法律服务。","B":"是，因为该律师将收取并非因法律服务而支付的费用。","C":"否，因为该合伙组织会确保公众获得高质量的税法和会计服务。","D":"否，因为该律师是合伙组织中唯一就法律事项提供意见的人。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。如果合伙组织的任何活动构成“法律执业活动”，律师就不得与非律师组成该合伙组织。这样做的律师是在协助他人擅自从事法律执业活动，并会受到纪律处分。《职业行为示范规则》5.5(a)。本题的关键是，由合伙组织提供法律服务，而 A 明确说明了这一点。B 不正确。该律师虽将获得因法律服务以外事项支付的费用，但首要问题是，与非律师组成的合伙组织将从事法律执业活动。C 不正确。关键事实是该合伙组织将提供法律服务。此外，作为合伙人，会计师会取得该实体的一部分利润，并有权控制业务的某些方面。根据规则 5.4(a)，律师不得与非律师分享法律服务费，而这显然会在本合伙协议下发生。该规则是阻止此类合伙组织的主要规定；即使与慈善机构分配费用也不允许。该规则仅对向已故律师遗产付款，以及律师事务所雇员报酬或退休计划规定例外，本题均不适用。即使二人意在提供高质量服务，也不会改变分析。D 不正确。如上所述，主要问题是建立提供法律服务的合伙组织，并与非律师分享费用。仅仅不让会计师干预律师的法律意见仍然不够。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-012","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":12,"topic":"Model Rule 1.5(a) / Model Rule 1.15 / Model Rule 1.15(e)","question":"A client retained an attorney to appeal his criminal conviction and to seek bail pending appeal. The agreed-upon fee for the appearance on the bail hearing was $100 per hour. The attorney received $1,600 from the client, of which $600 was a deposit to secure the attorney’s fee and $1,000 was for bail costs in the event that bail was obtained. The attorney maintained two office bank accounts: a fee account, in which all fees collected from clients were deposited and from which all office expenses were paid, and a clients’ trust account. The attorney deposited the $1,600 in the clients’ trust account the week before the bail hearing. She expended six hours of her time preparing for and appearing at the hearing. The effort to obtain bail was unsuccessful. Dissatisfied, the client immediately demanded return of the $1,600. What should the attorney do with the $1,600?","choices":{"A":"Transfer the $1,600 to the fee account.","B":"Transfer the $600 to the fee account and leave $1,000 in the clients’ trust account until the attorney’s fee for the final appeal is determined.","C":"Transfer $600 to the fee account and send the client a $1,000 check on the clients’ trust account.","D":"Send the client a $1,000 check and leave $600 in the clients’ trust account until the matter is resolved with the client."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. As stated in Part One, the MPRE will ask about the propriety of a fee that is in dispute. Remember that the Model Rules specify that a lawyer’s fee shall be reasonable (Model Rule 1.5(a)); it’s a safe bet, therefore, that a lower fee will be perceived as more reasonable than a higher one. Thus, when a question asks what part of a fee a lawyer may properly keep, you’re generally safe if you opt for the choice that best protects the client’s interests in the money under dispute. This answer recognizes the three basic principles a lawyer must observe when dealing with client funds: (1) client funds must always be kept separate from the lawyer’s funds in a designated clients’ escrow account; (2) as soon as the client’s interest in the funds is fixed, the client’s money must be delivered to the client; and (3) if both the client and the attorney claim any interest in any part of the funds—for example, if a dispute arises as to the lawyer’s fees— the funds must remain in the separate client account until there is an accounting and their respective interests can be severed. Model Rule 1.15. Here, the attorney’s maximum claim was for fees totaling 6 hours × $100 = $600. When the bail hearing was completed, the client’s interest in the advance was $1,000. The attorney had a duty to return this promptly. Because the client had demanded $1,600, the lawyer’s fee of $600 was in dispute. Consequently, the money representing the fee had to be retained in the clients’ trust account until the dispute was resolved. A is incorrect. The funds in dispute totaled only $600. The balance of $1,000 was not in dispute and belonged to the client. The attorney was under a duty to return $1,000 to the client because the money was not used for the purpose intended. B is incorrect. Because the fee of $600 was in dispute, the attorney had no right to transfer that sum to his fee account. He was required to retain the funds in his clients’ trust account until the dispute was resolved. Further, the attorney had no right to retain the balance of $1,000 in either account pending the appeal. The money was deposited with the attorney only in connection with the bail application, not the appeal itself. C is incorrect. The attorney was right to send the $1,000 to the client, but wrong in transferring $600 to his fee account. So long as his fee was in dispute, the $600 had to be retained in the Clients’ Fund Account. Model Rule 1.15(e).","rule":"D is the correct answer. As stated in Part One, the MPRE will ask about the propriety of a fee that is in dispute. Remember that the Model Rules specify that a lawyer’s fee shall be reasonable (Model Rule 1.5(a)); it’s a safe bet, therefore, that a lower fee will be perceived as more reasonable than a higher one. Thus, when a question asks what part of a fee a lawyer may properly keep, you’re generally safe if you opt for the choice that best protects the client’s interests in the money under dispute. This answer recognizes the three basic principles a lawyer must observe when dealing with client funds: (1) client funds must always be kept separate from the lawyer’s funds in a designated clients’ escrow account; (2) as soon as the client’s interest in the funds is fixed, the client’s money must be delivered to the client; and (3) if both the client and the attorney claim any interest in any part of the funds—for example, if a dispute arises as to the lawyer’s fees— the funds must remain in the separate client account until there is an accounting and their respective interests can be severed. Model Rule 1.15. Here, the attorney’s maximum claim was for fees totaling 6 hours × $100 = $600. When the bail hearing was completed, the client’s interest in the advance was $1,000. The attorney had a duty to return this promptly. Because the client had demanded $1,600, the lawyer’s fee of $600 was in dispute. Consequently, the money representing the fee had to be retained in the clients’ trust account until the dispute was resolved. A is incorrect. The funds in dispute totaled only $600. The balance of $1,000 was not in dispute and belonged to the client. The attorney was under a duty to return $1,000 to the client because the money was not used for the purpose intended. B is incorrect. Because the fee of $600 was in dispute, the attorney had no right to transfer that sum to his fee account. He was required to retain the funds in his clients’ trust account until the dispute was resolved. Further, the attorney had no right to retain the balance of $1,000 in either account pending the appeal. The money was deposited with the attorney only in connection with the bail application, not the appeal itself. C is incorrect. The attorney was right to send the $1,000 to the client, but wrong in transferring $600 to his fee account. So long as his fee was in dispute, the $600 had to be retained in the Clients’ Fund Account. Model Rule 1.15(e).","irac":{"issue":"What should the attorney do with the $1,600?","rule":"D is the correct answer. As stated in Part One, the MPRE will ask about the propriety of a fee that is in dispute. Remember that the Model Rules specify that a lawyer’s fee shall be reasonable (Model Rule 1.5(a)); it’s a safe bet, therefore, that a lower fee will be perceived as more reasonable than a higher one. Thus, when a question asks what part of a fee a lawyer may properly keep, you’re generally safe if you opt for the choice that best protects the client’s interests in the money under dispute. This answer recognizes the three basic principles a lawyer must observe when dealing with client funds: (1) client funds must always be kept separate from the lawyer’s funds in a designated clients’ escrow account; (2) as soon as the client’s interest in the funds is fixed, the client’s money must be delivered to the client; and (3) if both the client and the attorney claim any interest in any part of the funds—for example, if a dispute arises as to the lawyer’s fees— the funds must remain in the separate client account until there is an accounting and their respective interests can be severed. Model Rule 1.15. Here, the attorney’s maximum claim was for fees totaling 6 hours × $100 = $600. When the bail hearing was completed, the client’s interest in the advance was $1,000. The attorney had a duty to return this promptly. Because the client had demanded $1,600, the lawyer’s fee of $600 was in dispute. Consequently, the money representing the fee had to be retained in the clients’ trust account until the dispute was resolved. A is incorrect. The funds in dispute totaled only $600. The balance of $1,000 was not in dispute and belonged to the client. The attorney was under a duty to return $1,000 to the client because the money was not used for the purpose intended. B is incorrect. Because the fee of $600 was in dispute, the attorney had no right to transfer that sum to his fee account. He was required to retain the funds in his clients’ trust account until the dispute was resolved. Further, the attorney had no right to retain the balance of $1,000 in either account pending the appeal. The money was deposited with the attorney only in connection with the bail application, not the appeal itself. C is incorrect. The attorney was right to send the $1,000 to the client, but wrong in transferring $600 to his fee account. So long as his fee was in dispute, the $600 had to be retained in the Clients’ Fund Account. Model Rule 1.15(e).","analysis":"D is the correct answer. As stated in Part One, the MPRE will ask about the propriety of a fee that is in dispute. Remember that the Model Rules specify that a lawyer’s fee shall be reasonable (Model Rule 1.5(a)); it’s a safe bet, therefore, that a lower fee will be perceived as more reasonable than a higher one. Thus, when a question asks what part of a fee a lawyer may properly keep, you’re generally safe if you opt for the choice that best protects the client’s interests in the money under dispute. This answer recognizes the three basic principles a lawyer must observe when dealing with client funds: (1) client funds must always be kept separate from the lawyer’s funds in a designated clients’ escrow account; (2) as soon as the client’s interest in the funds is fixed, the client’s money must be delivered to the client; and (3) if both the client and the attorney claim any interest in any part of the funds—for example, if a dispute arises as to the lawyer’s fees— the funds must remain in the separate client account until there is an accounting and their respective interests can be severed. Model Rule 1.15. Here, the attorney’s maximum claim was for fees totaling 6 hours × $100 = $600. When the bail hearing was completed, the client’s interest in the advance was $1,000. The attorney had a duty to return this promptly. Because the client had demanded $1,600, the lawyer’s fee of $600 was in dispute. Consequently, the money representing the fee had to be retained in the clients’ trust account until the dispute was resolved. A is incorrect. The funds in dispute totaled only $600. The balance of $1,000 was not in dispute and belonged to the client. The attorney was under a duty to return $1,000 to the client because the money was not used for the purpose intended. B is incorrect. Because the fee of $600 was in dispute, the attorney had no right to transfer that sum to his fee account. He was required to retain the funds in his clients’ trust account until the dispute was resolved. Further, the attorney had no right to retain the balance of $1,000 in either account pending the appeal. The money was deposited with the attorney only in connection with the bail application, not the appeal itself. C is incorrect. The attorney was right to send the $1,000 to the client, but wrong in transferring $600 to his fee account. So long as his fee was in dispute, the $600 had to be retained in the Clients’ Fund Account. Model Rule 1.15(e).","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.5(a)","Model Rule 1.15","Model Rule 1.15(e)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[137],"sourceAnswerPages":[242,243],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名委托人聘请律师为其刑事定罪提出上诉，并申请在上诉期间保释。双方约定，律师参加保释听证的收费为每小时 100 美元。律师从委托人处收到 1,600 美元，其中 600 美元是用于确保支付律师费的预付款，1,000 美元用于在获准保释时支付保释费用。律师设有两个办公室银行账户：一个费用账户，存入从委托人处收取的所有律师费，并从中支付所有办公支出；另一个是委托人信托账户。保释听证前一周，律师将 1,600 美元存入委托人信托账户。她为准备和参加听证花费六小时。保释申请未获成功。委托人对此不满，立即要求退还 1,600 美元。律师应当如何处理这 1,600 美元？","choicesZh":{"A":"将 1,600 美元全部转入费用账户。","B":"将 600 美元转入费用账户，并将 1,000 美元留在委托人信托账户，直至确定最终上诉的律师费。","C":"将 600 美元转入费用账户，并从委托人信托账户向委托人开具一张 1,000 美元支票。","D":"向委托人开具一张 1,000 美元支票，并将 600 美元留在委托人信托账户，直至与委托人解决争议。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。如第一部分所述，MPRE 会询问有争议费用应如何处理。请记住，《职业行为示范规则》规定律师费必须合理（规则 1.5(a)）；因此通常可以认为，较低费用比更高费用更容易被视为合理。所以，当题目询问律师可以适当保留费用中的哪一部分时，选择最能保护委托人对争议款项之利益的选项通常较为安全。本选项体现了律师处理委托人资金时必须遵守的三项基本原则：(1) 委托人资金必须始终与律师资金分开，存放在指定的委托人托管账户中；(2) 委托人对资金的权益一经确定，必须立即将委托人的款项交付给委托人；以及 (3) 如果委托人与律师都对某部分资金主张权益——例如对律师费产生争议——该资金必须继续留在独立的委托人账户中，直至完成核算并可以区分双方权益。规则 1.15。本题中，律师能够主张的费用最高为 6 小时 × 100 美元，即 600 美元。保释听证结束时，委托人对预付款中 1,000 美元的权益已经确定，律师有义务立即退还。由于委托人要求退还全部 1,600 美元，600 美元律师费已处于争议状态。因此，代表该费用的款项必须留在委托人信托账户中，直至争议解决。A 不正确。争议款项只有 600 美元，其余 1,000 美元没有争议，属于委托人。由于该款项没有用于原定目的，律师有义务向委托人退还 1,000 美元。B 不正确。由于 600 美元费用存在争议，律师无权将其转入费用账户，必须将其留在委托人信托账户中，直至争议解决。此外，律师无权在上诉期间将其余 1,000 美元留在任何账户中。该款项交给律师只是用于保释申请，而不是用于上诉本身。C 不正确。律师向委托人退还 1,000 美元是正确的，但将 600 美元转入费用账户是错误的。只要费用仍有争议，这 600 美元就必须留在委托人资金账户中。规则 1.15(e)。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-013","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":13,"topic":"Model Rule 1.16(c)","question":"An attorney represents a client in commercial litigation that is scheduled to go to trial in two months. Over the past several weeks, the client has disagreed with almost every tactical decision that the attorney has made. Frustrated, the attorney finally said to the client that if she didn’t like the way he was handling the lawsuit, perhaps she should get another lawyer. The client was upset at the suggestion and accused the attorney of trying to get out of the case. Reasonably believing that he could no longer work effectively with the client, the attorney sought the client’s permission to withdraw from the representation, and the client reluctantly agreed. After giving the client sufficient notice to obtain replacement counsel, the attorney requested the court’s permission to withdraw from the litigation, but the court denied the request. May the attorney withdraw from the representation?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the client agreed, and the attorney gave the client sufficient notice to obtain replacement counsel.","B":"Yes, because the client had made it unreasonably difficult for the attorney to carry out the representation effectively.","C":"No, because the court denied the attorney’s request to withdraw.","D":"No, because the attorney’s withdrawal would cause material prejudice to the client, and the client’s agreement was not voluntary."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. “When ordered to do so by a tribunal, a lawyer shall continue representation notwithstanding good cause for terminating the representation.” Model Rule 1.16(c). Here, since the court denied the request, the attorney cannot withdraw. This question is a good example of carefully reading the question. A is incorrect. As stated above, the major issue is that the court denied the request. The attorney and client cannot simply ignore the court’s order. This should be an obviously wrong answer choice. B is incorrect. Even if the client is being unreasonable, the attorney still has to represent the client if the court orders him to do so. D is incorrect. The issue here is not potential prejudice to the client, but the fact that the court has ordered representation to continue.","rule":"C is the correct answer. “When ordered to do so by a tribunal, a lawyer shall continue representation notwithstanding good cause for terminating the representation.” Model Rule 1.16(c). Here, since the court denied the request, the attorney cannot withdraw. This question is a good example of carefully reading the question. A is incorrect. As stated above, the major issue is that the court denied the request. The attorney and client cannot simply ignore the court’s order. This should be an obviously wrong answer choice. B is incorrect. Even if the client is being unreasonable, the attorney still has to represent the client if the court orders him to do so. D is incorrect. The issue here is not potential prejudice to the client, but the fact that the court has ordered representation to continue.","irac":{"issue":"May the attorney withdraw from the representation?","rule":"C is the correct answer. “When ordered to do so by a tribunal, a lawyer shall continue representation notwithstanding good cause for terminating the representation.” Model Rule 1.16(c). Here, since the court denied the request, the attorney cannot withdraw. This question is a good example of carefully reading the question. A is incorrect. As stated above, the major issue is that the court denied the request. The attorney and client cannot simply ignore the court’s order. This should be an obviously wrong answer choice. B is incorrect. Even if the client is being unreasonable, the attorney still has to represent the client if the court orders him to do so. D is incorrect. The issue here is not potential prejudice to the client, but the fact that the court has ordered representation to continue.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. “When ordered to do so by a tribunal, a lawyer shall continue representation notwithstanding good cause for terminating the representation.” Model Rule 1.16(c). Here, since the court denied the request, the attorney cannot withdraw. This question is a good example of carefully reading the question. A is incorrect. As stated above, the major issue is that the court denied the request. The attorney and client cannot simply ignore the court’s order. This should be an obviously wrong answer choice. B is incorrect. Even if the client is being unreasonable, the attorney still has to represent the client if the court orders him to do so. D is incorrect. The issue here is not potential prejudice to the client, but the fact that the court has ordered representation to continue.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.16(c)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[137,138],"sourceAnswerPages":[243],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理委托人参加一宗定于两个月后开庭的商业诉讼。在过去几周中，委托人几乎不同意律师作出的每一项策略决定。律师感到沮丧，最终对委托人说，如果她不喜欢自己处理诉讼的方式，也许应该另找一名律师。委托人听后不满，指责律师试图摆脱案件。律师合理地认为自己已无法继续与委托人有效合作，于是请求委托人同意其退出代理，委托人勉强同意。律师给予委托人充分通知以便另聘律师后，请求法院准许其退出诉讼，但法院驳回了请求。该律师能否退出代理？","choicesZh":{"A":"能，因为委托人已经同意，而且律师给予了委托人充分通知，使其可以另聘律师。","B":"能，因为委托人使律师难以合理有效地完成代理。","C":"不能，因为法院驳回了律师退出代理的请求。","D":"不能，因为律师退出会对委托人造成重大不利影响，而且委托人的同意并非自愿。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。“审裁机构命令律师继续代理时，即使存在终止代理的正当理由，律师也应继续代理。”《职业行为示范规则》1.16(c)。本题中，法院已经驳回请求，因此律师不能退出。本题很好地说明了仔细阅读题目的重要性。A 不正确。如上所述，核心问题是法院已经驳回请求。律师与委托人不能无视法院命令。这显然是错误选项。B 不正确。即使委托人行为不合理，只要法院命令律师继续代理，律师仍必须继续。D 不正确。本题问题不在于委托人可能受到的损害，而在于法院已经命令继续代理。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-014","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":14,"topic":"Model Rule 3.1","question":"An attorney is employed in the legal department of a public utility company and represents that company in litigation. The company has been sued by a consumer group that has accused the company of various acts in violation of its charter. Through its general counsel, the company has instructed the attorney not to negotiate a settlement but to go to trial under any circumstances because a precedent needs to be established. The attorney believes the defense can be supported by a good faith argument, but also believes the case should be settled if possible. Must the attorney withdraw as counsel in this case?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the company is controlling the attorney’s judgment in refusing to settle the case.","B":"Yes, because a lawyer should endeavor to avoid litigation.","C":"No, because the company’s defense can be supported by a good faith argument.","D":"No, because as an employee, the attorney is bound by the instruction of the general counsel."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. If the lawyer did not believe there was a basis in law and fact for the defense, it would be improper for him to mount a defense of the company in court. Model Rule 3.1. However, there is no prohibition against arguing a meritorious defense simply because a settlement might be proper as well. A is incorrect. Although the company has told the attorney that it will not settle, simply telling the attorney this (when there is a good faith defense) does not make representation improper. B is incorrect. There is no Rule stating a lawyer should avoid litigation in all circumstances, especially when there is a good faith argument at issue. This answer choice should be tossed immediately, because it implies even those with good faith claims should be discouraged from having their day in court. D is incorrect. While the general counsel may be the attorney’s boss, this does not make the representation improper. Again, this is an answer choice that makes no logical sense in that it implies attorneys cannot be the employees of other people or entities. It should be tossed immediately.","rule":"C is the correct answer. If the lawyer did not believe there was a basis in law and fact for the defense, it would be improper for him to mount a defense of the company in court. Model Rule 3.1. However, there is no prohibition against arguing a meritorious defense simply because a settlement might be proper as well. A is incorrect. Although the company has told the attorney that it will not settle, simply telling the attorney this (when there is a good faith defense) does not make representation improper. B is incorrect. There is no Rule stating a lawyer should avoid litigation in all circumstances, especially when there is a good faith argument at issue. This answer choice should be tossed immediately, because it implies even those with good faith claims should be discouraged from having their day in court. D is incorrect. While the general counsel may be the attorney’s boss, this does not make the representation improper. Again, this is an answer choice that makes no logical sense in that it implies attorneys cannot be the employees of other people or entities. It should be tossed immediately.","irac":{"issue":"Must the attorney withdraw as counsel in this case?","rule":"C is the correct answer. If the lawyer did not believe there was a basis in law and fact for the defense, it would be improper for him to mount a defense of the company in court. Model Rule 3.1. However, there is no prohibition against arguing a meritorious defense simply because a settlement might be proper as well. A is incorrect. Although the company has told the attorney that it will not settle, simply telling the attorney this (when there is a good faith defense) does not make representation improper. B is incorrect. There is no Rule stating a lawyer should avoid litigation in all circumstances, especially when there is a good faith argument at issue. This answer choice should be tossed immediately, because it implies even those with good faith claims should be discouraged from having their day in court. D is incorrect. While the general counsel may be the attorney’s boss, this does not make the representation improper. Again, this is an answer choice that makes no logical sense in that it implies attorneys cannot be the employees of other people or entities. It should be tossed immediately.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. If the lawyer did not believe there was a basis in law and fact for the defense, it would be improper for him to mount a defense of the company in court. Model Rule 3.1. However, there is no prohibition against arguing a meritorious defense simply because a settlement might be proper as well. A is incorrect. Although the company has told the attorney that it will not settle, simply telling the attorney this (when there is a good faith defense) does not make representation improper. B is incorrect. There is no Rule stating a lawyer should avoid litigation in all circumstances, especially when there is a good faith argument at issue. This answer choice should be tossed immediately, because it implies even those with good faith claims should be discouraged from having their day in court. D is incorrect. While the general counsel may be the attorney’s boss, this does not make the representation improper. Again, this is an answer choice that makes no logical sense in that it implies attorneys cannot be the employees of other people or entities. It should be tossed immediately.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[138],"sourceAnswerPages":[243,244],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师受雇于一家公用事业公司的法务部门，并在诉讼中代理该公司。一个消费者团体起诉公司，指控公司实施了多项违反其章程的行为。公司通过总法律顾问指示律师，在任何情况下都不得谈判和解，而应进入审判，因为需要确立一项先例。律师认为可以善意论证公司的抗辩，但也认为如果可能，应当和解此案。该律师是否必须退出本案代理？","choicesZh":{"A":"必须，因为公司拒绝和解是在控制律师的专业判断。","B":"必须，因为律师应努力避免诉讼。","C":"不必须，因为可以善意论证公司的抗辩。","D":"不必须，因为作为雇员，该律师必须服从总法律顾问的指示。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。如果律师认为该抗辩在法律和事实方面均无依据，在法院为公司提出抗辩就会不适当。《职业行为示范规则》3.1。不过，仅仅因为和解也可能是适当选择，规则并不禁止律师提出有根据的抗辩。A 不正确。公司虽然告诉律师不会和解，但在存在善意抗辩时，仅仅作出这项指示并不会使代理变得不适当。B 不正确。没有任何规则规定律师在任何情况下都应避免诉讼，尤其是在存在善意主张时。该选项暗示，即使拥有善意主张的人也应被劝阻，不让其在法院获得陈述机会，因而应立即排除。D 不正确。总法律顾问虽然可能是该律师的上司，但这并不会使代理不适当。同样，该选项在逻辑上毫无意义，因为它暗示律师不能受雇于其他个人或实体，应立即排除。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-015","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":15,"topic":"Model Rule 3.4","question":"A defendant was on trial for murder. The victim was killed during a barroom brawl. In the course of closing arguments to the jury, the prosecutor said, “The defendant’s whole defense is based on the testimony of his friend, who said that the victim attacked the defendant with a knife before the defendant struck him. No other witness testified to such an attack by the victim. I don’t believe the defendant’s friend was telling the truth, and I don’t think you believe him either.” The prosecutor accurately stated the testimony, and truly believed the defendant’s friend was lying. Was the prosecutor’s statement proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the prosecutor accurately stated the testimony in the case.","B":"Yes, because the prosecutor, in fact, believed the defendant’s friend was lying.","C":"No, because the prosecutor alluded to the beliefs of the jurors.","D":"No, because the prosecutor asserted his personal opinion about the defendant’s credibility."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. At trial, an attorney is not allowed to state his or her personal opinion about the credibility of a witness. Model Rule 3.4. A is incorrect. Even if the statement is accurate, an attorney is not allowed to state his or her personal opinion about the credibility of a witness. B is incorrect. Even if the prosecutor really believed the witness was lying, he could not make such a statement. C is incorrect. The issue here is that the prosecutor is stating his beliefs, not that he may be alluding to the beliefs of jurors.","rule":"D is the correct answer. At trial, an attorney is not allowed to state his or her personal opinion about the credibility of a witness. Model Rule 3.4. A is incorrect. Even if the statement is accurate, an attorney is not allowed to state his or her personal opinion about the credibility of a witness. B is incorrect. Even if the prosecutor really believed the witness was lying, he could not make such a statement. C is incorrect. The issue here is that the prosecutor is stating his beliefs, not that he may be alluding to the beliefs of jurors.","irac":{"issue":"Was the prosecutor’s statement proper?","rule":"D is the correct answer. At trial, an attorney is not allowed to state his or her personal opinion about the credibility of a witness. Model Rule 3.4. A is incorrect. Even if the statement is accurate, an attorney is not allowed to state his or her personal opinion about the credibility of a witness. B is incorrect. Even if the prosecutor really believed the witness was lying, he could not make such a statement. C is incorrect. The issue here is that the prosecutor is stating his beliefs, not that he may be alluding to the beliefs of jurors.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. At trial, an attorney is not allowed to state his or her personal opinion about the credibility of a witness. Model Rule 3.4. A is incorrect. Even if the statement is accurate, an attorney is not allowed to state his or her personal opinion about the credibility of a witness. B is incorrect. Even if the prosecutor really believed the witness was lying, he could not make such a statement. C is incorrect. The issue here is that the prosecutor is stating his beliefs, not that he may be alluding to the beliefs of jurors.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.4"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[138,139],"sourceAnswerPages":[244],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名被告因谋杀罪受审。被害人在酒吧斗殴中死亡。检察官在向陪审团作结案陈词时说：“被告的全部抗辩都建立在他朋友的证言上；这名朋友说，被告击打被害人之前，被害人曾持刀攻击被告。没有其他证人作证说被害人实施过这种攻击。我不相信被告的朋友说了实话，我想你们也不相信他。”检察官准确陈述了案件证言，也确实相信被告的朋友在说谎。检察官的陈述是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为检察官准确陈述了案件证言。","B":"适当，因为检察官事实上相信被告的朋友在说谎。","C":"不适当，因为检察官提到了陪审员的看法。","D":"不适当，因为检察官就证人的可信度表达了个人意见。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。在审判中，律师不得就证人的可信度陈述个人意见。《职业行为示范规则》3.4。A 不正确。即使陈述准确，律师仍不得就证人的可信度表达个人意见。B 不正确。即使检察官确实相信证人在说谎，也不能作出这种陈述。C 不正确。本题问题在于检察官表达自己的看法，而不在于他可能提到陪审员的看法。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-016","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":16,"topic":"Model Rule 4.2","question":"An attorney represented a plaintiff in an action for defamation against a defendant. After the defendant’s lawyer had filed and served an answer, the attorney, at the plaintiff’s direction, hired a licensed private investigator and instructed him to attempt to interview the defendant without revealing his employment. The investigator succeeded in interviewing the defendant privately and obtained an admission from the defendant that the statements the defendant had made were based solely on unsubstantiated gossip. Is the attorney subject to discipline for obtaining the statement from the defendant in this matter?","choices":{"A":"No, because the attorney was following the plaintiff’s instructions.","B":"No, because the statement obtained was evidence that the defendant’s allegations were unfounded.","C":"Yes, because the attorney should have interviewed the defendant personally.","D":"Yes, because the attorney instructed the investigator to interview the defendant."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. “In representing a client, a lawyer shall not communicate about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows to be represented by another lawyer in the matter, unless the lawyer has the consent of the lawyer or is authorized to do so by law or a court order.” Model Rule 4.2. The fact the lawyer is using the investigator to communicate with the defendant does not change the analysis. A is incorrect. The simple fact that a client tells the attorney to do something does not mean the lawyer can go against the requirements of the Rules. This answer choice should have been discarded immediately. B is incorrect. The Rules bar the lawyer from communicating with a represented person. It would not make sense if an improper action could be made proper because of what was discovered through that improper action. C is incorrect. There is no Rule stating that attorneys cannot interview people through agents or employees. The issue here is that the person was represented by counsel.","rule":"D is the correct answer. “In representing a client, a lawyer shall not communicate about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows to be represented by another lawyer in the matter, unless the lawyer has the consent of the lawyer or is authorized to do so by law or a court order.” Model Rule 4.2. The fact the lawyer is using the investigator to communicate with the defendant does not change the analysis. A is incorrect. The simple fact that a client tells the attorney to do something does not mean the lawyer can go against the requirements of the Rules. This answer choice should have been discarded immediately. B is incorrect. The Rules bar the lawyer from communicating with a represented person. It would not make sense if an improper action could be made proper because of what was discovered through that improper action. C is incorrect. There is no Rule stating that attorneys cannot interview people through agents or employees. The issue here is that the person was represented by counsel.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for obtaining the statement from the defendant in this matter?","rule":"D is the correct answer. “In representing a client, a lawyer shall not communicate about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows to be represented by another lawyer in the matter, unless the lawyer has the consent of the lawyer or is authorized to do so by law or a court order.” Model Rule 4.2. The fact the lawyer is using the investigator to communicate with the defendant does not change the analysis. A is incorrect. The simple fact that a client tells the attorney to do something does not mean the lawyer can go against the requirements of the Rules. This answer choice should have been discarded immediately. B is incorrect. The Rules bar the lawyer from communicating with a represented person. It would not make sense if an improper action could be made proper because of what was discovered through that improper action. C is incorrect. There is no Rule stating that attorneys cannot interview people through agents or employees. The issue here is that the person was represented by counsel.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. “In representing a client, a lawyer shall not communicate about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows to be represented by another lawyer in the matter, unless the lawyer has the consent of the lawyer or is authorized to do so by law or a court order.” Model Rule 4.2. The fact the lawyer is using the investigator to communicate with the defendant does not change the analysis. A is incorrect. The simple fact that a client tells the attorney to do something does not mean the lawyer can go against the requirements of the Rules. This answer choice should have been discarded immediately. B is incorrect. The Rules bar the lawyer from communicating with a represented person. It would not make sense if an improper action could be made proper because of what was discovered through that improper action. C is incorrect. There is no Rule stating that attorneys cannot interview people through agents or employees. The issue here is that the person was represented by counsel.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 4.2"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[139,140],"sourceAnswerPages":[244],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理原告对被告提起诽谤诉讼。在被告律师已经提交并送达答辩状后，该律师依照原告指示，聘请一名持有执照的私人调查员，并指示调查员在不透露受雇关系的情况下设法询问被告。调查员成功私下询问被告，并使被告承认其所作陈述完全基于未经证实的传闻。该律师以这种方式从被告处取得陈述，是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为该律师是在遵从原告指示。","B":"否，因为取得的陈述证明被告的说法没有根据。","C":"是，因为该律师应当亲自询问被告。","D":"是，因为该律师指示调查员询问被告。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。“律师在代理委托人时，明知某人在该事项中由另一名律师代理，未经该律师同意或者没有法律或法院命令授权，不得就代理事项与此人沟通。”《职业行为示范规则》4.2。律师利用调查员与被告沟通，并不会改变分析。A 不正确。委托人指示律师做某事，并不意味着律师可以违反规则要求。该选项应立即排除。B 不正确。规则禁止律师与已有律师代理的人沟通。如果一项不当行为仅因通过该行为发现了某些内容就能变得适当，显然不合理。C 不正确。没有规则规定律师不能通过代理人或雇员询问他人。本题问题在于，被询问者已经有律师代理。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-017","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":17,"topic":"Model Rule 4.3","question":"An attorney was retained by a woman to advise her in negotiating a separation agreement with her husband. The husband, who was not a lawyer, had decided to act on his own behalf in the matter. The attorney never met or communicated with the husband during the negotiations. After several months, the woman advised the attorney that the parties had reached agreement and presented him with the terms. The attorney prepared a proposed agreement that contained all of the agreed-upon terms. The attorney mailed the proposed agreement to the husband, with a cover letter stating: “As you know, I represent your wife in this matter and I do not represent your interests. I enclose two copies of the separation agreement that I have drafted in accordance with my client’s directions. Please read the agreement and, if it meets with your approval, sign both copies before a notary and return them to me. I will then have your wife sign them and will furnish you with a fully executed copy.” Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney did not suggest that the husband seek the advice of independent counsel before signing the agreement.","B":"Yes, because the attorney directly communicated with an unrepresented person.","C":"No, because the attorney acted only as a scrivener.","D":"No, because the attorney’s letter did not imply that the attorney was disinterested and the attorney did not give legal advice to the husband."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Unlike a represented person, a lawyer may communicate with an unrepresented person as long as the lawyer does not imply he or she is disinterested and the lawyer does not give legal advice to a person whose interests are in conflict with the interests of the lawyer’s client. Model Rule 4.3. Here, the husband’s interests are in conflict with the wife’s. Importantly, the letter did not imply disinterest or give legal advice. A is incorrect. Under the Rule, the attorney does not have to give advice that the unrepresented person seek counsel, unless the attorney chooses to do so. Importantly, this is the only “legal” advice the attorney could properly give. B is incorrect. As stated above, a lawyer can communicate with an unrepresented person, unless he or she implies disinterest or gives legal advice. Model Rule 4.3. C is incorrect. A scrivener is someone who simply copies or records the communications of others. The attorney is not doing that here.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Unlike a represented person, a lawyer may communicate with an unrepresented person as long as the lawyer does not imply he or she is disinterested and the lawyer does not give legal advice to a person whose interests are in conflict with the interests of the lawyer’s client. Model Rule 4.3. Here, the husband’s interests are in conflict with the wife’s. Importantly, the letter did not imply disinterest or give legal advice. A is incorrect. Under the Rule, the attorney does not have to give advice that the unrepresented person seek counsel, unless the attorney chooses to do so. Importantly, this is the only “legal” advice the attorney could properly give. B is incorrect. As stated above, a lawyer can communicate with an unrepresented person, unless he or she implies disinterest or gives legal advice. Model Rule 4.3. C is incorrect. A scrivener is someone who simply copies or records the communications of others. The attorney is not doing that here.","irac":{"issue":"” Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Unlike a represented person, a lawyer may communicate with an unrepresented person as long as the lawyer does not imply he or she is disinterested and the lawyer does not give legal advice to a person whose interests are in conflict with the interests of the lawyer’s client. Model Rule 4.3. Here, the husband’s interests are in conflict with the wife’s. Importantly, the letter did not imply disinterest or give legal advice. A is incorrect. Under the Rule, the attorney does not have to give advice that the unrepresented person seek counsel, unless the attorney chooses to do so. Importantly, this is the only “legal” advice the attorney could properly give. B is incorrect. As stated above, a lawyer can communicate with an unrepresented person, unless he or she implies disinterest or gives legal advice. Model Rule 4.3. C is incorrect. A scrivener is someone who simply copies or records the communications of others. The attorney is not doing that here.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Unlike a represented person, a lawyer may communicate with an unrepresented person as long as the lawyer does not imply he or she is disinterested and the lawyer does not give legal advice to a person whose interests are in conflict with the interests of the lawyer’s client. Model Rule 4.3. Here, the husband’s interests are in conflict with the wife’s. Importantly, the letter did not imply disinterest or give legal advice. A is incorrect. Under the Rule, the attorney does not have to give advice that the unrepresented person seek counsel, unless the attorney chooses to do so. Importantly, this is the only “legal” advice the attorney could properly give. B is incorrect. As stated above, a lawyer can communicate with an unrepresented person, unless he or she implies disinterest or gives legal advice. Model Rule 4.3. C is incorrect. A scrivener is someone who simply copies or records the communications of others. The attorney is not doing that here.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 4.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[140],"sourceAnswerPages":[244,245],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名女子聘请律师，就与丈夫谈判分居协议提供意见。丈夫不是律师，并决定在该事项中自行处理。谈判期间，该律师从未与丈夫会面或沟通。数月后，女子告知律师，双方已经达成协议，并向律师提供了协议条款。律师起草一份包含所有约定条款的拟议协议。他将协议邮寄给丈夫，并在附函中写道：“如您所知，我在本事项中代理您的妻子，并不代表您的利益。现随函附上两份我依委托人指示起草的分居协议。请阅读协议；如您同意，请在公证人面前签署两份副本并寄回给我。届时我会让您的妻子签署，并向您提供一份双方完整签署的副本。”该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师没有建议丈夫在签署协议前寻求独立律师的意见。","B":"是，因为律师直接与没有律师代理的人进行了沟通。","C":"否，因为律师只是担任文书起草人。","D":"否，因为律师的信没有暗示自己是不偏不倚的，也没有向丈夫提供法律意见。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。与已有律师代理的人不同，律师可以与没有律师代理的人沟通，但不得暗示自己不偏不倚，也不得向其利益与本律师委托人相冲突的人提供法律意见。《职业行为示范规则》4.3。本题中，丈夫的利益与妻子相冲突。关键是，该信没有暗示律师不偏不倚，也没有提供法律意见。A 不正确。根据规则，除非律师自愿这样做，否则并不必须建议没有律师代理的人另聘律师。需要特别注意的是，这是律师可以适当向其提供的唯一“法律”建议。B 不正确。如上所述，只要律师不暗示自己不偏不倚，也不提供法律意见，就可以与没有律师代理的人沟通。规则 4.3。C 不正确。文书起草人是只对他人沟通内容进行抄写或记录的人，该律师在本题中并非仅仅从事这种工作。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-018","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":18,"topic":"Rule 2.11 / Rule 2.11(A)(6)(a) / Rule 2.11(A)","question":"A judge, prior to her appointment to the probate court, was a partner in a law firm. The law firm had an extensive probate practice. At the time of the judge’s appointment, the law firm had pending before the court to which the judge was appointed numerous matters in which requests were being made for allowances for attorney’s fees. The judge did not note her association on the record. When the judge left the law firm, she was paid a cash settlement. She has no further financial interest in any matter handled by the law firm. The judge is now being asked to rule on these requests for allowances for attorney’s fees. The requests are not contested. Is it proper for the judge to rule on these requests?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the judge has no financial interest in the outcome of these cases.","B":"Yes, because these requests are not contested.","C":"No, because the judge did not note on the record in each case her prior association with the law firm.","D":"No, because the judge was associated with the law firm when these matters were pending."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. A judge must disqualify himself or herself in any proceeding in which the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11. Such situations include when the judge “served as a lawyer in the matter in controversy, or was associated with a lawyer who participated substantially as a lawyer in the matter during such association.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(6)(a). Consequently, since the judge was associated with the law firm when these matters were pending, she must disqualify herself. A is incorrect. Whether or not the judge has a financial interest in the case, the primary issue is that she was a partner at the firm when these matters were pending. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(6)(a). B is incorrect. Even if the requests were not contested, the Rules still disqualify a judge if he or she served as a lawyer in the matter in controversy or were associated with a lawyer who did so. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(6)(a). This answer choice is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice that should be avoided. C is incorrect. The issue is that the judge was associated with the firm when these matters were pending. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A) (6)(a). Simply noting her involvement in record would not solve the problem of the judge’s potential lack of impartiality.","rule":"D is the correct answer. A judge must disqualify himself or herself in any proceeding in which the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11. Such situations include when the judge “served as a lawyer in the matter in controversy, or was associated with a lawyer who participated substantially as a lawyer in the matter during such association.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(6)(a). Consequently, since the judge was associated with the law firm when these matters were pending, she must disqualify herself. A is incorrect. Whether or not the judge has a financial interest in the case, the primary issue is that she was a partner at the firm when these matters were pending. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(6)(a). B is incorrect. Even if the requests were not contested, the Rules still disqualify a judge if he or she served as a lawyer in the matter in controversy or were associated with a lawyer who did so. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(6)(a). This answer choice is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice that should be avoided. C is incorrect. The issue is that the judge was associated with the firm when these matters were pending. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A) (6)(a). Simply noting her involvement in record would not solve the problem of the judge’s potential lack of impartiality.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the judge to rule on these requests?","rule":"D is the correct answer. A judge must disqualify himself or herself in any proceeding in which the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11. Such situations include when the judge “served as a lawyer in the matter in controversy, or was associated with a lawyer who participated substantially as a lawyer in the matter during such association.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(6)(a). Consequently, since the judge was associated with the law firm when these matters were pending, she must disqualify herself. A is incorrect. Whether or not the judge has a financial interest in the case, the primary issue is that she was a partner at the firm when these matters were pending. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(6)(a). B is incorrect. Even if the requests were not contested, the Rules still disqualify a judge if he or she served as a lawyer in the matter in controversy or were associated with a lawyer who did so. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(6)(a). This answer choice is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice that should be avoided. C is incorrect. The issue is that the judge was associated with the firm when these matters were pending. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A) (6)(a). Simply noting her involvement in record would not solve the problem of the judge’s potential lack of impartiality.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. A judge must disqualify himself or herself in any proceeding in which the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11. Such situations include when the judge “served as a lawyer in the matter in controversy, or was associated with a lawyer who participated substantially as a lawyer in the matter during such association.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(6)(a). Consequently, since the judge was associated with the law firm when these matters were pending, she must disqualify herself. A is incorrect. Whether or not the judge has a financial interest in the case, the primary issue is that she was a partner at the firm when these matters were pending. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(6)(a). B is incorrect. Even if the requests were not contested, the Rules still disqualify a judge if he or she served as a lawyer in the matter in controversy or were associated with a lawyer who did so. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(6)(a). This answer choice is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice that should be avoided. C is incorrect. The issue is that the judge was associated with the firm when these matters were pending. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A) (6)(a). Simply noting her involvement in record would not solve the problem of the judge’s potential lack of impartiality.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 2.11","Rule 2.11(A)(6)(a)","Rule 2.11(A)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[140,141],"sourceAnswerPages":[245],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名法官在被任命到遗嘱检验法院之前，是一家律师事务所的合伙人。该事务所办理大量遗嘱检验业务。法官获任命时，该事务所在法官即将任职的法院有大量待决事项，其中正在申请核准律师费。法官没有在案件记录中注明自己与该事务所的关系。法官离开事务所时取得了一笔现金结算款，对事务所办理的任何事项不再拥有经济利益。现在有人请求法官裁定这些律师费核准申请。相关申请均无人争议。法官裁定这些申请是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为法官对这些案件的结果不具有经济利益。","B":"适当，因为这些申请无人争议。","C":"不适当，因为法官没有在每宗案件的记录中注明自己先前与该事务所的关系。","D":"不适当，因为这些事项处于待决状态时，法官与该律师事务所有关联。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。在任何可能使人合理质疑法官公正性的程序中，法官必须回避。《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.11。这类情形包括法官“曾在争议事项中担任律师，或者在与某律师有关联期间，该律师曾作为律师实质性参与该事项”。规则 2.11(A)(6)(a)。因此，由于这些事项待决时法官与该律师事务所有关联，她必须回避。A 不正确。无论法官是否对案件拥有经济利益，首要问题都是这些事项待决时她是该事务所的合伙人。规则 2.11(A)(6)(a)。B 不正确。即使申请无人争议，只要法官曾在争议事项中担任律师，或者曾与实质性参与该事项的律师有关联，规则仍要求法官回避。规则 2.11(A)(6)(a)。这是典型的“没有损害，就没有违规”型错误选项，应予避免。C 不正确。问题在于这些事项待决时法官与事务所有关联。规则 2.11(A)(6)(a)。仅仅在记录中注明其参与情况，不能解决法官可能缺乏公正性的问题。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-019","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":19,"topic":"Model Rule 1.12(a) / Model Rule 1.12(d)","question":"An attorney’s law firm regularly represented a large company in its international business transactions. The company became involved in a contractual dispute with a foreign government. The company invoked a mandatory arbitration procedure contained in the contract. Under the arbitration clause, each party was allowed to choose a partisan arbitrator and the partisan arbitrators were to choose an additional arbitrator to sit on the panel. The company selected the attorney to be on the arbitration panel. Neither the attorney nor his law firm had represented the company in connection with the contract with the foreign government. The arbitration was completed, and the company was awarded the sum of $100,000. The company then hired the attorney to enforce the award. The attorney obtained the consent of the other arbitrators before accepting the representation. He was successful in enforcing the award. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney should not have represented the company in a matter in which the attorney had been an arbitrator.","B":"Yes, because the attorney should have declined the arbitration assignment in view of his law firm’s regular representation of the company.","C":"No, because the attorney obtained the consent of the other arbitrators before accepting the representation.","D":"No, because the attorney was appointed to the arbitration panel as a partisan arbitrator."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Generally, “a lawyer shall not represent anyone in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially” as an arbitrator. Model Rule 1.12(a). However, there is an exception when the lawyer is selected as a partisan arbitrator of a party in a multimember arbitration panel. In that case, the attorney is not prohibited from subsequently representing that party. Model Rule 1.12(d). Here, since the attorney was appointed to the panel as a partisan arbitrator, he can represent the company. A is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.12(d), if an attorney is selected as a partisan arbitrator of a party in a multimember arbitration panel, he or she can subsequently represent that party. B is incorrect. The attorney’s firm’s prior association with the company would not bar the attorney from serving as a partisan arbitrator. C is incorrect. This answer choice is a case of giving a Rule that doesn’t apply to these facts. Generally, a lawyer who has been an arbitrator on a matter cannot represent a party unless all parties to the proceeding give informed consent, confirmed in writing. Model Rule 1.12(a). However, since the attorney was a partisan arbitrator, he does not need to the parties’ consent to subsequently represent the company. Model Rule 1.12(d).","rule":"D is the correct answer. Generally, “a lawyer shall not represent anyone in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially” as an arbitrator. Model Rule 1.12(a). However, there is an exception when the lawyer is selected as a partisan arbitrator of a party in a multimember arbitration panel. In that case, the attorney is not prohibited from subsequently representing that party. Model Rule 1.12(d). Here, since the attorney was appointed to the panel as a partisan arbitrator, he can represent the company. A is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.12(d), if an attorney is selected as a partisan arbitrator of a party in a multimember arbitration panel, he or she can subsequently represent that party. B is incorrect. The attorney’s firm’s prior association with the company would not bar the attorney from serving as a partisan arbitrator. C is incorrect. This answer choice is a case of giving a Rule that doesn’t apply to these facts. Generally, a lawyer who has been an arbitrator on a matter cannot represent a party unless all parties to the proceeding give informed consent, confirmed in writing. Model Rule 1.12(a). However, since the attorney was a partisan arbitrator, he does not need to the parties’ consent to subsequently represent the company. Model Rule 1.12(d).","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Generally, “a lawyer shall not represent anyone in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially” as an arbitrator. Model Rule 1.12(a). However, there is an exception when the lawyer is selected as a partisan arbitrator of a party in a multimember arbitration panel. In that case, the attorney is not prohibited from subsequently representing that party. Model Rule 1.12(d). Here, since the attorney was appointed to the panel as a partisan arbitrator, he can represent the company. A is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.12(d), if an attorney is selected as a partisan arbitrator of a party in a multimember arbitration panel, he or she can subsequently represent that party. B is incorrect. The attorney’s firm’s prior association with the company would not bar the attorney from serving as a partisan arbitrator. C is incorrect. This answer choice is a case of giving a Rule that doesn’t apply to these facts. Generally, a lawyer who has been an arbitrator on a matter cannot represent a party unless all parties to the proceeding give informed consent, confirmed in writing. Model Rule 1.12(a). However, since the attorney was a partisan arbitrator, he does not need to the parties’ consent to subsequently represent the company. Model Rule 1.12(d).","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Generally, “a lawyer shall not represent anyone in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially” as an arbitrator. Model Rule 1.12(a). However, there is an exception when the lawyer is selected as a partisan arbitrator of a party in a multimember arbitration panel. In that case, the attorney is not prohibited from subsequently representing that party. Model Rule 1.12(d). Here, since the attorney was appointed to the panel as a partisan arbitrator, he can represent the company. A is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.12(d), if an attorney is selected as a partisan arbitrator of a party in a multimember arbitration panel, he or she can subsequently represent that party. B is incorrect. The attorney’s firm’s prior association with the company would not bar the attorney from serving as a partisan arbitrator. C is incorrect. This answer choice is a case of giving a Rule that doesn’t apply to these facts. Generally, a lawyer who has been an arbitrator on a matter cannot represent a party unless all parties to the proceeding give informed consent, confirmed in writing. Model Rule 1.12(a). However, since the attorney was a partisan arbitrator, he does not need to the parties’ consent to subsequently represent the company. Model Rule 1.12(d).","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.12(a)","Model Rule 1.12(d)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[141],"sourceAnswerPages":[245,246],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师所在的事务所经常在国际商业交易中代理一家大型公司。该公司与一外国政府发生合同争议，并启动了合同中规定的强制仲裁程序。根据仲裁条款，每一方可选择一名有倾向性的仲裁员，再由两名有倾向性的仲裁员选择一名额外仲裁员组成仲裁庭。公司选择该律师担任仲裁员。该律师及其事务所均未就与外国政府订立的这份合同代理过公司。仲裁结束后，公司获裁决 100,000 美元。公司随后聘请该律师执行仲裁裁决。律师在接受委托前取得了其他仲裁员的同意，并成功执行裁决。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该律师不应在自己曾担任仲裁员的事项中代理公司。","B":"是，因为鉴于其事务所经常代理公司，该律师本应拒绝仲裁任务。","C":"否，因为该律师在接受代理前取得了其他仲裁员的同意。","D":"否，因为该律师是作为公司选定的有倾向性仲裁员加入仲裁庭。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。一般而言，“律师不得就其曾作为仲裁员亲自且实质性参与的事项代理任何人”。《职业行为示范规则》1.12(a)。不过，如果律师是在由多名仲裁员组成的仲裁庭中被一方选为有倾向性的仲裁员，则存在例外。在这种情况下，律师之后并不被禁止代理该方。规则 1.12(d)。本题中，该律师是作为有倾向性的仲裁员被任命到仲裁庭，因此可以代理公司。A 不正确。根据规则 1.12(d)，如果律师在多名仲裁员组成的仲裁庭中被一方选为有倾向性的仲裁员，此后可以代理该方。B 不正确。该律师事务所先前与公司的代理关系，不会禁止律师担任有倾向性的仲裁员。C 不正确。该选项引用了一项不适用于本题事实的规则。通常，曾在某事项中担任仲裁员的律师，除非取得程序所有当事人经书面确认的知情同意，否则不得代理其中一方。规则 1.12(a)。但由于该律师是有倾向性的仲裁员，之后代理公司无须取得各方同意。规则 1.12(d)。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-020","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":20,"topic":"Model Rule 3.3","question":"An attorney represented a client who was injured when the television antenna he was attempting to erect in his yard came in contact with a power line. As part of its defense, the manufacturer of the antenna claimed that the antenna came with a warning label advising against erecting the antenna near power lines. The client told the attorney that he had not seen a warning label. The client’s wife told the attorney that she had kept the antenna and the box it came in and that she saw no warning label anywhere. When called by the attorney as witnesses, both the client and his wife testified that they had never seen a warning label. After their testimony, but while the trial was still in progress, the attorney learned from the wife’s sister that there indeed had been a warning label on the box, but that the wife had removed and destroyed it. When the attorney confronted the wife with her sister’s statement, the wife admitted destroying the label but insisted that her husband knew nothing about it. The attorney continued the trial, but made no reference to the absence of a warning label in his summation to the jury. Instead, the attorney argued that the warning label, even if seen, was insufficient to advise his client of the serious consequences that would ensue if the warning was not heeded. The jury found in favor of the manufacturer. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney called the wife as a witness and she gave perjured testimony.","B":"Yes, because the attorney failed to take reasonable remedial action after he realized that the wife had given perjured testimony.","C":"No, because the jury apparently disbelieved the wife’s testimony.","D":"No, because the attorney did not rely on the wife’s testimony once he discovered the perjury."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. “If a lawyer, the lawyer’s client, or a witness called by the lawyer, has offered material evidence and the lawyer comes to know of its falsity, the lawyer shall take reasonable remedial measures, including, if necessary, disclosure to the tribunal.” Model Rule 3.3. Simply not referring to the absence of the warning label in his summation is not a valid remedial measure. A is incorrect. While the attorney called the wife as a witness, the issue is that the attorney came to know of the falsity of the testimony and took no reasonable remedial measures to correct it. If the attorney never discovered the lie, he could not reasonably be expected to do something about it. C is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no foul” answer choice and should be discarded immediately. D is incorrect. While he may not have relied on the testimony, the attorney still knew of its falsity and did nothing to remediate matters.","rule":"B is the correct answer. “If a lawyer, the lawyer’s client, or a witness called by the lawyer, has offered material evidence and the lawyer comes to know of its falsity, the lawyer shall take reasonable remedial measures, including, if necessary, disclosure to the tribunal.” Model Rule 3.3. Simply not referring to the absence of the warning label in his summation is not a valid remedial measure. A is incorrect. While the attorney called the wife as a witness, the issue is that the attorney came to know of the falsity of the testimony and took no reasonable remedial measures to correct it. If the attorney never discovered the lie, he could not reasonably be expected to do something about it. C is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no foul” answer choice and should be discarded immediately. D is incorrect. While he may not have relied on the testimony, the attorney still knew of its falsity and did nothing to remediate matters.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. “If a lawyer, the lawyer’s client, or a witness called by the lawyer, has offered material evidence and the lawyer comes to know of its falsity, the lawyer shall take reasonable remedial measures, including, if necessary, disclosure to the tribunal.” Model Rule 3.3. Simply not referring to the absence of the warning label in his summation is not a valid remedial measure. A is incorrect. While the attorney called the wife as a witness, the issue is that the attorney came to know of the falsity of the testimony and took no reasonable remedial measures to correct it. If the attorney never discovered the lie, he could not reasonably be expected to do something about it. C is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no foul” answer choice and should be discarded immediately. D is incorrect. While he may not have relied on the testimony, the attorney still knew of its falsity and did nothing to remediate matters.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. “If a lawyer, the lawyer’s client, or a witness called by the lawyer, has offered material evidence and the lawyer comes to know of its falsity, the lawyer shall take reasonable remedial measures, including, if necessary, disclosure to the tribunal.” Model Rule 3.3. Simply not referring to the absence of the warning label in his summation is not a valid remedial measure. A is incorrect. While the attorney called the wife as a witness, the issue is that the attorney came to know of the falsity of the testimony and took no reasonable remedial measures to correct it. If the attorney never discovered the lie, he could not reasonably be expected to do something about it. C is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no foul” answer choice and should be discarded immediately. D is incorrect. While he may not have relied on the testimony, the attorney still knew of its falsity and did nothing to remediate matters.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[142],"sourceAnswerPages":[246],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理一名委托人，后者在自家庭院架设电视天线时，天线接触电线而受伤。作为抗辩的一部分，天线制造商声称，天线附有警告标签，提醒不得在电线附近架设。委托人告诉律师，他没有见过警告标签。委托人的妻子告诉律师，她保留了天线及包装箱，但在任何地方都没有看到警告标签。律师传唤二人作证时，委托人和妻子均作证说自己从未见过警告标签。作证后、审判仍在进行期间，律师从妻子的姐妹处得知，包装箱上事实上确有警告标签，但妻子将其撕下并销毁。律师以姐妹的陈述质问妻子时，妻子承认销毁标签，却坚持丈夫对此毫不知情。律师继续进行审判，但在向陪审团作总结陈词时没有提及缺少警告标签。相反，律师主张，即使看到了警告标签，其内容也不足以告知委托人，不遵守警告会造成何等严重的后果。陪审团最终支持制造商。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该律师传唤妻子作证，而她作出了伪证。","B":"是，因为该律师发现妻子作伪证后，没有采取合理补救措施。","C":"否，因为陪审团显然不相信妻子的证言。","D":"否，因为该律师发现伪证后没有再依赖妻子的证言。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。“如果律师、律师的委托人或者由律师传唤的证人已经提出重大证据，而律师后来得知该证据虚假，律师应采取合理补救措施，必要时包括向审裁机构披露。”《职业行为示范规则》3.3。仅仅在总结陈词中不提及警告标签的缺失，并不是有效的补救措施。A 不正确。律师虽然传唤妻子作证，但问题在于律师后来得知证言虚假，却没有采取合理补救措施予以纠正。如果律师从未发现谎言，就不能合理地期待他对此采取行动。C 不正确。这是典型的“没有损害，就没有违规”型选项，应立即排除。D 不正确。律师虽然可能没有再依赖该证言，但仍然知道证言虚假，却没有采取任何补救措施。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-021","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":21,"topic":"Model Rule 1.6","question":"The attorney represented a client in a claim involving a breach of the client’s employment contract. The case was settled without suit being filed. The proceeds of the settlement were paid directly to the client, who subsequently paid the attorney in full for the attorney’s fee and expenses. Thereafter, the attorney did no other work for the client. The client is now being audited by the Internal Revenue Service (IRS). The IRS has asked the attorney for details of the settlement, including the amount claimed for each item of damage and the amounts paid for the items. The attorney reported the request to the client, who told the attorney not to provide the information to the IRS. The attorney believed the disclosure could hurt his client. Is it proper for the attorney to furnish the information to the IRS?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the information does not involve the attorney’s work product.","B":"Yes, because the attorney no longer represents the client.","C":"No, because the client told the attorney not to provide the information.","D":"No, because the attorney did not believe the disclosure would be beneficial to the client."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent” or the disclosure falls within one of the exceptions related to preventing crime, defending the attorney, or compliance with law or court order. Model Rule 1.6. The IRS is asking the attorney for information regarding his representation in the employment contract case, and the client has not given his consent for the disclosure. Since there is no indication the disclosure falls within any of the exceptions, it would be improper for the attorney to give the information to the IRS. A is incorrect. “A lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent” or the disclosure falls within certain exceptions. Model Rule 1.6. Importantly, the “confidentiality rule . . . applies not only to matters communicated in confidence by the client, but also to all information relating to the representation.” Model Rule 1.6, Comment [3]. Consequently, the Rule covers more than the attorney’s work product. B is incorrect. “The duty of confidentiality continues after the client-lawyer relationship has terminated.” Model Rule 1.6, Comment [20]. D is incorrect. The important point in the confidentiality rule is that the client did not consent to disclosure. Consequently, even if the lawyer believed disclosing the information might be beneficial, he still could not do so without his client’s consent.","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent” or the disclosure falls within one of the exceptions related to preventing crime, defending the attorney, or compliance with law or court order. Model Rule 1.6. The IRS is asking the attorney for information regarding his representation in the employment contract case, and the client has not given his consent for the disclosure. Since there is no indication the disclosure falls within any of the exceptions, it would be improper for the attorney to give the information to the IRS. A is incorrect. “A lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent” or the disclosure falls within certain exceptions. Model Rule 1.6. Importantly, the “confidentiality rule . . . applies not only to matters communicated in confidence by the client, but also to all information relating to the representation.” Model Rule 1.6, Comment [3]. Consequently, the Rule covers more than the attorney’s work product. B is incorrect. “The duty of confidentiality continues after the client-lawyer relationship has terminated.” Model Rule 1.6, Comment [20]. D is incorrect. The important point in the confidentiality rule is that the client did not consent to disclosure. Consequently, even if the lawyer believed disclosing the information might be beneficial, he still could not do so without his client’s consent.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the attorney to furnish the information to the IRS?","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent” or the disclosure falls within one of the exceptions related to preventing crime, defending the attorney, or compliance with law or court order. Model Rule 1.6. The IRS is asking the attorney for information regarding his representation in the employment contract case, and the client has not given his consent for the disclosure. Since there is no indication the disclosure falls within any of the exceptions, it would be improper for the attorney to give the information to the IRS. A is incorrect. “A lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent” or the disclosure falls within certain exceptions. Model Rule 1.6. Importantly, the “confidentiality rule . . . applies not only to matters communicated in confidence by the client, but also to all information relating to the representation.” Model Rule 1.6, Comment [3]. Consequently, the Rule covers more than the attorney’s work product. B is incorrect. “The duty of confidentiality continues after the client-lawyer relationship has terminated.” Model Rule 1.6, Comment [20]. D is incorrect. The important point in the confidentiality rule is that the client did not consent to disclosure. Consequently, even if the lawyer believed disclosing the information might be beneficial, he still could not do so without his client’s consent.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent” or the disclosure falls within one of the exceptions related to preventing crime, defending the attorney, or compliance with law or court order. Model Rule 1.6. The IRS is asking the attorney for information regarding his representation in the employment contract case, and the client has not given his consent for the disclosure. Since there is no indication the disclosure falls within any of the exceptions, it would be improper for the attorney to give the information to the IRS. A is incorrect. “A lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent” or the disclosure falls within certain exceptions. Model Rule 1.6. Importantly, the “confidentiality rule . . . applies not only to matters communicated in confidence by the client, but also to all information relating to the representation.” Model Rule 1.6, Comment [3]. Consequently, the Rule covers more than the attorney’s work product. B is incorrect. “The duty of confidentiality continues after the client-lawyer relationship has terminated.” Model Rule 1.6, Comment [20]. D is incorrect. The important point in the confidentiality rule is that the client did not consent to disclosure. Consequently, even if the lawyer believed disclosing the information might be beneficial, he still could not do so without his client’s consent.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.6"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[142,143],"sourceAnswerPages":[247],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理委托人提出一项有关其劳动合同遭违反的索赔。案件在尚未提起诉讼时即达成和解。和解款直接支付给委托人，委托人随后全额向律师支付了律师费和开支。此后，律师没有再为委托人处理任何工作。现在，美国国税局（IRS）正在审计该委托人。IRS 要求律师提供和解详情，包括每一项损害所主张的金额，以及就各项损害实际支付的金额。律师将该要求告知委托人，委托人指示律师不要向 IRS 提供信息。律师认为披露可能损害委托人。律师向 IRS 提供信息是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为该信息不涉及律师的工作成果。","B":"适当，因为律师已不再代理该委托人。","C":"不适当，因为委托人已指示律师不要提供信息。","D":"不适当，因为律师认为披露不会使委托人受益。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。“除非委托人作出知情同意”，或者披露属于为防止犯罪、为律师自身辩护，或为遵守法律或法院命令等例外之一，否则“律师不得披露与委托人代理有关的信息”。《职业行为示范规则》1.6。IRS 要求律师提供其在劳动合同案件中代理委托人的相关信息，而委托人没有同意披露。没有迹象表明披露属于任何例外，因此律师向 IRS 提供该信息是不适当的。A 不正确。“除非委托人作出知情同意”或披露属于特定例外，否则“律师不得披露与委托人代理有关的信息”。规则 1.6。尤其要注意，“保密规则……不仅适用于委托人以保密方式传达的事项，也适用于与代理有关的全部信息”。规则 1.6 评注 [3]。因此，该规则所涵盖的不止是律师的工作成果。B 不正确。“保密义务在律师—委托人关系终止后仍然继续。”规则 1.6 评注 [20]。D 不正确。保密规则的关键是委托人没有同意披露。因此，即使律师认为披露信息可能对委托人有利，未经委托人同意仍不得披露。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-022","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":22,"topic":"Model Rule 7.2(b)","question":"An attorney and a licensed real estate broker entered into an agreement whereby the broker was to recommend the attorney to any customer of the broker who needed legal services, and the attorney was to recommend the broker to any client of the attorney who wished to buy or sell real estate. The attorney’s practice is limited almost entirely to real estate law. The attorney and the broker do not share fees with each other, and their prices are reasonable. Is the attorney subject to discipline for entering into the agreement with the broker?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney is compensating the broker for recommending the attorney’s legal services.","B":"Yes, because the arrangement constitutes the practice of law in association with a nonlawyer.","C":"No, because neither the attorney nor the broker shares in the other’s fees.","D":"No, because the fees of the attorney and the broker do not clearly exceed reasonable fees for the services rendered by each."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2(b), a lawyer may not give anything of value to a person for recommending the lawyer’s services except under certain circumstances. “Anything of value” refers to more than money, so the fact that the attorney is recommending the broker in exchange for the broker’s recommendations violates this Rule. B is incorrect. A close reading of the question shows that the parties have not entered into any association. They are merely recommending each other’s services, although the fact that the attorney is giving the broker something of value for the broker’s recommendations violates the Rules. C is incorrect. Even though they are not sharing fees, the arrangement violates Model Rule 7.2(b). D is incorrect. Although their fees are reasonable, the arrangement violates Model Rule 7.2(b). This is another “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer.","rule":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2(b), a lawyer may not give anything of value to a person for recommending the lawyer’s services except under certain circumstances. “Anything of value” refers to more than money, so the fact that the attorney is recommending the broker in exchange for the broker’s recommendations violates this Rule. B is incorrect. A close reading of the question shows that the parties have not entered into any association. They are merely recommending each other’s services, although the fact that the attorney is giving the broker something of value for the broker’s recommendations violates the Rules. C is incorrect. Even though they are not sharing fees, the arrangement violates Model Rule 7.2(b). D is incorrect. Although their fees are reasonable, the arrangement violates Model Rule 7.2(b). This is another “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for entering into the agreement with the broker?","rule":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2(b), a lawyer may not give anything of value to a person for recommending the lawyer’s services except under certain circumstances. “Anything of value” refers to more than money, so the fact that the attorney is recommending the broker in exchange for the broker’s recommendations violates this Rule. B is incorrect. A close reading of the question shows that the parties have not entered into any association. They are merely recommending each other’s services, although the fact that the attorney is giving the broker something of value for the broker’s recommendations violates the Rules. C is incorrect. Even though they are not sharing fees, the arrangement violates Model Rule 7.2(b). D is incorrect. Although their fees are reasonable, the arrangement violates Model Rule 7.2(b). This is another “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2(b), a lawyer may not give anything of value to a person for recommending the lawyer’s services except under certain circumstances. “Anything of value” refers to more than money, so the fact that the attorney is recommending the broker in exchange for the broker’s recommendations violates this Rule. B is incorrect. A close reading of the question shows that the parties have not entered into any association. They are merely recommending each other’s services, although the fact that the attorney is giving the broker something of value for the broker’s recommendations violates the Rules. C is incorrect. Even though they are not sharing fees, the arrangement violates Model Rule 7.2(b). D is incorrect. Although their fees are reasonable, the arrangement violates Model Rule 7.2(b). This is another “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.2(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[143],"sourceAnswerPages":[247,248],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师与一名持有执照的房地产经纪人达成协议：经纪人将向任何需要法律服务的顾客推荐律师，而律师将向任何希望买卖房地产的委托人推荐经纪人。律师的业务几乎完全限于房地产法。律师与经纪人互不分享费用，二人的收费也都合理。律师因与经纪人达成该协议是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师正在向经纪人提供回报，以换取其推荐律师的法律服务。","B":"是，因为该安排构成与非律师联合从事法律执业活动。","C":"否，因为律师和经纪人均不分享对方的费用。","D":"否，因为律师和经纪人的收费没有明显超过各自所提供服务的合理费用。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。根据《职业行为示范规则》7.2(b)，除特定情形外，律师不得为他人推荐律师服务而向其给予任何有价值之物。“任何有价值之物”不止指金钱，因此，律师以向经纪人作出推荐来换取经纪人的推荐，违反该规则。B 不正确。仔细阅读题目可知，双方没有组成任何联合组织。他们只是相互推荐服务；不过，律师为换取经纪人的推荐而向其给予有价值之物，确实违反规则。C 不正确。即使二人不分享费用，该安排仍违反规则 7.2(b)。D 不正确。二人的收费虽然合理，该安排仍违反规则 7.2(b)。这又是一个“没有损害，就没有违规”的错误选项。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-023","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":23,"topic":"Model Rule 4.3","question":"An attorney represented a respondent in proceedings instituted by a child protection services agency to establish the paternity of a child and to recover past-due child support. The mother of the child had refused to file a complaint, had refused to retain a lawyer, and in fact had asked that the agency not file any action whatsoever. However, state law permitted the agency to commence paternity and support proceedings in its own name in such circumstances. The attorney contacted the mother without the knowledge or consent of the agency or its lawyers. The attorney identified himself to the mother as “an officer of the court” and told the mother that he was investigating the matter. Based upon what she told him, the attorney prepared and the mother signed an affidavit truthfully stating that the respondent was not the father of the child. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney acted without the knowledge or consent of the agency or its lawyers.","B":"Yes, because the attorney implied that he was disinterested in the matter.","C":"No, because all of the attorney’s statements to the mother were true.","D":"No, because the attorney did not give the mother legal advice."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. “In dealing on behalf of a client with a person who is not represented by counsel, a lawyer shall not state or imply that the lawyer is disinterested.” Model Rule 4.3. Because the attorney simply identified himself as “an officer of the court,” he improperly implied he was disinterested in the outcome, when in fact he was working on behalf of the respondent. A is incorrect. Here, even if the agency had known or given its consent to the attorney’s actions, the primary issue is that the attorney contacted the woman and implied that he was disinterested when in fact he was representing the respondent. C is incorrect. It’s debatable whether all of the attorney’s statements were true, considering the fact that he implied he was only “an officer of the court.” Importantly, in doing so, he violated the Rule regarding implied disinterest. D is incorrect. As stated above, the primary issue was that the attorney implied he was disinterested. However, if he had given legal advice, it would also be a violation of Model Rule 4.3. Under Model Rule 4.3, “[t]he lawyer shall not give legal advice to an unrepresented person, other than the advice to secure counsel, if the lawyer knows or reasonably should know that the interests of such a person are or have a reasonable possibility of being in conflict with the interests of the client.” Here, the woman’s interests are likely in conflict with the respondent’s interests, since the matter of paternity and support for the child is in question. Even so, in communications questions, an answer choice saying an attorney is not subject to discipline because he or she did not give legal advice is usually incorrect.","rule":"B is the correct answer. “In dealing on behalf of a client with a person who is not represented by counsel, a lawyer shall not state or imply that the lawyer is disinterested.” Model Rule 4.3. Because the attorney simply identified himself as “an officer of the court,” he improperly implied he was disinterested in the outcome, when in fact he was working on behalf of the respondent. A is incorrect. Here, even if the agency had known or given its consent to the attorney’s actions, the primary issue is that the attorney contacted the woman and implied that he was disinterested when in fact he was representing the respondent. C is incorrect. It’s debatable whether all of the attorney’s statements were true, considering the fact that he implied he was only “an officer of the court.” Importantly, in doing so, he violated the Rule regarding implied disinterest. D is incorrect. As stated above, the primary issue was that the attorney implied he was disinterested. However, if he had given legal advice, it would also be a violation of Model Rule 4.3. Under Model Rule 4.3, “[t]he lawyer shall not give legal advice to an unrepresented person, other than the advice to secure counsel, if the lawyer knows or reasonably should know that the interests of such a person are or have a reasonable possibility of being in conflict with the interests of the client.” Here, the woman’s interests are likely in conflict with the respondent’s interests, since the matter of paternity and support for the child is in question. Even so, in communications questions, an answer choice saying an attorney is not subject to discipline because he or she did not give legal advice is usually incorrect.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. “In dealing on behalf of a client with a person who is not represented by counsel, a lawyer shall not state or imply that the lawyer is disinterested.” Model Rule 4.3. Because the attorney simply identified himself as “an officer of the court,” he improperly implied he was disinterested in the outcome, when in fact he was working on behalf of the respondent. A is incorrect. Here, even if the agency had known or given its consent to the attorney’s actions, the primary issue is that the attorney contacted the woman and implied that he was disinterested when in fact he was representing the respondent. C is incorrect. It’s debatable whether all of the attorney’s statements were true, considering the fact that he implied he was only “an officer of the court.” Importantly, in doing so, he violated the Rule regarding implied disinterest. D is incorrect. As stated above, the primary issue was that the attorney implied he was disinterested. However, if he had given legal advice, it would also be a violation of Model Rule 4.3. Under Model Rule 4.3, “[t]he lawyer shall not give legal advice to an unrepresented person, other than the advice to secure counsel, if the lawyer knows or reasonably should know that the interests of such a person are or have a reasonable possibility of being in conflict with the interests of the client.” Here, the woman’s interests are likely in conflict with the respondent’s interests, since the matter of paternity and support for the child is in question. Even so, in communications questions, an answer choice saying an attorney is not subject to discipline because he or she did not give legal advice is usually incorrect.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. “In dealing on behalf of a client with a person who is not represented by counsel, a lawyer shall not state or imply that the lawyer is disinterested.” Model Rule 4.3. Because the attorney simply identified himself as “an officer of the court,” he improperly implied he was disinterested in the outcome, when in fact he was working on behalf of the respondent. A is incorrect. Here, even if the agency had known or given its consent to the attorney’s actions, the primary issue is that the attorney contacted the woman and implied that he was disinterested when in fact he was representing the respondent. C is incorrect. It’s debatable whether all of the attorney’s statements were true, considering the fact that he implied he was only “an officer of the court.” Importantly, in doing so, he violated the Rule regarding implied disinterest. D is incorrect. As stated above, the primary issue was that the attorney implied he was disinterested. However, if he had given legal advice, it would also be a violation of Model Rule 4.3. Under Model Rule 4.3, “[t]he lawyer shall not give legal advice to an unrepresented person, other than the advice to secure counsel, if the lawyer knows or reasonably should know that the interests of such a person are or have a reasonable possibility of being in conflict with the interests of the client.” Here, the woman’s interests are likely in conflict with the respondent’s interests, since the matter of paternity and support for the child is in question. Even so, in communications questions, an answer choice saying an attorney is not subject to discipline because he or she did not give legal advice is usually incorrect.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 4.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[143,144],"sourceAnswerPages":[248],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理一名被申请人，参加由儿童保护服务机构发起的程序；该程序旨在确定一名儿童的父亲，并追讨拖欠的儿童抚养费。儿童的母亲拒绝提出投诉、拒绝聘请律师，事实上还请求该机构不要采取任何行动。不过，州法律允许该机构在这种情况下以自己名义启动亲子关系及抚养费程序。律师在该机构及其律师不知情、也未同意的情况下联系母亲。律师向母亲自称是“法院工作人员”，并告诉她自己正在调查该事项。根据母亲所说内容，律师起草了一份宣誓书，由母亲签署；宣誓书如实陈述被申请人不是儿童的父亲。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师在该机构及其律师不知情、也未同意的情况下采取行动。","B":"是，因为律师暗示自己在该事项中不偏不倚。","C":"否，因为律师向母亲作出的全部陈述均属真实。","D":"否，因为律师没有向母亲提供法律意见。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。“律师代表委托人与没有律师代理的人交涉时，不得声称或暗示律师不偏不倚。”《职业行为示范规则》4.3。该律师仅将自己称为“法院工作人员”，不当地暗示自己对结果没有倾向，而事实上他正在代表被申请人工作。A 不正确。本题中，即使该机构知道或同意律师的行为，首要问题仍是律师联系母亲时暗示自己不偏不倚，而事实上他正在代理被申请人。C 不正确。考虑到律师暗示自己只是“法院工作人员”，其全部陈述是否真实本就值得怀疑。更重要的是，他由此违反了禁止暗示不偏不倚的规则。D 不正确。如上所述，首要问题是律师暗示自己不偏不倚。不过，如果他提供了法律意见，也会违反规则 4.3。该规则规定：“律师明知或理应知道没有律师代理之人的利益与委托人利益相冲突或有合理可能发生冲突时，不得向其提供法律意见，但建议其聘请律师除外。”本题中，由于儿童的亲子关系与抚养问题存在争议，母亲的利益很可能与被申请人的利益相冲突。即便如此，在沟通类问题中，声称律师仅仅因为没有提供法律意见就不应受纪律处分的选项，通常仍是错误的。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-024","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":24,"topic":"Model Rule 1.11(a)(2) / Model Rule 1.11","question":"An attorney in his capacity as part-time assistant county attorney represented the county in a criminal nonsupport proceeding against a defendant. This proceeding concluded with an order directing the defendant to pay or be jailed. The defendant refused to pay. The attorney, pursuant to applicable rules, is permitted to maintain a private law practice. The defendant’s ex-wife has discovered some assets of the defendant. The attorney now has accepted employment from the defendant’s ex-wife to maintain a civil action against the defendant to recover out of those assets arrearages due to the ex-wife under the ex-wife’s support decree. The attorney did not obtain consent from the county attorney or from the defendant to represent the ex-wife in the civil action. Is the attorney subject to discipline for accepting employment in the ex-wife’s civil action against the defendant?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney did not obtain the defendant’s consent to the representation.","B":"Yes, because the attorney had personal and substantial responsibility in the first proceeding.","C":"No, because the attorney’s responsibility in his public employment has terminated.","D":"No, because the attorney is representing the ex-wife’s interest in both the criminal and the civil proceedings."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. An attorney who has formerly served as a public officer or government employee may not represent a client in connection “with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee, unless the appropriate government agency gives its informed consent, confirmed in writing, to the representation.” Model Rule 1.11(a)(2). Importantly, this Rule is “designed not only to protect the former client, but also to prevent a lawyer from exploiting public office for the advantage of another client.” Model Rule 1.11, Comment [3]. Here, since the attorney represented the county in the nonsupport proceeding against the defendant, he cannot now represent the wife in her action for support. A is incorrect. There is an exception under Model Rule 1.11 when the attorney obtains the informed consent of the government agency. However, the defendant’s consent would not change the analysis. C is incorrect. Model Rule 1.11 applies to attorneys who serve or have “formerly served” as a public officer or government employee. Consequently, whether or not the attorney’s responsibility in his public employment has terminated is irrelevant. D is incorrect. This answer choice shows the important of careful reading. The question specifically states that the attorney “represented the county” in the criminal proceeding. While the ex-wife may have benefited, he was not representing her interest.","rule":"B is the correct answer. An attorney who has formerly served as a public officer or government employee may not represent a client in connection “with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee, unless the appropriate government agency gives its informed consent, confirmed in writing, to the representation.” Model Rule 1.11(a)(2). Importantly, this Rule is “designed not only to protect the former client, but also to prevent a lawyer from exploiting public office for the advantage of another client.” Model Rule 1.11, Comment [3]. Here, since the attorney represented the county in the nonsupport proceeding against the defendant, he cannot now represent the wife in her action for support. A is incorrect. There is an exception under Model Rule 1.11 when the attorney obtains the informed consent of the government agency. However, the defendant’s consent would not change the analysis. C is incorrect. Model Rule 1.11 applies to attorneys who serve or have “formerly served” as a public officer or government employee. Consequently, whether or not the attorney’s responsibility in his public employment has terminated is irrelevant. D is incorrect. This answer choice shows the important of careful reading. The question specifically states that the attorney “represented the county” in the criminal proceeding. While the ex-wife may have benefited, he was not representing her interest.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for accepting employment in the ex-wife’s civil action against the defendant?","rule":"B is the correct answer. An attorney who has formerly served as a public officer or government employee may not represent a client in connection “with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee, unless the appropriate government agency gives its informed consent, confirmed in writing, to the representation.” Model Rule 1.11(a)(2). Importantly, this Rule is “designed not only to protect the former client, but also to prevent a lawyer from exploiting public office for the advantage of another client.” Model Rule 1.11, Comment [3]. Here, since the attorney represented the county in the nonsupport proceeding against the defendant, he cannot now represent the wife in her action for support. A is incorrect. There is an exception under Model Rule 1.11 when the attorney obtains the informed consent of the government agency. However, the defendant’s consent would not change the analysis. C is incorrect. Model Rule 1.11 applies to attorneys who serve or have “formerly served” as a public officer or government employee. Consequently, whether or not the attorney’s responsibility in his public employment has terminated is irrelevant. D is incorrect. This answer choice shows the important of careful reading. The question specifically states that the attorney “represented the county” in the criminal proceeding. While the ex-wife may have benefited, he was not representing her interest.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. An attorney who has formerly served as a public officer or government employee may not represent a client in connection “with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee, unless the appropriate government agency gives its informed consent, confirmed in writing, to the representation.” Model Rule 1.11(a)(2). Importantly, this Rule is “designed not only to protect the former client, but also to prevent a lawyer from exploiting public office for the advantage of another client.” Model Rule 1.11, Comment [3]. Here, since the attorney represented the county in the nonsupport proceeding against the defendant, he cannot now represent the wife in her action for support. A is incorrect. There is an exception under Model Rule 1.11 when the attorney obtains the informed consent of the government agency. However, the defendant’s consent would not change the analysis. C is incorrect. Model Rule 1.11 applies to attorneys who serve or have “formerly served” as a public officer or government employee. Consequently, whether or not the attorney’s responsibility in his public employment has terminated is irrelevant. D is incorrect. This answer choice shows the important of careful reading. The question specifically states that the attorney “represented the county” in the criminal proceeding. While the ex-wife may have benefited, he was not representing her interest.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.11(a)(2)","Model Rule 1.11"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[144,145],"sourceAnswerPages":[248,249],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师以兼职县助理律师身份，在一宗针对被告的刑事不履行扶养义务程序中代理县政府。该程序最终作出命令，要求被告付款，否则将被监禁。被告拒绝付款。根据适用规则，该律师可以同时从事私人执业。被告的前妻发现了被告的一些资产。律师现在接受前妻委托，准备对被告提起民事诉讼，从这些资产中追讨根据前妻的扶养判决所欠款项。律师没有取得县律师或被告同意，便在民事诉讼中代理前妻。该律师因接受前妻委托在民事诉讼中起诉被告，是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师没有取得被告对该代理的同意。","B":"是，因为律师在第一项程序中曾负有亲自且实质性的职责。","C":"否，因为律师在公职中的职责已经终止。","D":"否，因为律师在刑事与民事程序中都是在代表前妻的利益。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。先前曾担任公职人员或政府雇员的律师，不得代理委托人处理“律师作为公职人员或雇员时曾亲自且实质性参与的事项，除非有关政府机构对该代理作出经书面确认的知情同意”。《职业行为示范规则》1.11(a)(2)。需要特别注意的是，该规则“不仅旨在保护前委托人，也旨在防止律师利用公职为另一名委托人谋利”。规则 1.11 评注 [3]。本题中，由于该律师曾代理县政府参加针对被告的不履行扶养义务程序，现在不能在追讨扶养费的诉讼中代理前妻。A 不正确。规则 1.11 允许律师在取得政府机构知情同意时适用例外，但被告同意与否不会改变分析。C 不正确。规则 1.11 适用于正在或“先前曾”担任公职人员或政府雇员的律师。因此，律师的公职职责是否已经终止并不重要。D 不正确。该选项说明了仔细阅读的重要性。题目明确说，律师在刑事程序中“代理县政府”。前妻虽然可能从中受益，但律师当时并非代理她的利益。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-025","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":25,"topic":"Model Rule 4.2","question":"An attorney is a lawyer for the city and advises the city on all tort claims filed against it. The attorney’s advice is limited to recommending settlement and the amount thereof. If a claim is not settled and suit is filed, defense of the suit is handled either by lawyers for the city’s insurance carrier or by outside counsel specially retained for that purpose. In connection with any notice of claim and before suit is filed, the attorney arranges for an investigator to call upon the claimant at the claimant’s home and, with no one else present, to interview the claimant and endeavor to obtain a signed statement of the claimant’s version of the facts. A claimant has filed a notice of claim against the city. The attorney knows the claimant is represented by counsel and has sent an investigator to interview the claimant. The attorney is planning to use the claimant’s statements against him. Is the attorney subject to discipline for arranging an interview with the claimant?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the claimant was known by the attorney to be represented by counsel.","B":"Yes, because the statement taken will be used to the claimant’s disadvantage.","C":"No, because the claimant had not filed suit at the time of the interview.","D":"No, because the attorney would not be representing the city in any subsequent litigation on the claimant’s claim."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 4.2, a lawyer cannot “communicate about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows to be represented by another lawyer in the matter.” Since the attorney knew the claimant was represented by counsel, he cannot communicate with him through the use of the investigator. B is incorrect. Model Rule 4.2 applies even to communications initiated by the represented person. Importantly, the Rule focuses on the communication itself, not on its content. C is incorrect. Model Rule 4.2 applies to persons represented by counsel. Once the attorney knows the person is being represented by counsel, he or she cannot communicate with the person “unless the lawyer has the consent of the other lawyer or is authorized to do so by law or a court order.” Importantly, whether or not a suit has been filed is irrelevant. D is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney knows the claimant is represented by counsel. It does not matter that the attorney may not be representing the city in any subsequent litigation on the claimant’s claim.","rule":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 4.2, a lawyer cannot “communicate about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows to be represented by another lawyer in the matter.” Since the attorney knew the claimant was represented by counsel, he cannot communicate with him through the use of the investigator. B is incorrect. Model Rule 4.2 applies even to communications initiated by the represented person. Importantly, the Rule focuses on the communication itself, not on its content. C is incorrect. Model Rule 4.2 applies to persons represented by counsel. Once the attorney knows the person is being represented by counsel, he or she cannot communicate with the person “unless the lawyer has the consent of the other lawyer or is authorized to do so by law or a court order.” Importantly, whether or not a suit has been filed is irrelevant. D is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney knows the claimant is represented by counsel. It does not matter that the attorney may not be representing the city in any subsequent litigation on the claimant’s claim.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for arranging an interview with the claimant?","rule":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 4.2, a lawyer cannot “communicate about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows to be represented by another lawyer in the matter.” Since the attorney knew the claimant was represented by counsel, he cannot communicate with him through the use of the investigator. B is incorrect. Model Rule 4.2 applies even to communications initiated by the represented person. Importantly, the Rule focuses on the communication itself, not on its content. C is incorrect. Model Rule 4.2 applies to persons represented by counsel. Once the attorney knows the person is being represented by counsel, he or she cannot communicate with the person “unless the lawyer has the consent of the other lawyer or is authorized to do so by law or a court order.” Importantly, whether or not a suit has been filed is irrelevant. D is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney knows the claimant is represented by counsel. It does not matter that the attorney may not be representing the city in any subsequent litigation on the claimant’s claim.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 4.2, a lawyer cannot “communicate about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows to be represented by another lawyer in the matter.” Since the attorney knew the claimant was represented by counsel, he cannot communicate with him through the use of the investigator. B is incorrect. Model Rule 4.2 applies even to communications initiated by the represented person. Importantly, the Rule focuses on the communication itself, not on its content. C is incorrect. Model Rule 4.2 applies to persons represented by counsel. Once the attorney knows the person is being represented by counsel, he or she cannot communicate with the person “unless the lawyer has the consent of the other lawyer or is authorized to do so by law or a court order.” Importantly, whether or not a suit has been filed is irrelevant. D is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney knows the claimant is represented by counsel. It does not matter that the attorney may not be representing the city in any subsequent litigation on the claimant’s claim.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 4.2"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[145],"sourceAnswerPages":[249],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师是某市政府律师，就所有针对该市提出的侵权索赔向市政府提供意见。其意见仅限于建议是否和解以及和解金额。如果索赔没有和解并提起诉讼，则由该市保险公司的律师或为此特别聘请的外部律师负责抗辩。在收到任何索赔通知后、诉讼提起前，该律师都会安排调查员到索赔人家中，在没有其他人在场时询问索赔人，并争取取得一份由索赔人签署的事实经过陈述。现在，一名索赔人已向市政府提交索赔通知。律师知道该索赔人已有律师代理，却仍派调查员去询问，并计划用索赔人的陈述对其不利。律师安排询问索赔人是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师知道索赔人已有律师代理。","B":"是，因为取得的陈述将用于损害索赔人利益。","C":"否，因为询问发生时索赔人尚未提起诉讼。","D":"否，因为此后如就索赔提起诉讼，该律师不会在该诉讼中代理市政府。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。根据《职业行为示范规则》4.2，律师不得“就代理事项与律师明知在该事项中由另一名律师代理的人沟通”。由于该律师知道索赔人已有律师代理，就不能利用调查员与其沟通。B 不正确。规则 4.2 甚至适用于由已有律师代理的人主动发起的沟通。关键是，该规则关注沟通本身，而不是沟通内容。C 不正确。规则 4.2 适用于已有律师代理的人。律师一旦知道某人由律师代理，就不能与其沟通，“除非取得另一名律师同意，或者法律或法院命令授权这样做”。关键是，是否已经提起诉讼并不重要。D 不正确。本题问题是律师明知索赔人已有律师代理。该律师以后是否会在索赔诉讼中代理市政府，并不重要。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-026","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":26,"topic":"Model Rule 5.4(d)(2) / Model Rule 5.4","question":"An attorney joined a professional corporation engaged solely in the practice of law. As a salaried associate, she is not a member or shareholder of the professional corporation. The spouse of one of the partners, who is not a lawyer, is vice-president of the corporation and office manager. She does not participate in any decision regarding a client or a client’s case. All of the other officers are lawyers in the firm. All of the corporate shares are held by lawyers in the corporation, except for 10 shares held by an executor under the will of a lawyer-member who died one month previously and whose will is now being probated. The attorney knows that the partner’s wife is an officer and not a lawyer. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the partner’s wife is an officer of the corporation.","B":"Yes, because a nonlawyer holds the stock as the executor of the will of the deceased member.","C":"No, because the attorney is a salaried employee and not a member or shareholder of the corporation.","D":"No, because the partner’s wife does not participate in any decision regarding a client or a client’s case."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not practice with or in the form of a professional corporation or association authorized to practice law for a profit, if: . . . a nonlawyer is a corporate director or officer thereof or occupies the position of similar responsibility.” Model Rule 5.4(d)(2). Traditionally, the Rules place limitations on allowing a third party to direct or regulate the lawyer’s professional judgment. Model Rule 5.4, Comment [2]. Here, since the partner’s wife is vice-president of the corporation, her position in the corporation violates the Rule. B is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 expressly allows payments to a deceased lawyer’s estate. Since the executor is holding the stock while the deceased lawyer’s estate is being probated, it does not violate the Rule. C is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 prohibits lawyers from practicing with a corporation that is improperly sharing legal fees or decision-making responsibility with a nonlawyer. The Rule does not limit that prohibition to lawyers who only practice as members or shareholders of the corporation. D is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 prohibits nonlawyers from being directors or officers of the corporation. It does not make any distinctions regarding the specific responsibilities of that director or officer’s job.","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not practice with or in the form of a professional corporation or association authorized to practice law for a profit, if: . . . a nonlawyer is a corporate director or officer thereof or occupies the position of similar responsibility.” Model Rule 5.4(d)(2). Traditionally, the Rules place limitations on allowing a third party to direct or regulate the lawyer’s professional judgment. Model Rule 5.4, Comment [2]. Here, since the partner’s wife is vice-president of the corporation, her position in the corporation violates the Rule. B is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 expressly allows payments to a deceased lawyer’s estate. Since the executor is holding the stock while the deceased lawyer’s estate is being probated, it does not violate the Rule. C is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 prohibits lawyers from practicing with a corporation that is improperly sharing legal fees or decision-making responsibility with a nonlawyer. The Rule does not limit that prohibition to lawyers who only practice as members or shareholders of the corporation. D is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 prohibits nonlawyers from being directors or officers of the corporation. It does not make any distinctions regarding the specific responsibilities of that director or officer’s job.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not practice with or in the form of a professional corporation or association authorized to practice law for a profit, if: . . . a nonlawyer is a corporate director or officer thereof or occupies the position of similar responsibility.” Model Rule 5.4(d)(2). Traditionally, the Rules place limitations on allowing a third party to direct or regulate the lawyer’s professional judgment. Model Rule 5.4, Comment [2]. Here, since the partner’s wife is vice-president of the corporation, her position in the corporation violates the Rule. B is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 expressly allows payments to a deceased lawyer’s estate. Since the executor is holding the stock while the deceased lawyer’s estate is being probated, it does not violate the Rule. C is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 prohibits lawyers from practicing with a corporation that is improperly sharing legal fees or decision-making responsibility with a nonlawyer. The Rule does not limit that prohibition to lawyers who only practice as members or shareholders of the corporation. D is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 prohibits nonlawyers from being directors or officers of the corporation. It does not make any distinctions regarding the specific responsibilities of that director or officer’s job.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not practice with or in the form of a professional corporation or association authorized to practice law for a profit, if: . . . a nonlawyer is a corporate director or officer thereof or occupies the position of similar responsibility.” Model Rule 5.4(d)(2). Traditionally, the Rules place limitations on allowing a third party to direct or regulate the lawyer’s professional judgment. Model Rule 5.4, Comment [2]. Here, since the partner’s wife is vice-president of the corporation, her position in the corporation violates the Rule. B is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 expressly allows payments to a deceased lawyer’s estate. Since the executor is holding the stock while the deceased lawyer’s estate is being probated, it does not violate the Rule. C is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 prohibits lawyers from practicing with a corporation that is improperly sharing legal fees or decision-making responsibility with a nonlawyer. The Rule does not limit that prohibition to lawyers who only practice as members or shareholders of the corporation. D is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 prohibits nonlawyers from being directors or officers of the corporation. It does not make any distinctions regarding the specific responsibilities of that director or officer’s job.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.4(d)(2)","Model Rule 5.4"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[145,146],"sourceAnswerPages":[250],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师加入一家专门从事法律业务的专业公司。她是领取固定薪资的助理律师，并非该专业公司的成员或股东。一名合伙人的妻子不是律师，却担任公司副总裁兼办公室经理。她不参与任何有关委托人或委托人案件的决定。其他所有公司管理人员都是事务所律师。公司的全部股份均由公司内律师持有，但有 10 股由一名遗嘱执行人持有；该股份原属一名于一个月前去世的律师成员，其遗嘱目前正在检验。该律师知道合伙人的妻子是公司管理人员且不是律师。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为合伙人的妻子是公司管理人员。","B":"是，因为一名非律师以已故成员遗嘱执行人身份持有股份。","C":"否，因为该律师是领取固定薪资的雇员，而不是公司的成员或股东。","D":"否，因为合伙人的妻子不参与任何有关委托人或其案件的决定。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。“在下列情形下，律师不得在获准以营利为目的从事法律业务的专业公司或协会中执业，或以该形式执业：……非律师担任该公司的董事或管理人员，或者担任负有类似职责的职位。”《职业行为示范规则》5.4(d)(2)。传统上，规则对允许第三方指导或约束律师专业判断施加限制。规则 5.4 评注 [2]。本题中，合伙人的妻子担任公司副总裁，其职位违反了规则。B 不正确。规则 5.4 明确允许向已故律师的遗产付款。遗嘱执行人在检验已故律师遗产期间持有股份，并不违反规则。C 不正确。规则 5.4 禁止律师在不当地与非律师分享法律服务费或决策责任的公司中执业。该禁令并不只适用于以公司成员或股东身份执业的律师。D 不正确。规则 5.4 禁止非律师担任公司董事或管理人员，并未根据该董事或管理人员职位的具体职责作出区分。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-027","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":27,"topic":"Model Rule 1.8(d) / Model Rule 1.8","question":"An attorney is representing a plaintiff in a paternity suit against a defendant. Both the plaintiff and the defendant are well-known public figures, and the suit has attracted much publicity. The attorney has been billing the plaintiff at an agreed hourly fee for his services. Recently, the plaintiff told the attorney, “I’m going broke paying you. Why don’t you let me assign you all media rights to books, movies, or television programs based on my suit as full payment for all services you will render me between now and the conclusion of the suit?” The attorney replied, “I’ll consider it, but first you should seek independent advice about whether such an arrangement is in your own best interests. Why don’t you do so and call me next week?” The plaintiff received independent advice and entered into the agreement soon thereafter. Is the attorney subject to discipline if he agrees to the plaintiff’s offer?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the amount received by the attorney would be contingent on the receipts from the sale of media rights.","B":"Yes, because the attorney has not concluded the representation of the plaintiff.","C":"No, because the paternity suit is a civil and not a criminal matter.","D":"No, because the plaintiff received independent advice before entering into the agreement."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. “Prior to the conclusion of representation of a client, a lawyer shall not make or negotiate an agreement giving the lawyer literary or media rights to a portrayal or account based in substantial part on information relating to the representation.” Model Rule 1.8(d). The Rule notes that such agreements can create a conflict between the lawyer and client, because “measures suitable in the representation of the client may detract from the publication value.” Model Rule 1.8, Comment [9]. Specifically, the lawyer may be tempted to act in ways that make the story better, even if those actions are to the client’s detriment. A is incorrect. The problem here is not that the attorney is getting a fee contingent on the value of the media rights, the problem is that this agreement may encourage the attorney to conduct his representation in a way that is detrimental to the client. C is incorrect. This answer choice is a distractor, and it is trying to trick a student who only remembers the Rule that contingency fees are improper in criminal cases. If that’s the only relevant Rule the student can remember, he or she might be tempted to pick this choice. D is incorrect. This is another over-broad statement that should be avoided. The fact a client received independent advice on a matter does not insulate an attorney who may have violated the Rules.","rule":"B is the correct answer. “Prior to the conclusion of representation of a client, a lawyer shall not make or negotiate an agreement giving the lawyer literary or media rights to a portrayal or account based in substantial part on information relating to the representation.” Model Rule 1.8(d). The Rule notes that such agreements can create a conflict between the lawyer and client, because “measures suitable in the representation of the client may detract from the publication value.” Model Rule 1.8, Comment [9]. Specifically, the lawyer may be tempted to act in ways that make the story better, even if those actions are to the client’s detriment. A is incorrect. The problem here is not that the attorney is getting a fee contingent on the value of the media rights, the problem is that this agreement may encourage the attorney to conduct his representation in a way that is detrimental to the client. C is incorrect. This answer choice is a distractor, and it is trying to trick a student who only remembers the Rule that contingency fees are improper in criminal cases. If that’s the only relevant Rule the student can remember, he or she might be tempted to pick this choice. D is incorrect. This is another over-broad statement that should be avoided. The fact a client received independent advice on a matter does not insulate an attorney who may have violated the Rules.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline if he agrees to the plaintiff’s offer?","rule":"B is the correct answer. “Prior to the conclusion of representation of a client, a lawyer shall not make or negotiate an agreement giving the lawyer literary or media rights to a portrayal or account based in substantial part on information relating to the representation.” Model Rule 1.8(d). The Rule notes that such agreements can create a conflict between the lawyer and client, because “measures suitable in the representation of the client may detract from the publication value.” Model Rule 1.8, Comment [9]. Specifically, the lawyer may be tempted to act in ways that make the story better, even if those actions are to the client’s detriment. A is incorrect. The problem here is not that the attorney is getting a fee contingent on the value of the media rights, the problem is that this agreement may encourage the attorney to conduct his representation in a way that is detrimental to the client. C is incorrect. This answer choice is a distractor, and it is trying to trick a student who only remembers the Rule that contingency fees are improper in criminal cases. If that’s the only relevant Rule the student can remember, he or she might be tempted to pick this choice. D is incorrect. This is another over-broad statement that should be avoided. The fact a client received independent advice on a matter does not insulate an attorney who may have violated the Rules.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. “Prior to the conclusion of representation of a client, a lawyer shall not make or negotiate an agreement giving the lawyer literary or media rights to a portrayal or account based in substantial part on information relating to the representation.” Model Rule 1.8(d). The Rule notes that such agreements can create a conflict between the lawyer and client, because “measures suitable in the representation of the client may detract from the publication value.” Model Rule 1.8, Comment [9]. Specifically, the lawyer may be tempted to act in ways that make the story better, even if those actions are to the client’s detriment. A is incorrect. The problem here is not that the attorney is getting a fee contingent on the value of the media rights, the problem is that this agreement may encourage the attorney to conduct his representation in a way that is detrimental to the client. C is incorrect. This answer choice is a distractor, and it is trying to trick a student who only remembers the Rule that contingency fees are improper in criminal cases. If that’s the only relevant Rule the student can remember, he or she might be tempted to pick this choice. D is incorrect. This is another over-broad statement that should be avoided. The fact a client received independent advice on a matter does not insulate an attorney who may have violated the Rules.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.8(d)","Model Rule 1.8"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[146,147],"sourceAnswerPages":[250,251],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理原告对被告提起亲子关系诉讼。原、被告均为知名公众人物，案件受到广泛关注。律师一直按照约定的小时费率向原告收费。最近，原告告诉律师：“为了付你的费用，我快破产了。不如让我把所有基于这宗诉讼制作的图书、电影或电视节目之媒体权利转让给你，作为你从现在到诉讼结束向我提供全部服务的全额报酬，怎么样？”律师回答：“我会考虑，但你应当先取得独立意见，判断这种安排是否符合你自己的最佳利益。你先这样做，下周再打电话给我吧。”原告取得独立意见后不久便订立该协议。如果律师同意原告的提议，是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师取得的金额将取决于出售媒体权利的收益。","B":"是，因为律师尚未结束对原告的代理。","C":"否，因为亲子关系诉讼属于民事而非刑事事项。","D":"否，因为原告在订立协议前取得了独立意见。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。“在结束对委托人的代理之前，律师不得订立或谈判一项协议，使律师取得文学或媒体权利，而该权利所涉及的描述或叙述主要基于与代理有关的信息。”《职业行为示范规则》1.8(d)。规则指出，这类协议可能在律师与委托人之间造成冲突，因为“适合于代理委托人的措施可能降低作品的出版价值”。规则 1.8 评注 [9]。具体而言，律师可能受到诱惑，采取使故事更精彩、却损害委托人利益的行为。A 不正确。问题不在于律师取得的费用取决于媒体权利的价值，而在于该协议可能鼓励律师以损害委托人的方式进行代理。C 不正确。这是一个干扰选项，试图误导那些只记得刑事案件不得采用风险代理费这一规则的考生。如果考生只能想起这一规则，就可能受到诱惑选择本项。D 不正确。这又是一项应避免的过度宽泛陈述。委托人已经就某事项取得独立意见，并不能使可能违反规则的律师免于责任。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-028","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":28,"topic":"Model Rule 1.7(b) / Model Rule 1.7","question":"Four years ago, an attorney represented a husband and wife, both high school teachers, in the purchase of a new home. Since then, the attorney prepared their tax returns and drafted their wills. Recently, the husband called the attorney and told her that he and his wife had decided to divorce, but wanted the matter to be resolved amicably. The husband stated that they were planning to file and process their own divorce case, utilizing the state’s new streamlined divorce procedure, applicable in “no-fault” cases where there are no minor children. The husband asked if the attorney would agree to work with them to prepare a financial settlement agreement that could be presented to the divorce court, reminding the attorney that the couple’s assets were modest and that they wanted to “split it all down the middle.” After considering the risks of a conflict of interest arising in this limited representation, the attorney wrote to the couple separately, and advised each that he or she might be better off with separate lawyers, but that the attorney would assist with the financial settlement agreement, charging an hourly fee of $140 per hour, provided that they were in complete agreement and remained so. The attorney advised that if a conflict developed, or if either party was dissatisfied or uncomfortable about continuing with the joint representation, the attorney would withdraw and would not represent either party from that point forward, forcing them to start all over again with separate lawyers. Finally, the attorney cautioned the husband and wife that the attorney would be representing both of them equally, would not and could not favor one or the other, and that their separate communications to her could not be kept confidential from the other party. Both the husband and the wife signed their individual copy of the letter, consenting to the joint representation, and returned them to the attorney. Was it proper for the attorney to accept the representation on these terms?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because there was little risk that the interests of either the husband or the wife would be materially prejudiced if no settlement was reached.","B":"Yes, because the attorney had previously represented the husband and the wife in their joint affairs.","C":"No, because the attorney conditioned representation upon receiving a waiver of client confidentially.","D":"No, because the attorney did not advise both the husband and the wife, in writing, that they should seek independent counsel before agreeing to enter into the financial settlement on the terms proposed."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. Generally, a lawyer may not represent a client if it creates a concurrent conflict of interest with another client. However, “a lawyer may represent a client if: (1) the lawyer reasonably believes that the lawyer will be able to provide competent and diligent representation to each affected client; (2) the representation is not prohibited by law; (3) the representation does not involve the assertion of a claim by one client against another client represented by the lawyer in the same litigation or other proceeding before a tribunal; and (4) each affected client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing.” Model Rule 1.7(b). Here, there would be little risk that either the husband’s or wife’s interests would be hurt by the representation. Importantly, if no settlement is ultimately reached, the husband and wife would be back where they started, since the attorney has stated she will not represent either party if litigation continues. B is incorrect. While the attorney may have previously represented the husband and wife in their joint affairs, this would not automatically make a joint representation appropriate. Importantly, if the husband and wife were asserting differing claims and were not trying to settle their affairs amicably, the representation would be improper because of conflicts. C is incorrect. The Rules specifically state that “with regard to the attorney-client privilege, the prevailing rule is that, as between commonly represented clients, the privilege does not attach. Hence, it must be assumed that if litigation eventuates between the clients, the privilege will not protect any such communications, and the clients should be so advised.” Model Rule 1.7, Comment [30]. Consequently, “the lawyer should, at the outset of the common representation and as part of the process of obtaining each client’s informed consent, advise each client that information will be shared and that the lawyer will have to withdraw if one client decides that some matter material to the representation should be kept from the other.” Model Rule 1.7, Comment [31]. Ultimately, the lawyer was correct to condition representation on a waiver of confidentiality. D is incorrect. The Rules do not require an attorney to recommend independent counsel before clients agree to joint representation.","rule":"A is the correct answer. Generally, a lawyer may not represent a client if it creates a concurrent conflict of interest with another client. However, “a lawyer may represent a client if: (1) the lawyer reasonably believes that the lawyer will be able to provide competent and diligent representation to each affected client; (2) the representation is not prohibited by law; (3) the representation does not involve the assertion of a claim by one client against another client represented by the lawyer in the same litigation or other proceeding before a tribunal; and (4) each affected client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing.” Model Rule 1.7(b). Here, there would be little risk that either the husband’s or wife’s interests would be hurt by the representation. Importantly, if no settlement is ultimately reached, the husband and wife would be back where they started, since the attorney has stated she will not represent either party if litigation continues. B is incorrect. While the attorney may have previously represented the husband and wife in their joint affairs, this would not automatically make a joint representation appropriate. Importantly, if the husband and wife were asserting differing claims and were not trying to settle their affairs amicably, the representation would be improper because of conflicts. C is incorrect. The Rules specifically state that “with regard to the attorney-client privilege, the prevailing rule is that, as between commonly represented clients, the privilege does not attach. Hence, it must be assumed that if litigation eventuates between the clients, the privilege will not protect any such communications, and the clients should be so advised.” Model Rule 1.7, Comment [30]. Consequently, “the lawyer should, at the outset of the common representation and as part of the process of obtaining each client’s informed consent, advise each client that information will be shared and that the lawyer will have to withdraw if one client decides that some matter material to the representation should be kept from the other.” Model Rule 1.7, Comment [31]. Ultimately, the lawyer was correct to condition representation on a waiver of confidentiality. D is incorrect. The Rules do not require an attorney to recommend independent counsel before clients agree to joint representation.","irac":{"issue":"Was it proper for the attorney to accept the representation on these terms?","rule":"A is the correct answer. Generally, a lawyer may not represent a client if it creates a concurrent conflict of interest with another client. However, “a lawyer may represent a client if: (1) the lawyer reasonably believes that the lawyer will be able to provide competent and diligent representation to each affected client; (2) the representation is not prohibited by law; (3) the representation does not involve the assertion of a claim by one client against another client represented by the lawyer in the same litigation or other proceeding before a tribunal; and (4) each affected client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing.” Model Rule 1.7(b). Here, there would be little risk that either the husband’s or wife’s interests would be hurt by the representation. Importantly, if no settlement is ultimately reached, the husband and wife would be back where they started, since the attorney has stated she will not represent either party if litigation continues. B is incorrect. While the attorney may have previously represented the husband and wife in their joint affairs, this would not automatically make a joint representation appropriate. Importantly, if the husband and wife were asserting differing claims and were not trying to settle their affairs amicably, the representation would be improper because of conflicts. C is incorrect. The Rules specifically state that “with regard to the attorney-client privilege, the prevailing rule is that, as between commonly represented clients, the privilege does not attach. Hence, it must be assumed that if litigation eventuates between the clients, the privilege will not protect any such communications, and the clients should be so advised.” Model Rule 1.7, Comment [30]. Consequently, “the lawyer should, at the outset of the common representation and as part of the process of obtaining each client’s informed consent, advise each client that information will be shared and that the lawyer will have to withdraw if one client decides that some matter material to the representation should be kept from the other.” Model Rule 1.7, Comment [31]. Ultimately, the lawyer was correct to condition representation on a waiver of confidentiality. D is incorrect. The Rules do not require an attorney to recommend independent counsel before clients agree to joint representation.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. Generally, a lawyer may not represent a client if it creates a concurrent conflict of interest with another client. However, “a lawyer may represent a client if: (1) the lawyer reasonably believes that the lawyer will be able to provide competent and diligent representation to each affected client; (2) the representation is not prohibited by law; (3) the representation does not involve the assertion of a claim by one client against another client represented by the lawyer in the same litigation or other proceeding before a tribunal; and (4) each affected client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing.” Model Rule 1.7(b). Here, there would be little risk that either the husband’s or wife’s interests would be hurt by the representation. Importantly, if no settlement is ultimately reached, the husband and wife would be back where they started, since the attorney has stated she will not represent either party if litigation continues. B is incorrect. While the attorney may have previously represented the husband and wife in their joint affairs, this would not automatically make a joint representation appropriate. Importantly, if the husband and wife were asserting differing claims and were not trying to settle their affairs amicably, the representation would be improper because of conflicts. C is incorrect. The Rules specifically state that “with regard to the attorney-client privilege, the prevailing rule is that, as between commonly represented clients, the privilege does not attach. Hence, it must be assumed that if litigation eventuates between the clients, the privilege will not protect any such communications, and the clients should be so advised.” Model Rule 1.7, Comment [30]. Consequently, “the lawyer should, at the outset of the common representation and as part of the process of obtaining each client’s informed consent, advise each client that information will be shared and that the lawyer will have to withdraw if one client decides that some matter material to the representation should be kept from the other.” Model Rule 1.7, Comment [31]. Ultimately, the lawyer was correct to condition representation on a waiver of confidentiality. D is incorrect. The Rules do not require an attorney to recommend independent counsel before clients agree to joint representation.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.7(b)","Model Rule 1.7"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[147,148],"sourceAnswerPages":[251,252],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"四年前，一名律师代理一对均为高中教师的夫妻购买新房。此后，该律师为他们编制报税表并起草遗嘱。最近，丈夫致电律师，称他和妻子决定离婚，但希望友好解决。丈夫说，他们计划自行提交并办理离婚案件，利用州新设的简化离婚程序；该程序适用于没有未成年子女的“无过错”案件。丈夫询问律师是否愿意协助二人拟定一份可提交离婚法院的财产和解协议，并提醒律师，夫妻资产不多，二人希望“全部对半分”。律师考虑这项有限代理可能产生的利益冲突风险后，分别致函夫妻二人，告知各自分别聘请律师可能对其更有利；但只要二人完全达成一致并始终保持一致，律师愿按每小时 140 美元收费，协助制作财产和解协议。律师告知，如果产生冲突，或者任何一方对继续共同代理感到不满或不安，她将退出且此后不再代理任何一方，使他们必须另聘律师从头开始。最后，律师告诫夫妻，自己会平等代理二人，不会也不能偏向任何一方；二人分别向她作出的沟通也不能对另一方保密。丈夫和妻子各自签署了信函副本，同意共同代理，并寄还律师。律师依这些条件接受代理是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为如果未能达成和解，丈夫或妻子的利益受到重大损害的风险都很小。","B":"适当，因为该律师先前曾在夫妻共同事务中代理二人。","C":"不适当，因为该律师以委托人放弃保密为接受代理的条件。","D":"不适当，因为该律师没有以书面形式建议丈夫和妻子双方，在同意按拟议条款订立财产和解前寻求独立律师意见。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。一般而言，如果代理会与另一名委托人产生同时发生的利益冲突，律师不得接受。不过，“在以下条件下，律师可以代理委托人：(1) 律师合理地相信自己能够向每一名受影响的委托人提供称职且勤勉的代理；(2) 代理不为法律禁止；(3) 代理不涉及一名委托人在同一诉讼或审裁机构前的其他程序中向律师代理的另一名委托人主张权利；以及 (4) 每一名受影响的委托人均作出经书面确认的知情同意。”《职业行为示范规则》1.7(b)。本题中，丈夫或妻子的利益因该代理而受到损害的风险都很小。尤其是，如果最终未能达成和解，夫妻二人只是回到起点，因为律师已经说明，诉讼一旦继续，她不会代理任何一方。B 不正确。律师先前虽然可能曾在夫妻共同事务中代理二人，但这不会自动使本次共同代理适当。关键是，如果夫妻二人主张不同权利，并非试图友好解决事务，代理就会因利益冲突而不适当。C 不正确。规则明确指出，“关于律师—委托人特权，通行规则是，共同代理的委托人彼此之间不适用该特权。因此必须假定，如果委托人之间发生诉讼，该特权不会保护任何此类沟通，律师应当将此告知委托人。”规则 1.7 评注 [30]。所以，“律师应在共同代理开始时，并作为取得每名委托人知情同意过程的一部分，告知每名委托人信息将会共享；如果一名委托人决定某一与代理有重大关系的事项应对另一人保密，律师将不得不退出。”规则 1.7 评注 [31]。因此，律师以放弃相互保密为接受共同代理的条件是正确的。D 不正确。规则并不要求律师在委托人同意共同代理前建议其另聘独立律师。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-029","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":29,"topic":"Model Rule 1.6(b) / Model Rule 1.6","question":"An attorney represented a client in negotiating a large real estate transaction. The buyer who purchased the real estate from the client has filed suit against both the client and the attorney, alleging fraud and violation of the state unfair trade practices statute. The attorney had advised the client by letter against making the statements that are the basis for the buyer’s claim. The attorney and the client are each represented by separate counsel. In responding to a deposition under subpoena, the attorney wishes to reveal, to the extent the attorney reasonably believes necessary to defend herself, confidential information imparted to the attorney by the client that will be favorable to the attorney but damaging to the client. The client did not object to the disclosure. At this point, no criminal charges have been brought against the attorney. Is it proper for the attorney to reveal such information?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the client did not object to the disclosure.","B":"Yes, because the attorney may reveal such information to defend herself against a civil claim.","C":"No, because criminal charges have not also been brought against the attorney.","D":"No, because the disclosure will be detrimental to the client."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer may reveal confidential information “to establish a defense to a criminal charge or civil claim against the lawyer based upon conduct in which the client was involved.” Model Rule 1.6(b) (5). Here, since the buyer is suing both the client and attorney over the same real estate transaction, the attorney may reveal the information. A is incorrect. Since the attorney is defending herself against the buyer’s civil action, she does not have to obtain the client’s permission before revealing the confidential information. C is incorrect. Model Rule 1.6 covers both civil and criminal actions, so the attorney does not have to wait for criminal charges to be filed before revealing the information. D is incorrect. Although the disclosure may be detrimental to the client, the Model Rules allow the attorney to defend herself against civil and criminal actions.","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer may reveal confidential information “to establish a defense to a criminal charge or civil claim against the lawyer based upon conduct in which the client was involved.” Model Rule 1.6(b) (5). Here, since the buyer is suing both the client and attorney over the same real estate transaction, the attorney may reveal the information. A is incorrect. Since the attorney is defending herself against the buyer’s civil action, she does not have to obtain the client’s permission before revealing the confidential information. C is incorrect. Model Rule 1.6 covers both civil and criminal actions, so the attorney does not have to wait for criminal charges to be filed before revealing the information. D is incorrect. Although the disclosure may be detrimental to the client, the Model Rules allow the attorney to defend herself against civil and criminal actions.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the attorney to reveal such information?","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer may reveal confidential information “to establish a defense to a criminal charge or civil claim against the lawyer based upon conduct in which the client was involved.” Model Rule 1.6(b) (5). Here, since the buyer is suing both the client and attorney over the same real estate transaction, the attorney may reveal the information. A is incorrect. Since the attorney is defending herself against the buyer’s civil action, she does not have to obtain the client’s permission before revealing the confidential information. C is incorrect. Model Rule 1.6 covers both civil and criminal actions, so the attorney does not have to wait for criminal charges to be filed before revealing the information. D is incorrect. Although the disclosure may be detrimental to the client, the Model Rules allow the attorney to defend herself against civil and criminal actions.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer may reveal confidential information “to establish a defense to a criminal charge or civil claim against the lawyer based upon conduct in which the client was involved.” Model Rule 1.6(b) (5). Here, since the buyer is suing both the client and attorney over the same real estate transaction, the attorney may reveal the information. A is incorrect. Since the attorney is defending herself against the buyer’s civil action, she does not have to obtain the client’s permission before revealing the confidential information. C is incorrect. Model Rule 1.6 covers both civil and criminal actions, so the attorney does not have to wait for criminal charges to be filed before revealing the information. D is incorrect. Although the disclosure may be detrimental to the client, the Model Rules allow the attorney to defend herself against civil and criminal actions.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.6(b)","Model Rule 1.6"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[148],"sourceAnswerPages":[252],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理委托人谈判一项大型房地产交易。向委托人购买房地产的买方现已同时起诉委托人和律师，指控二人欺诈并违反州不公平交易行为法。律师曾致函建议委托人不要作出构成买方索赔依据的那些陈述。律师与委托人现各自由不同律师代理。在依传票参加证言录取并作答时，该律师希望在其合理认为为自身辩护所必需的范围内，披露委托人曾向其提供的保密信息；这些信息对律师有利，却会损害委托人。委托人没有反对披露。此时尚未对该律师提出刑事指控。该律师披露该信息是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为委托人没有反对披露。","B":"适当，因为律师可以披露该信息，为针对自己的民事主张进行抗辩。","C":"不适当，因为尚未同时对律师提出刑事指控。","D":"不适当，因为披露会损害委托人。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。律师可以披露保密信息，“以针对基于委托人参与之行为而对律师提出的刑事指控或民事主张确立抗辩”。《职业行为示范规则》1.6(b)(5)。本题中，买方因同一房地产交易同时起诉委托人和律师，因此律师可以披露信息。A 不正确。由于律师正在针对买方的民事诉讼为自己辩护，她披露保密信息前无须取得委托人许可。C 不正确。规则 1.6 同时涵盖民事和刑事诉讼，因此律师无须等待刑事指控提出后再披露信息。D 不正确。披露虽然可能损害委托人，《职业行为示范规则》仍允许律师针对民事和刑事程序为自己辩护。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-030","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":30,"topic":"Model Rule 8.4(c)","question":"An attorney, who is corporate counsel for a company, is investigating a possible theft ring in the parts department of the company. The attorney knows that an employee has worked in the parts department a long time and believes that the employee is a suspect in the thefts. The attorney believes that if the employee were questioned, the employee would not answer truthfully if she knew the real purpose of the questions. The attorney plans to question the employee and falsely tell her that she is not a suspect and that her answers to the questions will be held in confidence. Is the attorney subject to discipline if she so questions the employee?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney’s conduct involves misrepresentation.","B":"Yes, because the attorney did not first advise the employee to obtain counsel.","C":"No, because no legal proceedings are now pending.","D":"No, because the attorney did not give legal advice to the employee."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may not “engage in conduct involving dishonesty, fraud, deceit, or misrepresentation.” Model Rule 8.4(c). Here, the attorney is planning to falsely tell the employee she is not a suspect and that her answers will be held in confidence. This is a clear violation of the Rules. B is incorrect. The issue here is not that the attorney failed to advise the employee to obtain counsel. The issue is that the attorney is planning to lie to the employee regarding the theft investigation. C is incorrect. Although no legal proceedings are pending, the attorney is improperly planning to misrepresent the situation to the employee. D is incorrect. The limitations on what an attorney can tell another person involve more than simple legal advice. Importantly, the Rules also disallow dishonesty. For a communications question like this, an answer choice stating that the attorney is not subject to discipline because the attorney did not give legal advice is usually wrong.","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may not “engage in conduct involving dishonesty, fraud, deceit, or misrepresentation.” Model Rule 8.4(c). Here, the attorney is planning to falsely tell the employee she is not a suspect and that her answers will be held in confidence. This is a clear violation of the Rules. B is incorrect. The issue here is not that the attorney failed to advise the employee to obtain counsel. The issue is that the attorney is planning to lie to the employee regarding the theft investigation. C is incorrect. Although no legal proceedings are pending, the attorney is improperly planning to misrepresent the situation to the employee. D is incorrect. The limitations on what an attorney can tell another person involve more than simple legal advice. Importantly, the Rules also disallow dishonesty. For a communications question like this, an answer choice stating that the attorney is not subject to discipline because the attorney did not give legal advice is usually wrong.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline if she so questions the employee?","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may not “engage in conduct involving dishonesty, fraud, deceit, or misrepresentation.” Model Rule 8.4(c). Here, the attorney is planning to falsely tell the employee she is not a suspect and that her answers will be held in confidence. This is a clear violation of the Rules. B is incorrect. The issue here is not that the attorney failed to advise the employee to obtain counsel. The issue is that the attorney is planning to lie to the employee regarding the theft investigation. C is incorrect. Although no legal proceedings are pending, the attorney is improperly planning to misrepresent the situation to the employee. D is incorrect. The limitations on what an attorney can tell another person involve more than simple legal advice. Importantly, the Rules also disallow dishonesty. For a communications question like this, an answer choice stating that the attorney is not subject to discipline because the attorney did not give legal advice is usually wrong.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may not “engage in conduct involving dishonesty, fraud, deceit, or misrepresentation.” Model Rule 8.4(c). Here, the attorney is planning to falsely tell the employee she is not a suspect and that her answers will be held in confidence. This is a clear violation of the Rules. B is incorrect. The issue here is not that the attorney failed to advise the employee to obtain counsel. The issue is that the attorney is planning to lie to the employee regarding the theft investigation. C is incorrect. Although no legal proceedings are pending, the attorney is improperly planning to misrepresent the situation to the employee. D is incorrect. The limitations on what an attorney can tell another person involve more than simple legal advice. Importantly, the Rules also disallow dishonesty. For a communications question like this, an answer choice stating that the attorney is not subject to discipline because the attorney did not give legal advice is usually wrong.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 8.4(c)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[149],"sourceAnswerPages":[252],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师担任某公司的公司法律顾问，正在调查公司零部件部门可能存在的盗窃团伙。律师知道一名雇员长期在零部件部门工作，并认为该雇员是盗窃嫌疑人。律师相信，如果雇员知道提问的真实目的，接受询问时就不会如实回答。律师计划询问该雇员，并虚假地告诉她，她不是嫌疑人，而且她对问题的回答将予以保密。如果律师以这种方式询问雇员，是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师的行为涉及虚假陈述。","B":"是，因为律师没有先建议雇员聘请律师。","C":"否，因为目前没有待决法律程序。","D":"否，因为律师没有向雇员提供法律意见。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。律师不得“从事涉及不诚实、欺诈、欺骗或虚假陈述的行为”。《职业行为示范规则》8.4(c)。本题中，律师计划虚假地告诉雇员，她不是嫌疑人，而且她的回答将予保密。这显然违反规则。B 不正确。本题问题不在于律师没有建议雇员聘请律师，而在于律师计划就盗窃调查向雇员说谎。C 不正确。虽然没有待决法律程序，律师仍在不当地计划向雇员歪曲事实。D 不正确。律师可以向他人说什么所受到的限制，不只涉及法律意见。尤其重要的是，规则也禁止不诚实。对于此类沟通问题，声称律师仅仅因为没有提供法律意见就不应受纪律处分的选项，通常都是错误的。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-031","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":31,"topic":"Rule 2.9(A) / Rule 2.9","question":"A manufacturer sued a parts company for breach of warranty regarding machine components furnished by the parts company. A judge, who presided at the nonjury trial, sent her law clerk to the manufacturer’s plant to observe the machine that was malfunctioning due to the allegedly defective parts. The clerk returned and told the judge that the machine was indeed malfunctioning and that one of the manufacturer’s engineers had explained to the clerk how the parts delivered by the parts company caused the malfunction. There was testimony at the trial that supported what the clerk learned on his visit, although the engineer was not a witness. The judge rendered a judgment for the manufacturer. Was the judge’s conduct proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the judge’s judgment was supported by evidence at the trial.","B":"Yes, because the judge has the right to gather facts concerning the trial.","C":"No, because the judge has engaged in ex parte contacts that might influence the outcome of litigation.","D":"No, because the engineer was not a witness at the trial and subject to cross-examination by the parts company."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. A judge is not permitted to engage in ex parte communications or investigate facts independently. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A). The Rules recognize the fact that “to the extent reasonably possible, all parties or their lawyers shall be included in communications with a judge.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9, Comment [1]. Here, the judge violated the Code by sending her clerk to investigate the malfunctioning machine and having her clerk speak with the manufacturer’s engineer. A is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice, and should be avoided. B is incorrect. A judge is not allowed to undertake his or her own investigation regarding the facts of a pending trial. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9. D is incorrect. Even if the engineer was a witness at the trial, the judge still engaged in improper ex parte communications when she used her law clerk to investigate the machine.","rule":"C is the correct answer. A judge is not permitted to engage in ex parte communications or investigate facts independently. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A). The Rules recognize the fact that “to the extent reasonably possible, all parties or their lawyers shall be included in communications with a judge.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9, Comment [1]. Here, the judge violated the Code by sending her clerk to investigate the malfunctioning machine and having her clerk speak with the manufacturer’s engineer. A is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice, and should be avoided. B is incorrect. A judge is not allowed to undertake his or her own investigation regarding the facts of a pending trial. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9. D is incorrect. Even if the engineer was a witness at the trial, the judge still engaged in improper ex parte communications when she used her law clerk to investigate the machine.","irac":{"issue":"Was the judge’s conduct proper?","rule":"C is the correct answer. A judge is not permitted to engage in ex parte communications or investigate facts independently. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A). The Rules recognize the fact that “to the extent reasonably possible, all parties or their lawyers shall be included in communications with a judge.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9, Comment [1]. Here, the judge violated the Code by sending her clerk to investigate the malfunctioning machine and having her clerk speak with the manufacturer’s engineer. A is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice, and should be avoided. B is incorrect. A judge is not allowed to undertake his or her own investigation regarding the facts of a pending trial. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9. D is incorrect. Even if the engineer was a witness at the trial, the judge still engaged in improper ex parte communications when she used her law clerk to investigate the machine.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. A judge is not permitted to engage in ex parte communications or investigate facts independently. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A). The Rules recognize the fact that “to the extent reasonably possible, all parties or their lawyers shall be included in communications with a judge.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9, Comment [1]. Here, the judge violated the Code by sending her clerk to investigate the malfunctioning machine and having her clerk speak with the manufacturer’s engineer. A is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice, and should be avoided. B is incorrect. A judge is not allowed to undertake his or her own investigation regarding the facts of a pending trial. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9. D is incorrect. Even if the engineer was a witness at the trial, the judge still engaged in improper ex parte communications when she used her law clerk to investigate the machine.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 2.9(A)","Rule 2.9"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[149,150],"sourceAnswerPages":[253],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家制造商起诉一家零部件公司，主张该零部件公司提供的机器部件违反保证。一名法官主持无陪审团审判，并派自己的法官助理前往制造商工厂，观察因涉嫌存在缺陷的部件而发生故障的机器。助理返回后告诉法官，机器确实发生故障；制造商的一名工程师还向助理解释了零部件公司交付的部件如何导致故障。审判中有证言支持助理在访问时了解到的内容，但该工程师并未出庭作证。法官判决制造商胜诉。法官的行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为法官的判决受到审判中证据支持。","B":"适当，因为法官有权收集与审判有关的事实。","C":"不适当，因为法官进行了可能影响诉讼结果的单方联系。","D":"不适当，因为该工程师没有在审判中出庭作证，也未接受零部件公司的交叉询问。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。法官不得进行单方沟通，也不得独立调查事实。《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.9(A)。规则确认，“在合理可行的范围内，应让所有当事人或其律师参与与法官的沟通。”规则 2.9 评注 [1]。本题中，法官派助理调查发生故障的机器，并让助理与制造商工程师交谈，违反了准则。A 不正确。这是典型的“没有损害，就没有违规”型选项，应予避免。B 不正确。法官不得自行调查待决审判的事实。规则 2.9。D 不正确。即使工程师在审判中出庭作证，法官利用助理调查机器时仍进行了不当的单方沟通。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-032","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":32,"topic":"Model Rule 1.15","question":"When one of an attorney’s regular clients planned to leave on a world tour, the client delivered to the attorney sufficient money to pay the client’s property taxes when they became due. The attorney placed the money in her Clients’ Trust Account. When the tax payment date arrived, the attorney was in need of a temporary loan to close the purchase of a new personal residence. Because the penalty for late payment of taxes was only 2 percent while the rate for a personal loan was 6 percent, the attorney withdrew the client’s funds from the trust account to cover the attorney’s personal check for the closing. The attorney was confident that the client would not object. Ten days later, after the receipt of a large fee previously earned, the attorney paid the client’s property taxes and the 2 percent penalty, fully satisfying the client’s tax obligation. After the client returned, the attorney told the client what she had done, and the client approved the attorney’s conduct. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney failed to pay the client the 10 days of interest at the fair market rate.","B":"Yes, because the attorney used the client’s funds for a personal purpose.","C":"No, because the client was not harmed and the attorney reasonably believed at the time she withdrew the money that the client would not object.","D":"No, because when the attorney told the client what she had done, the client approved the attorney’s conduct."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer is required to keep a client’s funds separate from his or her own. Model Rule 1.15. Importantly, the attorney could not use the funds in her Clients’ Trust Account for her personal use, as those funds belonged to her client. A is incorrect. The important point here is that an attorney may not use a client’s funds for his or her own purpose. Whether or not the attorney paid interest on the money does not change the analysis. C is incorrect. This is an example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Whether or not the client is harmed, an attorney may not use a client’s funds for his or her personal use. D is incorrect. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Even though the client may have approved the attorney’s conduct, the attorney had already violated the Rules before she told her client and received the client’s approval.","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer is required to keep a client’s funds separate from his or her own. Model Rule 1.15. Importantly, the attorney could not use the funds in her Clients’ Trust Account for her personal use, as those funds belonged to her client. A is incorrect. The important point here is that an attorney may not use a client’s funds for his or her own purpose. Whether or not the attorney paid interest on the money does not change the analysis. C is incorrect. This is an example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Whether or not the client is harmed, an attorney may not use a client’s funds for his or her personal use. D is incorrect. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Even though the client may have approved the attorney’s conduct, the attorney had already violated the Rules before she told her client and received the client’s approval.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer is required to keep a client’s funds separate from his or her own. Model Rule 1.15. Importantly, the attorney could not use the funds in her Clients’ Trust Account for her personal use, as those funds belonged to her client. A is incorrect. The important point here is that an attorney may not use a client’s funds for his or her own purpose. Whether or not the attorney paid interest on the money does not change the analysis. C is incorrect. This is an example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Whether or not the client is harmed, an attorney may not use a client’s funds for his or her personal use. D is incorrect. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Even though the client may have approved the attorney’s conduct, the attorney had already violated the Rules before she told her client and received the client’s approval.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer is required to keep a client’s funds separate from his or her own. Model Rule 1.15. Importantly, the attorney could not use the funds in her Clients’ Trust Account for her personal use, as those funds belonged to her client. A is incorrect. The important point here is that an attorney may not use a client’s funds for his or her own purpose. Whether or not the attorney paid interest on the money does not change the analysis. C is incorrect. This is an example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Whether or not the client is harmed, an attorney may not use a client’s funds for his or her personal use. D is incorrect. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Even though the client may have approved the attorney’s conduct, the attorney had already violated the Rules before she told her client and received the client’s approval.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.15"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[150],"sourceAnswerPages":[253],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师的常年委托人计划开始环球旅行，将足够支付到期房产税的款项交给律师。律师将资金存入委托人信托账户。房产税到期时，律师需要一笔短期贷款，以完成购买个人新住宅的交易。由于逾期缴税罚金只有 2%，而个人贷款利率为 6%，律师从信托账户取出委托人资金，用于兑付自己在住宅交易交割时开出的个人支票。律师确信委托人不会反对。十天后，律师收到一笔先前已经赚取的大额费用，用该款支付了委托人的房产税和 2% 罚金，完全清偿了委托人的纳税义务。委托人返回后，律师告知自己所做之事，委托人认可了律师的行为。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师没有按照公平市场利率向委托人支付这十天的利息。","B":"是，因为律师将委托人资金用于个人目的。","C":"否，因为委托人没有受到损害，而且律师取款时合理地相信委托人不会反对。","D":"否，因为律师告知委托人自己所做之事后，委托人认可了该行为。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。律师必须将委托人资金与自己的资金分开保管。《职业行为示范规则》1.15。尤其重要的是，委托人信托账户中的资金属于委托人，律师不得将其用于个人用途。A 不正确。本题关键是律师不得将委托人资金用于自己的目的。律师是否就该款项支付利息不会改变分析。C 不正确。这是一个“没有损害，就没有违规”的错误选项。无论委托人是否受到损害，律师都不得将委托人资金用于个人用途。D 不正确。这又是一个“没有损害，就没有违规”的错误选项。即使委托人后来认可律师的行为，律师在告知委托人并取得认可之前已经违反规则。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-033","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":33,"topic":"Model Rule 1.5(d)(2) / Model Rule 1.5","question":"An attorney entered into a written retainer agreement with a defendant in a criminal case. The defendant agreed in writing to transfer title to the defendant’s automobile to the attorney if the attorney successfully prevented him from going to prison. Later, the charges against the defendant were dismissed. Is the attorney subject to discipline for entering into this retainer agreement?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney agreed to a fee contingent on the outcome of a criminal case.","B":"Yes, because a lawyer may not acquire a proprietary interest in a client’s property.","C":"No, because the charges against the defendant were dismissed.","D":"No, because the retainer agreement is in writing."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. An attorney cannot charge a contingent fee for representing a defendant in a criminal case. Model Rule 1.5(d)(2). This is an example of a question where careful reading of the facts is very important. B is incorrect. The issue here is that an attorney cannot charge a contingent fee in a criminal matter. Model Rule 1.5(d)(2). Importantly, “a lawyer may accept property in payment for services, such as an ownership interest in an enterprise, providing this does not involve acquisition of a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of the litigation.” Model Rule 1.5, Comment [4]. C is incorrect. Even though he obtained a good result for his client, the attorney still violated the prohibition against contingency fees in criminal cases. D is incorrect. The simple fact that a retainer agreement is in writing does not make that retainer agreement proper under the Rules. This answer choice should have been stricken immediately as an overbroad statement.","rule":"A is the correct answer. An attorney cannot charge a contingent fee for representing a defendant in a criminal case. Model Rule 1.5(d)(2). This is an example of a question where careful reading of the facts is very important. B is incorrect. The issue here is that an attorney cannot charge a contingent fee in a criminal matter. Model Rule 1.5(d)(2). Importantly, “a lawyer may accept property in payment for services, such as an ownership interest in an enterprise, providing this does not involve acquisition of a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of the litigation.” Model Rule 1.5, Comment [4]. C is incorrect. Even though he obtained a good result for his client, the attorney still violated the prohibition against contingency fees in criminal cases. D is incorrect. The simple fact that a retainer agreement is in writing does not make that retainer agreement proper under the Rules. This answer choice should have been stricken immediately as an overbroad statement.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for entering into this retainer agreement?","rule":"A is the correct answer. An attorney cannot charge a contingent fee for representing a defendant in a criminal case. Model Rule 1.5(d)(2). This is an example of a question where careful reading of the facts is very important. B is incorrect. The issue here is that an attorney cannot charge a contingent fee in a criminal matter. Model Rule 1.5(d)(2). Importantly, “a lawyer may accept property in payment for services, such as an ownership interest in an enterprise, providing this does not involve acquisition of a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of the litigation.” Model Rule 1.5, Comment [4]. C is incorrect. Even though he obtained a good result for his client, the attorney still violated the prohibition against contingency fees in criminal cases. D is incorrect. The simple fact that a retainer agreement is in writing does not make that retainer agreement proper under the Rules. This answer choice should have been stricken immediately as an overbroad statement.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. An attorney cannot charge a contingent fee for representing a defendant in a criminal case. Model Rule 1.5(d)(2). This is an example of a question where careful reading of the facts is very important. B is incorrect. The issue here is that an attorney cannot charge a contingent fee in a criminal matter. Model Rule 1.5(d)(2). Importantly, “a lawyer may accept property in payment for services, such as an ownership interest in an enterprise, providing this does not involve acquisition of a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of the litigation.” Model Rule 1.5, Comment [4]. C is incorrect. Even though he obtained a good result for his client, the attorney still violated the prohibition against contingency fees in criminal cases. D is incorrect. The simple fact that a retainer agreement is in writing does not make that retainer agreement proper under the Rules. This answer choice should have been stricken immediately as an overbroad statement.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.5(d)(2)","Model Rule 1.5"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[150,151],"sourceAnswerPages":[254],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师与一宗刑事案件的被告订立书面聘用协议。被告以书面形式同意，如果律师成功使其免于入狱，就把自己汽车的所有权转让给律师。后来，对被告的指控被撤销。律师因订立该聘用协议是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师约定的收费取决于刑事案件结果。","B":"是，因为律师不得取得委托人财产的所有权利益。","C":"否，因为对被告的指控已被撤销。","D":"否，因为聘用协议采用书面形式。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。律师代理刑事案件被告时不得收取风险代理费。《职业行为示范规则》1.5(d)(2)。本题很好地说明了仔细阅读事实的重要性。B 不正确。本题问题是律师不能在刑事事项中收取风险代理费。规则 1.5(d)(2)。尤其要注意，“律师可以接受财产作为服务报酬，例如企业中的所有权利益，但不得因此取得诉因或诉讼标的的所有权利益。”规则 1.5 评注 [4]。C 不正确。律师虽然为委托人取得了良好结果，仍违反刑事案件禁止风险代理费的规定。D 不正确。聘用协议采用书面形式这一事实本身，并不会使其在规则下变得适当。该选项过度宽泛，应立即排除。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-034","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":34,"topic":"Model Rule 4.2","question":"An attorney filed a complaint on behalf of her client against a corporation, alleging that the corporation had breached a valid oral contract entered into on the corporation’s behalf by its president and chief executive officer. The attorney representing the corporation has filed an answer denying the contract and asserting the statute of frauds as a defense. The corporation’s attorney has given notice that he will take the deposition of the corporation’s president on the grounds that he will be out of the country on the date the case is set for trial. The corporation’s president is not a shareholder of the corporation. The attorney would like to interview the corporation’s president prior to the taking of the deposition in order to better prepare her cross-examination. Is the attorney subject to discipline if she interviews the corporation’s president without the corporation’s attorney’s knowledge and consent?","choices":{"A":"No, because the corporation president will not be personally liable to the corporation for damages in the event judgment is rendered against the corporation.","B":"No, because the corporation’s president allegedly entered into the contract on behalf of the corporation.","C":"Yes, because the corporation’s president is being called as an adverse witness.","D":"Yes, because the corporation’s president is president of the corporation."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. In the case of a represented organization, Model Rule 4.2 prohibits communications with a member of the organization “who supervises, directs or regularly consults with the organization’s lawyer concerning the matter or who has authority to obligate the organization with respect to the matter.” Model Rule 4.2, Comment [7]. Here, the corporation’s president is clearly such a member of the organization, and the attorney cannot communicate with him without the corporation’s attorney’s knowledge and consent. A is incorrect. Although the corporation’s president may not be personally liable, he still is a person protected by Model Rule 4.2. Considering his position, he likely supervises, directs, or regularly consults with the organization’s lawyer, or even if he does not, he has the power to obligate the organization. B is incorrect. Even without the dictates of Model Rule 4.2, it wouldn’t make sense for the attorney to be allowed to talk to the corporation’s president alone considering his actions created potential liability for the corporation. C is incorrect. Even though the corporation’s president may be called as an adverse witness, this does not solve the issue of his position and responsibility in the corporation.","rule":"D is the correct answer. In the case of a represented organization, Model Rule 4.2 prohibits communications with a member of the organization “who supervises, directs or regularly consults with the organization’s lawyer concerning the matter or who has authority to obligate the organization with respect to the matter.” Model Rule 4.2, Comment [7]. Here, the corporation’s president is clearly such a member of the organization, and the attorney cannot communicate with him without the corporation’s attorney’s knowledge and consent. A is incorrect. Although the corporation’s president may not be personally liable, he still is a person protected by Model Rule 4.2. Considering his position, he likely supervises, directs, or regularly consults with the organization’s lawyer, or even if he does not, he has the power to obligate the organization. B is incorrect. Even without the dictates of Model Rule 4.2, it wouldn’t make sense for the attorney to be allowed to talk to the corporation’s president alone considering his actions created potential liability for the corporation. C is incorrect. Even though the corporation’s president may be called as an adverse witness, this does not solve the issue of his position and responsibility in the corporation.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline if she interviews the corporation’s president without the corporation’s attorney’s knowledge and consent?","rule":"D is the correct answer. In the case of a represented organization, Model Rule 4.2 prohibits communications with a member of the organization “who supervises, directs or regularly consults with the organization’s lawyer concerning the matter or who has authority to obligate the organization with respect to the matter.” Model Rule 4.2, Comment [7]. Here, the corporation’s president is clearly such a member of the organization, and the attorney cannot communicate with him without the corporation’s attorney’s knowledge and consent. A is incorrect. Although the corporation’s president may not be personally liable, he still is a person protected by Model Rule 4.2. Considering his position, he likely supervises, directs, or regularly consults with the organization’s lawyer, or even if he does not, he has the power to obligate the organization. B is incorrect. Even without the dictates of Model Rule 4.2, it wouldn’t make sense for the attorney to be allowed to talk to the corporation’s president alone considering his actions created potential liability for the corporation. C is incorrect. Even though the corporation’s president may be called as an adverse witness, this does not solve the issue of his position and responsibility in the corporation.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. In the case of a represented organization, Model Rule 4.2 prohibits communications with a member of the organization “who supervises, directs or regularly consults with the organization’s lawyer concerning the matter or who has authority to obligate the organization with respect to the matter.” Model Rule 4.2, Comment [7]. Here, the corporation’s president is clearly such a member of the organization, and the attorney cannot communicate with him without the corporation’s attorney’s knowledge and consent. A is incorrect. Although the corporation’s president may not be personally liable, he still is a person protected by Model Rule 4.2. Considering his position, he likely supervises, directs, or regularly consults with the organization’s lawyer, or even if he does not, he has the power to obligate the organization. B is incorrect. Even without the dictates of Model Rule 4.2, it wouldn’t make sense for the attorney to be allowed to talk to the corporation’s president alone considering his actions created potential liability for the corporation. C is incorrect. Even though the corporation’s president may be called as an adverse witness, this does not solve the issue of his position and responsibility in the corporation.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 4.2"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[151],"sourceAnswerPages":[254,255],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代表委托人起诉一家公司，主张公司总裁兼首席执行官代表公司订立了一份有效口头合同，而公司违反了该合同。公司律师已提交答辩，否认合同存在并援引防止欺诈法作为抗辩。公司律师发出通知称，将对公司总裁进行证言录取，理由是案件预定审判日期当天总裁将身在国外。公司总裁不是公司股东。该律师希望在证言录取前询问公司总裁，以便更充分地准备交叉询问。如果律师在公司律师不知情且未同意的情况下询问公司总裁，是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为如果判决公司败诉，公司总裁个人不必就损害赔偿对公司承担责任。","B":"否，因为据称是公司总裁代表公司订立合同。","C":"是，因为公司总裁将作为对方证人被传唤。","D":"是，因为该人是公司总裁。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。对于已有律师代理的组织，《职业行为示范规则》4.2 禁止与以下组织成员沟通：该成员“就有关事项监督、指导或经常咨询组织的律师，或者有权就有关事项使组织承担义务”。规则 4.2 评注 [7]。本题中，公司总裁显然属于这种组织成员，未经公司律师知情并同意，该律师不得与其沟通。A 不正确。公司总裁虽然可能不承担个人责任，仍是规则 4.2 保护的人。考虑其职位，他很可能监督、指导或经常咨询组织律师；即使没有，他也有权使组织承担义务。B 不正确。即使没有规则 4.2 的规定，考虑到公司总裁的行为造成了公司潜在责任，允许律师单独与他交谈也不合理。C 不正确。即使公司总裁可能作为对方证人被传唤，也不能解决其在公司中的职位和职责所产生的问题。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-035","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":35,"topic":"Model Rule 1.8(h)(1)","question":"An attorney agreed to represent a client in a lawsuit. The attorney and the client executed the attorney’s preprinted retainer form that provides, in part: “The client agrees to pay promptly the attorney’s fee for services. In addition, the client and the attorney agree to release each other from any and all liability arising from the representation. The client agrees that the attorney need not return the client’s file prior to receiving the client’s executed release. Upon full payment, the attorney will return the file to the client.” Although the attorney recommended that the client consult independent counsel before signing the retainer agreement, the client chose not to do so. The attorney reasonably believes that his fee is fair and that the quality of his work will be competent. Is the attorney’s retainer agreement with the client proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney furnished consideration by agreeing to return the client’s file.","B":"Yes, because the attorney reasonably believes that his fee is fair and that the quality of his work will be competent.","C":"No, because the attorney is attempting to limit his liability for malpractice.","D":"No, because the attorney uses a preprinted form for all retainers."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may not “make an agreement prospectively limiting the lawyer’s liability to a client for malpractice unless the client is independently represented in making the agreement.” Model Rule 1.8(h)(1). Here, the attorney is attempting to do so by having the client sign the retainer agreement. A is incorrect. This is not a question regarding the creation of a contract. The issue here is the content of that contract, and the content attempting to limit the attorney’s malpractice liability is improper under the Rules. B is incorrect. While his fee may be fair and his work adequate, this does not guarantee that a client may not have a valid malpractice claim. Importantly, the attorney cannot attempt to limit his liability through the use of this retainer agreement. D is incorrect. There’s no particular issue with using preprinted forms. This answer choice seems to be making the assumption that such preprinted forms will result in unfair agreements or fees, which is not necessarily the case.","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may not “make an agreement prospectively limiting the lawyer’s liability to a client for malpractice unless the client is independently represented in making the agreement.” Model Rule 1.8(h)(1). Here, the attorney is attempting to do so by having the client sign the retainer agreement. A is incorrect. This is not a question regarding the creation of a contract. The issue here is the content of that contract, and the content attempting to limit the attorney’s malpractice liability is improper under the Rules. B is incorrect. While his fee may be fair and his work adequate, this does not guarantee that a client may not have a valid malpractice claim. Importantly, the attorney cannot attempt to limit his liability through the use of this retainer agreement. D is incorrect. There’s no particular issue with using preprinted forms. This answer choice seems to be making the assumption that such preprinted forms will result in unfair agreements or fees, which is not necessarily the case.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney’s retainer agreement with the client proper?","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may not “make an agreement prospectively limiting the lawyer’s liability to a client for malpractice unless the client is independently represented in making the agreement.” Model Rule 1.8(h)(1). Here, the attorney is attempting to do so by having the client sign the retainer agreement. A is incorrect. This is not a question regarding the creation of a contract. The issue here is the content of that contract, and the content attempting to limit the attorney’s malpractice liability is improper under the Rules. B is incorrect. While his fee may be fair and his work adequate, this does not guarantee that a client may not have a valid malpractice claim. Importantly, the attorney cannot attempt to limit his liability through the use of this retainer agreement. D is incorrect. There’s no particular issue with using preprinted forms. This answer choice seems to be making the assumption that such preprinted forms will result in unfair agreements or fees, which is not necessarily the case.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may not “make an agreement prospectively limiting the lawyer’s liability to a client for malpractice unless the client is independently represented in making the agreement.” Model Rule 1.8(h)(1). Here, the attorney is attempting to do so by having the client sign the retainer agreement. A is incorrect. This is not a question regarding the creation of a contract. The issue here is the content of that contract, and the content attempting to limit the attorney’s malpractice liability is improper under the Rules. B is incorrect. While his fee may be fair and his work adequate, this does not guarantee that a client may not have a valid malpractice claim. Importantly, the attorney cannot attempt to limit his liability through the use of this retainer agreement. D is incorrect. There’s no particular issue with using preprinted forms. This answer choice seems to be making the assumption that such preprinted forms will result in unfair agreements or fees, which is not necessarily the case.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.8(h)(1)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[151,152],"sourceAnswerPages":[255],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师同意代理委托人参加诉讼。律师与委托人签署了律师预先印制的聘用表格，其中部分内容规定：“委托人同意及时支付律师服务费。此外，委托人与律师同意相互免除因代理产生的任何及全部责任。委托人同意，在律师收到委托人签署的免责文件前，可以不退还委托人案卷。律师收到全额付款后，会把案卷退还委托人。”律师虽然建议委托人在签署聘用协议前咨询独立律师，但委托人选择不这样做。律师合理地认为收费公平，工作质量也会达到称职标准。律师与委托人订立的聘用协议是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为律师以同意退还委托人案卷提供了合同对价。","B":"适当，因为律师合理地认为收费公平，工作质量也会达到称职标准。","C":"不适当，因为律师正试图限制自己对执业过失承担的责任。","D":"不适当，因为律师对所有聘用协议都使用预先印制的表格。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。律师不得“订立预先限制律师对委托人承担执业过失责任的协议，除非委托人在订立协议时由独立律师代理”。《职业行为示范规则》1.8(h)(1)。本题中，律师试图通过让委托人签署聘用协议来限制责任。A 不正确。这不是一个有关合同是否成立的问题，问题在于合同内容；其中试图限制律师执业过失责任的内容违反规则。B 不正确。即使收费公平且工作达到适当水平，也不能保证委托人不可能提出有效的执业过失主张。关键是，律师不能利用该聘用协议试图限制自己的责任。D 不正确。使用预先印制的表格本身没有特别问题。该选项似乎假定预印表格一定会造成不公平协议或收费，但事实未必如此。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-036","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":36,"topic":"Model Rule 8.3(a) / Model Rule 1.6 / Model Rule 8.3(c)","question":"An attorney serves on a bar association committee established to counsel and rehabilitate lawyers who suffer from substance abuse. The day before the attorney was to leave on a fishing trip, the attorney’s close friend, another attorney, disclosed to the attorney that, over the preceding two years, he had become heavily addicted to cocaine and was afraid he had committed criminal offenses in his banking activities as a result of his addiction. He then asked the attorney to represent him. The attorney agreed, but explained that he could do little for two weeks and would consult with his friend immediately upon his return. While on the fishing trip, an accountant who knew that the attorney was going to represent his friend told him that the accountant had been retained by the trust department of a commercial bank to audit several substantial trust accounts in which the bank and the attorney’s friend are cotrustees. The accountant also told the attorney that the audit furnished incontrovertible proof that the attorney’s friend had embezzled more than $100,000 from the trust accounts. Must the attorney report his friend’s embezzlement to the appropriate disciplinary authority?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney learned of his friend’s embezzlement from the accountant.","B":"Yes, because the attorney’s failure to report would assist the concealment of his friend’s breach of trust.","C":"No, because the attorney gained the information while representing his friend.","D":"No, because the information will probably be made public by the bank."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 8.3(a), an attorney who knows another attorney has violated the Rules of Professional Conduct must report that information to the appropriate professional authority. However, there is an exception when the attorney learns this information through representing the other attorney and disclosure of the information would violate the protections of confidentiality afforded by Model Rule 1.6. Model Rule 8.3(c). Since the attorney here gained the information while representing his friend, he is not required to report it. A is incorrect. The accountant merely confirmed the information the attorney had already learned through his representation of his friend. Consequently, he is not required to report it. B is incorrect. Because the information was protected by attorney-client privilege, the attorney does not need to report the embezzlement to the appropriate disciplinary authority. Additionally, the attorney’s failure to inform the disciplinary authority will do nothing to hinder the investigation that is apparently already in progress. D is incorrect. Although the bank may make the information public, there is no Rule requiring the attorney to “beat them to the punch” and inform the disciplinary authority first.","rule":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 8.3(a), an attorney who knows another attorney has violated the Rules of Professional Conduct must report that information to the appropriate professional authority. However, there is an exception when the attorney learns this information through representing the other attorney and disclosure of the information would violate the protections of confidentiality afforded by Model Rule 1.6. Model Rule 8.3(c). Since the attorney here gained the information while representing his friend, he is not required to report it. A is incorrect. The accountant merely confirmed the information the attorney had already learned through his representation of his friend. Consequently, he is not required to report it. B is incorrect. Because the information was protected by attorney-client privilege, the attorney does not need to report the embezzlement to the appropriate disciplinary authority. Additionally, the attorney’s failure to inform the disciplinary authority will do nothing to hinder the investigation that is apparently already in progress. D is incorrect. Although the bank may make the information public, there is no Rule requiring the attorney to “beat them to the punch” and inform the disciplinary authority first.","irac":{"issue":"Must the attorney report his friend’s embezzlement to the appropriate disciplinary authority?","rule":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 8.3(a), an attorney who knows another attorney has violated the Rules of Professional Conduct must report that information to the appropriate professional authority. However, there is an exception when the attorney learns this information through representing the other attorney and disclosure of the information would violate the protections of confidentiality afforded by Model Rule 1.6. Model Rule 8.3(c). Since the attorney here gained the information while representing his friend, he is not required to report it. A is incorrect. The accountant merely confirmed the information the attorney had already learned through his representation of his friend. Consequently, he is not required to report it. B is incorrect. Because the information was protected by attorney-client privilege, the attorney does not need to report the embezzlement to the appropriate disciplinary authority. Additionally, the attorney’s failure to inform the disciplinary authority will do nothing to hinder the investigation that is apparently already in progress. D is incorrect. Although the bank may make the information public, there is no Rule requiring the attorney to “beat them to the punch” and inform the disciplinary authority first.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 8.3(a), an attorney who knows another attorney has violated the Rules of Professional Conduct must report that information to the appropriate professional authority. However, there is an exception when the attorney learns this information through representing the other attorney and disclosure of the information would violate the protections of confidentiality afforded by Model Rule 1.6. Model Rule 8.3(c). Since the attorney here gained the information while representing his friend, he is not required to report it. A is incorrect. The accountant merely confirmed the information the attorney had already learned through his representation of his friend. Consequently, he is not required to report it. B is incorrect. Because the information was protected by attorney-client privilege, the attorney does not need to report the embezzlement to the appropriate disciplinary authority. Additionally, the attorney’s failure to inform the disciplinary authority will do nothing to hinder the investigation that is apparently already in progress. D is incorrect. Although the bank may make the information public, there is no Rule requiring the attorney to “beat them to the punch” and inform the disciplinary authority first.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 8.3(a)","Model Rule 1.6","Model Rule 8.3(c)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[152,153],"sourceAnswerPages":[255,256],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师在律师协会设立的委员会任职，该委员会负责辅导并帮助存在药物滥用问题的律师康复。律师准备外出钓鱼旅行的前一天，他的一名好友（也是一名律师）向其透露，在此前两年中自己已严重成瘾于可卡因，并担心因成瘾而在银行业务中实施了犯罪。好友随后请求该律师代理自己。律师同意，但解释说未来两周几乎无法处理此事，旅行归来后会立即与好友协商。钓鱼旅行期间，一名知道该律师将代理好友的会计师告诉律师，一家商业银行信托部门已聘请会计师审计几个金额巨大的信托账户；银行和律师好友是这些账户的共同受托人。会计师还告诉律师，审计提供了无可辩驳的证据，证明其好友从信托账户挪用了超过 100,000 美元。该律师是否必须向适当纪律机关举报好友挪用资金的行为？","choicesZh":{"A":"必须，因为律师是从会计师处得知好友挪用资金。","B":"必须，因为律师不举报会协助掩盖好友违反信托义务的行为。","C":"不必须，因为律师是在代理好友期间取得该信息。","D":"不必须，因为银行很可能会公开该信息。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。根据《职业行为示范规则》8.3(a)，律师知道另一名律师违反《职业行为规则》时，必须向适当的专业机关举报。不过，如果律师是在代理另一名律师的过程中得知信息，且披露会违反规则 1.6 所提供的保密保护，则属于例外。规则 8.3(c)。由于本题律师是在代理好友期间取得信息，无须举报。A 不正确。会计师只是确认了律师已经通过代理好友而得知的信息，因此律师无须举报。B 不正确。由于该信息受到律师—委托人特权保护，律师无须向适当纪律机关举报挪用行为。此外，律师不通知纪律机关，并不会阻碍显然已经在进行的调查。D 不正确。银行虽然可能公开信息，但没有规则要求律师“抢先一步”最先通知纪律机关。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-037","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":37,"topic":"Model Rule 7.1","question":"The following advertisement appeared in a daily newspaper in a state in which both parties are members of the bar: A. ALPHA, M.D., J.D. and B. BETA, J.D. Attorneys at Law 1000 “A” Street, City, State, 00000 Telephone (555) 555-5555 Are Alpha and Beta subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"No, because both law and medicine are licensed professions.","B":"No, because they possess the degree(s) stated.","C":"Yes, because the reference to the M.D. degree is self-laudatory.","D":"Yes, unless they limit their practice to areas in which a medical degree is relevant."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. In advertising his or her practice, an attorney may not make false or misleading statements. Model Rule 7.1. As long as none of the information regarding their degrees is false, the attorneys are free to list their degrees in their advertisement. A is incorrect. Although both law and medicine are licensed professions, this would not make the statement proper if the attorneys were lying about their licenses. C is incorrect. The Rules do not forbid an attorney from making statements that are self-laudatory, so long as they are not untruthful or misleading. D is incorrect. The attorney’s listing of his medical degree is not done in a way that indicates any specialty or limitation on his practice.","rule":"B is the correct answer. In advertising his or her practice, an attorney may not make false or misleading statements. Model Rule 7.1. As long as none of the information regarding their degrees is false, the attorneys are free to list their degrees in their advertisement. A is incorrect. Although both law and medicine are licensed professions, this would not make the statement proper if the attorneys were lying about their licenses. C is incorrect. The Rules do not forbid an attorney from making statements that are self-laudatory, so long as they are not untruthful or misleading. D is incorrect. The attorney’s listing of his medical degree is not done in a way that indicates any specialty or limitation on his practice.","irac":{"issue":"Attorneys at Law 1000 “A” Street, City, State, 00000 Telephone (555) 555-5555 Are Alpha and Beta subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. In advertising his or her practice, an attorney may not make false or misleading statements. Model Rule 7.1. As long as none of the information regarding their degrees is false, the attorneys are free to list their degrees in their advertisement. A is incorrect. Although both law and medicine are licensed professions, this would not make the statement proper if the attorneys were lying about their licenses. C is incorrect. The Rules do not forbid an attorney from making statements that are self-laudatory, so long as they are not untruthful or misleading. D is incorrect. The attorney’s listing of his medical degree is not done in a way that indicates any specialty or limitation on his practice.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. In advertising his or her practice, an attorney may not make false or misleading statements. Model Rule 7.1. As long as none of the information regarding their degrees is false, the attorneys are free to list their degrees in their advertisement. A is incorrect. Although both law and medicine are licensed professions, this would not make the statement proper if the attorneys were lying about their licenses. C is incorrect. The Rules do not forbid an attorney from making statements that are self-laudatory, so long as they are not untruthful or misleading. D is incorrect. The attorney’s listing of his medical degree is not done in a way that indicates any specialty or limitation on his practice.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[153],"sourceAnswerPages":[256],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"以下广告刊登在一份日报上；甲和乙均为该州律师协会成员：“甲，医学博士、法学博士；乙，法学博士；执业律师；A 街 1000 号，某市、某州，邮编 00000；电话 (555) 555-5555。”甲和乙是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为法律和医学均属于须取得执照的专业。","B":"否，因为他们确实拥有广告所列学位。","C":"是，因为提及医学博士学位属于自我吹捧。","D":"是，除非他们把业务限制在与医学学位有关的领域。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。律师为其业务做广告时，不得作出虚假或具有误导性的陈述。《职业行为示范规则》7.1。只要有关学位的信息并非虚假，律师就可以在广告中列出自己的学位。A 不正确。法律和医学虽然都属于须取得执照的专业，但如果律师谎报执照，这一点也不会使陈述适当。C 不正确。只要陈述并非虚假或具有误导性，规则并不禁止律师作出自我赞扬的陈述。D 不正确。律师列出自己的医学学位时，并未采用暗示其具有某一专业特长或限制其执业范围的方式。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-038","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":38,"topic":"Model Rule 1.1","question":"While working on a complex matter for her client, an attorney, a partner in a law firm, identified a particularly difficult issue of law that could prove decisive in the dispute. The attorney had not encountered this issue before and was uncertain of its effect. The attorney asked her partner for assistance. The attorney planned on including the consultation cost in her fee, and she did not identify the client to her partner. Was it proper for the attorney to consult with her partner?","choices":{"A":"No, because the total fee is increased by the consultation.","B":"No, because the client’s consent was not previously obtained.","C":"Yes, because the attorney did not identify the client to her partner.","D":"Yes, because the attorney and her partner are partners in the same firm."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. In order to provide competent representation as required by Model Rule 1.1, it is appropriate for the attorney to ask her partner for assistance since they are partners in the same firm. A is incorrect. In order to obtain competence in this matter, it was appropriate for the attorney to consult with her partner even if it did increase the total fee. In fact, there is nothing different in doing this as opposed to charging more because more research in books or online was necessary. B is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.1, Comment [6], client consent becomes necessary when the lawyer retains or contracts with other lawyers outside the lawyer’s own firm. Here, the attorney is conferring with her own partner, so no client consent is necessary. C is incorrect. An attorney is allowed to consult with members of his or her own firm, whether or not he or she reveals the identity of the client.","rule":"D is the correct answer. In order to provide competent representation as required by Model Rule 1.1, it is appropriate for the attorney to ask her partner for assistance since they are partners in the same firm. A is incorrect. In order to obtain competence in this matter, it was appropriate for the attorney to consult with her partner even if it did increase the total fee. In fact, there is nothing different in doing this as opposed to charging more because more research in books or online was necessary. B is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.1, Comment [6], client consent becomes necessary when the lawyer retains or contracts with other lawyers outside the lawyer’s own firm. Here, the attorney is conferring with her own partner, so no client consent is necessary. C is incorrect. An attorney is allowed to consult with members of his or her own firm, whether or not he or she reveals the identity of the client.","irac":{"issue":"Was it proper for the attorney to consult with her partner?","rule":"D is the correct answer. In order to provide competent representation as required by Model Rule 1.1, it is appropriate for the attorney to ask her partner for assistance since they are partners in the same firm. A is incorrect. In order to obtain competence in this matter, it was appropriate for the attorney to consult with her partner even if it did increase the total fee. In fact, there is nothing different in doing this as opposed to charging more because more research in books or online was necessary. B is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.1, Comment [6], client consent becomes necessary when the lawyer retains or contracts with other lawyers outside the lawyer’s own firm. Here, the attorney is conferring with her own partner, so no client consent is necessary. C is incorrect. An attorney is allowed to consult with members of his or her own firm, whether or not he or she reveals the identity of the client.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. In order to provide competent representation as required by Model Rule 1.1, it is appropriate for the attorney to ask her partner for assistance since they are partners in the same firm. A is incorrect. In order to obtain competence in this matter, it was appropriate for the attorney to consult with her partner even if it did increase the total fee. In fact, there is nothing different in doing this as opposed to charging more because more research in books or online was necessary. B is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.1, Comment [6], client consent becomes necessary when the lawyer retains or contracts with other lawyers outside the lawyer’s own firm. Here, the attorney is conferring with her own partner, so no client consent is necessary. C is incorrect. An attorney is allowed to consult with members of his or her own firm, whether or not he or she reveals the identity of the client.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[153,154],"sourceAnswerPages":[256],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师是某律师事务所的合伙人。在为委托人办理一项复杂事务时，她发现一个特别棘手的法律问题，而该问题可能对争议具有决定作用。律师此前从未遇到这个问题，不确定它会产生何种影响，于是请求自己的合伙人协助。律师计划将咨询成本计入收费，也没有向合伙人透露委托人身份。律师向合伙人咨询是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"不适当，因为咨询增加了总费用。","B":"不适当，因为没有事先取得委托人同意。","C":"适当，因为律师没有向合伙人透露委托人身份。","D":"适当，因为该律师与受咨询律师是同一事务所的合伙人。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。为按照《职业行为示范规则》1.1 的要求提供称职代理，该律师请求同一事务所的合伙人协助是适当的。A 不正确。即使咨询会增加总费用，为在本事项中达到称职要求，律师仍可适当咨询合伙人。事实上，这与因为需要查阅更多书籍或进行更多在线研究而增加收费并无不同。B 不正确。根据规则 1.1 评注 [6]，律师聘请或约请本事务所以外的其他律师时，才需要取得委托人同意。本题律师只是咨询自己的合伙人，因此无需委托人同意。C 不正确。无论是否透露委托人身份，律师均可咨询同一事务所的成员。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-039","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":39,"topic":"Rule 2.11(A) / Rule 2.11(C)","question":"A state court judge has presided over the pretrial proceedings in a case involving a novel contract question under the Uniform Commercial Code. During the pretrial proceedings, the judge has acquired considerable background knowledge of the facts and law of the matter and, therefore, is particularly well qualified to preside at the trial. Shortly before the trial date, the judge discovered that his brother owns a substantial block of stock in the defendant corporation. He determined that his brother’s financial interests would be substantially affected by the outcome of the case. Although the judge believed he would be impartial, he disclosed to the parties, on the record, his brother’s interest. Is it proper for the judge to hear the case?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the judge is particularly well qualified to preside at the trial.","B":"Yes, because the judge believes his judgment will not be affected by his brother’s stockholding.","C":"No, because disqualification based on a relative’s financial interest cannot be waived.","D":"No, because there have not yet been proceedings in which the judge did not participate, where all parties and their lawyers consent in writing that the judge may hear the case."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(A), a judge should disqualify himself or herself in any proceeding where the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned. However, as long as the judge does not have a personal bias or prejudice concerning a party or a party’s lawyer, or personal knowledge of facts that are in dispute in the proceeding, the judge “may disclose on the record the basis of the judge’s disqualification and may ask the parties and their lawyers to consider, outside the presence of the judge and court personnel, whether to waive disqualification. If, following the disclosure, the parties and lawyers agree, without participation by the judge or court personnel, that the judge should not be disqualified, the judge may participate in the proceeding.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(C). If such proceedings are held, the judge may properly hear the case. A is incorrect. Although the judge may be particularly well qualified, the issue is that his impartiality could reasonably be questioned because of his brother’s financial interest in the case. B is incorrect. The question in this situation is not what the judge himself or herself believes, but whether that impartiality could reasonably be questioned by someone else. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(A). C is incorrect. As stated above, if proceedings are held and the parties and lawyers agree to the judge hearing the case, the judge may properly do so.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(A), a judge should disqualify himself or herself in any proceeding where the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned. However, as long as the judge does not have a personal bias or prejudice concerning a party or a party’s lawyer, or personal knowledge of facts that are in dispute in the proceeding, the judge “may disclose on the record the basis of the judge’s disqualification and may ask the parties and their lawyers to consider, outside the presence of the judge and court personnel, whether to waive disqualification. If, following the disclosure, the parties and lawyers agree, without participation by the judge or court personnel, that the judge should not be disqualified, the judge may participate in the proceeding.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(C). If such proceedings are held, the judge may properly hear the case. A is incorrect. Although the judge may be particularly well qualified, the issue is that his impartiality could reasonably be questioned because of his brother’s financial interest in the case. B is incorrect. The question in this situation is not what the judge himself or herself believes, but whether that impartiality could reasonably be questioned by someone else. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(A). C is incorrect. As stated above, if proceedings are held and the parties and lawyers agree to the judge hearing the case, the judge may properly do so.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the judge to hear the case?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(A), a judge should disqualify himself or herself in any proceeding where the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned. However, as long as the judge does not have a personal bias or prejudice concerning a party or a party’s lawyer, or personal knowledge of facts that are in dispute in the proceeding, the judge “may disclose on the record the basis of the judge’s disqualification and may ask the parties and their lawyers to consider, outside the presence of the judge and court personnel, whether to waive disqualification. If, following the disclosure, the parties and lawyers agree, without participation by the judge or court personnel, that the judge should not be disqualified, the judge may participate in the proceeding.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(C). If such proceedings are held, the judge may properly hear the case. A is incorrect. Although the judge may be particularly well qualified, the issue is that his impartiality could reasonably be questioned because of his brother’s financial interest in the case. B is incorrect. The question in this situation is not what the judge himself or herself believes, but whether that impartiality could reasonably be questioned by someone else. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(A). C is incorrect. As stated above, if proceedings are held and the parties and lawyers agree to the judge hearing the case, the judge may properly do so.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(A), a judge should disqualify himself or herself in any proceeding where the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned. However, as long as the judge does not have a personal bias or prejudice concerning a party or a party’s lawyer, or personal knowledge of facts that are in dispute in the proceeding, the judge “may disclose on the record the basis of the judge’s disqualification and may ask the parties and their lawyers to consider, outside the presence of the judge and court personnel, whether to waive disqualification. If, following the disclosure, the parties and lawyers agree, without participation by the judge or court personnel, that the judge should not be disqualified, the judge may participate in the proceeding.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(C). If such proceedings are held, the judge may properly hear the case. A is incorrect. Although the judge may be particularly well qualified, the issue is that his impartiality could reasonably be questioned because of his brother’s financial interest in the case. B is incorrect. The question in this situation is not what the judge himself or herself believes, but whether that impartiality could reasonably be questioned by someone else. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(A). C is incorrect. As stated above, if proceedings are held and the parties and lawyers agree to the judge hearing the case, the judge may properly do so.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 2.11(A)","Rule 2.11(C)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[154],"sourceAnswerPages":[257],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名州法院法官主持了一宗涉及《统一商法典》下新颖合同问题之案件的审前程序。在此过程中，法官已对案件事实和法律背景有相当深入的了解，因此特别适合主持审判。审判日期前不久，法官发现自己的兄弟持有被告公司的大量股份。他认定，兄弟的经济利益会受到案件结果的重大影响。法官虽然相信自己能够保持公正，仍在记录中向各方披露了兄弟的利益。该法官审理本案是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为法官特别适合主持审判。","B":"适当，因为法官相信自己的判断不会受到兄弟持股的影响。","C":"不适当，因为基于亲属经济利益的回避事由不能放弃。","D":"不适当，因为尚未在法官不参与的程序中，由所有当事人及其律师书面同意该法官可以审理本案。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。根据《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.11(A)，在法官公正性可能受到合理质疑的任何程序中，法官都应回避。不过，只要法官不存在针对一方或其律师的个人偏见，也不实际知悉程序中有争议的事实，法官就“可以在记录中披露其回避依据，并可要求当事人及其律师在法官和法院人员不在场时考虑是否放弃回避。如果披露后，当事人和律师在法官或法院人员均不参与的情况下同意不应让法官回避，法官就可以参与该程序。”规则 2.11(C)。如果举行了这种程序，法官即可适当审理案件。A 不正确。该法官虽可能特别适合，但由于其兄弟在案件中拥有经济利益，其他人可以合理地质疑法官公正性。B 不正确。在这种情况下，问题不在于法官本人相信什么，而在于其他人是否可以合理地质疑其公正性。规则 2.11(A)。C 不正确。如上所述，如果举行了相关程序，而且当事人和律师同意由该法官审理，他就可以适当这样做。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-040","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":40,"topic":"Model Rule 3.3(a)(3)","question":"An attorney is representing a client on a charge of armed robbery. The client claims that the prosecution witness is mistaken in her identification. The client has produced a witness who will testify that the client was in another city 500 miles away when the robbery occurred. The attorney knows that the witness is lying, but the client insists that the witness be called on the client’s behalf. The attorney is not planning on relying on the alibi defense in her argument before the jury. Is the attorney subject to discipline if she calls the witness?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because before calling the witness, the attorney did not inform the court of her belief.","B":"Yes, because the attorney knows the witness will be testifying falsely.","C":"No, because the attorney will not rely on the alibi defense in her argument before the jury.","D":"No, because the client has insisted that the witness be called as a witness on the client’s behalf."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not knowingly . . . offer evidence that the lawyer knows to be false.” Model Rule 3.3(a)(3). Because the attorney knows the witness is lying, she cannot offer the witness’s testimony in court. A is incorrect. Even if the attorney informed the court of her belief, it would not excuse her actions if she later offered the testimony of a witness who she knows is lying. C is incorrect. The Rule states that the attorney may not offer false evidence. Importantly, the Rule does not allow the attorney to do so as long the attorney refrains from mentioning the false evidence during her argument before the jury. D is incorrect. “A lawyer may refuse to offer evidence, other than the testimony of a defendant in a criminal matter, that the lawyer reasonably believes is false.” Model Rule 3.3(a)(3). The client cannot force the lawyer to offer such evidence.","rule":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not knowingly . . . offer evidence that the lawyer knows to be false.” Model Rule 3.3(a)(3). Because the attorney knows the witness is lying, she cannot offer the witness’s testimony in court. A is incorrect. Even if the attorney informed the court of her belief, it would not excuse her actions if she later offered the testimony of a witness who she knows is lying. C is incorrect. The Rule states that the attorney may not offer false evidence. Importantly, the Rule does not allow the attorney to do so as long the attorney refrains from mentioning the false evidence during her argument before the jury. D is incorrect. “A lawyer may refuse to offer evidence, other than the testimony of a defendant in a criminal matter, that the lawyer reasonably believes is false.” Model Rule 3.3(a)(3). The client cannot force the lawyer to offer such evidence.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline if she calls the witness?","rule":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not knowingly . . . offer evidence that the lawyer knows to be false.” Model Rule 3.3(a)(3). Because the attorney knows the witness is lying, she cannot offer the witness’s testimony in court. A is incorrect. Even if the attorney informed the court of her belief, it would not excuse her actions if she later offered the testimony of a witness who she knows is lying. C is incorrect. The Rule states that the attorney may not offer false evidence. Importantly, the Rule does not allow the attorney to do so as long the attorney refrains from mentioning the false evidence during her argument before the jury. D is incorrect. “A lawyer may refuse to offer evidence, other than the testimony of a defendant in a criminal matter, that the lawyer reasonably believes is false.” Model Rule 3.3(a)(3). The client cannot force the lawyer to offer such evidence.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not knowingly . . . offer evidence that the lawyer knows to be false.” Model Rule 3.3(a)(3). Because the attorney knows the witness is lying, she cannot offer the witness’s testimony in court. A is incorrect. Even if the attorney informed the court of her belief, it would not excuse her actions if she later offered the testimony of a witness who she knows is lying. C is incorrect. The Rule states that the attorney may not offer false evidence. Importantly, the Rule does not allow the attorney to do so as long the attorney refrains from mentioning the false evidence during her argument before the jury. D is incorrect. “A lawyer may refuse to offer evidence, other than the testimony of a defendant in a criminal matter, that the lawyer reasonably believes is false.” Model Rule 3.3(a)(3). The client cannot force the lawyer to offer such evidence.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.3(a)(3)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[154,155],"sourceAnswerPages":[257,258],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理一名被控持械抢劫的委托人。委托人声称控方证人指认错误。委托人找来一名证人，该证人将作证说，抢劫发生时委托人身处 500 英里以外的另一座城市。律师知道该证人在说谎，但委托人坚持要求律师代表自己传唤该证人。律师不计划在向陪审团陈述时依赖不在场证明抗辩。如果律师传唤该证人，是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师在传唤证人前没有将自己的判断告知法院。","B":"是，因为律师知道该证人将作虚假证言。","C":"否，因为律师不会在向陪审团陈述时依赖不在场证明抗辩。","D":"否，因为委托人坚持要求代表自己传唤该证人。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。“律师不得明知而……提出律师明知虚假的证据。”《职业行为示范规则》3.3(a)(3)。由于律师知道该证人在说谎，不得在法院提出其证言。A 不正确。即使律师将自己的判断告知法院，如果后来仍提出明知说谎之证人的证言，也不能使其行为得到免责。C 不正确。规则规定律师不得提出虚假证据。尤其是，规则并不允许律师在向陪审团陈述时不提及虚假证据，就可以先行提出该证据。D 不正确。“律师可以拒绝提出其合理地认为虚假的证据，但刑事案件中被告本人的证言除外。”规则 3.3(a)(3)。委托人不能强迫律师提出这种证据。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-041","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":41,"topic":"Model Rule 7.1","question":"A recently graduated attorney began a plaintiffs’ personal injury practice, but was having a difficult time attracting clients. The attorney hired an advertising agency to prepare a television commercial in which the attorney appeared to be arguing a case before a jury. In the commercial, the jury brought back a large award for the attorney’s client. The voice-over stated that results would vary depending upon particular legal and factual circumstances. The attorney’s only experience at the time the commercial was filmed was in moot court. As a result of airing the commercial, the attorney received several significant cases. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the commercial created an unjustified expectation about the results that could be achieved in court.","B":"Yes, because the commercial implied that the attorney had successfully argued a case to a jury.","C":"No, because commercial speech is protected under the First Amendment.","D":"No, because the commercial contained an express disclaimer about the results a client could expect."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.1, “[a] lawyer shall not make a false or misleading communication about the lawyer or the lawyer’s services. A communication is false or misleading if it contains a material misrepresentation of fact or law, or omits a fact necessary to make the statement considered as a whole not materially misleading.” Here, the commercial is misleading in that it implies the young attorney has argued a case in front of a jury, when he has only done so in moot court. A is incorrect. Model Rule 7.1, Comment [3] states that it is improper for a lawyer to make an advertisement that would lead a reasonable person to form an unjustified expectation that the lawyer will reach the same results in his or her case. Here, however, the larger issue is that the commercial is misleading in that it leads a viewer to believe the lawyer has appeared in court before a jury, when in fact he has not done so. C is incorrect. A lawyer’s commercial speech is regulated by the Rules. This answer choice should be immediately discarded because it implies a lawyer can say anything he or she wants in a commercial due to the protections of the First Amendment. D is incorrect. While there was a disclaimer regarding the results, the commercial still misled viewers into believing the attorney had argued a case before a jury.","rule":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.1, “[a] lawyer shall not make a false or misleading communication about the lawyer or the lawyer’s services. A communication is false or misleading if it contains a material misrepresentation of fact or law, or omits a fact necessary to make the statement considered as a whole not materially misleading.” Here, the commercial is misleading in that it implies the young attorney has argued a case in front of a jury, when he has only done so in moot court. A is incorrect. Model Rule 7.1, Comment [3] states that it is improper for a lawyer to make an advertisement that would lead a reasonable person to form an unjustified expectation that the lawyer will reach the same results in his or her case. Here, however, the larger issue is that the commercial is misleading in that it leads a viewer to believe the lawyer has appeared in court before a jury, when in fact he has not done so. C is incorrect. A lawyer’s commercial speech is regulated by the Rules. This answer choice should be immediately discarded because it implies a lawyer can say anything he or she wants in a commercial due to the protections of the First Amendment. D is incorrect. While there was a disclaimer regarding the results, the commercial still misled viewers into believing the attorney had argued a case before a jury.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.1, “[a] lawyer shall not make a false or misleading communication about the lawyer or the lawyer’s services. A communication is false or misleading if it contains a material misrepresentation of fact or law, or omits a fact necessary to make the statement considered as a whole not materially misleading.” Here, the commercial is misleading in that it implies the young attorney has argued a case in front of a jury, when he has only done so in moot court. A is incorrect. Model Rule 7.1, Comment [3] states that it is improper for a lawyer to make an advertisement that would lead a reasonable person to form an unjustified expectation that the lawyer will reach the same results in his or her case. Here, however, the larger issue is that the commercial is misleading in that it leads a viewer to believe the lawyer has appeared in court before a jury, when in fact he has not done so. C is incorrect. A lawyer’s commercial speech is regulated by the Rules. This answer choice should be immediately discarded because it implies a lawyer can say anything he or she wants in a commercial due to the protections of the First Amendment. D is incorrect. While there was a disclaimer regarding the results, the commercial still misled viewers into believing the attorney had argued a case before a jury.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.1, “[a] lawyer shall not make a false or misleading communication about the lawyer or the lawyer’s services. A communication is false or misleading if it contains a material misrepresentation of fact or law, or omits a fact necessary to make the statement considered as a whole not materially misleading.” Here, the commercial is misleading in that it implies the young attorney has argued a case in front of a jury, when he has only done so in moot court. A is incorrect. Model Rule 7.1, Comment [3] states that it is improper for a lawyer to make an advertisement that would lead a reasonable person to form an unjustified expectation that the lawyer will reach the same results in his or her case. Here, however, the larger issue is that the commercial is misleading in that it leads a viewer to believe the lawyer has appeared in court before a jury, when in fact he has not done so. C is incorrect. A lawyer’s commercial speech is regulated by the Rules. This answer choice should be immediately discarded because it implies a lawyer can say anything he or she wants in a commercial due to the protections of the First Amendment. D is incorrect. While there was a disclaimer regarding the results, the commercial still misled viewers into believing the attorney had argued a case before a jury.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[155],"sourceAnswerPages":[258],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名刚毕业的律师开始从事原告方人身伤害业务，但很难吸引委托人。他聘请一家广告公司制作电视广告；广告中，该律师看似正在陪审团面前为案件辩论，并由陪审团向其委托人裁定一笔巨额赔偿。旁白说明，具体结果会因不同法律和事实情形而异。拍摄广告时，该律师唯一的相关经历是在模拟法庭。广告播出后，律师取得了几宗重大案件。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为广告使人对法院可能取得的结果产生没有正当依据的期待。","B":"是，因为广告暗示该律师曾成功在陪审团面前为案件辩论。","C":"否，因为商业言论受到《第一修正案》保护。","D":"否，因为广告明确声明委托人可以期待的结果可能不同。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。根据《职业行为示范规则》7.1，“律师不得就律师本人或律师服务作出虚假或具有误导性的沟通。如果一项沟通包含对事实或法律的重大虚假陈述，或遗漏了为使整体陈述不产生重大误导所必需的事实，该沟通就是虚假或具有误导性的。”本题中，广告暗示这名年轻律师曾在陪审团面前为案件辩论，而他事实上只有模拟法庭经历，因此具有误导性。A 不正确。规则 7.1 评注 [3] 指出，律师发布会使理性人产生没有正当依据的期待，认为律师将在其案件中取得相同结果的广告，是不适当的。不过，本题更严重的问题是，广告使观众相信该律师曾在陪审团面前出庭，而事实并非如此。C 不正确。律师的商业言论受到规则约束。该选项暗示律师因《第一修正案》保护就可以在商业广告中随意陈述，因而应立即排除。D 不正确。虽然广告对结果作出声明，仍然误导观众，使人相信律师曾在陪审团面前为案件辩论。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-042","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":42,"topic":"Rule 3.7(A) / Rule 3.12","question":"The state bar association has offered a judge and her spouse free transportation and lodging to attend its institute on judicial reform. The judge is expected to deliver a banquet speech. Members of the bar association regularly appear in the judge’s court. The value of the transportation and lodging is less than $500. Is it proper for the judge to accept this offer?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the value of the transportation and lodging does not exceed $500.","B":"Yes, because the activity is devoted to the improvement of law.","C":"No, because members of the bar association regularly appear in the judge’s court.","D":"No, because the bar association is offering free transportation to the judge’s spouse."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. “ . . . [A] judge may participate in activities sponsored by organizations concerned with the law, the legal system, or the administration of justice.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.7(A). Additionally, a judge may accept reasonable compensation for these extrajudicial activities unless such acceptance would appear to undermine the judge’s impartiality. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.12. Here, since the judge is engaging in an educational activity devoted to the improvement of law, her actions are proper. A is incorrect. There is no set limit on the amount a judge may receive for proper extrajudicial activities. The only limitation is that the compensation cannot be so large as to appear to undermine the judge’s impartiality. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.12. C is incorrect. Although members of the bar association will necessarily appear in the judge’s court, nothing in this situation would lead a reasonable person to believe that the association members are trying to buy the judge’s impartiality. D is incorrect. So long as there is no appearance of impropriety, there is no restriction on the bar association also offering free transportation to the judge’s spouse.","rule":"B is the correct answer. “ . . . [A] judge may participate in activities sponsored by organizations concerned with the law, the legal system, or the administration of justice.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.7(A). Additionally, a judge may accept reasonable compensation for these extrajudicial activities unless such acceptance would appear to undermine the judge’s impartiality. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.12. Here, since the judge is engaging in an educational activity devoted to the improvement of law, her actions are proper. A is incorrect. There is no set limit on the amount a judge may receive for proper extrajudicial activities. The only limitation is that the compensation cannot be so large as to appear to undermine the judge’s impartiality. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.12. C is incorrect. Although members of the bar association will necessarily appear in the judge’s court, nothing in this situation would lead a reasonable person to believe that the association members are trying to buy the judge’s impartiality. D is incorrect. So long as there is no appearance of impropriety, there is no restriction on the bar association also offering free transportation to the judge’s spouse.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the judge to accept this offer?","rule":"B is the correct answer. “ . . . [A] judge may participate in activities sponsored by organizations concerned with the law, the legal system, or the administration of justice.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.7(A). Additionally, a judge may accept reasonable compensation for these extrajudicial activities unless such acceptance would appear to undermine the judge’s impartiality. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.12. Here, since the judge is engaging in an educational activity devoted to the improvement of law, her actions are proper. A is incorrect. There is no set limit on the amount a judge may receive for proper extrajudicial activities. The only limitation is that the compensation cannot be so large as to appear to undermine the judge’s impartiality. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.12. C is incorrect. Although members of the bar association will necessarily appear in the judge’s court, nothing in this situation would lead a reasonable person to believe that the association members are trying to buy the judge’s impartiality. D is incorrect. So long as there is no appearance of impropriety, there is no restriction on the bar association also offering free transportation to the judge’s spouse.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. “ . . . [A] judge may participate in activities sponsored by organizations concerned with the law, the legal system, or the administration of justice.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.7(A). Additionally, a judge may accept reasonable compensation for these extrajudicial activities unless such acceptance would appear to undermine the judge’s impartiality. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.12. Here, since the judge is engaging in an educational activity devoted to the improvement of law, her actions are proper. A is incorrect. There is no set limit on the amount a judge may receive for proper extrajudicial activities. The only limitation is that the compensation cannot be so large as to appear to undermine the judge’s impartiality. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.12. C is incorrect. Although members of the bar association will necessarily appear in the judge’s court, nothing in this situation would lead a reasonable person to believe that the association members are trying to buy the judge’s impartiality. D is incorrect. So long as there is no appearance of impropriety, there is no restriction on the bar association also offering free transportation to the judge’s spouse.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 3.7(A)","Rule 3.12"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[155,156],"sourceAnswerPages":[258,259],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"州律师协会向一名法官及其配偶提供免费交通和住宿，邀请二人参加协会举办的司法改革研修会。法官预计会在宴会上发表演讲。律师协会成员经常在该法官所在法院出庭。交通和住宿价值低于 500 美元。法官接受该邀请是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为交通和住宿价值不超过 500 美元。","B":"适当，因为该活动致力于改进法律。","C":"不适当，因为律师协会成员经常在法官所在法院出庭。","D":"不适当，因为律师协会也向法官配偶提供免费交通。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。“……法官可以参加由关注法律、法律制度或司法运行之组织举办的活动。”《司法行为示范准则》规则 3.7(A)。此外，法官可以为此类司法职务外活动接受合理报酬，除非接受报酬会造成削弱法官公正性的观感。规则 3.12。本题中，法官参与的是一项致力于改进法律的教育活动，因此行为适当。A 不正确。法官可就适当的司法职务外活动获得的金额没有固定上限，唯一限制是报酬不得高到造成削弱法官公正性的观感。规则 3.12。C 不正确。律师协会成员虽必然会在法官所在法院出庭，但本题没有任何情况会使理性人相信协会成员正在试图收买法官的公正。D 不正确。只要没有造成不当观感，律师协会也向法官配偶提供免费交通不受限制。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-043","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":43,"topic":"Model Rule 8.3(b)","question":"An attorney regularly appears before a trial court judge who is running for reelection in six months. Over the past year, the attorney has noticed that the judge has become increasingly ill tempered on the bench. Not only is the judge abrupt and critical of lawyers appearing before him, he is also rude and abusive to litigants. On more than one occasion, the judge has thrown his gavel across the courtroom in a fit of temper. The judge’s conduct on the bench is often the subject of discussion whenever a group of lawyers meets. Some lawyers are automatically filing requests for judicial substitution whenever a case in which they are to appear is assigned to the judge. The attorney discussed the matter with her law partners, who rarely make court appearances. The attorney’s law partners suggested that she also file a request for judicial substitution whenever one of her cases is assigned to the judge. In addition, the attorney and her law partners discussed the possibility of reporting the judge to the appropriate disciplinary authority but are concerned that this would alienate the other judges to whom their cases are assigned. The attorney has reluctantly started filing for substitution of the judge in every one of her cases to which the judge is assigned, but she has taken no further action. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney failed to inform the appropriate authorities about the judge’s conduct.","B":"Yes, because, by filing automatic requests for substitution of the judge, the attorney undermined public confidence in the administration of justice.","C":"No, because the attorney has a duty to represent her clients zealously.","D":"No, because the judge is running for reelection and may not be reelected."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer who knows that a judge has committed a violation of applicable rules of judicial conduct that raises a substantial question as to the judge’s fitness for office shall inform the appropriate authority.” Model Rule 8.3(b). Since the judge is clearly behaving inappropriately, the attorney has a duty to report it. B is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney is failing to report the misconduct. The correct answer choice should be fairly clear in that if every attorney simply filed automatic requests for substitution, the judge would continue to remain on the bench indefinitely with no investigation of his conduct. Consequently, only attorneys who are new to the court would end up in front of him, presumably to the disadvantage of their clients. C is incorrect. Reporting the misconduct has nothing to do with zealous representation. This answer choice implies that zealous representation only occurs so long as the attorney is very careful not to upset a judge. D is incorrect. This answer choice implies that the attorney can properly bury her head in the sand and just hope the judge goes away.","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer who knows that a judge has committed a violation of applicable rules of judicial conduct that raises a substantial question as to the judge’s fitness for office shall inform the appropriate authority.” Model Rule 8.3(b). Since the judge is clearly behaving inappropriately, the attorney has a duty to report it. B is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney is failing to report the misconduct. The correct answer choice should be fairly clear in that if every attorney simply filed automatic requests for substitution, the judge would continue to remain on the bench indefinitely with no investigation of his conduct. Consequently, only attorneys who are new to the court would end up in front of him, presumably to the disadvantage of their clients. C is incorrect. Reporting the misconduct has nothing to do with zealous representation. This answer choice implies that zealous representation only occurs so long as the attorney is very careful not to upset a judge. D is incorrect. This answer choice implies that the attorney can properly bury her head in the sand and just hope the judge goes away.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer who knows that a judge has committed a violation of applicable rules of judicial conduct that raises a substantial question as to the judge’s fitness for office shall inform the appropriate authority.” Model Rule 8.3(b). Since the judge is clearly behaving inappropriately, the attorney has a duty to report it. B is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney is failing to report the misconduct. The correct answer choice should be fairly clear in that if every attorney simply filed automatic requests for substitution, the judge would continue to remain on the bench indefinitely with no investigation of his conduct. Consequently, only attorneys who are new to the court would end up in front of him, presumably to the disadvantage of their clients. C is incorrect. Reporting the misconduct has nothing to do with zealous representation. This answer choice implies that zealous representation only occurs so long as the attorney is very careful not to upset a judge. D is incorrect. This answer choice implies that the attorney can properly bury her head in the sand and just hope the judge goes away.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer who knows that a judge has committed a violation of applicable rules of judicial conduct that raises a substantial question as to the judge’s fitness for office shall inform the appropriate authority.” Model Rule 8.3(b). Since the judge is clearly behaving inappropriately, the attorney has a duty to report it. B is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney is failing to report the misconduct. The correct answer choice should be fairly clear in that if every attorney simply filed automatic requests for substitution, the judge would continue to remain on the bench indefinitely with no investigation of his conduct. Consequently, only attorneys who are new to the court would end up in front of him, presumably to the disadvantage of their clients. C is incorrect. Reporting the misconduct has nothing to do with zealous representation. This answer choice implies that zealous representation only occurs so long as the attorney is very careful not to upset a judge. D is incorrect. This answer choice implies that the attorney can properly bury her head in the sand and just hope the judge goes away.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 8.3(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[156,157],"sourceAnswerPages":[259],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师经常在一名将于六个月后竞选连任的初审法官面前出庭。过去一年中，律师注意到法官在法官席上的脾气越来越暴躁。法官不仅对出庭律师态度生硬、频繁批评，也对诉讼当事人粗鲁且出言辱骂。法官还不止一次在暴怒中将法槌扔过法庭。每当一群律师聚会时，法官在法官席上的行为经常成为讨论话题。一些律师只要自己的案件被分配给该法官，就会自动申请更换法官。该律师与很少出庭的事务所合伙人讨论此事。合伙人建议，只要其案件分配给该法官，她也应申请更换。此外，该律师与合伙人讨论了向适当纪律机关举报法官的可能性，但担心这样做会疏远其他受理其案件的法官。该律师不情愿地开始在所有被分配给该法官的案件中申请更换法官，但未采取进一步行动。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该律师没有将法官行为告知适当机关。","B":"是，因为律师自动申请更换法官，损害了公众对司法运行的信心。","C":"否，因为律师有义务热忱代理委托人。","D":"否，因为该法官正在竞选连任，可能不会再次当选。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。“律师知道法官违反适用司法行为规则，而且该违反会对法官是否适任产生重大疑问时，应通知适当机关。”《职业行为示范规则》8.3(b)。该法官显然行为不当，因此律师有义务举报。B 不正确。本题问题在于律师没有举报不当行为。正确答案应当相当明显：如果每名律师都只是自动申请更换法官，该法官就会无限期留任，其行为也不会受到调查。最终，只有刚来到该法院的律师会在他面前出庭，推定这会损害这些律师的委托人。C 不正确。举报不当行为与热忱代理无关。该选项暗示，律师只有在十分小心、不让法官不快时才是在热忱代理。D 不正确。该选项暗示律师可以把头埋进沙里，指望法官自己离开。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-044","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":44,"topic":"Model Rule 1.1 / Model Rule 1.8(h)(1)","question":"Leaving an airport, an attorney who primarily practices criminal law shared a cab with a medical doctor. The cab was involved in a collision, and the doctor was seriously injured, while the attorney was only shaken up. The attorney accompanied the doctor to the hospital in the ambulance. The doctor believed that she was dying and asked the attorney to prepare a simple will for her. The attorney told the doctor, “I have never prepared a will, but hope that I can remember the basics from law school.” The attorney then complied with the doctor’s request. The doctor signed the will, and the two paramedics in the ambulance signed as witnesses. The will was ultimately found valid. Was it proper for the attorney to prepare the will?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney did not omit some required formality that rendered the will invalid.","B":"Yes, because the attorney provided legal services that were reasonably necessary under the circumstances.","C":"No, because the doctor did not waive the attorney’s malpractice liability.","D":"No, because the attorney did not have the skill required for the representation."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. “In an emergency a lawyer may give advice or assistance in a matter in which the lawyer does not have the skill ordinarily required.” Model Rule 1.1, Comment [3]. Consequently, although the attorney does not have the general competency to write a will, the emergency situation caused by the accident allows the attorney to try to undertake the work without violating the Rule. A is incorrect. The fact of the emergency makes the attorney’s actions proper. Even if he did a good job on the will, he would still violate the Rules regarding competency if this was not an emergency situation. This is a variation of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. C is incorrect. A lawyer may not “make an agreement prospectively limiting the lawyer’s liability to a client for malpractice unless the client is independently represented in making the agreement.” Model Rule 1.8(h)(1). Consequently, if the attorney had in fact gotten the doctor to waive the attorney’s malpractice liability, he would be violating the Rules. D is incorrect. Even though the attorney did not have the required competence, the emergency situation made his actions proper under the Rules.","rule":"B is the correct answer. “In an emergency a lawyer may give advice or assistance in a matter in which the lawyer does not have the skill ordinarily required.” Model Rule 1.1, Comment [3]. Consequently, although the attorney does not have the general competency to write a will, the emergency situation caused by the accident allows the attorney to try to undertake the work without violating the Rule. A is incorrect. The fact of the emergency makes the attorney’s actions proper. Even if he did a good job on the will, he would still violate the Rules regarding competency if this was not an emergency situation. This is a variation of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. C is incorrect. A lawyer may not “make an agreement prospectively limiting the lawyer’s liability to a client for malpractice unless the client is independently represented in making the agreement.” Model Rule 1.8(h)(1). Consequently, if the attorney had in fact gotten the doctor to waive the attorney’s malpractice liability, he would be violating the Rules. D is incorrect. Even though the attorney did not have the required competence, the emergency situation made his actions proper under the Rules.","irac":{"issue":"Was it proper for the attorney to prepare the will?","rule":"B is the correct answer. “In an emergency a lawyer may give advice or assistance in a matter in which the lawyer does not have the skill ordinarily required.” Model Rule 1.1, Comment [3]. Consequently, although the attorney does not have the general competency to write a will, the emergency situation caused by the accident allows the attorney to try to undertake the work without violating the Rule. A is incorrect. The fact of the emergency makes the attorney’s actions proper. Even if he did a good job on the will, he would still violate the Rules regarding competency if this was not an emergency situation. This is a variation of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. C is incorrect. A lawyer may not “make an agreement prospectively limiting the lawyer’s liability to a client for malpractice unless the client is independently represented in making the agreement.” Model Rule 1.8(h)(1). Consequently, if the attorney had in fact gotten the doctor to waive the attorney’s malpractice liability, he would be violating the Rules. D is incorrect. Even though the attorney did not have the required competence, the emergency situation made his actions proper under the Rules.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. “In an emergency a lawyer may give advice or assistance in a matter in which the lawyer does not have the skill ordinarily required.” Model Rule 1.1, Comment [3]. Consequently, although the attorney does not have the general competency to write a will, the emergency situation caused by the accident allows the attorney to try to undertake the work without violating the Rule. A is incorrect. The fact of the emergency makes the attorney’s actions proper. Even if he did a good job on the will, he would still violate the Rules regarding competency if this was not an emergency situation. This is a variation of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. C is incorrect. A lawyer may not “make an agreement prospectively limiting the lawyer’s liability to a client for malpractice unless the client is independently represented in making the agreement.” Model Rule 1.8(h)(1). Consequently, if the attorney had in fact gotten the doctor to waive the attorney’s malpractice liability, he would be violating the Rules. D is incorrect. Even though the attorney did not have the required competence, the emergency situation made his actions proper under the Rules.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.1","Model Rule 1.8(h)(1)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[157],"sourceAnswerPages":[259,260],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名主要从事刑法业务的律师离开机场时，与一名医生合乘出租车。出租车发生碰撞，医生身受重伤，律师只是受到惊吓。律师在救护车上陪同医生前往医院。医生相信自己即将死亡，请求律师为她起草一份简单遗嘱。律师告诉医生：“我从未起草过遗嘱，但希望自己还能记得法学院学过的基本知识。”随后，律师依医生请求起草遗嘱。医生签署了遗嘱，救护车上的两名急救人员作为见证人签名。该遗嘱最终被认定有效。律师起草遗嘱是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为律师没有遗漏导致遗嘱无效的必要形式。","B":"适当，因为律师提供的是在当时情形下合理必要的法律服务。","C":"不适当，因为医生没有放弃追究律师执业过失责任。","D":"不适当，因为律师不具备该代理所需技能。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。“在紧急情况下，律师可以就其通常不具备所需技能的事项提供意见或协助。”《职业行为示范规则》1.1 评注 [3]。因此，该律师虽然通常不具备起草遗嘱的称职能力，但事故造成的紧急情况允许其尝试完成工作，而不违反规则。A 不正确。使律师行为适当的是紧急情况。即使遗嘱起草得很好，如果不存在紧急情况，律师仍会违反有关称职能力的规则。这是“没有损害，就没有违规”型错误选项的变体。C 不正确。律师不得“订立预先限制律师对委托人承担执业过失责任的协议，除非委托人在订立协议时由独立律师代理”。规则 1.8(h)(1)。因此，如果律师事实上让医生放弃追究其执业过失责任，反而会违反规则。D 不正确。即使律师不具备通常所需的称职能力，紧急情况仍使其行为在规则下适当。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-045","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":45,"topic":"Model Rule 5.4","question":"An attorney has recently started her own law firm with four other lawyers as associates. The law firm has moved into offices in a new building which is owned by a bank. The attorney has borrowed heavily from the bank to finance her new law firm. In addition, the bank provides the law firm with accounting services through its computer. At the bank’s suggestion, an employee of the bank, who is not a lawyer, serves as a part-time office manager for the law firm without compensation from the firm. The duties of the office manager are to advise the firm generally on fees and time charges, program matters for the computer services, and consult with the attorney on accounting and billing practices to ensure solvency. The attorney and the bank do not discuss client confidences. Is the arrangement with the bank proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because secrets or confidences of clients will not be disclosed to the bank.","B":"Yes, because the office manager is paid by the bank.","C":"No, because a nonlawyer will be advising the law firm on fees and time charges.","D":"No, because the bank will be involved in the practice of law."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. As long as client confidences are not exposed, the arrangement is proper under the Rules. Importantly, no fees are being shared with the bank employee, and the bank employee is not serving as a director or in a similar position in the firm. Model Rule 5.4. Here, the bank employee is merely acting as a part-time office manager, giving advice on how to keep the firm solvent. B is incorrect. Although there is no sharing of fees, the arrangement could cause a problem if confidences were being shared with the bank. C is incorrect. It is improper for a nonlawyer third party to direct the lawyer’s professional judgment in rendering legal services. Model Rule 5.4, Comment [1]. Here, the bank employee is not giving advice on representation, but merely on office billing and solvency matters. D is incorrect. There is no indication that this setup will result in the bank improperly practicing law. The bank employee is merely a part-time office manager, giving advice regarding the running of the office.","rule":"A is the correct answer. As long as client confidences are not exposed, the arrangement is proper under the Rules. Importantly, no fees are being shared with the bank employee, and the bank employee is not serving as a director or in a similar position in the firm. Model Rule 5.4. Here, the bank employee is merely acting as a part-time office manager, giving advice on how to keep the firm solvent. B is incorrect. Although there is no sharing of fees, the arrangement could cause a problem if confidences were being shared with the bank. C is incorrect. It is improper for a nonlawyer third party to direct the lawyer’s professional judgment in rendering legal services. Model Rule 5.4, Comment [1]. Here, the bank employee is not giving advice on representation, but merely on office billing and solvency matters. D is incorrect. There is no indication that this setup will result in the bank improperly practicing law. The bank employee is merely a part-time office manager, giving advice regarding the running of the office.","irac":{"issue":"Is the arrangement with the bank proper?","rule":"A is the correct answer. As long as client confidences are not exposed, the arrangement is proper under the Rules. Importantly, no fees are being shared with the bank employee, and the bank employee is not serving as a director or in a similar position in the firm. Model Rule 5.4. Here, the bank employee is merely acting as a part-time office manager, giving advice on how to keep the firm solvent. B is incorrect. Although there is no sharing of fees, the arrangement could cause a problem if confidences were being shared with the bank. C is incorrect. It is improper for a nonlawyer third party to direct the lawyer’s professional judgment in rendering legal services. Model Rule 5.4, Comment [1]. Here, the bank employee is not giving advice on representation, but merely on office billing and solvency matters. D is incorrect. There is no indication that this setup will result in the bank improperly practicing law. The bank employee is merely a part-time office manager, giving advice regarding the running of the office.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. As long as client confidences are not exposed, the arrangement is proper under the Rules. Importantly, no fees are being shared with the bank employee, and the bank employee is not serving as a director or in a similar position in the firm. Model Rule 5.4. Here, the bank employee is merely acting as a part-time office manager, giving advice on how to keep the firm solvent. B is incorrect. Although there is no sharing of fees, the arrangement could cause a problem if confidences were being shared with the bank. C is incorrect. It is improper for a nonlawyer third party to direct the lawyer’s professional judgment in rendering legal services. Model Rule 5.4, Comment [1]. Here, the bank employee is not giving advice on representation, but merely on office billing and solvency matters. D is incorrect. There is no indication that this setup will result in the bank improperly practicing law. The bank employee is merely a part-time office manager, giving advice regarding the running of the office.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.4"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[157,158],"sourceAnswerPages":[260],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师最近创办了自己的律师事务所，另有四名律师担任助理律师。事务所搬入一栋由某银行所有的新楼。律师为筹建新事务所从银行借入大量资金。此外，银行通过其计算机系统向事务所提供会计服务。依银行建议，一名不是律师的银行雇员担任事务所的兼职办公室经理，事务所不向其支付报酬。办公室经理的职责是就收费和工时计费向事务所提供一般性建议、为计算机服务编程，并与律师协商会计和账单处理方法，以确保事务所保持偿付能力。律师与银行不讨论委托人秘密。与银行达成的安排是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为委托人的秘密或保密信息不会向银行披露。","B":"适当，因为办公室经理由银行支付报酬。","C":"不适当，因为一名非律师将就收费和工时计费向律师事务所提供建议。","D":"不适当，因为银行将参与法律执业活动。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。只要不暴露委托人保密信息，该安排在规则下是适当的。特别是，事务所没有与银行雇员分享费用，该雇员也没有担任事务所董事或类似职位。《职业行为示范规则》5.4。本题中，银行雇员只是兼职办公室经理，就如何维持事务所偿付能力提供意见。B 不正确。虽然不存在费用分享，但如果与银行共享委托人保密信息，该安排仍会产生问题。C 不正确。由非律师第三方指导律师在提供法律服务时的专业判断是不适当的。规则 5.4 评注 [1]。本题中，银行雇员没有就代理事项提供意见，只就办公室账单和偿付能力事项提供建议。D 不正确。没有迹象表明该安排会导致银行擅自从事法律执业活动。银行雇员只是兼职办公室经理，就办公室运营提供意见。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-046","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":46,"topic":"Model Rule 1.8(e)(1) / Model Rule 1.8","question":"An attorney is representing the plaintiff in a personal injury case, on a contingent fee basis. The client is without resources to pay for the expenses of the investigation and the medical examinations necessary to prepare for trial. The client asked the attorney to pay for these expenses. The attorney declined to advance the funds but offered to guarantee the client’s promissory note to a local bank in order to secure the funds needed to cover those expenses. The client has agreed to reimburse the attorney in the event the attorney incurs liability on the guaranty. Is the attorney subject to discipline if she guarantees the client’s promissory note?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney is lending her credit to the client.","B":"Yes, because the attorney is helping to finance litigation.","C":"No, because the funds will be used for trial preparation.","D":"No, because the attorney took the case on a contingent fee basis."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not provide financial assistance to a client in connection with pending or contemplated litigation, except that: (1) a lawyer may advance court costs and expenses of litigation, the repayment of which may be contingent on the outcome of the matter.” Model Rule 1.8(e)(1). Here, since the funds are being used for trial preparation, they are proper under the Rules. A is incorrect. So long as an attorney’s financial assistance is used for court costs and expenses of litigation, the attorney may lend his or her credit to a client. B is incorrect. “Lawyers may not subsidize lawsuits or administrative proceedings brought on behalf of their clients, including making or guaranteeing loans to their clients for living expenses, because to do so would encourage clients to pursue lawsuits that might not otherwise be brought and because such assistance gives lawyers too great a financial stake in the litigation. These dangers do not warrant a prohibition on a lawyer lending a client court costs and litigation expenses, including the expenses of medical examination and the costs of obtaining and presenting evidence, because these advances are virtually indistinguishable from contingent fees and help ensure access to the courts.” Model Rule 1.8, Comment [10]. Here, the attorney is not financing the litigation, she is merely providing assistance with trial costs. D is incorrect. The Rules state that a lawyer may advance court costs and litigation expenses, even if repayment is on a contingency fee basis. Model Rule 1.8(e)(1).","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not provide financial assistance to a client in connection with pending or contemplated litigation, except that: (1) a lawyer may advance court costs and expenses of litigation, the repayment of which may be contingent on the outcome of the matter.” Model Rule 1.8(e)(1). Here, since the funds are being used for trial preparation, they are proper under the Rules. A is incorrect. So long as an attorney’s financial assistance is used for court costs and expenses of litigation, the attorney may lend his or her credit to a client. B is incorrect. “Lawyers may not subsidize lawsuits or administrative proceedings brought on behalf of their clients, including making or guaranteeing loans to their clients for living expenses, because to do so would encourage clients to pursue lawsuits that might not otherwise be brought and because such assistance gives lawyers too great a financial stake in the litigation. These dangers do not warrant a prohibition on a lawyer lending a client court costs and litigation expenses, including the expenses of medical examination and the costs of obtaining and presenting evidence, because these advances are virtually indistinguishable from contingent fees and help ensure access to the courts.” Model Rule 1.8, Comment [10]. Here, the attorney is not financing the litigation, she is merely providing assistance with trial costs. D is incorrect. The Rules state that a lawyer may advance court costs and litigation expenses, even if repayment is on a contingency fee basis. Model Rule 1.8(e)(1).","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline if she guarantees the client’s promissory note?","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not provide financial assistance to a client in connection with pending or contemplated litigation, except that: (1) a lawyer may advance court costs and expenses of litigation, the repayment of which may be contingent on the outcome of the matter.” Model Rule 1.8(e)(1). Here, since the funds are being used for trial preparation, they are proper under the Rules. A is incorrect. So long as an attorney’s financial assistance is used for court costs and expenses of litigation, the attorney may lend his or her credit to a client. B is incorrect. “Lawyers may not subsidize lawsuits or administrative proceedings brought on behalf of their clients, including making or guaranteeing loans to their clients for living expenses, because to do so would encourage clients to pursue lawsuits that might not otherwise be brought and because such assistance gives lawyers too great a financial stake in the litigation. These dangers do not warrant a prohibition on a lawyer lending a client court costs and litigation expenses, including the expenses of medical examination and the costs of obtaining and presenting evidence, because these advances are virtually indistinguishable from contingent fees and help ensure access to the courts.” Model Rule 1.8, Comment [10]. Here, the attorney is not financing the litigation, she is merely providing assistance with trial costs. D is incorrect. The Rules state that a lawyer may advance court costs and litigation expenses, even if repayment is on a contingency fee basis. Model Rule 1.8(e)(1).","analysis":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not provide financial assistance to a client in connection with pending or contemplated litigation, except that: (1) a lawyer may advance court costs and expenses of litigation, the repayment of which may be contingent on the outcome of the matter.” Model Rule 1.8(e)(1). Here, since the funds are being used for trial preparation, they are proper under the Rules. A is incorrect. So long as an attorney’s financial assistance is used for court costs and expenses of litigation, the attorney may lend his or her credit to a client. B is incorrect. “Lawyers may not subsidize lawsuits or administrative proceedings brought on behalf of their clients, including making or guaranteeing loans to their clients for living expenses, because to do so would encourage clients to pursue lawsuits that might not otherwise be brought and because such assistance gives lawyers too great a financial stake in the litigation. These dangers do not warrant a prohibition on a lawyer lending a client court costs and litigation expenses, including the expenses of medical examination and the costs of obtaining and presenting evidence, because these advances are virtually indistinguishable from contingent fees and help ensure access to the courts.” Model Rule 1.8, Comment [10]. Here, the attorney is not financing the litigation, she is merely providing assistance with trial costs. D is incorrect. The Rules state that a lawyer may advance court costs and litigation expenses, even if repayment is on a contingency fee basis. Model Rule 1.8(e)(1).","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.8(e)(1)","Model Rule 1.8"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[158],"sourceAnswerPages":[260,261],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师以风险代理费方式代理一宗人身伤害案件的原告。委托人没有资金支付为准备审判所需的调查和医学检查费用，因而请求律师支付这些费用。律师拒绝垫付资金，但提出可以为委托人向当地银行开具的本票提供担保，以取得支付这些费用所需的款项。委托人同意，如果律师因担保而承担责任，将向律师偿还。律师为委托人的本票提供担保，是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师正在把自己的信用借给委托人。","B":"是，因为律师正在帮助资助诉讼。","C":"否，因为资金将用于审判准备。","D":"否，因为律师以风险代理费方式接受了案件。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。“律师不得就待决或拟议诉讼向委托人提供经济援助，但下列情形除外：(1) 律师可以垫付诉讼费和诉讼开支，而偿还可取决于事项结果。”《职业行为示范规则》1.8(e)(1)。本题中，资金用于审判准备，因此在规则下是适当的。A 不正确。只要律师的经济援助用于诉讼费和诉讼开支，就可以把自己的信用借给委托人。B 不正确。“律师不得补贴代表委托人提起的诉讼或行政程序，包括向委托人提供生活费贷款或为此担保，因为这样做会鼓励委托人提起原本可能不会提起的诉讼，也会使律师在诉讼中拥有过大的经济利益。不过，这些危险不足以禁止律师向委托人垫付诉讼费和诉讼开支，包括医学检查费用以及取得和提出证据的费用，因为这种垫付与风险代理费几乎无法区分，并有助于确保诉诸法院的机会。”规则 1.8 评注 [10]。本题中，律师并非资助诉讼，只是在审判费用方面提供协助。D 不正确。规则规定，即使偿还取决于案件结果，律师仍可垫付诉讼费和诉讼开支。规则 1.8(e)(1)。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-047","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":47,"topic":"Model Rule 4.2","question":"An attorney represents a defendant in an action for personal injuries. The attorney, pursuant to the defendant’s authorization, made an offer of settlement to the attorney who represents the plaintiff. The plaintiff’s attorney has not responded to the offer, and the attorney is convinced that the plaintiff’s attorney has not communicated the offer to the plaintiff. State law authorizes a defendant to move for a settlement conference and to tender an offer of settlement. If such a motion is made and the offer is rejected by the plaintiff and the eventual judgment does not exceed the amount of the offer, the plaintiff must bear all costs of litigation, including reasonable fees, as determined by the court, for the defendant’s counsel. The attorney, with the defendant’s consent, filed a motion requesting a settlement conference, tendered an offer to settle for $25,000, and served copies of the motion and tender on the plaintiff’s attorney and on the plaintiff personally. Is the attorney subject to discipline for serving the plaintiff with a copy of the motion and tender?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because service of copies of the motion and tender on the plaintiff were not authorized by statute or rule of court.","B":"Yes, because the attorney did not first inform the plaintiff’s attorney of the attorney’s intention to serve copies of the motion and tender on the plaintiff.","C":"No, because the decision to accept or reject a settlement offer rests with the client.","D":"No, because the motion and tender became public documents when they were filed in court."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. “In representing a client, a lawyer shall not communicate about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows to be represented by another lawyer in the matter, unless the lawyer has the consent of the other lawyer or is authorized to do so by law or a court order.” Model Rule 4.2. Here, the attorney is communicating directly with the plaintiff by serving the plaintiff with a copy of the motion and tender. This is prohibited under the Rules, as the attorney clearly knows the plaintiff is represented by counsel. B is incorrect. This is an answer choice showing the importance of careful reading. The Rules require the other lawyer’s “consent” before an attorney can communicate with a represented person. This answer choice states that the communication would be proper even if the attorney only informed the plaintiff’s attorney, which is clearly not enough under the Rules. C is incorrect. The Rules prohibit communication with a represented person. The subject of that communication does not change the Rule. D is incorrect. The Rules prohibit communication with a represented person. The simple fact that the plaintiff might learn the information elsewhere does not change the analysis.","rule":"A is the correct answer. “In representing a client, a lawyer shall not communicate about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows to be represented by another lawyer in the matter, unless the lawyer has the consent of the other lawyer or is authorized to do so by law or a court order.” Model Rule 4.2. Here, the attorney is communicating directly with the plaintiff by serving the plaintiff with a copy of the motion and tender. This is prohibited under the Rules, as the attorney clearly knows the plaintiff is represented by counsel. B is incorrect. This is an answer choice showing the importance of careful reading. The Rules require the other lawyer’s “consent” before an attorney can communicate with a represented person. This answer choice states that the communication would be proper even if the attorney only informed the plaintiff’s attorney, which is clearly not enough under the Rules. C is incorrect. The Rules prohibit communication with a represented person. The subject of that communication does not change the Rule. D is incorrect. The Rules prohibit communication with a represented person. The simple fact that the plaintiff might learn the information elsewhere does not change the analysis.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for serving the plaintiff with a copy of the motion and tender?","rule":"A is the correct answer. “In representing a client, a lawyer shall not communicate about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows to be represented by another lawyer in the matter, unless the lawyer has the consent of the other lawyer or is authorized to do so by law or a court order.” Model Rule 4.2. Here, the attorney is communicating directly with the plaintiff by serving the plaintiff with a copy of the motion and tender. This is prohibited under the Rules, as the attorney clearly knows the plaintiff is represented by counsel. B is incorrect. This is an answer choice showing the importance of careful reading. The Rules require the other lawyer’s “consent” before an attorney can communicate with a represented person. This answer choice states that the communication would be proper even if the attorney only informed the plaintiff’s attorney, which is clearly not enough under the Rules. C is incorrect. The Rules prohibit communication with a represented person. The subject of that communication does not change the Rule. D is incorrect. The Rules prohibit communication with a represented person. The simple fact that the plaintiff might learn the information elsewhere does not change the analysis.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. “In representing a client, a lawyer shall not communicate about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows to be represented by another lawyer in the matter, unless the lawyer has the consent of the other lawyer or is authorized to do so by law or a court order.” Model Rule 4.2. Here, the attorney is communicating directly with the plaintiff by serving the plaintiff with a copy of the motion and tender. This is prohibited under the Rules, as the attorney clearly knows the plaintiff is represented by counsel. B is incorrect. This is an answer choice showing the importance of careful reading. The Rules require the other lawyer’s “consent” before an attorney can communicate with a represented person. This answer choice states that the communication would be proper even if the attorney only informed the plaintiff’s attorney, which is clearly not enough under the Rules. C is incorrect. The Rules prohibit communication with a represented person. The subject of that communication does not change the Rule. D is incorrect. The Rules prohibit communication with a represented person. The simple fact that the plaintiff might learn the information elsewhere does not change the analysis.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 4.2"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[158,159],"sourceAnswerPages":[261,262],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师在一宗人身伤害诉讼中代理被告。律师经被告授权，向代理原告的律师提出和解要约。原告律师没有回应，律师确信原告律师没有把要约告知原告。州法律允许被告申请和解会议并提出和解要约。如果提出申请而原告拒绝要约，且最终判决金额不超过要约金额，则原告必须承担全部诉讼费用，包括法院确定的被告律师合理费用。律师经被告同意，提交申请，请求召开和解会议，提出 25,000 美元和解要约，并将申请及要约副本送达原告律师和原告本人。律师向原告送达申请及要约副本，是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为法律或法院规则没有授权向原告送达申请及要约副本。","B":"是，因为律师没有先通知原告律师，自己打算向原告送达申请及要约副本。","C":"否，因为是否接受或拒绝和解要约由委托人决定。","D":"否，因为申请及要约提交法院后即成为公开文件。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。“律师在代理委托人时，明知某人在该事项中由另一名律师代理，未经该律师同意或者没有法律或法院命令授权，不得就代理事项与此人沟通。”《职业行为示范规则》4.2。本题中，律师通过向原告送达申请及要约副本而直接与其沟通。律师显然知道原告已有律师代理，因此规则禁止该行为。B 不正确。该选项说明仔细阅读的重要性。规则要求取得另一名律师的“同意”后，律师才能与已有律师代理的人沟通。本选项认为只要通知原告律师，沟通就会适当，而这显然不足以满足规则。C 不正确。规则禁止与已有律师代理的人沟通，沟通的具体主题不会改变规则。D 不正确。规则禁止与已有律师代理的人沟通。原告可能从其他来源获知信息这一事实，不会改变分析。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-048","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":48,"topic":"Model Rule 5.6(b)","question":"An attorney represents a wife in a marriage dissolution proceeding that involves bitterly contested issues of property division and child custody. After one day of trial, the husband, through his attorney, made a settlement offer. Because of the husband’s intense dislike for the attorney, the proposed settlement requires that the attorney agree not to represent the wife in any subsequent proceeding, brought by either party, to modify or enforce the provisions of the decree. The wife wants to accept the offer, and the attorney believes that the settlement offer made by the husband is better than any award the wife would get if the case went to judgment. Although the attorney thought it was in the wife’s best interest to accept the offer, he was concerned that another attorney may not be able to adequately protect the wife’s interests in the future. Is it proper for the attorney to agree that he will not represent the wife in any subsequent proceeding?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the restriction on the attorney is limited to subsequent proceedings in the same matter.","B":"Yes, because the attorney believes that it is in the wife’s best interests to accept the proposed settlement.","C":"No, because the proposed settlement would restrict the attorney’s right to represent the wife in the future.","D":"No, because the attorney did not believe that the wife’s interests can be adequately protected by another lawyer in the future."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 5.6(b), an attorney shall not participate in offering or making “an agreement in which a restriction on the lawyer’s right to practice is part of the settlement of a client controversy.” Consequently, the proposed settlement violates the Rules. A is incorrect. As stated above, an attorney cannot be part of a settlement restricting his or her right to represent a client. B is incorrect. This is clearly a wrong answer choice, as it implies that as long as the attorney believes something is in the client’s best interest, it is proper under the Rules. D is incorrect. This answer choice also centers around the attorney’s beliefs, instead of addressing the issue of what the attorney is actually doing; specifically, restricting his right to represent the client in the future.","rule":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 5.6(b), an attorney shall not participate in offering or making “an agreement in which a restriction on the lawyer’s right to practice is part of the settlement of a client controversy.” Consequently, the proposed settlement violates the Rules. A is incorrect. As stated above, an attorney cannot be part of a settlement restricting his or her right to represent a client. B is incorrect. This is clearly a wrong answer choice, as it implies that as long as the attorney believes something is in the client’s best interest, it is proper under the Rules. D is incorrect. This answer choice also centers around the attorney’s beliefs, instead of addressing the issue of what the attorney is actually doing; specifically, restricting his right to represent the client in the future.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the attorney to agree that he will not represent the wife in any subsequent proceeding?","rule":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 5.6(b), an attorney shall not participate in offering or making “an agreement in which a restriction on the lawyer’s right to practice is part of the settlement of a client controversy.” Consequently, the proposed settlement violates the Rules. A is incorrect. As stated above, an attorney cannot be part of a settlement restricting his or her right to represent a client. B is incorrect. This is clearly a wrong answer choice, as it implies that as long as the attorney believes something is in the client’s best interest, it is proper under the Rules. D is incorrect. This answer choice also centers around the attorney’s beliefs, instead of addressing the issue of what the attorney is actually doing; specifically, restricting his right to represent the client in the future.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 5.6(b), an attorney shall not participate in offering or making “an agreement in which a restriction on the lawyer’s right to practice is part of the settlement of a client controversy.” Consequently, the proposed settlement violates the Rules. A is incorrect. As stated above, an attorney cannot be part of a settlement restricting his or her right to represent a client. B is incorrect. This is clearly a wrong answer choice, as it implies that as long as the attorney believes something is in the client’s best interest, it is proper under the Rules. D is incorrect. This answer choice also centers around the attorney’s beliefs, instead of addressing the issue of what the attorney is actually doing; specifically, restricting his right to represent the client in the future.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.6(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[159,160],"sourceAnswerPages":[262],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理妻子参加一宗婚姻解除程序，其中财产分割和子女监护问题争议十分激烈。审判进行一天后，丈夫通过其律师提出和解要约。由于丈夫极度厌恶妻子的律师，拟议和解要求该律师同意，不得在以后由任何一方提起的、旨在修改或执行判决条款的任何程序中代理妻子。妻子希望接受要约，而律师认为，丈夫提出的和解比案件裁判后妻子可能获得的任何结果都好。律师虽然认为接受要约符合妻子的最佳利益，却担心其他律师将来可能无法充分保护妻子利益。该律师同意不在任何后续程序中代理妻子，是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为对律师的限制仅适用于同一事项中的后续程序。","B":"适当，因为律师认为接受拟议和解符合妻子的最佳利益。","C":"不适当，因为拟议和解会限制律师将来代理妻子的权利。","D":"不适当，因为律师不相信其他律师将来能够充分保护妻子的利益。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。根据《职业行为示范规则》5.6(b)，律师不得参与提出或订立“把限制律师执业权作为委托人争议和解一部分的协议”。因此，拟议和解违反规则。A 不正确。如上所述，律师不得成为限制自己代理委托人权利之和解协议的一方。B 不正确。这显然是错误选项，因为它暗示只要律师认为某事符合委托人最佳利益，该行为在规则下就适当。D 不正确。该选项同样围绕律师的看法展开，而没有处理律师实际所做之事，即限制自己将来代理委托人的权利。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-049","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":49,"topic":"Model Rule 1.5(b) / Model Rule 1.5","question":"A company’s president telephoned his city’s best-known employment attorney and asked her to represent the company in a dispute that had just arisen with the company’s chief financial officer. The attorney, who had never previously represented the company, agreed. At the president’s insistence, she immediately commenced the representation. A few days later, during a meeting with the president, the attorney first revealed the amount of her customary hourly fee and then explained that the company would also be responsible for reimbursing her expenses. The president responded that her fee was higher than he had expected but that he would be happy for the company to pay it, given her excellent work to date. Although the attorney intended to follow up with a confirming letter, she never did so. For several more months, she assisted the company in resolving its employment dispute. Afterward, she sent the company a bill accurately reflecting her hourly fee and expenses, which were reasonable. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because she did not disclose the basis of her fee before commencing the representation.","B":"Yes, because she did not confirm her fee agreement in writing.","C":"No, because she disclosed the basis of her fee within a reasonable time after commencing the representation.","D":"No, because she was not required to advise the client of her customary hourly fee, unless requested to do so."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. “The scope of the representation and the basis or rate of the fee and expenses for which the client will be responsible shall be communicated to the client, preferably in writing, before or within a reasonable time after commencing the representation.” Model Rule 1.5(b). Within a few days of beginning her representation, and apparently at her first meeting with the president, the attorney revealed her fees. This was clearly within a reasonable time and proper under the Rules. A is incorrect. As long as an attorney reveals his or her fees within a reasonable time, he or she is not in violation of the Rules. B is incorrect. The Rule only states that the communication be “preferably in writing,” it does not absolutely require it. However, the Rule recognizes the fact that a written statement reduces the possibility of a misunderstanding. Model Rule 1.5, Comment [2]. D is incorrect. An attorney is required to explain his or her fee within a reasonable time. Importantly, this requirement is not triggered by the client’s request.","rule":"C is the correct answer. “The scope of the representation and the basis or rate of the fee and expenses for which the client will be responsible shall be communicated to the client, preferably in writing, before or within a reasonable time after commencing the representation.” Model Rule 1.5(b). Within a few days of beginning her representation, and apparently at her first meeting with the president, the attorney revealed her fees. This was clearly within a reasonable time and proper under the Rules. A is incorrect. As long as an attorney reveals his or her fees within a reasonable time, he or she is not in violation of the Rules. B is incorrect. The Rule only states that the communication be “preferably in writing,” it does not absolutely require it. However, the Rule recognizes the fact that a written statement reduces the possibility of a misunderstanding. Model Rule 1.5, Comment [2]. D is incorrect. An attorney is required to explain his or her fee within a reasonable time. Importantly, this requirement is not triggered by the client’s request.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"C is the correct answer. “The scope of the representation and the basis or rate of the fee and expenses for which the client will be responsible shall be communicated to the client, preferably in writing, before or within a reasonable time after commencing the representation.” Model Rule 1.5(b). Within a few days of beginning her representation, and apparently at her first meeting with the president, the attorney revealed her fees. This was clearly within a reasonable time and proper under the Rules. A is incorrect. As long as an attorney reveals his or her fees within a reasonable time, he or she is not in violation of the Rules. B is incorrect. The Rule only states that the communication be “preferably in writing,” it does not absolutely require it. However, the Rule recognizes the fact that a written statement reduces the possibility of a misunderstanding. Model Rule 1.5, Comment [2]. D is incorrect. An attorney is required to explain his or her fee within a reasonable time. Importantly, this requirement is not triggered by the client’s request.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. “The scope of the representation and the basis or rate of the fee and expenses for which the client will be responsible shall be communicated to the client, preferably in writing, before or within a reasonable time after commencing the representation.” Model Rule 1.5(b). Within a few days of beginning her representation, and apparently at her first meeting with the president, the attorney revealed her fees. This was clearly within a reasonable time and proper under the Rules. A is incorrect. As long as an attorney reveals his or her fees within a reasonable time, he or she is not in violation of the Rules. B is incorrect. The Rule only states that the communication be “preferably in writing,” it does not absolutely require it. However, the Rule recognizes the fact that a written statement reduces the possibility of a misunderstanding. Model Rule 1.5, Comment [2]. D is incorrect. An attorney is required to explain his or her fee within a reasonable time. Importantly, this requirement is not triggered by the client’s request.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.5(b)","Model Rule 1.5"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[160],"sourceAnswerPages":[262,263],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家公司的总裁致电本市最知名的劳动法律师，请求她代理公司处理刚刚与公司首席财务官发生的争议。该律师此前从未代理过公司，但同意接受委托。应总裁坚持，她立即开始代理。数日后，律师第一次在与总裁会面时透露自己的惯常小时费率，并解释公司还须偿还她的开支。总裁回答，收费高于预期，但鉴于她迄今为止的出色工作，他乐意让公司支付。律师虽然打算随后发送确认信，却一直没有发送。接下来几个月，她继续协助公司解决劳动争议。事项结束后，她向公司发送账单，其中准确列明自己的小时费用和开支，且金额合理。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为她没有在开始代理前披露收费依据。","B":"是，因为她没有以书面形式确认收费协议。","C":"否，因为她在开始代理后的合理时间内披露了收费依据。","D":"否，因为除非委托人提出请求，她没有义务告知自己的惯常小时费率。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。“律师应在开始代理前或开始后的合理时间内，向委托人说明代理范围，以及委托人应承担之费用和开支的依据或费率，最好采用书面形式。”《职业行为示范规则》1.5(b)。在开始代理后几天内，而且显然是在第一次与总裁会面时，律师即披露了收费。这显然属于合理时间，符合规则。A 不正确。只要律师在合理时间内披露费用，就不违反规则。B 不正确。规则只说沟通“最好采用书面形式”，并非绝对要求。不过，规则承认书面陈述会降低发生误解的可能性。规则 1.5 评注 [2]。D 不正确。律师必须在合理时间内说明收费，而且这项要求并不以委托人提出请求为前提。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-050","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":50,"topic":"Model Rule 3.4(e)","question":"In the attorney’s closing statement to the court in a bench trial, the attorney said, “Your honor, I drive on the street in question every day and I know that a driver cannot see cars backing out of driveways as the one did in this case. I believe that my client was not negligent, and I ask you to so find.” The attorney was not trying to deceive the court with his statement. There was no other evidence in the record regarding the facts asserted by the attorney. Was the attorney’s closing argument proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney was speaking truthfully and not trying to deceive the court.","B":"Yes, because the rules of evidence are very liberal when the trial is before a judge without a jury.","C":"No, because the attorney asserted her personal knowledge of facts in issue.","D":"No, because there is no other evidence in the record about the facts asserted by the attorney."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. During trial, a lawyer may not “assert personal knowledge of facts in issue except when testifying as a witness.” Model Rule 3.4(e). Here, by stating his belief regarding the street and driveways, he is clearly violating that Rule. A is incorrect. While the attorney may have been telling the truth, he still violated the Rule against asserting personal knowledge of the facts. This is another variation on the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. B is incorrect. While the Rules of Evidence may be more liberal in certain circumstances, keep in mind that this is a test on the Model Rules of Professional Conduct. D is incorrect. Even if there were evidence related to the facts asserted by the attorney, he would still be violating the Rule against asserting his personal knowledge.","rule":"C is the correct answer. During trial, a lawyer may not “assert personal knowledge of facts in issue except when testifying as a witness.” Model Rule 3.4(e). Here, by stating his belief regarding the street and driveways, he is clearly violating that Rule. A is incorrect. While the attorney may have been telling the truth, he still violated the Rule against asserting personal knowledge of the facts. This is another variation on the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. B is incorrect. While the Rules of Evidence may be more liberal in certain circumstances, keep in mind that this is a test on the Model Rules of Professional Conduct. D is incorrect. Even if there were evidence related to the facts asserted by the attorney, he would still be violating the Rule against asserting his personal knowledge.","irac":{"issue":"Was the attorney’s closing argument proper?","rule":"C is the correct answer. During trial, a lawyer may not “assert personal knowledge of facts in issue except when testifying as a witness.” Model Rule 3.4(e). Here, by stating his belief regarding the street and driveways, he is clearly violating that Rule. A is incorrect. While the attorney may have been telling the truth, he still violated the Rule against asserting personal knowledge of the facts. This is another variation on the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. B is incorrect. While the Rules of Evidence may be more liberal in certain circumstances, keep in mind that this is a test on the Model Rules of Professional Conduct. D is incorrect. Even if there were evidence related to the facts asserted by the attorney, he would still be violating the Rule against asserting his personal knowledge.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. During trial, a lawyer may not “assert personal knowledge of facts in issue except when testifying as a witness.” Model Rule 3.4(e). Here, by stating his belief regarding the street and driveways, he is clearly violating that Rule. A is incorrect. While the attorney may have been telling the truth, he still violated the Rule against asserting personal knowledge of the facts. This is another variation on the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. B is incorrect. While the Rules of Evidence may be more liberal in certain circumstances, keep in mind that this is a test on the Model Rules of Professional Conduct. D is incorrect. Even if there were evidence related to the facts asserted by the attorney, he would still be violating the Rule against asserting his personal knowledge.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.4(e)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[161],"sourceAnswerPages":[263],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"在一宗法官审判的案件中，律师向法院作结案陈词时说：“法官阁下，我每天都在涉案街道上开车，我知道驾驶人无法看到像本案一样从车道倒出的汽车。我相信我的委托人没有过失，请求您作出相应认定。”律师并非试图用该陈述欺骗法院。案卷中没有其他证据涉及该律师所主张的事实。律师的结案陈词是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为律师所说属实，也没有试图欺骗法院。","B":"适当，因为在法官进行无陪审团审判时，证据规则十分宽松。","C":"不适当，因为律师主张了自己对争议事实的亲身了解。","D":"不适当，因为案卷中没有其他证据涉及律师所主张的事实。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。在审判期间，律师不得“主张自己对争议事实具有亲身了解，但以证人身份作证时除外”。《职业行为示范规则》3.4(e)。本题中，律师陈述自己对街道和车道的看法，显然违反该规则。A 不正确。律师即使所言属实，仍违反不得主张对事实具有亲身了解的规则。这又是“没有损害，就没有违规”型错误选项的一种变体。B 不正确。即使证据规则在某些情况下可能更为宽松，也应记住本题考查的是《职业行为示范规则》。D 不正确。即使存在与律师所主张事实有关的其他证据，律师仍会因主张自己的亲身了解而违反规则。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-051","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":51,"topic":"Professional Responsibility","question":"A plaintiff who is not a lawyer is representing himself in small claims court in an action to recover his security deposit from his former landlord. The plaintiff told an attorney, a close friend who lived near him, about this case, but did not ask the attorney for any advice. The attorney said, “I’ll give you some free advice. It would help your case if the new tenants would testify that the apartment was in good shape when they moved in, and, contrary to the allegations of your former landlord, it was not, in fact, repainted for them.” The plaintiff followed the attorney’s advice and won his case. Is the attorney subject to discipline for assisting the plaintiff in preparing for his court appearance?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney assisted the plaintiff in the practice of law.","B":"Yes, because the attorney offered unsolicited, in-person legal advice.","C":"No, because the plaintiff was representing himself in the proceedings.","D":"No, because the attorney was not compensated for his advice."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may provide legal assistance to pro se litigants. ABA Formal Opinion 07-446 (2007). Here, the attorney is not otherwise involved in the case, and his friend is representing himself. A is incorrect. Here, the attorney is not assisting the plaintiff in the practice of law. Improperly assisting someone in the practice of law does not mean helping a pro se litigant. Improper assistance refers to situations where the lawyer is helping a nonlawyer represent others and collect fees. B is incorrect. There is no prohibition under the Rules regarding unsolicited legal advice, unless that unsolicited legal advice violates some other Rule, such as communications with represented people or improper advertising. D is incorrect. Although the attorney was not compensated, the simple fact that no money exchanged hands would not protect the attorney if the attorney was violating some other Rule by giving the advice. Consequently, this is not the best answer choice.","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may provide legal assistance to pro se litigants. ABA Formal Opinion 07-446 (2007). Here, the attorney is not otherwise involved in the case, and his friend is representing himself. A is incorrect. Here, the attorney is not assisting the plaintiff in the practice of law. Improperly assisting someone in the practice of law does not mean helping a pro se litigant. Improper assistance refers to situations where the lawyer is helping a nonlawyer represent others and collect fees. B is incorrect. There is no prohibition under the Rules regarding unsolicited legal advice, unless that unsolicited legal advice violates some other Rule, such as communications with represented people or improper advertising. D is incorrect. Although the attorney was not compensated, the simple fact that no money exchanged hands would not protect the attorney if the attorney was violating some other Rule by giving the advice. Consequently, this is not the best answer choice.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for assisting the plaintiff in preparing for his court appearance?","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may provide legal assistance to pro se litigants. ABA Formal Opinion 07-446 (2007). Here, the attorney is not otherwise involved in the case, and his friend is representing himself. A is incorrect. Here, the attorney is not assisting the plaintiff in the practice of law. Improperly assisting someone in the practice of law does not mean helping a pro se litigant. Improper assistance refers to situations where the lawyer is helping a nonlawyer represent others and collect fees. B is incorrect. There is no prohibition under the Rules regarding unsolicited legal advice, unless that unsolicited legal advice violates some other Rule, such as communications with represented people or improper advertising. D is incorrect. Although the attorney was not compensated, the simple fact that no money exchanged hands would not protect the attorney if the attorney was violating some other Rule by giving the advice. Consequently, this is not the best answer choice.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may provide legal assistance to pro se litigants. ABA Formal Opinion 07-446 (2007). Here, the attorney is not otherwise involved in the case, and his friend is representing himself. A is incorrect. Here, the attorney is not assisting the plaintiff in the practice of law. Improperly assisting someone in the practice of law does not mean helping a pro se litigant. Improper assistance refers to situations where the lawyer is helping a nonlawyer represent others and collect fees. B is incorrect. There is no prohibition under the Rules regarding unsolicited legal advice, unless that unsolicited legal advice violates some other Rule, such as communications with represented people or improper advertising. D is incorrect. Although the attorney was not compensated, the simple fact that no money exchanged hands would not protect the attorney if the attorney was violating some other Rule by giving the advice. Consequently, this is not the best answer choice.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":[],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[161,162],"sourceAnswerPages":[263],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名不是律师的原告在小额索赔法院自行代理，起诉前房东要求退还保证金。原告把案件告诉一名住在附近的律师好友，但没有向律师请求任何意见。律师说：“我免费给你一些建议。如果新承租人能够作证说，他们入住时公寓状况良好，而且与前房东的指控相反，公寓事实上没有为他们重新粉刷，这会对你的案件有帮助。”原告依律师建议行事并胜诉。该律师因协助原告准备出庭是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师协助原告从事法律执业活动。","B":"是，因为律师当面主动提供了未经请求的法律意见。","C":"否，因为原告在程序中自行代理。","D":"否，因为律师没有因提供意见而获得报酬。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。律师可以向自行诉讼的当事人提供法律协助。ABA 正式意见 07-446（2007）。本题中，该律师没有以其他方式参与案件，其好友正在自行代理。A 不正确。本题律师不是在协助原告从事法律执业活动。所谓不当地协助他人从事法律执业，并不是指帮助自行诉讼的当事人；不当协助是指律师帮助非律师代理他人并收取费用等情形。B 不正确。规则并不禁止未经请求而主动提供法律意见，除非该意见同时违反其他规则，例如与已有律师代理的人沟通或进行不当广告。D 不正确。该律师虽然没有获得报酬，但如果他在提供意见时违反了其他规则，没有金钱往来也不能保护他。因此，这不是最佳答案。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-052","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":52,"topic":"Model Rule 1.6 / Model Rule 3.3(a)(3)","question":"An attorney is defending a client who has been indicted for burglary. During an interview, the client stated to the attorney that before he had consulted the attorney, the client had committed perjury while testifying before the grand jury that indicted him. The attorney is subject to discipline if she:","choices":{"A":"continues to represent the client.","B":"continues to represent the client unless he admits his perjury.","C":"does not inform the authorities of the perjury.","D":"informs the authorities of the perjury."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer must keep a client’s confidences unless the information needs to be revealed to prevent death or substantial bodily harm, crime or fraud, secure legal advice about the lawyer’s compliance with the Rules, establish a lawyer’s claim or defense, comply with a law or court order, or detect and resolve conflicts. Model Rule 1.6. The information revealed by the client involves none of these potential problems. Importantly, the client committed perjury before the lawyer’s representation. Had the perjury occurred during the lawyer’s representation, the lawyer would have been required to take remedial measures under Model Rule 3.3(a)(3). But, since the client acted before representation began, it would be a violation of the Rules to reveal the client’s confidential information. A is incorrect. The attorney is not required to withdraw from representation as the client has simply informed the attorney of a perjury that happened before representation began. This is not a situation where the client is attempting to have the attorney aid in lying to the tribunal. B is incorrect. As stated above, the attorney should not reveal the client’s perjury in this situation. C is incorrect. As stated above, the client’s information was told in confidence, and it meets no exception allowing the attorney to reveal it.","rule":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer must keep a client’s confidences unless the information needs to be revealed to prevent death or substantial bodily harm, crime or fraud, secure legal advice about the lawyer’s compliance with the Rules, establish a lawyer’s claim or defense, comply with a law or court order, or detect and resolve conflicts. Model Rule 1.6. The information revealed by the client involves none of these potential problems. Importantly, the client committed perjury before the lawyer’s representation. Had the perjury occurred during the lawyer’s representation, the lawyer would have been required to take remedial measures under Model Rule 3.3(a)(3). But, since the client acted before representation began, it would be a violation of the Rules to reveal the client’s confidential information. A is incorrect. The attorney is not required to withdraw from representation as the client has simply informed the attorney of a perjury that happened before representation began. This is not a situation where the client is attempting to have the attorney aid in lying to the tribunal. B is incorrect. As stated above, the attorney should not reveal the client’s perjury in this situation. C is incorrect. As stated above, the client’s information was told in confidence, and it meets no exception allowing the attorney to reveal it.","irac":{"issue":"An attorney is defending a client who has been indicted for burglary. During an interview, the client stated to the attorney that before he had consulted the attorney, the client had committed perjury while testifying before the grand jury that indicted him. The attorney is subject to discipline if she:","rule":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer must keep a client’s confidences unless the information needs to be revealed to prevent death or substantial bodily harm, crime or fraud, secure legal advice about the lawyer’s compliance with the Rules, establish a lawyer’s claim or defense, comply with a law or court order, or detect and resolve conflicts. Model Rule 1.6. The information revealed by the client involves none of these potential problems. Importantly, the client committed perjury before the lawyer’s representation. Had the perjury occurred during the lawyer’s representation, the lawyer would have been required to take remedial measures under Model Rule 3.3(a)(3). But, since the client acted before representation began, it would be a violation of the Rules to reveal the client’s confidential information. A is incorrect. The attorney is not required to withdraw from representation as the client has simply informed the attorney of a perjury that happened before representation began. This is not a situation where the client is attempting to have the attorney aid in lying to the tribunal. B is incorrect. As stated above, the attorney should not reveal the client’s perjury in this situation. C is incorrect. As stated above, the client’s information was told in confidence, and it meets no exception allowing the attorney to reveal it.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer must keep a client’s confidences unless the information needs to be revealed to prevent death or substantial bodily harm, crime or fraud, secure legal advice about the lawyer’s compliance with the Rules, establish a lawyer’s claim or defense, comply with a law or court order, or detect and resolve conflicts. Model Rule 1.6. The information revealed by the client involves none of these potential problems. Importantly, the client committed perjury before the lawyer’s representation. Had the perjury occurred during the lawyer’s representation, the lawyer would have been required to take remedial measures under Model Rule 3.3(a)(3). But, since the client acted before representation began, it would be a violation of the Rules to reveal the client’s confidential information. A is incorrect. The attorney is not required to withdraw from representation as the client has simply informed the attorney of a perjury that happened before representation began. This is not a situation where the client is attempting to have the attorney aid in lying to the tribunal. B is incorrect. As stated above, the attorney should not reveal the client’s perjury in this situation. C is incorrect. As stated above, the client’s information was told in confidence, and it meets no exception allowing the attorney to reveal it.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.6","Model Rule 3.3(a)(3)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[162],"sourceAnswerPages":[263,264],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师正在为一名因入室盗窃受到起诉的委托人进行抗辩。一次会谈中，委托人告诉律师，在咨询律师之前，自己曾在起诉他的大陪审团面前作伪证。下列何种情形会使该律师受到纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"继续代理委托人。","B":"继续代理委托人，除非委托人承认自己作伪证。","C":"不将伪证告知有关机关。","D":"将伪证告知有关机关。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。律师必须保守委托人秘密，除非为防止死亡或重大身体伤害、犯罪或欺诈，为取得有关律师遵守规则的法律意见，为确立律师的权利主张或抗辩，为遵守法律或法院命令，或者为发现并解决利益冲突而需要披露。《职业行为示范规则》1.6。委托人披露的信息不涉及任何这些潜在问题。关键是，委托人在律师开始代理前已经作伪证。如果伪证发生在代理期间，律师必须根据规则 3.3(a)(3) 采取补救措施。但由于委托人的行为发生在代理开始前，披露其保密信息会违反规则。A 不正确。委托人只是告知律师一项发生在代理开始前的伪证，律师无需退出。这并非委托人试图让律师协助欺骗审裁机构的情形。B 不正确。如上所述，律师在这种情况下不应披露委托人的伪证。C 不正确。如上所述，该信息以保密方式告知律师，也不符合允许律师披露的任何例外。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-053","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":53,"topic":"Model Rule 1.6(a)","question":"Attorney Alpha, a sole practitioner, recently suffered a heart attack and was advised that she could not return to work for six months. Alpha delivered all of her clients’ files to Attorney Beta, who is also a sole practitioner. Beta agreed to review each client’s file promptly, take any action necessary to protect each client’s interests, and treat the information in the files as confidential. Alpha then wrote her clients, advising each client that the client’s file had been delivered to Beta for review and for any action necessary to protect the client’s interest, and that the client was free to select another lawyer. Alpha knows that Beta is a competent attorney. Beta did not accept the file of any person whose interests were, or could be, adverse to the interests of any of Beta’s own clients. Was it proper for Alpha to deliver the files to Beta for review?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because Alpha knows that Beta is competent to protect the clients’ interests.","B":"Yes, because Beta agreed to treat the information in the files as confidential.","C":"Yes, because given her medical condition, Alpha’s delivery of the files was necessary to protect the clients’ interests.","D":"No, because Alpha did not obtain the prior consent of each client whose file was delivered to Beta."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent.” Model Rule 1.6(a). Here, it would be improper for the attorney to turn over all the client files and reveal all the confidences therein before receiving consent from the clients. A is incorrect. While Beta may be competent, this does not mean a client would necessarily want his or her confidences shared with her. B is incorrect. The simple fact that Beta has agreed to keep the files confidential would not change the fact that Alpha has already shared confidences by sharing the files in the first place. C is incorrect. While finding alternative representation may have been necessary, Alpha could not share client information without first gaining consent.","rule":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent.” Model Rule 1.6(a). Here, it would be improper for the attorney to turn over all the client files and reveal all the confidences therein before receiving consent from the clients. A is incorrect. While Beta may be competent, this does not mean a client would necessarily want his or her confidences shared with her. B is incorrect. The simple fact that Beta has agreed to keep the files confidential would not change the fact that Alpha has already shared confidences by sharing the files in the first place. C is incorrect. While finding alternative representation may have been necessary, Alpha could not share client information without first gaining consent.","irac":{"issue":"Was it proper for Alpha to deliver the files to Beta for review?","rule":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent.” Model Rule 1.6(a). Here, it would be improper for the attorney to turn over all the client files and reveal all the confidences therein before receiving consent from the clients. A is incorrect. While Beta may be competent, this does not mean a client would necessarily want his or her confidences shared with her. B is incorrect. The simple fact that Beta has agreed to keep the files confidential would not change the fact that Alpha has already shared confidences by sharing the files in the first place. C is incorrect. While finding alternative representation may have been necessary, Alpha could not share client information without first gaining consent.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent.” Model Rule 1.6(a). Here, it would be improper for the attorney to turn over all the client files and reveal all the confidences therein before receiving consent from the clients. A is incorrect. While Beta may be competent, this does not mean a client would necessarily want his or her confidences shared with her. B is incorrect. The simple fact that Beta has agreed to keep the files confidential would not change the fact that Alpha has already shared confidences by sharing the files in the first place. C is incorrect. While finding alternative representation may have been necessary, Alpha could not share client information without first gaining consent.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.6(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[162,163],"sourceAnswerPages":[264],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"独立执业的律师甲最近心脏病发作，医生建议她六个月内不能复工。甲将所有委托人案卷交给同样独立执业的律师乙。乙同意迅速审查每名委托人的案卷，采取保护其利益所需的任何行动，并对案卷信息保密。随后，甲致函各委托人，告知其案卷已交给乙审查并采取保护其利益所需的行动，同时告知委托人可自由选择另一名律师。甲知道乙是一名称职律师。乙没有接受任何其利益已经或可能与乙自己委托人利益相冲突之人的案卷。甲将案卷交给乙审查是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为甲知道乙有能力保护委托人利益。","B":"适当，因为乙同意对案卷中的信息保密。","C":"适当，因为鉴于甲的健康状况，交付案卷是保护委托人利益所必需的。","D":"不适当，因为甲在交付每名委托人的案卷前，没有取得该委托人同意。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。“除非委托人作出知情同意，律师不得披露与委托人代理有关的信息。”《职业行为示范规则》1.6(a)。本题中，律师在取得委托人同意前交出所有案卷，并披露其中全部保密信息，是不适当的。A 不正确。乙虽然可能称职，但这并不意味着委托人一定愿意与她分享自己的保密信息。B 不正确。乙同意对案卷保密，不能改变甲在一开始交出案卷时就已经披露保密信息这一事实。C 不正确。寻找替代代理虽然可能是必要的，但甲仍不能在未先取得同意的情况下分享委托人信息。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-054","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":54,"topic":"Model Rule 1.12","question":"A judge has recently resigned from the state trial court bench. When she was a judge, she supervised activity in cases pending before another judge while he was on vacation. In one pending case, she entered an administrative order changing the courtroom in which a particular case was to be tried. After trial and appeal, the case was remanded for a new trial. The plaintiff in that case decided to change lawyers and has asked the recently resigned judge to try the case. Will the judge be subject to discipline if she tries this case on behalf of the plaintiff?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the judge acted officially as a judge with respect to an aspect of the case.","B":"Yes, because the judge would try the case before a judge of the court on which she previously sat.","C":"No, because the judge did not act as a judge with respect to a substantial matter in or the merits of the case.","D":"No, because any information that the judge learned about the case while acting as a judge was a matter of public record."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.12, a lawyer cannot represent anyone in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a judge. However, a lawyer may represent someone in connection with a matter if the lawyer’s judicial actions regarding that matter were merely exercising remote or incidental administrative responsibility that did not affect the merits. Model Rule 1.12, Comment [1]. Here, all the lawyer did was enter an administrative order changing a courtroom. Consequently, she did not participate personally and substantially in the matter, and can thus represent the plaintiff. A is incorrect. While the judge did act officially as a judge with respect to an aspect of the case, she only changed a courtroom, and did not deal personally or substantially with the merits of the action. B is incorrect. There is no indication here that the court’s impartiality is being called into question. D is incorrect. Although information the judge learned could be a matter of public record, the main concern is the potential bias of the judge if she had already acted personally and substantially in the matter.","rule":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.12, a lawyer cannot represent anyone in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a judge. However, a lawyer may represent someone in connection with a matter if the lawyer’s judicial actions regarding that matter were merely exercising remote or incidental administrative responsibility that did not affect the merits. Model Rule 1.12, Comment [1]. Here, all the lawyer did was enter an administrative order changing a courtroom. Consequently, she did not participate personally and substantially in the matter, and can thus represent the plaintiff. A is incorrect. While the judge did act officially as a judge with respect to an aspect of the case, she only changed a courtroom, and did not deal personally or substantially with the merits of the action. B is incorrect. There is no indication here that the court’s impartiality is being called into question. D is incorrect. Although information the judge learned could be a matter of public record, the main concern is the potential bias of the judge if she had already acted personally and substantially in the matter.","irac":{"issue":"Will the judge be subject to discipline if she tries this case on behalf of the plaintiff?","rule":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.12, a lawyer cannot represent anyone in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a judge. However, a lawyer may represent someone in connection with a matter if the lawyer’s judicial actions regarding that matter were merely exercising remote or incidental administrative responsibility that did not affect the merits. Model Rule 1.12, Comment [1]. Here, all the lawyer did was enter an administrative order changing a courtroom. Consequently, she did not participate personally and substantially in the matter, and can thus represent the plaintiff. A is incorrect. While the judge did act officially as a judge with respect to an aspect of the case, she only changed a courtroom, and did not deal personally or substantially with the merits of the action. B is incorrect. There is no indication here that the court’s impartiality is being called into question. D is incorrect. Although information the judge learned could be a matter of public record, the main concern is the potential bias of the judge if she had already acted personally and substantially in the matter.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.12, a lawyer cannot represent anyone in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a judge. However, a lawyer may represent someone in connection with a matter if the lawyer’s judicial actions regarding that matter were merely exercising remote or incidental administrative responsibility that did not affect the merits. Model Rule 1.12, Comment [1]. Here, all the lawyer did was enter an administrative order changing a courtroom. Consequently, she did not participate personally and substantially in the matter, and can thus represent the plaintiff. A is incorrect. While the judge did act officially as a judge with respect to an aspect of the case, she only changed a courtroom, and did not deal personally or substantially with the merits of the action. B is incorrect. There is no indication here that the court’s impartiality is being called into question. D is incorrect. Although information the judge learned could be a matter of public record, the main concern is the potential bias of the judge if she had already acted personally and substantially in the matter.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.12"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[163],"sourceAnswerPages":[265],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名法官最近辞去州初审法院法官职务。任职期间，她在另一名法官休假时监督其待决案件中的活动。在其中一宗待决案件中，她签发了一项行政命令，变更该案预定审判的法庭。案件审判并上诉后，被发回重审。该案原告决定更换律师，请求最近辞职的法官出庭审理此案。如果该前法官代表原告出庭，是否会受到纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"会，因为她曾以法官身份正式处理案件的一个方面。","B":"会，因为她将会在自己曾经任职之法院的法官面前审理案件。","C":"不会，因为她没有以法官身份处理案件中的实质事项或实体问题。","D":"不会，因为她以法官身份了解到的任何案件信息均属公开记录。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。根据《职业行为示范规则》1.12，律师不得代理任何人处理其曾作为法官亲自且实质性参与的事项。不过，如果律师作为法官对该事项所采取的行动，仅是行使不影响实体问题的间接或附带行政职责，则可以代理。规则 1.12 评注 [1]。本题中，该律师所做的只是签发变更法庭的行政命令。因此，她没有亲自且实质性参与有关事项，可以代理原告。A 不正确。该法官虽然曾正式处理案件的一个方面，但只是变更法庭，并未亲自且实质性处理诉讼实体问题。B 不正确。没有迹象表明法院的公正性在此受到质疑。D 不正确。法官所了解的信息虽然可能属于公开记录，但主要关切在于，如果她已经亲自且实质性处理过该事项，就可能存在偏见。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-055","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":55,"topic":"Model Rule 1.8(a)","question":"An attorney decided to obtain a master’s degree in taxation, but lacked the funds required for tuition and expenses. The attorney consulted one of his clients, a wealthy banker, for advice about obtaining a loan. To the attorney’s surprise, the client offered the attorney a personal loan of $10,000. The attorney told the client that he would prepare the required note without charge. Without further consultation with the client, the attorney prepared and signed a promissory note bearing interest at the current bank rate. The note provided for repayment in the form of legal services to be rendered by the attorney to the client without charge until the value of the attorney’s services equaled the principal and interest due. The note further provided that if the client died before the note was fully repaid, any remaining principal and interest would be forgiven as a gift. The attorney mailed the executed note to the client with a transmittal letter encouraging the client to look it over and call with any questions. The client accepted the note and sent the attorney a personal check for $10,000, which the attorney used to obtain his master’s degree. A month after the degree was awarded, the client was killed in a car accident. The attorney had not rendered any legal services to the client from the date of the note’s execution to the date of the client’s death. Thereafter, in an action brought by the client’s estate to recover on the note, the court ruled that the note was discharged as a gift. Was the attorney’s conduct proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the client, without being requested by the attorney to do so, voluntarily made the loan.","B":"Yes, because the court ruled that the note had been discharged as a gift.","C":"No, because a lawyer may never accept a loan from a client.","D":"No, because the attorney did not comply with the requirements for entering into a business transaction with a client."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not enter into a business transaction with a client . . . unless: (1) the transaction and terms on which the lawyer acquires the interest are fair and reasonable to the client and are fully disclosed and transmitted in writing in a manner that can be reasonably understood by the client; (2) the client is advised in writing of the desirability of seeking and is given a reasonable opportunity to seek the advice of independent legal counsel on the transaction; and (3) the client gives informed consent, in a writing signed by the client, to the essential terms of the transaction and the lawyer’s role in the transaction, including whether the lawyer is representing the client in the transaction.” Model Rule 1.8(a). Here, importantly, the attorney did nothing regarding the desirability of seeking the advice of independent counsel. It’s also debatable whether the terms are fair and reasonable. A is incorrect. Many wrong answer choices highlight the fact that the questionable transaction was initiated by the client (for example, in client communication questions). Remember, simply because the client initiated something does not make it proper under the Rules. B is incorrect. The fact that the note operated as the attorney intended it to do did not make the note valid under the Rules. C is incorrect. An attorney can enter into a business transaction with a client, so long as he or she follows the Rules. Consequently, a loan is not completely improper under the Rules.","rule":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not enter into a business transaction with a client . . . unless: (1) the transaction and terms on which the lawyer acquires the interest are fair and reasonable to the client and are fully disclosed and transmitted in writing in a manner that can be reasonably understood by the client; (2) the client is advised in writing of the desirability of seeking and is given a reasonable opportunity to seek the advice of independent legal counsel on the transaction; and (3) the client gives informed consent, in a writing signed by the client, to the essential terms of the transaction and the lawyer’s role in the transaction, including whether the lawyer is representing the client in the transaction.” Model Rule 1.8(a). Here, importantly, the attorney did nothing regarding the desirability of seeking the advice of independent counsel. It’s also debatable whether the terms are fair and reasonable. A is incorrect. Many wrong answer choices highlight the fact that the questionable transaction was initiated by the client (for example, in client communication questions). Remember, simply because the client initiated something does not make it proper under the Rules. B is incorrect. The fact that the note operated as the attorney intended it to do did not make the note valid under the Rules. C is incorrect. An attorney can enter into a business transaction with a client, so long as he or she follows the Rules. Consequently, a loan is not completely improper under the Rules.","irac":{"issue":"Was the attorney’s conduct proper?","rule":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not enter into a business transaction with a client . . . unless: (1) the transaction and terms on which the lawyer acquires the interest are fair and reasonable to the client and are fully disclosed and transmitted in writing in a manner that can be reasonably understood by the client; (2) the client is advised in writing of the desirability of seeking and is given a reasonable opportunity to seek the advice of independent legal counsel on the transaction; and (3) the client gives informed consent, in a writing signed by the client, to the essential terms of the transaction and the lawyer’s role in the transaction, including whether the lawyer is representing the client in the transaction.” Model Rule 1.8(a). Here, importantly, the attorney did nothing regarding the desirability of seeking the advice of independent counsel. It’s also debatable whether the terms are fair and reasonable. A is incorrect. Many wrong answer choices highlight the fact that the questionable transaction was initiated by the client (for example, in client communication questions). Remember, simply because the client initiated something does not make it proper under the Rules. B is incorrect. The fact that the note operated as the attorney intended it to do did not make the note valid under the Rules. C is incorrect. An attorney can enter into a business transaction with a client, so long as he or she follows the Rules. Consequently, a loan is not completely improper under the Rules.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not enter into a business transaction with a client . . . unless: (1) the transaction and terms on which the lawyer acquires the interest are fair and reasonable to the client and are fully disclosed and transmitted in writing in a manner that can be reasonably understood by the client; (2) the client is advised in writing of the desirability of seeking and is given a reasonable opportunity to seek the advice of independent legal counsel on the transaction; and (3) the client gives informed consent, in a writing signed by the client, to the essential terms of the transaction and the lawyer’s role in the transaction, including whether the lawyer is representing the client in the transaction.” Model Rule 1.8(a). Here, importantly, the attorney did nothing regarding the desirability of seeking the advice of independent counsel. It’s also debatable whether the terms are fair and reasonable. A is incorrect. Many wrong answer choices highlight the fact that the questionable transaction was initiated by the client (for example, in client communication questions). Remember, simply because the client initiated something does not make it proper under the Rules. B is incorrect. The fact that the note operated as the attorney intended it to do did not make the note valid under the Rules. C is incorrect. An attorney can enter into a business transaction with a client, so long as he or she follows the Rules. Consequently, a loan is not completely improper under the Rules.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.8(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[163,164],"sourceAnswerPages":[265,266],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师决定攻读税务硕士学位，但没有足够资金支付学费和开支。律师向自己的一名委托人——一位富有的银行家——咨询如何取得贷款。出乎律师意料，委托人主动提出向律师提供 10,000 美元个人贷款。律师告诉委托人，自己会免费起草所需本票。律师没有与委托人进一步协商，便起草并签署了一张按当时银行利率计息的本票。本票规定，律师将以免费向委托人提供法律服务的方式偿还，直至服务价值等于到期本金及利息。本票还规定，如果委托人在本票全部清偿前去世，剩余本金和利息将作为赠与予以免除。律师将签署后的本票邮寄给委托人，并附信鼓励委托人审阅，有任何疑问就致电。委托人接受本票，向律师寄送 10,000 美元个人支票，律师用该款取得硕士学位。学位授予一个月后，委托人在车祸中死亡。从签署本票到委托人死亡，律师没有向委托人提供任何法律服务。此后，委托人遗产提起诉讼要求偿付本票，法院裁定本票债务已作为赠与而免除。律师的行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为是委托人在律师没有提出请求的情况下自愿提供贷款。","B":"适当，因为法院裁定本票债务已经作为赠与而免除。","C":"不适当，因为律师绝不能接受委托人提供的贷款。","D":"不适当，因为律师没有遵守与委托人订立商业交易的要求。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。“律师不得与委托人订立商业交易……除非：(1) 交易及律师取得利益所依据的条款对委托人公平合理，并以委托人可以合理理解的方式充分披露并书面传达；(2) 以书面形式告知委托人寻求独立法律顾问意见的可取性，并给予其合理机会寻求该意见；以及 (3) 委托人在自己签署的书面文件中，就交易的重大条款及律师在交易中的角色——包括律师是否在交易中代理委托人——作出知情同意。”《职业行为示范规则》1.8(a)。本题中，尤其重要的是，律师没有就寻求独立律师意见的可取性采取任何措施。而且，交易条款是否公平合理也值得怀疑。A 不正确。许多错误选项都会强调，是委托人主动发起了可疑交易（例如委托人沟通类问题）。请记住，仅仅因为委托人主动提出，并不会使其在规则下变得适当。B 不正确。本票按照律师意图发生效力，并不会使其在规则下有效。C 不正确。只要遵守规则，律师可以与委托人订立商业交易。因此，贷款在规则下并非绝对不当。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-056","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":56,"topic":"Model Rule 7.1 / Model Rule 7.3 / Model Rule 7.3(b)","question":"An attorney served as a state congressman immediately prior to reopening his law office in the state. The attorney printed and mailed an announcement of his return to private practice to members of the bar, persons who had previously been his clients, and personal friends whom he had never represented. The printed announcement stated that the attorney had reopened his law office, gave his address and telephone number, and added that he had recently served as a state congressman. Is the attorney subject to discipline for the announcement?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because it was mailed to persons who had not been his clients.","B":"Yes, because his service as a congressman is unrelated to his ability as a lawyer.","C":"No, because the information in the announcement is true.","D":"No, because all of the information was already in the public domain."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. In advertising his or her services, a lawyer may not make a false or misleading statement. Model Rule 7.1. Here, nothing in the mailing is false, and it appears that its main purpose is simply to alert members of the community that the lawyer is returning to private practice. A is incorrect. Although the mailing was sent to people who were not his clients, this does not violate Model Rule 7.3’s prohibitions regarding solicitation of clients. Importantly, written contact is prohibited when the lawyer knows the person does not want to be solicited or the solicitation involves coercion, duress, or harassment. Model Rule 7.3(b). Here, the mailing announcing the new law office appears to do none of these things. B is incorrect. Although his service as a congressman is unrelated to his ability as a lawyer, there is no prohibition on advertising this fact as long as the statement is truthful. Model Rule 7.1. Additionally, although the lawyer states he was once a congressman, the mailing does not appear to violate Model Rule 8.4(e)’s prohibition against implying an ability to influence improperly government agencies or officials. D is incorrect. Although all the information was in the public domain, it could still be presented or phrased in a way to create an improper misrepresentation under Model Rule 7.1 or an improper implication of influence under Model Rule 8.4(e). Consequently, the simple fact the information was in the public domain does not make the advertisement proper.","rule":"C is the correct answer. In advertising his or her services, a lawyer may not make a false or misleading statement. Model Rule 7.1. Here, nothing in the mailing is false, and it appears that its main purpose is simply to alert members of the community that the lawyer is returning to private practice. A is incorrect. Although the mailing was sent to people who were not his clients, this does not violate Model Rule 7.3’s prohibitions regarding solicitation of clients. Importantly, written contact is prohibited when the lawyer knows the person does not want to be solicited or the solicitation involves coercion, duress, or harassment. Model Rule 7.3(b). Here, the mailing announcing the new law office appears to do none of these things. B is incorrect. Although his service as a congressman is unrelated to his ability as a lawyer, there is no prohibition on advertising this fact as long as the statement is truthful. Model Rule 7.1. Additionally, although the lawyer states he was once a congressman, the mailing does not appear to violate Model Rule 8.4(e)’s prohibition against implying an ability to influence improperly government agencies or officials. D is incorrect. Although all the information was in the public domain, it could still be presented or phrased in a way to create an improper misrepresentation under Model Rule 7.1 or an improper implication of influence under Model Rule 8.4(e). Consequently, the simple fact the information was in the public domain does not make the advertisement proper.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for the announcement?","rule":"C is the correct answer. In advertising his or her services, a lawyer may not make a false or misleading statement. Model Rule 7.1. Here, nothing in the mailing is false, and it appears that its main purpose is simply to alert members of the community that the lawyer is returning to private practice. A is incorrect. Although the mailing was sent to people who were not his clients, this does not violate Model Rule 7.3’s prohibitions regarding solicitation of clients. Importantly, written contact is prohibited when the lawyer knows the person does not want to be solicited or the solicitation involves coercion, duress, or harassment. Model Rule 7.3(b). Here, the mailing announcing the new law office appears to do none of these things. B is incorrect. Although his service as a congressman is unrelated to his ability as a lawyer, there is no prohibition on advertising this fact as long as the statement is truthful. Model Rule 7.1. Additionally, although the lawyer states he was once a congressman, the mailing does not appear to violate Model Rule 8.4(e)’s prohibition against implying an ability to influence improperly government agencies or officials. D is incorrect. Although all the information was in the public domain, it could still be presented or phrased in a way to create an improper misrepresentation under Model Rule 7.1 or an improper implication of influence under Model Rule 8.4(e). Consequently, the simple fact the information was in the public domain does not make the advertisement proper.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. In advertising his or her services, a lawyer may not make a false or misleading statement. Model Rule 7.1. Here, nothing in the mailing is false, and it appears that its main purpose is simply to alert members of the community that the lawyer is returning to private practice. A is incorrect. Although the mailing was sent to people who were not his clients, this does not violate Model Rule 7.3’s prohibitions regarding solicitation of clients. Importantly, written contact is prohibited when the lawyer knows the person does not want to be solicited or the solicitation involves coercion, duress, or harassment. Model Rule 7.3(b). Here, the mailing announcing the new law office appears to do none of these things. B is incorrect. Although his service as a congressman is unrelated to his ability as a lawyer, there is no prohibition on advertising this fact as long as the statement is truthful. Model Rule 7.1. Additionally, although the lawyer states he was once a congressman, the mailing does not appear to violate Model Rule 8.4(e)’s prohibition against implying an ability to influence improperly government agencies or officials. D is incorrect. Although all the information was in the public domain, it could still be presented or phrased in a way to create an improper misrepresentation under Model Rule 7.1 or an improper implication of influence under Model Rule 8.4(e). Consequently, the simple fact the information was in the public domain does not make the advertisement proper.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.1","Model Rule 7.3","Model Rule 7.3(b)","Model Rule 8.4(e)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[164],"sourceAnswerPages":[266],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师在重新于本州开办律师事务所之前，刚刚结束州议员任期。律师印制了一份重返私人执业的公告，邮寄给律师协会成员、先前的委托人，以及从未由其代理过的私人朋友。印刷公告说明律师已经重新开办事务所，列出其地址和电话号码，并补充说明他最近曾担任州议员。律师是否因该公告而应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为公告被邮寄给从未由其代理过的人。","B":"是，因为担任州议员与其律师能力无关。","C":"否，因为公告所载信息真实。","D":"否，因为所有信息原本都已属于公共领域。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。律师为自己的服务做广告时，不得作出虚假或具有误导性的陈述。《职业行为示范规则》7.1。本题中，邮件内容没有任何虚假之处，其主要目的似乎只是告知社区成员该律师将重返私人执业。A 不正确。公告虽然寄给了一些从未由其代理过的人，但这并不违反规则 7.3 对招揽委托人的禁止。关键是，律师明知某人不愿被招揽，或者招揽涉及胁迫、强制或骚扰时，书面联系才受到禁止。规则 7.3(b)。本题的事务所开业公告显然不涉及这些情况。B 不正确。担任州议员虽然与律师能力无关，但只要陈述真实，规则并不禁止对此做广告。规则 7.1。此外，律师虽说明自己曾担任州议员，邮件看起来并未违反规则 8.4(e) 关于不得暗示有能力不当地影响政府机关或官员的规定。D 不正确。即使全部信息都在公共领域，仍可能以违反规则 7.1 的方式呈现或措辞，造成不当虚假陈述，也可能违反规则 8.4(e)，不当地暗示影响力。因此，仅仅因为信息处于公共领域，并不会使广告当然适当。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-057","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":57,"topic":"Model Rule 5.1(c)(1) / Model Rule 1.1","question":"An attorney placed an associate, recently admitted to the bar, in complete charge of the work of the paralegals in the attorney’s office. That work consisted of searching titles to real property, an area in which the associate had no familiarity. The attorney instructed the associate to review the searches prepared by the paralegals, and thereafter to sign the attorney’s name to the required certifications of title if the associate was satisfied that the search accurately reflected the condition of the title. This arrangement enabled the attorney to lower office operating expenses. The attorney told the associate that the associate should resolve any legal questions that might arise and not to bother the attorney because the attorney was too busy handling major litigation. Is it proper for the attorney to assign the associate this responsibility?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the paralegals are experienced in searching titles.","B":"Yes, because the attorney is ultimately liable for the accuracy of the title searches.","C":"Yes, because it enables the attorney to charge lower fees for title certification.","D":"No, because the attorney is not adequately supervising the work of the associate."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall be responsible for another lawyer’s violation of the Rules of Professional Conduct if: (1) the lawyer orders or, with knowledge of the specific conduct, ratifies the conduct involved.” Model Rule 5.1(c)(1). Here, the supervising attorney is ordering the associate to violate Model Rule 1.1 regarding competence. “Competent representation requires the legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness, and preparation reasonably necessary for representation.” Model Rule 1.1. Here, the new associate has no familiarity with title searches, and the attorney is apparently too busy to oversee his work. Consequently, this setup is a violation of the Rules. A is incorrect. Although the paralegals may know what is going on, this in no way justifies the senior attorney ordering the associate to do title work despite his lack of competence. B is incorrect. Although the attorney may be ultimately liable, the primary issue is that the attorney is ordering the associate to violate the Model Rules by doing work he lacks competence in. C is incorrect. This should be an obvious wrong answer choice. Many things could lead to lower fees (for example, having nonlawyers do legal work, or capping all legal work to what can be done in 15 minutes), so a simple price cut cannot justify a lawyer’s actions under the Model Rules.","rule":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall be responsible for another lawyer’s violation of the Rules of Professional Conduct if: (1) the lawyer orders or, with knowledge of the specific conduct, ratifies the conduct involved.” Model Rule 5.1(c)(1). Here, the supervising attorney is ordering the associate to violate Model Rule 1.1 regarding competence. “Competent representation requires the legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness, and preparation reasonably necessary for representation.” Model Rule 1.1. Here, the new associate has no familiarity with title searches, and the attorney is apparently too busy to oversee his work. Consequently, this setup is a violation of the Rules. A is incorrect. Although the paralegals may know what is going on, this in no way justifies the senior attorney ordering the associate to do title work despite his lack of competence. B is incorrect. Although the attorney may be ultimately liable, the primary issue is that the attorney is ordering the associate to violate the Model Rules by doing work he lacks competence in. C is incorrect. This should be an obvious wrong answer choice. Many things could lead to lower fees (for example, having nonlawyers do legal work, or capping all legal work to what can be done in 15 minutes), so a simple price cut cannot justify a lawyer’s actions under the Model Rules.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the attorney to assign the associate this responsibility?","rule":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall be responsible for another lawyer’s violation of the Rules of Professional Conduct if: (1) the lawyer orders or, with knowledge of the specific conduct, ratifies the conduct involved.” Model Rule 5.1(c)(1). Here, the supervising attorney is ordering the associate to violate Model Rule 1.1 regarding competence. “Competent representation requires the legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness, and preparation reasonably necessary for representation.” Model Rule 1.1. Here, the new associate has no familiarity with title searches, and the attorney is apparently too busy to oversee his work. Consequently, this setup is a violation of the Rules. A is incorrect. Although the paralegals may know what is going on, this in no way justifies the senior attorney ordering the associate to do title work despite his lack of competence. B is incorrect. Although the attorney may be ultimately liable, the primary issue is that the attorney is ordering the associate to violate the Model Rules by doing work he lacks competence in. C is incorrect. This should be an obvious wrong answer choice. Many things could lead to lower fees (for example, having nonlawyers do legal work, or capping all legal work to what can be done in 15 minutes), so a simple price cut cannot justify a lawyer’s actions under the Model Rules.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall be responsible for another lawyer’s violation of the Rules of Professional Conduct if: (1) the lawyer orders or, with knowledge of the specific conduct, ratifies the conduct involved.” Model Rule 5.1(c)(1). Here, the supervising attorney is ordering the associate to violate Model Rule 1.1 regarding competence. “Competent representation requires the legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness, and preparation reasonably necessary for representation.” Model Rule 1.1. Here, the new associate has no familiarity with title searches, and the attorney is apparently too busy to oversee his work. Consequently, this setup is a violation of the Rules. A is incorrect. Although the paralegals may know what is going on, this in no way justifies the senior attorney ordering the associate to do title work despite his lack of competence. B is incorrect. Although the attorney may be ultimately liable, the primary issue is that the attorney is ordering the associate to violate the Model Rules by doing work he lacks competence in. C is incorrect. This should be an obvious wrong answer choice. Many things could lead to lower fees (for example, having nonlawyers do legal work, or capping all legal work to what can be done in 15 minutes), so a simple price cut cannot justify a lawyer’s actions under the Model Rules.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.1(c)(1)","Model Rule 1.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[165],"sourceAnswerPages":[266,267],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师让一位最近刚取得执业资格的助理律师全面负责其办公室中律师助理的工作。该工作包括检索不动产产权，而助理律师对这一领域毫不熟悉。律师指示助理律师审查律师助理制作的检索结果；如果认为检索准确反映产权状况，就在所需产权证明上签署该律师的姓名。这项安排降低了律师的办公室运营成本。律师告诉助理律师，任何可能产生的法律问题都应由助理自行解决，不要打扰自己，因为自己忙于处理重大诉讼。律师将这项职责交给助理律师是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为律师助理拥有产权检索经验。","B":"适当，因为该律师最终会对产权检索的准确性承担责任。","C":"适当，因为这使律师可以降低产权证明收费。","D":"不适当，因为该律师没有充分监督助理律师的工作。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。“在以下情形下，律师应对另一名律师违反《职业行为规则》的行为负责：(1) 该律师命令实施有关行为，或在知悉具体行为时予以认可。”《职业行为示范规则》5.1(c)(1)。本题中，监督律师正在命令助理律师违反规则 1.1 有关称职能力的要求。“称职代理要求具备该代理合理需要的法律知识、技能、周全程度和准备。”规则 1.1。本题中，新助理律师不熟悉产权检索，而该律师显然过于忙碌，无法监督其工作。因此，这项安排违反规则。A 不正确。律师助理虽然可能了解工作，但这绝不能正当化高级律师命令不具备能力的助理律师办理产权工作。B 不正确。该律师虽然可能最终承担责任，但首要问题是，他正在命令助理律师完成自己不具备称职能力的工作，由此违反《职业行为示范规则》。C 不正确。这显然是错误选项。许多做法都可能降低收费（例如让非律师完成法律工作，或把所有法律工作限制为只能用 15 分钟完成），所以仅仅降价不能正当化律师在《职业行为示范规则》下的行为。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-058","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":58,"topic":"Model Rule 1.5(b)","question":"A law firm agreed to represent a client in various business matters. The written retainer agreement called for the client to pay hourly rates of $180 per hour for a partner’s time and $110 per hour for an associate’s time. The representation proceeded and the firm submitted monthly bills, which the client paid promptly, After two years, the firm decided to increase their hourly rates by $10. The firm thereafter billed the client at their new rates, but did not specifically inform the client of the increase. The client continued to pay monthly bills promptly. Are the firm’s partners subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the entire original fee agreement was required to be in writing.","B":"Yes, because the client did not consent to the increase.","C":"No, because the $10 hourly increase is reasonable.","D":"No, because the client agreed in writing to pay the firm’s hourly rate."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.5(b), any changes in the basis or rate of the fee or expenses shall be communicated to the client. Here, there was no indication that the client had any notice of the change in fee. The necessity of informing a client of a fee change and having that client consent to the increase is particularly apparent when an attorney represents an organization, as payments may be automated or done by some other person or department who may have no idea what the agreed-upon rate is. A is incorrect. Fees may change over time, so the original fee agreement is really irrelevant to the question of a change in fees. C is incorrect. Although the fee change may be reasonable, the issue is that the client may be paying it without knowledge or consent. D is incorrect. Careful reading of the question reveals that hourly rates of $180 and $110 were agreed to. Importantly, the agreement did not say the client would pay the firm’s “hourly rate” without any further clarification.","rule":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.5(b), any changes in the basis or rate of the fee or expenses shall be communicated to the client. Here, there was no indication that the client had any notice of the change in fee. The necessity of informing a client of a fee change and having that client consent to the increase is particularly apparent when an attorney represents an organization, as payments may be automated or done by some other person or department who may have no idea what the agreed-upon rate is. A is incorrect. Fees may change over time, so the original fee agreement is really irrelevant to the question of a change in fees. C is incorrect. Although the fee change may be reasonable, the issue is that the client may be paying it without knowledge or consent. D is incorrect. Careful reading of the question reveals that hourly rates of $180 and $110 were agreed to. Importantly, the agreement did not say the client would pay the firm’s “hourly rate” without any further clarification.","irac":{"issue":"Are the firm’s partners subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.5(b), any changes in the basis or rate of the fee or expenses shall be communicated to the client. Here, there was no indication that the client had any notice of the change in fee. The necessity of informing a client of a fee change and having that client consent to the increase is particularly apparent when an attorney represents an organization, as payments may be automated or done by some other person or department who may have no idea what the agreed-upon rate is. A is incorrect. Fees may change over time, so the original fee agreement is really irrelevant to the question of a change in fees. C is incorrect. Although the fee change may be reasonable, the issue is that the client may be paying it without knowledge or consent. D is incorrect. Careful reading of the question reveals that hourly rates of $180 and $110 were agreed to. Importantly, the agreement did not say the client would pay the firm’s “hourly rate” without any further clarification.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.5(b), any changes in the basis or rate of the fee or expenses shall be communicated to the client. Here, there was no indication that the client had any notice of the change in fee. The necessity of informing a client of a fee change and having that client consent to the increase is particularly apparent when an attorney represents an organization, as payments may be automated or done by some other person or department who may have no idea what the agreed-upon rate is. A is incorrect. Fees may change over time, so the original fee agreement is really irrelevant to the question of a change in fees. C is incorrect. Although the fee change may be reasonable, the issue is that the client may be paying it without knowledge or consent. D is incorrect. Careful reading of the question reveals that hourly rates of $180 and $110 were agreed to. Importantly, the agreement did not say the client would pay the firm’s “hourly rate” without any further clarification.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.5(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[165,166],"sourceAnswerPages":[267],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家律师事务所同意代理一名委托人处理各种商业事务。书面聘用协议规定，合伙人工时按每小时 180 美元收费，助理律师工时按每小时 110 美元收费。代理开始后，事务所每月提交账单，委托人均及时付款。两年后，事务所决定将两类小时费率各提高 10 美元。此后，事务所按新费率开具账单，却没有特别通知委托人费率提高。委托人继续及时支付每月账单。事务所合伙人是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为完整的原收费协议必须采用书面形式。","B":"是，因为委托人没有同意提高收费。","C":"否，因为每小时提高 10 美元属于合理金额。","D":"否，因为委托人已书面同意支付事务所的小时费率。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。根据《职业行为示范规则》1.5(b)，收费或开支的依据或费率发生任何变化时，均须告知委托人。本题没有迹象表明委托人知悉收费变化。在律师代理组织时，通知委托人收费变化并取得其同意尤为必要，因为付款可能自动完成，也可能由完全不知道约定费率的其他人员或部门处理。A 不正确。费用可能随时间变化，因此原收费协议与收费变更问题没有实际关系。C 不正确。收费变化虽然可能合理，问题却在于委托人可能在不知情或未同意的情况下付款。D 不正确。仔细阅读题目可以发现，双方约定的是 180 美元和 110 美元的小时费率。关键是，协议没有仅仅规定委托人将支付未作进一步说明的事务所“小时费率”。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-059","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":59,"topic":"Model Rule 1.3 / Model Rule 1.5","question":"A sole practitioner was appointed to represent a criminal defendant on appeal. A recently admitted lawyer who shared office space with the sole practitioner agreed to write the brief if the sole practitioner would pay him one-half of the statutory fee. The defendant agreed to the arrangement in writing, after full consultation. The recently admitted lawyer entered an appearance as co-counsel for the defendant and, with the sole practitioner’s knowledge, applied for and received several extensions of time to file the brief. Subsequently, the appellate court dismissed the appeal for failure to pursue the appeal. A third lawyer was later appointed to represent the defendant, whose conviction was affirmed after the appeal was reinstated. Is the sole practitioner subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because he neglected the defendant’s case.","B":"Yes, because he shared fees with the recently admitted lawyer.","C":"No, because the defendant agreed in writing to the co-counsel arrangement.","D":"No, because the affirmance by the appellate court indicated that the defendant’s appeal was without merit."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Model Rule 1.3. Importantly, delay can result in anxiety, statute of limitations issues, and possible destruction of the client’s legal position. Model Rule 1.3, Comment [3]. Also, unless the relationship is officially terminated, the lawyer should carry through to conclusion all matters undertaken for a client. Model Rule 1.3, Comment [4]. Although the attorney had gotten another lawyer to do the work, he never officially terminated his relationship with the client, and the lawyer repeatedly sought extensions that resulted in dismissal. Consequently, the attorney is subject to discipline for his lack of diligence in pursuing the matter. B is incorrect. Fees can be shared between lawyers who are not in the same firm if the division is proportional or each lawyer assumes joint responsibility, the client agrees in writing, and the total fee is reasonable. Model Rule 1.5[e]. However, it should be apparent from the question that the major harm here is the dismissal of the appeal, so the correct answer choice will clearly address that question. C is incorrect. Although the client agreed in writing to the arrangement, the major problem caused by this arrangement was the dismissal of the appeal. Consequently, the correct answer choice will address the dismissal. Although the client agreed in writing, this did not excuse the attorneys from diligently pursuing the matter. D is incorrect. This is another “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Although the defendant’s appeal might have been without merit, this did not excuse the attorney’s lack of diligence in pursuing the matter.","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Model Rule 1.3. Importantly, delay can result in anxiety, statute of limitations issues, and possible destruction of the client’s legal position. Model Rule 1.3, Comment [3]. Also, unless the relationship is officially terminated, the lawyer should carry through to conclusion all matters undertaken for a client. Model Rule 1.3, Comment [4]. Although the attorney had gotten another lawyer to do the work, he never officially terminated his relationship with the client, and the lawyer repeatedly sought extensions that resulted in dismissal. Consequently, the attorney is subject to discipline for his lack of diligence in pursuing the matter. B is incorrect. Fees can be shared between lawyers who are not in the same firm if the division is proportional or each lawyer assumes joint responsibility, the client agrees in writing, and the total fee is reasonable. Model Rule 1.5[e]. However, it should be apparent from the question that the major harm here is the dismissal of the appeal, so the correct answer choice will clearly address that question. C is incorrect. Although the client agreed in writing to the arrangement, the major problem caused by this arrangement was the dismissal of the appeal. Consequently, the correct answer choice will address the dismissal. Although the client agreed in writing, this did not excuse the attorneys from diligently pursuing the matter. D is incorrect. This is another “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Although the defendant’s appeal might have been without merit, this did not excuse the attorney’s lack of diligence in pursuing the matter.","irac":{"issue":"Is the sole practitioner subject to discipline?","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Model Rule 1.3. Importantly, delay can result in anxiety, statute of limitations issues, and possible destruction of the client’s legal position. Model Rule 1.3, Comment [3]. Also, unless the relationship is officially terminated, the lawyer should carry through to conclusion all matters undertaken for a client. Model Rule 1.3, Comment [4]. Although the attorney had gotten another lawyer to do the work, he never officially terminated his relationship with the client, and the lawyer repeatedly sought extensions that resulted in dismissal. Consequently, the attorney is subject to discipline for his lack of diligence in pursuing the matter. B is incorrect. Fees can be shared between lawyers who are not in the same firm if the division is proportional or each lawyer assumes joint responsibility, the client agrees in writing, and the total fee is reasonable. Model Rule 1.5[e]. However, it should be apparent from the question that the major harm here is the dismissal of the appeal, so the correct answer choice will clearly address that question. C is incorrect. Although the client agreed in writing to the arrangement, the major problem caused by this arrangement was the dismissal of the appeal. Consequently, the correct answer choice will address the dismissal. Although the client agreed in writing, this did not excuse the attorneys from diligently pursuing the matter. D is incorrect. This is another “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Although the defendant’s appeal might have been without merit, this did not excuse the attorney’s lack of diligence in pursuing the matter.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Model Rule 1.3. Importantly, delay can result in anxiety, statute of limitations issues, and possible destruction of the client’s legal position. Model Rule 1.3, Comment [3]. Also, unless the relationship is officially terminated, the lawyer should carry through to conclusion all matters undertaken for a client. Model Rule 1.3, Comment [4]. Although the attorney had gotten another lawyer to do the work, he never officially terminated his relationship with the client, and the lawyer repeatedly sought extensions that resulted in dismissal. Consequently, the attorney is subject to discipline for his lack of diligence in pursuing the matter. B is incorrect. Fees can be shared between lawyers who are not in the same firm if the division is proportional or each lawyer assumes joint responsibility, the client agrees in writing, and the total fee is reasonable. Model Rule 1.5[e]. However, it should be apparent from the question that the major harm here is the dismissal of the appeal, so the correct answer choice will clearly address that question. C is incorrect. Although the client agreed in writing to the arrangement, the major problem caused by this arrangement was the dismissal of the appeal. Consequently, the correct answer choice will address the dismissal. Although the client agreed in writing, this did not excuse the attorneys from diligently pursuing the matter. D is incorrect. This is another “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Although the defendant’s appeal might have been without merit, this did not excuse the attorney’s lack of diligence in pursuing the matter.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.3","Model Rule 1.5"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[166],"sourceAnswerPages":[267,268],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名独立执业律师被指定代理刑事被告提出上诉。另一名与其共用办公空间、最近刚取得执业资格的律师同意起草上诉书状，条件是独立执业律师向其支付法定费用的一半。被告在充分协商后，以书面形式同意该安排。新律师登记为被告的共同代理律师，并在独立执业律师知情的情况下，多次申请延期提交书状。随后，上诉法院因未推进上诉而驳回案件。之后，第三名律师被指定代理被告；上诉恢复后，被告定罪被维持。独立执业律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为他疏于处理被告案件。","B":"是，因为他与新律师分享费用。","C":"否，因为被告书面同意共同代理安排。","D":"否，因为上诉法院维持定罪表明被告的上诉没有依据。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。“律师代理委托人时应以合理的勤勉和及时程度行事。”《职业行为示范规则》1.3。延误尤其可能造成焦虑、时效问题，并可能破坏委托人的法律地位。规则 1.3 评注 [3]。此外，除非代理关系正式终止，律师应将为委托人接受的所有事项贯彻至终结。规则 1.3 评注 [4]。该律师虽然让另一名律师完成工作，却从未正式终止与委托人的关系，而另一名律师反复请求延期，最终导致上诉被驳回。因此，该律师因未勤勉推进事项而应受纪律处分。B 不正确。不在同一事务所的律师可以分享费用，只要分配与工作比例相符或每名律师承担共同责任，委托人书面同意，且总费用合理。规则 1.5(e)。不过，题目明显表明，本题的主要损害是上诉被驳回，所以正确选项应直接处理该问题。C 不正确。委托人虽然书面同意安排，但该安排造成的主要问题是上诉被驳回。因此，正确选项会处理驳回问题。委托人书面同意并不能免除律师勤勉推进事项的义务。D 不正确。这又是一个“没有损害，就没有违规”型错误选项。即使被告的上诉可能没有依据，也不能免除律师勤勉推进事项的责任。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice1-060","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 1","questionNumber":60,"topic":"Model Rule 1.5(d)(1)","question":"An attorney’s standard retainer contract in divorce cases provides for the payment of a fee of one-third of the amount of alimony or property settlement secured by the attorney. The attorney declines to represent clients who do not agree to this arrangement. Is the attorney’s standard retainer contract proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because clients often prefer to pay a lawyer a fee based on the outcome of the case.","B":"Yes, if a fee of one-third is not excessive.","C":"No, because a lawyer may not acquire a proprietary interest in a cause of action.","D":"No, because the fee is contingent."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not enter into an arrangement for, charge, or collect: (1) any fee in a domestic relations matter, the payment or amount of which is contingent upon the securing of a divorce or upon the amount of alimony or support, or property settlement in lieu thereof.” Model Rule 1.5(d)(1). Here, the attorney’s standard retainer contract violates this Rule. A is incorrect. While many clients may prefer to pay a contingent fee, this does not mean contingent fees are proper in all situations. This answer choice is another variation of the wrong answer choice that claims an attorney’s actions are proper because they are the client’s idea. B is incorrect. Although the fee may be reasonable, the contingent agreement violates the Rules. C is incorrect. The issue here is not the interest being acquired by the lawyer, but the fact that the lawyer is charging a contingent fee in this domestic matter.","rule":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not enter into an arrangement for, charge, or collect: (1) any fee in a domestic relations matter, the payment or amount of which is contingent upon the securing of a divorce or upon the amount of alimony or support, or property settlement in lieu thereof.” Model Rule 1.5(d)(1). Here, the attorney’s standard retainer contract violates this Rule. A is incorrect. While many clients may prefer to pay a contingent fee, this does not mean contingent fees are proper in all situations. This answer choice is another variation of the wrong answer choice that claims an attorney’s actions are proper because they are the client’s idea. B is incorrect. Although the fee may be reasonable, the contingent agreement violates the Rules. C is incorrect. The issue here is not the interest being acquired by the lawyer, but the fact that the lawyer is charging a contingent fee in this domestic matter.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney’s standard retainer contract proper?","rule":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not enter into an arrangement for, charge, or collect: (1) any fee in a domestic relations matter, the payment or amount of which is contingent upon the securing of a divorce or upon the amount of alimony or support, or property settlement in lieu thereof.” Model Rule 1.5(d)(1). Here, the attorney’s standard retainer contract violates this Rule. A is incorrect. While many clients may prefer to pay a contingent fee, this does not mean contingent fees are proper in all situations. This answer choice is another variation of the wrong answer choice that claims an attorney’s actions are proper because they are the client’s idea. B is incorrect. Although the fee may be reasonable, the contingent agreement violates the Rules. C is incorrect. The issue here is not the interest being acquired by the lawyer, but the fact that the lawyer is charging a contingent fee in this domestic matter.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not enter into an arrangement for, charge, or collect: (1) any fee in a domestic relations matter, the payment or amount of which is contingent upon the securing of a divorce or upon the amount of alimony or support, or property settlement in lieu thereof.” Model Rule 1.5(d)(1). Here, the attorney’s standard retainer contract violates this Rule. A is incorrect. While many clients may prefer to pay a contingent fee, this does not mean contingent fees are proper in all situations. This answer choice is another variation of the wrong answer choice that claims an attorney’s actions are proper because they are the client’s idea. B is incorrect. Although the fee may be reasonable, the contingent agreement violates the Rules. C is incorrect. The issue here is not the interest being acquired by the lawyer, but the fact that the lawyer is charging a contingent fee in this domestic matter.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.5(d)(1)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[166,167],"sourceAnswerPages":[268,269],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师在离婚案件中使用的标准聘用合同规定，律师费为该律师帮助取得的赡养费或财产和解金额的三分之一。对于不同意该安排的委托人，律师拒绝代理。该律师的标准聘用合同是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为委托人常常更愿意按照案件结果向律师支付费用。","B":"适当，只要三分之一的费用并不过高。","C":"不适当，因为律师不得取得诉因中的所有权利益。","D":"不适当，因为该费用取决于案件结果。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。“律师不得订立、收取或收取以下费用：(1) 家事关系事项中，以取得离婚、赡养费、扶养费或替代性财产和解的金额为付款条件或金额计算依据的任何费用。”《职业行为示范规则》1.5(d)(1)。本题中，律师的标准聘用合同违反该规则。A 不正确。许多委托人虽然可能更愿意支付风险代理费，但这并不意味着风险代理费在所有情况下都适当。该选项是另一种声称律师行为因由委托人提出就适当的错误选项。B 不正确。该费用虽然可能合理，风险代理安排仍违反规则。C 不正确。本题问题不在于律师取得了何种利益，而在于律师在这项家事关系事项中收取风险代理费。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-001","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":1,"topic":"Model Rule 1.3","question":"An attorney was retained to represent the client in defense of an action brought against the client by a plaintiff. In order to obtain ample time for settlement negotiations, the attorney immediately requested and obtained from opposing counsel a stipulation extending the client’s time to answer the complaint until 10 days after receipt of written demand from the plaintiff’s attorney. Four months later, no settlement had been reached, and on May 1, the plaintiff’s attorney wrote to the attorney demanding that an answer be filed within 10 days. When no answer was filed by May 15, the plaintiff’s attorney had a default judgment entered in favor of the plaintiff. The attorney was away on a two-month vacation when the plaintiff’s attorney’s letter was received in her office. When the attorney returned on June 15, she promptly moved to have the default set aside and her motion was granted. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because she did not make restitution to the client for any loss sustained by the client.","B":"Yes, because she did not make provision for the handling of her pending cases while she was away.","C":"No, because the default judgment was set aside.","D":"No, because she did not know that the plaintiff’s attorney had demanded that an answer be filed within 10 days."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Model Rule 1.3. Importantly, this means that if the attorney is away for a long time or suffers from a disability that causes an attorney to potentially neglect client matters, the lawyer must make some provision for dealing with ongoing matters. Here, the lawyer violated the Rules by failing to do so. A is incorrect. The issue here is not potential restitution. The bigger issue is that the attorney went away for two months without making provisions for her ongoing cases. This clearly violates the Rules regarding an attorney’s diligence. C is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice. While the default judgment was set aside, there was no guarantee that a court would do so. D is incorrect. The attorney should have known of the plaintiff’s attorney’s demand. If she had behaved diligently by making provisions for her very long absence, she would have.","rule":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Model Rule 1.3. Importantly, this means that if the attorney is away for a long time or suffers from a disability that causes an attorney to potentially neglect client matters, the lawyer must make some provision for dealing with ongoing matters. Here, the lawyer violated the Rules by failing to do so. A is incorrect. The issue here is not potential restitution. The bigger issue is that the attorney went away for two months without making provisions for her ongoing cases. This clearly violates the Rules regarding an attorney’s diligence. C is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice. While the default judgment was set aside, there was no guarantee that a court would do so. D is incorrect. The attorney should have known of the plaintiff’s attorney’s demand. If she had behaved diligently by making provisions for her very long absence, she would have.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Model Rule 1.3. Importantly, this means that if the attorney is away for a long time or suffers from a disability that causes an attorney to potentially neglect client matters, the lawyer must make some provision for dealing with ongoing matters. Here, the lawyer violated the Rules by failing to do so. A is incorrect. The issue here is not potential restitution. The bigger issue is that the attorney went away for two months without making provisions for her ongoing cases. This clearly violates the Rules regarding an attorney’s diligence. C is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice. While the default judgment was set aside, there was no guarantee that a court would do so. D is incorrect. The attorney should have known of the plaintiff’s attorney’s demand. If she had behaved diligently by making provisions for her very long absence, she would have.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Model Rule 1.3. Importantly, this means that if the attorney is away for a long time or suffers from a disability that causes an attorney to potentially neglect client matters, the lawyer must make some provision for dealing with ongoing matters. Here, the lawyer violated the Rules by failing to do so. A is incorrect. The issue here is not potential restitution. The bigger issue is that the attorney went away for two months without making provisions for her ongoing cases. This clearly violates the Rules regarding an attorney’s diligence. C is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice. While the default judgment was set aside, there was no guarantee that a court would do so. D is incorrect. The attorney should have known of the plaintiff’s attorney’s demand. If she had behaved diligently by making provisions for her very long absence, she would have.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[167],"sourceAnswerPages":[269],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师受聘代理委托人抗辩原告对其提起的诉讼。为争取充足时间进行和解谈判，律师立即请求对方律师同意延长委托人提交答辩状的期限；双方约定，期限延至收到原告律师书面要求后的第 10 日。四个月后，双方仍未和解。5 月 1 日，原告律师致函该律师，要求在 10 日内提交答辩状。至 5 月 15 日仍未提交，原告律师遂取得原告胜诉的缺席判决。原告律师的信送达该律师办公室时，律师正在进行为期两个月的休假。6 月 15 日律师返回后，立即申请撤销缺席判决，申请获准。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为她没有赔偿委托人遭受的任何损失。","B":"是，因为她休假期间没有安排人员处理待决案件。","C":"否，因为缺席判决已经撤销。","D":"否，因为她不知道原告律师要求在 10 日内提交答辩状。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。“律师代理委托人时应以合理的勤勉和及时程度行事。”《职业行为示范规则》1.3。尤其重要的是，如果律师长期离开，或因身体障碍而可能疏于处理委托人事务，就必须为正在办理的事项作出安排。本题中，律师未作任何安排，违反规则。A 不正确。本题问题不在于可能的赔偿，而在于律师离开两个月，却没有为待决案件作出安排。这显然违反律师勤勉义务。C 不正确。这是典型的“没有损害，就没有违规”型选项。缺席判决虽被撤销，但法院并不一定会这样做。D 不正确。律师理应知道原告律师的要求。如果她以勤勉方式为长时间离开作出安排，就会知悉。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-002","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":2,"topic":"Model Rule 7.1 / Model Rule 7.2(b)","question":"An attorney represented a plaintiff in an action against a manufacturer of a drain cleaner. The plaintiff’s complaint alleged that the manufacturer’s product exploded in use and caused her serious and permanent injuries. The jury agreed and awarded the plaintiff $5,000,000 in actual damages and an additional $5,000,000 in punitive damages. The manufacturer paid the judgment. The attorney made this recovery the cornerstone of an aggressive television advertising campaign for his law practice. In those ads, a voice-over discussed the $10,000,000 recovery obtained in the plaintiff’s case. The plaintiff praised the attorney’s legal skills in an on-camera statement, saying that no one would work harder on a case than the attorney. The plaintiff prepared her on-camera statement in response to the attorney’s request, but without any further involvement by the attorney, and she believed it to be entirely true. Is the attorney subject to discipline for using the television advertisement described above?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the advertisement is likely to create an unjustified expectation about the results the attorney will be able to achieve and is therefore misleading.","B":"Yes, because the attorney’s advertisement contains a client testimonial.","C":"No, because the plaintiff prepared the entire statement without any involvement by the attorney.","D":"No, because the result obtained in the plaintiff’s case was reported accurately, and the plaintiff believed that everything she said about the attorney was true."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer is not allowed to make false or misleading communications about the lawyer or the lawyer’s services. Model Rule 7.1. Importantly, “[a]n advertisement that truthfully reports a lawyer’s achievements on behalf of clients or former clients may be misleading if presented so as to lead a reasonable person to form an unjustified expectation that the same results could be obtained for other clients in similar matters without reference to the specific factual and legal circumstances of each client’s case.” Model Rule 7.1, Comment [3]. Here, the attorney’s advertisement is clearly trying to give prospective clients the idea that they are also likely to receive a large recovery if they hire the attorney. This is clearly misleading and a violation of the Rules. B is incorrect. A lawyer may not give anything of value to a person for recommending the lawyer’s services. Model Rule 7.2(b). However, as long as the attorney is not giving anything of value for the testimonial, and as long as the client’s testimonial is true and not misleading, it is not prohibited under the Rules. C is incorrect. Although the plaintiff prepared the statement, the advertisement is still misleading in that it implies anyone using the attorney’s services will receive a large recovery. Again, answer choices that imply something is proper because the client did it should be avoided. D is incorrect. While everything was accurate, the advertisement was improper because it would likely lead a reasonable person to conclude the same results could be reached for other clients without any reference to the specifics of that client’s case.","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer is not allowed to make false or misleading communications about the lawyer or the lawyer’s services. Model Rule 7.1. Importantly, “[a]n advertisement that truthfully reports a lawyer’s achievements on behalf of clients or former clients may be misleading if presented so as to lead a reasonable person to form an unjustified expectation that the same results could be obtained for other clients in similar matters without reference to the specific factual and legal circumstances of each client’s case.” Model Rule 7.1, Comment [3]. Here, the attorney’s advertisement is clearly trying to give prospective clients the idea that they are also likely to receive a large recovery if they hire the attorney. This is clearly misleading and a violation of the Rules. B is incorrect. A lawyer may not give anything of value to a person for recommending the lawyer’s services. Model Rule 7.2(b). However, as long as the attorney is not giving anything of value for the testimonial, and as long as the client’s testimonial is true and not misleading, it is not prohibited under the Rules. C is incorrect. Although the plaintiff prepared the statement, the advertisement is still misleading in that it implies anyone using the attorney’s services will receive a large recovery. Again, answer choices that imply something is proper because the client did it should be avoided. D is incorrect. While everything was accurate, the advertisement was improper because it would likely lead a reasonable person to conclude the same results could be reached for other clients without any reference to the specifics of that client’s case.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for using the television advertisement described above?","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer is not allowed to make false or misleading communications about the lawyer or the lawyer’s services. Model Rule 7.1. Importantly, “[a]n advertisement that truthfully reports a lawyer’s achievements on behalf of clients or former clients may be misleading if presented so as to lead a reasonable person to form an unjustified expectation that the same results could be obtained for other clients in similar matters without reference to the specific factual and legal circumstances of each client’s case.” Model Rule 7.1, Comment [3]. Here, the attorney’s advertisement is clearly trying to give prospective clients the idea that they are also likely to receive a large recovery if they hire the attorney. This is clearly misleading and a violation of the Rules. B is incorrect. A lawyer may not give anything of value to a person for recommending the lawyer’s services. Model Rule 7.2(b). However, as long as the attorney is not giving anything of value for the testimonial, and as long as the client’s testimonial is true and not misleading, it is not prohibited under the Rules. C is incorrect. Although the plaintiff prepared the statement, the advertisement is still misleading in that it implies anyone using the attorney’s services will receive a large recovery. Again, answer choices that imply something is proper because the client did it should be avoided. D is incorrect. While everything was accurate, the advertisement was improper because it would likely lead a reasonable person to conclude the same results could be reached for other clients without any reference to the specifics of that client’s case.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer is not allowed to make false or misleading communications about the lawyer or the lawyer’s services. Model Rule 7.1. Importantly, “[a]n advertisement that truthfully reports a lawyer’s achievements on behalf of clients or former clients may be misleading if presented so as to lead a reasonable person to form an unjustified expectation that the same results could be obtained for other clients in similar matters without reference to the specific factual and legal circumstances of each client’s case.” Model Rule 7.1, Comment [3]. Here, the attorney’s advertisement is clearly trying to give prospective clients the idea that they are also likely to receive a large recovery if they hire the attorney. This is clearly misleading and a violation of the Rules. B is incorrect. A lawyer may not give anything of value to a person for recommending the lawyer’s services. Model Rule 7.2(b). However, as long as the attorney is not giving anything of value for the testimonial, and as long as the client’s testimonial is true and not misleading, it is not prohibited under the Rules. C is incorrect. Although the plaintiff prepared the statement, the advertisement is still misleading in that it implies anyone using the attorney’s services will receive a large recovery. Again, answer choices that imply something is proper because the client did it should be avoided. D is incorrect. While everything was accurate, the advertisement was improper because it would likely lead a reasonable person to conclude the same results could be reached for other clients without any reference to the specifics of that client’s case.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.1","Model Rule 7.2(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[167,168],"sourceAnswerPages":[269,270],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理原告起诉一家下水道清洁剂制造商。原告起诉状称，制造商的产品在使用时爆炸，造成她严重且永久的伤害。陪审团支持原告，裁定实际损害赔偿 5,000,000 美元，另加惩罚性赔偿 5,000,000 美元。制造商支付了判决款。律师把这次赔偿作为其律师业务大规模电视广告宣传的核心。广告旁白讲述原告案件取得的 10,000,000 美元赔偿。原告在镜头前称赞律师的法律技能，并说没有人会比该律师更努力地办理案件。原告是应律师请求准备镜头前陈述的，但除此以外律师没有进一步参与；原告相信陈述全部属实。律师使用上述电视广告是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为广告很可能使人对律师能够取得的结果产生没有正当依据的期待，因而具有误导性。","B":"是，因为律师广告包含委托人见证陈述。","C":"否，因为整段陈述由原告自行准备，律师没有参与。","D":"否，因为广告准确报告了原告案件取得的结果，而且原告相信自己对律师所说的一切都是真实的。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。律师不得就律师本人或律师服务作出虚假或具有误导性的沟通。《职业行为示范规则》7.1。尤其要注意，“如律师如实报告为委托人或前委托人取得的成就，但呈现方式会使理性人在不考虑每名委托人案件具体事实和法律情形的情况下，产生没有正当依据的期待，认为其他委托人在类似事项中也会取得同样结果，该广告仍可能具有误导性。”规则 7.1 评注 [3]。本题中，律师广告显然试图使潜在委托人相信，聘请该律师也很可能获得巨额赔偿。这具有误导性并违反规则。B 不正确。律师不得为了他人推荐律师服务而给予任何有价值之物。规则 7.2(b)。不过，只要律师没有为见证陈述给予任何有价值之物，而且委托人陈述真实且不具误导性，规则并不禁止这种陈述。C 不正确。虽然陈述由原告准备，广告仍具有误导性，因为它暗示任何使用该律师服务的人都会获得巨额赔偿。应避免那些暗示因某事由委托人完成就当然适当的选项。D 不正确。虽然所有内容都准确，但广告仍不适当，因为它很可能使理性人在不考虑该委托人案件具体情况时，认为其他委托人也能取得同样结果。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-003","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":3,"topic":"Model Rule 1.6","question":"A corporation hired a law firm to handle all of its corporate work. The firm had not previously represented a corporation on an ongoing basis, but decided that it wanted to attract additional corporate clients. Accordingly, the partners handling the corporation’s work began a practice of giving to all lawyers in the firm, on a monthly basis, detailed descriptions of the work they were doing for the corporation for the purpose of illustrating what the firm could do for corporate clients. One of the partners mentioned this practice to the corporation’s management, and the corporation complained that its confidences had been violated. Was it proper for the partners to give detailed descriptions of the work being done for the corporation to other lawyers in the firm?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because, absent client instructions to the contrary, lawyers may discuss client information with other lawyers in the firm.","B":"Yes, because lawyers may discuss client information with other lawyers in the firm, regardless of client instructions to the contrary, so long as the disclosure does not disadvantage the client.","C":"No, because sharing the information increased the risk that it might be improperly disclosed to third parties outside the firm.","D":"No, because lawyers may not disclose client information, even to other firm lawyers, unless the disclosure is in furtherance of the representation."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. “Except to the extent that the client’s instructions or special circumstances limit that authority, a lawyer is impliedly authorized to make disclosures about a client when appropriate in carrying out the representation . . . . Lawyers in a firm may, in the course of the firm’s practice, disclose to each other information relating to a client of the firm, unless the client has instructed that particular information be confined to specified lawyers.” Model Rule 1.6, Comment [5]. Consequently, it was proper for the partners to give detailed descriptions of the work being done. B is incorrect. Lawyers may discuss cases within a firm so long as the client has not given instructions to the contrary. This answer choice should have been crossed out immediately since it implies that lawyers can willfully ignore client instructions regarding confidences. C is incorrect. Although more people knowing about something could theoretically increase the risk of that information getting out, the Rules allow lawyers to share information within a firm. D is incorrect. The Rules allow lawyers to disclose information to other lawyers within a firm. This makes sense, considering the desire for competent and efficient representation.","rule":"A is the correct answer. “Except to the extent that the client’s instructions or special circumstances limit that authority, a lawyer is impliedly authorized to make disclosures about a client when appropriate in carrying out the representation . . . . Lawyers in a firm may, in the course of the firm’s practice, disclose to each other information relating to a client of the firm, unless the client has instructed that particular information be confined to specified lawyers.” Model Rule 1.6, Comment [5]. Consequently, it was proper for the partners to give detailed descriptions of the work being done. B is incorrect. Lawyers may discuss cases within a firm so long as the client has not given instructions to the contrary. This answer choice should have been crossed out immediately since it implies that lawyers can willfully ignore client instructions regarding confidences. C is incorrect. Although more people knowing about something could theoretically increase the risk of that information getting out, the Rules allow lawyers to share information within a firm. D is incorrect. The Rules allow lawyers to disclose information to other lawyers within a firm. This makes sense, considering the desire for competent and efficient representation.","irac":{"issue":"Was it proper for the partners to give detailed descriptions of the work being done for the corporation to other lawyers in the firm?","rule":"A is the correct answer. “Except to the extent that the client’s instructions or special circumstances limit that authority, a lawyer is impliedly authorized to make disclosures about a client when appropriate in carrying out the representation . . . . Lawyers in a firm may, in the course of the firm’s practice, disclose to each other information relating to a client of the firm, unless the client has instructed that particular information be confined to specified lawyers.” Model Rule 1.6, Comment [5]. Consequently, it was proper for the partners to give detailed descriptions of the work being done. B is incorrect. Lawyers may discuss cases within a firm so long as the client has not given instructions to the contrary. This answer choice should have been crossed out immediately since it implies that lawyers can willfully ignore client instructions regarding confidences. C is incorrect. Although more people knowing about something could theoretically increase the risk of that information getting out, the Rules allow lawyers to share information within a firm. D is incorrect. The Rules allow lawyers to disclose information to other lawyers within a firm. This makes sense, considering the desire for competent and efficient representation.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. “Except to the extent that the client’s instructions or special circumstances limit that authority, a lawyer is impliedly authorized to make disclosures about a client when appropriate in carrying out the representation . . . . Lawyers in a firm may, in the course of the firm’s practice, disclose to each other information relating to a client of the firm, unless the client has instructed that particular information be confined to specified lawyers.” Model Rule 1.6, Comment [5]. Consequently, it was proper for the partners to give detailed descriptions of the work being done. B is incorrect. Lawyers may discuss cases within a firm so long as the client has not given instructions to the contrary. This answer choice should have been crossed out immediately since it implies that lawyers can willfully ignore client instructions regarding confidences. C is incorrect. Although more people knowing about something could theoretically increase the risk of that information getting out, the Rules allow lawyers to share information within a firm. D is incorrect. The Rules allow lawyers to disclose information to other lawyers within a firm. This makes sense, considering the desire for competent and efficient representation.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.6"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[168,169],"sourceAnswerPages":[270],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家公司聘请一家律师事务所处理其全部公司事务。该事务所此前从未持续代理一家公司，但决定吸引更多公司委托人。因此，处理该公司事务的合伙人开始每月向事务所全体律师详细介绍其为公司完成的工作，用于展示事务所可以为公司委托人做什么。一名合伙人向公司管理层提到这项做法，公司投诉自己的保密信息受到侵犯。合伙人向事务所其他律师详细说明正在为公司完成的工作，是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为只要委托人没有作出相反指示，律师就可以与事务所其他律师讨论委托人信息。","B":"适当，因为无论委托人是否作出相反指示，只要披露不会损害委托人，律师都可以与事务所其他律师讨论委托人信息。","C":"不适当，因为分享信息增加了信息被不当地披露给事务所外第三方的风险。","D":"不适当，因为除非披露有助于实施代理，否则律师即使向事务所其他律师也不得披露委托人信息。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。“除非委托人指示或特殊情形限制该权限，律师在适合实施代理时，获得默示授权披露委托人信息……律师事务所内的律师可以在事务所执业过程中相互披露与事务所委托人有关的信息，除非委托人已经指示某一特定信息仅可由指定律师知悉。”《职业行为示范规则》1.6 评注 [5]。因此，合伙人详细介绍正在完成的工作是适当的。B 不正确。只要委托人没有作出相反指示，律师可以在事务所内部讨论案件。该选项暗示律师可以故意无视委托人有关保密信息的指示，应立即排除。C 不正确。知道信息的人越多，理论上虽然会增加信息外泄风险，但规则允许律师在事务所内部分享信息。D 不正确。规则允许律师向同一事务所的其他律师披露信息。考虑到提供称职且高效代理的需要，这也合乎情理。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-004","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":4,"topic":"Model Rule 1.16(b)(6) / Model Rule 1.16(b) / Model Rule 1.1","question":"An attorney has been representing the client in a matter in litigation. During protracted pretrial proceedings, the client complained bitterly about the time and expense involved and insisted that the attorney take steps to terminate the pretrial proceedings. The attorney believes that to do so would jeopardize the client’s interests and has so informed the client. The attorney believes that the case cannot be adequately prepared for trial without further pretrial proceedings that will require an additional six months’ delay and involve further expense. The client insists that the attorney forgo any further pretrial proceedings and set the case for trial at the earliest available date. There are several other competent lawyers who are willing to undertake the representation. Is it proper for the attorney to ask leave of the court to withdraw?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because a lawyer may discontinue representation in a civil case at any time before trial.","B":"Yes, because the client’s conduct makes it unreasonably difficult for the attorney to represent the client effectively and competently.","C":"No, because the attorney must follow the client’s instructions.","D":"No, because the client did not consent to the attorney’s withdrawal."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer may withdraw from representing a client if the client insists on taking action with which the lawyer had a fundamental disagreement or if the representation has been rendered unreasonably difficult by the client. Model Rule 1.16(b)(6). Here, the client’s insistence on forgoing additional pretrial proceedings has made the representation unreasonably difficult. Importantly, the attorney believes he needs another six months to adequately represent the client’s interests, a very significant amount of time, while the client is insisting that they go to trial as soon as possible. A is incorrect. According to the Rules, an attorney must have cause before withdrawing from representation. Model Rule 1.16(b). Otherwise, a client’s rights could be in serious jeopardy if an attorney could withdraw at any time for any reason. C is incorrect. This answer choice should have been discarded immediately, as it implies that an attorney must absolutely follow a client’s instructions, even if those instructions cause the attorney to violate the Rules. Here, the client’s insistence on a quick trial would likely lead to incompetent representation, a violation of Model Rule 1.1. D is incorrect. The Rules regarding withdrawal only require an attorney to act reasonably to protect the client’s interests, such as by giving reasonable notice. Model Rule 1.16(d). The Rules do not require the client’s consent.","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer may withdraw from representing a client if the client insists on taking action with which the lawyer had a fundamental disagreement or if the representation has been rendered unreasonably difficult by the client. Model Rule 1.16(b)(6). Here, the client’s insistence on forgoing additional pretrial proceedings has made the representation unreasonably difficult. Importantly, the attorney believes he needs another six months to adequately represent the client’s interests, a very significant amount of time, while the client is insisting that they go to trial as soon as possible. A is incorrect. According to the Rules, an attorney must have cause before withdrawing from representation. Model Rule 1.16(b). Otherwise, a client’s rights could be in serious jeopardy if an attorney could withdraw at any time for any reason. C is incorrect. This answer choice should have been discarded immediately, as it implies that an attorney must absolutely follow a client’s instructions, even if those instructions cause the attorney to violate the Rules. Here, the client’s insistence on a quick trial would likely lead to incompetent representation, a violation of Model Rule 1.1. D is incorrect. The Rules regarding withdrawal only require an attorney to act reasonably to protect the client’s interests, such as by giving reasonable notice. Model Rule 1.16(d). The Rules do not require the client’s consent.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the attorney to ask leave of the court to withdraw?","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer may withdraw from representing a client if the client insists on taking action with which the lawyer had a fundamental disagreement or if the representation has been rendered unreasonably difficult by the client. Model Rule 1.16(b)(6). Here, the client’s insistence on forgoing additional pretrial proceedings has made the representation unreasonably difficult. Importantly, the attorney believes he needs another six months to adequately represent the client’s interests, a very significant amount of time, while the client is insisting that they go to trial as soon as possible. A is incorrect. According to the Rules, an attorney must have cause before withdrawing from representation. Model Rule 1.16(b). Otherwise, a client’s rights could be in serious jeopardy if an attorney could withdraw at any time for any reason. C is incorrect. This answer choice should have been discarded immediately, as it implies that an attorney must absolutely follow a client’s instructions, even if those instructions cause the attorney to violate the Rules. Here, the client’s insistence on a quick trial would likely lead to incompetent representation, a violation of Model Rule 1.1. D is incorrect. The Rules regarding withdrawal only require an attorney to act reasonably to protect the client’s interests, such as by giving reasonable notice. Model Rule 1.16(d). The Rules do not require the client’s consent.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer may withdraw from representing a client if the client insists on taking action with which the lawyer had a fundamental disagreement or if the representation has been rendered unreasonably difficult by the client. Model Rule 1.16(b)(6). Here, the client’s insistence on forgoing additional pretrial proceedings has made the representation unreasonably difficult. Importantly, the attorney believes he needs another six months to adequately represent the client’s interests, a very significant amount of time, while the client is insisting that they go to trial as soon as possible. A is incorrect. According to the Rules, an attorney must have cause before withdrawing from representation. Model Rule 1.16(b). Otherwise, a client’s rights could be in serious jeopardy if an attorney could withdraw at any time for any reason. C is incorrect. This answer choice should have been discarded immediately, as it implies that an attorney must absolutely follow a client’s instructions, even if those instructions cause the attorney to violate the Rules. Here, the client’s insistence on a quick trial would likely lead to incompetent representation, a violation of Model Rule 1.1. D is incorrect. The Rules regarding withdrawal only require an attorney to act reasonably to protect the client’s interests, such as by giving reasonable notice. Model Rule 1.16(d). The Rules do not require the client’s consent.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.16(b)(6)","Model Rule 1.16(b)","Model Rule 1.1","Model Rule 1.16(d)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[169],"sourceAnswerPages":[270,271],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师一直在一项诉讼事项中代理委托人。在漫长的审前程序中，委托人强烈抱怨耗时和费用，并坚持要求律师采取措施结束审前程序。律师认为这样做会危及委托人利益，并已告知委托人。律师认为，如果不继续进行审前程序，就无法为审判进行充分准备；而继续准备会造成额外六个月延误和更多开支。委托人坚持要求律师放弃任何进一步审前程序，尽早确定审判日期。另有数名称职律师愿意接受代理。该律师请求法院准许退出代理是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为律师可以在审判前任何时间停止代理民事案件。","B":"适当，因为委托人的行为使律师难以合理有效且称职地进行代理。","C":"不适当，因为律师必须服从委托人指示。","D":"不适当，因为委托人没有同意律师退出。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。如果委托人坚持采取律师存在根本分歧的行动，或者委托人使代理变得不合理地困难，律师可以退出代理。《职业行为示范规则》1.16(b)(6)。本题中，委托人坚持放弃额外审前程序，使代理变得不合理地困难。关键是，律师认为还需要六个月才能充分代理委托人利益，这是相当长的一段时间，而委托人却坚持尽快审判。A 不正确。根据规则，律师必须具备理由才能退出代理。规则 1.16(b)。否则，如果律师可以随时以任何理由退出，委托人权利可能受到严重危害。C 不正确。该选项暗示律师绝对必须服从委托人指示，即使指示会导致律师违反规则，应立即排除。本题中，委托人坚持迅速审判很可能导致不称职代理，违反规则 1.1。D 不正确。有关退出代理的规则只要求律师采取合理措施保护委托人利益，例如给予合理通知。规则 1.16(d)。规则并不要求委托人同意。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-005","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":5,"topic":"Rule 2.5(A) / Rule 2.5","question":"The judicial district in which a judge sits has a rule that allows litigants two postponements as a matter of right. After that, a litigant who moves for a postponement must convince the presiding judge that a postponement is appropriate. The judge routinely grants additional postponements because, in her view, “What harm is done if one of the litigants wants a postponement? The worst that can happen is that the parties have more time to negotiate and thus are more likely to settle.” Are the judge’s actions proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the judge is exercising her judicial discretion.","B":"Yes, because a party objecting to a postponement can seek appellate review.","C":"No, because judges have no official obligation to encourage private settlements.","D":"No, because the judge should expedite the determination of matters before her."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. A judge is required to perform his or her duties diligently. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.5(A). Importantly, issues should be resolved without unnecessary cost or delay. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.5, Comment [4]. Here, the judge is granting extensions without any consideration of the individual cases, likely leading to unnecessary cost and delay for the parties involved. A is incorrect. While a judge has discretion in his or her decision-making, this discretion is not absolute and is regulated by the Model Code of Judicial Conduct. B is incorrect. The fact that a party could seek appellate review would not have any effect on whether the judge was violating the Rules in the first place. At most, it would give the party recourse for the Rule violation after the Rule violation has already occurred. C is incorrect. The issue here is the possible harm caused by routine delays. This answer choice fails to address this problem and should be discarded.","rule":"D is the correct answer. A judge is required to perform his or her duties diligently. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.5(A). Importantly, issues should be resolved without unnecessary cost or delay. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.5, Comment [4]. Here, the judge is granting extensions without any consideration of the individual cases, likely leading to unnecessary cost and delay for the parties involved. A is incorrect. While a judge has discretion in his or her decision-making, this discretion is not absolute and is regulated by the Model Code of Judicial Conduct. B is incorrect. The fact that a party could seek appellate review would not have any effect on whether the judge was violating the Rules in the first place. At most, it would give the party recourse for the Rule violation after the Rule violation has already occurred. C is incorrect. The issue here is the possible harm caused by routine delays. This answer choice fails to address this problem and should be discarded.","irac":{"issue":"” Are the judge’s actions proper?","rule":"D is the correct answer. A judge is required to perform his or her duties diligently. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.5(A). Importantly, issues should be resolved without unnecessary cost or delay. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.5, Comment [4]. Here, the judge is granting extensions without any consideration of the individual cases, likely leading to unnecessary cost and delay for the parties involved. A is incorrect. While a judge has discretion in his or her decision-making, this discretion is not absolute and is regulated by the Model Code of Judicial Conduct. B is incorrect. The fact that a party could seek appellate review would not have any effect on whether the judge was violating the Rules in the first place. At most, it would give the party recourse for the Rule violation after the Rule violation has already occurred. C is incorrect. The issue here is the possible harm caused by routine delays. This answer choice fails to address this problem and should be discarded.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. A judge is required to perform his or her duties diligently. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.5(A). Importantly, issues should be resolved without unnecessary cost or delay. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.5, Comment [4]. Here, the judge is granting extensions without any consideration of the individual cases, likely leading to unnecessary cost and delay for the parties involved. A is incorrect. While a judge has discretion in his or her decision-making, this discretion is not absolute and is regulated by the Model Code of Judicial Conduct. B is incorrect. The fact that a party could seek appellate review would not have any effect on whether the judge was violating the Rules in the first place. At most, it would give the party recourse for the Rule violation after the Rule violation has already occurred. C is incorrect. The issue here is the possible harm caused by routine delays. This answer choice fails to address this problem and should be discarded.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 2.5(A)","Rule 2.5"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[169,170],"sourceAnswerPages":[271],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"某法官所在司法区有一项规则，允许诉讼当事人当然获得两次延期。此后，申请延期的当事人必须说服主审法官延期适当。该法官通常都会批准额外延期，因为她认为：“如果一方当事人希望延期，会有什么危害？最坏的结果只是双方有更多时间谈判，因而更可能和解。”该法官的行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为法官正在行使司法裁量权。","B":"适当，因为反对延期的一方可以寻求上诉审查。","C":"不适当，因为法官没有促进私人和解的职务义务。","D":"不适当，因为法官应加快处理其面前的事项。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。法官必须勤勉履行职责。《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.5(A)。尤其是，应当在不产生不必要费用或延误的情况下解决问题。规则 2.5 评注 [4]。本题中，法官不考虑个案情况一律批准延期，很可能给有关当事人造成不必要的费用和延误。A 不正确。法官虽在决策中享有裁量权，但该裁量并非绝对，仍受《司法行为示范准则》约束。B 不正确。一方可以寻求上诉审查，并不影响法官是否首先违反规则。上诉至多只是在违规已经发生后向该方提供救济。C 不正确。本题问题在于例行延期可能造成的损害。该选项没有处理这一问题，应予排除。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-006","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":6,"topic":"Rule 2.9 / Rule 2.9(A)(1) / Rule 2.9(A)(1)(b)","question":"A trial court judge had instructed his court clerk and his secretary that one of them should be present in the office during working hours to answer the telephone. One day, however, the secretary was out sick. The judge was in his office when his court clerk was at lunch, and when the telephone rang, the judge answered it. The call was from a lawyer in a case presently pending before the judge. The lawyer was calling to attempt to reschedule a pretrial conference set for the next day because of a sudden family emergency. The lawyer had tried to call opposing counsel on the case, but she was not answering his calls. The judge agreed to reschedule the pretrial conference for the following week. When the judge’s court clerk returned from lunch, the judge instructed the clerk to contact opposing counsel to inform her of the telephone call and the fact that the pretrial conference had been rescheduled. Did the judge act properly?","choices":{"A":"No, because the judge participated in an ex parte communication.","B":"No, because there was still time for the calling lawyer to notify opposing counsel in order to reach agreement on rescheduling the pretrial conference.","C":"Yes, because the ex parte communication was for scheduling purposes only and did not deal with substantive matters or issues.","D":"Yes, because there was no one else in the office to take the lawyer’s call."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. Generally, a judge is not allowed to engage in ex parte communications. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9. However, “when circumstances require it, ex parte communication for scheduling, administrative, or emergency purposes, which does not address substantive matters, is permitted.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1). If a judge does engage in such communication, he or she should make provision to promptly notify all the parties of the substance of the communication. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1)(b). Here, the circumstances of the situation made the judge’s actions proper, as no one else was there to answer the phone, the lawyer had an emergency and had tried to call opposing counsel, the matter merely involved rescheduling, and the judge promptly informed the opposing party of the rescheduling. A is incorrect. As stated above, in certain circumstances, it is proper for a judge to engage in ex parte communications. B is incorrect. While there may have been time to reach opposing counsel, the attorney was not required to do so since the ex parte communication merely involved scheduling issues. D is incorrect. Although there was no one else to take the call, the communication could have been improper if it had involved some other matter besides scheduling.","rule":"C is the correct answer. Generally, a judge is not allowed to engage in ex parte communications. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9. However, “when circumstances require it, ex parte communication for scheduling, administrative, or emergency purposes, which does not address substantive matters, is permitted.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1). If a judge does engage in such communication, he or she should make provision to promptly notify all the parties of the substance of the communication. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1)(b). Here, the circumstances of the situation made the judge’s actions proper, as no one else was there to answer the phone, the lawyer had an emergency and had tried to call opposing counsel, the matter merely involved rescheduling, and the judge promptly informed the opposing party of the rescheduling. A is incorrect. As stated above, in certain circumstances, it is proper for a judge to engage in ex parte communications. B is incorrect. While there may have been time to reach opposing counsel, the attorney was not required to do so since the ex parte communication merely involved scheduling issues. D is incorrect. Although there was no one else to take the call, the communication could have been improper if it had involved some other matter besides scheduling.","irac":{"issue":"Did the judge act properly?","rule":"C is the correct answer. Generally, a judge is not allowed to engage in ex parte communications. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9. However, “when circumstances require it, ex parte communication for scheduling, administrative, or emergency purposes, which does not address substantive matters, is permitted.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1). If a judge does engage in such communication, he or she should make provision to promptly notify all the parties of the substance of the communication. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1)(b). Here, the circumstances of the situation made the judge’s actions proper, as no one else was there to answer the phone, the lawyer had an emergency and had tried to call opposing counsel, the matter merely involved rescheduling, and the judge promptly informed the opposing party of the rescheduling. A is incorrect. As stated above, in certain circumstances, it is proper for a judge to engage in ex parte communications. B is incorrect. While there may have been time to reach opposing counsel, the attorney was not required to do so since the ex parte communication merely involved scheduling issues. D is incorrect. Although there was no one else to take the call, the communication could have been improper if it had involved some other matter besides scheduling.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. Generally, a judge is not allowed to engage in ex parte communications. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9. However, “when circumstances require it, ex parte communication for scheduling, administrative, or emergency purposes, which does not address substantive matters, is permitted.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1). If a judge does engage in such communication, he or she should make provision to promptly notify all the parties of the substance of the communication. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1)(b). Here, the circumstances of the situation made the judge’s actions proper, as no one else was there to answer the phone, the lawyer had an emergency and had tried to call opposing counsel, the matter merely involved rescheduling, and the judge promptly informed the opposing party of the rescheduling. A is incorrect. As stated above, in certain circumstances, it is proper for a judge to engage in ex parte communications. B is incorrect. While there may have been time to reach opposing counsel, the attorney was not required to do so since the ex parte communication merely involved scheduling issues. D is incorrect. Although there was no one else to take the call, the communication could have been improper if it had involved some other matter besides scheduling.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 2.9","Rule 2.9(A)(1)","Rule 2.9(A)(1)(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[170],"sourceAnswerPages":[272],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名初审法官曾指示法院书记员和秘书，工作时间内二人之一应留在办公室接听电话。然而有一天秘书因病缺勤。法院书记员外出午餐时，法官在办公室，电话响起便接听了。来电者是一宗当前由该法官审理之案件的律师。律师因突发家庭紧急情况，想重新安排定于次日的审前会议。他曾尝试联系对方律师，但对方没有接听。法官同意将审前会议改至下一周。法院书记员午餐回来后，法官指示其联系对方律师，告知来电内容及审前会议已经改期。法官行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"不适当，因为法官参与了单方沟通。","B":"不适当，因为来电律师仍有时间通知对方律师，以就审前会议改期达成协议。","C":"适当，因为单方沟通仅为排期目的，不涉及实体事项或问题。","D":"适当，因为办公室中没有其他人可以接听律师电话。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。一般而言，法官不得进行单方沟通。《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.9。不过，“情势需要时，不涉及实体事项、仅为排期、行政或紧急目的的单方沟通可以进行”。规则 2.9(A)(1)。法官进行此类沟通后，应作出安排，立即将沟通实质内容通知所有当事人。规则 2.9(A)(1)(b)。本题情形使法官行为适当：没有其他人接听电话，律师遇到紧急情况且尝试联系对方律师，事项仅涉及重新排期，而且法官立即通知对方改期情况。A 不正确。如上所述，在某些情况下法官可以适当地进行单方沟通。B 不正确。虽然可能仍有时间联系对方律师，但由于单方沟通只涉及排期，来电律师无需先取得对方同意。D 不正确。虽然没有其他人接听电话，但如果沟通涉及排期以外的事项，仍可能不适当。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-007","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":7,"topic":"Model Rule 3.7(a)(1)","question":"An attorney, who had represented a client for many years, prepared the client’s will and acted as one of the two subscribing witnesses to its execution. The client’s sister and brother were his sole heirs. The will left the client’s entire estate to his sister and nothing to his brother. Upon the client’s death two years later the executor named in the will asked the attorney to act as his lawyer in the probate of the will and the administration of the estate. At that time, the executor informed the attorney that the client’s brother would concede that the will was properly executed but intended to contest the will on the ground that he had been excluded because of fraud previously practiced on the client by the client’s sister. The other subscribing witness to the will predeceased the client, and the attorney will be called as a witness solely for the purpose of establishing the due execution of the will. Is it proper for the attorney to accept the representation?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because there is no contested issue of fact with respect to the formal execution of the will.","B":"Yes, because the executor has no beneficial interest under the will.","C":"No, because the attorney’s services are not necessary to avoid substantial hardship to the executor.","D":"No, because the attorney will be called as a witness in the case."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not act as advocate at a trial in which the lawyer is likely to be a necessary witness unless: (1) the testimony relates to an uncontested issue.” Model Rule 3.7(a)(1). Here, since there is no contested issue of fact with respect to the formal execution of the will, the attorney is not barred from accepting representation. B is incorrect. While the executor may have no beneficial interest, the issue is that the attorney is probating and administrating a will he was also a subscribing witness for. The executor’s financial position would not address this particular problem. C is incorrect. Since the attorney’s testimony relates to an uncontested issue, there is no need for an evaluation of the possible hardships caused by the attorney’s services being unutilized. D is incorrect. As stated above, if the testimony relates to an uncontested issue, the lawyer can still act as an advocate at trial.","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not act as advocate at a trial in which the lawyer is likely to be a necessary witness unless: (1) the testimony relates to an uncontested issue.” Model Rule 3.7(a)(1). Here, since there is no contested issue of fact with respect to the formal execution of the will, the attorney is not barred from accepting representation. B is incorrect. While the executor may have no beneficial interest, the issue is that the attorney is probating and administrating a will he was also a subscribing witness for. The executor’s financial position would not address this particular problem. C is incorrect. Since the attorney’s testimony relates to an uncontested issue, there is no need for an evaluation of the possible hardships caused by the attorney’s services being unutilized. D is incorrect. As stated above, if the testimony relates to an uncontested issue, the lawyer can still act as an advocate at trial.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the attorney to accept the representation?","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not act as advocate at a trial in which the lawyer is likely to be a necessary witness unless: (1) the testimony relates to an uncontested issue.” Model Rule 3.7(a)(1). Here, since there is no contested issue of fact with respect to the formal execution of the will, the attorney is not barred from accepting representation. B is incorrect. While the executor may have no beneficial interest, the issue is that the attorney is probating and administrating a will he was also a subscribing witness for. The executor’s financial position would not address this particular problem. C is incorrect. Since the attorney’s testimony relates to an uncontested issue, there is no need for an evaluation of the possible hardships caused by the attorney’s services being unutilized. D is incorrect. As stated above, if the testimony relates to an uncontested issue, the lawyer can still act as an advocate at trial.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not act as advocate at a trial in which the lawyer is likely to be a necessary witness unless: (1) the testimony relates to an uncontested issue.” Model Rule 3.7(a)(1). Here, since there is no contested issue of fact with respect to the formal execution of the will, the attorney is not barred from accepting representation. B is incorrect. While the executor may have no beneficial interest, the issue is that the attorney is probating and administrating a will he was also a subscribing witness for. The executor’s financial position would not address this particular problem. C is incorrect. Since the attorney’s testimony relates to an uncontested issue, there is no need for an evaluation of the possible hardships caused by the attorney’s services being unutilized. D is incorrect. As stated above, if the testimony relates to an uncontested issue, the lawyer can still act as an advocate at trial.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.7(a)(1)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[171],"sourceAnswerPages":[272,273],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师为某委托人提供多年法律服务，曾起草其遗嘱，并作为两名签署见证人之一见证遗嘱签立。委托人的姐妹和兄弟是其仅有的法定继承人。遗嘱把全部遗产留给姐妹，没有给兄弟留下任何财产。两年后委托人去世，遗嘱指定的执行人请求该律师代理遗嘱检验和遗产管理。此时，执行人告知律师，委托人的兄弟承认遗嘱形式上已适当签立，但拟以姐妹先前对委托人实施欺诈、因而导致自己被排除为由，对遗嘱提出异议。另一名签署见证人先于委托人去世，而该律师将仅为证明遗嘱适当签立而被传唤作证。律师接受代理是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为遗嘱的形式签立不存在有争议的事实问题。","B":"适当，因为遗嘱执行人在遗嘱下没有受益利益。","C":"不适当，因为为避免执行人遭受重大困难，并非必须由该律师提供服务。","D":"不适当，因为该律师将在案件中被传唤作证。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。“如果律师很可能成为必要证人，就不得在审判中担任辩护人，但下列情形除外：(1) 证言涉及没有争议的问题。”《职业行为示范规则》3.7(a)(1)。本题中，遗嘱的形式签立不存在有争议的事实问题，因此律师不受禁止，可以接受代理。B 不正确。执行人虽然可能没有受益利益，但问题在于律师正在检验并管理一份其本人同时担任签署见证人的遗嘱。执行人的经济地位不能解决这个具体问题。C 不正确。由于律师证言涉及没有争议的问题，无须评估不使用其服务可能造成的困难。D 不正确。如上所述，如果证言涉及没有争议的问题，律师仍可在审判中担任辩护人。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-008","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":8,"topic":"Model Rule 4.4(a)","question":"An attorney has been retained to defend an adult charged with a sex offense involving a minor. The attorney believes that, in order to win the case, she must keep parents of minor children off the jury. The attorney instructed her investigator as follows: “Visit the neighborhood of those prospective jurors on the panel with minor children. Ask the neighbors if they know of any kind of unusual sex activity of the prospective juror or any member of the family. This talk will get back to the prospective jurors, and they will think of excuses not to serve. But don’t under any circumstances talk directly with any prospective juror or member of the family.” Many of the prospective jurors investigated were, in fact, selected to serve on the jury. Is the attorney subject to discipline for so instructing her investigator?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the prospective jurors investigated were, in fact, selected to serve on the jury in the case.","B":"Yes, because the investigation is intended to harass prospective jurors and members of their families.","C":"No, because the matters inquired into might be relevant to a prospective juror’s qualifications to serve in the case.","D":"No, because no prospective juror was directly contacted."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. “In representing a client, a lawyer shall not use means that have no substantial purpose other than to embarrass, delay, or burden a third person.” Model Rule 4.4(a). Here, the lawyer’s plan is simply to start malicious gossip that will embarrass the prospective jurors into choosing not to serve. A is incorrect. Although many of the jurors were selected to serve on the jury, this did not excuse the lawyer’s actions in trying to embarrass them. C is incorrect. Importantly, the lawyer’s intent here is not to engage in any real investigation—he just wants to keep all of the parents of minor children off the jury through embarrassment and intimidation. D is incorrect. Although the lawyer did not directly contact a prospective juror, he did use means that were intended to embarrass them and keep them from serving on the jury.","rule":"B is the correct answer. “In representing a client, a lawyer shall not use means that have no substantial purpose other than to embarrass, delay, or burden a third person.” Model Rule 4.4(a). Here, the lawyer’s plan is simply to start malicious gossip that will embarrass the prospective jurors into choosing not to serve. A is incorrect. Although many of the jurors were selected to serve on the jury, this did not excuse the lawyer’s actions in trying to embarrass them. C is incorrect. Importantly, the lawyer’s intent here is not to engage in any real investigation—he just wants to keep all of the parents of minor children off the jury through embarrassment and intimidation. D is incorrect. Although the lawyer did not directly contact a prospective juror, he did use means that were intended to embarrass them and keep them from serving on the jury.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for so instructing her investigator?","rule":"B is the correct answer. “In representing a client, a lawyer shall not use means that have no substantial purpose other than to embarrass, delay, or burden a third person.” Model Rule 4.4(a). Here, the lawyer’s plan is simply to start malicious gossip that will embarrass the prospective jurors into choosing not to serve. A is incorrect. Although many of the jurors were selected to serve on the jury, this did not excuse the lawyer’s actions in trying to embarrass them. C is incorrect. Importantly, the lawyer’s intent here is not to engage in any real investigation—he just wants to keep all of the parents of minor children off the jury through embarrassment and intimidation. D is incorrect. Although the lawyer did not directly contact a prospective juror, he did use means that were intended to embarrass them and keep them from serving on the jury.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. “In representing a client, a lawyer shall not use means that have no substantial purpose other than to embarrass, delay, or burden a third person.” Model Rule 4.4(a). Here, the lawyer’s plan is simply to start malicious gossip that will embarrass the prospective jurors into choosing not to serve. A is incorrect. Although many of the jurors were selected to serve on the jury, this did not excuse the lawyer’s actions in trying to embarrass them. C is incorrect. Importantly, the lawyer’s intent here is not to engage in any real investigation—he just wants to keep all of the parents of minor children off the jury through embarrassment and intimidation. D is incorrect. Although the lawyer did not directly contact a prospective juror, he did use means that were intended to embarrass them and keep them from serving on the jury.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 4.4(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[171,172],"sourceAnswerPages":[273],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师受聘为一名被控对未成年人实施性犯罪的成年人进行抗辩。律师认为，要胜诉就必须使有未成年子女的父母不能进入陪审团。她指示调查员：“去那些陪审员候选人中有未成年子女者的住所附近，询问邻居是否知道该候选人或其家庭成员有任何异常性行为。这些谈话会传到候选人耳中，他们就会找借口不参加。但无论如何都不要直接与候选人或其家庭成员交谈。”许多受到调查的候选人事实上被选为陪审员。律师因这样指示调查员是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为受到调查的候选人事实上被选为本案陪审员。","B":"是，因为该调查意在骚扰候选陪审员及其家人。","C":"否，因为所询问的事项可能与候选人是否具备本案陪审员资格有关。","D":"否，因为没有直接联系任何候选陪审员。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。“律师代理委托人时，不得采用除使第三人难堪、延误或增加负担外没有重大目的的手段。”《职业行为示范规则》4.4(a)。本题中，律师的计划只是散播恶意流言，使候选陪审员因难堪而选择不参加。A 不正确。即使许多候选人最终成为陪审员，也不能使律师试图令其难堪的行为得到免责。C 不正确。尤其要注意，律师的意图并非进行任何真正的调查，而是通过羞辱和恐吓，使所有未成年子女的父母退出陪审团。D 不正确。律师虽然没有直接联系候选陪审员，却使用了意在令其难堪并使其退出陪审团的手段。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-009","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":9,"topic":"Model Rule 1.15","question":"A client telephoned an attorney who had previously represented him. The client described a problem on which he needed advice and made an appointment for the following week to discuss the matter with the attorney. Prior to the appointment, the attorney performed five hours of preliminary research on the client’s problem. At the end of the appointment the client agreed that the attorney should pursue the matter and agreed to a fee of $100 per hour. The client then gave the attorney a check for $5,000 to cover the five hours already worked and as an advance on additional fees and expenses. The attorney gave the check to the office bookkeeper with directions to deposit the check into the client trust account and immediately transfer $3,000 to the general office account to cover the five hours of research already conducted plus the 25 additional hours she would spend on the matter the following week. At that time, the attorney reasonably believed that she would spend 25 additional hours on the case. The bookkeeper followed these directions. The next week, the attorney worked diligently on the matter for 23 hours. Reasonably believing that no significant work remained to be done on the matter, the attorney directed the bookkeeper to transfer $200 from the general office account to the client trust account. The attorney then called the client and made an appointment to discuss the status of the matter. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney accepted legal fees in advance of performing the work.","B":"Yes, because the attorney transferred funds for unearned fees to the general office account.","C":"No, because the attorney transferred the $200 owed to the client from the general office account to the client trust account.","D":"No, because the attorney reasonably believed that she would spend 25 additional hours on the case."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. When dealing with client funds: (1) client funds must always be kept separate from the lawyer’s funds in a designated clients’ escrow account; (2) as soon as the client’s interest in the funds is fixed, the client’s money must be delivered to the client; and (3) if both the client and the attorney claim any interest in any part of the funds—for example, if a dispute arises as to the lawyer’s fees—the funds must remain in the separate client account until there is an accounting and their respective interests can be severed. Model Rule 1.15. Since the fees were unearned, they still belonged to the client, and it was improper for the attorney to transfer those fees to the general office account. A is incorrect. An attorney can accept legal fees in advance of doing the work, but those fees must be kept separate from the lawyer’s funds in a designated clients’ escrow account until the fees are earned by the attorney. C is incorrect. Although the attorney attempted to fix the situation by replacing the money, he had already removed the client funds without justification and violated the Rules. D is incorrect. The issue is that client funds were taken from the client account and put into the attorney’s account. What the attorney believed when he was moving the money is irrelevant.","rule":"B is the correct answer. When dealing with client funds: (1) client funds must always be kept separate from the lawyer’s funds in a designated clients’ escrow account; (2) as soon as the client’s interest in the funds is fixed, the client’s money must be delivered to the client; and (3) if both the client and the attorney claim any interest in any part of the funds—for example, if a dispute arises as to the lawyer’s fees—the funds must remain in the separate client account until there is an accounting and their respective interests can be severed. Model Rule 1.15. Since the fees were unearned, they still belonged to the client, and it was improper for the attorney to transfer those fees to the general office account. A is incorrect. An attorney can accept legal fees in advance of doing the work, but those fees must be kept separate from the lawyer’s funds in a designated clients’ escrow account until the fees are earned by the attorney. C is incorrect. Although the attorney attempted to fix the situation by replacing the money, he had already removed the client funds without justification and violated the Rules. D is incorrect. The issue is that client funds were taken from the client account and put into the attorney’s account. What the attorney believed when he was moving the money is irrelevant.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. When dealing with client funds: (1) client funds must always be kept separate from the lawyer’s funds in a designated clients’ escrow account; (2) as soon as the client’s interest in the funds is fixed, the client’s money must be delivered to the client; and (3) if both the client and the attorney claim any interest in any part of the funds—for example, if a dispute arises as to the lawyer’s fees—the funds must remain in the separate client account until there is an accounting and their respective interests can be severed. Model Rule 1.15. Since the fees were unearned, they still belonged to the client, and it was improper for the attorney to transfer those fees to the general office account. A is incorrect. An attorney can accept legal fees in advance of doing the work, but those fees must be kept separate from the lawyer’s funds in a designated clients’ escrow account until the fees are earned by the attorney. C is incorrect. Although the attorney attempted to fix the situation by replacing the money, he had already removed the client funds without justification and violated the Rules. D is incorrect. The issue is that client funds were taken from the client account and put into the attorney’s account. What the attorney believed when he was moving the money is irrelevant.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. When dealing with client funds: (1) client funds must always be kept separate from the lawyer’s funds in a designated clients’ escrow account; (2) as soon as the client’s interest in the funds is fixed, the client’s money must be delivered to the client; and (3) if both the client and the attorney claim any interest in any part of the funds—for example, if a dispute arises as to the lawyer’s fees—the funds must remain in the separate client account until there is an accounting and their respective interests can be severed. Model Rule 1.15. Since the fees were unearned, they still belonged to the client, and it was improper for the attorney to transfer those fees to the general office account. A is incorrect. An attorney can accept legal fees in advance of doing the work, but those fees must be kept separate from the lawyer’s funds in a designated clients’ escrow account until the fees are earned by the attorney. C is incorrect. Although the attorney attempted to fix the situation by replacing the money, he had already removed the client funds without justification and violated the Rules. D is incorrect. The issue is that client funds were taken from the client account and put into the attorney’s account. What the attorney believed when he was moving the money is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.15"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[172,173],"sourceAnswerPages":[273,274],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名委托人致电先前曾代理过自己的律师，说明一个需要意见的问题，并预约下一周讨论。在会面前，律师已就委托人的问题进行五小时初步研究。会面结束时，委托人同意律师继续处理事项，也同意每小时 100 美元的收费。委托人随后向律师开具 5,000 美元支票，用于支付已经完成的五小时工作，并预付额外费用和开支。律师把支票交给办公室记账员，指示将其存入委托人信托账户，然后立即把 3,000 美元转入一般办公账户，用于五小时研究以及她预计下一周将在该事项上花费的另外 25 小时。当时，律师合理地认为会再花 25 小时。记账员依指示操作。下一周，律师勤勉工作 23 小时。律师合理地认为没有重要工作尚待完成，便指示记账员从一般办公账户向委托人信托账户转回 200 美元。随后，律师致电委托人并预约讨论事项状态。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师在完成工作前接受了法律服务费。","B":"是，因为律师把尚未赚取的费用转入一般办公账户。","C":"否，因为律师从一般办公账户把应退还委托人的 200 美元转回委托人信托账户。","D":"否，因为律师合理地认为自己会再花 25 小时处理案件。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。处理委托人资金时：(1) 委托人资金必须始终与律师资金分开，存入指定的委托人托管账户；(2) 委托人对资金的权益一经确定，必须向其交付委托人款项；以及 (3) 如果委托人与律师都对任何部分资金主张权益——例如律师费发生争议——资金必须留在独立的委托人账户中，直至完成核算并可以区分双方权益。《职业行为示范规则》1.15。由于这些费用尚未赚取，仍属于委托人，律师将其转入一般办公账户是不适当的。A 不正确。律师可以在完成工作前接受法律服务费，但在律师赚取费用前，必须将其与律师资金分开，留在指定的委托人托管账户中。C 不正确。律师虽试图通过把钱转回账户来纠正问题，却已无正当理由取走委托人资金并违反规则。D 不正确。问题在于委托人资金从委托人账户转入律师账户。律师转移款项时相信什么并不重要。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-010","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":10,"topic":"Model Rule 1.3","question":"An attorney represented a plaintiff in an action against several defendants. The retainer agreement provided that the plaintiff would pay all costs and expenses of litigation and would, on demand, reimburse the attorney for any costs or expenses advanced by the attorney. After serving process on two defendants, the attorney had difficulty locating and serving the remaining defendants. The plaintiff approved the hiring of an investigator to locate and serve the defendants, and the attorney advanced the costs for the investigator. When the attorney asked the plaintiff for reimbursement, the plaintiff refused to pay. The attorney then told the plaintiff that the attorney would do no more work on the case until the attorney was reimbursed for the amount advanced. Thereafter, one of the defendants filed a counterclaim that required a responsive pleading within 30 days. Because the attorney had not been paid, the attorney permitted the time to respond to the counterclaim to expire without filing a responsive pleading, and a default was entered on the counterclaim. Later, the plaintiff reimbursed the attorney for the costs the attorney had advanced, and the attorney was successful in having the default on the counterclaim set aside. The case was tried, and the plaintiff prevailed on the plaintiff’s complaint, and the counterclaimant recovered nothing. Is the attorney subject to discipline for not initially filing a responsive pleading to the counterclaim?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney neglected the plaintiff’s cause.","B":"Yes, because the attorney had not asked leave of court to withdraw.","C":"No, because the plaintiff breached the agreement to reimburse the attorney.","D":"No, because the plaintiff did not sustain any prejudice as a result of the attorney’s action."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Model Rule 1.3. Importantly, unless the relationship is terminated, the lawyer should carry to conclusion all matters undertaken for a client. Model Rule 1.3, Comment [4]. While the attorney was holding out for payment, he had not terminated representation, and was responsible for diligently continuing the representation. B is incorrect. While this situation may have been helped if the attorney had withdrawn, the attorney had not terminated representation. Importantly, this answer choice seems to state that the attorney’s lack of diligence would be excused merely if the attorney had asked the court to withdraw, not whether in fact the attorney had done so. C is incorrect. While the plaintiff breached the agreement, the attorney had not terminated representation and was required to continue to carry all matters to conclusion. D is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice. The attorney still violated the Rules, although it ultimately did not hurt the client.","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Model Rule 1.3. Importantly, unless the relationship is terminated, the lawyer should carry to conclusion all matters undertaken for a client. Model Rule 1.3, Comment [4]. While the attorney was holding out for payment, he had not terminated representation, and was responsible for diligently continuing the representation. B is incorrect. While this situation may have been helped if the attorney had withdrawn, the attorney had not terminated representation. Importantly, this answer choice seems to state that the attorney’s lack of diligence would be excused merely if the attorney had asked the court to withdraw, not whether in fact the attorney had done so. C is incorrect. While the plaintiff breached the agreement, the attorney had not terminated representation and was required to continue to carry all matters to conclusion. D is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice. The attorney still violated the Rules, although it ultimately did not hurt the client.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline for not initially filing a responsive pleading to the counterclaim?","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Model Rule 1.3. Importantly, unless the relationship is terminated, the lawyer should carry to conclusion all matters undertaken for a client. Model Rule 1.3, Comment [4]. While the attorney was holding out for payment, he had not terminated representation, and was responsible for diligently continuing the representation. B is incorrect. While this situation may have been helped if the attorney had withdrawn, the attorney had not terminated representation. Importantly, this answer choice seems to state that the attorney’s lack of diligence would be excused merely if the attorney had asked the court to withdraw, not whether in fact the attorney had done so. C is incorrect. While the plaintiff breached the agreement, the attorney had not terminated representation and was required to continue to carry all matters to conclusion. D is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice. The attorney still violated the Rules, although it ultimately did not hurt the client.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Model Rule 1.3. Importantly, unless the relationship is terminated, the lawyer should carry to conclusion all matters undertaken for a client. Model Rule 1.3, Comment [4]. While the attorney was holding out for payment, he had not terminated representation, and was responsible for diligently continuing the representation. B is incorrect. While this situation may have been helped if the attorney had withdrawn, the attorney had not terminated representation. Importantly, this answer choice seems to state that the attorney’s lack of diligence would be excused merely if the attorney had asked the court to withdraw, not whether in fact the attorney had done so. C is incorrect. While the plaintiff breached the agreement, the attorney had not terminated representation and was required to continue to carry all matters to conclusion. D is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice. The attorney still violated the Rules, although it ultimately did not hurt the client.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[173],"sourceAnswerPages":[274],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理原告起诉数名被告。聘用协议规定，原告承担全部诉讼费和开支，并应律师要求偿还律师垫付的任何诉讼费或开支。向两名被告送达诉状后，律师难以找到并送达其余被告。原告同意聘请调查员寻找并送达这些被告，律师垫付了调查员费用。律师要求原告偿还时，原告拒绝付款。律师于是告诉原告，在偿还垫付款前，律师不会再处理案件。之后，一名被告提出反诉，须在 30 日内提交答辩文书。由于律师尚未得到付款，他让反诉答辩期限届满而没有提交，反诉遂作出缺席裁定。后来，原告偿还律师垫付的费用，律师成功撤销反诉缺席裁定。案件审判后，原告就本诉胜诉，反诉人一无所获。律师最初没有对反诉提交答辩文书，是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师疏于处理原告的诉讼事务。","B":"是，因为律师没有请求法院准许退出。","C":"否，因为原告违反了偿还垫付款的协议。","D":"否，因为原告没有因律师行为受到任何损害。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。“律师代理委托人时应以合理的勤勉和及时程度行事。”《职业行为示范规则》1.3。尤其是，除非代理关系已经终止，律师应将为委托人接受的全部事项贯彻至终结。规则 1.3 评注 [4]。律师等待付款期间并未终止代理，仍有责任勤勉地继续代理。B 不正确。如果律师退出，这种情况可能会得到改善，但律师没有终止代理。关键是，该选项似乎声称，只要律师曾向法院请求退出，即使没有真正退出，其不勤勉行为也会得到免责。C 不正确。原告虽然违反协议，但律师没有终止代理，仍必须继续把所有事项办理至终结。D 不正确。这是典型的“没有损害，就没有违规”型选项。律师虽然最终没有伤害委托人，仍然违反了规则。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-011","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":11,"topic":"Rule 2.11(A) / Rule 2.11(A)(1)","question":"A judge, prior to her recent appointment to the federal court, had been an outspoken and effective opponent of the racial segregation policies of a foreign country’s government. As part of its worldwide tour, that country’s national soccer team scheduled a soccer match with a team in this country. Several civil rights groups have applied to the judge for an order enjoining the playing of the proposed match. The matter is now pending. Only legal issues are presented. The judge, after painstaking consideration, has privately concluded that she cannot decide the legal questions without bias against the representatives of the country’s government. However, no one has made a motion to disqualify the judge. The judge does not believe she has any greater expertise in racial matters than other judges on the court. Must the judge recuse herself in the pending matter?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the judge does not believe she has greater expertise than other judges on the court in legal issues involving racial segregation.","B":"Yes, because the judge believes that she cannot be impartial.","C":"No, because the only issues presented for decision are legal questions.","D":"No, because none of the interested parties has moved to disqualify the judge."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. “A judge shall disqualify himself or herself in any proceeding in which the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(A). Importantly, if the judge has a personal bias or prejudice concerning one of the parties, the judge should disqualify himself or herself. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(A)(1). Here, considering her bias against the representatives of the country’s government, she should disqualify herself. A is incorrect. The issue here is her potential bias, not who might be best qualified to hear the action. C is incorrect. Although only legal questions are being presented, the judge’s bias against the country could deeply affect the outcome of those questions. In any event, even if the judge could control her bias (which she seems to feel she cannot), an outside observer would be justified in questioning any decision the judge made in this case because of potential bias. D is incorrect. Although none of the interested parties have moved to disqualify the judge, her awareness of her potential bias means she needs to disqualify herself.","rule":"B is the correct answer. “A judge shall disqualify himself or herself in any proceeding in which the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(A). Importantly, if the judge has a personal bias or prejudice concerning one of the parties, the judge should disqualify himself or herself. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(A)(1). Here, considering her bias against the representatives of the country’s government, she should disqualify herself. A is incorrect. The issue here is her potential bias, not who might be best qualified to hear the action. C is incorrect. Although only legal questions are being presented, the judge’s bias against the country could deeply affect the outcome of those questions. In any event, even if the judge could control her bias (which she seems to feel she cannot), an outside observer would be justified in questioning any decision the judge made in this case because of potential bias. D is incorrect. Although none of the interested parties have moved to disqualify the judge, her awareness of her potential bias means she needs to disqualify herself.","irac":{"issue":"Must the judge recuse herself in the pending matter?","rule":"B is the correct answer. “A judge shall disqualify himself or herself in any proceeding in which the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(A). Importantly, if the judge has a personal bias or prejudice concerning one of the parties, the judge should disqualify himself or herself. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(A)(1). Here, considering her bias against the representatives of the country’s government, she should disqualify herself. A is incorrect. The issue here is her potential bias, not who might be best qualified to hear the action. C is incorrect. Although only legal questions are being presented, the judge’s bias against the country could deeply affect the outcome of those questions. In any event, even if the judge could control her bias (which she seems to feel she cannot), an outside observer would be justified in questioning any decision the judge made in this case because of potential bias. D is incorrect. Although none of the interested parties have moved to disqualify the judge, her awareness of her potential bias means she needs to disqualify herself.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. “A judge shall disqualify himself or herself in any proceeding in which the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(A). Importantly, if the judge has a personal bias or prejudice concerning one of the parties, the judge should disqualify himself or herself. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11(A)(1). Here, considering her bias against the representatives of the country’s government, she should disqualify herself. A is incorrect. The issue here is her potential bias, not who might be best qualified to hear the action. C is incorrect. Although only legal questions are being presented, the judge’s bias against the country could deeply affect the outcome of those questions. In any event, even if the judge could control her bias (which she seems to feel she cannot), an outside observer would be justified in questioning any decision the judge made in this case because of potential bias. D is incorrect. Although none of the interested parties have moved to disqualify the judge, her awareness of her potential bias means she needs to disqualify herself.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 2.11(A)","Rule 2.11(A)(1)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[174],"sourceAnswerPages":[274,275],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名法官在最近获任联邦法院法官之前，曾公开且有力地反对某外国政府的种族隔离政策。作为世界巡回赛的一部分，该国国家足球队计划与本国一支球队比赛。数个民权团体向该法官申请禁令，要求禁止举行拟议比赛。该事项现正待决，只涉及法律问题。法官经过慎重考虑，已私下得出结论：自己无法在不对该国政府代表抱有偏见的情况下裁判法律问题。不过，没有任何人申请取消法官资格。法官不认为自己在种族问题上比法院其他法官更有专长。该法官是否必须在待决事项中回避？","choicesZh":{"A":"必须，因为法官不认为自己在涉及种族隔离的法律问题上比法院其他法官更有专长。","B":"必须，因为法官认为自己不能保持公正。","C":"不必须，因为需要裁判的只有法律问题。","D":"不必须，因为任何利害关系方都没有申请取消法官资格。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。“在法官公正性可能受到合理质疑的任何程序中，法官均应回避。”《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.11(A)。尤其是，如果法官对一方有个人偏见，就应回避。规则 2.11(A)(1)。本题中，考虑到她对该国政府代表的偏见，她应当回避。A 不正确。本题问题在于其潜在偏见，而不是谁最有资格审理该事项。C 不正确。即使只涉及法律问题，法官对该国的偏见仍可能严重影响问题的裁判。无论如何，即使法官可以控制偏见（而她本人似乎认为不能），鉴于潜在偏见，外部观察者也有理由质疑她在本案中作出的任何决定。D 不正确。虽然任何利害关系方都没有申请法官回避，但她已意识到自己可能存在偏见，因而必须自行回避。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-012","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":12,"topic":"Model Rule 1.16(b)","question":"An attorney agreed to represent a plaintiff in a personal injury matter. The original agreement between the attorney and the plaintiff specified a 30 percent contingent fee, which was a reasonable fee for the type of cases the attorney handled. One year into the litigation, the attorney noted that he was extremely busy and that many potential clients sought his services. As a result, the attorney raised his standard fee to a 35 percent contingent fee, which was also a reasonable fee. The attorney’s agreement with the plaintiff was silent on the possibility of a fee increase. He approached the plaintiff and proposed that she agree to modify the contingent fee percentage from 30 percent to 35 percent. The attorney informed the plaintiff that if she did not agree, the attorney would find her another experienced personal injury lawyer at the original fee, but that the attorney was unwilling to continue the representation unless the fee was modified. The plaintiff reluctantly agreed to modify the fee agreement as the attorney proposed. Subsequently, the plaintiff’s case was settled. The plaintiff, however, refused to pay the attorney more than a 30 percent contingent fee, and the attorney sued the plaintiff to recover under the modified fee agreement. Is the attorney likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney offered to find the plaintiff another experienced personal injury lawyer at the original rate.","B":"Yes, because a contingent fee of 35 percent constituted a reasonable fee.","C":"No, because the attorney did not suggest that the plaintiff seek the advice of independent counsel before accepting the increased fee.","D":"No, because there were no special circumstances justifying the attorney’s insistence on a fee increase."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. An attorney may withdraw from representation if withdrawal can be accomplished without adverse effect, the client persists in fraudulent action, the client is using the lawyer’s services to commit a crime, the attorney fundamentally disagrees with the client’s actions, the client has failed to fulfill an obligation, the representation will result in an unreasonable financial burden, or other good cause exists. Model Rule 1.16(b). Here, the attorney is threatening to withdraw unless the client agrees to the fee increase. Importantly, the fee increase meets none of the potentially valid reasons for withdrawal, as the only reason for the fee increase is that the attorney has realized he could be making more money. If something else had occurred (such as the fee increase being necessary to avoid an unreasonable financial burden), the fee increase might have been reasonable. However, as it is now, the attorney is basically extorting the client into paying more to continue the representation. A is incorrect. While the attorney offered to find another experienced attorney, he had no valid reason for the withdrawal. B is incorrect. While the contingent fee may have been reasonable, the attorney had already agreed to the 30 percent fee. Consequently, he is threatening to withdraw simply because he thinks he can make more money, not that 35 percent is the only reasonable fee. The Rules do not allow this type of extortion from clients. C is incorrect. Even if the attorney had suggested independent counsel, the bigger issue is that the attorney is threatening withdrawal without a valid reason to do so.","rule":"D is the correct answer. An attorney may withdraw from representation if withdrawal can be accomplished without adverse effect, the client persists in fraudulent action, the client is using the lawyer’s services to commit a crime, the attorney fundamentally disagrees with the client’s actions, the client has failed to fulfill an obligation, the representation will result in an unreasonable financial burden, or other good cause exists. Model Rule 1.16(b). Here, the attorney is threatening to withdraw unless the client agrees to the fee increase. Importantly, the fee increase meets none of the potentially valid reasons for withdrawal, as the only reason for the fee increase is that the attorney has realized he could be making more money. If something else had occurred (such as the fee increase being necessary to avoid an unreasonable financial burden), the fee increase might have been reasonable. However, as it is now, the attorney is basically extorting the client into paying more to continue the representation. A is incorrect. While the attorney offered to find another experienced attorney, he had no valid reason for the withdrawal. B is incorrect. While the contingent fee may have been reasonable, the attorney had already agreed to the 30 percent fee. Consequently, he is threatening to withdraw simply because he thinks he can make more money, not that 35 percent is the only reasonable fee. The Rules do not allow this type of extortion from clients. C is incorrect. Even if the attorney had suggested independent counsel, the bigger issue is that the attorney is threatening withdrawal without a valid reason to do so.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney likely to prevail?","rule":"D is the correct answer. An attorney may withdraw from representation if withdrawal can be accomplished without adverse effect, the client persists in fraudulent action, the client is using the lawyer’s services to commit a crime, the attorney fundamentally disagrees with the client’s actions, the client has failed to fulfill an obligation, the representation will result in an unreasonable financial burden, or other good cause exists. Model Rule 1.16(b). Here, the attorney is threatening to withdraw unless the client agrees to the fee increase. Importantly, the fee increase meets none of the potentially valid reasons for withdrawal, as the only reason for the fee increase is that the attorney has realized he could be making more money. If something else had occurred (such as the fee increase being necessary to avoid an unreasonable financial burden), the fee increase might have been reasonable. However, as it is now, the attorney is basically extorting the client into paying more to continue the representation. A is incorrect. While the attorney offered to find another experienced attorney, he had no valid reason for the withdrawal. B is incorrect. While the contingent fee may have been reasonable, the attorney had already agreed to the 30 percent fee. Consequently, he is threatening to withdraw simply because he thinks he can make more money, not that 35 percent is the only reasonable fee. The Rules do not allow this type of extortion from clients. C is incorrect. Even if the attorney had suggested independent counsel, the bigger issue is that the attorney is threatening withdrawal without a valid reason to do so.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. An attorney may withdraw from representation if withdrawal can be accomplished without adverse effect, the client persists in fraudulent action, the client is using the lawyer’s services to commit a crime, the attorney fundamentally disagrees with the client’s actions, the client has failed to fulfill an obligation, the representation will result in an unreasonable financial burden, or other good cause exists. Model Rule 1.16(b). Here, the attorney is threatening to withdraw unless the client agrees to the fee increase. Importantly, the fee increase meets none of the potentially valid reasons for withdrawal, as the only reason for the fee increase is that the attorney has realized he could be making more money. If something else had occurred (such as the fee increase being necessary to avoid an unreasonable financial burden), the fee increase might have been reasonable. However, as it is now, the attorney is basically extorting the client into paying more to continue the representation. A is incorrect. While the attorney offered to find another experienced attorney, he had no valid reason for the withdrawal. B is incorrect. While the contingent fee may have been reasonable, the attorney had already agreed to the 30 percent fee. Consequently, he is threatening to withdraw simply because he thinks he can make more money, not that 35 percent is the only reasonable fee. The Rules do not allow this type of extortion from clients. C is incorrect. Even if the attorney had suggested independent counsel, the bigger issue is that the attorney is threatening withdrawal without a valid reason to do so.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.16(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[174,175],"sourceAnswerPages":[275],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师同意代理一宗人身伤害案件的原告。律师与原告最初约定 30% 风险代理费，对该律师处理的同类案件而言，这属于合理收费。诉讼进行一年后，律师注意到自己业务极为繁忙，还有许多潜在委托人寻求其服务。因此，律师把标准费用提高到 35% 风险代理费，这也属于合理收费。律师与原告的协议没有提到可能提高费用。他找到原告，提议把风险代理费比例从 30% 改为 35%。律师告知原告，如果她不同意，他会以原收费标准为其找到另一名经验丰富的人身伤害律师，但自己不愿在不修改收费的情况下继续代理。原告勉强同意按律师提议修改收费协议。此后，原告案件达成和解。但原告拒绝支付超过 30% 的风险代理费，律师遂起诉原告，要求依修改后协议付款。律师是否可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"可能，因为律师提出按原收费标准为原告寻找另一名经验丰富的人身伤害律师。","B":"可能，因为 35% 风险代理费属于合理收费。","C":"不可能，因为律师没有建议原告在接受提高费用前寻求独立律师意见。","D":"不可能，因为不存在正当化律师坚持提高费用的特殊情形。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。在不造成不利影响、委托人持续实施欺诈行为、利用律师服务实施犯罪、律师与委托人行为存在根本分歧、委托人未履行义务、代理会造成不合理经济负担，或者存在其他正当理由时，律师可以退出代理。《职业行为示范规则》1.16(b)。本题中，律师威胁除非委托人同意提高费用，否则将退出。关键是，提高费用不符合任何可能有效的退出理由，唯一原因只是律师发现自己可以赚更多钱。如果发生其他情况（例如为避免不合理经济负担而确有必要提高费用），提高费用可能合理。但在当前情形下，律师实质上是在胁迫委托人支付更多钱，才肯继续代理。A 不正确。律师虽提出寻找另一名经验丰富的律师，却没有有效的退出理由。B 不正确。风险代理费虽然可能合理，但律师已经同意 30% 的费用。因此，他威胁退出只是因为认为自己可以赚更多钱，而不是因为 35% 才是唯一合理费用。规则不允许对委托人进行这种胁迫。C 不正确。即使律师建议寻求独立律师意见，更大的问题仍是他在没有有效理由时威胁退出。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-013","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":13,"topic":"Rule 4.1(A)(8) / Rule 2.11 / Rule 4.4(B)(3)","question":"A member of the bar is a candidate for judicial office in an election. The candidate personally asked several of his friends to contribute $1,000 each to kick off his campaign. After the candidate’s friends made the contributions, the candidate, who was elated by the support, formed a committee to collect more contributions. The committee does not include lawyers likely to practice before the candidate. The candidate then turned over the contributions to the committee and began campaigning in earnest. Is the candidate subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"No, because the candidate turned over the funds to his committee.","B":"No, because the committee does not include lawyers likely to practice before the candidate.","C":"Yes, because at least one of the original contributors was a lawyer.","D":"Yes, because the candidate personally solicited funds."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. A judicial candidate may not personally solicit or accept campaign contributions other than through a campaign committee. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.1(A)(8). Here, the candidate violated this Rule when he personally asked his friends to contribute to his campaign. A is incorrect. While the funds were ultimately turned over to a committee, the Rule was already violated when the candidate personally solicited the funds. B is incorrect. A campaign committee may include lawyers likely to practice before the candidate, but the candidate needs to be aware of the dangers of potential disqualification under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11 if the committee includes such lawyers. C is incorrect. A campaign committee is required to keep records of the employer of each person who makes a campaign contribution. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.4(B)(3). However, there is no requirement that at least one of the original contributors be a lawyer.","rule":"D is the correct answer. A judicial candidate may not personally solicit or accept campaign contributions other than through a campaign committee. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.1(A)(8). Here, the candidate violated this Rule when he personally asked his friends to contribute to his campaign. A is incorrect. While the funds were ultimately turned over to a committee, the Rule was already violated when the candidate personally solicited the funds. B is incorrect. A campaign committee may include lawyers likely to practice before the candidate, but the candidate needs to be aware of the dangers of potential disqualification under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11 if the committee includes such lawyers. C is incorrect. A campaign committee is required to keep records of the employer of each person who makes a campaign contribution. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.4(B)(3). However, there is no requirement that at least one of the original contributors be a lawyer.","irac":{"issue":"Is the candidate subject to discipline?","rule":"D is the correct answer. A judicial candidate may not personally solicit or accept campaign contributions other than through a campaign committee. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.1(A)(8). Here, the candidate violated this Rule when he personally asked his friends to contribute to his campaign. A is incorrect. While the funds were ultimately turned over to a committee, the Rule was already violated when the candidate personally solicited the funds. B is incorrect. A campaign committee may include lawyers likely to practice before the candidate, but the candidate needs to be aware of the dangers of potential disqualification under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11 if the committee includes such lawyers. C is incorrect. A campaign committee is required to keep records of the employer of each person who makes a campaign contribution. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.4(B)(3). However, there is no requirement that at least one of the original contributors be a lawyer.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. A judicial candidate may not personally solicit or accept campaign contributions other than through a campaign committee. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.1(A)(8). Here, the candidate violated this Rule when he personally asked his friends to contribute to his campaign. A is incorrect. While the funds were ultimately turned over to a committee, the Rule was already violated when the candidate personally solicited the funds. B is incorrect. A campaign committee may include lawyers likely to practice before the candidate, but the candidate needs to be aware of the dangers of potential disqualification under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11 if the committee includes such lawyers. C is incorrect. A campaign committee is required to keep records of the employer of each person who makes a campaign contribution. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.4(B)(3). However, there is no requirement that at least one of the original contributors be a lawyer.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 4.1(A)(8)","Rule 2.11","Rule 4.4(B)(3)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[175],"sourceAnswerPages":[275,276],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师协会成员作为候选人参加法官职位选举。候选人亲自请求数名朋友各捐 1,000 美元，以启动竞选活动。朋友捐款后，候选人对获得支持感到十分兴奋，遂成立委员会募集更多捐款。委员会成员中不包括可能在候选人面前出庭的律师。候选人随后把已收捐款移交委员会，正式开展竞选。该候选人是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为候选人把资金移交给了委员会。","B":"否，因为委员会成员中没有可能在候选人面前出庭的律师。","C":"是，因为最初捐款人中至少有一人是律师。","D":"是，因为候选人亲自募集资金。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。司法职位候选人不得亲自募集或接受竞选捐款，只能通过竞选委员会进行。《司法行为示范准则》规则 4.1(A)(8)。本题中，候选人亲自请求朋友向竞选捐款时，已经违反该规则。A 不正确。资金虽然最终移交委员会，但候选人亲自募集资金时，规则已经受到违反。B 不正确。竞选委员会可以包括可能在候选人面前出庭的律师，但候选人应当注意，如果委员会包括这类律师，可能会根据规则 2.11 产生回避风险。C 不正确。竞选委员会必须记录每名捐款人的雇主。规则 4.4(B)(3)。不过，没有规定最初捐款人中至少一人必须是律师。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-014","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":14,"topic":"Model Rule 3.1","question":"An attorney was hired to file a lawsuit against the client’s former employer, a corporation, for wrongful discharge. The attorney filed the suit in federal district court based upon three grounds. It turned out that a unanimous U.S. Supreme Court decision had recently eliminated the third ground as a theory available to plaintiffs in wrongful discharge cases. The corporation filed a motion alleging that the complaint was based upon a theory (the third ground) that is no longer supported by existing law and cited the new decision. Within 10 days after the filing of the complaint, the attorney withdrew the third ground and continued with the litigation. Is the attorney subject to litigation sanction?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney did not discuss the adverse legal authority with the client before filing the complaint.","B":"Yes, because the client should have cited the U.S. Supreme Court decision in the complaint.","C":"No, because the attorney withdrew the third ground within 10 days after filing the complaint.","D":"No, because the attorney did not know or should not have known of the recent decision when the complaint was filed."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not bring or defend a proceeding, or assert or controvert an issue therein, unless there is a basis in law and fact for doing so that is not frivolous, which includes a good faith argument for an extension, modification or reversal of existing law.” Model Rule 3.1. Importantly, the Rule recognizes that the law is “not always clear and never is static” and only requires that the attorney act in good faith when making an argument. Model Rule 3.1, Comments [1] & [2]. Here, the attorney made a good faith argument and withdrew it when it became clear that the argument was no longer valid (importantly, it was a recent decision). Consequently, he acted as required under the Rules. A is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney made a claim that was no longer supported by the law. Whether or not he discussed this with the client would not change the analysis of the potential conflict with the Rules. B is incorrect. While the decision should have been cited, the attorney’s failure to do so under these circumstances was not a violation of the Rules. D is incorrect. This answer choice implies that the attorney could keep arguing the invalid point if he did not know and should not have known of the decision when the complaint was filed. This is clearly a misstatement of the Rule.","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not bring or defend a proceeding, or assert or controvert an issue therein, unless there is a basis in law and fact for doing so that is not frivolous, which includes a good faith argument for an extension, modification or reversal of existing law.” Model Rule 3.1. Importantly, the Rule recognizes that the law is “not always clear and never is static” and only requires that the attorney act in good faith when making an argument. Model Rule 3.1, Comments [1] & [2]. Here, the attorney made a good faith argument and withdrew it when it became clear that the argument was no longer valid (importantly, it was a recent decision). Consequently, he acted as required under the Rules. A is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney made a claim that was no longer supported by the law. Whether or not he discussed this with the client would not change the analysis of the potential conflict with the Rules. B is incorrect. While the decision should have been cited, the attorney’s failure to do so under these circumstances was not a violation of the Rules. D is incorrect. This answer choice implies that the attorney could keep arguing the invalid point if he did not know and should not have known of the decision when the complaint was filed. This is clearly a misstatement of the Rule.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to litigation sanction?","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not bring or defend a proceeding, or assert or controvert an issue therein, unless there is a basis in law and fact for doing so that is not frivolous, which includes a good faith argument for an extension, modification or reversal of existing law.” Model Rule 3.1. Importantly, the Rule recognizes that the law is “not always clear and never is static” and only requires that the attorney act in good faith when making an argument. Model Rule 3.1, Comments [1] & [2]. Here, the attorney made a good faith argument and withdrew it when it became clear that the argument was no longer valid (importantly, it was a recent decision). Consequently, he acted as required under the Rules. A is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney made a claim that was no longer supported by the law. Whether or not he discussed this with the client would not change the analysis of the potential conflict with the Rules. B is incorrect. While the decision should have been cited, the attorney’s failure to do so under these circumstances was not a violation of the Rules. D is incorrect. This answer choice implies that the attorney could keep arguing the invalid point if he did not know and should not have known of the decision when the complaint was filed. This is clearly a misstatement of the Rule.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not bring or defend a proceeding, or assert or controvert an issue therein, unless there is a basis in law and fact for doing so that is not frivolous, which includes a good faith argument for an extension, modification or reversal of existing law.” Model Rule 3.1. Importantly, the Rule recognizes that the law is “not always clear and never is static” and only requires that the attorney act in good faith when making an argument. Model Rule 3.1, Comments [1] & [2]. Here, the attorney made a good faith argument and withdrew it when it became clear that the argument was no longer valid (importantly, it was a recent decision). Consequently, he acted as required under the Rules. A is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney made a claim that was no longer supported by the law. Whether or not he discussed this with the client would not change the analysis of the potential conflict with the Rules. B is incorrect. While the decision should have been cited, the attorney’s failure to do so under these circumstances was not a violation of the Rules. D is incorrect. This answer choice implies that the attorney could keep arguing the invalid point if he did not know and should not have known of the decision when the complaint was filed. This is clearly a misstatement of the Rule.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[175,176],"sourceAnswerPages":[276],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师受聘起诉委托人的前雇主（一家公司）违法解雇。律师基于三项理由向联邦地区法院提起诉讼。后来发现，美国最高法院最近作出的一项全体一致判决已经排除第三项理由，不再允许原告在违法解雇案件中使用该理论。公司提出动议，主张起诉状以现行法律已经不再支持的理论（第三项理由）为依据，并援引新判决。起诉状提交后 10 日内，律师撤回第三项理由，继续进行诉讼。该律师是否会受到诉讼制裁？","choicesZh":{"A":"会，因为律师在提交起诉状前没有与委托人讨论不利法律权威。","B":"会，因为委托人本应在起诉状中援引美国最高法院判决。","C":"不会，因为律师在提交起诉状后 10 日内撤回了第三项理由。","D":"不会，因为律师提交起诉状时并不知道且理应不知道最近的判决。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。“律师不得提起或抗辩一项程序，也不得在其中主张或反驳某一问题，除非其在法律和事实方面具有并非轻率无据的基础；这包括为扩张、修改或推翻现行法律而提出的善意论证。”《职业行为示范规则》3.1。该规则尤其承认法律“并非总是明确，也从不静止”，只要求律师在提出论证时善意行事。规则 3.1 评注 [1]、[2]。本题中，律师提出了善意论证，并在明确该论证已不再有效时撤回（尤其考虑这是近期判决）。因此，他依照规则行事。A 不正确。本题问题是律师提出了现行法律已不支持的主张。是否与委托人讨论，不会改变其与规则潜在冲突的分析。B 不正确。虽然本应援引该判决，但在本题情况下，律师未援引并不违反规则。D 不正确。该选项暗示，如果提交起诉状时律师不知道且理应不知道判决，就可以继续主张无效观点。这显然错误陈述了规则。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-015","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":15,"topic":"Model Rule 8.4(c)","question":"Although licensed to practice law in the state, an attorney does not practice law but works as an investment broker. The attorney could have elected inactive status as a member of the bar, but chose not to do so. Recently, in connection with a sale of worthless securities, the attorney made materially false representations to an investment customer. The customer sued the attorney for civil fraud, and a jury returned a verdict in the customer’s favor. The attorney did not appeal. The standard of proof in the state was not the same in lawyer disciplinary cases and civil cases. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney was pursuing a nonlegal occupation while an active member of the bar.","B":"Yes, because the attorney’s conduct was fraudulent.","C":"No, because the attorney was not convicted of a crime.","D":"No, because the standard of proof in the state was not the same in lawyer disciplinary cases and civil cases."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. It is professional misconduct for an attorney to engage in fraud. Model Rule 8.4(c). Importantly, while the attorney was no longer practicing law, he was still on active status as an attorney and was still required to abide by the Rules. A is incorrect. There is no prohibition on an attorney pursuing a nonlegal occupation while still an active member of the bar. C is incorrect. While the attorney was not convicted of a crime, he was still found guilty of fraud, which is a violation of the Rules. D is incorrect. There is no requirement that the standard of proof be the same. The important point is that the attorney committed fraud and thus violated the Rules.","rule":"B is the correct answer. It is professional misconduct for an attorney to engage in fraud. Model Rule 8.4(c). Importantly, while the attorney was no longer practicing law, he was still on active status as an attorney and was still required to abide by the Rules. A is incorrect. There is no prohibition on an attorney pursuing a nonlegal occupation while still an active member of the bar. C is incorrect. While the attorney was not convicted of a crime, he was still found guilty of fraud, which is a violation of the Rules. D is incorrect. There is no requirement that the standard of proof be the same. The important point is that the attorney committed fraud and thus violated the Rules.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. It is professional misconduct for an attorney to engage in fraud. Model Rule 8.4(c). Importantly, while the attorney was no longer practicing law, he was still on active status as an attorney and was still required to abide by the Rules. A is incorrect. There is no prohibition on an attorney pursuing a nonlegal occupation while still an active member of the bar. C is incorrect. While the attorney was not convicted of a crime, he was still found guilty of fraud, which is a violation of the Rules. D is incorrect. There is no requirement that the standard of proof be the same. The important point is that the attorney committed fraud and thus violated the Rules.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. It is professional misconduct for an attorney to engage in fraud. Model Rule 8.4(c). Importantly, while the attorney was no longer practicing law, he was still on active status as an attorney and was still required to abide by the Rules. A is incorrect. There is no prohibition on an attorney pursuing a nonlegal occupation while still an active member of the bar. C is incorrect. While the attorney was not convicted of a crime, he was still found guilty of fraud, which is a violation of the Rules. D is incorrect. There is no requirement that the standard of proof be the same. The important point is that the attorney committed fraud and thus violated the Rules.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 8.4(c)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[176],"sourceAnswerPages":[276,277],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师虽获准在本州执业，却没有从事法律业务，而是担任投资经纪人。律师本可以选择转为律师协会非执业状态，但没有这样做。最近，在出售毫无价值的证券时，律师向一名投资客户作出重大虚假陈述。客户以民事欺诈起诉律师，陪审团裁定客户胜诉。律师没有上诉。该州律师纪律案件与民事案件采用不同的证明标准。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师作为律师协会活跃成员从事非法律职业。","B":"是，因为律师的行为构成欺诈。","C":"否，因为律师没有受到刑事定罪。","D":"否，因为该州律师纪律案件与民事案件的证明标准不同。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。律师从事欺诈行为构成职业不当行为。《职业行为示范规则》8.4(c)。关键是，律师虽然不再从事法律业务，却仍保持律师活跃状态，仍须遵守规则。A 不正确。律师协会活跃成员并不被禁止从事非法律职业。C 不正确。律师虽然没有受到刑事定罪，仍被认定实施欺诈，而欺诈违反规则。D 不正确。规则不要求证明标准相同。关键在于律师实施欺诈，因而违反规则。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-016","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":16,"topic":"Model Rule 1.5(d) / Model Rule 1.5(c) / Model Rule 1.8(i)","question":"A client was an experienced oil and gas developer. The client asked an attorney for representation in a suit to establish the client’s ownership of certain oil and gas royalties. The client did not have available the necessary funds to pay the attorney’s reasonable hourly rate for undertaking the case. The client proposed instead to pay the attorney an amount in cash equal to 20 percent of the value of the proceeds received from the first year royalties the client might recover as a result of the suit. The attorney accepted the proposal and took the case. The fee will likely not exceed the fee the attorney would have earned from his hourly rate. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the agreement gave the attorney a proprietary interest in the client’s cause of action.","B":"Yes, because the fee the attorney receives will likely exceed that which the attorney would have received by charging a reasonable hourly rate.","C":"No, because the client rather than the attorney proposed the fee arrangement.","D":"No, because the attorney may contract with the client for a reasonable contingent fee."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. A fee may be contingent on the outcome of the matter for which the service is rendered, except in a matter in which a contingent fee involves a domestic matter or a criminal case. Model Rule 1.5(d). “A contingent fee agreement shall be in a writing signed by the client and shall state the method by which the fee is to be determined, including the percentage or percentages that shall accrue to the lawyer in the event of settlement, trial or appeal; litigation and other expenses to be deducted from the recovery; and whether such expenses are to be deducted before or after the contingent fee is calculated. The agreement must clearly notify the client of any expenses for which the client will be liable whether or not the client is the prevailing party. Upon conclusion of a contingent fee matter, the lawyer shall provide the client with a written statement stating the outcome of the matter and, if there is a recovery, showing the remittance to the client and the method of its determination.” Model Rule 1.5(c). Here, there is nothing improper in the parties’ agreement, and it appears to be reasonable under the circumstances. A is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.8(i), “a lawyer shall not acquire a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of litigation . . . except that the lawyer may . . . contract with a client for a reasonable contingent fee in a civil case.” Model Rule 1.8(i)(2). Consequently, there is no general ban against an attorney receiving a proprietary interest in a client’s cause of action, so long it is as part of a contract with a client for a reasonable contingent fee in a civil case. Here, the agreement appears reasonable. B is incorrect. Although the overall contingent fee may exceed the hourly rate, so long as the agreement is reasonable to the parties, there is nothing making such an agreement automatically improper. Importantly, this answer choice does not say that the fee will be grossly in excess of the hourly rate —it could exceed the hourly cost by only a dollar. C is incorrect. This is the classic “it was the client’s idea” wrong answer choice. Remember, simply because a client thinks something is a good idea does not make it proper under the Rules.","rule":"D is the correct answer. A fee may be contingent on the outcome of the matter for which the service is rendered, except in a matter in which a contingent fee involves a domestic matter or a criminal case. Model Rule 1.5(d). “A contingent fee agreement shall be in a writing signed by the client and shall state the method by which the fee is to be determined, including the percentage or percentages that shall accrue to the lawyer in the event of settlement, trial or appeal; litigation and other expenses to be deducted from the recovery; and whether such expenses are to be deducted before or after the contingent fee is calculated. The agreement must clearly notify the client of any expenses for which the client will be liable whether or not the client is the prevailing party. Upon conclusion of a contingent fee matter, the lawyer shall provide the client with a written statement stating the outcome of the matter and, if there is a recovery, showing the remittance to the client and the method of its determination.” Model Rule 1.5(c). Here, there is nothing improper in the parties’ agreement, and it appears to be reasonable under the circumstances. A is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.8(i), “a lawyer shall not acquire a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of litigation . . . except that the lawyer may . . . contract with a client for a reasonable contingent fee in a civil case.” Model Rule 1.8(i)(2). Consequently, there is no general ban against an attorney receiving a proprietary interest in a client’s cause of action, so long it is as part of a contract with a client for a reasonable contingent fee in a civil case. Here, the agreement appears reasonable. B is incorrect. Although the overall contingent fee may exceed the hourly rate, so long as the agreement is reasonable to the parties, there is nothing making such an agreement automatically improper. Importantly, this answer choice does not say that the fee will be grossly in excess of the hourly rate —it could exceed the hourly cost by only a dollar. C is incorrect. This is the classic “it was the client’s idea” wrong answer choice. Remember, simply because a client thinks something is a good idea does not make it proper under the Rules.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"D is the correct answer. A fee may be contingent on the outcome of the matter for which the service is rendered, except in a matter in which a contingent fee involves a domestic matter or a criminal case. Model Rule 1.5(d). “A contingent fee agreement shall be in a writing signed by the client and shall state the method by which the fee is to be determined, including the percentage or percentages that shall accrue to the lawyer in the event of settlement, trial or appeal; litigation and other expenses to be deducted from the recovery; and whether such expenses are to be deducted before or after the contingent fee is calculated. The agreement must clearly notify the client of any expenses for which the client will be liable whether or not the client is the prevailing party. Upon conclusion of a contingent fee matter, the lawyer shall provide the client with a written statement stating the outcome of the matter and, if there is a recovery, showing the remittance to the client and the method of its determination.” Model Rule 1.5(c). Here, there is nothing improper in the parties’ agreement, and it appears to be reasonable under the circumstances. A is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.8(i), “a lawyer shall not acquire a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of litigation . . . except that the lawyer may . . . contract with a client for a reasonable contingent fee in a civil case.” Model Rule 1.8(i)(2). Consequently, there is no general ban against an attorney receiving a proprietary interest in a client’s cause of action, so long it is as part of a contract with a client for a reasonable contingent fee in a civil case. Here, the agreement appears reasonable. B is incorrect. Although the overall contingent fee may exceed the hourly rate, so long as the agreement is reasonable to the parties, there is nothing making such an agreement automatically improper. Importantly, this answer choice does not say that the fee will be grossly in excess of the hourly rate —it could exceed the hourly cost by only a dollar. C is incorrect. This is the classic “it was the client’s idea” wrong answer choice. Remember, simply because a client thinks something is a good idea does not make it proper under the Rules.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. A fee may be contingent on the outcome of the matter for which the service is rendered, except in a matter in which a contingent fee involves a domestic matter or a criminal case. Model Rule 1.5(d). “A contingent fee agreement shall be in a writing signed by the client and shall state the method by which the fee is to be determined, including the percentage or percentages that shall accrue to the lawyer in the event of settlement, trial or appeal; litigation and other expenses to be deducted from the recovery; and whether such expenses are to be deducted before or after the contingent fee is calculated. The agreement must clearly notify the client of any expenses for which the client will be liable whether or not the client is the prevailing party. Upon conclusion of a contingent fee matter, the lawyer shall provide the client with a written statement stating the outcome of the matter and, if there is a recovery, showing the remittance to the client and the method of its determination.” Model Rule 1.5(c). Here, there is nothing improper in the parties’ agreement, and it appears to be reasonable under the circumstances. A is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.8(i), “a lawyer shall not acquire a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of litigation . . . except that the lawyer may . . . contract with a client for a reasonable contingent fee in a civil case.” Model Rule 1.8(i)(2). Consequently, there is no general ban against an attorney receiving a proprietary interest in a client’s cause of action, so long it is as part of a contract with a client for a reasonable contingent fee in a civil case. Here, the agreement appears reasonable. B is incorrect. Although the overall contingent fee may exceed the hourly rate, so long as the agreement is reasonable to the parties, there is nothing making such an agreement automatically improper. Importantly, this answer choice does not say that the fee will be grossly in excess of the hourly rate —it could exceed the hourly cost by only a dollar. C is incorrect. This is the classic “it was the client’s idea” wrong answer choice. Remember, simply because a client thinks something is a good idea does not make it proper under the Rules.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.5(d)","Model Rule 1.5(c)","Model Rule 1.8(i)","Model Rule 1.8(i)(2)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[176,177],"sourceAnswerPages":[277,278],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名委托人是经验丰富的石油与天然气开发商。他请求一名律师代理诉讼，以确认自己对某些石油和天然气特许权使用费的所有权。委托人没有足够现款按照律师的合理小时费率支付案件费用，因而提议改为以现金向律师支付一笔金额，等于他因诉讼可能追回的第一年特许权使用费收益价值的 20%。律师接受提议并承办案件。该费用可能不会超过律师按小时费率可赚取的费用。律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该协议使律师取得委托人诉因的所有权利益。","B":"是，因为律师取得的费用很可能超过按合理小时费率可取得的费用。","C":"否，因为收费安排由委托人而非律师提出。","D":"否，因为律师可以与委托人约定合理的风险代理费。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。除涉及家事事项或刑事案件外，费用可以取决于所提供服务之事项的结果。《职业行为示范规则》1.5(d)。“风险代理费协议须采用由委托人签署的书面形式，并说明费用确定方法，包括和解、审判或上诉时律师可取得的一个或多个百分比；从赔偿款中扣除的诉讼费及其他开支；以及这些开支是在计算风险代理费之前还是之后扣除。协议必须明确告知委托人，无论其是否胜诉，仍须承担哪些开支。风险代理事项结束后，律师应向委托人提供书面陈述，说明事项结果；如有赔偿，则说明向委托人支付的金额及其确定方法。”规则 1.5(c)。本题中，双方协议没有任何不当之处，且在具体情形下显得合理。A 不正确。根据规则 1.8(i)，“律师不得取得诉因或诉讼标的中的所有权利益……但律师可以……在民事案件中与委托人约定合理的风险代理费。”规则 1.8(i)(2)。因此，只要属于与委托人在民事案件中订立的合理风险代理费合同，规则并不一般性禁止律师取得委托人诉因中的所有权利益。本题协议显得合理。B 不正确。即使风险代理费总额可能超过小时费率，只要协议对双方而言合理，就不会因此自动不当。关键是，该选项没有说费用会远远超过小时费率——可能只高出一美元。C 不正确。这是典型的“由委托人提出”型错误选项。请记住，仅仅因为委托人认为某事是好主意，并不会使其在规则下适当。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-017","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":17,"topic":"Model Rule 1.6(b)(1) / Model Rule 1.6","question":"An attorney has been hired by a client to represent the client in a civil commitment proceeding initiated by the state. The client is now undergoing psychiatric evaluation to determine whether civil commitment should be ordered. The client told the attorney that the client intends to commit suicide as soon as the tests are completed, and the attorney believes that the client will carry out this threat. Suicide and attempted suicide are crimes in the state. Is it proper for the attorney to disclose the client’s intentions to the authorities?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the information concerns a future crime and is not protected by the attorney-client evidentiary privilege.","B":"Yes, because the information concerns a future crime that is likely to result in the client’s imminent death.","C":"No, because the attorney does not know whether the client has attempted suicide in the past.","D":"No, because disclosure would aid the state in its civil commitment case against the client."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer may reveal information learned in confidence if disclosure of that information is necessary to prevent reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm. Model Rule 1.6(b)(1). Here, if the attorney does not reveal the information, it will likely result in the death of the client. Consequently, it would be proper for the attorney to disclose the client’s intentions to the authorities. A is incorrect. The starting point of any confidential information provided by a client is that it is protected under Model Rule 1.6. Here, the information is in fact protected. However, it falls under an exception for information relating to certain death or bodily harm, and can be revealed despite being protected. C is incorrect. The issue is that the client has told the attorney he is intending to commit suicide. The analysis does not change in relation to how likely it may be that the client will in fact try to go through with it. D is incorrect. This Rule is most concerned with a person’s safety. Although there is a risk that disclosure could aid in a civil commitment, the primary concern is that a person in danger receive potential help.","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer may reveal information learned in confidence if disclosure of that information is necessary to prevent reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm. Model Rule 1.6(b)(1). Here, if the attorney does not reveal the information, it will likely result in the death of the client. Consequently, it would be proper for the attorney to disclose the client’s intentions to the authorities. A is incorrect. The starting point of any confidential information provided by a client is that it is protected under Model Rule 1.6. Here, the information is in fact protected. However, it falls under an exception for information relating to certain death or bodily harm, and can be revealed despite being protected. C is incorrect. The issue is that the client has told the attorney he is intending to commit suicide. The analysis does not change in relation to how likely it may be that the client will in fact try to go through with it. D is incorrect. This Rule is most concerned with a person’s safety. Although there is a risk that disclosure could aid in a civil commitment, the primary concern is that a person in danger receive potential help.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the attorney to disclose the client’s intentions to the authorities?","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer may reveal information learned in confidence if disclosure of that information is necessary to prevent reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm. Model Rule 1.6(b)(1). Here, if the attorney does not reveal the information, it will likely result in the death of the client. Consequently, it would be proper for the attorney to disclose the client’s intentions to the authorities. A is incorrect. The starting point of any confidential information provided by a client is that it is protected under Model Rule 1.6. Here, the information is in fact protected. However, it falls under an exception for information relating to certain death or bodily harm, and can be revealed despite being protected. C is incorrect. The issue is that the client has told the attorney he is intending to commit suicide. The analysis does not change in relation to how likely it may be that the client will in fact try to go through with it. D is incorrect. This Rule is most concerned with a person’s safety. Although there is a risk that disclosure could aid in a civil commitment, the primary concern is that a person in danger receive potential help.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer may reveal information learned in confidence if disclosure of that information is necessary to prevent reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm. Model Rule 1.6(b)(1). Here, if the attorney does not reveal the information, it will likely result in the death of the client. Consequently, it would be proper for the attorney to disclose the client’s intentions to the authorities. A is incorrect. The starting point of any confidential information provided by a client is that it is protected under Model Rule 1.6. Here, the information is in fact protected. However, it falls under an exception for information relating to certain death or bodily harm, and can be revealed despite being protected. C is incorrect. The issue is that the client has told the attorney he is intending to commit suicide. The analysis does not change in relation to how likely it may be that the client will in fact try to go through with it. D is incorrect. This Rule is most concerned with a person’s safety. Although there is a risk that disclosure could aid in a civil commitment, the primary concern is that a person in danger receive potential help.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.6(b)(1)","Model Rule 1.6"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[177,178],"sourceAnswerPages":[278],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师受委托人聘请，代理其参加州政府发起的民事强制收治程序。委托人正在接受精神评估，以判断是否应下令强制收治。委托人告诉律师，检查一结束就会自杀，而律师相信委托人会实施这一威胁。在该州，自杀和企图自杀均构成犯罪。律师向有关机关披露委托人的意图是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为该信息涉及未来犯罪，不受律师—委托人证据特权保护。","B":"适当，因为该信息涉及很可能造成委托人迫在眉睫死亡的未来犯罪。","C":"不适当，因为律师不知道委托人过去是否曾企图自杀。","D":"不适当，因为披露会帮助州政府在针对委托人的民事强制收治案件中胜诉。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。如果披露保密得知的信息是防止合理确定的死亡或重大身体伤害所必需的，律师可以披露。《职业行为示范规则》1.6(b)(1)。本题中，如果律师不披露，委托人很可能死亡。因此，律师向有关机关披露委托人的意图是适当的。A 不正确。对于委托人提供的任何保密信息，分析起点都是其受到规则 1.6 保护。本题信息事实上受到保护；但它属于有关确定死亡或身体伤害信息的例外，因而即使受到保护也可以披露。C 不正确。问题在于委托人已经告诉律师自己打算自杀。委托人事实上实施自杀的可能性大小，不会因其过去是否尝试过而改变本题分析。D 不正确。该规则最关心人的安全。披露虽然可能有助于民事强制收治，但首要关切是让面临危险的人获得可能的帮助。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-018","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":18,"topic":"Model Rule 3.5(a)","question":"An attorney is a long-time member of the state legislature and serves on the legislative budget committee that funds the local trial courts in the state. The attorney also maintains a part-time law practice as is permitted in the state. An influential businessperson who regularly makes significant contributions to the attorney’s political campaigns asked the attorney to help his uncle, who was involved in a bitter divorce. The attorney called the trial judge sitting on the uncle’s case, a personal friend of the attorney. In discussing some upcoming votes of the budget committee with the judge, the attorney mentioned that the uncle was the type of solid citizen and influential person who could help garner support for the budget and thus ensure the economic health of the judicial system. The trial judge ultimately ruled in the uncle’s favor. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the trial judge ruled in the uncle’s favor.","B":"Yes, because the attorney used her public position to attempt to influence a tribunal in a pending matter.","C":"No, because the attorney called the trial judge in her capacity as a legislator and not as the uncle’s lawyer.","D":"No, because members of the state legislature are permitted by law to engage in part-time legal practice."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not seek to influence a judge by means prohibited by law. Model Rule 3.5(a). Here, the attorney used her public position to attempt to influence the judge. Specifically, she noted that the uncle was the type of solid citizen and influential person who could help garner support for the budget. Clearly, this was an attempt to have the judge rule in the uncle’s favor in the pending divorce. A is incorrect. Although the trial judge ruled in the uncle’s favor, if there had been no improper influence brought by the attorney, the simple fact that the judge ruled in the uncle’s favor would not lead to a violation of the Rules. C is incorrect. Whether calling as a legislator or an attorney, the attorney still violated the Rules by improperly trying to influence the judge. D is incorrect. The ability to engage in law practice does not give an attorney the ability to attempt to improperly influence a judge. A broad answer choice like this should have been thrown out immediately.","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not seek to influence a judge by means prohibited by law. Model Rule 3.5(a). Here, the attorney used her public position to attempt to influence the judge. Specifically, she noted that the uncle was the type of solid citizen and influential person who could help garner support for the budget. Clearly, this was an attempt to have the judge rule in the uncle’s favor in the pending divorce. A is incorrect. Although the trial judge ruled in the uncle’s favor, if there had been no improper influence brought by the attorney, the simple fact that the judge ruled in the uncle’s favor would not lead to a violation of the Rules. C is incorrect. Whether calling as a legislator or an attorney, the attorney still violated the Rules by improperly trying to influence the judge. D is incorrect. The ability to engage in law practice does not give an attorney the ability to attempt to improperly influence a judge. A broad answer choice like this should have been thrown out immediately.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not seek to influence a judge by means prohibited by law. Model Rule 3.5(a). Here, the attorney used her public position to attempt to influence the judge. Specifically, she noted that the uncle was the type of solid citizen and influential person who could help garner support for the budget. Clearly, this was an attempt to have the judge rule in the uncle’s favor in the pending divorce. A is incorrect. Although the trial judge ruled in the uncle’s favor, if there had been no improper influence brought by the attorney, the simple fact that the judge ruled in the uncle’s favor would not lead to a violation of the Rules. C is incorrect. Whether calling as a legislator or an attorney, the attorney still violated the Rules by improperly trying to influence the judge. D is incorrect. The ability to engage in law practice does not give an attorney the ability to attempt to improperly influence a judge. A broad answer choice like this should have been thrown out immediately.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not seek to influence a judge by means prohibited by law. Model Rule 3.5(a). Here, the attorney used her public position to attempt to influence the judge. Specifically, she noted that the uncle was the type of solid citizen and influential person who could help garner support for the budget. Clearly, this was an attempt to have the judge rule in the uncle’s favor in the pending divorce. A is incorrect. Although the trial judge ruled in the uncle’s favor, if there had been no improper influence brought by the attorney, the simple fact that the judge ruled in the uncle’s favor would not lead to a violation of the Rules. C is incorrect. Whether calling as a legislator or an attorney, the attorney still violated the Rules by improperly trying to influence the judge. D is incorrect. The ability to engage in law practice does not give an attorney the ability to attempt to improperly influence a judge. A broad answer choice like this should have been thrown out immediately.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.5(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[178],"sourceAnswerPages":[278,279],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师长期担任州议员，并在负责向州内地方初审法院拨款的立法预算委员会任职。该州允许她同时维持兼职律师业务。一名经常向该律师政治竞选活动作出巨额捐款的有影响力商人，请求律师帮助其正卷入激烈离婚诉讼的叔叔。律师致电负责叔叔案件的初审法官；该法官也是律师的私人朋友。律师与法官讨论预算委员会即将进行的一些表决时提到，叔叔属于可靠公民和有影响力人士，可以帮助争取对预算的支持，从而确保司法系统的经济健康。初审法官最终作出有利于叔叔的裁判。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为初审法官作出了有利于叔叔的裁判。","B":"是，因为律师利用其公职地位，试图影响审裁机构处理待决事项。","C":"否，因为律师是以议员身份而非叔叔的律师身份致电初审法官。","D":"否，因为法律允许州议员兼职从事法律执业。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。律师不得以法律禁止的方式试图影响法官。《职业行为示范规则》3.5(a)。本题中，律师利用公职地位试图影响法官。具体而言，她指出叔叔是可靠且有影响力的人士，可以帮助争取预算支持。这显然是企图让法官在待决离婚案件中作出有利于叔叔的裁判。A 不正确。初审法官虽作出了有利裁判，但如果律师没有施加不当影响，仅仅裁判有利并不会违反规则。C 不正确。无论是以议员还是律师身份来电，律师不当地试图影响法官都违反规则。D 不正确。能够从事法律业务，并不会赋予律师不当影响法官的权利。这种宽泛选项应立即排除。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-019","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":19,"topic":"Rule 3.8(A) / Rule 3.12 / Rule 3.1","question":"A judge in a criminal trial court of the state wishes to serve as guardian of her father, who has been declared incompetent. Accepting the responsibilities of the position would not interfere with the performance of the judge’s official duties. Although the position in all likelihood would not involve contested litigation, it would be necessary for the judge to prepare and sign various pleadings, motions, and other papers and to appear in civil court on her father’s behalf. Would it be proper for the judge to undertake this guardianship?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the judge does not receive compensation for her services as guardian.","B":"Yes, because the position involves a close family member and will not interfere with the judge’s performance of her judicial duties.","C":"No, because the position will require the judge to appear in court.","D":"No, because the position will require the judge to prepare and sign pleadings, motions, and other papers."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. “A judge shall not accept appointment to serve in a fiduciary position, such as executor, administrator, trustee, guardian, attorney in fact, or other personal representative, except for the estate, trust, or person of a member of the judge’s family, and then only if such service will not interfere with the proper performance of judicial duties.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.8(A). Here, the judge is serving for her father, and it does not appear to interfere with the judge’s performance of her official duties. A is incorrect. “A judge may accept reasonable compensation for extrajudicial activities permitted by this Code or other law unless such acceptance would appear to a reasonable person to undermine the judge’s independence, integrity, or impartiality.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.12. Because a judge can accept compensation, there is no Rule stating that if a judge does not receive compensation that judge may take on any particular extrajudicial activity. Importantly, even a noncompensatory position could undermine the judge’s impartiality. C is incorrect. A judge may engage in extrajudicial activities so long as they do not interfere with proper performance of the judge’s judicial duties or create the danger of undermining the judge’s impartiality. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.1. Simply appearing in court would be unlikely to do that. D is incorrect. A judge may engage in extrajudicial activities so long as they do not interfere with proper performance of the judge’s judicial duties or create the danger of undermining the judge’s impartiality. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.1. Preparing and signing papers will not necessarily interfere with judicial activities.","rule":"B is the correct answer. “A judge shall not accept appointment to serve in a fiduciary position, such as executor, administrator, trustee, guardian, attorney in fact, or other personal representative, except for the estate, trust, or person of a member of the judge’s family, and then only if such service will not interfere with the proper performance of judicial duties.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.8(A). Here, the judge is serving for her father, and it does not appear to interfere with the judge’s performance of her official duties. A is incorrect. “A judge may accept reasonable compensation for extrajudicial activities permitted by this Code or other law unless such acceptance would appear to a reasonable person to undermine the judge’s independence, integrity, or impartiality.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.12. Because a judge can accept compensation, there is no Rule stating that if a judge does not receive compensation that judge may take on any particular extrajudicial activity. Importantly, even a noncompensatory position could undermine the judge’s impartiality. C is incorrect. A judge may engage in extrajudicial activities so long as they do not interfere with proper performance of the judge’s judicial duties or create the danger of undermining the judge’s impartiality. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.1. Simply appearing in court would be unlikely to do that. D is incorrect. A judge may engage in extrajudicial activities so long as they do not interfere with proper performance of the judge’s judicial duties or create the danger of undermining the judge’s impartiality. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.1. Preparing and signing papers will not necessarily interfere with judicial activities.","irac":{"issue":"Would it be proper for the judge to undertake this guardianship?","rule":"B is the correct answer. “A judge shall not accept appointment to serve in a fiduciary position, such as executor, administrator, trustee, guardian, attorney in fact, or other personal representative, except for the estate, trust, or person of a member of the judge’s family, and then only if such service will not interfere with the proper performance of judicial duties.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.8(A). Here, the judge is serving for her father, and it does not appear to interfere with the judge’s performance of her official duties. A is incorrect. “A judge may accept reasonable compensation for extrajudicial activities permitted by this Code or other law unless such acceptance would appear to a reasonable person to undermine the judge’s independence, integrity, or impartiality.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.12. Because a judge can accept compensation, there is no Rule stating that if a judge does not receive compensation that judge may take on any particular extrajudicial activity. Importantly, even a noncompensatory position could undermine the judge’s impartiality. C is incorrect. A judge may engage in extrajudicial activities so long as they do not interfere with proper performance of the judge’s judicial duties or create the danger of undermining the judge’s impartiality. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.1. Simply appearing in court would be unlikely to do that. D is incorrect. A judge may engage in extrajudicial activities so long as they do not interfere with proper performance of the judge’s judicial duties or create the danger of undermining the judge’s impartiality. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.1. Preparing and signing papers will not necessarily interfere with judicial activities.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. “A judge shall not accept appointment to serve in a fiduciary position, such as executor, administrator, trustee, guardian, attorney in fact, or other personal representative, except for the estate, trust, or person of a member of the judge’s family, and then only if such service will not interfere with the proper performance of judicial duties.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.8(A). Here, the judge is serving for her father, and it does not appear to interfere with the judge’s performance of her official duties. A is incorrect. “A judge may accept reasonable compensation for extrajudicial activities permitted by this Code or other law unless such acceptance would appear to a reasonable person to undermine the judge’s independence, integrity, or impartiality.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.12. Because a judge can accept compensation, there is no Rule stating that if a judge does not receive compensation that judge may take on any particular extrajudicial activity. Importantly, even a noncompensatory position could undermine the judge’s impartiality. C is incorrect. A judge may engage in extrajudicial activities so long as they do not interfere with proper performance of the judge’s judicial duties or create the danger of undermining the judge’s impartiality. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.1. Simply appearing in court would be unlikely to do that. D is incorrect. A judge may engage in extrajudicial activities so long as they do not interfere with proper performance of the judge’s judicial duties or create the danger of undermining the judge’s impartiality. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.1. Preparing and signing papers will not necessarily interfere with judicial activities.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 3.8(A)","Rule 3.12","Rule 3.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[178,179],"sourceAnswerPages":[279],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名州刑事初审法院法官希望担任其已被宣告无行为能力之父亲的监护人。接受该职位职责不会妨碍法官履行职务。虽然该职位很可能不涉及争议诉讼，但法官需要代表父亲准备并签署各种诉状、动议和其他文件，并在民事法院出庭。法官接受这项监护职责是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为法官担任监护人不获得报酬。","B":"适当，因为该职位涉及近亲，而且不会妨碍法官履行司法职责。","C":"不适当，因为该职位要求法官在法院出庭。","D":"不适当，因为该职位要求法官准备并签署诉状、动议和其他文件。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。“法官不得接受任命担任受信人职位，例如遗嘱执行人、遗产管理人、受托人、监护人、事实上的代理人或其他个人代表，但为法官家属的遗产、信托或本人提供服务除外；且即使属于家属，也只有在该服务不会妨碍适当履行司法职责时才可以。”《司法行为示范准则》规则 3.8(A)。本题中，法官为父亲提供服务，看起来不会妨碍其履行职务。A 不正确。“法官可以就准则或其他法律允许的司法职务外活动接受合理报酬，除非在理性人看来，接受报酬会削弱法官的独立性、诚信或公正性。”规则 3.12。法官既然可以接受报酬，就不存在一项规定称只要不收报酬，就可以从事任何特定司法职务外活动。关键是，即使没有报酬的职位也可能削弱法官公正性。C 不正确。只要不会妨碍法官适当履行司法职责，也不会产生削弱其公正性的危险，法官可以从事司法职务外活动。规则 3.1。仅仅在法院出庭不太可能造成这些问题。D 不正确。只要不妨碍司法职责或产生削弱公正性的危险，法官可以从事司法职务外活动。规则 3.1。准备和签署文件未必妨碍司法活动。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-020","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":20,"topic":"Model Rule 3.4(a) / Model Rule 3.4","question":"An attorney represents a corporation that is a defendant in a product liability case. An engineer who is a corporation employee and nearing retirement was likely to be a key witness in the case, as she had been in charge of all of the corporation’s product safety testing during the relevant period. The engineer had been very critical of the corporation’s safety testing procedures during that period and had repeatedly complained that the product at issue had not been adequately tested. The engineer’s views were reduced to writing and were well known to many employees of the corporation. Because of the early stage of the case, however, plaintiff’s counsel was not yet aware of the engineer’s existence or her views. Aware of the engineer’s views, the attorney approached the corporation’s officials and recommended that it offer the engineer a special package of severance benefits if she would retire immediately and move to the Bahamas. The attorney believed that if the engineer accepted this offer, she would be beyond the subpoena power of the court in which the suit against the corporation was pending. The corporation adopted the attorney’s recommendation and made the offer. The engineer accepted it. The attorney did not disclose the engineer’s identity to plaintiff’s counsel. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney caused the engineer to leave the jurisdiction of the court for the purpose of making her unavailable as a witness.","B":"Yes, because opposing counsel had not yet had a reasonable opportunity to learn of the engineer’s views.","C":"No, because the engineer’s views were reduced to writing and are well known to many other employees of the corporation.","D":"No, because there was no pending request for the engineer’s testimony at the time the retirement offer was made to the engineer."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not: (a) unlawfully obstruct another party’s access to evidence.” Model Rule 3.4(a). “Fair competition in the adversary system is secured by prohibitions against destruction or concealment of evidence, improperly influencing witnesses, obstructive tactics in discovery procedure, and the like.” Model Rule 3.4, Comment [1]. Here, the attorney’s actions in putting the engineer out of the reach of the court are clearly improper. B is incorrect. Even if the opposing counsel had learned of the engineer’s views, the attorney would have still violated the Rules by trying to make access to the evidence impossible. C is incorrect. This still would not negate the fact that the attorney is clearly trying to get the engineer beyond the reach of the court. D is incorrect. The main issue is that the attorney is actively trying to hide evidence that he knows might be relevant in the case.","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not: (a) unlawfully obstruct another party’s access to evidence.” Model Rule 3.4(a). “Fair competition in the adversary system is secured by prohibitions against destruction or concealment of evidence, improperly influencing witnesses, obstructive tactics in discovery procedure, and the like.” Model Rule 3.4, Comment [1]. Here, the attorney’s actions in putting the engineer out of the reach of the court are clearly improper. B is incorrect. Even if the opposing counsel had learned of the engineer’s views, the attorney would have still violated the Rules by trying to make access to the evidence impossible. C is incorrect. This still would not negate the fact that the attorney is clearly trying to get the engineer beyond the reach of the court. D is incorrect. The main issue is that the attorney is actively trying to hide evidence that he knows might be relevant in the case.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not: (a) unlawfully obstruct another party’s access to evidence.” Model Rule 3.4(a). “Fair competition in the adversary system is secured by prohibitions against destruction or concealment of evidence, improperly influencing witnesses, obstructive tactics in discovery procedure, and the like.” Model Rule 3.4, Comment [1]. Here, the attorney’s actions in putting the engineer out of the reach of the court are clearly improper. B is incorrect. Even if the opposing counsel had learned of the engineer’s views, the attorney would have still violated the Rules by trying to make access to the evidence impossible. C is incorrect. This still would not negate the fact that the attorney is clearly trying to get the engineer beyond the reach of the court. D is incorrect. The main issue is that the attorney is actively trying to hide evidence that he knows might be relevant in the case.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not: (a) unlawfully obstruct another party’s access to evidence.” Model Rule 3.4(a). “Fair competition in the adversary system is secured by prohibitions against destruction or concealment of evidence, improperly influencing witnesses, obstructive tactics in discovery procedure, and the like.” Model Rule 3.4, Comment [1]. Here, the attorney’s actions in putting the engineer out of the reach of the court are clearly improper. B is incorrect. Even if the opposing counsel had learned of the engineer’s views, the attorney would have still violated the Rules by trying to make access to the evidence impossible. C is incorrect. This still would not negate the fact that the attorney is clearly trying to get the engineer beyond the reach of the court. D is incorrect. The main issue is that the attorney is actively trying to hide evidence that he knows might be relevant in the case.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.4(a)","Model Rule 3.4"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[179],"sourceAnswerPages":[280],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理一家公司抗辩产品责任案件。公司一名接近退休的工程师很可能成为关键证人，因为相关期间公司全部产品安全测试均由她负责。该工程师一直严厉批评公司当时的安全测试程序，并多次投诉涉案产品未得到充分测试。工程师的观点已形成书面文件，也为许多公司雇员熟知。但由于案件尚处早期阶段，原告律师还不知道工程师的存在及其观点。律师知悉工程师观点后，找到公司管理人员，建议公司向工程师提供特别离职福利方案，条件是她立即退休并移居巴哈马。律师认为，如果工程师接受，就会超出受理公司诉讼之法院的传票权范围。公司采纳建议并提出方案，工程师也予以接受。律师没有向原告律师披露工程师身份。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师为了使工程师无法作为证人出庭，促使她离开法院管辖区。","B":"是，因为对方律师尚未获得合理机会了解工程师的观点。","C":"否，因为工程师观点已经形成书面文件，也为公司许多其他雇员熟知。","D":"否，因为向工程师提出退休方案时，没有任何要求其作证的待决请求。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。“律师不得：(a) 非法阻碍另一方取得证据。”《职业行为示范规则》3.4(a)。“对抗制度中的公平竞争，通过禁止销毁或隐匿证据、不当影响证人、在证据开示程序中采取阻挠策略等方式得到保障。”规则 3.4 评注 [1]。本题中，律师使工程师脱离法院可及范围的行为显然不当。B 不正确。即使对方律师已经了解到工程师观点，律师试图使对方无法取得证据仍会违反规则。C 不正确。这仍不能否定律师明确试图使工程师脱离法院可及范围这一事实。D 不正确。核心问题是，律师正主动试图隐藏其知道可能与案件有关的证据。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-021","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":21,"topic":"Model Rule 1.15","question":"An attorney represented a client in a personal injury action against the driver of the car in which the client was injured while a passenger. The personal injury action was settled, and the attorney received a check in the amount of $10,000 payable to the attorney. The attorney deposited the check in her Clients’ Trust Account. One day later, the attorney received a letter from a bank that had heard of the settlement of the personal injury lawsuit. The bank informed the attorney that the client had failed to make his monthly mortgage payments for the last three months and demanded that the attorney immediately release $900 of the proceeds of the settlement to the bank or the bank would institute mortgage foreclosure proceedings against the client. The attorney informed the client of the bank’s letter. The client responded: “I don’t care what the bank does. The property is essentially worthless, so let the bank foreclose. If the bank wants to sue me, I’ll be easy enough to find. I don’t think they’ll even bother. You just take your legal fees and turn the rest of the proceeds over to me.” Is the attorney subject to discipline if she follows the client’s instructions?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the client does not dispute the $900 debt to the bank.","B":"Yes, because the attorney knew that the client was planning to force the bank to sue him.","C":"No, because the attorney had no reason to believe that the client would not have sufficient funds to pay any subsequent judgment obtained by the bank.","D":"No, because the bank has no established right to the specific proceeds of the client’s personal injury judgment."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer is required to safeguard a client’s property. While in some situations, a third party may have a lawful claim against specific funds or other property in a lawyer’s custody, such as a client’s creditor who has a lien on funds recovered in a personal injury, unless there is such lawful claim, the lawyer’s first priority is to safeguard those funds for the client. Model Rule 1.15, Comment [4]. Here, since the bank has not established any legal right to the funds, the attorney is not required to turn them over. A is incorrect. While the client does not dispute the debt, there is still no indication that the bank has done anything to gain a legal interest in the funds. B is incorrect. Although the attorney may know the client is forcing the bank to sue him, this does not create any change regarding legal rights in the money. Additionally, an attorney is under no duty to actively dissuade clients from creating or engaging in litigation so long as that litigation is meritorious. C is incorrect. The issue here is who has lawful interest in the money. It is not the attorney’s responsibility to make sure his client pays his debts.","rule":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer is required to safeguard a client’s property. While in some situations, a third party may have a lawful claim against specific funds or other property in a lawyer’s custody, such as a client’s creditor who has a lien on funds recovered in a personal injury, unless there is such lawful claim, the lawyer’s first priority is to safeguard those funds for the client. Model Rule 1.15, Comment [4]. Here, since the bank has not established any legal right to the funds, the attorney is not required to turn them over. A is incorrect. While the client does not dispute the debt, there is still no indication that the bank has done anything to gain a legal interest in the funds. B is incorrect. Although the attorney may know the client is forcing the bank to sue him, this does not create any change regarding legal rights in the money. Additionally, an attorney is under no duty to actively dissuade clients from creating or engaging in litigation so long as that litigation is meritorious. C is incorrect. The issue here is who has lawful interest in the money. It is not the attorney’s responsibility to make sure his client pays his debts.","irac":{"issue":"” Is the attorney subject to discipline if she follows the client’s instructions?","rule":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer is required to safeguard a client’s property. While in some situations, a third party may have a lawful claim against specific funds or other property in a lawyer’s custody, such as a client’s creditor who has a lien on funds recovered in a personal injury, unless there is such lawful claim, the lawyer’s first priority is to safeguard those funds for the client. Model Rule 1.15, Comment [4]. Here, since the bank has not established any legal right to the funds, the attorney is not required to turn them over. A is incorrect. While the client does not dispute the debt, there is still no indication that the bank has done anything to gain a legal interest in the funds. B is incorrect. Although the attorney may know the client is forcing the bank to sue him, this does not create any change regarding legal rights in the money. Additionally, an attorney is under no duty to actively dissuade clients from creating or engaging in litigation so long as that litigation is meritorious. C is incorrect. The issue here is who has lawful interest in the money. It is not the attorney’s responsibility to make sure his client pays his debts.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer is required to safeguard a client’s property. While in some situations, a third party may have a lawful claim against specific funds or other property in a lawyer’s custody, such as a client’s creditor who has a lien on funds recovered in a personal injury, unless there is such lawful claim, the lawyer’s first priority is to safeguard those funds for the client. Model Rule 1.15, Comment [4]. Here, since the bank has not established any legal right to the funds, the attorney is not required to turn them over. A is incorrect. While the client does not dispute the debt, there is still no indication that the bank has done anything to gain a legal interest in the funds. B is incorrect. Although the attorney may know the client is forcing the bank to sue him, this does not create any change regarding legal rights in the money. Additionally, an attorney is under no duty to actively dissuade clients from creating or engaging in litigation so long as that litigation is meritorious. C is incorrect. The issue here is who has lawful interest in the money. It is not the attorney’s responsibility to make sure his client pays his debts.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.15"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[180],"sourceAnswerPages":[280],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理委托人提起人身伤害诉讼，起诉事故汽车的驾驶人；委托人作为乘客在事故中受伤。人身伤害诉讼达成和解，律师收到一张以自己为收款人、金额 10,000 美元的支票。律师将支票存入委托人信托账户。一天后，律师收到一家银行来函；银行听说人身伤害诉讼已经和解。银行告知律师，委托人过去三个月没有按月支付房屋抵押贷款，要求律师立即从和解款中向银行支付 900 美元，否则将对委托人启动抵押物止赎程序。律师把银行来函告知委托人。委托人回答：“银行做什么我都不在乎。房产基本上一文不值，就让银行止赎吧。如果银行想起诉我，要找到我很容易。我想他们甚至不会费这个劲。你只需扣除律师费，把剩余款项全部交给我。”如果律师遵从委托人指示，是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为委托人并不否认欠银行 900 美元。","B":"是，因为律师知道委托人计划迫使银行起诉。","C":"否，因为律师没有理由认为委托人没有足够资金支付银行日后取得的任何判决。","D":"否，因为银行尚未对委托人人身伤害判决的特定款项确立权利。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。律师必须妥善保管委托人财产。在某些情况下，第三方可能对律师保管的特定资金或其他财产拥有合法主张，例如委托人债权人对人身伤害案件追回的款项拥有留置权；但如果不存在这种合法主张，律师首先应为委托人保管资金。《职业行为示范规则》1.15 评注 [4]。本题中，银行尚未对资金确立任何法律权利，律师没有义务将其交给银行。A 不正确。委托人虽然不否认债务，但没有迹象表明银行已经采取任何措施取得对该资金的法律利益。B 不正确。律师虽然可能知道委托人正在迫使银行起诉，但这并不会改变款项上的法律权利。此外，只要诉讼有依据，律师没有义务积极劝阻委托人制造或参与诉讼。C 不正确。本题问题是谁对款项拥有合法利益。确保委托人偿还债务并非律师责任。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-022","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":22,"topic":"Model Rule 7.5(b)","question":"Three lawyers, Alpha, Beta, and Delta, formed a partnership to practice law with offices in both State First and State Second. Alpha is admitted to practice only in State First. Beta is admitted to practice only in State Second, and Delta is admitted to practice in both States First and Second. The following letterhead is on stationery used by their offices in both states: Alpha, Beta, and Delta Attorneys at Law 100 State Street 200 Bank Building City, State First City, State Second (200) 555-5555 (202) 555-5555 Attorney Alpha Admitted to practice only in State First Attorney Beta Admitted to practice only in State Second Attorney Delta Admitted to practice in States First and Second Are the members of the partnership subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"No, because the letterhead states the jurisdictions in which each partner is admitted.","B":"Yes, because there is no jurisdiction in which both Alpha and Beta are admitted to practice.","C":"Yes, because the firm name used by each office contains the name of a lawyer not admitted to practice in that jurisdiction.","D":"Yes, unless Delta actively practices law in both States First and Second."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. “A law firm with offices in more than one jurisdiction may use the same name or other professional designation in each jurisdiction, but identification of the lawyers in an office of the firm shall indicate the jurisdictional limitations on those not licensed to practice in the jurisdiction where the office is located.” Model Rule 7.5(b). Here, since the letterhead states the jurisdictions in which each partner is admitted, it is proper under the Rule. B is incorrect. There is no Rule stating that a lawyer in a firm has to be licensed in every jurisdiction in which the firm has offices. C is incorrect. As stated above, as long as the jurisdictional limitations are made clear, the letterhead is not improper. D is incorrect. This answer implies that the propriety of the situation depends upon Delta splitting his practice between both states (perhaps to the point of limiting the contributions of Alpha and Beta to the firm). As stated above, the law does not require this.","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A law firm with offices in more than one jurisdiction may use the same name or other professional designation in each jurisdiction, but identification of the lawyers in an office of the firm shall indicate the jurisdictional limitations on those not licensed to practice in the jurisdiction where the office is located.” Model Rule 7.5(b). Here, since the letterhead states the jurisdictions in which each partner is admitted, it is proper under the Rule. B is incorrect. There is no Rule stating that a lawyer in a firm has to be licensed in every jurisdiction in which the firm has offices. C is incorrect. As stated above, as long as the jurisdictional limitations are made clear, the letterhead is not improper. D is incorrect. This answer implies that the propriety of the situation depends upon Delta splitting his practice between both states (perhaps to the point of limiting the contributions of Alpha and Beta to the firm). As stated above, the law does not require this.","irac":{"issue":"The following letterhead is on stationery used by their offices in both states: Alpha, Beta, and Delta Attorneys at Law 100 State Street 200 Bank Building City, State First City, State Second (200) 555-5555 (202) 555-5555 Attorney Alpha Admitted to practice only in State First Attorney Beta Admitted to practice only in State Second Attorney Delta Admitted to practice in States First and Second Are the members of the partnership subject to discipline?","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A law firm with offices in more than one jurisdiction may use the same name or other professional designation in each jurisdiction, but identification of the lawyers in an office of the firm shall indicate the jurisdictional limitations on those not licensed to practice in the jurisdiction where the office is located.” Model Rule 7.5(b). Here, since the letterhead states the jurisdictions in which each partner is admitted, it is proper under the Rule. B is incorrect. There is no Rule stating that a lawyer in a firm has to be licensed in every jurisdiction in which the firm has offices. C is incorrect. As stated above, as long as the jurisdictional limitations are made clear, the letterhead is not improper. D is incorrect. This answer implies that the propriety of the situation depends upon Delta splitting his practice between both states (perhaps to the point of limiting the contributions of Alpha and Beta to the firm). As stated above, the law does not require this.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. “A law firm with offices in more than one jurisdiction may use the same name or other professional designation in each jurisdiction, but identification of the lawyers in an office of the firm shall indicate the jurisdictional limitations on those not licensed to practice in the jurisdiction where the office is located.” Model Rule 7.5(b). Here, since the letterhead states the jurisdictions in which each partner is admitted, it is proper under the Rule. B is incorrect. There is no Rule stating that a lawyer in a firm has to be licensed in every jurisdiction in which the firm has offices. C is incorrect. As stated above, as long as the jurisdictional limitations are made clear, the letterhead is not improper. D is incorrect. This answer implies that the propriety of the situation depends upon Delta splitting his practice between both states (perhaps to the point of limiting the contributions of Alpha and Beta to the firm). As stated above, the law does not require this.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.5(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[180,181],"sourceAnswerPages":[281],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"三名律师甲、乙和丁组成合伙律师事务所，在第一州和第二州均设有办公室。甲仅获准在第一州执业，乙仅获准在第二州执业，丁则获准在第一州和第二州执业。两州办公室均使用以下信笺：“甲、乙、丁律师事务所；第一州某市州街 100 号；第二州某市银行大厦 200 号；电话……；律师甲仅获准在第一州执业；律师乙仅获准在第二州执业；律师丁获准在第一州和第二州执业。”该合伙事务所成员是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为信笺注明了每名合伙人获准执业的司法管辖区。","B":"是，因为没有任何一个司法管辖区同时准许甲和乙执业。","C":"是，因为各办公室使用的事务所名称中，包含未获准在该司法管辖区执业之律师的姓名。","D":"是，除非丁在第一州和第二州都实际从事法律业务。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。“在一个以上司法管辖区设有办公室的律师事务所，可以在每个管辖区使用相同名称或其他专业称谓；但在事务所某一办公室列明律师时，应注明其中未获准在该办公室所在司法管辖区执业之律师所受的管辖区限制。”《职业行为示范规则》7.5(b)。本题信笺注明了每名合伙人获准执业的管辖区，因此符合规则。B 不正确。没有规则要求事务所律师必须在该事务所设有办公室的每一个司法管辖区都取得执业资格。C 不正确。如上所述，只要明确说明管辖区限制，信笺就并非不当。D 不正确。该选项暗示情形是否适当取决于丁是否把业务分配在两州（或许甚至要限制甲和乙对事务所的贡献）。如上所述，法律没有这项要求。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-023","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":23,"topic":"Model Rule 3.9","question":"An attorney was engaged under a general retainer agreement to represent a corporation involved in the uranium industry. Under the agreement, the attorney handled all of the corporation’s legal work, which typically involved regulatory issues and litigation. The corporation told the attorney that a congressional committee was holding hearings concerning the extent of regulation in the copper industry. Because the corporation was considering buying a copper mine during the next fiscal year, the corporation wanted the attorney to testify that the industry was overregulated. The attorney subsequently testified before the relevant congressional committee. The attorney registered his appearance under his own name and did not disclose that he was appearing on behalf of a client. Afterward, the attorney billed the corporation for fees and expenses related to his testimony. Was the attorney’s conduct proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the duty of confidentiality prevented the attorney from disclosing the identity of his client.","B":"Yes, because the attorney-client evidentiary privilege prevented disclosure of the identity of his client in this context.","C":"No, because the attorney failed to disclose that he was appearing and testifying in a representative capacity.","D":"No, because the attorney accepted compensation in return for his testimony."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer representing a client before a legislative body or administrative agency in a nonadjudicative proceeding shall disclose that the appearance is in a representative capacity.” Model Rule 3.9. Here, it was improper for the attorney to fail to inform the committee that he was appearing on behalf of the corporation. A is incorrect. Because of the special Rule concerning legislative bodies or administrative agencies and that Rule’s concern for honesty and candor, the attorney could not prevent the disclosure of the identity of his client by claiming it was protected by confidentiality. B is incorrect. Because of the special Rule concerning legislative bodies or administrative agencies and that Rule’s concern for honesty and candor, the attorney could not prevent the disclosure of the identity of his client by claiming attorney-client privilege. D is incorrect. The issue here is not compensation. The issue is that the attorney is attempting to conceal the fact that he was appearing on behalf of a client and not on behalf of himself. Remember, the correct answer choice will always address the primary problem—here, the problem of the legislative body not knowing the existence of the client behind the scenes.","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer representing a client before a legislative body or administrative agency in a nonadjudicative proceeding shall disclose that the appearance is in a representative capacity.” Model Rule 3.9. Here, it was improper for the attorney to fail to inform the committee that he was appearing on behalf of the corporation. A is incorrect. Because of the special Rule concerning legislative bodies or administrative agencies and that Rule’s concern for honesty and candor, the attorney could not prevent the disclosure of the identity of his client by claiming it was protected by confidentiality. B is incorrect. Because of the special Rule concerning legislative bodies or administrative agencies and that Rule’s concern for honesty and candor, the attorney could not prevent the disclosure of the identity of his client by claiming attorney-client privilege. D is incorrect. The issue here is not compensation. The issue is that the attorney is attempting to conceal the fact that he was appearing on behalf of a client and not on behalf of himself. Remember, the correct answer choice will always address the primary problem—here, the problem of the legislative body not knowing the existence of the client behind the scenes.","irac":{"issue":"Was the attorney’s conduct proper?","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer representing a client before a legislative body or administrative agency in a nonadjudicative proceeding shall disclose that the appearance is in a representative capacity.” Model Rule 3.9. Here, it was improper for the attorney to fail to inform the committee that he was appearing on behalf of the corporation. A is incorrect. Because of the special Rule concerning legislative bodies or administrative agencies and that Rule’s concern for honesty and candor, the attorney could not prevent the disclosure of the identity of his client by claiming it was protected by confidentiality. B is incorrect. Because of the special Rule concerning legislative bodies or administrative agencies and that Rule’s concern for honesty and candor, the attorney could not prevent the disclosure of the identity of his client by claiming attorney-client privilege. D is incorrect. The issue here is not compensation. The issue is that the attorney is attempting to conceal the fact that he was appearing on behalf of a client and not on behalf of himself. Remember, the correct answer choice will always address the primary problem—here, the problem of the legislative body not knowing the existence of the client behind the scenes.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer representing a client before a legislative body or administrative agency in a nonadjudicative proceeding shall disclose that the appearance is in a representative capacity.” Model Rule 3.9. Here, it was improper for the attorney to fail to inform the committee that he was appearing on behalf of the corporation. A is incorrect. Because of the special Rule concerning legislative bodies or administrative agencies and that Rule’s concern for honesty and candor, the attorney could not prevent the disclosure of the identity of his client by claiming it was protected by confidentiality. B is incorrect. Because of the special Rule concerning legislative bodies or administrative agencies and that Rule’s concern for honesty and candor, the attorney could not prevent the disclosure of the identity of his client by claiming attorney-client privilege. D is incorrect. The issue here is not compensation. The issue is that the attorney is attempting to conceal the fact that he was appearing on behalf of a client and not on behalf of himself. Remember, the correct answer choice will always address the primary problem—here, the problem of the legislative body not knowing the existence of the client behind the scenes.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.9"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[181,182],"sourceAnswerPages":[281,282],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师根据一般聘用协议，代理一家铀行业公司。根据协议，律师处理公司的全部法律事务，通常涉及监管问题和诉讼。公司告知律师，一个国会委员会正就铜行业监管程度举行听证。由于公司考虑在下一财政年度收购一座铜矿，希望律师作证说明该行业受到过度监管。律师随后在有关国会委员会面前作证。他以个人名义登记出席，没有披露自己代表委托人出席。事后，律师向公司收取与证言有关的费用和开支。律师的行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为保密义务禁止律师披露委托人身份。","B":"适当，因为在这一背景下，律师—委托人证据特权禁止披露委托人身份。","C":"不适当，因为律师没有披露自己以代表他人的身份出席并作证。","D":"不适当，因为律师为作证接受了报酬。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。“律师代表委托人在立法机关或行政机构的非裁判程序中出席时，应披露其以代表他人的身份出席。”《职业行为示范规则》3.9。本题中，律师未告知委员会自己代表公司出席，是不适当的。A 不正确。鉴于有关立法机关或行政机构的特别规则，以及该规则对诚实和坦率的关注，律师不能以委托人身份受保密义务保护为由拒绝披露。B 不正确。基于同一特别规则及其对诚实和坦率的关注，律师也不能以律师—委托人特权为由拒绝披露委托人身份。D 不正确。本题问题不在于报酬，而在于律师试图隐瞒自己是在代表委托人，而非仅代表本人出席。请记住，正确选项总会处理首要问题——本题中就是立法机关不知道幕后委托人的存在。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-024","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":24,"topic":"Model Rule 3.5(b)","question":"An attorney represented a man in a case set for a jury trial. After the list of potential jurors was made available, the attorney hired a private investigator to interview the potential jurors and their family members concerning their relevant past experiences related to the subject matter of the action. The investigator did not inform the jurors or their family members that he was working on behalf of the attorney. The interviews were entirely voluntary and were not harassing. The attorney did not provide the report of the interviews to opposing counsel. He used the report to make decisions regarding jury selection. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney did not provide the report of the interviews to opposing counsel.","B":"Yes, because the investigator, at the attorney’s direction, communicated with potential jurors prior to trial.","C":"Yes, because the investigator did not inform the jurors or their family members that he was working on behalf of the attorney.","D":"No, because the interviews were entirely voluntary and not harassing."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not communicate ex parte with a judge, juror, prospective juror, or other official during the proceeding unless authorized to do so by law or court order. Model Rule 3.5(b). Here, the attorney violated the Rule by using his investigator to communicate with potential jurors prior to trial. A is incorrect. The Rule bars communication because an attorney could use such communications to improperly influence a jury. Simply giving a report of the interviews to opposing counsel would not fix this problem. C is incorrect. The Rule specifically bans communications. If the attorney had informed the jurors or their family members of his affiliation, this would not solve the problem of potential improper influence. D is incorrect. While voluntary and not harassing, the interviews could still have led to improper influence.","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not communicate ex parte with a judge, juror, prospective juror, or other official during the proceeding unless authorized to do so by law or court order. Model Rule 3.5(b). Here, the attorney violated the Rule by using his investigator to communicate with potential jurors prior to trial. A is incorrect. The Rule bars communication because an attorney could use such communications to improperly influence a jury. Simply giving a report of the interviews to opposing counsel would not fix this problem. C is incorrect. The Rule specifically bans communications. If the attorney had informed the jurors or their family members of his affiliation, this would not solve the problem of potential improper influence. D is incorrect. While voluntary and not harassing, the interviews could still have led to improper influence.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not communicate ex parte with a judge, juror, prospective juror, or other official during the proceeding unless authorized to do so by law or court order. Model Rule 3.5(b). Here, the attorney violated the Rule by using his investigator to communicate with potential jurors prior to trial. A is incorrect. The Rule bars communication because an attorney could use such communications to improperly influence a jury. Simply giving a report of the interviews to opposing counsel would not fix this problem. C is incorrect. The Rule specifically bans communications. If the attorney had informed the jurors or their family members of his affiliation, this would not solve the problem of potential improper influence. D is incorrect. While voluntary and not harassing, the interviews could still have led to improper influence.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not communicate ex parte with a judge, juror, prospective juror, or other official during the proceeding unless authorized to do so by law or court order. Model Rule 3.5(b). Here, the attorney violated the Rule by using his investigator to communicate with potential jurors prior to trial. A is incorrect. The Rule bars communication because an attorney could use such communications to improperly influence a jury. Simply giving a report of the interviews to opposing counsel would not fix this problem. C is incorrect. The Rule specifically bans communications. If the attorney had informed the jurors or their family members of his affiliation, this would not solve the problem of potential improper influence. D is incorrect. While voluntary and not harassing, the interviews could still have led to improper influence.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.5(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[182],"sourceAnswerPages":[282],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理一名男子参加预定由陪审团审理的案件。候选陪审员名单公布后，律师聘请私人调查员询问候选人及其家属，了解与诉讼标的有关的过往经历。调查员没有告诉候选人或其家属自己为该律师工作。询问完全自愿，也不构成骚扰。律师没有向对方律师提供询问报告，而是利用报告作出选择陪审员的决定。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师没有向对方律师提供询问报告。","B":"是，因为调查员依律师指示，在审判前与候选陪审员沟通。","C":"是，因为调查员没有告知候选人或其家属自己为该律师工作。","D":"否，因为询问完全自愿且不构成骚扰。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。除非法律或法院命令授权，律师不得在程序期间与法官、陪审员、候选陪审员或其他官员进行单方沟通。《职业行为示范规则》3.5(b)。本题中，律师利用调查员在审判前与候选陪审员沟通，违反规则。A 不正确。规则禁止沟通，是因为律师可能利用这种沟通不当影响陪审团。仅仅向对方律师提供询问报告，不能解决问题。C 不正确。规则明确禁止沟通。即使律师告知候选人或其家属自己的关联关系，也不能解决潜在不当影响问题。D 不正确。询问虽自愿且不构成骚扰，仍可能造成不当影响。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-025","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":25,"topic":"Model Rule 1.16(b)(2) / Model Rule 1.16(a)(1) / Model Rule 1.16(b)(1)","question":"For many years, an attorney has served as outside counsel to a corporation. Shortly after a change in management, the attorney discovered what she reasonably believed to be a material misstatement in a document she had drafted that the attorney was about to file on the corporation’s behalf with a government agency. The attorney advised the corporation’s board of directors that filing the document was probably criminal. However, the board disagreed that there was any material misstatement and directed the attorney to proceed with the filing. When the attorney indicated her intention to resign, the corporation argued that a resignation at this time would send a signal that there was a problem with the filing. The corporation urged the attorney to continue the representation, but offered to use in-house counsel to complete the work on the filing. Although she does not know for certain that filing the document is illegal, the attorney reasonably believes that it is. In any event, the attorney is personally uncomfortable with the representation and wants to withdraw. May the attorney withdraw from her representation of the corporation?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because withdrawal is permitted but not required when a client insists on conduct that the lawyer reasonably believes, but does not know, will be criminal.","B":"Yes, because withdrawal is required when a client insists on conduct which the lawyer reasonably believes, but does not know, will be criminal.","C":"No, because the corporation is correct that withdrawal would breach confidentiality by sending a signal that the filing is problematic.","D":"No, because the attorney’s withdrawal as outside counsel might affect the corporation’s ability to complete the filing in a timely fashion."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may withdraw from representing a client if “the client persists in a course of action involving the lawyer’s services that the lawyer reasonably believes is criminal or fraudulent.” Model Rule 1.16(b)(2). Here, since the attorney is unsure but reasonably believes there is a material misstatement in the document, she may withdraw. B is incorrect. When a lawyer reasonably believes an action is criminal or fraudulent, the withdrawal is optional, not mandatory. Withdrawal is mandatory when the attorney knows the representation will result in violation of the Rules of Professional Conduct or other law. Model Rule 1.16(a)(1). C is incorrect. In this situation, a lawyer may withdraw so long as the withdrawal “can be accomplished without material adverse effect on the interests of the client.” Model Rule 1.16(b)(1). Here, the mere possibility that the opposing counsel might realize there is a problem in the filing through the withdrawal would be unlikely to materially affect the client’s interests. D is incorrect. There is no indication that the attorney’s withdrawal would affect the corporation’s ability to complete the filing in a timely fashion. Even so, this potential issue would not create such a material adverse effect on the client’s interests that the attorney would be forced to continue representation.","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may withdraw from representing a client if “the client persists in a course of action involving the lawyer’s services that the lawyer reasonably believes is criminal or fraudulent.” Model Rule 1.16(b)(2). Here, since the attorney is unsure but reasonably believes there is a material misstatement in the document, she may withdraw. B is incorrect. When a lawyer reasonably believes an action is criminal or fraudulent, the withdrawal is optional, not mandatory. Withdrawal is mandatory when the attorney knows the representation will result in violation of the Rules of Professional Conduct or other law. Model Rule 1.16(a)(1). C is incorrect. In this situation, a lawyer may withdraw so long as the withdrawal “can be accomplished without material adverse effect on the interests of the client.” Model Rule 1.16(b)(1). Here, the mere possibility that the opposing counsel might realize there is a problem in the filing through the withdrawal would be unlikely to materially affect the client’s interests. D is incorrect. There is no indication that the attorney’s withdrawal would affect the corporation’s ability to complete the filing in a timely fashion. Even so, this potential issue would not create such a material adverse effect on the client’s interests that the attorney would be forced to continue representation.","irac":{"issue":"May the attorney withdraw from her representation of the corporation?","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may withdraw from representing a client if “the client persists in a course of action involving the lawyer’s services that the lawyer reasonably believes is criminal or fraudulent.” Model Rule 1.16(b)(2). Here, since the attorney is unsure but reasonably believes there is a material misstatement in the document, she may withdraw. B is incorrect. When a lawyer reasonably believes an action is criminal or fraudulent, the withdrawal is optional, not mandatory. Withdrawal is mandatory when the attorney knows the representation will result in violation of the Rules of Professional Conduct or other law. Model Rule 1.16(a)(1). C is incorrect. In this situation, a lawyer may withdraw so long as the withdrawal “can be accomplished without material adverse effect on the interests of the client.” Model Rule 1.16(b)(1). Here, the mere possibility that the opposing counsel might realize there is a problem in the filing through the withdrawal would be unlikely to materially affect the client’s interests. D is incorrect. There is no indication that the attorney’s withdrawal would affect the corporation’s ability to complete the filing in a timely fashion. Even so, this potential issue would not create such a material adverse effect on the client’s interests that the attorney would be forced to continue representation.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may withdraw from representing a client if “the client persists in a course of action involving the lawyer’s services that the lawyer reasonably believes is criminal or fraudulent.” Model Rule 1.16(b)(2). Here, since the attorney is unsure but reasonably believes there is a material misstatement in the document, she may withdraw. B is incorrect. When a lawyer reasonably believes an action is criminal or fraudulent, the withdrawal is optional, not mandatory. Withdrawal is mandatory when the attorney knows the representation will result in violation of the Rules of Professional Conduct or other law. Model Rule 1.16(a)(1). C is incorrect. In this situation, a lawyer may withdraw so long as the withdrawal “can be accomplished without material adverse effect on the interests of the client.” Model Rule 1.16(b)(1). Here, the mere possibility that the opposing counsel might realize there is a problem in the filing through the withdrawal would be unlikely to materially affect the client’s interests. D is incorrect. There is no indication that the attorney’s withdrawal would affect the corporation’s ability to complete the filing in a timely fashion. Even so, this potential issue would not create such a material adverse effect on the client’s interests that the attorney would be forced to continue representation.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.16(b)(2)","Model Rule 1.16(a)(1)","Model Rule 1.16(b)(1)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[183],"sourceAnswerPages":[282,283],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"多年来，一名律师一直担任某公司的外部法律顾问。公司管理层更换不久后，律师发现自己起草的一份文件中存在其合理认为属于重大的虚假陈述；她正准备代表公司向政府机构提交该文件。律师告知公司董事会，提交文件可能构成犯罪。但董事会不同意其中存在任何重大虚假陈述，指示律师继续提交。律师表示打算辞任时，公司主张此时辞任会发出文件有问题的信号。公司敦促律师继续代理，但提出可以由内部法律顾问完成文件提交工作。律师虽不能确定提交文件违法，却合理地相信确实违法。无论如何，律师本人对代理感到不安，希望退出。她能否退出对公司的代理？","choicesZh":{"A":"能，因为委托人坚持律师合理地认为、但并不确知属于犯罪的行为时，允许但不要求律师退出。","B":"能，因为委托人坚持律师合理地认为、但并不确知属于犯罪的行为时，律师必须退出。","C":"不能，因为公司正确指出，退出会发出提交存在问题的信号，从而违反保密义务。","D":"不能，因为外部法律顾问退出可能影响公司及时完成文件提交。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。如果“委托人持续从事涉及律师服务、而律师合理地认为属于犯罪或欺诈的行为”，律师可以退出代理。《职业行为示范规则》1.16(b)(2)。本题中，律师虽不确定，却合理地认为文件包含重大虚假陈述，因而可以退出。B 不正确。律师合理地认为某项行为属于犯罪或欺诈时，退出属于可选而非强制。律师知道代理将导致违反《职业行为规则》或其他法律时，才必须退出。规则 1.16(a)(1)。C 不正确。在这种情况下，只要退出“可以在不对委托人利益造成重大不利影响的情况下完成”，律师就可以退出。规则 1.16(b)(1)。本题中，对方律师可能从退出察觉文件存在问题，这种可能性不太可能对委托人利益造成重大影响。D 不正确。没有迹象表明律师退出会影响公司及时完成文件提交。即便可能产生这一问题，也不会对委托人利益造成严重到迫使律师继续代理的重大不利影响。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-026","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":26,"topic":"Rule 2.11","question":"A judge and an attorney were formerly law partners. During their partnership, they acquired several parcels of real property as cotenants. After the judge was elected to the trial court in the county, she remained a cotenant with the attorney, but left the management of the properties to the attorney. The judge’s term of office will expire soon and she is opposed for reelection by two members of the bar. The attorney, who has not discussed the matter with the judge, intends to make a substantial contribution to the judge’s campaign for reelection. The judge is one of 15 judges sitting as trial court judges in the county. The attorney frequently represents clients in cases tried in the trial court of the county. Is the attorney subject to discipline if the attorney contributes $10,000 to the judge’s reelection campaign committee?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney frequently represents clients in cases tried in the trial court of the county.","B":"Yes, because the judge and the attorney have not discussed the matter of a campaign contribution.","C":"No, because the contribution is made to a campaign committee organized to support the judge’s reelection.","D":"No, because the attorney and the judge have a long-standing personal and business relationship."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. There is no prohibition on the attorney making a contribution to the judge’s campaign committee. However, if the attorney does so, the judge has to be aware of potential conflicts that could arise under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11 involving potential bias and disqualification if the attorney appears in the judge’s court. A is incorrect. As long as the judge follows the rules for disqualification in Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11, the attorney may still donate to the judge’s campaign. B is incorrect. Whether or not the judge and attorney have discussed the matter is irrelevant, as the donation itself is what is at issue. D is incorrect. Even if the attorney and judge did not have a long-standing relationship, the attorney would not violate the Rules by giving a donation to the committee.","rule":"C is the correct answer. There is no prohibition on the attorney making a contribution to the judge’s campaign committee. However, if the attorney does so, the judge has to be aware of potential conflicts that could arise under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11 involving potential bias and disqualification if the attorney appears in the judge’s court. A is incorrect. As long as the judge follows the rules for disqualification in Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11, the attorney may still donate to the judge’s campaign. B is incorrect. Whether or not the judge and attorney have discussed the matter is irrelevant, as the donation itself is what is at issue. D is incorrect. Even if the attorney and judge did not have a long-standing relationship, the attorney would not violate the Rules by giving a donation to the committee.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline if the attorney contributes $10,000 to the judge’s reelection campaign committee?","rule":"C is the correct answer. There is no prohibition on the attorney making a contribution to the judge’s campaign committee. However, if the attorney does so, the judge has to be aware of potential conflicts that could arise under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11 involving potential bias and disqualification if the attorney appears in the judge’s court. A is incorrect. As long as the judge follows the rules for disqualification in Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11, the attorney may still donate to the judge’s campaign. B is incorrect. Whether or not the judge and attorney have discussed the matter is irrelevant, as the donation itself is what is at issue. D is incorrect. Even if the attorney and judge did not have a long-standing relationship, the attorney would not violate the Rules by giving a donation to the committee.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. There is no prohibition on the attorney making a contribution to the judge’s campaign committee. However, if the attorney does so, the judge has to be aware of potential conflicts that could arise under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11 involving potential bias and disqualification if the attorney appears in the judge’s court. A is incorrect. As long as the judge follows the rules for disqualification in Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11, the attorney may still donate to the judge’s campaign. B is incorrect. Whether or not the judge and attorney have discussed the matter is irrelevant, as the donation itself is what is at issue. D is incorrect. Even if the attorney and judge did not have a long-standing relationship, the attorney would not violate the Rules by giving a donation to the committee.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 2.11"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[183,184],"sourceAnswerPages":[283],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名法官和一名律师过去曾是法律业务合伙人。合伙期间，二人以共有方式取得了数宗不动产。法官当选为本县初审法院法官后，仍与律师共同拥有这些财产，但将财产管理交给律师。法官任期即将届满，有两名律师协会成员与她竞争连任。律师没有与法官讨论此事，但打算向法官连任竞选活动作出一笔巨额捐款。该法官是本县 15 名初审法官之一。律师经常代理委托人在本县初审法院审理案件。如果律师向法官连任竞选委员会捐款 10,000 美元，是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师经常代理委托人在本县初审法院审理案件。","B":"是，因为法官和律师没有讨论竞选捐款事宜。","C":"否，因为捐款交给为支持法官连任而设立的竞选委员会。","D":"否，因为律师与法官具有长期私人和商业关系。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。规则不禁止律师向法官的竞选委员会捐款。不过，如果律师捐款，法官必须注意《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.11 下可能产生的冲突；当该律师在法官所在法庭出庭时，可能涉及潜在偏见和回避。A 不正确。只要法官遵守规则 2.11 的回避规定，律师仍可向其竞选活动捐款。B 不正确。法官和律师是否讨论过此事无关，争议在于捐款本身。D 不正确。即使律师与法官没有长期关系，律师向委员会捐款也不违反规则。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-027","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":27,"topic":"Model Rule 3.3","question":"Attorney Alpha, a partner in the law firm of Alpha & Beta, was retained by the plaintiff in a personal injury action against the defendant. The jury rendered a verdict in favor of the defendant, and Alpha filed an appeal on the plaintiff’s behalf. Alpha reviewed the trial transcript and wrote the brief. The brief stated, “It is uncontroverted that the defendant failed to signal before turning left into the intersection.” In fact, a witness called by the defendant testified that the defendant did signal before turning. Alpha was aware of this testimony, having read it while reviewing the trial transcript. Three days before the appeal was scheduled to be argued in the state’s intermediate appellate court, Alpha suffered a heart attack. Attorney Beta, one of Alpha’s partners, agreed to argue the appeal. Beta knew nothing about the case and had no opportunity to confer with Alpha. In preparing for the argument, Beta read Alpha’s brief thoroughly and read as much of the trial transcript as was possible in the limited time available, but did not read the witness’s testimony. In oral argument, Beta stated to the court, “Your honors, as stated in our brief, it is uncontroverted that the defendant failed to signal before turning left into the intersection.” Beta assumed that Alpha’s statement in the brief to that effect was correct. Is Beta subject to discipline for making this statement during oral argument?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the statement was false.","B":"Yes, because Beta did not know whether or not the statement was true.","C":"No, because Beta did not know that the statement was false.","D":"No, because all Beta did was to truthfully recount the statement made by Alpha in the brief."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not knowingly make a false statement of fact or law to a tribunal.” Model Rule 3.3 (a)(1). Consequently, since the attorney did not know the statement was false, he is not subject to discipline. A is incorrect. The important point is that the attorney did not actually know the statement was false when he made it. B is incorrect. “An advocate is responsible for pleadings and other documents prepared for litigation, but is usually not required to have personal knowledge of the matters asserted therein, for litigation documents ordinarily present assertions by the client, or by someone on the client’s behalf.” Model Rule 3.3, Comment [3]. Consequently, the lawyer was not required to investigate every statement personally in this emergency situation where the litigation materials were prepared by other members of his firm. D is incorrect. This is a bad choice because if Beta had reason to know the statement was false, he would not be protected simply because he was recounting a statement made by Alpha.","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not knowingly make a false statement of fact or law to a tribunal.” Model Rule 3.3 (a)(1). Consequently, since the attorney did not know the statement was false, he is not subject to discipline. A is incorrect. The important point is that the attorney did not actually know the statement was false when he made it. B is incorrect. “An advocate is responsible for pleadings and other documents prepared for litigation, but is usually not required to have personal knowledge of the matters asserted therein, for litigation documents ordinarily present assertions by the client, or by someone on the client’s behalf.” Model Rule 3.3, Comment [3]. Consequently, the lawyer was not required to investigate every statement personally in this emergency situation where the litigation materials were prepared by other members of his firm. D is incorrect. This is a bad choice because if Beta had reason to know the statement was false, he would not be protected simply because he was recounting a statement made by Alpha.","irac":{"issue":"Is Beta subject to discipline for making this statement during oral argument?","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not knowingly make a false statement of fact or law to a tribunal.” Model Rule 3.3 (a)(1). Consequently, since the attorney did not know the statement was false, he is not subject to discipline. A is incorrect. The important point is that the attorney did not actually know the statement was false when he made it. B is incorrect. “An advocate is responsible for pleadings and other documents prepared for litigation, but is usually not required to have personal knowledge of the matters asserted therein, for litigation documents ordinarily present assertions by the client, or by someone on the client’s behalf.” Model Rule 3.3, Comment [3]. Consequently, the lawyer was not required to investigate every statement personally in this emergency situation where the litigation materials were prepared by other members of his firm. D is incorrect. This is a bad choice because if Beta had reason to know the statement was false, he would not be protected simply because he was recounting a statement made by Alpha.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not knowingly make a false statement of fact or law to a tribunal.” Model Rule 3.3 (a)(1). Consequently, since the attorney did not know the statement was false, he is not subject to discipline. A is incorrect. The important point is that the attorney did not actually know the statement was false when he made it. B is incorrect. “An advocate is responsible for pleadings and other documents prepared for litigation, but is usually not required to have personal knowledge of the matters asserted therein, for litigation documents ordinarily present assertions by the client, or by someone on the client’s behalf.” Model Rule 3.3, Comment [3]. Consequently, the lawyer was not required to investigate every statement personally in this emergency situation where the litigation materials were prepared by other members of his firm. D is incorrect. This is a bad choice because if Beta had reason to know the statement was false, he would not be protected simply because he was recounting a statement made by Alpha.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[184,185],"sourceAnswerPages":[283,284],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"律师甲是甲乙律师事务所的合伙人，受聘代理一宗人身伤害诉讼的原告起诉被告。陪审团裁定被告胜诉，甲代表原告提起上诉。甲审查审判记录并撰写上诉书状。书状称：“没有争议的是，被告左转进入路口前没有打转向灯。”事实上，被告传唤的一名证人作证说，被告转弯前确实打了转向灯。甲在审查审判记录时读过该证言，知道这一点。距离州中级上诉法院预定口头辩论还有三天时，甲心脏病发作。甲的合伙人乙同意参加上诉辩论。乙对案件毫不了解，也没有机会与甲协商。为准备辩论，乙仔细阅读甲的书状，并在有限时间内尽可能多地阅读审判记录，但没有读到该证人的证言。口头辩论时，乙向法院陈述：“各位法官阁下，正如我们的书状所述，没有争议的是，被告左转进入路口前没有打转向灯。”乙假定甲在书状中的陈述正确。乙是否因口头辩论中作出该陈述而应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为陈述虚假。","B":"是，因为乙不知道陈述是否真实。","C":"否，因为乙不知道陈述虚假。","D":"否，因为乙所做的只是如实复述甲在书状中作出的陈述。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。“律师不得明知而向审裁机构作出虚假的事实或法律陈述。”《职业行为示范规则》3.3(a)(1)。因此，乙既然不知道陈述虚假，就不应受纪律处分。A 不正确。关键是律师作出陈述时事实上不知道其虚假。B 不正确。“辩护人应对为诉讼准备的诉状和其他文件负责，但通常不要求其亲身知悉其中所主张的事项，因为诉讼文件通常提出委托人或代表委托人之人所作的主张。”规则 3.3 评注 [3]。因此，在这项诉讼材料由同一事务所其他成员准备的紧急情形中，乙无需亲自调查每一项陈述。D 不正确。这不是好选项，因为如果乙有理由知道陈述虚假，仅仅因为是在复述甲的陈述也不能保护他。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-028","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":28,"topic":"Rule 1.2","question":"A judge needed to obtain a loan to be secured by a second mortgage on his house. A bank offered him a loan at a very favorable interest rate. The vice-president at the bank told the judge: “Frankly, we normally don’t give such a large loan when the security is a second mortgage, and your interest rate will be 2 percent less than we charge our other customers. But we know that your salary is inadequate, and we are giving you special consideration.” The judge did not act in any case involving the bank, and was unlikely to do so. However, the same terms are not available to all judges in the state. Is it proper for the judge to accept the loan?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the judge does not act in any case involving the bank.","B":"Yes, because the bank is not likely to be involved in litigation in the court on which the judge sits.","C":"No, because the same terms are not available to all judges in the state.","D":"No, because the amount of the loan and interest rate were not available to persons who were not judges."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. “A judge shall act at all times in a manner that promotes public confidence in the independence, integrity, and impartiality of the judiciary, and shall avoid impropriety and the appearance of impropriety.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 1.2. Here, this large, unusual loan clearly creates an appearance of impropriety, and the judge should not accept it. A is incorrect. Although the judge does not act in any case involving the bank, this loan could clearly create the appearance of impropriety and hurt public confidence in the judiciary. B is incorrect. As stated above, the problem here is the appearance of impropriety. “A judge should expect to be the subject of public scrutiny that might be viewed as burdensome if applied to other citizens.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 1.2, Comment [2]. C is incorrect. In many ways, it would be worse if the same terms were available to all state judges, as it would create the appearance of widespread impropriety.","rule":"D is the correct answer. “A judge shall act at all times in a manner that promotes public confidence in the independence, integrity, and impartiality of the judiciary, and shall avoid impropriety and the appearance of impropriety.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 1.2. Here, this large, unusual loan clearly creates an appearance of impropriety, and the judge should not accept it. A is incorrect. Although the judge does not act in any case involving the bank, this loan could clearly create the appearance of impropriety and hurt public confidence in the judiciary. B is incorrect. As stated above, the problem here is the appearance of impropriety. “A judge should expect to be the subject of public scrutiny that might be viewed as burdensome if applied to other citizens.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 1.2, Comment [2]. C is incorrect. In many ways, it would be worse if the same terms were available to all state judges, as it would create the appearance of widespread impropriety.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the judge to accept the loan?","rule":"D is the correct answer. “A judge shall act at all times in a manner that promotes public confidence in the independence, integrity, and impartiality of the judiciary, and shall avoid impropriety and the appearance of impropriety.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 1.2. Here, this large, unusual loan clearly creates an appearance of impropriety, and the judge should not accept it. A is incorrect. Although the judge does not act in any case involving the bank, this loan could clearly create the appearance of impropriety and hurt public confidence in the judiciary. B is incorrect. As stated above, the problem here is the appearance of impropriety. “A judge should expect to be the subject of public scrutiny that might be viewed as burdensome if applied to other citizens.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 1.2, Comment [2]. C is incorrect. In many ways, it would be worse if the same terms were available to all state judges, as it would create the appearance of widespread impropriety.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. “A judge shall act at all times in a manner that promotes public confidence in the independence, integrity, and impartiality of the judiciary, and shall avoid impropriety and the appearance of impropriety.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 1.2. Here, this large, unusual loan clearly creates an appearance of impropriety, and the judge should not accept it. A is incorrect. Although the judge does not act in any case involving the bank, this loan could clearly create the appearance of impropriety and hurt public confidence in the judiciary. B is incorrect. As stated above, the problem here is the appearance of impropriety. “A judge should expect to be the subject of public scrutiny that might be viewed as burdensome if applied to other citizens.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 1.2, Comment [2]. C is incorrect. In many ways, it would be worse if the same terms were available to all state judges, as it would create the appearance of widespread impropriety.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 1.2"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[185],"sourceAnswerPages":[284],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名法官需要取得一笔以其房屋第二顺位抵押权担保的贷款。某银行提出以非常优惠的利率向其贷款。银行副总裁告诉法官：“坦率地说，以第二顺位抵押权作担保时，我们通常不会提供这么大的贷款；而且您的利率会比其他客户低两个百分点。但我们知道您的薪资不足，所以给予您特别照顾。”该法官没有办理任何涉及该银行的案件，未来也不太可能办理。不过，并非该州所有法官都可获得相同条件。法官接受贷款是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为法官没有办理任何涉及该银行的案件。","B":"适当，因为该银行不太可能卷入该法官所在法院的诉讼。","C":"不适当，因为并非该州所有法官都可获得相同条件。","D":"不适当，因为非司法人员无法获得这种贷款金额和利率。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。“法官在任何时候都应以提升公众对司法体系独立性、诚信及公正性之信心的方式行事，并应避免不当行为及不当观感。”《司法行为示范准则》规则 1.2。本题中，这笔金额大且异常优惠的贷款显然会造成不当观感，法官不应接受。A 不正确。法官虽然没有办理涉及银行的案件，该贷款仍明显会造成不当观感，并损害公众对司法体系的信心。B 不正确。如上所述，问题在于不当观感。“法官应当预期自己会受到公众审视，而这种审视如果适用于其他公民，可能会被认为负担沉重。”规则 1.2 评注 [2]。C 不正确。在许多方面，如果全州所有法官都可获得同等条件，情况反而更糟，因为这会造成普遍不当的观感。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-029","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":29,"topic":"Model Rule 5.4(d)(3) / Model Rule 5.4 / Model Rule 5.4(a)(3)","question":"A law firm has 300 lawyers in 10 states. It has placed the supervision of all routine administrative and financial matters in the hands of a nonlawyer. The nonlawyer is paid a regular monthly salary and a year-end bonus of 1 percent of the law firm’s net income from fees. Organizationally, the nonlawyer reports to an attorney who acts as the managing partner of the law firm. The attorney deals with all issues related to the law firm’s supervision of the practice of law. The nonlawyer does not have access to client files and does not control the professional judgment of lawyers in the firm. Is it proper for the attorney to participate in the law firm’s use of the nonlawyer’s services in this fashion?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the nonlawyer does not have access to client files.","B":"Yes, because the nonlawyer does not control the professional judgment of the lawyers in the firm.","C":"No, because the law firm is sharing legal fees with a nonlawyer.","D":"No, because the law firm is assisting a nonlawyer in the unauthorized practice of law."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not practice with or in the form of a professional corporation or association authorized to practice law for a profit, if: . . . a nonlawyer has the right to direct or control the professional judgment of a lawyer.” Model Rule 5.4(d)(3). Here, the nonlawyer only supervises routine administrative and financial matters and does not control the professional judgment of lawyers in the firm. Importantly, this Rule’s limitations “are to protect the lawyer’s professional independence of judgment.” Model Rule 5.4, Comment [1]. Consequently, this arrangement is proper under the Rules. A is incorrect. The main issue here would not be the nonlawyer’s access to client files. The main issue would be whether the nonlawyer had some ability to control the actions of the attorneys. C is incorrect. “A lawyer or law firm may include nonlawyer employees in a compensation or retirement plan, even though the plan is based in whole or in part on a profit-sharing arrangement.” Model Rule 5.4(a)(3). Consequently, as long as the nonlawyer’s position is not violating the Rules in some other way (namely, by controlling the judgment of lawyers), it is proper to share fees with the nonlawyer employee. D is incorrect. Here, since the nonlawyer is not giving any sort of legal advice, the nonlawyer is not engaged in the unauthorized practice of law.","rule":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not practice with or in the form of a professional corporation or association authorized to practice law for a profit, if: . . . a nonlawyer has the right to direct or control the professional judgment of a lawyer.” Model Rule 5.4(d)(3). Here, the nonlawyer only supervises routine administrative and financial matters and does not control the professional judgment of lawyers in the firm. Importantly, this Rule’s limitations “are to protect the lawyer’s professional independence of judgment.” Model Rule 5.4, Comment [1]. Consequently, this arrangement is proper under the Rules. A is incorrect. The main issue here would not be the nonlawyer’s access to client files. The main issue would be whether the nonlawyer had some ability to control the actions of the attorneys. C is incorrect. “A lawyer or law firm may include nonlawyer employees in a compensation or retirement plan, even though the plan is based in whole or in part on a profit-sharing arrangement.” Model Rule 5.4(a)(3). Consequently, as long as the nonlawyer’s position is not violating the Rules in some other way (namely, by controlling the judgment of lawyers), it is proper to share fees with the nonlawyer employee. D is incorrect. Here, since the nonlawyer is not giving any sort of legal advice, the nonlawyer is not engaged in the unauthorized practice of law.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the attorney to participate in the law firm’s use of the nonlawyer’s services in this fashion?","rule":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not practice with or in the form of a professional corporation or association authorized to practice law for a profit, if: . . . a nonlawyer has the right to direct or control the professional judgment of a lawyer.” Model Rule 5.4(d)(3). Here, the nonlawyer only supervises routine administrative and financial matters and does not control the professional judgment of lawyers in the firm. Importantly, this Rule’s limitations “are to protect the lawyer’s professional independence of judgment.” Model Rule 5.4, Comment [1]. Consequently, this arrangement is proper under the Rules. A is incorrect. The main issue here would not be the nonlawyer’s access to client files. The main issue would be whether the nonlawyer had some ability to control the actions of the attorneys. C is incorrect. “A lawyer or law firm may include nonlawyer employees in a compensation or retirement plan, even though the plan is based in whole or in part on a profit-sharing arrangement.” Model Rule 5.4(a)(3). Consequently, as long as the nonlawyer’s position is not violating the Rules in some other way (namely, by controlling the judgment of lawyers), it is proper to share fees with the nonlawyer employee. D is incorrect. Here, since the nonlawyer is not giving any sort of legal advice, the nonlawyer is not engaged in the unauthorized practice of law.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not practice with or in the form of a professional corporation or association authorized to practice law for a profit, if: . . . a nonlawyer has the right to direct or control the professional judgment of a lawyer.” Model Rule 5.4(d)(3). Here, the nonlawyer only supervises routine administrative and financial matters and does not control the professional judgment of lawyers in the firm. Importantly, this Rule’s limitations “are to protect the lawyer’s professional independence of judgment.” Model Rule 5.4, Comment [1]. Consequently, this arrangement is proper under the Rules. A is incorrect. The main issue here would not be the nonlawyer’s access to client files. The main issue would be whether the nonlawyer had some ability to control the actions of the attorneys. C is incorrect. “A lawyer or law firm may include nonlawyer employees in a compensation or retirement plan, even though the plan is based in whole or in part on a profit-sharing arrangement.” Model Rule 5.4(a)(3). Consequently, as long as the nonlawyer’s position is not violating the Rules in some other way (namely, by controlling the judgment of lawyers), it is proper to share fees with the nonlawyer employee. D is incorrect. Here, since the nonlawyer is not giving any sort of legal advice, the nonlawyer is not engaged in the unauthorized practice of law.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.4(d)(3)","Model Rule 5.4","Model Rule 5.4(a)(3)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[185,186],"sourceAnswerPages":[284,285],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家律师事务所在 10 个州拥有 300 名律师。事务所将所有日常行政和财务事务交由一名非律师监督。该非律师获得固定月薪和相当于事务所法律服务费净收入 1% 的年终奖金。在组织关系上，该非律师向一名担任事务所管理合伙人的律师汇报。该律师负责处理与事务所监督法律业务有关的全部问题。非律师无权查阅委托人案卷，也不控制事务所律师的专业判断。该律师参与事务所以这种方式使用非律师服务是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为非律师无权查阅委托人案卷。","B":"适当，因为非律师不控制事务所律师的专业判断。","C":"不适当，因为律师事务所正在与非律师分享法律服务费。","D":"不适当，因为律师事务所正在协助非律师擅自从事法律执业。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。“在下列情形下，律师不得在获准以营利为目的从事法律业务的专业公司或协会中执业，或以该形式执业：……非律师有权指导或控制律师的专业判断。”《职业行为示范规则》5.4(d)(3)。本题中，非律师只监督日常行政和财务事务，并不控制事务所律师的专业判断。尤其是，该规则的限制“旨在保护律师专业判断的独立性”。规则 5.4 评注 [1]。因此，该安排符合规则。A 不正确。本题主要问题并非非律师是否可以查阅委托人案卷，而是非律师是否能够控制律师行为。C 不正确。“律师或律师事务所可以让非律师雇员参加报酬或退休计划，即使该计划全部或部分以利润分享安排为依据。”规则 5.4(a)(3)。因此，只要非律师职位没有以其他方式违反规则（尤其是控制律师判断），就可以与该非律师雇员分享收益。D 不正确。本题中，非律师没有提供任何法律意见，因而没有擅自从事法律执业活动。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-030","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":30,"topic":"Model Rule 1.16(b) / Model Rule 1.16(b)(6)","question":"An attorney experienced several instances when clients failed to pay their fees in a timely manner, but it was too late in the representation to withdraw without prejudicing the clients. To avoid a recurrence of this situation, the attorney has drafted a stipulation of consent to withdraw if fees are not paid according to the fee agreement. She proposes to have all clients sign the stipulation at the outset of the representation. Is it proper for the attorney to use the stipulation to withdraw from representation whenever a client fails to pay fees?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because a lawyer may withdraw when the financial burden of continuing the representation would be substantially greater than the parties anticipated at the time of the fee agreement.","B":"Yes, because the clients consented to the withdrawal in the stipulation.","C":"No, because a client’s failure to pay fees when due may be insufficient in itself to justify withdrawal.","D":"No, because clients are not provided an opportunity to seek independent legal advice before signing the stipulation."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. According to the Rules, an attorney may withdraw from representation if the withdrawal can be accomplished without material adverse effect, the client persists in criminal or fraudulent action, the client uses the lawyer’s services to perpetrate crime or fraud, the client insists on repugnant action, the client fails to fulfill an obligation and has been given reasonable warning, representation will result in an unreasonable financial burden, or other good cause exists. Model Rule 1.16(b). Consequently, in some cases, the simple fact that a client has failed to pay fees may be insufficient to justify a withdrawal. For example, under the proposed fee agreement, the attorney could withdraw if a client is a day late in paying a fee, which clearly does not meet any of the possible reasons for withdrawal proposed by the Rules. A is incorrect. The Rules allow withdrawal for “unreasonable” financial burden, not simply a financial burden that was not considered at the onset of representation. Model Rule 1.16(b)(6). B is incorrect. Although the clients may consent, this does not make the agreement proper under the Rules. Importantly, simply because an attorney gets a client to agree to something does not make it proper. D is incorrect. The possibility of independent legal advice would still not make the primary agreement proper.","rule":"C is the correct answer. According to the Rules, an attorney may withdraw from representation if the withdrawal can be accomplished without material adverse effect, the client persists in criminal or fraudulent action, the client uses the lawyer’s services to perpetrate crime or fraud, the client insists on repugnant action, the client fails to fulfill an obligation and has been given reasonable warning, representation will result in an unreasonable financial burden, or other good cause exists. Model Rule 1.16(b). Consequently, in some cases, the simple fact that a client has failed to pay fees may be insufficient to justify a withdrawal. For example, under the proposed fee agreement, the attorney could withdraw if a client is a day late in paying a fee, which clearly does not meet any of the possible reasons for withdrawal proposed by the Rules. A is incorrect. The Rules allow withdrawal for “unreasonable” financial burden, not simply a financial burden that was not considered at the onset of representation. Model Rule 1.16(b)(6). B is incorrect. Although the clients may consent, this does not make the agreement proper under the Rules. Importantly, simply because an attorney gets a client to agree to something does not make it proper. D is incorrect. The possibility of independent legal advice would still not make the primary agreement proper.","irac":{"issue":"Is it proper for the attorney to use the stipulation to withdraw from representation whenever a client fails to pay fees?","rule":"C is the correct answer. According to the Rules, an attorney may withdraw from representation if the withdrawal can be accomplished without material adverse effect, the client persists in criminal or fraudulent action, the client uses the lawyer’s services to perpetrate crime or fraud, the client insists on repugnant action, the client fails to fulfill an obligation and has been given reasonable warning, representation will result in an unreasonable financial burden, or other good cause exists. Model Rule 1.16(b). Consequently, in some cases, the simple fact that a client has failed to pay fees may be insufficient to justify a withdrawal. For example, under the proposed fee agreement, the attorney could withdraw if a client is a day late in paying a fee, which clearly does not meet any of the possible reasons for withdrawal proposed by the Rules. A is incorrect. The Rules allow withdrawal for “unreasonable” financial burden, not simply a financial burden that was not considered at the onset of representation. Model Rule 1.16(b)(6). B is incorrect. Although the clients may consent, this does not make the agreement proper under the Rules. Importantly, simply because an attorney gets a client to agree to something does not make it proper. D is incorrect. The possibility of independent legal advice would still not make the primary agreement proper.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. According to the Rules, an attorney may withdraw from representation if the withdrawal can be accomplished without material adverse effect, the client persists in criminal or fraudulent action, the client uses the lawyer’s services to perpetrate crime or fraud, the client insists on repugnant action, the client fails to fulfill an obligation and has been given reasonable warning, representation will result in an unreasonable financial burden, or other good cause exists. Model Rule 1.16(b). Consequently, in some cases, the simple fact that a client has failed to pay fees may be insufficient to justify a withdrawal. For example, under the proposed fee agreement, the attorney could withdraw if a client is a day late in paying a fee, which clearly does not meet any of the possible reasons for withdrawal proposed by the Rules. A is incorrect. The Rules allow withdrawal for “unreasonable” financial burden, not simply a financial burden that was not considered at the onset of representation. Model Rule 1.16(b)(6). B is incorrect. Although the clients may consent, this does not make the agreement proper under the Rules. Importantly, simply because an attorney gets a client to agree to something does not make it proper. D is incorrect. The possibility of independent legal advice would still not make the primary agreement proper.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.16(b)","Model Rule 1.16(b)(6)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[186],"sourceAnswerPages":[285],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师曾多次遇到委托人未及时支付费用的情况，但当时代理已进入后期，退出会损害委托人。为避免再次发生这种情况，律师起草一份同意书，约定如果未依收费协议付款，委托人即同意律师退出。她打算让所有委托人在代理开始时签署该同意书。只要委托人未支付费用，律师使用该同意书退出代理是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为继续代理产生的经济负担大幅超过双方订立收费协议时的预期时，律师可以退出。","B":"适当，因为委托人在同意书中同意了律师退出。","C":"不适当，因为委托人未按期付款这一事实本身可能不足以正当化退出。","D":"不适当，因为委托人在签署同意书前没有机会寻求独立法律意见。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。根据规则，在不造成重大不利影响、委托人持续实施犯罪或欺诈、利用律师服务实施犯罪或欺诈、坚持采取律师反感的行动、未履行义务且已收到合理警告、代理会造成不合理经济负担，或者存在其他正当理由时，律师可以退出。《职业行为示范规则》1.16(b)。因此，在某些案件中，仅仅未支付费用可能不足以正当化退出。例如，根据拟议收费协议，委托人只要迟一天付款，律师就可以退出，而这显然不符合规则提出的任何退出理由。A 不正确。规则允许在经济负担“不合理”时退出，而不是仅仅因为该负担在代理开始时未被考虑。规则 1.16(b)(6)。B 不正确。委托人即使同意，也不会使协议在规则下适当。尤其要记住，律师让委托人同意某事，并不会使其当然适当。D 不正确。即使可以取得独立法律意见，仍不能使首要协议变得适当。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-031","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":31,"topic":"Model Rule 1.8(f)(1) / Model Rule 1.8(f)","question":"An attorney was retained by a client to represent him in a paternity suit. The client’s aunt believed the suit was unfounded and motivated by malice. The client’s aunt sent the attorney a check for $1,000 and asked the attorney to apply it to the payment of the client’s fee. The client’s aunt told the attorney not to tell the client of the payment because the client “is too proud to accept gifts, but I know he really needs the money.” Is it proper for the attorney to accept the aunt’s check?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the client’s aunt is not attempting to influence the attorney’s conduct of the case.","B":"Yes, because the attorney’s charges to the client are reduced accordingly.","C":"No, because the client’s aunt is attempting to finance litigation to which she is not a party.","D":"No, because the attorney is not informing the client and obtaining the client’s consent to retain the payment."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not accept compensation for representing a client from one other than the client unless: (1) the client gives informed consent.” Model Rule 1.8(f)(1). Consequently, the attorney cannot accept the aunt’s check without informing the client. A is incorrect. Although it would clearly be improper for the aunt to attempt to influence the attorney’s conduct, the primary issue is that the attorney cannot accept the check without getting the client’s consent. B is incorrect. Even if the charges are reduced, the primary issue is that the aunt is attempting to pay without the client’s knowledge. C is incorrect. Under the Rules, if a client gives informed consent, there is no interference with the attorney’s professional judgment or with the client-lawyer relationship, and information relating to the representation is protected, a person other than the client can pay for the representation. Model Rule 1.8(f).","rule":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not accept compensation for representing a client from one other than the client unless: (1) the client gives informed consent.” Model Rule 1.8(f)(1). Consequently, the attorney cannot accept the aunt’s check without informing the client. A is incorrect. Although it would clearly be improper for the aunt to attempt to influence the attorney’s conduct, the primary issue is that the attorney cannot accept the check without getting the client’s consent. B is incorrect. Even if the charges are reduced, the primary issue is that the aunt is attempting to pay without the client’s knowledge. C is incorrect. Under the Rules, if a client gives informed consent, there is no interference with the attorney’s professional judgment or with the client-lawyer relationship, and information relating to the representation is protected, a person other than the client can pay for the representation. Model Rule 1.8(f).","irac":{"issue":"” Is it proper for the attorney to accept the aunt’s check?","rule":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not accept compensation for representing a client from one other than the client unless: (1) the client gives informed consent.” Model Rule 1.8(f)(1). Consequently, the attorney cannot accept the aunt’s check without informing the client. A is incorrect. Although it would clearly be improper for the aunt to attempt to influence the attorney’s conduct, the primary issue is that the attorney cannot accept the check without getting the client’s consent. B is incorrect. Even if the charges are reduced, the primary issue is that the aunt is attempting to pay without the client’s knowledge. C is incorrect. Under the Rules, if a client gives informed consent, there is no interference with the attorney’s professional judgment or with the client-lawyer relationship, and information relating to the representation is protected, a person other than the client can pay for the representation. Model Rule 1.8(f).","analysis":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not accept compensation for representing a client from one other than the client unless: (1) the client gives informed consent.” Model Rule 1.8(f)(1). Consequently, the attorney cannot accept the aunt’s check without informing the client. A is incorrect. Although it would clearly be improper for the aunt to attempt to influence the attorney’s conduct, the primary issue is that the attorney cannot accept the check without getting the client’s consent. B is incorrect. Even if the charges are reduced, the primary issue is that the aunt is attempting to pay without the client’s knowledge. C is incorrect. Under the Rules, if a client gives informed consent, there is no interference with the attorney’s professional judgment or with the client-lawyer relationship, and information relating to the representation is protected, a person other than the client can pay for the representation. Model Rule 1.8(f).","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.8(f)(1)","Model Rule 1.8(f)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[186,187],"sourceAnswerPages":[285,286],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师受聘代理委托人参加亲子关系诉讼。委托人的姑妈认为诉讼没有依据，且出于恶意。姑妈向律师寄送一张 1,000 美元支票，请求用于支付委托人的律师费。姑妈告诉律师不要将付款告知委托人，因为委托人“自尊心太强，不愿接受赠与，但我知道他真的需要这笔钱”。律师接受姑妈的支票是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为姑妈没有试图影响律师办理案件。","B":"适当，因为委托人应付费用会相应减少。","C":"不适当，因为姑妈试图资助一宗自己并非当事人的诉讼。","D":"不适当，因为律师没有告知委托人并取得其对保留该付款的同意。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。“律师不得从委托人以外的人处接受代理报酬，除非：(1) 委托人作出知情同意。”《职业行为示范规则》1.8(f)(1)。因此，律师未经委托人知情，不能接受姑妈的支票。A 不正确。姑妈试图影响律师办理案件显然会不适当，但首要问题是，律师未经委托人同意不能接受支票。B 不正确。即使委托人费用相应减少，首要问题仍是姑妈试图在委托人不知情的情况下付款。C 不正确。根据规则，只要委托人作出知情同意，不干扰律师的专业判断或律师—委托人关系，并保护与代理有关的信息，委托人以外的人可以支付代理费用。规则 1.8(f)。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-032","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":32,"topic":"Model Rule 5.3(b) / Model Rule 5.3","question":"An attorney has a highly efficient staff of paraprofessional legal assistants, all of whom are graduates of recognized legal assistant educational programs. Recently, the statute of limitations ran against a claim of a client of the attorney’s when a legal assistant negligently misplaced a client’s file and suit was not filed within the time permitted by law. Which of the following correctly states the attorney’s professional responsibility?","choices":{"A":"The attorney is subject to civil liability and is also subject to discipline on the theory of respondeat superior.","B":"The attorney is subject to civil liability or is subject to discipline at the client’s election.","C":"The attorney is subject to civil liability but is NOT subject to discipline unless the attorney failed to supervise the legal assistant adequately.","D":"The attorney is NOT subject to civil liability and is NOT subject to discipline if the attorney personally was not negligent."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer having direct supervisory authority over the nonlawyer shall make reasonable efforts to ensure that the person’s conduct is compatible with the professional obligations of the lawyer.” Model Rule 5.3(b). Importantly, “[a] lawyer must give such assistants appropriate instruction and supervision concerning the ethical aspects of their employment, particularly regarding the obligation not to disclose information relating to representation of the client, and should be responsible for their work product.” Model Rule 5.3, Comment [2]. Consequently, unless there is some allegation that the lawyer’s supervision was inadequate, he should not be subject to discipline. Obviously, the mistake made by his office would subject him to civil liability. A is incorrect. As stated above, if the attorney failed to supervise his employees adequately, he would be subject to discipline for his own actions and there would be no need to apply the concept of respondeat superior. B is incorrect. The decision to sue for civil liability is the client’s, but the client has no right to make a decision regarding discipline under the Rules. D is incorrect. This answer choice should have been discarded immediately, considering it was clear the attorney would be subject to civil liability.","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer having direct supervisory authority over the nonlawyer shall make reasonable efforts to ensure that the person’s conduct is compatible with the professional obligations of the lawyer.” Model Rule 5.3(b). Importantly, “[a] lawyer must give such assistants appropriate instruction and supervision concerning the ethical aspects of their employment, particularly regarding the obligation not to disclose information relating to representation of the client, and should be responsible for their work product.” Model Rule 5.3, Comment [2]. Consequently, unless there is some allegation that the lawyer’s supervision was inadequate, he should not be subject to discipline. Obviously, the mistake made by his office would subject him to civil liability. A is incorrect. As stated above, if the attorney failed to supervise his employees adequately, he would be subject to discipline for his own actions and there would be no need to apply the concept of respondeat superior. B is incorrect. The decision to sue for civil liability is the client’s, but the client has no right to make a decision regarding discipline under the Rules. D is incorrect. This answer choice should have been discarded immediately, considering it was clear the attorney would be subject to civil liability.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following correctly states the attorney’s professional responsibility?","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer having direct supervisory authority over the nonlawyer shall make reasonable efforts to ensure that the person’s conduct is compatible with the professional obligations of the lawyer.” Model Rule 5.3(b). Importantly, “[a] lawyer must give such assistants appropriate instruction and supervision concerning the ethical aspects of their employment, particularly regarding the obligation not to disclose information relating to representation of the client, and should be responsible for their work product.” Model Rule 5.3, Comment [2]. Consequently, unless there is some allegation that the lawyer’s supervision was inadequate, he should not be subject to discipline. Obviously, the mistake made by his office would subject him to civil liability. A is incorrect. As stated above, if the attorney failed to supervise his employees adequately, he would be subject to discipline for his own actions and there would be no need to apply the concept of respondeat superior. B is incorrect. The decision to sue for civil liability is the client’s, but the client has no right to make a decision regarding discipline under the Rules. D is incorrect. This answer choice should have been discarded immediately, considering it was clear the attorney would be subject to civil liability.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer having direct supervisory authority over the nonlawyer shall make reasonable efforts to ensure that the person’s conduct is compatible with the professional obligations of the lawyer.” Model Rule 5.3(b). Importantly, “[a] lawyer must give such assistants appropriate instruction and supervision concerning the ethical aspects of their employment, particularly regarding the obligation not to disclose information relating to representation of the client, and should be responsible for their work product.” Model Rule 5.3, Comment [2]. Consequently, unless there is some allegation that the lawyer’s supervision was inadequate, he should not be subject to discipline. Obviously, the mistake made by his office would subject him to civil liability. A is incorrect. As stated above, if the attorney failed to supervise his employees adequately, he would be subject to discipline for his own actions and there would be no need to apply the concept of respondeat superior. B is incorrect. The decision to sue for civil liability is the client’s, but the client has no right to make a decision regarding discipline under the Rules. D is incorrect. This answer choice should have been discarded immediately, considering it was clear the attorney would be subject to civil liability.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.3(b)","Model Rule 5.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[187],"sourceAnswerPages":[286],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师拥有一支高效的非律师专业法律助理团队，所有成员都毕业于获得认可的法律助理教育项目。最近，一名法律助理因疏忽放错一名委托人的案卷，未能在法律允许的期限内提起诉讼，导致该委托人的一项主张超过时效。以下哪项正确说明律师的专业责任？","choicesZh":{"A":"根据上级对下级行为负责的理论，律师承担民事责任，也应受纪律处分。","B":"由委托人选择，律师承担民事责任或者受到纪律处分。","C":"律师承担民事责任，但除非未充分监督法律助理，否则不应受纪律处分。","D":"如果律师本人没有疏忽，则既不承担民事责任，也不应受纪律处分。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。“对非律师具有直接监督权的律师，应作出合理努力，确保该人的行为符合律师的专业义务。”《职业行为示范规则》5.3(b)。尤其是，“律师必须就非律师助理工作中的职业伦理方面给予适当指导和监督，特别是不得披露与委托人代理有关的信息之义务，并应对其工作成果负责。”规则 5.3 评注 [2]。因此，除非存在律师监督不充分的主张，否则律师不应受纪律处分。显然，其办公室的错误会使律师承担民事责任。A 不正确。如上所述，如果律师未充分监督雇员，会因自己的行为受到纪律处分，无需适用上级对下级行为负责的理论。B 不正确。是否提起民事诉讼由委托人决定，但委托人无权就规则下的纪律处分作出决定。D 不正确。题目已清楚表明律师会承担民事责任，该选项应立即排除。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-033","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":33,"topic":"Rule 4.1(a) / Model Rule 4.3","question":"A seller was engaged in negotiations to sell his interest in a large tract of land to a buyer who was unrepresented in the transaction. Before the seller went out of town for a few days, he told the buyer to call his attorney if the buyer had any questions about the property. The buyer called the seller’s attorney, asked certain questions about the size of the tract, and expressed hesitations concerning the high asking price for the tract. The attorney responded that, based on his experience handling real estate transactions in the neighborhood, the buyer would be getting a lot of property for the price. At the time the attorney spoke to the buyer, the attorney knew that there was a defect in the title and that the buyer’s attempt to purchase the seller’s interest in the tract would not result in the buyer’s acquisition of any interest in the property. Relying on the attorney’s assurance, the buyer agreed to make the purchase. Shortly after the sale closed, the buyer discovered that his acquisition was worthless. Is the attorney subject to civil liability to the buyer?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney knowingly made false representations of fact to the buyer.","B":"Yes, because the attorney implied that his opinion regarding the value of the property was a disinterested opinion.","C":"No, because the attorney’s statement that the buyer would be getting a lot of property for the money was a statement of opinion regarding the value of the property.","D":"No, because the buyer was not a client of the attorney."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. “In the course of representing a client a lawyer shall not knowingly: (a) make a false statement of material fact or law to a third person.” Rule 4.1(a). Here, the attorney knows of the title defect and is basically committing fraud by encouraging the buyer to purchase it. B is incorrect. “In dealing on behalf of a client with a person who is not represented by counsel, a lawyer shall not state or imply that the lawyer is disinterested.” Model Rule 4.3. This Rule is meant to deal with situations where a person may not know that an attorney is in fact interested in a matter. Here, since the seller told the buyer to contact the seller’s attorney, it’s clear that the buyer should understand the attorney is interested and working for the seller. Consequently, this answer choice should have been discarded immediately. C is incorrect. The attorney’s statement was not an opinion, considering the title defect made this statement an outright lie. D is incorrect. In dealing with persons other than the client, the Rules make it very clear that a lawyer is not allowed to make false statements. This answer implies that a lawyer owes a duty to no one but the client, a clearly false statement that should be discarded immediately.","rule":"A is the correct answer. “In the course of representing a client a lawyer shall not knowingly: (a) make a false statement of material fact or law to a third person.” Rule 4.1(a). Here, the attorney knows of the title defect and is basically committing fraud by encouraging the buyer to purchase it. B is incorrect. “In dealing on behalf of a client with a person who is not represented by counsel, a lawyer shall not state or imply that the lawyer is disinterested.” Model Rule 4.3. This Rule is meant to deal with situations where a person may not know that an attorney is in fact interested in a matter. Here, since the seller told the buyer to contact the seller’s attorney, it’s clear that the buyer should understand the attorney is interested and working for the seller. Consequently, this answer choice should have been discarded immediately. C is incorrect. The attorney’s statement was not an opinion, considering the title defect made this statement an outright lie. D is incorrect. In dealing with persons other than the client, the Rules make it very clear that a lawyer is not allowed to make false statements. This answer implies that a lawyer owes a duty to no one but the client, a clearly false statement that should be discarded immediately.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to civil liability to the buyer?","rule":"A is the correct answer. “In the course of representing a client a lawyer shall not knowingly: (a) make a false statement of material fact or law to a third person.” Rule 4.1(a). Here, the attorney knows of the title defect and is basically committing fraud by encouraging the buyer to purchase it. B is incorrect. “In dealing on behalf of a client with a person who is not represented by counsel, a lawyer shall not state or imply that the lawyer is disinterested.” Model Rule 4.3. This Rule is meant to deal with situations where a person may not know that an attorney is in fact interested in a matter. Here, since the seller told the buyer to contact the seller’s attorney, it’s clear that the buyer should understand the attorney is interested and working for the seller. Consequently, this answer choice should have been discarded immediately. C is incorrect. The attorney’s statement was not an opinion, considering the title defect made this statement an outright lie. D is incorrect. In dealing with persons other than the client, the Rules make it very clear that a lawyer is not allowed to make false statements. This answer implies that a lawyer owes a duty to no one but the client, a clearly false statement that should be discarded immediately.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. “In the course of representing a client a lawyer shall not knowingly: (a) make a false statement of material fact or law to a third person.” Rule 4.1(a). Here, the attorney knows of the title defect and is basically committing fraud by encouraging the buyer to purchase it. B is incorrect. “In dealing on behalf of a client with a person who is not represented by counsel, a lawyer shall not state or imply that the lawyer is disinterested.” Model Rule 4.3. This Rule is meant to deal with situations where a person may not know that an attorney is in fact interested in a matter. Here, since the seller told the buyer to contact the seller’s attorney, it’s clear that the buyer should understand the attorney is interested and working for the seller. Consequently, this answer choice should have been discarded immediately. C is incorrect. The attorney’s statement was not an opinion, considering the title defect made this statement an outright lie. D is incorrect. In dealing with persons other than the client, the Rules make it very clear that a lawyer is not allowed to make false statements. This answer implies that a lawyer owes a duty to no one but the client, a clearly false statement that should be discarded immediately.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 4.1(a)","Model Rule 4.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[187,188],"sourceAnswerPages":[286,287],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名卖方正与一名在交易中没有律师代理的买方谈判，出售自己在一大片土地中的权益。卖方外出数日前告诉买方，如对房产有任何疑问，可以致电卖方的律师。买方致电该律师，询问地块面积的若干问题，并对高昂要价表示犹豫。律师回答，根据自己办理附近房地产交易的经验，以该价格买方会得到很多土地。律师与买方交谈时，明知产权存在缺陷，买方试图购买卖方的土地权益不会使买方取得任何财产权益。买方依赖律师保证，同意购买。交易完成后不久，买方发现所取得的权益一文不值。该律师是否对买方承担民事责任？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师明知而向买方作出虚假的事实陈述。","B":"是，因为律师暗示其有关房产价值的意见是不偏不倚的。","C":"否，因为律师称买方会以该价款取得很多土地，只是有关房产价值的意见。","D":"否，因为买方不是律师的委托人。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。“律师在代理委托人过程中不得明知而：(a) 向第三人作出虚假的重大事实或法律陈述。”规则 4.1(a)。本题中，律师知道产权缺陷，却仍鼓励买方购买，实质上是在实施欺诈。B 不正确。“律师代表委托人与没有律师代理的人交涉时，不得声称或暗示律师不偏不倚。”《职业行为示范规则》4.3。该规则旨在处理某人可能不知道律师事实上对事项有利害关系的情形。本题中，卖方已经让买方联系卖方的律师，买方显然应理解律师有利害关系并为卖方工作。因此，该选项应立即排除。C 不正确。鉴于产权缺陷使该陈述成为彻底的谎言，律师所说并非仅仅一种意见。D 不正确。规则明确规定，律师与委托人以外的人交涉时也不得作出虚假陈述。该选项暗示律师只对委托人负有义务，显然错误，应立即排除。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-034","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":34,"topic":"Model Rule 3.8(d)","question":"A prosecutor was assigned to try a criminal case against a defendant who was charged with robbery of a convenience store. The defendant denied any involvement, contending he was home watching television with his mother on the night in question. At the trial, a customer at the convenience store testified that he had identified the defendant in a police line-up and provided other testimony connecting the defendant to the crime. In addition, the prosecutor entered into evidence a poor-quality videotape of the robbery as recorded by the store surveillance camera. The jury could not make its own identification of the defendant from the videotape. The jury convicted the defendant of the crime charged. Unknown to the defendant’s court-appointed lawyer, the customer had first identified another person in the police line-up and selected the defendant only after encouragement by the detective. This information would have been unlikely to lead to the defendant’s acquittal, however. The prosecutor was aware of these facts but did not notify the defendant’s counsel who made no pretrial discovery request to obtain this information. Is the prosecutor subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the jury could not make its own identification of the defendant from the videotape.","B":"Yes, because this information tended to negate the defendant’s guilt.","C":"No, because the defendant’s counsel made no pretrial discovery request to obtain this information.","D":"No, because it is unlikely that the jury would have acquitted the defendant had it known that the customer first identified someone else."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. “The prosecutor in a criminal case shall: . . . (d) make timely disclosure to the defense of all evidence or information known to the prosecutor that tends to negate the guilt of the accused or mitigates the offense.” Model Rule 3.8(d). Here, since the prosecutor knew of the prior identification and the detective’s encouragement, and since this information tended to negate the guilt of the accused, he was required to disclose these facts to the defendant’s counsel. A is incorrect. The issue here is the fact that the prosecutor has special duties under the Rules not to withhold information from the defense. Whether or not the videotape was clear enough to make an identification is irrelevant. C is incorrect. Even though the defendant’s counsel has not made a discovery request, this does not relieve the prosecutor from his duty to inform the defense counsel of the earlier identification. D is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice that should be avoided.","rule":"B is the correct answer. “The prosecutor in a criminal case shall: . . . (d) make timely disclosure to the defense of all evidence or information known to the prosecutor that tends to negate the guilt of the accused or mitigates the offense.” Model Rule 3.8(d). Here, since the prosecutor knew of the prior identification and the detective’s encouragement, and since this information tended to negate the guilt of the accused, he was required to disclose these facts to the defendant’s counsel. A is incorrect. The issue here is the fact that the prosecutor has special duties under the Rules not to withhold information from the defense. Whether or not the videotape was clear enough to make an identification is irrelevant. C is incorrect. Even though the defendant’s counsel has not made a discovery request, this does not relieve the prosecutor from his duty to inform the defense counsel of the earlier identification. D is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice that should be avoided.","irac":{"issue":"Is the prosecutor subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. “The prosecutor in a criminal case shall: . . . (d) make timely disclosure to the defense of all evidence or information known to the prosecutor that tends to negate the guilt of the accused or mitigates the offense.” Model Rule 3.8(d). Here, since the prosecutor knew of the prior identification and the detective’s encouragement, and since this information tended to negate the guilt of the accused, he was required to disclose these facts to the defendant’s counsel. A is incorrect. The issue here is the fact that the prosecutor has special duties under the Rules not to withhold information from the defense. Whether or not the videotape was clear enough to make an identification is irrelevant. C is incorrect. Even though the defendant’s counsel has not made a discovery request, this does not relieve the prosecutor from his duty to inform the defense counsel of the earlier identification. D is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice that should be avoided.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. “The prosecutor in a criminal case shall: . . . (d) make timely disclosure to the defense of all evidence or information known to the prosecutor that tends to negate the guilt of the accused or mitigates the offense.” Model Rule 3.8(d). Here, since the prosecutor knew of the prior identification and the detective’s encouragement, and since this information tended to negate the guilt of the accused, he was required to disclose these facts to the defendant’s counsel. A is incorrect. The issue here is the fact that the prosecutor has special duties under the Rules not to withhold information from the defense. Whether or not the videotape was clear enough to make an identification is irrelevant. C is incorrect. Even though the defendant’s counsel has not made a discovery request, this does not relieve the prosecutor from his duty to inform the defense counsel of the earlier identification. D is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice that should be avoided.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.8(d)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[188,189],"sourceAnswerPages":[287],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名检察官被指派审理针对一名被控抢劫便利店之被告的刑事案件。被告否认参与，主张事发当晚自己在家与母亲看电视。审判中，一名便利店顾客作证说，他在警方列队辨认中指认了被告，并提供了把被告与犯罪联系起来的其他证言。此外，检察官把商店监控摄像机拍摄的低质量抢劫录像作为证据提交。陪审团无法从录像自行指认被告。陪审团裁定被告实施所控犯罪。被告的法院指定律师不知道的是，该顾客在警方列队辨认中最初指认了另一人，只是在警探鼓励后才选择被告。不过，该信息不太可能导致被告无罪。检察官知道这些事实，却没有通知被告律师，而被告律师也没有在审前提出证据开示请求以取得信息。该检察官是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为陪审团无法从录像自行指认被告。","B":"是，因为该信息倾向于否定被告有罪。","C":"否，因为被告律师没有在审前提出证据开示请求以取得信息。","D":"否，因为即使知道顾客最初指认了他人，陪审团也不太可能判被告无罪。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。“刑事案件中的检察官应：……(d) 及时向辩方披露检察官所知悉的、倾向于否定被告有罪或减轻犯罪的全部证据或信息。”《职业行为示范规则》3.8(d)。本题中，检察官知道先前指认及警探鼓励的事实，而这些信息倾向于否定被告有罪，因此必须向被告律师披露。A 不正确。本题问题是检察官在规则下负有不得向辩方隐瞒信息的特殊义务。录像是否清晰到可以辨认与此无关。C 不正确。即使被告律师没有提出证据开示请求，也不能免除检察官告知辩方先前指认的义务。D 不正确。这是典型的“没有损害，就没有违规”型选项，应予避免。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-035","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":35,"topic":"Model Rule 1.15(a) / Model Rule 1.15(c) / Model Rule 1.15(b)","question":"An attorney and a client entered into a written retainer and hourly fee agreement that required the client to pay $5,000 in advance of any services rendered by the attorney and which required the attorney to return any portion of the $5,000 that was not earned. The agreement further provided that the attorney would render monthly statements and withdraw her fees as billed. The agreement was silent as to whether the $5,000 advance was to be deposited in the attorney’s Clients’ Trust Account or in a general account. The attorney deposited the entire fund in her Clients’ Trust Account, which also contained the funds of other persons that had been entrusted to the attorney. Thereafter, the attorney rendered monthly progress reports and statements for services to the client after services were rendered, showing the balance of the client’s fee advance. However, the attorney did not withdraw any of the $5,000 advance until one year later when the matter was concluded to the client’s complete satisfaction. At that time, the attorney had billed the client reasonable legal fees of $4,500. The attorney wrote two checks on her Clients’ Trust Account: one to herself for $4,500, which she deposited in her general office account, and one for $500 to the client. Was the attorney’s conduct proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney deposited the funds in her Clients’ Trust Account.","B":"Yes, because the attorney rendered periodic and accurate billings.","C":"No, because the attorney’s failure to withdraw her fees as billed resulted in an impermissible commingling of her funds and the clients’ funds.","D":"No, because the attorney required an advanced payment against her fee."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall hold property of clients or third persons that is in a lawyer’s possession in connection with a representation separate from the lawyer’s own property.” Model Rule 1.15(a). Additionally, “a lawyer shall deposit into a client trust account legal fees and expenses that have been paid in advance, to be withdrawn by the lawyer only as fees are earned or expenses incurred.” Model Rule 1.15(c). Importantly, once the funds were identified as belonging to the attorney (i.e., the funds were earned), they should have been removed from the client account. A is incorrect. Before the fees were earned, it was absolutely proper to place them in the Clients’ Trust Account. However, once the fees were earned and became the attorney’s property, they had to be removed from the account. The only time a lawyer’s own funds should be in the client trust account is when those funds are used for the sole purpose of paying bank service charges on the account, and those funds should only be in the amount necessary for that purpose. Model Rule 1.15(b). B is incorrect. Although the billings were accurate, the funds remained impermissibly commingled in the account. D is incorrect. It is proper for a lawyer to accept fees in advance, so long as those fees are kept in the client trust account until they are earned by the lawyer. Model Rule 1.15(c).","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall hold property of clients or third persons that is in a lawyer’s possession in connection with a representation separate from the lawyer’s own property.” Model Rule 1.15(a). Additionally, “a lawyer shall deposit into a client trust account legal fees and expenses that have been paid in advance, to be withdrawn by the lawyer only as fees are earned or expenses incurred.” Model Rule 1.15(c). Importantly, once the funds were identified as belonging to the attorney (i.e., the funds were earned), they should have been removed from the client account. A is incorrect. Before the fees were earned, it was absolutely proper to place them in the Clients’ Trust Account. However, once the fees were earned and became the attorney’s property, they had to be removed from the account. The only time a lawyer’s own funds should be in the client trust account is when those funds are used for the sole purpose of paying bank service charges on the account, and those funds should only be in the amount necessary for that purpose. Model Rule 1.15(b). B is incorrect. Although the billings were accurate, the funds remained impermissibly commingled in the account. D is incorrect. It is proper for a lawyer to accept fees in advance, so long as those fees are kept in the client trust account until they are earned by the lawyer. Model Rule 1.15(c).","irac":{"issue":"Was the attorney’s conduct proper?","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall hold property of clients or third persons that is in a lawyer’s possession in connection with a representation separate from the lawyer’s own property.” Model Rule 1.15(a). Additionally, “a lawyer shall deposit into a client trust account legal fees and expenses that have been paid in advance, to be withdrawn by the lawyer only as fees are earned or expenses incurred.” Model Rule 1.15(c). Importantly, once the funds were identified as belonging to the attorney (i.e., the funds were earned), they should have been removed from the client account. A is incorrect. Before the fees were earned, it was absolutely proper to place them in the Clients’ Trust Account. However, once the fees were earned and became the attorney’s property, they had to be removed from the account. The only time a lawyer’s own funds should be in the client trust account is when those funds are used for the sole purpose of paying bank service charges on the account, and those funds should only be in the amount necessary for that purpose. Model Rule 1.15(b). B is incorrect. Although the billings were accurate, the funds remained impermissibly commingled in the account. D is incorrect. It is proper for a lawyer to accept fees in advance, so long as those fees are kept in the client trust account until they are earned by the lawyer. Model Rule 1.15(c).","analysis":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall hold property of clients or third persons that is in a lawyer’s possession in connection with a representation separate from the lawyer’s own property.” Model Rule 1.15(a). Additionally, “a lawyer shall deposit into a client trust account legal fees and expenses that have been paid in advance, to be withdrawn by the lawyer only as fees are earned or expenses incurred.” Model Rule 1.15(c). Importantly, once the funds were identified as belonging to the attorney (i.e., the funds were earned), they should have been removed from the client account. A is incorrect. Before the fees were earned, it was absolutely proper to place them in the Clients’ Trust Account. However, once the fees were earned and became the attorney’s property, they had to be removed from the account. The only time a lawyer’s own funds should be in the client trust account is when those funds are used for the sole purpose of paying bank service charges on the account, and those funds should only be in the amount necessary for that purpose. Model Rule 1.15(b). B is incorrect. Although the billings were accurate, the funds remained impermissibly commingled in the account. D is incorrect. It is proper for a lawyer to accept fees in advance, so long as those fees are kept in the client trust account until they are earned by the lawyer. Model Rule 1.15(c).","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.15(a)","Model Rule 1.15(c)","Model Rule 1.15(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[189,190],"sourceAnswerPages":[287,288],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师与委托人订立书面聘用及小时收费协议，要求委托人在律师提供任何服务前预付 5,000 美元，并要求律师退还其中未赚取的部分。协议还规定律师每月提供对账单，并按账单取出费用。协议没有说明 5,000 美元预付款应存入律师的委托人信托账户还是一般账户。律师将全部款项存入委托人信托账户，该账户还存有其他人委托律师保管的资金。此后，律师在提供服务后，定期向委托人提供进度报告及服务对账单，显示预付费用余额。不过，律师一直没有取出 5,000 美元预付款中的任何部分，直到一年后事项结束，委托人完全满意。此时，律师已向委托人开具合理法律服务费 4,500 美元。律师从委托人信托账户开出两张支票：一张向自己支付 4,500 美元，存入一般办公账户；另一张向委托人支付 500 美元。律师行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为律师将资金存入委托人信托账户。","B":"适当，因为律师定期提供了准确账单。","C":"不适当，因为律师没有在费用赚取后按账单取出，导致自己的资金与委托人资金被不当混同。","D":"不适当，因为律师要求预付费用。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。“律师应将其在代理关系中占有的委托人或第三人财产，与律师自己的财产分开保管。”《职业行为示范规则》1.15(a)。此外，“律师应把预付法律服务费和开支存入委托人信托账户，只有在费用已经赚取或开支已经发生时才可取出。”规则 1.15(c)。关键是，一旦资金被确定属于律师（即费用已经赚取），就应从委托人账户取出。A 不正确。在费用赚取前，将其存入委托人信托账户完全适当。但一旦费用已经赚取并成为律师财产，就必须从该账户取出。律师自己的资金只能为支付该账户银行服务费这一唯一目的而存入委托人信托账户，而且金额只能以该目的所需为限。规则 1.15(b)。B 不正确。账单虽然准确，资金仍被不当地混同在账户中。D 不正确。律师可以接受预付费用，只要在律师赚取前将其留在委托人信托账户中。规则 1.15(c)。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-036","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":36,"topic":"Model Rule 8.2(a)","question":"Attorneys Alpha and Beta had been political opponents. Alpha was elected to the state legislature after a bitter race in which Beta had managed the campaign of Alpha’s opponent. Alpha had publicly blamed Beta at that time for what Alpha reasonably believed were illegal and unethical campaign practices and later had publicly objected to Beta’s appointment as a judge. Alpha represented a client in a widely publicized case tried in Judge Beta’s court. At the conclusion of the trial, Beta ruled against Alpha’s client. Alpha then held a press conference and said, “All that you reporters have to do is check your files and you will know what I think about Judge Beta’s character and fitness.” Is Alpha subject to discipline for making this statement?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because Alpha’s statement might lessen confidence in the legal system.","B":"Yes, because Alpha’s past accusations were unrelated to Beta’s legal knowledge.","C":"No, because Alpha reasonably believed that the statements about Beta were true.","D":"No, because Beta had equal access to the press."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not make a statement that the lawyer knows to be false or with reckless disregard to its truth or falsity concerning the qualifications or integrity of a judge.” Model Rule 8.2(a). Here, since the question states that the attorney reasonably believed the accusations to be true, he is not subject to discipline for making the statements. A is incorrect. As long as a lawyer does not make a statement that is false or with reckless disregard to the truth, a lawyer may properly criticize members of the judiciary. This answer choice should have been discarded immediately, as it implies that judges may never be criticized by attorneys. B is incorrect. An attorney is allowed to question the character and fitness of judges, not only the extent of their legal knowledge. D is incorrect. If the attorney was making false statements, the simple fact Beta had equal access to the press would not solve the matter (since Beta’s reputation would have already been unfairly damaged).","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not make a statement that the lawyer knows to be false or with reckless disregard to its truth or falsity concerning the qualifications or integrity of a judge.” Model Rule 8.2(a). Here, since the question states that the attorney reasonably believed the accusations to be true, he is not subject to discipline for making the statements. A is incorrect. As long as a lawyer does not make a statement that is false or with reckless disregard to the truth, a lawyer may properly criticize members of the judiciary. This answer choice should have been discarded immediately, as it implies that judges may never be criticized by attorneys. B is incorrect. An attorney is allowed to question the character and fitness of judges, not only the extent of their legal knowledge. D is incorrect. If the attorney was making false statements, the simple fact Beta had equal access to the press would not solve the matter (since Beta’s reputation would have already been unfairly damaged).","irac":{"issue":"” Is Alpha subject to discipline for making this statement?","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not make a statement that the lawyer knows to be false or with reckless disregard to its truth or falsity concerning the qualifications or integrity of a judge.” Model Rule 8.2(a). Here, since the question states that the attorney reasonably believed the accusations to be true, he is not subject to discipline for making the statements. A is incorrect. As long as a lawyer does not make a statement that is false or with reckless disregard to the truth, a lawyer may properly criticize members of the judiciary. This answer choice should have been discarded immediately, as it implies that judges may never be criticized by attorneys. B is incorrect. An attorney is allowed to question the character and fitness of judges, not only the extent of their legal knowledge. D is incorrect. If the attorney was making false statements, the simple fact Beta had equal access to the press would not solve the matter (since Beta’s reputation would have already been unfairly damaged).","analysis":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not make a statement that the lawyer knows to be false or with reckless disregard to its truth or falsity concerning the qualifications or integrity of a judge.” Model Rule 8.2(a). Here, since the question states that the attorney reasonably believed the accusations to be true, he is not subject to discipline for making the statements. A is incorrect. As long as a lawyer does not make a statement that is false or with reckless disregard to the truth, a lawyer may properly criticize members of the judiciary. This answer choice should have been discarded immediately, as it implies that judges may never be criticized by attorneys. B is incorrect. An attorney is allowed to question the character and fitness of judges, not only the extent of their legal knowledge. D is incorrect. If the attorney was making false statements, the simple fact Beta had equal access to the press would not solve the matter (since Beta’s reputation would have already been unfairly damaged).","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 8.2(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[190],"sourceAnswerPages":[288],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"律师甲和律师乙过去是政治对手。在一场激烈竞选中，乙曾为甲的对手管理竞选活动，最终甲当选州议员。当时，甲公开指责乙实施了甲合理认为违法且违反职业伦理的竞选行为；后来乙获任法官时，甲也公开表示反对。甲代理委托人在乙法官的法庭审理一宗受到广泛关注的案件。审判结束时，乙作出不利于甲委托人的裁判。甲随后召开记者会，说：“各位记者只需查看你们的档案，就会知道我对乙法官的品格和适任性有何看法。”甲是否因该陈述而应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为甲的陈述可能降低公众对法律制度的信心。","B":"是，因为甲过去的指控与乙的法律知识无关。","C":"否，因为甲合理地相信有关乙的陈述真实。","D":"否，因为乙同样有机会接触媒体。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。“律师不得就法官的资历或诚信，作出律师明知虚假或罔顾其真假的陈述。”《职业行为示范规则》8.2(a)。本题明确说明律师合理地相信指控真实，因此不应因陈述受到纪律处分。A 不正确。只要律师没有作出虚假或罔顾真假的陈述，就可以适当地批评司法人员。该选项暗示律师永远不能批评法官，应立即排除。B 不正确。律师可以质疑法官的品格和适任性，而不只限于其法律知识水平。D 不正确。如果律师作出虚假陈述，乙同样有机会接触媒体并不能解决问题，因为乙的声誉已经受到不公平损害。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-037","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":37,"topic":"Model Rule 3.2","question":"An attorney who was a member of the state legislature was allowed to engage in private practice under state law. The attorney represented a plaintiff in a personal injury case. The attorney reasonably believed the trial of the case will last at least two weeks. When the case was first scheduled for trial, the attorney requested a continuance, truthfully stating, “As the court knows, I am a member of the legislature, which will be going into special session next week. Because of my legislative duties, I must be in the state capitol for the duration of the session.” The defendant objected to the continuance, but the court granted it. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the defendant objected to the continuance.","B":"Yes, because the attorney used her public position to influence a tribunal.","C":"No, because the attorney’s statements to the court were truthful.","D":"No, because the continuance will not give the plaintiff an advantage in the litigation."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. Although Model Rule 3.2 requires a lawyer to make reasonable efforts to expedite litigation, it also notes that “there will be occasions when a lawyer may properly seek a postponement for personal reasons.” Model Rule 3.2, Comment [1]. Here, the lawyer requested a continuance to fulfill her legislative duties, which is a valid reason for a postponement. A is incorrect. Although the defendant objected to the continuance, the defendant’s objection did not make the lawyer’s request improper. B is incorrect. The facts in this question do not contain any indication that the lawyer used her public position to influence the tribunal. She only noted that she was a member of the legislature to explain her need for the continuance. D is incorrect. If the lawyer had asked for a continuance for an improper reason, she would still be subject to discipline whether the request helped her client or not.","rule":"C is the correct answer. Although Model Rule 3.2 requires a lawyer to make reasonable efforts to expedite litigation, it also notes that “there will be occasions when a lawyer may properly seek a postponement for personal reasons.” Model Rule 3.2, Comment [1]. Here, the lawyer requested a continuance to fulfill her legislative duties, which is a valid reason for a postponement. A is incorrect. Although the defendant objected to the continuance, the defendant’s objection did not make the lawyer’s request improper. B is incorrect. The facts in this question do not contain any indication that the lawyer used her public position to influence the tribunal. She only noted that she was a member of the legislature to explain her need for the continuance. D is incorrect. If the lawyer had asked for a continuance for an improper reason, she would still be subject to discipline whether the request helped her client or not.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"C is the correct answer. Although Model Rule 3.2 requires a lawyer to make reasonable efforts to expedite litigation, it also notes that “there will be occasions when a lawyer may properly seek a postponement for personal reasons.” Model Rule 3.2, Comment [1]. Here, the lawyer requested a continuance to fulfill her legislative duties, which is a valid reason for a postponement. A is incorrect. Although the defendant objected to the continuance, the defendant’s objection did not make the lawyer’s request improper. B is incorrect. The facts in this question do not contain any indication that the lawyer used her public position to influence the tribunal. She only noted that she was a member of the legislature to explain her need for the continuance. D is incorrect. If the lawyer had asked for a continuance for an improper reason, she would still be subject to discipline whether the request helped her client or not.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. Although Model Rule 3.2 requires a lawyer to make reasonable efforts to expedite litigation, it also notes that “there will be occasions when a lawyer may properly seek a postponement for personal reasons.” Model Rule 3.2, Comment [1]. Here, the lawyer requested a continuance to fulfill her legislative duties, which is a valid reason for a postponement. A is incorrect. Although the defendant objected to the continuance, the defendant’s objection did not make the lawyer’s request improper. B is incorrect. The facts in this question do not contain any indication that the lawyer used her public position to influence the tribunal. She only noted that she was a member of the legislature to explain her need for the continuance. D is incorrect. If the lawyer had asked for a continuance for an improper reason, she would still be subject to discipline whether the request helped her client or not.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.2"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[190,191],"sourceAnswerPages":[289],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名州议会议员兼律师，根据州法律可以从事私人执业。律师在一宗人身伤害案件中代理原告，并合理地认为审判至少持续两周。案件最初确定审判日期时，律师请求延期，如实陈述：“正如法院所知，我是议会议员，而议会下周将召开特别会议。由于立法职责，我必须在整个会议期间留在州首府。”被告反对延期，但法院批准。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为被告反对延期。","B":"是，因为律师利用公职地位影响审裁机构。","C":"否，因为律师向法院作出的陈述真实。","D":"否，因为延期不会使原告在诉讼中取得优势。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》3.2 虽要求律师作出合理努力加快诉讼，但也指出，“有时律师可以出于个人原因适当地请求延期。”规则 3.2 评注 [1]。本题中，律师为履行立法职责而请求延期，属于有效理由。A 不正确。被告虽然反对延期，其反对不会使律师的请求不适当。B 不正确。题目没有任何迹象表明律师利用公职地位影响审裁机构。她只是提到自己是议员，以解释为何需要延期。D 不正确。如果律师出于不当理由请求延期，无论该请求是否有利于委托人，仍应受纪律处分。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-038","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":38,"topic":"Model Rule 4.2","question":"Last year, a man’s house was severely damaged when a large tree in his neighbor’s yard toppled onto the man’s roof and porch. The man hired an attorney, who filed a suit against the neighbor on the man’s behalf to recover over $40,000 in damages. The neighbor was represented by another attorney, who was uncooperative. When the man’s attorney communicated a settlement offer to the neighbor’s attorney, the neighbor’s attorney said he would consider it. However, the neighbor’s attorney did not respond further, and the man’s attorney suspected the offer was never communicated to the neighbor. When the man learned of his attorney’s suspicions, he asked whether he could go talk to the neighbor himself. The man’s attorney said that he should do so and see if they could “put an end to this unpleasantness.” Is the man’s attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the man’s attorney suggested that the man communicate about the subject matter of the representation with a person known to be represented by another lawyer.","B":"Yes, because there was no proof the neighbor’s attorney had failed to inform the neighbor of the settlement offer.","C":"No, because the man and the neighbor may communicate with each other.","D":"No, because by entering into settlement negotiations, the neighbor’s attorney impliedly consented to direct communications with the neighbor."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. “Parties to a matter may communicate directly with each other, and a lawyer is not prohibited from advising a client concerning a communication that the client is legally entitled to make.” Model Rule 4.2 Comment [4]. Consequently, the lawyer could suggest that his client talk to the other party. A is incorrect. Model Rule 4.2 only prohibits the lawyer from talking to the opposing party. It does not prohibit any communications between the parties themselves. B is incorrect. Parties to a matter can communicate directly with each other, whether or not there is any evidence of wrongdoing on an attorney’s part. D is incorrect. Since the parties are allowed to communicate with each other directly, there was no need for the attorney’s express or implied consent.","rule":"C is the correct answer. “Parties to a matter may communicate directly with each other, and a lawyer is not prohibited from advising a client concerning a communication that the client is legally entitled to make.” Model Rule 4.2 Comment [4]. Consequently, the lawyer could suggest that his client talk to the other party. A is incorrect. Model Rule 4.2 only prohibits the lawyer from talking to the opposing party. It does not prohibit any communications between the parties themselves. B is incorrect. Parties to a matter can communicate directly with each other, whether or not there is any evidence of wrongdoing on an attorney’s part. D is incorrect. Since the parties are allowed to communicate with each other directly, there was no need for the attorney’s express or implied consent.","irac":{"issue":"” Is the man’s attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"C is the correct answer. “Parties to a matter may communicate directly with each other, and a lawyer is not prohibited from advising a client concerning a communication that the client is legally entitled to make.” Model Rule 4.2 Comment [4]. Consequently, the lawyer could suggest that his client talk to the other party. A is incorrect. Model Rule 4.2 only prohibits the lawyer from talking to the opposing party. It does not prohibit any communications between the parties themselves. B is incorrect. Parties to a matter can communicate directly with each other, whether or not there is any evidence of wrongdoing on an attorney’s part. D is incorrect. Since the parties are allowed to communicate with each other directly, there was no need for the attorney’s express or implied consent.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. “Parties to a matter may communicate directly with each other, and a lawyer is not prohibited from advising a client concerning a communication that the client is legally entitled to make.” Model Rule 4.2 Comment [4]. Consequently, the lawyer could suggest that his client talk to the other party. A is incorrect. Model Rule 4.2 only prohibits the lawyer from talking to the opposing party. It does not prohibit any communications between the parties themselves. B is incorrect. Parties to a matter can communicate directly with each other, whether or not there is any evidence of wrongdoing on an attorney’s part. D is incorrect. Since the parties are allowed to communicate with each other directly, there was no need for the attorney’s express or implied consent.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 4.2"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[191],"sourceAnswerPages":[289],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"去年，一名男子的房屋因邻居院内一棵大树倒在其屋顶和门廊上而严重受损。男子聘请一名律师，后者代表他起诉邻居，要求超过 40,000 美元损害赔偿。邻居由另一名不合作的律师代理。男子的律师把和解要约告知邻居律师时，对方说会考虑，但此后没有回应；男子律师怀疑要约从未转达给邻居。男子得知律师的怀疑后，询问自己能否亲自与邻居交谈。男子律师说他应该这样做，看看二人能否“结束这件不愉快的事”。男子律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为男子律师建议男子就代理事项与明知由另一名律师代理的人沟通。","B":"是，因为没有证据证明邻居律师没有把和解要约告知邻居。","C":"否，因为男子和邻居可以彼此沟通。","D":"否，因为邻居律师参与和解谈判，即默示同意直接与邻居沟通。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。“事项当事人可以彼此直接沟通，而且律师并不被禁止就委托人在法律上有权作出的沟通向其提供意见。”《职业行为示范规则》4.2 评注 [4]。因此，律师可以建议委托人与另一方交谈。A 不正确。规则 4.2 只禁止律师本人联系对方当事人，并不禁止当事人彼此沟通。B 不正确。无论是否有任何证据表明律师存在不当行为，事项当事人都可以彼此直接沟通。D 不正确。既然当事人可以直接沟通，就不需要律师明示或默示同意。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-039","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":39,"topic":"Model Rule 3.1 / Model Rule 3.2 / Model Rule 3.3","question":"An attorney is defending a corporation against a lawsuit brought in federal court by the plaintiff, a consumer injured by one of the corporation’s products. The plaintiff is seeking both compensatory and punitive damages. During discovery, the plaintiff’s lawyer served a set of interrogatories on the corporation, including requests for the financial data of the corporation. The president of the corporation directed the attorney to resist providing this information, although the attorney has informed him that, under the rules of discovery, the plaintiff is entitled to the information requested. The president then demanded that the attorney assert that the information is confidential, privileged work product, and a trade secret, but the attorney correctly informed him that it was well settled that such claims would be regarded as frivolous by the courts. The president nonetheless directed the attorney to file objections on the bases stated, so that at least the plaintiff will have to incur the expense of compelling discovery. The attorney filed the objections as directed by the president. Which of the following statements is true?","choices":{"A":"The attorney is subject to discipline.","B":"The attorney is subject to litigation sanction.","C":"The attorney is subject to both discipline and litigation sanction.","D":"The attorney is subject to neither discipline nor litigation sanction."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. By doing what the president asked, the lawyer has violated Model Rule 3.1, which requires meritorious claims and contentions; Model Rule 3.2, which requires the lawyer to expedite litigation; Model Rule 3.3, which requires candor toward the tribunal; and Model Rule 3.4, which requires fairness to the opposing party and counsel. Consequently, the lawyer is subject to discipline under Model Rule 8.4. Additionally, Federal Rules of Civil Procedure Rule 11 provides litigation sanctions for an attorney who files a pleading or other motion for “any improper purpose, such as to harass, cause unnecessary delay, or needlessly increase the cost of litigation.” Consequently, A, B, and D are incorrect. As a side note, although there have been several “C”s in a row, this fact should not have changed your analysis.","rule":"C is the correct answer. By doing what the president asked, the lawyer has violated Model Rule 3.1, which requires meritorious claims and contentions; Model Rule 3.2, which requires the lawyer to expedite litigation; Model Rule 3.3, which requires candor toward the tribunal; and Model Rule 3.4, which requires fairness to the opposing party and counsel. Consequently, the lawyer is subject to discipline under Model Rule 8.4. Additionally, Federal Rules of Civil Procedure Rule 11 provides litigation sanctions for an attorney who files a pleading or other motion for “any improper purpose, such as to harass, cause unnecessary delay, or needlessly increase the cost of litigation.” Consequently, A, B, and D are incorrect. As a side note, although there have been several “C”s in a row, this fact should not have changed your analysis.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following statements is true?","rule":"C is the correct answer. By doing what the president asked, the lawyer has violated Model Rule 3.1, which requires meritorious claims and contentions; Model Rule 3.2, which requires the lawyer to expedite litigation; Model Rule 3.3, which requires candor toward the tribunal; and Model Rule 3.4, which requires fairness to the opposing party and counsel. Consequently, the lawyer is subject to discipline under Model Rule 8.4. Additionally, Federal Rules of Civil Procedure Rule 11 provides litigation sanctions for an attorney who files a pleading or other motion for “any improper purpose, such as to harass, cause unnecessary delay, or needlessly increase the cost of litigation.” Consequently, A, B, and D are incorrect. As a side note, although there have been several “C”s in a row, this fact should not have changed your analysis.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. By doing what the president asked, the lawyer has violated Model Rule 3.1, which requires meritorious claims and contentions; Model Rule 3.2, which requires the lawyer to expedite litigation; Model Rule 3.3, which requires candor toward the tribunal; and Model Rule 3.4, which requires fairness to the opposing party and counsel. Consequently, the lawyer is subject to discipline under Model Rule 8.4. Additionally, Federal Rules of Civil Procedure Rule 11 provides litigation sanctions for an attorney who files a pleading or other motion for “any improper purpose, such as to harass, cause unnecessary delay, or needlessly increase the cost of litigation.” Consequently, A, B, and D are incorrect. As a side note, although there have been several “C”s in a row, this fact should not have changed your analysis.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.1","Model Rule 3.2","Model Rule 3.3","Model Rule 3.4","Model Rule 8.4","Rule 11"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[192],"sourceAnswerPages":[289,290],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师正在联邦法院代理一家公司，抗辩一名因公司产品受伤的消费者原告提起的诉讼。原告请求补偿性和惩罚性损害赔偿。在证据开示中，原告律师向公司送达一组书面询问，其中要求提供公司的财务数据。公司总裁指示律师拒绝提供；但律师已告知总裁，根据证据开示规则，原告有权取得所请求信息。总裁随后要求律师主张信息属于保密信息、受特权保护的工作成果和商业秘密，但律师正确告知他，既有判例已明确，这些主张会被法院视为轻率无据。总裁仍指示律师以这些理由提出异议，至少迫使原告承担申请强制开示的费用。律师依总裁指示提出异议。下列哪项正确？","choicesZh":{"A":"律师应受纪律处分。","B":"律师会受到诉讼制裁。","C":"律师既应受纪律处分，也会受到诉讼制裁。","D":"律师既不应受纪律处分，也不会受到诉讼制裁。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。律师依总裁要求行事，违反了要求权利主张及论点具有依据的《职业行为示范规则》3.1、要求加快诉讼的规则 3.2、要求对审裁机构坦率的规则 3.3，以及要求公平对待对方当事人及其律师的规则 3.4。因此，律师会根据规则 8.4 受到纪律处分。此外，《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 11 条规定，律师为“任何不当目的，例如骚扰、造成不必要延误或无谓增加诉讼成本”而提交诉状或其他动议时，会受到诉讼制裁。因此，A、B 和 D 均不正确。另请注意，即使连续数题的答案都是 C，也不应因此改变分析。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-040","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":40,"topic":"Model Rule 1.18","question":"An attorney is a general practitioner with extensive experience in personal injury litigation, including legal and medical malpractice. A man contacted the attorney by telephone and requested that the attorney represent him in a legal malpractice case that the man wanted to file against the attorney who handled the man’s divorce. The attorney refused to meet with the man, saying: “Look, I just finished renewing my own malpractice insurance policy, and I can’t believe how high the premiums have gotten. I’m not taking on any new clients with legal malpractice cases.” The man tried to contact several other lawyers, each of whom indicated that he or she would be happy to accept the representation but was too busy to take on any new matters at this time. Six months later, the statute of limitations expired without the man filing his lawsuit. If the man can establish that a legal malpractice action against the divorce attorney would have succeeded, is the attorney subject to civil liability for refusing to accept the representation?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney did not have good cause to refuse the representation.","B":"Yes, because the attorney did not make reasonable efforts to find a competent lawyer to represent the man.","C":"No, because the attorney did not hold herself out as specially experienced in legal malpractice cases.","D":"No, because the attorney had no legal obligation to accept the man’s case."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.18 Comment [1] notes that “[a] lawyer’s consultations with a prospective client usually are limited in time and depth and leave both the prospective client and the lawyer free (and sometimes required) to proceed no further. Hence, prospective clients should receive some but not all of the protection afforded clients.” Consequently, the lawyer was not legally obligated to accept the man’s case. A is incorrect. A lawyer’s decision not to represent a client is not limited by a need to show good cause to refuse the representation. B is incorrect. As noted in Model Rule 1.18, while a prospective client receives some protections (such as prohibitions regarding the disclosure of information), a lawyer is not required to help a prospective client find another lawyer to represent him or her if the lawyer decides to decline the representation. C is incorrect. Even if the lawyer had held herself out as an expert in legal malpractice, the Rules would not have required her to represent this potential client.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.18 Comment [1] notes that “[a] lawyer’s consultations with a prospective client usually are limited in time and depth and leave both the prospective client and the lawyer free (and sometimes required) to proceed no further. Hence, prospective clients should receive some but not all of the protection afforded clients.” Consequently, the lawyer was not legally obligated to accept the man’s case. A is incorrect. A lawyer’s decision not to represent a client is not limited by a need to show good cause to refuse the representation. B is incorrect. As noted in Model Rule 1.18, while a prospective client receives some protections (such as prohibitions regarding the disclosure of information), a lawyer is not required to help a prospective client find another lawyer to represent him or her if the lawyer decides to decline the representation. C is incorrect. Even if the lawyer had held herself out as an expert in legal malpractice, the Rules would not have required her to represent this potential client.","irac":{"issue":"If the man can establish that a legal malpractice action against the divorce attorney would have succeeded, is the attorney subject to civil liability for refusing to accept the representation?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.18 Comment [1] notes that “[a] lawyer’s consultations with a prospective client usually are limited in time and depth and leave both the prospective client and the lawyer free (and sometimes required) to proceed no further. Hence, prospective clients should receive some but not all of the protection afforded clients.” Consequently, the lawyer was not legally obligated to accept the man’s case. A is incorrect. A lawyer’s decision not to represent a client is not limited by a need to show good cause to refuse the representation. B is incorrect. As noted in Model Rule 1.18, while a prospective client receives some protections (such as prohibitions regarding the disclosure of information), a lawyer is not required to help a prospective client find another lawyer to represent him or her if the lawyer decides to decline the representation. C is incorrect. Even if the lawyer had held herself out as an expert in legal malpractice, the Rules would not have required her to represent this potential client.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.18 Comment [1] notes that “[a] lawyer’s consultations with a prospective client usually are limited in time and depth and leave both the prospective client and the lawyer free (and sometimes required) to proceed no further. Hence, prospective clients should receive some but not all of the protection afforded clients.” Consequently, the lawyer was not legally obligated to accept the man’s case. A is incorrect. A lawyer’s decision not to represent a client is not limited by a need to show good cause to refuse the representation. B is incorrect. As noted in Model Rule 1.18, while a prospective client receives some protections (such as prohibitions regarding the disclosure of information), a lawyer is not required to help a prospective client find another lawyer to represent him or her if the lawyer decides to decline the representation. C is incorrect. Even if the lawyer had held herself out as an expert in legal malpractice, the Rules would not have required her to represent this potential client.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.18"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[192,193],"sourceAnswerPages":[290],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师从事综合业务，具有丰富的人身伤害诉讼经验，包括法律及医疗执业过失。一名男子致电律师，请求律师代理他起诉曾办理其离婚案件的律师，提起法律执业过失诉讼。律师拒绝与男子会面，说：“听着，我刚续保自己的执业过失保险，简直无法相信保费涨得多高。我不再接受任何新的法律执业过失案件委托人。”男子尝试联系数名其他律师，每人都表示乐意接受代理，但目前太忙，无法接受任何新事项。六个月后，男子未提起诉讼，诉讼时效届满。如果男子能够证明针对离婚律师的执业过失诉讼本可胜诉，该律师是否因拒绝代理而承担民事责任？","choicesZh":{"A":"承担，因为律师没有拒绝代理的正当理由。","B":"承担，因为律师没有作出合理努力，为男子寻找一名称职律师。","C":"不承担，因为律师没有向外宣称自己特别擅长法律执业过失案件。","D":"不承担，因为律师没有接受男子案件的法律义务。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》1.18 评注 [1] 指出：“律师与潜在委托人的协商通常在时间和深度上有限，使潜在委托人和律师都可自由（有时也必须）不再继续。因此，潜在委托人应获得委托人所享有的部分而非全部保护。”所以，律师没有接受男子案件的法律义务。A 不正确。律师决定不代理委托人，并不以必须证明具有拒绝代理的正当理由为条件。B 不正确。如规则 1.18 所述，潜在委托人虽获得某些保护（例如禁止披露信息），但律师决定拒绝代理时，没有义务帮助潜在委托人寻找另一名律师。C 不正确。即使律师向外宣称自己是法律执业过失专家，规则仍不会要求其代理该潜在委托人。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-041","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":41,"topic":"Model Rule 7.2 / Model Rule 7.1 / Model Rule 7.3","question":"An attorney, a member of the state bar, placed a printed flyer in the booth of each artist exhibiting works at a county fair. The face of the flyer contained the following information: “I am an attorney with offices in 800 Bank Building, telephone (555) 555-5555. I have a J.D. from State Law School and an M.A. in fine arts from State University. My practice includes representing artists in negotiating contracts between artists and dealers and protecting artists’ interests. You can find me in the van parked at the fair entrance.” All factual information on the face of the flyer was correct. There was a retainer agreement on the back of the flyer. At the entrance to the fair, the attorney parked a van with a sign that read “Attorney at Law.” For which action, if any, is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Parking the van with the sign on it at the fair entrance.","B":"Placing copies of the flyer in the booth of each artist and including a retainer agreement on the back of the flyer.","C":"Parking the van with the sign on it at the fair entrance, placing copies of the flyer in the booth of each artist, and including a retainer agreement on the back of the flyer.","D":"None of the above."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2, a lawyer may advertise his or her services through written and public media, subject to Model Rule 7.1’s prohibition against false or misleading statements. Here, everything the attorney has stated about his business is true. Importantly, by placing flyers in the artists’ booths and parking his van outside the fair, he did not engage in face-to-face or real-time communication as prohibited in Model Rule 7.3. Additionally, there is no prohibition in the Rules stating that the attorney cannot have a retainer agreement on the back of the flyer. Finally, Model Rule 7.4(a) states that “[a] lawyer may communicate the fact that the lawyer does or does not practice in particular fields of law.” The attorney is not indicating any sort of certification regarding art law, but is merely stating his degree and general practice area. Consequently, A, B, and C are incorrect.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2, a lawyer may advertise his or her services through written and public media, subject to Model Rule 7.1’s prohibition against false or misleading statements. Here, everything the attorney has stated about his business is true. Importantly, by placing flyers in the artists’ booths and parking his van outside the fair, he did not engage in face-to-face or real-time communication as prohibited in Model Rule 7.3. Additionally, there is no prohibition in the Rules stating that the attorney cannot have a retainer agreement on the back of the flyer. Finally, Model Rule 7.4(a) states that “[a] lawyer may communicate the fact that the lawyer does or does not practice in particular fields of law.” The attorney is not indicating any sort of certification regarding art law, but is merely stating his degree and general practice area. Consequently, A, B, and C are incorrect.","irac":{"issue":"” For which action, if any, is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2, a lawyer may advertise his or her services through written and public media, subject to Model Rule 7.1’s prohibition against false or misleading statements. Here, everything the attorney has stated about his business is true. Importantly, by placing flyers in the artists’ booths and parking his van outside the fair, he did not engage in face-to-face or real-time communication as prohibited in Model Rule 7.3. Additionally, there is no prohibition in the Rules stating that the attorney cannot have a retainer agreement on the back of the flyer. Finally, Model Rule 7.4(a) states that “[a] lawyer may communicate the fact that the lawyer does or does not practice in particular fields of law.” The attorney is not indicating any sort of certification regarding art law, but is merely stating his degree and general practice area. Consequently, A, B, and C are incorrect.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2, a lawyer may advertise his or her services through written and public media, subject to Model Rule 7.1’s prohibition against false or misleading statements. Here, everything the attorney has stated about his business is true. Importantly, by placing flyers in the artists’ booths and parking his van outside the fair, he did not engage in face-to-face or real-time communication as prohibited in Model Rule 7.3. Additionally, there is no prohibition in the Rules stating that the attorney cannot have a retainer agreement on the back of the flyer. Finally, Model Rule 7.4(a) states that “[a] lawyer may communicate the fact that the lawyer does or does not practice in particular fields of law.” The attorney is not indicating any sort of certification regarding art law, but is merely stating his degree and general practice area. Consequently, A, B, and C are incorrect.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.2","Model Rule 7.1","Model Rule 7.3","Model Rule 7.4(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[193],"sourceAnswerPages":[290,291],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名州律师协会成员律师，在县集市每名参展艺术家的展位中放置一份印刷传单。传单正面包含以下信息：“本人是一名律师，办公室位于银行大厦 800 号，电话 (555) 555-5555。本人拥有州立法学院法学博士学位和州立大学美术硕士学位。本人业务包括代理艺术家谈判艺术家与经销商之间的合同，并保护艺术家利益。您可以在停放于集市入口处的厢式车中找到我。”传单正面的全部事实信息均正确。传单背面印有聘用协议。律师在集市入口停放一辆写有“执业律师”的厢式车。律师因下列哪项行为应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"在集市入口停放带有标牌的厢式车。","B":"在每名艺术家的展位放置传单，并在背面印有聘用协议。","C":"在集市入口停放带有标牌的厢式车、在每名艺术家的展位放置传单，并在背面印有聘用协议。","D":"以上均不构成纪律处分事由。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。根据《职业行为示范规则》7.2，律师可以通过书面及公共媒体为服务做广告，但须遵守规则 7.1 关于不得作出虚假或具有误导性陈述的规定。本题中，律师对其业务作出的全部陈述均真实。尤其是，在艺术家展位放置传单并把厢式车停在集市外，并不构成规则 7.3 禁止的面对面或实时沟通。此外，规则没有禁止律师在传单背面印制聘用协议。最后，规则 7.4(a) 规定：“律师可以说明自己从事或不从事特定法律领域的业务。”该律师没有表示自己获得任何艺术法认证，只是说明学位和一般业务领域。因此，A、B、C 均不正确。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-042","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":42,"topic":"Model Rule 1.9(a) / Model Rule 5.6","question":"Five years ago, an attorney represented a seller in the sale of the seller’s home. The attorney has not represented the seller since that time. Recently, the attorney was approached by the seller’s partner in a venture capital company formed two years ago. The partner and seller have agreed to dissolve their partnership, but cannot agree on the terms of the dissolution. The partner asked the attorney to sue the seller for an accounting of the partnership assets. If the attorney accepts the representation, is the attorney subject to disqualification?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the representation is directly adverse to the seller.","B":"Yes, because there is no indication that at the time of the sale of the seller’s home, the seller agreed that the attorney would not subsequently be precluded from representing other clients in suits against the seller.","C":"No, because the partnership dissolution is unrelated to the sale of the seller’s home.","D":"No, because the seller did not sell the home while in the partnership."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. According to Model Rule 1.9(a), “[a] lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client.” Here, the important point is that the partnership dissolution and the sale of the home are completely unrelated, so the attorney may accept the representation. A is incorrect. Although the representation may be directly adverse to his former client, it does not involve the same or a substantially related matter, so the attorney may accept the representation. B is incorrect. This answer choice is meant to be a distractor, as it touches on another Rule, but fails to state it correctly. While this choice implies that the attorney could represent other parties adverse to the seller so long as the attorney did not agree not to do so during the earlier representation, a lawyer restricting his or her right to practice in such a way is prohibited by Model Rule 5.6. D is incorrect. Since the two matters are unrelated, it is immaterial when the sale of the home occurred.","rule":"C is the correct answer. According to Model Rule 1.9(a), “[a] lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client.” Here, the important point is that the partnership dissolution and the sale of the home are completely unrelated, so the attorney may accept the representation. A is incorrect. Although the representation may be directly adverse to his former client, it does not involve the same or a substantially related matter, so the attorney may accept the representation. B is incorrect. This answer choice is meant to be a distractor, as it touches on another Rule, but fails to state it correctly. While this choice implies that the attorney could represent other parties adverse to the seller so long as the attorney did not agree not to do so during the earlier representation, a lawyer restricting his or her right to practice in such a way is prohibited by Model Rule 5.6. D is incorrect. Since the two matters are unrelated, it is immaterial when the sale of the home occurred.","irac":{"issue":"If the attorney accepts the representation, is the attorney subject to disqualification?","rule":"C is the correct answer. According to Model Rule 1.9(a), “[a] lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client.” Here, the important point is that the partnership dissolution and the sale of the home are completely unrelated, so the attorney may accept the representation. A is incorrect. Although the representation may be directly adverse to his former client, it does not involve the same or a substantially related matter, so the attorney may accept the representation. B is incorrect. This answer choice is meant to be a distractor, as it touches on another Rule, but fails to state it correctly. While this choice implies that the attorney could represent other parties adverse to the seller so long as the attorney did not agree not to do so during the earlier representation, a lawyer restricting his or her right to practice in such a way is prohibited by Model Rule 5.6. D is incorrect. Since the two matters are unrelated, it is immaterial when the sale of the home occurred.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. According to Model Rule 1.9(a), “[a] lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client.” Here, the important point is that the partnership dissolution and the sale of the home are completely unrelated, so the attorney may accept the representation. A is incorrect. Although the representation may be directly adverse to his former client, it does not involve the same or a substantially related matter, so the attorney may accept the representation. B is incorrect. This answer choice is meant to be a distractor, as it touches on another Rule, but fails to state it correctly. While this choice implies that the attorney could represent other parties adverse to the seller so long as the attorney did not agree not to do so during the earlier representation, a lawyer restricting his or her right to practice in such a way is prohibited by Model Rule 5.6. D is incorrect. Since the two matters are unrelated, it is immaterial when the sale of the home occurred.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.9(a)","Model Rule 5.6"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[194],"sourceAnswerPages":[291],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"五年前，一名律师代理卖方出售住宅。此后，律师未再代理卖方。最近，两年前与卖方共同成立一家风险投资公司的合伙人联系该律师。合伙人与卖方已同意解散合伙关系，但无法就解散条款达成一致。合伙人请求律师起诉卖方，要求对合伙财产进行核算。如果律师接受代理，是否会被取消代理资格？","choicesZh":{"A":"会，因为该代理直接不利于卖方。","B":"会，因为没有迹象表明，出售住宅时卖方曾同意律师此后不会因先前代理而不得在针对卖方的诉讼中代理其他委托人。","C":"不会，因为合伙关系解散与卖方出售住宅无关。","D":"不会，因为卖方出售住宅时尚未加入该合伙关系。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。根据《职业行为示范规则》1.9(a)，“律师先前曾就某一事项代理委托人的，此后不得在同一事项或与其实质相关的事项中，代理其利益与前委托人存在重大不利关系的另一人。”本题关键是合伙关系解散与住宅出售完全无关，因此律师可以接受代理。A 不正确。该代理虽然可能直接不利于前委托人，却不涉及同一事项或实质相关事项，因此可以接受。B 不正确。该选项试图用另一项规则干扰，但陈述并不正确。选项暗示，只要律师在先前代理时没有同意以后不得代理对抗卖方的其他当事人，就可以这样做；然而，规则 5.6 禁止律师以这种方式限制执业权。D 不正确。两项事务既然无关，住宅出售发生在何时并不重要。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-043","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":43,"topic":"Model Rule 1.6(b)","question":"An attorney represents a company that produces chemical manufacturing processes that are highly toxic and are reasonably certain to cause substantial bodily harm if disposed of improperly. The president of the company recently informed the attorney that a new employee mistakenly disposed of the waste products in the ground behind the company plant, an area that is part of the source of the city’s water supply. The attorney advised the president that, although the conduct was not criminal, the company could be civilly liable for negligence in lawsuits brought by any persons harmed by the waste products. The attorney advised the president to immediately report the problem to city authorities. Fearful of adverse publicity, the president declined to do so. The attorney further advised the president that she believed the president’s decision was immoral. The president continued to decline to report the matter. The attorney then informed the president that she was withdrawing from the representation and would inform the authorities herself. Immediately after withdrawing, the attorney reported the company’s conduct to the authorities. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the information was given to the attorney in confidence and may not be revealed without the client’s consent.","B":"Yes, because the company’s conduct was not criminal.","C":"No, because the attorney reasonably believed that the company’s disposal of the waste products was reasonably certain to cause substantial bodily harm.","D":"No, because the attorney reasonably believed that the president was pursuing an imprudent course of conduct."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer may reveal information relating to the representation of a client to the extent the lawyer reasonably believes necessary: (1) to prevent reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm.” Model Rule 1.6(b). Here, since the waste products are “reasonably certain to cause substantial bodily harm,” the attorney was not subject to discipline for reporting the company’s conduct to the authorities. A is incorrect. As noted above, a lawyer can reveal confidential information without the client’s consent if the lawyer reasonably believes the revelation is necessary to prevent substantial bodily harm. B is incorrect. The Rules’ permission regarding revealing confidential information is not limited to criminal matters. D is incorrect. The important point is that the waste products could cause substantial bodily harm, not whether or not the president was pursuing an impudent course of conduct.","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer may reveal information relating to the representation of a client to the extent the lawyer reasonably believes necessary: (1) to prevent reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm.” Model Rule 1.6(b). Here, since the waste products are “reasonably certain to cause substantial bodily harm,” the attorney was not subject to discipline for reporting the company’s conduct to the authorities. A is incorrect. As noted above, a lawyer can reveal confidential information without the client’s consent if the lawyer reasonably believes the revelation is necessary to prevent substantial bodily harm. B is incorrect. The Rules’ permission regarding revealing confidential information is not limited to criminal matters. D is incorrect. The important point is that the waste products could cause substantial bodily harm, not whether or not the president was pursuing an impudent course of conduct.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer may reveal information relating to the representation of a client to the extent the lawyer reasonably believes necessary: (1) to prevent reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm.” Model Rule 1.6(b). Here, since the waste products are “reasonably certain to cause substantial bodily harm,” the attorney was not subject to discipline for reporting the company’s conduct to the authorities. A is incorrect. As noted above, a lawyer can reveal confidential information without the client’s consent if the lawyer reasonably believes the revelation is necessary to prevent substantial bodily harm. B is incorrect. The Rules’ permission regarding revealing confidential information is not limited to criminal matters. D is incorrect. The important point is that the waste products could cause substantial bodily harm, not whether or not the president was pursuing an impudent course of conduct.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer may reveal information relating to the representation of a client to the extent the lawyer reasonably believes necessary: (1) to prevent reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm.” Model Rule 1.6(b). Here, since the waste products are “reasonably certain to cause substantial bodily harm,” the attorney was not subject to discipline for reporting the company’s conduct to the authorities. A is incorrect. As noted above, a lawyer can reveal confidential information without the client’s consent if the lawyer reasonably believes the revelation is necessary to prevent substantial bodily harm. B is incorrect. The Rules’ permission regarding revealing confidential information is not limited to criminal matters. D is incorrect. The important point is that the waste products could cause substantial bodily harm, not whether or not the president was pursuing an impudent course of conduct.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.6(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[194,195],"sourceAnswerPages":[291,292],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理一家采用剧毒化学制造工艺的公司；如果废物处置不当，合理确定会造成重大身体伤害。公司总裁最近告知律师，一名新雇员误将废物倾倒在公司厂区后方的地下，而该区域是本市供水来源的一部分。律师告知总裁，虽然该行为不构成犯罪，但公司可能在任何因废物受害者提起的诉讼中因过失承担民事责任。律师建议总裁立即向市政府有关机关报告问题。总裁担心负面报道，拒绝报告。律师进一步告知总裁，她认为该决定不道德。总裁仍拒绝报告。律师随后告知总裁自己将退出代理，并亲自通知有关机关。退出后，律师立即向有关机关报告公司行为。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为信息以保密方式告知律师，未经委托人同意不得披露。","B":"是，因为公司行为不构成犯罪。","C":"否，因为律师合理地认为公司处置废物的行为合理确定会造成重大身体伤害。","D":"否，因为律师合理地认为总裁正在采取不审慎的行动方案。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。“律师可以在其合理认为为以下目的所必需的范围内，披露与委托人代理有关的信息：(1) 防止合理确定的死亡或重大身体伤害。”《职业行为示范规则》1.6(b)。本题中，废物“合理确定会造成重大身体伤害”，因此律师向有关机关报告公司行为，不应受纪律处分。A 不正确。如上所述，律师合理地认为为防止重大身体伤害有必要披露时，可以未经委托人同意披露保密信息。B 不正确。规则对披露保密信息的许可并不限于刑事事项。D 不正确。关键在于废物可能造成重大身体伤害，而不是总裁是否在采取不审慎的行动。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-044","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":44,"topic":"Model Rule 8.1(a)","question":"An attorney’s new client asked the attorney to write a letter recommending the client’s nephew for admission to the bar. The client told the attorney that he had no direct contact with his nephew, but his sister assured him that his nephew was qualified for the bar because he has never been arrested or accused of any dishonest actions. The attorney knew nothing about the client’s nephew. Which of the following would be proper for the attorney to do?","choices":{"A":"Write the letter based on the client’s assurances.","B":"Write the letter based on the client’s assurances, because the attorney has no unfavorable information about the nephew.","C":"Write the letter after making an independent investigation into the client’s assurances.","D":"Write the letter after speaking with the nephew himself."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.1(a) prohibits a lawyer from knowingly making a false statement of material fact in connection with a bar admission application. Here, the attorney shouldn’t write the letter until he has made an investigation into the client’s statements. A is incorrect. Since the attorney knows nothing about his new client’s nephew, he should not write the letter simply based on the client’s assurances. B is incorrect. This answer choice is the same as A, in that the attorney would be making a statement regarding the nephew’s qualifications for the bar without actually knowing anything about the nephew. D is incorrect. This answer choice simply states that the attorney could write the letter after speaking with the nephew. There is no indication that they would necessarily discuss the client’s assurances regarding the nephew’s qualifications.","rule":"C is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.1(a) prohibits a lawyer from knowingly making a false statement of material fact in connection with a bar admission application. Here, the attorney shouldn’t write the letter until he has made an investigation into the client’s statements. A is incorrect. Since the attorney knows nothing about his new client’s nephew, he should not write the letter simply based on the client’s assurances. B is incorrect. This answer choice is the same as A, in that the attorney would be making a statement regarding the nephew’s qualifications for the bar without actually knowing anything about the nephew. D is incorrect. This answer choice simply states that the attorney could write the letter after speaking with the nephew. There is no indication that they would necessarily discuss the client’s assurances regarding the nephew’s qualifications.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following would be proper for the attorney to do?","rule":"C is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.1(a) prohibits a lawyer from knowingly making a false statement of material fact in connection with a bar admission application. Here, the attorney shouldn’t write the letter until he has made an investigation into the client’s statements. A is incorrect. Since the attorney knows nothing about his new client’s nephew, he should not write the letter simply based on the client’s assurances. B is incorrect. This answer choice is the same as A, in that the attorney would be making a statement regarding the nephew’s qualifications for the bar without actually knowing anything about the nephew. D is incorrect. This answer choice simply states that the attorney could write the letter after speaking with the nephew. There is no indication that they would necessarily discuss the client’s assurances regarding the nephew’s qualifications.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.1(a) prohibits a lawyer from knowingly making a false statement of material fact in connection with a bar admission application. Here, the attorney shouldn’t write the letter until he has made an investigation into the client’s statements. A is incorrect. Since the attorney knows nothing about his new client’s nephew, he should not write the letter simply based on the client’s assurances. B is incorrect. This answer choice is the same as A, in that the attorney would be making a statement regarding the nephew’s qualifications for the bar without actually knowing anything about the nephew. D is incorrect. This answer choice simply states that the attorney could write the letter after speaking with the nephew. There is no indication that they would necessarily discuss the client’s assurances regarding the nephew’s qualifications.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 8.1(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[195],"sourceAnswerPages":[292],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师的新委托人请求律师写信，推荐其外甥取得律师协会准入资格。委托人告诉律师，自己与外甥没有直接接触，但其姐妹向他保证，外甥符合准入条件，因为他从未被捕，也从未被指控实施任何不诚实行为。律师对委托人的外甥一无所知。下列哪项做法适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"根据委托人的保证写推荐信。","B":"根据委托人的保证写推荐信，因为律师没有关于外甥的不利信息。","C":"在独立调查委托人保证的内容后写推荐信。","D":"在与外甥本人交谈后写推荐信。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》8.1(a) 禁止律师在与律师协会准入申请有关的事项中，明知而作出虚假的重大事实陈述。本题中，在调查委托人的说法前，律师不应写信。A 不正确。律师对新委托人的外甥一无所知，不能仅根据委托人保证写信。B 不正确。该选项与 A 相同：律师实际上不了解外甥，却对其准入资格作出陈述。D 不正确。该选项只说律师与外甥交谈后即可写信，没有迹象表明二人一定会讨论委托人有关外甥资格的保证。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-045","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":45,"topic":"Model Rule 1.8(d) / Model Rule 1.8 / Rule 1.8","question":"An attorney wants to make it easier for her clients to pay their bills for her fees. Which of the following would be improper for the attorney to do?","choices":{"A":"Suggest that the client give publication rights concerning the case as payment.","B":"Arrange for clients to obtain bank loans for the purpose of paying the attorney’s fees.","C":"Accept property in payment for services.","D":"Accept bank credit cards."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.8(d) states “ . . . a lawyer shall not make or negotiate an agreement giving the lawyer literary or media rights to a portrayal or account based in substantial part on information relating to the representation.” This Rule guards against a lawyer doing things during representation simply to improve the value of the rights, such as spicing up or complicating the litigation to make it more entertaining. B is incorrect. There is no prohibition in the Rules that states a lawyer cannot aid a client in obtaining independent bank loans. “However, Lawyers may not subsidize lawsuits or administrative proceedings brought on behalf of their clients, including making or guaranteeing loans to their clients for living expenses, because to do so would encourage clients to pursue lawsuits that might not otherwise be brought and because such assistance gives lawyers too great a financial stake in the litigation.” Model Rule 1.8 Comment [10]. C is incorrect. “A lawyer may accept property in payment for services, such as an ownership interest in an enterprise, providing this does not involve acquisition of a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of the litigation contrary to Rule 1.8 (i). However, a fee paid in property instead of money may be subject to the requirements of Rule 1.8(a) because such fees often have the essential qualities of a business transaction with the client.” Model Rule 1.5 Comment [4]. D is incorrect. There is no prohibition in the Rules regarding a client paying a lawyer with bank credit cards.","rule":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.8(d) states “ . . . a lawyer shall not make or negotiate an agreement giving the lawyer literary or media rights to a portrayal or account based in substantial part on information relating to the representation.” This Rule guards against a lawyer doing things during representation simply to improve the value of the rights, such as spicing up or complicating the litigation to make it more entertaining. B is incorrect. There is no prohibition in the Rules that states a lawyer cannot aid a client in obtaining independent bank loans. “However, Lawyers may not subsidize lawsuits or administrative proceedings brought on behalf of their clients, including making or guaranteeing loans to their clients for living expenses, because to do so would encourage clients to pursue lawsuits that might not otherwise be brought and because such assistance gives lawyers too great a financial stake in the litigation.” Model Rule 1.8 Comment [10]. C is incorrect. “A lawyer may accept property in payment for services, such as an ownership interest in an enterprise, providing this does not involve acquisition of a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of the litigation contrary to Rule 1.8 (i). However, a fee paid in property instead of money may be subject to the requirements of Rule 1.8(a) because such fees often have the essential qualities of a business transaction with the client.” Model Rule 1.5 Comment [4]. D is incorrect. There is no prohibition in the Rules regarding a client paying a lawyer with bank credit cards.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following would be improper for the attorney to do?","rule":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.8(d) states “ . . . a lawyer shall not make or negotiate an agreement giving the lawyer literary or media rights to a portrayal or account based in substantial part on information relating to the representation.” This Rule guards against a lawyer doing things during representation simply to improve the value of the rights, such as spicing up or complicating the litigation to make it more entertaining. B is incorrect. There is no prohibition in the Rules that states a lawyer cannot aid a client in obtaining independent bank loans. “However, Lawyers may not subsidize lawsuits or administrative proceedings brought on behalf of their clients, including making or guaranteeing loans to their clients for living expenses, because to do so would encourage clients to pursue lawsuits that might not otherwise be brought and because such assistance gives lawyers too great a financial stake in the litigation.” Model Rule 1.8 Comment [10]. C is incorrect. “A lawyer may accept property in payment for services, such as an ownership interest in an enterprise, providing this does not involve acquisition of a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of the litigation contrary to Rule 1.8 (i). However, a fee paid in property instead of money may be subject to the requirements of Rule 1.8(a) because such fees often have the essential qualities of a business transaction with the client.” Model Rule 1.5 Comment [4]. D is incorrect. There is no prohibition in the Rules regarding a client paying a lawyer with bank credit cards.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.8(d) states “ . . . a lawyer shall not make or negotiate an agreement giving the lawyer literary or media rights to a portrayal or account based in substantial part on information relating to the representation.” This Rule guards against a lawyer doing things during representation simply to improve the value of the rights, such as spicing up or complicating the litigation to make it more entertaining. B is incorrect. There is no prohibition in the Rules that states a lawyer cannot aid a client in obtaining independent bank loans. “However, Lawyers may not subsidize lawsuits or administrative proceedings brought on behalf of their clients, including making or guaranteeing loans to their clients for living expenses, because to do so would encourage clients to pursue lawsuits that might not otherwise be brought and because such assistance gives lawyers too great a financial stake in the litigation.” Model Rule 1.8 Comment [10]. C is incorrect. “A lawyer may accept property in payment for services, such as an ownership interest in an enterprise, providing this does not involve acquisition of a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of the litigation contrary to Rule 1.8 (i). However, a fee paid in property instead of money may be subject to the requirements of Rule 1.8(a) because such fees often have the essential qualities of a business transaction with the client.” Model Rule 1.5 Comment [4]. D is incorrect. There is no prohibition in the Rules regarding a client paying a lawyer with bank credit cards.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.8(d)","Model Rule 1.8","Rule 1.8","Rule 1.8(a)","Model Rule 1.5"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[195,196],"sourceAnswerPages":[292,293],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师希望让委托人更容易支付律师费账单。下列哪项做法不适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"建议委托人以案件有关的出版权作为付款。","B":"安排委托人取得银行贷款，用于支付律师费。","C":"接受财产作为服务报酬。","D":"接受银行信用卡付款。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》1.8(d) 规定：“……律师不得订立或谈判一项协议，使律师取得文学或媒体权利，而该权利所涉及的描述或叙述主要基于与代理有关的信息。”该规则防止律师在代理期间仅仅为了提高权利价值而采取行动，例如使诉讼更加刺激或复杂，以增加娱乐性。B 不正确。规则没有禁止律师协助委托人取得独立银行贷款。不过，“律师不得补贴代表委托人提起的诉讼或行政程序，包括向委托人提供生活费贷款或为此担保，因为这样会鼓励委托人提起原本可能不会提起的诉讼，也会使律师在诉讼中拥有过大的经济利益。”规则 1.8 评注 [10]。C 不正确。“律师可以接受财产作为服务报酬，例如企业中的所有权利益，但不得因此违反规则 1.8(i)，取得诉因或诉讼标的的所有权利益。不过，以财产而非金钱支付的费用可能受规则 1.8(a) 要求约束，因为这类费用常常具有与委托人订立商业交易的本质特征。”规则 1.5 评注 [4]。D 不正确。规则没有禁止委托人使用银行信用卡向律师付款。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-046","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":46,"topic":"Model Rule 1.15","question":"An attorney practices law in a state that has experienced a business recession and where several banks have failed and others are severely pressed to preserve their solvency. The attorney maintains a Clients’ Trust Account in a bank and that account is insured by the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation against losses up to $250,000. The attorney also maintains his regular office account in the same bank and that account is insured up to $250,000. During a particularly busy time, the attorney’s bookkeeper told the attorney that the balance in the Clients’ Trust Account had increased to $300,000. The bookkeeper noted that the office account had a balance of $30,000. Which of the following courses of action by the attorney would be proper?","choices":{"A":"Leave the Clients’ Trust Account as is.","B":"Open another Clients’ Trust Account in another bank and transfer some funds to the second account to maintain a fully insured balance in both accounts.","C":"Temporarily transfer $50,000 from the Clients’ Trust Account to the office account so the balance in both accounts is fully within insured limits.","D":"Leave the Clients’ Trust Account as is if the balance is likely to decrease to less than $250,000 within the next 10 days."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15 Comment [1] states that “[a] lawyer should hold property of others with the care required of a professional fiduciary.” Consequently, the lawyer should open another Clients’ Trust Account in order to make sure the entire $300,000 is protected by insurance. A is incorrect. If the lawyer fails to take action regarding the funds, a significant portion of them may be lost. C is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.15, a lawyer should keep his or her property separate from clients’ property. Consequently, it would be improper for the attorney to move clients’ funds into his own bank account. D is incorrect. As in Choice A, this basically means the attorney is crossing his fingers and hoping for the best. A 10-day window does not change the analysis.","rule":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15 Comment [1] states that “[a] lawyer should hold property of others with the care required of a professional fiduciary.” Consequently, the lawyer should open another Clients’ Trust Account in order to make sure the entire $300,000 is protected by insurance. A is incorrect. If the lawyer fails to take action regarding the funds, a significant portion of them may be lost. C is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.15, a lawyer should keep his or her property separate from clients’ property. Consequently, it would be improper for the attorney to move clients’ funds into his own bank account. D is incorrect. As in Choice A, this basically means the attorney is crossing his fingers and hoping for the best. A 10-day window does not change the analysis.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following courses of action by the attorney would be proper?","rule":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15 Comment [1] states that “[a] lawyer should hold property of others with the care required of a professional fiduciary.” Consequently, the lawyer should open another Clients’ Trust Account in order to make sure the entire $300,000 is protected by insurance. A is incorrect. If the lawyer fails to take action regarding the funds, a significant portion of them may be lost. C is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.15, a lawyer should keep his or her property separate from clients’ property. Consequently, it would be improper for the attorney to move clients’ funds into his own bank account. D is incorrect. As in Choice A, this basically means the attorney is crossing his fingers and hoping for the best. A 10-day window does not change the analysis.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15 Comment [1] states that “[a] lawyer should hold property of others with the care required of a professional fiduciary.” Consequently, the lawyer should open another Clients’ Trust Account in order to make sure the entire $300,000 is protected by insurance. A is incorrect. If the lawyer fails to take action regarding the funds, a significant portion of them may be lost. C is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.15, a lawyer should keep his or her property separate from clients’ property. Consequently, it would be improper for the attorney to move clients’ funds into his own bank account. D is incorrect. As in Choice A, this basically means the attorney is crossing his fingers and hoping for the best. A 10-day window does not change the analysis.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.15"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[196],"sourceAnswerPages":[293],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师在某州执业，该州经历商业衰退，多家银行倒闭，其他银行也为维持偿付能力承受巨大压力。律师在一家银行设有委托人信托账户，由美国联邦存款保险公司承保，损失保险上限为 250,000 美元。律师在同一家银行还设有一般办公账户，同样承保至 250,000 美元。业务特别繁忙期间，记账员告诉律师，委托人信托账户余额已增加至 300,000 美元；办公账户余额为 30,000 美元。律师采取下列哪项做法适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"维持委托人信托账户现状。","B":"在另一家银行开设另一个委托人信托账户，并把部分资金转入第二个账户，使两个账户的余额均得到全额保险保障。","C":"暂时从委托人信托账户向办公账户转移 50,000 美元，使两个账户余额均完全处于保险限额内。","D":"如果委托人信托账户余额很可能在未来 10 日内降至 250,000 美元以下，则维持账户现状。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》1.15 评注 [1] 规定：“律师应以专业受信人所应具备的谨慎程度保管他人财产。”因此，律师应另开委托人信托账户，以确保全部 300,000 美元均受保险保护。A 不正确。如果律师不就资金采取行动，其中相当一部分可能损失。C 不正确。根据规则 1.15，律师应将自己的财产与委托人财产分开。因此，把委托人资金转入自己的银行账户是不适当的。D 不正确。与 A 一样，这实质上是让律师祈祷一切顺利。10 日时间并不改变分析。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-047","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":47,"topic":"Model Rule 5.4(d)(2) / Model Rule 5.4(d)(1) / Model Rule 5.4(b)","question":"A law firm, a professional corporation with five lawyer shareholders, employs 25 additional lawyers. Which of the following is proper?","choices":{"A":"Employees who are members of the bar are not made shareholders until they have been with the law firm for 10 years.","B":"The office manager, who is not a member of the bar, is executive vice president of the law firm.","C":"A widow whose husband was a lawyer shareholder in the firm until he died five years ago continues to hold her husband’s shares in the firm.","D":"The firm’s accountant, who is not a member of the bar, is a shareholder."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. There is nothing in the Rules that requires lawyers in a firm to be made shareholders within a certain amount of time. B is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4(d)(2) states that a lawyer cannot be part of a firm where “a nonlawyer is a corporate director or officer thereof or occupies the position of similar responsibility in any form of association other than a corporation.” Here, the office manager’s position of executive vice president violates the Rule. C is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4(d)(1) states that a lawyer cannot be part of a firm where “a nonlawyer owns any interest therein, except that a fiduciary representative of the estate of a lawyer may hold the stock or interest of the lawyer for a reasonable time during administration.” Here, the widow appears to be a nonlawyer and her husband has been deceased for five years, which would not likely be a reasonable amount of time even if she were a fiduciary representative of the estate. D is incorrect. Under Model Rule 5.4(b), “[a] lawyer shall not form a partnership with a nonlawyer if any of the activities of the partnership consist of the practice of law.” Consequently, the firm’s nonlawyer accountant cannot be a shareholder in the firm.","rule":"A is the correct answer. There is nothing in the Rules that requires lawyers in a firm to be made shareholders within a certain amount of time. B is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4(d)(2) states that a lawyer cannot be part of a firm where “a nonlawyer is a corporate director or officer thereof or occupies the position of similar responsibility in any form of association other than a corporation.” Here, the office manager’s position of executive vice president violates the Rule. C is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4(d)(1) states that a lawyer cannot be part of a firm where “a nonlawyer owns any interest therein, except that a fiduciary representative of the estate of a lawyer may hold the stock or interest of the lawyer for a reasonable time during administration.” Here, the widow appears to be a nonlawyer and her husband has been deceased for five years, which would not likely be a reasonable amount of time even if she were a fiduciary representative of the estate. D is incorrect. Under Model Rule 5.4(b), “[a] lawyer shall not form a partnership with a nonlawyer if any of the activities of the partnership consist of the practice of law.” Consequently, the firm’s nonlawyer accountant cannot be a shareholder in the firm.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is proper?","rule":"A is the correct answer. There is nothing in the Rules that requires lawyers in a firm to be made shareholders within a certain amount of time. B is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4(d)(2) states that a lawyer cannot be part of a firm where “a nonlawyer is a corporate director or officer thereof or occupies the position of similar responsibility in any form of association other than a corporation.” Here, the office manager’s position of executive vice president violates the Rule. C is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4(d)(1) states that a lawyer cannot be part of a firm where “a nonlawyer owns any interest therein, except that a fiduciary representative of the estate of a lawyer may hold the stock or interest of the lawyer for a reasonable time during administration.” Here, the widow appears to be a nonlawyer and her husband has been deceased for five years, which would not likely be a reasonable amount of time even if she were a fiduciary representative of the estate. D is incorrect. Under Model Rule 5.4(b), “[a] lawyer shall not form a partnership with a nonlawyer if any of the activities of the partnership consist of the practice of law.” Consequently, the firm’s nonlawyer accountant cannot be a shareholder in the firm.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. There is nothing in the Rules that requires lawyers in a firm to be made shareholders within a certain amount of time. B is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4(d)(2) states that a lawyer cannot be part of a firm where “a nonlawyer is a corporate director or officer thereof or occupies the position of similar responsibility in any form of association other than a corporation.” Here, the office manager’s position of executive vice president violates the Rule. C is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4(d)(1) states that a lawyer cannot be part of a firm where “a nonlawyer owns any interest therein, except that a fiduciary representative of the estate of a lawyer may hold the stock or interest of the lawyer for a reasonable time during administration.” Here, the widow appears to be a nonlawyer and her husband has been deceased for five years, which would not likely be a reasonable amount of time even if she were a fiduciary representative of the estate. D is incorrect. Under Model Rule 5.4(b), “[a] lawyer shall not form a partnership with a nonlawyer if any of the activities of the partnership consist of the practice of law.” Consequently, the firm’s nonlawyer accountant cannot be a shareholder in the firm.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.4(d)(2)","Model Rule 5.4(d)(1)","Model Rule 5.4(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[196,197],"sourceAnswerPages":[293,294],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家由五名律师股东组成的专业公司制律师事务所，另雇用 25 名律师。下列哪项安排适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"作为律师协会成员的雇员，只有在事务所任职满 10 年后才可成为股东。","B":"不是律师协会成员的办公室经理担任事务所执行副总裁。","C":"一名寡妇的丈夫五年前去世前是事务所律师股东，该寡妇继续持有丈夫的事务所股份。","D":"不是律师协会成员的事务所会计师是事务所股东。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。规则没有要求事务所律师必须在某一特定年限内成为股东。B 不正确。《职业行为示范规则》5.4(d)(2) 规定，律师不得参加以下事务所：“非律师担任公司董事或管理人员，或者在公司以外的任何组织形式中担任负有类似职责的职位。”本题办公室经理担任执行副总裁，违反规则。C 不正确。规则 5.4(d)(1) 规定，律师不得参加以下事务所：“非律师在其中拥有任何利益；但律师遗产的受信代表可以在遗产管理期间的合理时间内持有该律师的股份或权益。”本题寡妇似乎不是律师，而其丈夫已去世五年；即使她是遗产受信代表，这也不太可能属于合理时间。D 不正确。根据规则 5.4(b)，“如果合伙组织的任何活动构成法律执业活动，律师不得与非律师组成该合伙组织。”因此，事务所的非律师会计师不能成为股东。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-048","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":48,"topic":"Model Rule 1.1","question":"An attorney, who was recently admitted to the bar, has been appointed by the court as counsel for an indigent defendant charged with a felony. After consulting with the defendant and attempting for two days to prepare the case for trial, the attorney became convinced that he lacked the knowledge and experience to represent the defendant effectively. Which of the following would be proper for the attorney?","choices":{"A":"Request permission of the court to withdraw from representing the defendant.","B":"Request a two-week continuance to allow more time to prepare for trial.","C":"Explain the circumstances to the defendant, and if the defendant consents, proceed to represent the defendant to the best of his ability.","D":"Explain the circumstances to the defendant, and if the defendant consents in writing, proceed to represent the defendant to the best of his ability."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.1 requires a lawyer to give competent representation to a client. Here, since the attorney does not believe he can give competent representation, he should ask the court for permission to withdraw. B is incorrect. Model Rule 1.1 Comment [2] states that “[a] lawyer can provide adequate representation in a wholly novel field through necessary study.” However, this choice simply says that the attorney should ask for a “two-week continuance.” It says nothing about whether this would be an adequate amount of time for the lawyer to study this particular area of the law. C is incorrect. A lawyer cannot represent a client if he or she is not competent to do so, even if the client consents to the representation. D is incorrect. A lawyer cannot represent a client if he or she is not competent to do so, even if the client consents in writing.","rule":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.1 requires a lawyer to give competent representation to a client. Here, since the attorney does not believe he can give competent representation, he should ask the court for permission to withdraw. B is incorrect. Model Rule 1.1 Comment [2] states that “[a] lawyer can provide adequate representation in a wholly novel field through necessary study.” However, this choice simply says that the attorney should ask for a “two-week continuance.” It says nothing about whether this would be an adequate amount of time for the lawyer to study this particular area of the law. C is incorrect. A lawyer cannot represent a client if he or she is not competent to do so, even if the client consents to the representation. D is incorrect. A lawyer cannot represent a client if he or she is not competent to do so, even if the client consents in writing.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following would be proper for the attorney?","rule":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.1 requires a lawyer to give competent representation to a client. Here, since the attorney does not believe he can give competent representation, he should ask the court for permission to withdraw. B is incorrect. Model Rule 1.1 Comment [2] states that “[a] lawyer can provide adequate representation in a wholly novel field through necessary study.” However, this choice simply says that the attorney should ask for a “two-week continuance.” It says nothing about whether this would be an adequate amount of time for the lawyer to study this particular area of the law. C is incorrect. A lawyer cannot represent a client if he or she is not competent to do so, even if the client consents to the representation. D is incorrect. A lawyer cannot represent a client if he or she is not competent to do so, even if the client consents in writing.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.1 requires a lawyer to give competent representation to a client. Here, since the attorney does not believe he can give competent representation, he should ask the court for permission to withdraw. B is incorrect. Model Rule 1.1 Comment [2] states that “[a] lawyer can provide adequate representation in a wholly novel field through necessary study.” However, this choice simply says that the attorney should ask for a “two-week continuance.” It says nothing about whether this would be an adequate amount of time for the lawyer to study this particular area of the law. C is incorrect. A lawyer cannot represent a client if he or she is not competent to do so, even if the client consents to the representation. D is incorrect. A lawyer cannot represent a client if he or she is not competent to do so, even if the client consents in writing.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[197],"sourceAnswerPages":[294],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名最近刚取得执业资格的律师被法院指定，为一名被控重罪、无力支付律师费的被告提供代理。在与被告协商并尝试用两天准备审判后，律师确信自己缺乏有效代理被告所需的知识和经验。下列哪项做法适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"请求法院准许退出对被告的代理。","B":"请求延期两周，以获得更多时间准备审判。","C":"向被告说明情况；如被告同意，尽其所能继续代理。","D":"向被告说明情况；如被告书面同意，尽其所能继续代理。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》1.1 要求律师向委托人提供称职代理。本题中，律师认为自己不能提供称职代理，应请求法院准许退出。B 不正确。规则 1.1 评注 [2] 规定：“律师可以通过必要学习，在完全陌生的领域提供充分代理。”但该选项只是说律师应请求“延期两周”，没有说明这是否足以让律师学习这一特定法律领域。C 不正确。如果律师不具备称职能力，就不能代理委托人，即使委托人同意也不行。D 不正确。如果律师不具备称职能力，即使委托人书面同意也不能代理。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-049","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":49,"topic":"Rule 4.1(A)(12) / Rule 4.1","question":"A judge is presently serving on a state intermediate appellate court. This court, in opinions written by her, has decided several controversial cases in which the court has held that the Fourteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution does not guarantee due process protection to state prison inmates who are disciplined by prison authorities for violating the prison’s rules of conduct. The judge is now a candidate for election to a vacancy on the state supreme court. She is vigorously opposed by several organizations concerned with the conditions under which prisoners are incarcerated in the state’s prison. The judge is scheduled to be interviewed on television and has been informed that questions will be asked of her concerning those decisions and her attitude on the subject of prisoners’ rights. Which of the following is proper for the judge to say during the interview?","choices":{"A":"“I believe that the issues raised by the organizations opposing me are appropriate matters for legislative consideration.”","B":"“In my opinion, incarceration for the commission of a crime carries with it a loss of civil liberties in prison discipline proceedings.”","C":"“I am convinced I was right in those cases and will make the same decision in similar cases in the future.”","D":"“I would never rule against prison authorities because they have ultimate responsibility for the health and safety of prisoners.”"},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.1(A)(12), a judge or judicial candidate shall not “make any statement that would reasonably be expected to affect the outcome or impair the fairness of a matter pending or impending in any court.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.1 Comment [13] notes that “[t]he making of a pledge, promise, or commitment is not dependent upon, or limited to, the use of any specific words or phrases; instead, the totality of the statement must be examined to determine if a reasonable person would believe that the candidate for judicial office has specifically undertaken to reach a particular result. Pledges, promises, or commitments must be contrasted with statements or announcements of personal views on legal, political, or other issues, which are not prohibited. When making such statements, a judge should acknowledge the overarching judicial obligation to apply and uphold the law, without regard to his or her personal views.” Here, choice A is the only statement that does not appear to be promising a specific result. B is incorrect. This statement implies that the judge will automatically rule in such a way as to limit a prisoner’s civil liberties. C is incorrect. This statement implies that the judge will automatically make the same decision in similar cases without regard to the particular or unique facts of the case. D is incorrect. This statement explicitly states that the judge will never rule in favor of a prisoner.","rule":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.1(A)(12), a judge or judicial candidate shall not “make any statement that would reasonably be expected to affect the outcome or impair the fairness of a matter pending or impending in any court.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.1 Comment [13] notes that “[t]he making of a pledge, promise, or commitment is not dependent upon, or limited to, the use of any specific words or phrases; instead, the totality of the statement must be examined to determine if a reasonable person would believe that the candidate for judicial office has specifically undertaken to reach a particular result. Pledges, promises, or commitments must be contrasted with statements or announcements of personal views on legal, political, or other issues, which are not prohibited. When making such statements, a judge should acknowledge the overarching judicial obligation to apply and uphold the law, without regard to his or her personal views.” Here, choice A is the only statement that does not appear to be promising a specific result. B is incorrect. This statement implies that the judge will automatically rule in such a way as to limit a prisoner’s civil liberties. C is incorrect. This statement implies that the judge will automatically make the same decision in similar cases without regard to the particular or unique facts of the case. D is incorrect. This statement explicitly states that the judge will never rule in favor of a prisoner.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is proper for the judge to say during the interview?","rule":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.1(A)(12), a judge or judicial candidate shall not “make any statement that would reasonably be expected to affect the outcome or impair the fairness of a matter pending or impending in any court.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.1 Comment [13] notes that “[t]he making of a pledge, promise, or commitment is not dependent upon, or limited to, the use of any specific words or phrases; instead, the totality of the statement must be examined to determine if a reasonable person would believe that the candidate for judicial office has specifically undertaken to reach a particular result. Pledges, promises, or commitments must be contrasted with statements or announcements of personal views on legal, political, or other issues, which are not prohibited. When making such statements, a judge should acknowledge the overarching judicial obligation to apply and uphold the law, without regard to his or her personal views.” Here, choice A is the only statement that does not appear to be promising a specific result. B is incorrect. This statement implies that the judge will automatically rule in such a way as to limit a prisoner’s civil liberties. C is incorrect. This statement implies that the judge will automatically make the same decision in similar cases without regard to the particular or unique facts of the case. D is incorrect. This statement explicitly states that the judge will never rule in favor of a prisoner.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.1(A)(12), a judge or judicial candidate shall not “make any statement that would reasonably be expected to affect the outcome or impair the fairness of a matter pending or impending in any court.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.1 Comment [13] notes that “[t]he making of a pledge, promise, or commitment is not dependent upon, or limited to, the use of any specific words or phrases; instead, the totality of the statement must be examined to determine if a reasonable person would believe that the candidate for judicial office has specifically undertaken to reach a particular result. Pledges, promises, or commitments must be contrasted with statements or announcements of personal views on legal, political, or other issues, which are not prohibited. When making such statements, a judge should acknowledge the overarching judicial obligation to apply and uphold the law, without regard to his or her personal views.” Here, choice A is the only statement that does not appear to be promising a specific result. B is incorrect. This statement implies that the judge will automatically rule in such a way as to limit a prisoner’s civil liberties. C is incorrect. This statement implies that the judge will automatically make the same decision in similar cases without regard to the particular or unique facts of the case. D is incorrect. This statement explicitly states that the judge will never rule in favor of a prisoner.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 4.1(A)(12)","Rule 4.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[197,198],"sourceAnswerPages":[294,295],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名法官现任州中级上诉法院法官。该法院在她撰写的数份判决意见中裁定，《美国宪法第十四修正案》不保障州监狱囚犯在因违反监狱行为规则而受到监狱机关纪律处分时享有正当程序保护。这些案件颇具争议。法官目前正竞选州最高法院的一个空缺职位，受到数个关注本州囚犯监禁条件之组织的强烈反对。法官即将接受电视采访，并已获告知，采访会询问有关这些判决及她对囚犯权利的态度。法官在采访中作出下列哪项陈述适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"“我认为反对我的组织提出的问题，适合由立法机关考虑。”","B":"“在我看来，因犯罪受到监禁，就同时意味着在监狱纪律程序中丧失公民自由。”","C":"“我确信自己在那些案件中的裁判正确，未来在类似案件中也会作出相同裁判。”","D":"“我绝不会作出不利于监狱机关的裁判，因为它们对囚犯健康和安全承担最终责任。”"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。根据《司法行为示范准则》规则 4.1(A)(12)，法官或司法职位候选人不得“作出可被合理预期会影响任何法院中待决或即将发生事项之结果，或损害其公平性的陈述”。规则 4.1 评注 [13] 指出：“作出保证、承诺或许诺并不取决于或限于使用任何特定词语；应审视陈述整体，以判断理性人是否会认为司法职位候选人已明确承诺取得某一结果。保证、承诺或许诺必须与有关法律、政治或其他问题的个人观点陈述或公告相区别，后者并不受禁止。法官作出这类陈述时，应承认不顾个人观点、适用并维护法律这一最高司法义务。”本题中，只有 A 看起来没有承诺具体结果。B 不正确。该陈述暗示法官会自动以限制囚犯公民自由的方式裁判。C 不正确。该陈述暗示法官会不考虑案件的具体或独特事实，在类似案件中自动作出相同裁判。D 不正确。该陈述明确表示法官绝不会作出有利于囚犯的裁判。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-050","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":50,"topic":"Model Rule 1.9(a) / Model Rule 1.10(a)","question":"An attorney was formerly employed by an insurance company as a lawyer who solely handled fire insurance claims. While so employed, she investigated a man’s fire loss claim against the insurance company. The attorney is now in private practice. The claim remains unsettled and the man consults the attorney and asks the attorney to represent him for a suit on the claim. Which of the following would be proper for the attorney to do?","choices":{"A":"Refuse to discuss the matter with the man.","B":"Represent the man.","C":"Refer the man to an associate in her law firm.","D":"Refer the man to an associate in her law firm, provided the attorney does not share in any fee."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client unless the former client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing.” Model Rule 1.9(a). Here, since the attorney formerly represented the insurance company in the same matter, she cannot represent the man. Choice A is the only choice that reflects this Rule. B is incorrect. As stated above, the Rules prohibit the attorney from representing the man. C is incorrect. Model Rule 1.10(a) states that, except in circumstances not implicated here, lawyers who are associated in a firm shall not knowingly represent a client when any one of them practicing alone would be prohibited from doing so by Rules 1.7 or 1.9. Consequently, an associate in the attorney’s firm could not take the case. D is incorrect. The prohibition in Model Rule 1.10(a) applies whether or not the attorney shares in the fee.","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client unless the former client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing.” Model Rule 1.9(a). Here, since the attorney formerly represented the insurance company in the same matter, she cannot represent the man. Choice A is the only choice that reflects this Rule. B is incorrect. As stated above, the Rules prohibit the attorney from representing the man. C is incorrect. Model Rule 1.10(a) states that, except in circumstances not implicated here, lawyers who are associated in a firm shall not knowingly represent a client when any one of them practicing alone would be prohibited from doing so by Rules 1.7 or 1.9. Consequently, an associate in the attorney’s firm could not take the case. D is incorrect. The prohibition in Model Rule 1.10(a) applies whether or not the attorney shares in the fee.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following would be proper for the attorney to do?","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client unless the former client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing.” Model Rule 1.9(a). Here, since the attorney formerly represented the insurance company in the same matter, she cannot represent the man. Choice A is the only choice that reflects this Rule. B is incorrect. As stated above, the Rules prohibit the attorney from representing the man. C is incorrect. Model Rule 1.10(a) states that, except in circumstances not implicated here, lawyers who are associated in a firm shall not knowingly represent a client when any one of them practicing alone would be prohibited from doing so by Rules 1.7 or 1.9. Consequently, an associate in the attorney’s firm could not take the case. D is incorrect. The prohibition in Model Rule 1.10(a) applies whether or not the attorney shares in the fee.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client unless the former client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing.” Model Rule 1.9(a). Here, since the attorney formerly represented the insurance company in the same matter, she cannot represent the man. Choice A is the only choice that reflects this Rule. B is incorrect. As stated above, the Rules prohibit the attorney from representing the man. C is incorrect. Model Rule 1.10(a) states that, except in circumstances not implicated here, lawyers who are associated in a firm shall not knowingly represent a client when any one of them practicing alone would be prohibited from doing so by Rules 1.7 or 1.9. Consequently, an associate in the attorney’s firm could not take the case. D is incorrect. The prohibition in Model Rule 1.10(a) applies whether or not the attorney shares in the fee.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.9(a)","Model Rule 1.10(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[198],"sourceAnswerPages":[295,296],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师过去受雇于一家保险公司，作为律师专门处理火灾保险索赔。受雇期间，她调查过一名男子针对保险公司提出的火灾损失索赔。该律师现已私人执业。索赔仍未解决，男子咨询该律师，请求她代理自己就该索赔提起诉讼。律师采取下列哪项做法适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"拒绝与男子讨论该事项。","B":"代理男子。","C":"把男子转介给本事务所的一名助理律师。","D":"把男子转介给本事务所的一名助理律师，但该律师不分享任何费用。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。“律师先前曾就某一事项代理委托人的，此后不得在同一事项或与其实质相关的事项中，代理其利益与前委托人存在重大不利关系的另一人，除非前委托人作出经书面确认的知情同意。”《职业行为示范规则》1.9(a)。本题中，律师先前曾在同一事项中代理保险公司，因此不能代理该男子。只有 A 体现了这项规则。B 不正确。如上所述，规则禁止律师代理男子。C 不正确。规则 1.10(a) 规定，除本题不涉及的例外外，同一事务所的律师不得明知而代理以下委托人：如果其中任何一名律师单独执业，会因规则 1.7 或 1.9 而受到禁止。因此，该律师所在事务所的助理律师也不能接受案件。D 不正确。无论该律师是否分享费用，规则 1.10(a) 的禁止均适用。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-051","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":51,"topic":"Model Rule 1.9(a)","question":"An attorney worked at an electric company. While there, he worked on a claim against the company brought by a woman for sexual harassment. That claim was settled and the attorney left the company and went into private practice. Last week, the woman slipped and fell in the company’s offices. She now asks the attorney to represent her. Which of the following would be proper for the attorney to do?","choices":{"A":"Refuse to discuss the matter with the woman.","B":"Represent the woman.","C":"Give the woman a list of lawyers who the attorney knows are competent and specialize in such claims.","D":"All of the above."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client unless the former client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing.” Model Rule 1.9(a). Here, the important point is that the two matters, a sexual harassment claim and a slip-and-fall claim, are not substantially related. Consequently, the attorney can represent the woman if he wants to, refuse to discuss the matter with her, or simply give her a list of competent lawyers. A, B, and C are therefore incorrect.","rule":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client unless the former client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing.” Model Rule 1.9(a). Here, the important point is that the two matters, a sexual harassment claim and a slip-and-fall claim, are not substantially related. Consequently, the attorney can represent the woman if he wants to, refuse to discuss the matter with her, or simply give her a list of competent lawyers. A, B, and C are therefore incorrect.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following would be proper for the attorney to do?","rule":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client unless the former client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing.” Model Rule 1.9(a). Here, the important point is that the two matters, a sexual harassment claim and a slip-and-fall claim, are not substantially related. Consequently, the attorney can represent the woman if he wants to, refuse to discuss the matter with her, or simply give her a list of competent lawyers. A, B, and C are therefore incorrect.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer who has formerly represented a client in a matter shall not thereafter represent another person in the same or a substantially related matter in which that person’s interests are materially adverse to the interests of the former client unless the former client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing.” Model Rule 1.9(a). Here, the important point is that the two matters, a sexual harassment claim and a slip-and-fall claim, are not substantially related. Consequently, the attorney can represent the woman if he wants to, refuse to discuss the matter with her, or simply give her a list of competent lawyers. A, B, and C are therefore incorrect.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.9(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[198,199],"sourceAnswerPages":[296],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师曾在一家电力公司工作。在职期间，他办理过一名女子因性骚扰对公司提出的索赔。该索赔已经和解，律师离开公司开始私人执业。上周，该女子在公司办公室滑倒受伤。她现在请求该律师代理自己。律师采取下列哪项做法适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"拒绝与女子讨论该事项。","B":"代理女子。","C":"向女子提供一份律师名单，其中都是该律师知道称职且专门办理此类索赔的人。","D":"以上均可。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。“律师先前曾就某一事项代理委托人的，此后不得在同一事项或与其实质相关的事项中，代理其利益与前委托人存在重大不利关系的另一人，除非前委托人作出经书面确认的知情同意。”《职业行为示范规则》1.9(a)。本题关键是性骚扰索赔与滑倒索赔并非实质相关。因此，律师可以自行选择代理女子、拒绝讨论该事项，或者只是向她提供称职律师名单。所以，A、B、C 均不是单独的最佳答案。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-052","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":52,"topic":"Model Rule 1.16 / Model Rule 1.6(a) / Model Rule 1.8(i)","question":"A man has retained an attorney to represent him in a contract suit. The attorney’s retainer agreement provided that the attorney’s fees would be based on a fixed hourly rate, payable at the end of each calendar month. Two months before trial, the man fell behind in the payment of the attorney’s monthly billing for fees. The attorney included the following statement on the attorney’s last billing to the man: “Your account is more than 30 days past due. If amounts due are not paid promptly in accordance with our agreement, I will terminate the representation. If you cannot pay the amount due, I will accept an assignment of your cause of action as security for your fee to me.” Two weeks after the last billing, the attorney telephoned the man and told him that the attorney would withdraw from representing the man if the bill was not paid within 48 hours or adequate security given for its payment. If the bill remains unpaid or unsecured after 48 hours, it would be proper for the attorney to:","choices":{"A":"Upon notice to the man, move the court for permission to withdraw.","B":"Turn the man’s file over to another experienced lawyer in town and notify the man that the attorney no longer represents him.","C":"Accept an assignment of the man’s cause of action as security for the attorney’s fee.","D":"Stop work on the case until the man pays his bill."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may withdraw from representation if the client fails to abide by the terms of an agreement concerning fees. Model Rule 1.16 Comment [8]. Consequently, the attorney may ask the court for permission to withdraw. B is incorrect. Model Rule 1.6(a) states that “[a] lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent.” Here, the client has not given informed consent, and no exception to the Rule applies, so the attorney should not pass the file onto another lawyer. C is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.8(i), a lawyer is prohibited from acquiring a proprietary interest in the cause of action. Thus, the attorney cannot accept an assignment from the client as security. D is incorrect. If the attorney simply stopped working on the case, he would violate Model Rule 1.3’s requirement of reasonable diligence in representing a client.","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may withdraw from representation if the client fails to abide by the terms of an agreement concerning fees. Model Rule 1.16 Comment [8]. Consequently, the attorney may ask the court for permission to withdraw. B is incorrect. Model Rule 1.6(a) states that “[a] lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent.” Here, the client has not given informed consent, and no exception to the Rule applies, so the attorney should not pass the file onto another lawyer. C is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.8(i), a lawyer is prohibited from acquiring a proprietary interest in the cause of action. Thus, the attorney cannot accept an assignment from the client as security. D is incorrect. If the attorney simply stopped working on the case, he would violate Model Rule 1.3’s requirement of reasonable diligence in representing a client.","irac":{"issue":"A man has retained an attorney to represent him in a contract suit. The attorney’s retainer agreement provided that the attorney’s fees would be based on a fixed hourly rate, payable at the end of each calendar month. Two months before trial, the man fell behind in the payment of the attorney’s monthly billing for fees. The attorney included the following statement on the attorney’s last billing to the man: “Your account is more than 30 days past due. If amounts due are not paid promptly in accordance with our agreement, I will terminate the representation. If you cannot pay the amount due, I will accept an assignment of your cause of action as security for your fee to me.” Two weeks after the last billing, the attorney telephoned the man and told him that the attorney would withdraw from representing the man if the bill was not paid within 48 hours or adequate security given for its payment. If the bill remains unpaid or unsecured after 48 hours, it would be proper for the attorney to:","rule":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may withdraw from representation if the client fails to abide by the terms of an agreement concerning fees. Model Rule 1.16 Comment [8]. Consequently, the attorney may ask the court for permission to withdraw. B is incorrect. Model Rule 1.6(a) states that “[a] lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent.” Here, the client has not given informed consent, and no exception to the Rule applies, so the attorney should not pass the file onto another lawyer. C is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.8(i), a lawyer is prohibited from acquiring a proprietary interest in the cause of action. Thus, the attorney cannot accept an assignment from the client as security. D is incorrect. If the attorney simply stopped working on the case, he would violate Model Rule 1.3’s requirement of reasonable diligence in representing a client.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. A lawyer may withdraw from representation if the client fails to abide by the terms of an agreement concerning fees. Model Rule 1.16 Comment [8]. Consequently, the attorney may ask the court for permission to withdraw. B is incorrect. Model Rule 1.6(a) states that “[a] lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent.” Here, the client has not given informed consent, and no exception to the Rule applies, so the attorney should not pass the file onto another lawyer. C is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.8(i), a lawyer is prohibited from acquiring a proprietary interest in the cause of action. Thus, the attorney cannot accept an assignment from the client as security. D is incorrect. If the attorney simply stopped working on the case, he would violate Model Rule 1.3’s requirement of reasonable diligence in representing a client.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.16","Model Rule 1.6(a)","Model Rule 1.8(i)","Model Rule 1.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[199],"sourceAnswerPages":[296],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名男子聘请律师代理合同诉讼。聘用协议规定，律师费按固定小时费率计算，并在每个公历月末支付。审判前两个月，男子开始拖欠律师的月度账单。律师在最后一份账单中写道：“您的账户已逾期超过 30 日。如未按照协议迅速支付到期款项，我将终止代理。如果您无法支付，我愿接受您的诉因转让，作为您向我支付律师费的担保。”最后账单发出两周后，律师致电男子，告知如果在 48 小时内不支付账单或提供充分付款担保，就会退出代理。如果 48 小时后账单仍未支付，也没有担保，律师采取下列哪项做法适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"通知男子后，向法院申请准许退出。","B":"把男子案卷交给本市另一名经验丰富的律师，并通知男子自己不再代理。","C":"接受男子转让诉因，作为律师费担保。","D":"在男子支付账单前停止处理案件。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。如果委托人不遵守有关费用协议的条款，律师可以退出代理。《职业行为示范规则》1.16 评注 [8]。因此，律师可以请求法院准许退出。B 不正确。规则 1.6(a) 规定：“除非委托人作出知情同意，律师不得披露与委托人代理有关的信息。”本题中委托人没有作出知情同意，也没有任何例外适用，所以律师不应把案卷转交另一名律师。C 不正确。规则 1.8(i) 禁止律师取得诉因的所有权利益。因此，律师不能接受委托人转让诉因作为担保。D 不正确。如果律师直接停止处理案件，就会违反规则 1.3 关于以合理勤勉程度代理委托人的要求。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-053","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":53,"topic":"Model Rule 7.2","question":"An attorney’s advertisement in the local newspaper includes the following information, all of which is true: “I have a B.A., magna cum laude, Eastern College; J.D., summa cum laude, State Law School; LL.M., Eastern Law School. My offices are open Monday through Friday from 9:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m., but you may call my answering service 24 hours a day, seven days a week. I speak modern Greek fluently.” For which, if any, of these statements is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"“I speak modern Greek fluently.”","B":"“J.D., summa cum laude, State Law School.”","C":"“You may call my answering service 24 hours a day.”","D":"None of the statements in the advertisement subjects the attorney to discipline."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 7.2 Comment [2] “permits public dissemination of information concerning a lawyer’s name or firm name, address, email address, website, and telephone number; the kinds of services the lawyer will undertake; the basis on which the lawyer’s fees are determined, including prices for specific services and payment and credit arrangements; a lawyer’s foreign language ability; names of references and, with their consent, names of clients regularly represented; and other information that might invite the attention of those seeking legal assistance.” There is no prohibition regarding listing a lawyer’s degrees or encouraging potential clients to leave voice messages. Therefore, A, B, and C are incorrect.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 7.2 Comment [2] “permits public dissemination of information concerning a lawyer’s name or firm name, address, email address, website, and telephone number; the kinds of services the lawyer will undertake; the basis on which the lawyer’s fees are determined, including prices for specific services and payment and credit arrangements; a lawyer’s foreign language ability; names of references and, with their consent, names of clients regularly represented; and other information that might invite the attention of those seeking legal assistance.” There is no prohibition regarding listing a lawyer’s degrees or encouraging potential clients to leave voice messages. Therefore, A, B, and C are incorrect.","irac":{"issue":"” For which, if any, of these statements is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 7.2 Comment [2] “permits public dissemination of information concerning a lawyer’s name or firm name, address, email address, website, and telephone number; the kinds of services the lawyer will undertake; the basis on which the lawyer’s fees are determined, including prices for specific services and payment and credit arrangements; a lawyer’s foreign language ability; names of references and, with their consent, names of clients regularly represented; and other information that might invite the attention of those seeking legal assistance.” There is no prohibition regarding listing a lawyer’s degrees or encouraging potential clients to leave voice messages. Therefore, A, B, and C are incorrect.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 7.2 Comment [2] “permits public dissemination of information concerning a lawyer’s name or firm name, address, email address, website, and telephone number; the kinds of services the lawyer will undertake; the basis on which the lawyer’s fees are determined, including prices for specific services and payment and credit arrangements; a lawyer’s foreign language ability; names of references and, with their consent, names of clients regularly represented; and other information that might invite the attention of those seeking legal assistance.” There is no prohibition regarding listing a lawyer’s degrees or encouraging potential clients to leave voice messages. Therefore, A, B, and C are incorrect.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.2"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[199,200],"sourceAnswerPages":[296,297],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师在当地报纸广告中包含以下信息，且全部属实：“本人获东方学院文学学士学位，最优等荣誉；州立法学院法学博士学位，最优等荣誉；东方法学院法学硕士学位。本人办公室周一至周五上午 9 时至下午 5 时营业，但您每周七天、每天 24 小时均可致电留言服务。本人可流利使用现代希腊语。”上述哪项陈述会使律师应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"“本人可流利使用现代希腊语。”","B":"“州立法学院法学博士学位，最优等荣誉。”","C":"“您每天 24 小时均可致电留言服务。”","D":"广告中没有任何陈述会使律师应受纪律处分。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》7.2 评注 [2]“允许公开传播有关律师姓名或事务所名称、地址、电子邮件地址、网站和电话号码；律师愿意承办的服务种类；律师收费确定依据，包括特定服务价格及付款和信用安排；律师的外语能力；推荐人姓名，以及在取得同意后经常代理之委托人的姓名；以及其他可能引起寻求法律帮助之人注意的信息。”规则没有禁止列明律师学位，也没有禁止鼓励潜在委托人留下语音信息。因此，A、B、C 均不正确。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-054","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":54,"topic":"Model Rule 4.2","question":"An attorney represents a man in bitter and protracted litigation. The attorney at the man’s request, has made several offers of settlement to plaintiff’s lawyer, all of which have been rejected. During a week’s recess in the trial, the attorney and the plaintiff were both present at a cocktail party. The plaintiff went over to the attorney and said: “Why can’t we settle this case for $50,000? This trial is costing both sides more than it’s worth.” Which of the following is a proper response by the attorney?","choices":{"A":"“I can’t discuss the matter with you.”","B":"“If that’s the way you feel, why don’t you and my client get together?”","C":"“I agree. We’ve already made several offers to settle you might not know about.”","D":"“Let me get confirmation from my client, but I think this settlement is appropriate.”"},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 4.2 prohibits a lawyer from communicating about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows is represented by another lawyer in the matter. This prohibition applies even if the represented person is the one to initiate the communication. Model Rule 4.2 Comment [3]. B is incorrect. Although the plaintiff could speak directly with the man if he chose to do so, the attorney cannot give him that advice since he knows the plaintiff is represented by another lawyer. C is incorrect. Even if the attorney has made several settlement offers, this does not allow him to speak with the plaintiff. D is incorrect. Even if the attorney believes the settlement is appropriate, he still cannot speak to the plaintiff.","rule":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 4.2 prohibits a lawyer from communicating about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows is represented by another lawyer in the matter. This prohibition applies even if the represented person is the one to initiate the communication. Model Rule 4.2 Comment [3]. B is incorrect. Although the plaintiff could speak directly with the man if he chose to do so, the attorney cannot give him that advice since he knows the plaintiff is represented by another lawyer. C is incorrect. Even if the attorney has made several settlement offers, this does not allow him to speak with the plaintiff. D is incorrect. Even if the attorney believes the settlement is appropriate, he still cannot speak to the plaintiff.","irac":{"issue":"” Which of the following is a proper response by the attorney?","rule":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 4.2 prohibits a lawyer from communicating about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows is represented by another lawyer in the matter. This prohibition applies even if the represented person is the one to initiate the communication. Model Rule 4.2 Comment [3]. B is incorrect. Although the plaintiff could speak directly with the man if he chose to do so, the attorney cannot give him that advice since he knows the plaintiff is represented by another lawyer. C is incorrect. Even if the attorney has made several settlement offers, this does not allow him to speak with the plaintiff. D is incorrect. Even if the attorney believes the settlement is appropriate, he still cannot speak to the plaintiff.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 4.2 prohibits a lawyer from communicating about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows is represented by another lawyer in the matter. This prohibition applies even if the represented person is the one to initiate the communication. Model Rule 4.2 Comment [3]. B is incorrect. Although the plaintiff could speak directly with the man if he chose to do so, the attorney cannot give him that advice since he knows the plaintiff is represented by another lawyer. C is incorrect. Even if the attorney has made several settlement offers, this does not allow him to speak with the plaintiff. D is incorrect. Even if the attorney believes the settlement is appropriate, he still cannot speak to the plaintiff.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 4.2"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[200],"sourceAnswerPages":[297],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理一名男子参加激烈而漫长的诉讼。依男子请求，律师多次向原告律师提出和解要约，均被拒绝。审判休庭一周期间，律师和原告同时参加一场鸡尾酒会。原告走到律师面前说：“为什么我们不能以 50,000 美元和解？这场审判给双方造成的成本已经超过案件价值。”律师作出下列哪项回应适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"“我不能和你讨论这件事。”","B":"“既然你这样想，为什么不和我的委托人直接谈谈？”","C":"“我同意。我们已经提出数个你可能不知道的和解要约。”","D":"“让我先向委托人确认，但我认为这项和解适当。”"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》4.2 禁止律师就代理事项与其明知在该事项中由另一名律师代理的人沟通。即使沟通由已有律师代理的人发起，该禁令仍然适用。规则 4.2 评注 [3]。B 不正确。原告虽然可以自行选择直接与男子交谈，但律师知道原告已有律师代理，不能向原告提供这项建议。C 不正确。即使律师已经提出数个和解要约，也不允许其与原告交谈。D 不正确。即使律师认为和解适当，仍不能与原告交谈。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-055","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":55,"topic":"Model Rule 7.2","question":"The law firm of Alpha and Beta has a radio commercial which states: “Do you have a legal problem? Are you being sued? Consult Alpha and Beta, licensed attorneys at law. Initial conference charge is $25 for one hour. Act now and protect your interests. Call at 1234 Main Street; telephone area code (555) 555-5555.” All the statements in the advertisement are true. The commercial can be heard in several neighboring states. Are Alpha and Beta subject to discipline for the commercial?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the qualifications of the lawyers are not stated.","B":"Yes, because the radio broadcast may encourage litigation.","C":"Yes, because the radio broadcast is heard outside the state in which they are licensed","D":"No, because all of the statements in the radio broadcast are true."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Model Rules 7.1 and 7.2 allow lawyers to have radio advertisements as long as the statements made in the advertisement are truthful. Model Rule 7.2 Comment [2] notes that “[t]his Rule permits public dissemination of information concerning a lawyer’s name or firm name, address, email address, website, and telephone number; the kinds of services the lawyer will undertake; the basis on which the lawyer’s fees are determined, including prices for specific services and payment and credit arrangements; a lawyer’s foreign language ability; names of references and, with their consent, names of clients regularly represented; and other information that might invite the attention of those seeking legal assistance.” A is incorrect. Although the lawyers could include their qualifications in the radio advertisement, they are not required to do so. B is incorrect. A close reading of the advertisement shows that it is not actually encouraging litigation. It merely states that if a person is being sued or has another legal problem, that person should contact the attorneys for help. Model Rule 7.2 Comment [1] notes that “[t]o assist the public in learning about and obtaining legal services, lawyers should be allowed to make known their services not only through reputation but also through organized information campaigns in the form of advertising. Advertising involves an active quest for clients, contrary to the tradition that a lawyer should not seek clientele.” C is incorrect. Even though the radio advertisement can be heard outside of the state in which the lawyers are licensed, this does not cause the advertisement to violate the Rules. The question here is clearly focused on the content of the lawyers’ message.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Model Rules 7.1 and 7.2 allow lawyers to have radio advertisements as long as the statements made in the advertisement are truthful. Model Rule 7.2 Comment [2] notes that “[t]his Rule permits public dissemination of information concerning a lawyer’s name or firm name, address, email address, website, and telephone number; the kinds of services the lawyer will undertake; the basis on which the lawyer’s fees are determined, including prices for specific services and payment and credit arrangements; a lawyer’s foreign language ability; names of references and, with their consent, names of clients regularly represented; and other information that might invite the attention of those seeking legal assistance.” A is incorrect. Although the lawyers could include their qualifications in the radio advertisement, they are not required to do so. B is incorrect. A close reading of the advertisement shows that it is not actually encouraging litigation. It merely states that if a person is being sued or has another legal problem, that person should contact the attorneys for help. Model Rule 7.2 Comment [1] notes that “[t]o assist the public in learning about and obtaining legal services, lawyers should be allowed to make known their services not only through reputation but also through organized information campaigns in the form of advertising. Advertising involves an active quest for clients, contrary to the tradition that a lawyer should not seek clientele.” C is incorrect. Even though the radio advertisement can be heard outside of the state in which the lawyers are licensed, this does not cause the advertisement to violate the Rules. The question here is clearly focused on the content of the lawyers’ message.","irac":{"issue":"Are Alpha and Beta subject to discipline for the commercial?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Model Rules 7.1 and 7.2 allow lawyers to have radio advertisements as long as the statements made in the advertisement are truthful. Model Rule 7.2 Comment [2] notes that “[t]his Rule permits public dissemination of information concerning a lawyer’s name or firm name, address, email address, website, and telephone number; the kinds of services the lawyer will undertake; the basis on which the lawyer’s fees are determined, including prices for specific services and payment and credit arrangements; a lawyer’s foreign language ability; names of references and, with their consent, names of clients regularly represented; and other information that might invite the attention of those seeking legal assistance.” A is incorrect. Although the lawyers could include their qualifications in the radio advertisement, they are not required to do so. B is incorrect. A close reading of the advertisement shows that it is not actually encouraging litigation. It merely states that if a person is being sued or has another legal problem, that person should contact the attorneys for help. Model Rule 7.2 Comment [1] notes that “[t]o assist the public in learning about and obtaining legal services, lawyers should be allowed to make known their services not only through reputation but also through organized information campaigns in the form of advertising. Advertising involves an active quest for clients, contrary to the tradition that a lawyer should not seek clientele.” C is incorrect. Even though the radio advertisement can be heard outside of the state in which the lawyers are licensed, this does not cause the advertisement to violate the Rules. The question here is clearly focused on the content of the lawyers’ message.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Model Rules 7.1 and 7.2 allow lawyers to have radio advertisements as long as the statements made in the advertisement are truthful. Model Rule 7.2 Comment [2] notes that “[t]his Rule permits public dissemination of information concerning a lawyer’s name or firm name, address, email address, website, and telephone number; the kinds of services the lawyer will undertake; the basis on which the lawyer’s fees are determined, including prices for specific services and payment and credit arrangements; a lawyer’s foreign language ability; names of references and, with their consent, names of clients regularly represented; and other information that might invite the attention of those seeking legal assistance.” A is incorrect. Although the lawyers could include their qualifications in the radio advertisement, they are not required to do so. B is incorrect. A close reading of the advertisement shows that it is not actually encouraging litigation. It merely states that if a person is being sued or has another legal problem, that person should contact the attorneys for help. Model Rule 7.2 Comment [1] notes that “[t]o assist the public in learning about and obtaining legal services, lawyers should be allowed to make known their services not only through reputation but also through organized information campaigns in the form of advertising. Advertising involves an active quest for clients, contrary to the tradition that a lawyer should not seek clientele.” C is incorrect. Even though the radio advertisement can be heard outside of the state in which the lawyers are licensed, this does not cause the advertisement to violate the Rules. The question here is clearly focused on the content of the lawyers’ message.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.2"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[200,201],"sourceAnswerPages":[297,298],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"甲乙律师事务所播放一则广播广告：“您遇到法律问题了吗？您正在被起诉吗？请咨询甲乙律师事务所的持照执业律师。首次咨询一小时收费 25 美元。立即行动，保护您的权益。地址：大街 1234 号；电话区号 (555) 555-5555。”广告中全部陈述均真实。广播在数个邻近州也能收听。甲和乙是否因该广告而应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为广告没有说明律师资历。","B":"是，因为广播可能鼓励诉讼。","C":"是，因为在律师取得执业资格之州以外也能收听广播。","D":"否，因为广播中全部陈述均真实。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》7.1 和 7.2 允许律师投放广播广告，只要其中陈述真实。规则 7.2 评注 [2] 指出：“本规则允许公开传播有关律师姓名或事务所名称、地址、电子邮件地址、网站和电话号码；律师愿意承办的服务种类；律师收费确定依据，包括特定服务价格及付款和信用安排；律师的外语能力；推荐人姓名，以及在取得同意后经常代理之委托人的姓名；以及其他可能引起寻求法律帮助之人注意的信息。”A 不正确。律师虽然可以在广播广告中列明资历，却没有义务这样做。B 不正确。仔细阅读广告可知，它实际上没有鼓励诉讼，只是说如果某人正在被起诉或有其他法律问题，应联系律师寻求帮助。规则 7.2 评注 [1] 指出：“为帮助公众了解并取得法律服务，应允许律师不仅通过声誉，也通过广告形式的有组织信息宣传让公众知悉其服务。广告包含主动寻求委托人，这与律师不应招揽业务的传统相反。”C 不正确。即使在律师取得执业资格的州以外也能听到广播，也不会因此使广告违反规则。本题显然关注律师信息的内容。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-056","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":56,"topic":"Model Rule 8.3(a) / Model Rule 8.3(c) / Model Rule 1.6","question":"Attorney Alpha was retained by a client to incorporate a client’s business, which previously had been operated as a sole proprietorship. Alpha noticed in the client’s file copies of some correspondence from the client to Attorney Beta concerning the possibility of Beta’s incorporating the client’s business. Alpha questioned the client to make certain that any attorney-client relationship between Beta and the client had been terminated. The client told Alpha, “It certainly has been terminated. When I discussed the matter with Beta six months ago, he asked for a retainer of $1,000, which I paid him. He did absolutely nothing after he got the money, even though I called him weekly, and finally, last week when I again complained, he returned the retainer, which satisfied me. Don’t say anything about it because Beta is an old friend of my family.” Based on what the client told her, Alpha believes that Beta clearly was guilty of professional misconduct, although she didn’t think he usually neglected cases entrusted to him. Is Alpha subject to discipline if she does not report her knowledge of Beta’s conduct to the appropriate authority?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because Alpha believes Beta clearly was guilty of professional misconduct.","B":"Yes, because Alpha believes Beta does not usually neglect matters entrusted to him.","C":"No, because the client was satisfied by Beta’s return of the retainer.","D":"No, because the client does not want Alpha to report the information."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.3(a) requires that a lawyer report violations of the Rules that raise “a substantial question as to that lawyer’s honesty, trustworthiness, or fitness as a lawyer.” However, Model Rule 8.3(c) states that disclosure is not required if the information is protected by Model Rule 1.6’s confidentiality rules. Here, the client told the attorney in confidence and does not want her to reveal the information. Consequently, the attorney is not required to report it. A is incorrect. Even though the attorney believes Beta was guilty of professional misconduct, reporting the information would require revealing the client’s confidential information. Importantly, the information does not involve the possibility of substantial harm or any other exception under Model Rule 1.6. B is incorrect. Beta’s normal course of action would not affect the analysis of this question. Importantly, the question is focused on this particular instance of Beta’s misconduct. C is incorrect. Although the client was satisfied with the return of the retainer, the question is really asking about whether the attorney needs to reveal her client’s confidential information.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.3(a) requires that a lawyer report violations of the Rules that raise “a substantial question as to that lawyer’s honesty, trustworthiness, or fitness as a lawyer.” However, Model Rule 8.3(c) states that disclosure is not required if the information is protected by Model Rule 1.6’s confidentiality rules. Here, the client told the attorney in confidence and does not want her to reveal the information. Consequently, the attorney is not required to report it. A is incorrect. Even though the attorney believes Beta was guilty of professional misconduct, reporting the information would require revealing the client’s confidential information. Importantly, the information does not involve the possibility of substantial harm or any other exception under Model Rule 1.6. B is incorrect. Beta’s normal course of action would not affect the analysis of this question. Importantly, the question is focused on this particular instance of Beta’s misconduct. C is incorrect. Although the client was satisfied with the return of the retainer, the question is really asking about whether the attorney needs to reveal her client’s confidential information.","irac":{"issue":"Is Alpha subject to discipline if she does not report her knowledge of Beta’s conduct to the appropriate authority?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.3(a) requires that a lawyer report violations of the Rules that raise “a substantial question as to that lawyer’s honesty, trustworthiness, or fitness as a lawyer.” However, Model Rule 8.3(c) states that disclosure is not required if the information is protected by Model Rule 1.6’s confidentiality rules. Here, the client told the attorney in confidence and does not want her to reveal the information. Consequently, the attorney is not required to report it. A is incorrect. Even though the attorney believes Beta was guilty of professional misconduct, reporting the information would require revealing the client’s confidential information. Importantly, the information does not involve the possibility of substantial harm or any other exception under Model Rule 1.6. B is incorrect. Beta’s normal course of action would not affect the analysis of this question. Importantly, the question is focused on this particular instance of Beta’s misconduct. C is incorrect. Although the client was satisfied with the return of the retainer, the question is really asking about whether the attorney needs to reveal her client’s confidential information.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.3(a) requires that a lawyer report violations of the Rules that raise “a substantial question as to that lawyer’s honesty, trustworthiness, or fitness as a lawyer.” However, Model Rule 8.3(c) states that disclosure is not required if the information is protected by Model Rule 1.6’s confidentiality rules. Here, the client told the attorney in confidence and does not want her to reveal the information. Consequently, the attorney is not required to report it. A is incorrect. Even though the attorney believes Beta was guilty of professional misconduct, reporting the information would require revealing the client’s confidential information. Importantly, the information does not involve the possibility of substantial harm or any other exception under Model Rule 1.6. B is incorrect. Beta’s normal course of action would not affect the analysis of this question. Importantly, the question is focused on this particular instance of Beta’s misconduct. C is incorrect. Although the client was satisfied with the return of the retainer, the question is really asking about whether the attorney needs to reveal her client’s confidential information.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 8.3(a)","Model Rule 8.3(c)","Model Rule 1.6"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[201],"sourceAnswerPages":[298],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"律师甲受聘将一名委托人先前以独资企业形式经营的业务改组为公司。甲在委托人案卷中看到一些委托人与律师乙之间关于可能由乙办理公司设立的通信副本。甲询问委托人，以确认乙与委托人之间的任何律师—委托人关系已经终止。委托人告诉甲：“当然已经终止。六个月前我和乙讨论此事时，他要求 1,000 美元预付费用，我付了钱。他拿到钱后完全没有做任何工作，尽管我每周都打电话。最后，上周我再次投诉时，他退还了预付款，我也就满意了。不要说出去，因为乙是我家的老朋友。”根据委托人所说，甲相信乙显然实施了职业不当行为，尽管她不认为乙通常会疏于办理受托事务。如果甲不向适当机关举报自己知道的乙之行为，是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为甲相信乙显然实施了职业不当行为。","B":"是，因为甲相信乙通常不会疏于办理受托事务。","C":"否，因为乙退还预付款后，委托人已经满意。","D":"否，因为委托人不希望甲举报该信息。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》8.3(a) 要求律师举报对另一名律师之“诚实、可信或是否适任产生重大疑问”的违反规则行为。不过，规则 8.3(c) 规定，如果信息受到规则 1.6 保密规则保护，就无需披露。本题中，委托人以保密方式告知律师，也不希望她披露。因此，律师无须举报。A 不正确。即使律师相信乙实施了职业不当行为，举报仍需披露委托人保密信息。尤其是，该信息不涉及重大伤害可能性，也不符合规则 1.6 的任何其他例外。B 不正确。乙通常如何行事不影响本题分析。本题关注的是乙这一次的具体不当行为。C 不正确。委托人虽然因预付款退还而满意，但真正问题是律师是否需要披露委托人的保密信息。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-057","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":57,"topic":"Model Rule 5.1(a) / Model Rule 5.1","question":"A litigation firm and general partnership hires new law school graduates as associates. These new lawyers are largely left to their own resources to practice law. The firm accepts many small litigation matters and assigns them to the associates for training purposes. No senior partners are assigned to supervise this work. It is assumed that if an associate needs help on a case, he or she will seek the guidance of a more senior attorney. A client retained the firm to pursue a claim for breach of contract against the city. A first-year associate was assigned the case. The associate failed to comply with the applicable 30-day notice requirement for filing a complaint against the city, and the client lost the chance to recover $5,000. When the complaint was dismissed for failure to comply with the notice requirement, the associate told the client that the case was dismissed on the merits. Which of the following statements is correct?","choices":{"A":"The law firm is subject to discipline for failure to supervise the associate.","B":"The individual partners are subject to discipline for failure to make reasonable efforts to establish a system providing reasonable assurance that all lawyers in the firm comply with the rules of professional conduct.","C":"The associate, an unsupervised subordinate lawyer, is not subject to discipline.","D":"The law firm is not subject to civil liability for the client’s loss."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 5.1(a) states “A partner in a law firm, and a lawyer who individually or together with other lawyers possesses comparable managerial authority in a law firm, shall make reasonable efforts to ensure that the firm has in effect measures giving reasonable assurance that all lawyers in the firm conform to the Rules of Professional Conduct.” As the question notes, this law firm doesn’t do anything to supervise its new attorneys. A is incorrect. Model Rule 5.1 specifically states that it applies to the individual partners and managing lawyers, not the firm itself. C is incorrect. Although the associate is unsupervised, he would still be subject to discipline for his apparent lack of competence and lying to his client. D is incorrect. Because the client retained the law firm for his claim and the firm had supervisory authority over the associate, the firm would be subject to civil liability for the client’s loss.","rule":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 5.1(a) states “A partner in a law firm, and a lawyer who individually or together with other lawyers possesses comparable managerial authority in a law firm, shall make reasonable efforts to ensure that the firm has in effect measures giving reasonable assurance that all lawyers in the firm conform to the Rules of Professional Conduct.” As the question notes, this law firm doesn’t do anything to supervise its new attorneys. A is incorrect. Model Rule 5.1 specifically states that it applies to the individual partners and managing lawyers, not the firm itself. C is incorrect. Although the associate is unsupervised, he would still be subject to discipline for his apparent lack of competence and lying to his client. D is incorrect. Because the client retained the law firm for his claim and the firm had supervisory authority over the associate, the firm would be subject to civil liability for the client’s loss.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following statements is correct?","rule":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 5.1(a) states “A partner in a law firm, and a lawyer who individually or together with other lawyers possesses comparable managerial authority in a law firm, shall make reasonable efforts to ensure that the firm has in effect measures giving reasonable assurance that all lawyers in the firm conform to the Rules of Professional Conduct.” As the question notes, this law firm doesn’t do anything to supervise its new attorneys. A is incorrect. Model Rule 5.1 specifically states that it applies to the individual partners and managing lawyers, not the firm itself. C is incorrect. Although the associate is unsupervised, he would still be subject to discipline for his apparent lack of competence and lying to his client. D is incorrect. Because the client retained the law firm for his claim and the firm had supervisory authority over the associate, the firm would be subject to civil liability for the client’s loss.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 5.1(a) states “A partner in a law firm, and a lawyer who individually or together with other lawyers possesses comparable managerial authority in a law firm, shall make reasonable efforts to ensure that the firm has in effect measures giving reasonable assurance that all lawyers in the firm conform to the Rules of Professional Conduct.” As the question notes, this law firm doesn’t do anything to supervise its new attorneys. A is incorrect. Model Rule 5.1 specifically states that it applies to the individual partners and managing lawyers, not the firm itself. C is incorrect. Although the associate is unsupervised, he would still be subject to discipline for his apparent lack of competence and lying to his client. D is incorrect. Because the client retained the law firm for his claim and the firm had supervisory authority over the associate, the firm would be subject to civil liability for the client’s loss.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.1(a)","Model Rule 5.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[201,202],"sourceAnswerPages":[298,299],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家诉讼律师事务所采用普通合伙形式，聘用法学院应届毕业生担任助理律师。这些新律师在执业时基本自行其是。事务所接受大量小型诉讼事项，把它们交给助理律师作为培训，但没有指派高级合伙人监督。事务所假定，助理律师如果在案件中需要帮助，会自行寻求资深律师指导。一名委托人聘请事务所向市政府提出违约索赔。案件交给一名第一年助理律师。助理律师没有遵守针对市政府提起诉讼所适用的 30 日通知要求，委托人因而失去追回 5,000 美元的机会。起诉状因未遵守通知要求而被驳回时，助理律师告诉委托人，案件是因实体理由被驳回。下列哪项正确？","choicesZh":{"A":"律师事务所因未监督助理律师而应受纪律处分。","B":"各合伙人因未作出合理努力建立制度，以合理确保事务所所有律师遵守职业行为规则，而应受纪律处分。","C":"该助理律师作为无人监督的下级律师，不应受纪律处分。","D":"律师事务所不因委托人损失承担民事责任。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》5.1(a) 规定：“律师事务所合伙人，以及单独或与其他律师共同在事务所拥有类似管理权的律师，应作出合理努力，确保事务所采取能够合理保证所有律师遵守《职业行为规则》的措施。”正如题目所述，该事务所根本没有监督新律师。A 不正确。规则 5.1 明确适用于各合伙人和管理律师，而不是事务所实体本身。C 不正确。助理律师即使无人监督，仍会因明显缺乏称职能力并向委托人说谎而受到纪律处分。D 不正确。由于委托人聘请事务所处理索赔，而事务所对助理律师具有监督权，事务所会因委托人损失承担民事责任。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-058","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":58,"topic":"Model Rule 1.15(a)","question":"An attorney received a check from a defendant payable to the attorney’s order in the sum of $10,000 in settlement of a plaintiff’s claim against the defendant. The plaintiff had previously paid the attorney a fee, so no part of the $10,000 was owed to the attorney. Which of the following would be proper?","choices":{"A":"Endorse the check and send it to the plaintiff.","B":"Deposit the check in the attorney’s personal bank account and send a personal check for $10,000 to the plaintiff.","C":"Deposit the check in the attorney’s personal bank account and await the plaintiff’s instructions.","D":"Deposit the check in the attorney’s personal bank account and immediately wire $10,000 to the plaintiff."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15(a) requires that a lawyer keep a client’s property separate from his or her own personal property. A is the only choice where the lawyer is not placing the client’s funds in his personal bank account. Because the attorney should not place the plaintiff’s funds in his personal bank account, B, C, and D are incorrect, no matter how quickly the attorney gets the funds to the plaintiff.","rule":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15(a) requires that a lawyer keep a client’s property separate from his or her own personal property. A is the only choice where the lawyer is not placing the client’s funds in his personal bank account. Because the attorney should not place the plaintiff’s funds in his personal bank account, B, C, and D are incorrect, no matter how quickly the attorney gets the funds to the plaintiff.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following would be proper?","rule":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15(a) requires that a lawyer keep a client’s property separate from his or her own personal property. A is the only choice where the lawyer is not placing the client’s funds in his personal bank account. Because the attorney should not place the plaintiff’s funds in his personal bank account, B, C, and D are incorrect, no matter how quickly the attorney gets the funds to the plaintiff.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15(a) requires that a lawyer keep a client’s property separate from his or her own personal property. A is the only choice where the lawyer is not placing the client’s funds in his personal bank account. Because the attorney should not place the plaintiff’s funds in his personal bank account, B, C, and D are incorrect, no matter how quickly the attorney gets the funds to the plaintiff.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.15(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[202],"sourceAnswerPages":[299],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师收到被告开具的一张以律师为收款人、金额 10,000 美元的支票，用于和解原告对被告的索赔。原告先前已经向律师支付费用，因此这 10,000 美元没有任何部分属于律师。下列哪项做法适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"在支票上背书并寄给原告。","B":"将支票存入律师个人银行账户，并向原告开具一张 10,000 美元个人支票。","C":"将支票存入律师个人银行账户，等待原告指示。","D":"将支票存入律师个人银行账户，并立即向原告电汇 10,000 美元。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》1.15(a) 要求律师将委托人财产与个人财产分开。A 是唯一没有把委托人资金存入律师个人银行账户的选项。由于律师不得把原告资金存入个人账户，无论其多快把资金交给原告，B、C、D 均不正确。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-059","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":59,"topic":"Model Rule 8.2(a) / Model Rule 8.2","question":"Alpha and Beta are members of the bar in the same community but have never practiced together. Beta is a candidate in a contested election for judicial office. Beta is opposed by Delta, another lawyer in the community. Alpha believes Beta is better qualified than Delta for the judiciary and is supporting Beta’s candidacy. She has also heard rumors regarding Delta’s possible drug problems. Which of the following would be improper for Alpha to do?","choices":{"A":"Solicit public endorsements for Beta’s candidacy by other attorneys in the community.","B":"Solicit contributions to Beta’s campaign committee from other attorneys in the community, including those who are likely to appear before Beta if Beta becomes a judge.","C":"Publicly oppose the candidacy of Delta.","D":"Report the rumors she has heard regarding Delta’s possible drug use."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Alpha should not pass along the rumors she has heard regarding Delta. Model Rule 8.2(a) states that a lawyer should not make statements made with reckless disregard as to their truth concerning the qualifications or integrity of a candidate for election or appointment to judicial office. Here, Alpha’s passing along of the rumors would likely be in reckless disregard concerning their truth. A, B, and C are incorrect. Model Rule 8.2 Comment [1] specifically allows lawyers to express their honest and candid opinions regarding judicial candidates. Consequently, Alpha can seek contributions from other lawyers in the community for Beta’s campaign committee and publicly oppose Delta, even if the attorneys being contacted are likely to appear before Beta in court.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Alpha should not pass along the rumors she has heard regarding Delta. Model Rule 8.2(a) states that a lawyer should not make statements made with reckless disregard as to their truth concerning the qualifications or integrity of a candidate for election or appointment to judicial office. Here, Alpha’s passing along of the rumors would likely be in reckless disregard concerning their truth. A, B, and C are incorrect. Model Rule 8.2 Comment [1] specifically allows lawyers to express their honest and candid opinions regarding judicial candidates. Consequently, Alpha can seek contributions from other lawyers in the community for Beta’s campaign committee and publicly oppose Delta, even if the attorneys being contacted are likely to appear before Beta in court.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following would be improper for Alpha to do?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Alpha should not pass along the rumors she has heard regarding Delta. Model Rule 8.2(a) states that a lawyer should not make statements made with reckless disregard as to their truth concerning the qualifications or integrity of a candidate for election or appointment to judicial office. Here, Alpha’s passing along of the rumors would likely be in reckless disregard concerning their truth. A, B, and C are incorrect. Model Rule 8.2 Comment [1] specifically allows lawyers to express their honest and candid opinions regarding judicial candidates. Consequently, Alpha can seek contributions from other lawyers in the community for Beta’s campaign committee and publicly oppose Delta, even if the attorneys being contacted are likely to appear before Beta in court.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Alpha should not pass along the rumors she has heard regarding Delta. Model Rule 8.2(a) states that a lawyer should not make statements made with reckless disregard as to their truth concerning the qualifications or integrity of a candidate for election or appointment to judicial office. Here, Alpha’s passing along of the rumors would likely be in reckless disregard concerning their truth. A, B, and C are incorrect. Model Rule 8.2 Comment [1] specifically allows lawyers to express their honest and candid opinions regarding judicial candidates. Consequently, Alpha can seek contributions from other lawyers in the community for Beta’s campaign committee and publicly oppose Delta, even if the attorneys being contacted are likely to appear before Beta in court.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 8.2(a)","Model Rule 8.2"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[203],"sourceAnswerPages":[299,300],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"甲和乙都是同一社区的律师协会成员，但从未共同执业。乙正在参加一场有竞争对手的法官职位选举，其对手是社区中的另一名律师丁。甲认为乙比丁更适合担任法官，因而支持乙参选。她还听到有关丁可能存在药物问题的传言。甲采取下列哪项做法不适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"向社区其他律师征求对乙参选的公开支持。","B":"向社区其他律师为乙的竞选委员会募集捐款，包括乙当选后可能在其面前出庭的律师。","C":"公开反对丁参选。","D":"举报她听到的有关丁可能使用药物的传言。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。甲不应传播有关丁的传言。《职业行为示范规则》8.2(a) 规定，律师不得就司法职位选举或任命候选人的资历或诚信，作出罔顾真假的陈述。本题中，甲传播传言很可能属于罔顾其真实性。A、B、C 不正确。规则 8.2 评注 [1] 明确允许律师就司法职位候选人表达诚实而坦率的意见。因此，甲可以向社区其他律师为乙的竞选委员会募集捐款，也可以公开反对丁，即使所联系的律师可能日后在乙面前出庭。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice2-060","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 2","questionNumber":60,"topic":"Model Rule 4.2 / Model Rule 3.8 / Model Rule 3.8(a)","question":"An attorney represents a defendant who has been indicted for auto theft. The prosecutor reasonably believes that the defendant committed the offense, but, because of the defendant’s youth, it is in the interest of justice to permit the defendant to plead guilty to the lesser offense of “joyriding” in return for an agreement by the prosecutor to recommend probation. The prosecutor has so advised the attorney, but the attorney told the prosecutor she would not plea bargain and would insist on a jury trial. The attorney informed the defendant of the prosecutor’s offer and advised the defendant not to accept it. The defendant followed the attorney’s advice. The attorney is a candidate for public office, and the prosecutor suspects that the attorney is insisting on a trial of the case to secure publicity for herself. Which of the following would be improper for the prosecutor to do?","choices":{"A":"Send a member of his staff who is not a lawyer to consult with the defendant.","B":"Move the trial court to dismiss the indictment and accept a new complaint charging the offense of “joyriding.”","C":"Proceed to trial on the indictment and prosecute the case vigorously.","D":"Disclose to the defense all evidence or information that tends to negate the defendant’s guilt."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 4.2 prohibits communications with persons represented by counsel. Here, the fact the prosecutor would be using a nonlawyer employee to do the communicating does not change the analysis. B is incorrect. Model Rule 3.8 Comment [1] notes that a prosecutor “has the responsibility of a minister of justice and not simply that of an advocate.” If the prosecutor believes “joyriding” is the most appropriate charge, he may file a new complaint reflecting that. C is incorrect. While Model Rule 3.8(a) states that a prosecutor should not prosecute a claim unsupported by probable cause. That does not seem to be the case here. As the question notes, the prosecutor reasonably believes that the defendant committed the offense. The only issue is that he believes the defendant’s youth is a mitigating circumstance. D is incorrect. Model Rule 3.8(d) specifically states that a prosecutor is required to make such information available to the defense.","rule":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 4.2 prohibits communications with persons represented by counsel. Here, the fact the prosecutor would be using a nonlawyer employee to do the communicating does not change the analysis. B is incorrect. Model Rule 3.8 Comment [1] notes that a prosecutor “has the responsibility of a minister of justice and not simply that of an advocate.” If the prosecutor believes “joyriding” is the most appropriate charge, he may file a new complaint reflecting that. C is incorrect. While Model Rule 3.8(a) states that a prosecutor should not prosecute a claim unsupported by probable cause. That does not seem to be the case here. As the question notes, the prosecutor reasonably believes that the defendant committed the offense. The only issue is that he believes the defendant’s youth is a mitigating circumstance. D is incorrect. Model Rule 3.8(d) specifically states that a prosecutor is required to make such information available to the defense.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following would be improper for the prosecutor to do?","rule":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 4.2 prohibits communications with persons represented by counsel. Here, the fact the prosecutor would be using a nonlawyer employee to do the communicating does not change the analysis. B is incorrect. Model Rule 3.8 Comment [1] notes that a prosecutor “has the responsibility of a minister of justice and not simply that of an advocate.” If the prosecutor believes “joyriding” is the most appropriate charge, he may file a new complaint reflecting that. C is incorrect. While Model Rule 3.8(a) states that a prosecutor should not prosecute a claim unsupported by probable cause. That does not seem to be the case here. As the question notes, the prosecutor reasonably believes that the defendant committed the offense. The only issue is that he believes the defendant’s youth is a mitigating circumstance. D is incorrect. Model Rule 3.8(d) specifically states that a prosecutor is required to make such information available to the defense.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 4.2 prohibits communications with persons represented by counsel. Here, the fact the prosecutor would be using a nonlawyer employee to do the communicating does not change the analysis. B is incorrect. Model Rule 3.8 Comment [1] notes that a prosecutor “has the responsibility of a minister of justice and not simply that of an advocate.” If the prosecutor believes “joyriding” is the most appropriate charge, he may file a new complaint reflecting that. C is incorrect. While Model Rule 3.8(a) states that a prosecutor should not prosecute a claim unsupported by probable cause. That does not seem to be the case here. As the question notes, the prosecutor reasonably believes that the defendant committed the offense. The only issue is that he believes the defendant’s youth is a mitigating circumstance. D is incorrect. Model Rule 3.8(d) specifically states that a prosecutor is required to make such information available to the defense.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 4.2","Model Rule 3.8","Model Rule 3.8(a)","Model Rule 3.8(d)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[203,204],"sourceAnswerPages":[300],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理一名因汽车盗窃受到起诉的被告。检察官合理地相信被告实施了犯罪，但考虑被告年幼，为实现司法公正，适宜允许被告承认较轻的“擅自驾车”罪，作为交换，由检察官同意建议缓刑。检察官已把方案告知律师，但律师告诉检察官不会进行认罪协商，坚持陪审团审判。律师将检察官要约告知被告，并建议不接受。被告遵从律师建议。该律师正竞选公职，检察官怀疑她坚持审判是为了给自己争取曝光。检察官采取下列哪项做法不适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"派一名不是律师的工作人员与被告协商。","B":"申请初审法院撤销起诉，并接受一份指控“擅自驾车”的新起诉书。","C":"就原起诉进行审判，并积极起诉案件。","D":"向辩方披露所有倾向于否定被告有罪的证据或信息。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》4.2 禁止与已有律师代理的人沟通。检察官利用非律师雇员进行沟通，并不会改变分析。B 不正确。规则 3.8 评注 [1] 指出，检察官“承担的是司法维护者的责任，而不仅仅是辩护人的责任”。如果检察官认为“擅自驾车”是最适当的指控，可以提交反映该指控的新起诉书。C 不正确。规则 3.8(a) 虽规定检察官不得起诉没有合理根据支持的指控，但本题显然并非如此。题目说明检察官合理地相信被告实施犯罪，唯一问题是他认为被告年幼属于减轻情节。D 不正确。规则 3.8(d) 明确要求检察官向辩方提供这类信息。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-001","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":1,"topic":"Model Rule 8.3(a)","question":"A prosecutor reasonably believed that a defendant committed a burglary, but thought it was in the interest of justice for the defendant to plead guilty to a lesser offense of “criminal mischief” because of the defendant’s age. The defendant’s attorney advised the defendant about the potential plea bargain, but advised the defendant not to accept it. The defendant followed the attorney’s advice. The attorney zealously and competently represented the defendant at trial, but the defendant was ultimately convicted and sentenced to two years in prison. The prosecutor suspected that the attorney, a candidate for public office, insisted on a trial as a way to secure publicity for herself. In fact, because of the trial, the attorney appeared in several newspapers and on several television shows. Must the prosecutor report to the disciplinary authority his suspicions about the attorney’s conduct in this case?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the defendant suffered a detriment from the attorney’s refusal to plea bargain.","B":"Yes, because the attorney in fact received widespread publicity as a result of the trial.","C":"No, because the prosecutor does not have actual knowledge that the attorney’s refusal to plea bargain was due to personal motives.","D":"No, because the attorney zealously and competently represented the defendant at trial."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.3(a) states that “A lawyer who knows that another lawyer has committed a violation of the Rules of Professional Conduct that raises a substantial question as to that lawyer’s honesty, trustworthiness or fitness as a lawyer in other respects, shall inform the appropriate professional authority.” The import word here is “knows.” In this question, the prosecutor has his suspicions regarding the attorney’s motives, but he doesn’t know anything. Importantly, in a question like this, don’t read into it facts that are not there, or connect dots that are not connected in the question. As far as the prosecutor actually knows, the attorney has done nothing wrong. A is incorrect. A lawyer is not subject to discipline simply because a client suffered a detriment by relying on the lawyer’s advice. B is incorrect. The fact the attorney gained publicity as a result of the trial does not necessarily mean she did anything wrong. D is incorrect. While the attorney did competently represent the defendant, if the prosecutor knew that the refusal to plea bargain was due to the attorney’s personal motives and not due to what the attorney thought would be best for the client, the prosecutor would still need to report the attorney.","rule":"C is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.3(a) states that “A lawyer who knows that another lawyer has committed a violation of the Rules of Professional Conduct that raises a substantial question as to that lawyer’s honesty, trustworthiness or fitness as a lawyer in other respects, shall inform the appropriate professional authority.” The import word here is “knows.” In this question, the prosecutor has his suspicions regarding the attorney’s motives, but he doesn’t know anything. Importantly, in a question like this, don’t read into it facts that are not there, or connect dots that are not connected in the question. As far as the prosecutor actually knows, the attorney has done nothing wrong. A is incorrect. A lawyer is not subject to discipline simply because a client suffered a detriment by relying on the lawyer’s advice. B is incorrect. The fact the attorney gained publicity as a result of the trial does not necessarily mean she did anything wrong. D is incorrect. While the attorney did competently represent the defendant, if the prosecutor knew that the refusal to plea bargain was due to the attorney’s personal motives and not due to what the attorney thought would be best for the client, the prosecutor would still need to report the attorney.","irac":{"issue":"Must the prosecutor report to the disciplinary authority his suspicions about the attorney’s conduct in this case?","rule":"C is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.3(a) states that “A lawyer who knows that another lawyer has committed a violation of the Rules of Professional Conduct that raises a substantial question as to that lawyer’s honesty, trustworthiness or fitness as a lawyer in other respects, shall inform the appropriate professional authority.” The import word here is “knows.” In this question, the prosecutor has his suspicions regarding the attorney’s motives, but he doesn’t know anything. Importantly, in a question like this, don’t read into it facts that are not there, or connect dots that are not connected in the question. As far as the prosecutor actually knows, the attorney has done nothing wrong. A is incorrect. A lawyer is not subject to discipline simply because a client suffered a detriment by relying on the lawyer’s advice. B is incorrect. The fact the attorney gained publicity as a result of the trial does not necessarily mean she did anything wrong. D is incorrect. While the attorney did competently represent the defendant, if the prosecutor knew that the refusal to plea bargain was due to the attorney’s personal motives and not due to what the attorney thought would be best for the client, the prosecutor would still need to report the attorney.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.3(a) states that “A lawyer who knows that another lawyer has committed a violation of the Rules of Professional Conduct that raises a substantial question as to that lawyer’s honesty, trustworthiness or fitness as a lawyer in other respects, shall inform the appropriate professional authority.” The import word here is “knows.” In this question, the prosecutor has his suspicions regarding the attorney’s motives, but he doesn’t know anything. Importantly, in a question like this, don’t read into it facts that are not there, or connect dots that are not connected in the question. As far as the prosecutor actually knows, the attorney has done nothing wrong. A is incorrect. A lawyer is not subject to discipline simply because a client suffered a detriment by relying on the lawyer’s advice. B is incorrect. The fact the attorney gained publicity as a result of the trial does not necessarily mean she did anything wrong. D is incorrect. While the attorney did competently represent the defendant, if the prosecutor knew that the refusal to plea bargain was due to the attorney’s personal motives and not due to what the attorney thought would be best for the client, the prosecutor would still need to report the attorney.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 8.3(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[204],"sourceAnswerPages":[300,301],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名检察官合理地相信一名被告实施了入室盗窃，但考虑被告年龄，认为允许被告承认较轻的“故意毁损财物”罪符合司法公正。被告律师向被告说明了潜在认罪协商，却建议不要接受。被告遵从律师建议。律师在审判中热忱且称职地代理被告，但被告最终被定罪并判处两年监禁。检察官怀疑，该律师正竞选公职，坚持审判是为了给自己争取曝光。事实上，审判使该律师出现在数家报纸和多个电视节目中。检察官是否必须向纪律机关举报自己对律师在本案中行为的怀疑？","choicesZh":{"A":"必须，因为律师拒绝认罪协商使被告遭受不利后果。","B":"必须，因为审判事实上使律师获得广泛曝光。","C":"不必须，因为检察官并不实际知道律师拒绝认罪协商是出于个人动机。","D":"不必须，因为律师在审判中热忱且称职地代理了被告。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》8.3(a) 规定：“律师知道另一名律师违反《职业行为规则》，且该违反对另一名律师的诚实、可信或其他方面是否适任产生重大疑问时，应通知适当专业机关。”此处关键词是“知道”。本题中，检察官只是怀疑律师动机，并不实际知道任何事情。尤其是在这类问题中，不要读入题目没有的事实，也不要自行连接题目没有连接的线索。据检察官实际所知，律师并未做错任何事。A 不正确。委托人依赖律师意见后受到不利结果，本身并不会使律师应受纪律处分。B 不正确。律师因审判获得曝光，并不必然意味着她实施了不当行为。D 不正确。律师虽称职代理被告，但如果检察官知道她拒绝认罪协商是出于个人动机，而不是为了委托人最佳利益，检察官仍须举报。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-002","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":2,"topic":"Model Rule 3.4 / Model Rule 3.4(b)","question":"An attorney represents a plaintiff in a personal injury action. An eyewitness to the accident lives about 500 miles distant from the city where the case will be tried. The attorney interviewed the witness and determined that the witness’s testimony would be favorable for the client. Which of the following would be improper for the attorney to pay to secure the witness’s testimony?","choices":{"A":"Statutory witness fees.","B":"Reimbursement for travel expenses.","C":"Reimbursement for lost wages while present at the trial.","D":"An amount equal to 5 percent of any recovery in the matter."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 3.4 Comment [3] states that “it is not improper to pay a witness’s expenses or to compensate an expert witness on terms permitted by law. The common law rule in most jurisdictions is that it is improper to pay an occurrence witness any fee for testifying and that it is improper to pay an expert witness a contingent fee.” Also, Model Rule 3.4(b) prohibits giving a witness an inducement to give false testimony. Here, giving the witness a percentage of the claim is an improper payment that would likely encourage false testimony. A is incorrect. As noted above, a lawyer is not prohibited from paying witnesses fees permitted by law. B is incorrect. Clearly, travel expenses would fall under those expenses that an attorney can pay. C is incorrect. Lost wages would be part of the witness’s expenses to testify, so they could be paid for by the attorney.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 3.4 Comment [3] states that “it is not improper to pay a witness’s expenses or to compensate an expert witness on terms permitted by law. The common law rule in most jurisdictions is that it is improper to pay an occurrence witness any fee for testifying and that it is improper to pay an expert witness a contingent fee.” Also, Model Rule 3.4(b) prohibits giving a witness an inducement to give false testimony. Here, giving the witness a percentage of the claim is an improper payment that would likely encourage false testimony. A is incorrect. As noted above, a lawyer is not prohibited from paying witnesses fees permitted by law. B is incorrect. Clearly, travel expenses would fall under those expenses that an attorney can pay. C is incorrect. Lost wages would be part of the witness’s expenses to testify, so they could be paid for by the attorney.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following would be improper for the attorney to pay to secure the witness’s testimony?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 3.4 Comment [3] states that “it is not improper to pay a witness’s expenses or to compensate an expert witness on terms permitted by law. The common law rule in most jurisdictions is that it is improper to pay an occurrence witness any fee for testifying and that it is improper to pay an expert witness a contingent fee.” Also, Model Rule 3.4(b) prohibits giving a witness an inducement to give false testimony. Here, giving the witness a percentage of the claim is an improper payment that would likely encourage false testimony. A is incorrect. As noted above, a lawyer is not prohibited from paying witnesses fees permitted by law. B is incorrect. Clearly, travel expenses would fall under those expenses that an attorney can pay. C is incorrect. Lost wages would be part of the witness’s expenses to testify, so they could be paid for by the attorney.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 3.4 Comment [3] states that “it is not improper to pay a witness’s expenses or to compensate an expert witness on terms permitted by law. The common law rule in most jurisdictions is that it is improper to pay an occurrence witness any fee for testifying and that it is improper to pay an expert witness a contingent fee.” Also, Model Rule 3.4(b) prohibits giving a witness an inducement to give false testimony. Here, giving the witness a percentage of the claim is an improper payment that would likely encourage false testimony. A is incorrect. As noted above, a lawyer is not prohibited from paying witnesses fees permitted by law. B is incorrect. Clearly, travel expenses would fall under those expenses that an attorney can pay. C is incorrect. Lost wages would be part of the witness’s expenses to testify, so they could be paid for by the attorney.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.4","Model Rule 3.4(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[204,205],"sourceAnswerPages":[301],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师在一宗人身伤害诉讼中代理原告。一名事故目击证人居住在距案件审理城市约 500 英里的地方。律师询问证人后确定，其证言对委托人有利。为取得证人证言，律师支付下列哪项费用不适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"法定证人费。","B":"偿还差旅开支。","C":"偿还出庭审判期间损失的工资。","D":"支付相当于本事项任何赔偿款 5% 的金额。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》3.4 评注 [3] 规定：“支付证人的开支，或者依法律允许的条件向专家证人支付报酬，并非不适当。多数司法管辖区的普通法规则是，向事实目击证人支付任何作证费用不适当，向专家证人支付风险报酬也不适当。”此外，规则 3.4(b) 禁止给予证人利益诱因使其作虚假证言。本题中，向证人支付索赔的一定比例属于不当付款，很可能鼓励虚假证言。A 不正确。如上所述，律师不被禁止支付法律允许的证人费。B 不正确。差旅开支显然属于律师可以支付的费用。C 不正确。损失工资属于证人为作证产生的开支，因此律师可以支付。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-003","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":3,"topic":"Model Rule 1.15","question":"With her client’s approval, an attorney settled a claim against a defendant for $60,000. The settlement agreement provided that one-half would be paid by the defendant’s primary insurance carrier, and one-half by a coinsurer. The attorney’s agreed fee was 30 percent of the amount of the settlement. The attorney received the primary carrier’s check for $30,000 and a letter from the coinsurer advising that its check would be sent in two weeks. The attorney promptly advised her client and deposited the $30,000 in her Clients’ Trust Account. Her client demanded that the attorney send him the entire $30,000 and take her fee out of the funds to be received from the coinsurer. Which of the following would now be proper for the attorney to do?","choices":{"A":"Send the client $21,000, and retain $9,000 in her Clients’ Trust Account.","B":"Send the client $21,000, and transfer $9,000 to her personal account.","C":"Send the client $12,000, and transfer $18,000 to her personal account.","D":"Send the client $12,000, and transfer $18,000 to her Clients’ Trust Account."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15 Comment [3] states that “[l]awyers often receive funds from which the lawyer’s fee will be paid. The lawyer is not required to remit to the client funds that the lawyer reasonably believes represent fees owed. However, a lawyer may not hold funds to coerce a client into accepting the lawyer’s contention. The disputed portion of the funds must be kept in a trust account and the lawyer should suggest means for prompt resolution of the dispute, such as arbitration. The undisputed portion of the funds shall be promptly distributed.” Here, the lawyer has received $30,000, and is entitled to 30 percent of the settlement per her agreed fee. Since she has received $30,000, 30 percent, or $9,000, is currently in dispute. Consequently, she should send the client $21,000 and retain the disputed $9,000 in her Clients’ Trust Account until the matter is settled. B is incorrect. As noted above, the lawyer should keep the disputed portion of the settlement in a trust account. C is incorrect. While the ultimate settlement may be $60,000, only $30,000 has been received. Thus, only 30 percent of that amount is currently in dispute. Additionally, any disputed funds should not be put in the lawyer’s personal account. D is incorrect. As stated above, only $30,000 of the settlement has been received, so only $9,000 is in dispute at this time.","rule":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15 Comment [3] states that “[l]awyers often receive funds from which the lawyer’s fee will be paid. The lawyer is not required to remit to the client funds that the lawyer reasonably believes represent fees owed. However, a lawyer may not hold funds to coerce a client into accepting the lawyer’s contention. The disputed portion of the funds must be kept in a trust account and the lawyer should suggest means for prompt resolution of the dispute, such as arbitration. The undisputed portion of the funds shall be promptly distributed.” Here, the lawyer has received $30,000, and is entitled to 30 percent of the settlement per her agreed fee. Since she has received $30,000, 30 percent, or $9,000, is currently in dispute. Consequently, she should send the client $21,000 and retain the disputed $9,000 in her Clients’ Trust Account until the matter is settled. B is incorrect. As noted above, the lawyer should keep the disputed portion of the settlement in a trust account. C is incorrect. While the ultimate settlement may be $60,000, only $30,000 has been received. Thus, only 30 percent of that amount is currently in dispute. Additionally, any disputed funds should not be put in the lawyer’s personal account. D is incorrect. As stated above, only $30,000 of the settlement has been received, so only $9,000 is in dispute at this time.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following would now be proper for the attorney to do?","rule":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15 Comment [3] states that “[l]awyers often receive funds from which the lawyer’s fee will be paid. The lawyer is not required to remit to the client funds that the lawyer reasonably believes represent fees owed. However, a lawyer may not hold funds to coerce a client into accepting the lawyer’s contention. The disputed portion of the funds must be kept in a trust account and the lawyer should suggest means for prompt resolution of the dispute, such as arbitration. The undisputed portion of the funds shall be promptly distributed.” Here, the lawyer has received $30,000, and is entitled to 30 percent of the settlement per her agreed fee. Since she has received $30,000, 30 percent, or $9,000, is currently in dispute. Consequently, she should send the client $21,000 and retain the disputed $9,000 in her Clients’ Trust Account until the matter is settled. B is incorrect. As noted above, the lawyer should keep the disputed portion of the settlement in a trust account. C is incorrect. While the ultimate settlement may be $60,000, only $30,000 has been received. Thus, only 30 percent of that amount is currently in dispute. Additionally, any disputed funds should not be put in the lawyer’s personal account. D is incorrect. As stated above, only $30,000 of the settlement has been received, so only $9,000 is in dispute at this time.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15 Comment [3] states that “[l]awyers often receive funds from which the lawyer’s fee will be paid. The lawyer is not required to remit to the client funds that the lawyer reasonably believes represent fees owed. However, a lawyer may not hold funds to coerce a client into accepting the lawyer’s contention. The disputed portion of the funds must be kept in a trust account and the lawyer should suggest means for prompt resolution of the dispute, such as arbitration. The undisputed portion of the funds shall be promptly distributed.” Here, the lawyer has received $30,000, and is entitled to 30 percent of the settlement per her agreed fee. Since she has received $30,000, 30 percent, or $9,000, is currently in dispute. Consequently, she should send the client $21,000 and retain the disputed $9,000 in her Clients’ Trust Account until the matter is settled. B is incorrect. As noted above, the lawyer should keep the disputed portion of the settlement in a trust account. C is incorrect. While the ultimate settlement may be $60,000, only $30,000 has been received. Thus, only 30 percent of that amount is currently in dispute. Additionally, any disputed funds should not be put in the lawyer’s personal account. D is incorrect. As stated above, only $30,000 of the settlement has been received, so only $9,000 is in dispute at this time.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.15"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[205],"sourceAnswerPages":[301,302],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"经委托人同意，一名律师以 60,000 美元和解针对被告的索赔。和解协议规定，一半由被告的主要保险公司支付，另一半由共同保险人支付。律师约定费用为和解金额的 30%。律师收到主要保险公司 30,000 美元支票，以及共同保险人来函，称会在两周内寄出支票。律师立即通知委托人，并把 30,000 美元存入委托人信托账户。委托人要求律师把全部 30,000 美元寄给自己，从日后共同保险人支付的资金中扣除律师费。律师现采取下列哪项做法适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"向委托人支付 21,000 美元，并在委托人信托账户中保留 9,000 美元。","B":"向委托人支付 21,000 美元，并把 9,000 美元转入个人账户。","C":"向委托人支付 12,000 美元，并把 18,000 美元转入个人账户。","D":"向委托人支付 12,000 美元，并把 18,000 美元转入委托人信托账户。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》1.15 评注 [3] 规定：“律师经常会收到将从中支付律师费的资金。律师无须向委托人交付律师合理认为代表所欠费用的资金。不过，律师不得扣留资金来迫使委托人接受律师主张。争议部分必须保留在信托账户中，律师应建议迅速解决争议的方法，例如仲裁。没有争议的资金应立即分配。”本题中，律师已收到 30,000 美元，按约定费用有权取得和解款的 30%。因此，目前存在争议的是 30,000 美元的 30%，即 9,000 美元。律师应向委托人支付 21,000 美元，并将有争议的 9,000 美元保留在委托人信托账户中，直至问题解决。B 不正确。如上所述，律师应把有争议的和解款保留在信托账户。C 不正确。最终和解总额虽为 60,000 美元，但目前只收到 30,000 美元。因此，现阶段只有该金额的 30% 存在争议。此外，任何有争议的资金均不得存入律师个人账户。D 不正确。如上所述，目前只收到和解款 30,000 美元，因此只有 9,000 美元存在争议。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-004","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":4,"topic":"Rule 4.1","question":"An attorney is a candidate for a judicial office that has been occupied by the incumbent for six years. The attorney has conducted a thorough investigation of the incumbent’s personal and professional life. Assume all factual statements below are accurate. Which of the following statements is it improper for the attorney to make during the campaign?","choices":{"A":"“The incumbent has been reversed by the appellate courts more than any other judge in the state during the preceding two years.”","B":"“The incumbent was publicly censured by the state Judicial Qualification Commission on one occasion for his overbearing conduct in court.”","C":"“The incumbent was given a poor rating for judicial temperament in a county bar association poll.”","D":"“During the previous year, the average sentence in armed robbery cases tried in the incumbent’s court was three years, and in murder cases, eight years. If I am elected, I won’t be soft on crime.”"},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.1 Comment [1], a judicial candidate should “be free and appear to be free from political influence and political pressure.” Here, the attorney is apparently promising that his sentences will be longer than three years in armed robbery cases and eight years in murder cases, no matter what the particular circumstances are of each case. Also, if you didn’t remember the Rule at all, D is clearly different from the other three choices because it includes a statement regarding the attorney’s plans should he be elected judge, while the other three choices are simply statements of fact regarding the incumbent. A, B, and C are incorrect because the attorney is simply making factual statements regarding the incumbent, and the question states that these statements are accurate.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.1 Comment [1], a judicial candidate should “be free and appear to be free from political influence and political pressure.” Here, the attorney is apparently promising that his sentences will be longer than three years in armed robbery cases and eight years in murder cases, no matter what the particular circumstances are of each case. Also, if you didn’t remember the Rule at all, D is clearly different from the other three choices because it includes a statement regarding the attorney’s plans should he be elected judge, while the other three choices are simply statements of fact regarding the incumbent. A, B, and C are incorrect because the attorney is simply making factual statements regarding the incumbent, and the question states that these statements are accurate.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following statements is it improper for the attorney to make during the campaign?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.1 Comment [1], a judicial candidate should “be free and appear to be free from political influence and political pressure.” Here, the attorney is apparently promising that his sentences will be longer than three years in armed robbery cases and eight years in murder cases, no matter what the particular circumstances are of each case. Also, if you didn’t remember the Rule at all, D is clearly different from the other three choices because it includes a statement regarding the attorney’s plans should he be elected judge, while the other three choices are simply statements of fact regarding the incumbent. A, B, and C are incorrect because the attorney is simply making factual statements regarding the incumbent, and the question states that these statements are accurate.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.1 Comment [1], a judicial candidate should “be free and appear to be free from political influence and political pressure.” Here, the attorney is apparently promising that his sentences will be longer than three years in armed robbery cases and eight years in murder cases, no matter what the particular circumstances are of each case. Also, if you didn’t remember the Rule at all, D is clearly different from the other three choices because it includes a statement regarding the attorney’s plans should he be elected judge, while the other three choices are simply statements of fact regarding the incumbent. A, B, and C are incorrect because the attorney is simply making factual statements regarding the incumbent, and the question states that these statements are accurate.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 4.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[205,206],"sourceAnswerPages":[302],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师正在竞选一个已由现任法官担任六年的司法职位。律师对现任法官的私人和职业经历进行了彻底调查。假定下列所有事实陈述均准确，律师在竞选中作出哪项陈述不适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"“过去两年中，现任法官被上诉法院撤销裁判的次数超过本州其他任何法官。”","B":"“现任法官曾有一次因在法庭上专横跋扈而受到州司法资格委员会公开谴责。”","C":"“县律师协会民意调查对现任法官的司法性情给出了较差评价。”","D":"“去年，在现任法官的法庭审理的持械抢劫案件中，平均刑期为三年；谋杀案件中为八年。如果我当选，绝不会对犯罪手软。”"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。根据《司法行为示范准则》规则 4.1 评注 [1]，司法职位候选人应当“免受并在外观上免受政治影响和政治压力”。本题中，律师似乎在承诺，无论每宗案件具体情况如何，其对持械抢劫和谋杀案件判处的刑期都会分别超过三年和八年。即使完全不记得规则，D 也明显不同于其他三个选项，因为它包含律师当选后的行动计划，而其他三项只是有关现任法官的事实陈述。A、B、C 不正确，因为律师只是对现任法官作出事实陈述，而且题目说明这些陈述准确。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-005","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":5,"topic":"Model Rule 8.4(e)","question":"An attorney and former state senator represents a well-known contractor before a state administrative agency. The agency has ordered the client to show cause why the client’s license as a contractor should not be revoked for violation of agency regulations. In a newspaper interview prior to the administrative hearing, the attorney made the following statements. Which of the following statements is improper?","choices":{"A":"“My client denies the charge made by the agency that he engaged in conduct constituting grounds for revocation of his license as a contractor.”","B":"“The next step in the administrative process is the administrative hearing; if the agency is successful, we will appeal, and the agency still cannot revoke my client’s license until the court affirms the finding for the agency.”","C":"“My client needs witnesses who are aware of the incidents that are the subject of the hearing.”","D":"“I would not have agreed to represent my client unless I knew he did not violate the agency regulations; I appointed everyone in the agency and they’ll follow my lead.”"},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.4(e) prohibits a lawyer from implying an ability to improperly influence a government agency. Here, the attorney’s statement seems to imply the agency employees owe him for their appointment. Additionally, it is the only choice that contains more than plain factual statements. A is incorrect because it simply states the client’s position in the matter, which is fairly obvious considering he is fighting the charge. B is incorrect because it is only outlining how the claim is likely to proceed through the agency and the courts. C is incorrect because the attorney is only stating that witnesses to the incident are needed to contest the claim.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.4(e) prohibits a lawyer from implying an ability to improperly influence a government agency. Here, the attorney’s statement seems to imply the agency employees owe him for their appointment. Additionally, it is the only choice that contains more than plain factual statements. A is incorrect because it simply states the client’s position in the matter, which is fairly obvious considering he is fighting the charge. B is incorrect because it is only outlining how the claim is likely to proceed through the agency and the courts. C is incorrect because the attorney is only stating that witnesses to the incident are needed to contest the claim.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following statements is improper?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.4(e) prohibits a lawyer from implying an ability to improperly influence a government agency. Here, the attorney’s statement seems to imply the agency employees owe him for their appointment. Additionally, it is the only choice that contains more than plain factual statements. A is incorrect because it simply states the client’s position in the matter, which is fairly obvious considering he is fighting the charge. B is incorrect because it is only outlining how the claim is likely to proceed through the agency and the courts. C is incorrect because the attorney is only stating that witnesses to the incident are needed to contest the claim.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Model Rule 8.4(e) prohibits a lawyer from implying an ability to improperly influence a government agency. Here, the attorney’s statement seems to imply the agency employees owe him for their appointment. Additionally, it is the only choice that contains more than plain factual statements. A is incorrect because it simply states the client’s position in the matter, which is fairly obvious considering he is fighting the charge. B is incorrect because it is only outlining how the claim is likely to proceed through the agency and the courts. C is incorrect because the attorney is only stating that witnesses to the incident are needed to contest the claim.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 8.4(e)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[206],"sourceAnswerPages":[302],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师曾任州参议员，现代理一名知名承包商在州行政机构前参加程序。该机构已命令委托人说明理由，解释为什么不应因违反机构规章而撤销其承包商执照。在行政听证前接受报纸采访时，律师作出以下陈述。哪项陈述不适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"“我的委托人否认该机构指控其实施了构成撤销承包商执照理由的行为。”","B":"“行政程序下一步是行政听证；如果机构胜诉，我们会提出上诉，而且在法院维持该机构认定前，机构仍不能撤销委托人执照。”","C":"“我的委托人需要了解听证所涉事件的证人。”","D":"“如果我不知道委托人没有违反机构规章，就不会同意代理他；机构里的每个人都是我任命的，他们会听我的。”"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》8.4(e) 禁止律师暗示自己有能力不当地影响政府机构。本题中，律师陈述似乎暗示机构雇员因获得其任命而欠他人情。此外，这是唯一不只是平实事实陈述的选项。A 不正确，因为它只说明委托人在事项中的立场；鉴于委托人正在抗辩指控，这一点相当明显。B 不正确，因为它只是说明主张可能如何经过机构和法院程序。C 不正确，因为律师只是说需要事件证人来抗辩主张。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-006","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":6,"topic":"Model Rule 1.15","question":"An attorney represented a plaintiff in litigation that was settled, with the plaintiff’s approval, for $25,000. The attorney received a check in that amount from the defendant, payable to the attorney’s order. The attorney endorsed and deposited the check in the attorney’s Clients’ Trust Account. The attorney promptly notified the plaintiff and billed the plaintiff $5,000 for legal fees. The plaintiff disputed the amount of the fee and wrote the attorney, stating, “I will agree to pay $3,000 as a reasonable fee for the work you did, but I will not pay anything more than that.” Which of the following is proper for the attorney to do?","choices":{"A":"Retain the entire $25,000 in the attorney’s Clients’ Trust Account until the fee dispute is settled.","B":"Send the plaintiff $20,000, transfer $3,000 to the attorney’s office account, and retain $2,000 in the attorney’s Clients’ Trust Account until the dispute is settled.","C":"Send the plaintiff $20,000 and transfer $5,000 to the attorney’s office account.","D":"Retain $22,000 in the Clients’ Trust account, and transfer $3,000 to the attorney’s office account."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15 Comment [3] states that “[l]awyers often receive funds from which the lawyer’s fee will be paid. The lawyer is not required to remit to the client funds that the lawyer reasonably believes represent fees owed. However, a lawyer may not hold funds to coerce a client into accepting the lawyer’s contention. The disputed portion of the funds must be kept in a trust account and the lawyer should suggest means for prompt resolution of the dispute, such as arbitration. The undisputed portion of the funds shall be promptly distributed.” In this case, $2,000 is in dispute. Consequently, that portion should remain in the Clients’ Trust Account, and the remainder should be distributed. A is incorrect. Since the entire amount is not in dispute, it would be incorrect for the lawyer to retain the entire amount in the Clients’ Trust Account instead of sending the nondisputed amount to the client. C is incorrect. Here, $2,000 is in dispute, so the attorney cannot simply place the entire $5,000 he believes he is owed into his office account. D is incorrect. The amount not in dispute should be sent to the client, as those funds belong to the client and not the attorney.","rule":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15 Comment [3] states that “[l]awyers often receive funds from which the lawyer’s fee will be paid. The lawyer is not required to remit to the client funds that the lawyer reasonably believes represent fees owed. However, a lawyer may not hold funds to coerce a client into accepting the lawyer’s contention. The disputed portion of the funds must be kept in a trust account and the lawyer should suggest means for prompt resolution of the dispute, such as arbitration. The undisputed portion of the funds shall be promptly distributed.” In this case, $2,000 is in dispute. Consequently, that portion should remain in the Clients’ Trust Account, and the remainder should be distributed. A is incorrect. Since the entire amount is not in dispute, it would be incorrect for the lawyer to retain the entire amount in the Clients’ Trust Account instead of sending the nondisputed amount to the client. C is incorrect. Here, $2,000 is in dispute, so the attorney cannot simply place the entire $5,000 he believes he is owed into his office account. D is incorrect. The amount not in dispute should be sent to the client, as those funds belong to the client and not the attorney.","irac":{"issue":"” Which of the following is proper for the attorney to do?","rule":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15 Comment [3] states that “[l]awyers often receive funds from which the lawyer’s fee will be paid. The lawyer is not required to remit to the client funds that the lawyer reasonably believes represent fees owed. However, a lawyer may not hold funds to coerce a client into accepting the lawyer’s contention. The disputed portion of the funds must be kept in a trust account and the lawyer should suggest means for prompt resolution of the dispute, such as arbitration. The undisputed portion of the funds shall be promptly distributed.” In this case, $2,000 is in dispute. Consequently, that portion should remain in the Clients’ Trust Account, and the remainder should be distributed. A is incorrect. Since the entire amount is not in dispute, it would be incorrect for the lawyer to retain the entire amount in the Clients’ Trust Account instead of sending the nondisputed amount to the client. C is incorrect. Here, $2,000 is in dispute, so the attorney cannot simply place the entire $5,000 he believes he is owed into his office account. D is incorrect. The amount not in dispute should be sent to the client, as those funds belong to the client and not the attorney.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15 Comment [3] states that “[l]awyers often receive funds from which the lawyer’s fee will be paid. The lawyer is not required to remit to the client funds that the lawyer reasonably believes represent fees owed. However, a lawyer may not hold funds to coerce a client into accepting the lawyer’s contention. The disputed portion of the funds must be kept in a trust account and the lawyer should suggest means for prompt resolution of the dispute, such as arbitration. The undisputed portion of the funds shall be promptly distributed.” In this case, $2,000 is in dispute. Consequently, that portion should remain in the Clients’ Trust Account, and the remainder should be distributed. A is incorrect. Since the entire amount is not in dispute, it would be incorrect for the lawyer to retain the entire amount in the Clients’ Trust Account instead of sending the nondisputed amount to the client. C is incorrect. Here, $2,000 is in dispute, so the attorney cannot simply place the entire $5,000 he believes he is owed into his office account. D is incorrect. The amount not in dispute should be sent to the client, as those funds belong to the client and not the attorney.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.15"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[206,207],"sourceAnswerPages":[303],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理原告参加诉讼，经原告同意，案件以 25,000 美元和解。律师收到被告开具的一张以律师为收款人、金额 25,000 美元的支票。律师背书后将支票存入委托人信托账户，立即通知原告，并向原告开具 5,000 美元法律服务费账单。原告对费用金额有异议，致函律师称：“我同意支付 3,000 美元，作为你所完成工作的合理费用，但不会支付更多。”律师采取下列哪项做法适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"在费用争议解决前，将全部 25,000 美元留在委托人信托账户。","B":"向原告支付 20,000 美元，把 3,000 美元转入律师办公账户，并在争议解决前把 2,000 美元留在委托人信托账户。","C":"向原告支付 20,000 美元，并把 5,000 美元转入律师办公账户。","D":"在委托人信托账户中保留 22,000 美元，并把 3,000 美元转入律师办公账户。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》1.15 评注 [3] 规定：“律师经常会收到将从中支付律师费的资金。律师无须向委托人交付律师合理认为代表所欠费用的资金。不过，律师不得扣留资金来迫使委托人接受律师主张。争议部分必须保留在信托账户中，律师应建议迅速解决争议的方法，例如仲裁。没有争议的资金应立即分配。”本题中，争议金额为 2,000 美元。因此，该部分应留在委托人信托账户，其余款项应予分配。A 不正确。并非全部金额均有争议，律师不应把全部款项留在信托账户而不向委托人支付无争议金额。C 不正确。2,000 美元存在争议，律师不能直接把自己主张的全部 5,000 美元转入办公账户。D 不正确。无争议金额应支付给委托人，因为这些资金属于委托人而非律师。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-007","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":7,"topic":"Model Rule 3.4(e)","question":"During the closing argument to the jury in a civil tax fraud case, the attorney representing the government quoted a portion of the defendant’s testimony. Which of the following statements by the attorney regarding the testimony would be improper?","choices":{"A":"“The testimony of the defendant directly contradicts the testimony of the two witnesses for the government.”","B":"“I ask you, who has the reason to lie, the two witnesses for the government or the defendant?”","C":"“I can truthfully say I have never seen a witness less worthy of belief.”","D":"“Someone has to be lying here because the different stories don’t add up.”"},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. Model Rule 3.4(e) prohibits a lawyer from stating a personal opinion regarding the credibility of a witness during trial. This statement is clearly an example of the attorney’s personal opinion. A is incorrect. This statement is merely a statement of fact regarding the testimony in the case. B is incorrect. While this statement seems to imply that a witness is lying, the attorney is not stating his personal opinion. D is incorrect. This statement is similar to the statement in choice B in that it implies a witness is lying, but doesn’t actually state the lawyer’s personal opinion regarding the credibility of the witnesses.","rule":"C is the correct answer. Model Rule 3.4(e) prohibits a lawyer from stating a personal opinion regarding the credibility of a witness during trial. This statement is clearly an example of the attorney’s personal opinion. A is incorrect. This statement is merely a statement of fact regarding the testimony in the case. B is incorrect. While this statement seems to imply that a witness is lying, the attorney is not stating his personal opinion. D is incorrect. This statement is similar to the statement in choice B in that it implies a witness is lying, but doesn’t actually state the lawyer’s personal opinion regarding the credibility of the witnesses.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following statements by the attorney regarding the testimony would be improper?","rule":"C is the correct answer. Model Rule 3.4(e) prohibits a lawyer from stating a personal opinion regarding the credibility of a witness during trial. This statement is clearly an example of the attorney’s personal opinion. A is incorrect. This statement is merely a statement of fact regarding the testimony in the case. B is incorrect. While this statement seems to imply that a witness is lying, the attorney is not stating his personal opinion. D is incorrect. This statement is similar to the statement in choice B in that it implies a witness is lying, but doesn’t actually state the lawyer’s personal opinion regarding the credibility of the witnesses.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. Model Rule 3.4(e) prohibits a lawyer from stating a personal opinion regarding the credibility of a witness during trial. This statement is clearly an example of the attorney’s personal opinion. A is incorrect. This statement is merely a statement of fact regarding the testimony in the case. B is incorrect. While this statement seems to imply that a witness is lying, the attorney is not stating his personal opinion. D is incorrect. This statement is similar to the statement in choice B in that it implies a witness is lying, but doesn’t actually state the lawyer’s personal opinion regarding the credibility of the witnesses.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.4(e)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[207],"sourceAnswerPages":[303],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"在一宗民事税务欺诈案件中，代理政府的律师向陪审团作结案陈词时引用被告证言的一部分。律师就该证言作出下列哪项陈述不适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"“被告证言与政府两名证人的证言直接矛盾。”","B":"“请各位思考，谁有理由说谎，是政府的两名证人，还是被告？”","C":"“我可以如实地说，我从未见过一个更不值得相信的证人。”","D":"“这里一定有人说谎，因为不同说法根本对不上。”"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》3.4(e) 禁止律师在审判中就证人可信度表达个人意见。该陈述明显属于律师个人意见。A 不正确。该陈述只是说明案件证言的事实。B 不正确。该陈述虽似乎暗示一名证人在说谎，律师却没有表达个人意见。D 不正确。该陈述与 B 类似，暗示一名证人在说谎，但没有真正陈述律师对证人可信度的个人意见。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-008","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":8,"topic":"Model Rule 3.6(a) / Model Rule 3.6(b)(1) / Model Rule 3.6(b)(5)","question":"An attorney represents a prominent businessman in a civil paternity suit brought by his former employee. Blood tests did not exclude the businessman’s paternity, and the case is being tried before a jury. The result turns on questions of fact. The businessman has steadfastly denied that he had sexual relations with the employee, while the employee has testified that they had sexual relations while on business trips and in her home. The trial has generated great public interest and is closely followed by the news media. When the employee completed her testimony, the attorney was interviewed by a newspaper reporter. Which of the following statements, if believed by the attorney to be true, would be improper for the attorney to make?","choices":{"A":"“As stated in our pleadings, we expect to prove that other men could be the father of the plaintiff’s child.”","B":"“We have scientific medical tests proving that my client is sterile.”","C":"“We have been unable to locate several people whose testimony will be helpful to us, and I implore them to contact me immediately.”","D":"“The plaintiff’s public tax records show that she’s had financial problems.”"},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 3.6(a) regarding trial publicity prohibits a lawyer from making “an extrajudicial statement that the lawyer knows or reasonably should know will be disseminated by means of public communication and will have a substantial likelihood of materially prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding in the matter.” Here, telling the reporter that the defense has medical proof that the client is infertile would clearly prejudice the proceedings by making it impossible for the businessman to have fathered the child, despite witness statements and other evidence to the contrary. Additionally, out of the available choices, choice B is the only statement not specifically allowed by the Rules. A is incorrect. Model Rule 3.6(b)(1) allows the attorney to tell the reporter the defense involved in the claim. C is incorrect. Model Rule 3.6(b)(5) allows lawyers to request assistance in obtaining evidence and information regarding the case. D is incorrect. Model Rule 3.6(b)(2) allows lawyers to state information contained in a public record. Here, the statement notes that the tax records he is referring to are public.","rule":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 3.6(a) regarding trial publicity prohibits a lawyer from making “an extrajudicial statement that the lawyer knows or reasonably should know will be disseminated by means of public communication and will have a substantial likelihood of materially prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding in the matter.” Here, telling the reporter that the defense has medical proof that the client is infertile would clearly prejudice the proceedings by making it impossible for the businessman to have fathered the child, despite witness statements and other evidence to the contrary. Additionally, out of the available choices, choice B is the only statement not specifically allowed by the Rules. A is incorrect. Model Rule 3.6(b)(1) allows the attorney to tell the reporter the defense involved in the claim. C is incorrect. Model Rule 3.6(b)(5) allows lawyers to request assistance in obtaining evidence and information regarding the case. D is incorrect. Model Rule 3.6(b)(2) allows lawyers to state information contained in a public record. Here, the statement notes that the tax records he is referring to are public.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following statements, if believed by the attorney to be true, would be improper for the attorney to make?","rule":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 3.6(a) regarding trial publicity prohibits a lawyer from making “an extrajudicial statement that the lawyer knows or reasonably should know will be disseminated by means of public communication and will have a substantial likelihood of materially prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding in the matter.” Here, telling the reporter that the defense has medical proof that the client is infertile would clearly prejudice the proceedings by making it impossible for the businessman to have fathered the child, despite witness statements and other evidence to the contrary. Additionally, out of the available choices, choice B is the only statement not specifically allowed by the Rules. A is incorrect. Model Rule 3.6(b)(1) allows the attorney to tell the reporter the defense involved in the claim. C is incorrect. Model Rule 3.6(b)(5) allows lawyers to request assistance in obtaining evidence and information regarding the case. D is incorrect. Model Rule 3.6(b)(2) allows lawyers to state information contained in a public record. Here, the statement notes that the tax records he is referring to are public.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. Model Rule 3.6(a) regarding trial publicity prohibits a lawyer from making “an extrajudicial statement that the lawyer knows or reasonably should know will be disseminated by means of public communication and will have a substantial likelihood of materially prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding in the matter.” Here, telling the reporter that the defense has medical proof that the client is infertile would clearly prejudice the proceedings by making it impossible for the businessman to have fathered the child, despite witness statements and other evidence to the contrary. Additionally, out of the available choices, choice B is the only statement not specifically allowed by the Rules. A is incorrect. Model Rule 3.6(b)(1) allows the attorney to tell the reporter the defense involved in the claim. C is incorrect. Model Rule 3.6(b)(5) allows lawyers to request assistance in obtaining evidence and information regarding the case. D is incorrect. Model Rule 3.6(b)(2) allows lawyers to state information contained in a public record. Here, the statement notes that the tax records he is referring to are public.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.6(a)","Model Rule 3.6(b)(1)","Model Rule 3.6(b)(5)","Model Rule 3.6(b)(2)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[207,208],"sourceAnswerPages":[303,304],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理一位知名商人，抗辩其前雇员提起的民事亲子关系诉讼。血液检查未排除商人为父亲的可能性，案件正由陪审团审理，结果取决于事实问题。商人一直坚决否认与雇员发生性关系，而雇员已作证称，二人在商务旅行和她家中发生过性关系。审判引起公众高度关注，新闻媒体密切跟踪。雇员完成证言后，律师接受一名报纸记者采访。如果律师相信下列陈述真实，哪项仍不适合对记者作出？","choicesZh":{"A":"“正如我们的诉状所述，我们预计会证明另有其他男子可能是原告孩子的父亲。”","B":"“我们拥有科学医学检查，证明我的委托人没有生育能力。”","C":"“我们一直无法找到几名证言对我方有帮助的人，恳请他们立即与我联系。”","D":"“原告的公开税务记录显示，她存在财务问题。”"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》3.6(a) 关于审判宣传的规定禁止律师作出“其知道或理应知道会通过公开传播方式扩散，并且极有可能对该事项的裁判程序造成重大不利影响的庭外陈述”。本题中，告诉记者辩方拥有证明委托人不育的医学证据，会明确损害程序，因为这将使商人不可能成为孩子父亲，尽管存在相反的证人陈述和其他证据。此外，各选项中只有 B 不属于规则明确允许的陈述。A 不正确。规则 3.6(b)(1) 允许律师告诉记者本案抗辩内容。C 不正确。规则 3.6(b)(5) 允许律师请求协助取得案件有关证据和信息。D 不正确。规则 3.6(b)(2) 允许律师陈述公开记录中包含的信息。本项明确说明所指税务记录是公开的。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-009","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":9,"topic":"Model Rule 1.15(a)","question":"An attorney represented her client on a minor personal injury claim against an uninsured motorist. The attorney represented the client on a 30 percent contingent fee basis. Pursuant to a negotiated settlement in the amount of $2,000, the motorist agreed to send the attorney a $100 check, made payable to the attorney, in each of the ensuing 20 months. Which of the following dispositions of each monthly check would be improper?","choices":{"A":"Deposit the check into her office account and immediately write her client a check for $70 from that account.","B":"Deposit the check into a separate account established for the client and immediately request the client pay the attorney $30.","C":"Deposit the check into a trust account in which funds belonging to all of the attorney’s clients are deposited and immediately write the client a check for $70 and herself a check for $30 from that account.","D":"Endorse the check over to the client, send it to the client, and request immediate payment of $30."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15(a) states that a lawyer should hold a client’s property separate from the lawyer’s own property. Consequently, the lawyer should not put the money into her own account, whether or not she immediately writes a check to the client. B is incorrect. Although the attorney is not immediately sending the funds to the client, the important point is that she is keeping the client’s money and her own separate. C is incorrect. This is probably the best way to handle the checks, as it keeps the funds separate and immediately gives the client the money he is entitled to. D is incorrect. There is no prohibition against the attorney sending the client the entire check.","rule":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15(a) states that a lawyer should hold a client’s property separate from the lawyer’s own property. Consequently, the lawyer should not put the money into her own account, whether or not she immediately writes a check to the client. B is incorrect. Although the attorney is not immediately sending the funds to the client, the important point is that she is keeping the client’s money and her own separate. C is incorrect. This is probably the best way to handle the checks, as it keeps the funds separate and immediately gives the client the money he is entitled to. D is incorrect. There is no prohibition against the attorney sending the client the entire check.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following dispositions of each monthly check would be improper?","rule":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15(a) states that a lawyer should hold a client’s property separate from the lawyer’s own property. Consequently, the lawyer should not put the money into her own account, whether or not she immediately writes a check to the client. B is incorrect. Although the attorney is not immediately sending the funds to the client, the important point is that she is keeping the client’s money and her own separate. C is incorrect. This is probably the best way to handle the checks, as it keeps the funds separate and immediately gives the client the money he is entitled to. D is incorrect. There is no prohibition against the attorney sending the client the entire check.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. Model Rule 1.15(a) states that a lawyer should hold a client’s property separate from the lawyer’s own property. Consequently, the lawyer should not put the money into her own account, whether or not she immediately writes a check to the client. B is incorrect. Although the attorney is not immediately sending the funds to the client, the important point is that she is keeping the client’s money and her own separate. C is incorrect. This is probably the best way to handle the checks, as it keeps the funds separate and immediately gives the client the money he is entitled to. D is incorrect. There is no prohibition against the attorney sending the client the entire check.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.15(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[208],"sourceAnswerPages":[304],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理委托人向一名无保险驾驶人提出小额人身伤害索赔，采用 30% 风险代理费。经谈判，以 2,000 美元达成和解；驾驶人同意在之后 20 个月中，每月向律师寄送一张以律师为收款人、金额 100 美元的支票。律师按下列哪种方式处理每月支票不适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"将支票存入办公账户，并立即从该账户向委托人开具 70 美元支票。","B":"将支票存入为该委托人单独设立的账户，并立即请求委托人向律师支付 30 美元。","C":"将支票存入存放该律师所有委托人资金的信托账户，并立即从该账户向委托人开具 70 美元支票、向自己开具 30 美元支票。","D":"将支票背书转让给委托人并寄出，同时请求立即支付 30 美元。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。《职业行为示范规则》1.15(a) 规定，律师应将委托人财产与自己的财产分开保管。因此，律师不应把资金存入自己的账户，即使立即向委托人开具支票也不行。B 不正确。律师虽没有立即把资金交给委托人，关键是她把委托人资金与自己的资金分开保管。C 不正确。这可能是处理支票的最佳方式，因为它使资金分开，并立即向委托人支付其有权取得的金额。D 不正确。规则不禁止律师把整张支票寄给委托人。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-010","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":10,"topic":"Model Rule 3.4(e) / Model Rule 8.2(a)","question":"An attorney represented a plaintiff who sued a defendant for injuries sustained in a car accident. The defendant was the son of a very rich businessman. Prior to trial, the attorney interviewed a witness who stated that she had observed the defendant drinking heavily hours before the accident. Unfortunately, on the eve of trial, the witness informed the attorney that she was ill and could not testify at trial. The attorney tried but could not obtain a continuance. As a result, the plaintiff’s direct case rested solely on the plaintiff’s testimony that the defendant was speeding and that the defendant’s car crossed the middle line and hit the plaintiff’s car. The defendant testified that he was driving safely in compliance with all rules and that the accident was entirely the plaintiff’s fault. On cross-examination, the attorney asked the defendant, “Isn’t it a fact that you were drinking prior to the accident?” The defendant answered that he had not consumed alcoholic beverages on the day of the accident. In summation to the jury, the attorney stated: “Ladies and gentlemen of the jury, you and I know that the defendant lied when he stated that he had not consumed alcoholic beverages on the day of the accident. We know that he was impaired. While we don’t have the money to buy off the judge like he does, I know you won’t let him buy off you!” Which of the following is least likely to make the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"The attorney’s question to the defendant implying that the defendant had consumed alcoholic beverages when the attorney knew that he could not offer evidence of the defendant’s drinking.","B":"The attorney’s statement to the jury asserting that the attorney knew that the defendant was drunk when no evidence in the record supported this allegation.","C":"The attorney’s statement asserting a personal belief that the defendant was drunk and lying.","D":"The attorney’s statement asserting that the defendant’s money could buy off the judge."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. The attorney violated no Rule of Professional Conduct or Evidence when he asked the defendant on cross-examination to confess to drinking prior to the accident. Although he could not offer any evidence, he was within legal boundaries when he asked the question. B is incorrect. Model Rule 3.4(e) prohibits a lawyer during trial from asserting personal knowledge or alluding to any matter that will not be supported by admissible evidence. C is incorrect. As stated above, Model Rule 3.4(e) prohibits a lawyer during trial from asserting personal knowledge regarding the facts at issue. D is incorrect. Model Rule 8.2(a) prohibits a lawyer from falsely or recklessly attacking the integrity of a judge. The attorney’s statement regarding the defendant’s money seems to do just that.","rule":"A is the correct answer. The attorney violated no Rule of Professional Conduct or Evidence when he asked the defendant on cross-examination to confess to drinking prior to the accident. Although he could not offer any evidence, he was within legal boundaries when he asked the question. B is incorrect. Model Rule 3.4(e) prohibits a lawyer during trial from asserting personal knowledge or alluding to any matter that will not be supported by admissible evidence. C is incorrect. As stated above, Model Rule 3.4(e) prohibits a lawyer during trial from asserting personal knowledge regarding the facts at issue. D is incorrect. Model Rule 8.2(a) prohibits a lawyer from falsely or recklessly attacking the integrity of a judge. The attorney’s statement regarding the defendant’s money seems to do just that.","irac":{"issue":"While we don’t have the money to buy off the judge like he does, I know you won’t let him buy off you!” Which of the following is least likely to make the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"A is the correct answer. The attorney violated no Rule of Professional Conduct or Evidence when he asked the defendant on cross-examination to confess to drinking prior to the accident. Although he could not offer any evidence, he was within legal boundaries when he asked the question. B is incorrect. Model Rule 3.4(e) prohibits a lawyer during trial from asserting personal knowledge or alluding to any matter that will not be supported by admissible evidence. C is incorrect. As stated above, Model Rule 3.4(e) prohibits a lawyer during trial from asserting personal knowledge regarding the facts at issue. D is incorrect. Model Rule 8.2(a) prohibits a lawyer from falsely or recklessly attacking the integrity of a judge. The attorney’s statement regarding the defendant’s money seems to do just that.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. The attorney violated no Rule of Professional Conduct or Evidence when he asked the defendant on cross-examination to confess to drinking prior to the accident. Although he could not offer any evidence, he was within legal boundaries when he asked the question. B is incorrect. Model Rule 3.4(e) prohibits a lawyer during trial from asserting personal knowledge or alluding to any matter that will not be supported by admissible evidence. C is incorrect. As stated above, Model Rule 3.4(e) prohibits a lawyer during trial from asserting personal knowledge regarding the facts at issue. D is incorrect. Model Rule 8.2(a) prohibits a lawyer from falsely or recklessly attacking the integrity of a judge. The attorney’s statement regarding the defendant’s money seems to do just that.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.4(e)","Model Rule 8.2(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[208,209],"sourceAnswerPages":[304,305],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-derived or modified; exact unmodified-original status is not individually identified","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理原告起诉被告，请求赔偿车祸伤害。被告是一位非常富有的商人之子。审判前，律师询问一名证人，该证人称自己看到被告在事故发生数小时前大量饮酒。不幸的是，审判前夕证人告知律师自己患病，无法出庭。律师尝试申请延期但未成功。因此，原告的直接举证完全依赖原告证言，即被告超速，其汽车越过中线撞上原告汽车。被告作证称自己遵守全部规则安全驾驶，事故完全由原告造成。交叉询问时，律师问被告：“事故前你是不是喝过酒？”被告回答，事故当天没有饮用酒精饮料。律师向陪审团作总结陈词时说：“陪审团各位女士、先生，你们和我都知道，被告声称事故当天没有饮酒时说了谎。我们知道他当时能力受损。我们虽然不像他那样有钱收买法官，但我知道你们不会让他收买你们！”下列哪项最不可能使律师应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"律师询问被告，暗示被告曾饮酒，而律师知道自己无法提供被告饮酒的证据。","B":"律师向陪审团声称自己知道被告喝醉，而案卷中没有证据支持该指控。","C":"律师表达个人看法，称被告喝醉并且说谎。","D":"律师声称被告的金钱可以买通法官。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。律师在交叉询问中要求被告承认事故前饮酒，没有违反任何《职业行为规则》或证据规则。即使律师无法提供其他证据，提出这个问题仍处于法律允许范围。B 不正确。《职业行为示范规则》3.4(e) 禁止律师在审判中主张亲身了解，或提及不会得到可采证据支持的任何事项。C 不正确。如上所述，规则 3.4(e) 禁止律师在审判中主张自己亲身了解争议事实。D 不正确。规则 8.2(a) 禁止律师虚假或罔顾真相地攻击法官诚信。律师有关被告金钱的陈述似乎正是这样做。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-011","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":11,"topic":"Model Rule 1.1","question":"An attorney tells his firm he will not attend any continuing legal education courses because they are expensive and the state doesn’t require it. However, because the attorney does not have malpractice insurance, he assures his colleagues that he will keep up to date on legal topics by studying on his own. Is the attorney’s action proper?","choices":{"A":"No, because the attorney does not have malpractice insurance.","B":"No, because the attorney cannot maintain competence without attending continuing legal education courses.","C":"Yes, because the attorney will independently undertake continuing study and education in the law.","D":"Yes, because the state does not offer free continuing legal education courses."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. To maintain the Rules’ required level of knowledge, competence, and skill, a lawyer should keep abreast of changes in the law. Model Rule 1.1. Independent study is just as valid as continuing legal education courses in maintaining the attorney’s competence. Importantly, the state does not require the attorney to attend continuing legal education. A is incorrect. The possession of malpractice insurance does not change a lawyer’s duty to remain competent. B is incorrect. Clearly, continuing legal education courses are not the only way a lawyer can maintain his or her competence. D is incorrect. A lawyer’s duty to remain competent is not affected by the cost of doing so.","rule":"C is the correct answer. To maintain the Rules’ required level of knowledge, competence, and skill, a lawyer should keep abreast of changes in the law. Model Rule 1.1. Independent study is just as valid as continuing legal education courses in maintaining the attorney’s competence. Importantly, the state does not require the attorney to attend continuing legal education. A is incorrect. The possession of malpractice insurance does not change a lawyer’s duty to remain competent. B is incorrect. Clearly, continuing legal education courses are not the only way a lawyer can maintain his or her competence. D is incorrect. A lawyer’s duty to remain competent is not affected by the cost of doing so.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney’s action proper?","rule":"C is the correct answer. To maintain the Rules’ required level of knowledge, competence, and skill, a lawyer should keep abreast of changes in the law. Model Rule 1.1. Independent study is just as valid as continuing legal education courses in maintaining the attorney’s competence. Importantly, the state does not require the attorney to attend continuing legal education. A is incorrect. The possession of malpractice insurance does not change a lawyer’s duty to remain competent. B is incorrect. Clearly, continuing legal education courses are not the only way a lawyer can maintain his or her competence. D is incorrect. A lawyer’s duty to remain competent is not affected by the cost of doing so.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. To maintain the Rules’ required level of knowledge, competence, and skill, a lawyer should keep abreast of changes in the law. Model Rule 1.1. Independent study is just as valid as continuing legal education courses in maintaining the attorney’s competence. Importantly, the state does not require the attorney to attend continuing legal education. A is incorrect. The possession of malpractice insurance does not change a lawyer’s duty to remain competent. B is incorrect. Clearly, continuing legal education courses are not the only way a lawyer can maintain his or her competence. D is incorrect. A lawyer’s duty to remain competent is not affected by the cost of doing so.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[209,210],"sourceAnswerPages":[305],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师告诉事务所，因为继续法律教育课程费用高昂，而且州政府也不要求参加，所以他不会参加任何课程。不过，由于律师没有执业过失保险，他向同事保证，自己会通过独立学习及时了解法律主题。律师的做法是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"不适当，因为律师没有执业过失保险。","B":"不适当，因为律师不参加继续法律教育课程就无法维持称职能力。","C":"适当，因为律师会独立持续学习并接受法律教育。","D":"适当，因为本州不提供免费继续法律教育课程。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。为维持规则要求的知识、称职能力和技能水平，律师应及时掌握法律变化。《职业行为示范规则》1.1。在维持律师称职能力方面，独立学习与继续法律教育课程同样有效。尤其是，本州没有要求律师参加继续法律教育。A 不正确。是否拥有执业过失保险不会改变律师保持称职能力的义务。B 不正确。继续法律教育课程显然不是律师保持称职能力的唯一方法。D 不正确。律师保持称职能力的义务不受所需成本影响。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-012","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":12,"topic":"Model Rule 1.11(a)(2) / Model Rule 1.11","question":"An attorney was employed by the state’s water department for five years. During the last year of her employment, the attorney spent most of her time in the preparation of a case against a corporation for the corporation’s overuse of state water and equipment. The corporation lost the case and was assessed a large fine. All of the information regarding the case was entered into the public record. The attorney recently went into private practice. Several individual plaintiffs have now brought private claims against the corporation over the same alleged overuse of the state’s water and equipment. The corporation’s CEO, who was impressed with her work on the earlier case, called and asked if she would represent the corporation in these new claims. May the attorney represent the corporation in these matters?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney is likely the most competent lawyer to do so.","B":"Yes, because all information acquired by the attorney while representing the water department is now a matter of public record.","C":"No, because the judgment in the prior case could be determinative of the corporation’s liability.","D":"No, because the attorney had substantial responsibility in the matter while employed by the water department."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.11(a)(2), “a lawyer who has formerly served as a public officer or employee of the government . . . shall not otherwise represent a client in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee” unless authorized to do so by the government agency. Importantly, Comment [3] states that these provisions are “designed . . . to prevent a lawyer from exploiting public office for the advantage of another client.” Model Rule 1.11, Comment [3]. Here, the attorney participated personally and substantially in the preparation of the water department’s case against the corporation. Consequently, she cannot now represent the corporation in individual claims involving the same case. A is incorrect. While the attorney may be the most competent lawyer to represent the corporation in this case, her special competence does not allow her to violate the Rules’ prohibition. B is incorrect. The Rule is concerned with the attorney using her former job as an unfair advantage in the case. Whether or not the information is in the public record doesn’t change the basic analysis. C is incorrect. As stated above, the Rule is concerned with the attorney’s exploitation of her prior work experience, not whether the case’s prior judgment could be determinative.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.11(a)(2), “a lawyer who has formerly served as a public officer or employee of the government . . . shall not otherwise represent a client in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee” unless authorized to do so by the government agency. Importantly, Comment [3] states that these provisions are “designed . . . to prevent a lawyer from exploiting public office for the advantage of another client.” Model Rule 1.11, Comment [3]. Here, the attorney participated personally and substantially in the preparation of the water department’s case against the corporation. Consequently, she cannot now represent the corporation in individual claims involving the same case. A is incorrect. While the attorney may be the most competent lawyer to represent the corporation in this case, her special competence does not allow her to violate the Rules’ prohibition. B is incorrect. The Rule is concerned with the attorney using her former job as an unfair advantage in the case. Whether or not the information is in the public record doesn’t change the basic analysis. C is incorrect. As stated above, the Rule is concerned with the attorney’s exploitation of her prior work experience, not whether the case’s prior judgment could be determinative.","irac":{"issue":"May the attorney represent the corporation in these matters?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.11(a)(2), “a lawyer who has formerly served as a public officer or employee of the government . . . shall not otherwise represent a client in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee” unless authorized to do so by the government agency. Importantly, Comment [3] states that these provisions are “designed . . . to prevent a lawyer from exploiting public office for the advantage of another client.” Model Rule 1.11, Comment [3]. Here, the attorney participated personally and substantially in the preparation of the water department’s case against the corporation. Consequently, she cannot now represent the corporation in individual claims involving the same case. A is incorrect. While the attorney may be the most competent lawyer to represent the corporation in this case, her special competence does not allow her to violate the Rules’ prohibition. B is incorrect. The Rule is concerned with the attorney using her former job as an unfair advantage in the case. Whether or not the information is in the public record doesn’t change the basic analysis. C is incorrect. As stated above, the Rule is concerned with the attorney’s exploitation of her prior work experience, not whether the case’s prior judgment could be determinative.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.11(a)(2), “a lawyer who has formerly served as a public officer or employee of the government . . . shall not otherwise represent a client in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee” unless authorized to do so by the government agency. Importantly, Comment [3] states that these provisions are “designed . . . to prevent a lawyer from exploiting public office for the advantage of another client.” Model Rule 1.11, Comment [3]. Here, the attorney participated personally and substantially in the preparation of the water department’s case against the corporation. Consequently, she cannot now represent the corporation in individual claims involving the same case. A is incorrect. While the attorney may be the most competent lawyer to represent the corporation in this case, her special competence does not allow her to violate the Rules’ prohibition. B is incorrect. The Rule is concerned with the attorney using her former job as an unfair advantage in the case. Whether or not the information is in the public record doesn’t change the basic analysis. C is incorrect. As stated above, the Rule is concerned with the attorney’s exploitation of her prior work experience, not whether the case’s prior judgment could be determinative.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.11(a)(2)","Model Rule 1.11"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[210],"sourceAnswerPages":[305,306],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师曾在州水务部门工作五年。任职最后一年，她把大部分时间用于准备一宗针对某公司的案件，涉及公司过度使用州水资源和设备。公司败诉，被处以巨额罚款。案件全部信息均进入公开记录。律师最近开始私人执业。数名个人原告现已针对公司提出私人索赔，涉及相同的过度使用州水资源和设备行为。公司首席执行官对她在先前案件中的工作印象深刻，致电询问她是否愿意在这些新索赔中代理公司。该律师能否在这些事项中代理公司？","choicesZh":{"A":"能，因为她很可能是最有能力这样做的律师。","B":"能，因为她代理水务部门期间取得的全部信息现在均属公开记录。","C":"不能，因为先前案件判决可能决定公司的责任。","D":"不能，因为该律师受雇于水务部门时，曾在该事项中承担重大职责。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。根据《职业行为示范规则》1.11(a)(2)，“先前曾担任政府公职人员或政府雇员的律师……不得以其他方式代理委托人处理其作为公职人员或雇员时曾亲自且实质性参与的事项”，除非取得政府机构授权。评注 [3] 特别指出，这些规定“旨在……防止律师利用公职为另一名委托人谋利”。规则 1.11 评注 [3]。本题中，律师曾亲自且实质性参与准备水务部门针对公司的案件。因此，她现在不能在涉及同一事项的个人索赔中代理公司。A 不正确。律师虽然可能是最有能力代理公司的人，但其特别能力并不允许违反规则禁令。B 不正确。该规则关心的是律师把先前工作作为案件中的不公平优势。信息是否属于公开记录不改变基本分析。C 不正确。如上所述，规则关注律师是否利用先前工作经历，而不是先前判决能否决定责任。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-013","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":13,"topic":"Rule 2.9(A)(2)","question":"A judge is presiding in a case that has, as its main issue, a complicated point of environmental law. After the two parties present their cases, she still feels that she doesn’t understand the claim. Consequently, she calls a friend of hers who specializes in environmental law and asks him several questions. Her friend has no interest in the case, but knows a lot about this area, so he emails her a lot of helpful information. She does not tell the two parties about the call or the information she received. Was it proper for the judge to consult her friend?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because her friend has no interest in the case.","B":"Yes, because the judge did not feel like she understood the claim.","C":"No, because the parties in the case did not give their consent to the call.","D":"No, because the judge did not inform the parties of the call."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(2) permits a judge to “obtain the written advice of a disinterested expert on the law applicable to a proceeding before the judge, if the judge gives advance notice to the parties of the person to be consulted and the subject matter of the advice to be solicited, and affords the parties a reasonable opportunity to object and respond to the notice and to the advice received.” Here, the judge did not inform the parties, which clearly implies that they had no opportunity to object or respond. A is incorrect. While the friend may have no interest in the case, the important issue is that the parties were not informed. B is incorrect. Even if the judge did not understand the claim, this does not relieve her of the duty to inform the parties if she seeks outside advice. C is incorrect. As noted in the Rule above, the judge does not need to get the parties’ consent, but she does need to inform them of the advice and give them an opportunity to respond.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(2) permits a judge to “obtain the written advice of a disinterested expert on the law applicable to a proceeding before the judge, if the judge gives advance notice to the parties of the person to be consulted and the subject matter of the advice to be solicited, and affords the parties a reasonable opportunity to object and respond to the notice and to the advice received.” Here, the judge did not inform the parties, which clearly implies that they had no opportunity to object or respond. A is incorrect. While the friend may have no interest in the case, the important issue is that the parties were not informed. B is incorrect. Even if the judge did not understand the claim, this does not relieve her of the duty to inform the parties if she seeks outside advice. C is incorrect. As noted in the Rule above, the judge does not need to get the parties’ consent, but she does need to inform them of the advice and give them an opportunity to respond.","irac":{"issue":"Was it proper for the judge to consult her friend?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(2) permits a judge to “obtain the written advice of a disinterested expert on the law applicable to a proceeding before the judge, if the judge gives advance notice to the parties of the person to be consulted and the subject matter of the advice to be solicited, and affords the parties a reasonable opportunity to object and respond to the notice and to the advice received.” Here, the judge did not inform the parties, which clearly implies that they had no opportunity to object or respond. A is incorrect. While the friend may have no interest in the case, the important issue is that the parties were not informed. B is incorrect. Even if the judge did not understand the claim, this does not relieve her of the duty to inform the parties if she seeks outside advice. C is incorrect. As noted in the Rule above, the judge does not need to get the parties’ consent, but she does need to inform them of the advice and give them an opportunity to respond.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(2) permits a judge to “obtain the written advice of a disinterested expert on the law applicable to a proceeding before the judge, if the judge gives advance notice to the parties of the person to be consulted and the subject matter of the advice to be solicited, and affords the parties a reasonable opportunity to object and respond to the notice and to the advice received.” Here, the judge did not inform the parties, which clearly implies that they had no opportunity to object or respond. A is incorrect. While the friend may have no interest in the case, the important issue is that the parties were not informed. B is incorrect. Even if the judge did not understand the claim, this does not relieve her of the duty to inform the parties if she seeks outside advice. C is incorrect. As noted in the Rule above, the judge does not need to get the parties’ consent, but she does need to inform them of the advice and give them an opportunity to respond.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 2.9(A)(2)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[210,211],"sourceAnswerPages":[306],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名法官正在审理一宗以复杂环境法问题为核心的案件。双方陈述完毕后，她仍觉得自己不理解该主张。因此，她致电一名专门从事环境法的朋友，询问数个问题。朋友与案件没有利害关系，但十分了解该领域，因而通过电子邮件向法官发送大量有帮助的信息。法官没有把电话或所收信息告知双方。法官咨询朋友是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为朋友与案件没有利害关系。","B":"适当，因为法官觉得自己不理解该主张。","C":"不适当，因为案件双方没有同意此次通话。","D":"不适当，因为法官没有把此次通话告知双方。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.9(A)(2) 允许法官“就法官面前某一诉讼程序所适用的法律，取得一名无利害关系专家的书面意见，但法官须事先将拟咨询人员及拟征求意见的事项通知各方，并给予各方合理机会对通知及所收到的意见提出异议和作出回应。”本题中，法官没有通知双方，这显然意味着双方没有机会提出异议或回应。A 不正确。朋友虽与案件没有利害关系，关键问题仍是双方没有获得通知。B 不正确。法官即使不理解主张，向外部寻求意见时也不能免除通知各方的义务。C 不正确。如上述规则所示，法官无需取得双方同意，但必须把意见告知各方并给予回应机会。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-014","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":14,"topic":"Model Rule 1.16(b) / Model Rule 1.16(b)(6)","question":"An attorney drafted a stipulation of consent to withdraw if fees are not paid on time. She has her clients sign the stipulation at the outset of the representation. Is it improper for the attorney to use the stipulation to withdraw from representation whenever a client fails to pay fees on time?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because a client’s failure to pay fees on time may be insufficient in itself to justify withdrawal.","B":"Yes, because clients are not provided an opportunity to seek independent legal advice before signing the stipulation.","C":"No, because a lawyer may withdraw when the financial burden of continuing the representation is unreasonable.","D":"No, because the clients consented to the withdrawal."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. An attorney may withdraw from representation if the withdrawal can be accomplished without material adverse effect, the client persists in criminal or fraudulent action, the client has used the lawyer’s services to perpetrate crime or fraud, the client insists on repugnant action, the client fails to fulfill an obligation and has been given reasonable warning, representation will result in an unreasonable financial burden, or other good cause exists. Model Rule 1.16(b). Thus, the simple fact that a client has failed to pay fees may be insufficient to justify a withdrawal. For example, the attorney in this question could withdraw if a client is a day late in paying a fee, which clearly does not meet any of the possible reasons for withdrawal included in the Rules. B is incorrect. Even if a client received independent legal advice before signing the stipulation, it would still be improper under the Rules. C is incorrect. The Rules allow withdrawal for “unreasonable” financial burden. Model Rule 1.16(b)(6). Here, the stipulation comes into effect simply because the client is late paying a fee, which may not be an unreasonable financial burden on the attorney (for example, if the client is one day late in paying). D is incorrect. Although a client may have consented to the withdrawal, this does not make the underlying stipulation proper under the Rules.","rule":"A is the correct answer. An attorney may withdraw from representation if the withdrawal can be accomplished without material adverse effect, the client persists in criminal or fraudulent action, the client has used the lawyer’s services to perpetrate crime or fraud, the client insists on repugnant action, the client fails to fulfill an obligation and has been given reasonable warning, representation will result in an unreasonable financial burden, or other good cause exists. Model Rule 1.16(b). Thus, the simple fact that a client has failed to pay fees may be insufficient to justify a withdrawal. For example, the attorney in this question could withdraw if a client is a day late in paying a fee, which clearly does not meet any of the possible reasons for withdrawal included in the Rules. B is incorrect. Even if a client received independent legal advice before signing the stipulation, it would still be improper under the Rules. C is incorrect. The Rules allow withdrawal for “unreasonable” financial burden. Model Rule 1.16(b)(6). Here, the stipulation comes into effect simply because the client is late paying a fee, which may not be an unreasonable financial burden on the attorney (for example, if the client is one day late in paying). D is incorrect. Although a client may have consented to the withdrawal, this does not make the underlying stipulation proper under the Rules.","irac":{"issue":"Is it improper for the attorney to use the stipulation to withdraw from representation whenever a client fails to pay fees on time?","rule":"A is the correct answer. An attorney may withdraw from representation if the withdrawal can be accomplished without material adverse effect, the client persists in criminal or fraudulent action, the client has used the lawyer’s services to perpetrate crime or fraud, the client insists on repugnant action, the client fails to fulfill an obligation and has been given reasonable warning, representation will result in an unreasonable financial burden, or other good cause exists. Model Rule 1.16(b). Thus, the simple fact that a client has failed to pay fees may be insufficient to justify a withdrawal. For example, the attorney in this question could withdraw if a client is a day late in paying a fee, which clearly does not meet any of the possible reasons for withdrawal included in the Rules. B is incorrect. Even if a client received independent legal advice before signing the stipulation, it would still be improper under the Rules. C is incorrect. The Rules allow withdrawal for “unreasonable” financial burden. Model Rule 1.16(b)(6). Here, the stipulation comes into effect simply because the client is late paying a fee, which may not be an unreasonable financial burden on the attorney (for example, if the client is one day late in paying). D is incorrect. Although a client may have consented to the withdrawal, this does not make the underlying stipulation proper under the Rules.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. An attorney may withdraw from representation if the withdrawal can be accomplished without material adverse effect, the client persists in criminal or fraudulent action, the client has used the lawyer’s services to perpetrate crime or fraud, the client insists on repugnant action, the client fails to fulfill an obligation and has been given reasonable warning, representation will result in an unreasonable financial burden, or other good cause exists. Model Rule 1.16(b). Thus, the simple fact that a client has failed to pay fees may be insufficient to justify a withdrawal. For example, the attorney in this question could withdraw if a client is a day late in paying a fee, which clearly does not meet any of the possible reasons for withdrawal included in the Rules. B is incorrect. Even if a client received independent legal advice before signing the stipulation, it would still be improper under the Rules. C is incorrect. The Rules allow withdrawal for “unreasonable” financial burden. Model Rule 1.16(b)(6). Here, the stipulation comes into effect simply because the client is late paying a fee, which may not be an unreasonable financial burden on the attorney (for example, if the client is one day late in paying). D is incorrect. Although a client may have consented to the withdrawal, this does not make the underlying stipulation proper under the Rules.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.16(b)","Model Rule 1.16(b)(6)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[211],"sourceAnswerPages":[306,307],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师起草一份同意书，约定如果未按时支付费用，委托人同意律师退出。她让委托人在代理开始时签署该同意书。只要委托人未按时支付费用，律师就利用同意书退出代理，是否不适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为委托人未按时支付费用这一事实本身可能不足以正当化退出。","B":"是，因为委托人在签署同意书前没有机会寻求独立法律意见。","C":"否，因为继续代理造成不合理经济负担时，律师可以退出。","D":"否，因为委托人已经同意退出。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。在不造成重大不利影响、委托人持续实施犯罪或欺诈、利用律师服务实施犯罪或欺诈、坚持采取律师反感的行动、未履行义务且已收到合理警告、代理会造成不合理经济负担，或者存在其他正当理由时，律师可以退出代理。《职业行为示范规则》1.16(b)。因此，仅仅未支付费用可能不足以正当化退出。例如，本题律师在委托人只迟一天付款时就可以退出，而这显然不符合规则列出的任何退出理由。B 不正确。即使委托人在签署同意书前取得独立法律意见，该同意书在规则下仍不适当。C 不正确。规则确实允许因“不合理”经济负担而退出。规则 1.16(b)(6)。但本题同意书只要委托人迟付费用就生效，而这未必给律师造成不合理经济负担（例如只迟一天）。D 不正确。委托人虽然可能同意退出，也不会使基础同意书在规则下适当。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-015","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":15,"topic":"Model Rule 1.2(d) / Model Rule 3.3(a)(3)","question":"An attorney represented a client who was being prosecuted for securities violations. During the course of the representation, the client told the attorney that he had failed to file five years of tax returns, which was a felony and could add to the amount of any sentence that was imposed by the court because it indicated general financial malfeasance. The client asked the attorney not to disclose his failure to file returns during the course of the representation and told the attorney that, if called as a witness, he would lie about the tax returns. When the attorney called the client as a witness, the attorney asked him about the tax returns. The client said that he was up to date in filing his taxes. Were the attorney’s actions proper under the Rule?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the tax returns were not the primary issue in the case.","B":"Yes, because the attorney obtained the information during the course of representation.","C":"No, because the client’s failure to file tax returns is a felony.","D":"No, because the attorney knowingly used false testimony."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.2(d), a lawyer may not counsel or assist a client in conduct that is criminal or fraudulent. Model Rule 3.3(a)(3) prohibits the use of false evidence. When the attorney asked the client about his tax returns, he knew that the response would be a falsehood that would mislead the court in its judgment of the client’s offense. A is incorrect. The fact that the tax returns were not the primary issue does not change the analysis. Importantly, it could change the sentence. B is incorrect. While the information would ordinarily be protected because it was disclosed to the attorney as a confidence during the course of representation, it cannot be protected if it results in the presentation of testimony that the attorney knows is false. C is incorrect. The important point in this question is that the testimony is false. The seriousness of the crime is immaterial.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.2(d), a lawyer may not counsel or assist a client in conduct that is criminal or fraudulent. Model Rule 3.3(a)(3) prohibits the use of false evidence. When the attorney asked the client about his tax returns, he knew that the response would be a falsehood that would mislead the court in its judgment of the client’s offense. A is incorrect. The fact that the tax returns were not the primary issue does not change the analysis. Importantly, it could change the sentence. B is incorrect. While the information would ordinarily be protected because it was disclosed to the attorney as a confidence during the course of representation, it cannot be protected if it results in the presentation of testimony that the attorney knows is false. C is incorrect. The important point in this question is that the testimony is false. The seriousness of the crime is immaterial.","irac":{"issue":"Were the attorney’s actions proper under the Rule?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.2(d), a lawyer may not counsel or assist a client in conduct that is criminal or fraudulent. Model Rule 3.3(a)(3) prohibits the use of false evidence. When the attorney asked the client about his tax returns, he knew that the response would be a falsehood that would mislead the court in its judgment of the client’s offense. A is incorrect. The fact that the tax returns were not the primary issue does not change the analysis. Importantly, it could change the sentence. B is incorrect. While the information would ordinarily be protected because it was disclosed to the attorney as a confidence during the course of representation, it cannot be protected if it results in the presentation of testimony that the attorney knows is false. C is incorrect. The important point in this question is that the testimony is false. The seriousness of the crime is immaterial.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.2(d), a lawyer may not counsel or assist a client in conduct that is criminal or fraudulent. Model Rule 3.3(a)(3) prohibits the use of false evidence. When the attorney asked the client about his tax returns, he knew that the response would be a falsehood that would mislead the court in its judgment of the client’s offense. A is incorrect. The fact that the tax returns were not the primary issue does not change the analysis. Importantly, it could change the sentence. B is incorrect. While the information would ordinarily be protected because it was disclosed to the attorney as a confidence during the course of representation, it cannot be protected if it results in the presentation of testimony that the attorney knows is false. C is incorrect. The important point in this question is that the testimony is false. The seriousness of the crime is immaterial.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.2(d)","Model Rule 3.3(a)(3)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[211,212],"sourceAnswerPages":[307],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理一名因证券违法受到起诉的委托人。代理过程中，委托人告诉律师，自己五年没有提交报税表；这构成重罪，而且表明其一般性财务违法，可能增加法院判处的刑罚。委托人要求律师在代理过程中不要披露未报税之事，并告诉律师，如果被传唤作证，会就报税表说谎。律师传唤委托人作证时，询问了报税情况。委托人回答称自己一直按时申报税款。律师的行为在规则下是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为报税表不是案件主要问题。","B":"适当，因为律师在代理过程中取得该信息。","C":"不适当，因为委托人未提交报税表构成重罪。","D":"不适当，因为律师明知而使用虚假证言。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。根据《职业行为示范规则》1.2(d)，律师不得建议或协助委托人实施犯罪或欺诈行为。规则 3.3(a)(3) 禁止使用虚假证据。律师询问委托人报税情况时，明知回答将是虚假的，并会在法院判断委托人违法行为时误导法院。A 不正确。报税表并非主要问题，并不会改变分析。关键是，它可能影响刑罚。B 不正确。该信息通常会因委托人在代理过程中以保密方式披露而受到保护，但如果保护会导致提出律师明知虚假的证言，就不能继续保护。C 不正确。本题关键是证言虚假，犯罪严重程度无关。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-016","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":16,"topic":"Model Rule 4.2 / Model Rule 8.4(a)","question":"An attorney representing a plaintiff in a car accident reasonably believed that the defendant’s lawyer had not informed the defendant about the attorney’s recent settlement offer. She suspected the defendant’s lawyer hadn’t done so in order to run up his fees. The attorney instructed her office assistant, who was not a lawyer, to tell the defendant about the settlement offer. Are the attorney’s actions proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the lawyer reasonably believed that the defendant’s lawyer had not informed him of the settlement offer.","B":"Yes, because the defendant’s lawyer was attempting to run up his fees.","C":"No, because the defendant did not consent to the contact.","D":"No, because the defendant’s lawyer did not consent to the contact."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from communicating about a matter with a person known to be represented in the matter unless the person’s lawyer consents to the communication or the communication is authorized by law. Model Rule 4.2. In addition, a lawyer may not violate the Rules directly or through the acts of another. Model Rule 8.4(a). Here, the attorney used a nonlawyer agent, the office assistant, to violate the no-contact rule. A is incorrect. The fact the defendant’s lawyer might not have relayed the settlement offer did not justify the prohibited communication. B is incorrect. As in the case of her belief regarding the settlement offer, her suspicion regarding the lawyer’s attempt to run up fees does not excuse the communication. C is incorrect. As stated above, the important point is that it is the lawyer’s consent that is necessary, not the client’s.","rule":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from communicating about a matter with a person known to be represented in the matter unless the person’s lawyer consents to the communication or the communication is authorized by law. Model Rule 4.2. In addition, a lawyer may not violate the Rules directly or through the acts of another. Model Rule 8.4(a). Here, the attorney used a nonlawyer agent, the office assistant, to violate the no-contact rule. A is incorrect. The fact the defendant’s lawyer might not have relayed the settlement offer did not justify the prohibited communication. B is incorrect. As in the case of her belief regarding the settlement offer, her suspicion regarding the lawyer’s attempt to run up fees does not excuse the communication. C is incorrect. As stated above, the important point is that it is the lawyer’s consent that is necessary, not the client’s.","irac":{"issue":"Are the attorney’s actions proper?","rule":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from communicating about a matter with a person known to be represented in the matter unless the person’s lawyer consents to the communication or the communication is authorized by law. Model Rule 4.2. In addition, a lawyer may not violate the Rules directly or through the acts of another. Model Rule 8.4(a). Here, the attorney used a nonlawyer agent, the office assistant, to violate the no-contact rule. A is incorrect. The fact the defendant’s lawyer might not have relayed the settlement offer did not justify the prohibited communication. B is incorrect. As in the case of her belief regarding the settlement offer, her suspicion regarding the lawyer’s attempt to run up fees does not excuse the communication. C is incorrect. As stated above, the important point is that it is the lawyer’s consent that is necessary, not the client’s.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from communicating about a matter with a person known to be represented in the matter unless the person’s lawyer consents to the communication or the communication is authorized by law. Model Rule 4.2. In addition, a lawyer may not violate the Rules directly or through the acts of another. Model Rule 8.4(a). Here, the attorney used a nonlawyer agent, the office assistant, to violate the no-contact rule. A is incorrect. The fact the defendant’s lawyer might not have relayed the settlement offer did not justify the prohibited communication. B is incorrect. As in the case of her belief regarding the settlement offer, her suspicion regarding the lawyer’s attempt to run up fees does not excuse the communication. C is incorrect. As stated above, the important point is that it is the lawyer’s consent that is necessary, not the client’s.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 4.2","Model Rule 8.4(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[212],"sourceAnswerPages":[307,308],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名代理车祸原告的律师合理地认为，被告律师没有把她最近提出的和解要约告知被告。她怀疑被告律师这样做是为了增加费用。该律师指示一名不是律师的办公室助理把和解要约告知被告。律师的行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为律师合理地认为被告律师没有告知被告和解要约。","B":"适当，因为被告律师试图增加费用。","C":"不适当，因为被告没有同意联系。","D":"不适当，因为被告律师没有同意联系。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。律师明知某人在事项中由另一名律师代理，除非该人的律师同意沟通或法律授权，否则不得就该事项与此人沟通。《职业行为示范规则》4.2。此外，律师不得亲自或通过他人行为违反规则。规则 8.4(a)。本题中，律师利用非律师代理人即办公室助理违反禁止联系规则。A 不正确。被告律师可能未转达和解要约这一事实，不能正当化受到禁止的沟通。B 不正确。与她对和解要约的看法一样，她怀疑对方律师试图增加费用也不能免除该沟通。C 不正确。如上所述，关键是需要取得律师同意，而不是委托人同意。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-017","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":17,"topic":"Model Rule 1.5(d)(1)","question":"An attorney’s standard retainer contract in divorce cases provides for the payment of a fee of one-third of the amount of alimony or property settlement secured by the attorney. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the fee is contingent.","B":"Yes, because a lawyer may not acquire a proprietary interest in a cause of action.","C":"No, because this helps clients who may not otherwise be able to afford an attorney.","D":"No, because clients consent to the fee arrangement by signing the retainer contract."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not enter into an arrangement for, charge, or collect: (1) any fee in a domestic relations matter, the payment or amount of which is contingent upon the securing of a divorce or upon the amount of alimony or support, or property settlement in lieu thereof.” Model Rule 1.5(d)(1). Here, the attorney’s standard retainer contract sets up a prohibited contingent fee arrangement. B is incorrect. The issue here is not the type of interest being acquired by the lawyer, but the fact that the lawyer is charging a contingent fee in a domestic matter. C is incorrect. While many clients may find it easier to pay a contingent fee, this does not make contingent fees proper in this situation. D is incorrect. While the clients may consent, this does not make the arrangement proper under the Rules. Always be wary of answer choices that claim something is right because it was the client’s idea.","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not enter into an arrangement for, charge, or collect: (1) any fee in a domestic relations matter, the payment or amount of which is contingent upon the securing of a divorce or upon the amount of alimony or support, or property settlement in lieu thereof.” Model Rule 1.5(d)(1). Here, the attorney’s standard retainer contract sets up a prohibited contingent fee arrangement. B is incorrect. The issue here is not the type of interest being acquired by the lawyer, but the fact that the lawyer is charging a contingent fee in a domestic matter. C is incorrect. While many clients may find it easier to pay a contingent fee, this does not make contingent fees proper in this situation. D is incorrect. While the clients may consent, this does not make the arrangement proper under the Rules. Always be wary of answer choices that claim something is right because it was the client’s idea.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not enter into an arrangement for, charge, or collect: (1) any fee in a domestic relations matter, the payment or amount of which is contingent upon the securing of a divorce or upon the amount of alimony or support, or property settlement in lieu thereof.” Model Rule 1.5(d)(1). Here, the attorney’s standard retainer contract sets up a prohibited contingent fee arrangement. B is incorrect. The issue here is not the type of interest being acquired by the lawyer, but the fact that the lawyer is charging a contingent fee in a domestic matter. C is incorrect. While many clients may find it easier to pay a contingent fee, this does not make contingent fees proper in this situation. D is incorrect. While the clients may consent, this does not make the arrangement proper under the Rules. Always be wary of answer choices that claim something is right because it was the client’s idea.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not enter into an arrangement for, charge, or collect: (1) any fee in a domestic relations matter, the payment or amount of which is contingent upon the securing of a divorce or upon the amount of alimony or support, or property settlement in lieu thereof.” Model Rule 1.5(d)(1). Here, the attorney’s standard retainer contract sets up a prohibited contingent fee arrangement. B is incorrect. The issue here is not the type of interest being acquired by the lawyer, but the fact that the lawyer is charging a contingent fee in a domestic matter. C is incorrect. While many clients may find it easier to pay a contingent fee, this does not make contingent fees proper in this situation. D is incorrect. While the clients may consent, this does not make the arrangement proper under the Rules. Always be wary of answer choices that claim something is right because it was the client’s idea.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.5(d)(1)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[212,213],"sourceAnswerPages":[308],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师在离婚案件中使用的标准聘用合同规定，律师费为律师帮助取得的赡养费或财产和解金额的三分之一。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为费用取决于案件结果。","B":"是，因为律师不得取得诉因的所有权利益。","C":"否，因为这有助于原本可能无力聘请律师的委托人。","D":"否，因为委托人签署聘用合同，即同意收费安排。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。“律师不得订立、收取或收取以下费用：(1) 家事关系事项中，以取得离婚、赡养费、扶养费或替代性财产和解的金额为付款条件或金额计算依据的任何费用。”《职业行为示范规则》1.5(d)(1)。本题中，律师的标准聘用合同设立了受禁止的风险代理费安排。B 不正确。本题问题不在于律师取得何种利益，而在于律师在家事事项中收取风险代理费。C 不正确。许多委托人虽然可能更容易支付风险代理费，但这不会使该情形下的风险代理费适当。D 不正确。委托人即使同意，也不会使安排符合规则。应始终警惕那些声称某事因由委托人提出就正确的选项。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-018","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":18,"topic":"Model Rule 1.3","question":"In order to obtain ample time for settlement negotiations, an attorney immediately requested and obtained from opposing counsel a stipulation extending the client’s time to answer the complaint until 10 days after receipt of written demand from the plaintiff’s attorney. Several months later, no settlement had been reached, and on March 1, the plaintiff’s attorney wrote to the attorney demanding that an answer be filed within 10 days. When no answer was filed by March 15, the plaintiff’s attorney had a default judgment entered in favor of the plaintiff. The attorney was away on a month-long writing retreat when the opposing counsel’s letter was received in her office. Because the writing retreat was extremely remote, she had no reasonable way of receiving any messages from her office. When the attorney returned from her writing retreat on April 1, she promptly moved to have the default set aside and her motion was granted. Are the attorney’s actions proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the default judgment was set aside.","B":"Yes, because she had no reasonable way of knowing that the plaintiff’s attorney had demanded an answer be filed within 10 days.","C":"No, because she didn’t offer to provide her client with restitution for any losses.","D":"No, because the attorney didn’t make provisions for handling her pending cases while she was away."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Model Rule 1.3. Importantly, if the attorney is away from his or her office for a long time, the attorney must make some provision for dealing with ongoing matters. A is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice. While the default judgment was set aside, there was no guarantee that a court would do so, and the attorney put her client’s claim at unreasonable risk. B is incorrect. If the attorney was going to be unreachable for a long time, she should have made provisions for the handling of her pending cases. C is incorrect. The issue here is not potential restitution. The bigger issue is that the attorney was unreachable for a month without making provisions for her ongoing cases.","rule":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Model Rule 1.3. Importantly, if the attorney is away from his or her office for a long time, the attorney must make some provision for dealing with ongoing matters. A is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice. While the default judgment was set aside, there was no guarantee that a court would do so, and the attorney put her client’s claim at unreasonable risk. B is incorrect. If the attorney was going to be unreachable for a long time, she should have made provisions for the handling of her pending cases. C is incorrect. The issue here is not potential restitution. The bigger issue is that the attorney was unreachable for a month without making provisions for her ongoing cases.","irac":{"issue":"Are the attorney’s actions proper?","rule":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Model Rule 1.3. Importantly, if the attorney is away from his or her office for a long time, the attorney must make some provision for dealing with ongoing matters. A is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice. While the default judgment was set aside, there was no guarantee that a court would do so, and the attorney put her client’s claim at unreasonable risk. B is incorrect. If the attorney was going to be unreachable for a long time, she should have made provisions for the handling of her pending cases. C is incorrect. The issue here is not potential restitution. The bigger issue is that the attorney was unreachable for a month without making provisions for her ongoing cases.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall act with reasonable diligence and promptness in representing a client.” Model Rule 1.3. Importantly, if the attorney is away from his or her office for a long time, the attorney must make some provision for dealing with ongoing matters. A is incorrect. This is the classic “no-harm, no-foul” answer choice. While the default judgment was set aside, there was no guarantee that a court would do so, and the attorney put her client’s claim at unreasonable risk. B is incorrect. If the attorney was going to be unreachable for a long time, she should have made provisions for the handling of her pending cases. C is incorrect. The issue here is not potential restitution. The bigger issue is that the attorney was unreachable for a month without making provisions for her ongoing cases.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[213],"sourceAnswerPages":[308,309],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"为争取充足时间进行和解谈判，一名律师立即请求对方律师同意延长委托人提交答辩状的期限；双方约定，期限延至收到原告律师书面要求后的第 10 日。数月后，双方仍未和解。3 月 1 日，原告律师致函该律师，要求在 10 日内提交答辩状。至 3 月 15 日仍未提交，原告律师遂取得原告胜诉的缺席判决。对方律师来函送达其办公室时，该律师正在进行为期一个月的写作闭关。闭关地点极为偏远，她没有合理方式从办公室接收任何消息。4 月 1 日闭关结束后，律师立即申请撤销缺席判决，申请获准。律师的行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为缺席判决已经撤销。","B":"适当，因为她没有合理方式得知原告律师要求在 10 日内提交答辩状。","C":"不适当，因为她没有提出赔偿委托人的任何损失。","D":"不适当，因为律师离开期间没有安排人员处理待决案件。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。“律师代理委托人时应以合理的勤勉和及时程度行事。”《职业行为示范规则》1.3。尤其是，律师长时间离开办公室时，必须为正在办理的事项作出安排。A 不正确。这是典型的“没有损害，就没有违规”型选项。缺席判决虽被撤销，但法院并不保证一定会这样做，律师让委托人的主张承担了不合理风险。B 不正确。律师既然将长时间无法联系，就应为待决案件的处理作出安排。C 不正确。本题问题不在于潜在赔偿，而在于律师一个月无法联系，也没有为正在办理的案件作出安排。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-019","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":19,"topic":"Model Rule 1.6(a) / Model Rule 1.6","question":"While representing a client in a personal injury case, the client told the attorney that she had previously killed a man and buried his body in a nearby swamp. The murder is completely unrelated to the personal injury case. The body has not been found, and the client is not a suspect in the crime, which remains unsolved. Must the attorney voluntarily disclose to the authorities his knowledge of the prior murder and the location of the victim’s body?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney is an officer of the court.","B":"Yes, because the murder remains unsolved.","C":"No, because the information was obtained by the attorney in the course of representation.","D":"No, because the attorney did not represent or advise his client regarding the prior murder."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not reveal any information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent. Model Rule 1.6(a). Here, the information was obtained during the course of the attorney’s representation, and consequently “relates” to that representation. Consequently, the attorney should not reveal it. A is incorrect. Although there are several exceptions to Model Rule 1.6’s confidentiality requirement, none of the exceptions requires an attorney to disclose a client’s past crimes of violence. Often, when testing this particular issue, the MPRE uses the idea of an attorney as “an officer of the court” in an incorrect answer choice. B is incorrect. Although the murder remains unsolved, this does not relieve the attorney from the Rules’ prohibition. D is incorrect. Here, the attorney discovered that information during his representation of the client, so it is protected as confidential information. If the attorney had advised the client regarding how to commit the prior crime or how to hide the body, he would of course be subject to discipline and criminal prosecution.","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not reveal any information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent. Model Rule 1.6(a). Here, the information was obtained during the course of the attorney’s representation, and consequently “relates” to that representation. Consequently, the attorney should not reveal it. A is incorrect. Although there are several exceptions to Model Rule 1.6’s confidentiality requirement, none of the exceptions requires an attorney to disclose a client’s past crimes of violence. Often, when testing this particular issue, the MPRE uses the idea of an attorney as “an officer of the court” in an incorrect answer choice. B is incorrect. Although the murder remains unsolved, this does not relieve the attorney from the Rules’ prohibition. D is incorrect. Here, the attorney discovered that information during his representation of the client, so it is protected as confidential information. If the attorney had advised the client regarding how to commit the prior crime or how to hide the body, he would of course be subject to discipline and criminal prosecution.","irac":{"issue":"Must the attorney voluntarily disclose to the authorities his knowledge of the prior murder and the location of the victim’s body?","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not reveal any information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent. Model Rule 1.6(a). Here, the information was obtained during the course of the attorney’s representation, and consequently “relates” to that representation. Consequently, the attorney should not reveal it. A is incorrect. Although there are several exceptions to Model Rule 1.6’s confidentiality requirement, none of the exceptions requires an attorney to disclose a client’s past crimes of violence. Often, when testing this particular issue, the MPRE uses the idea of an attorney as “an officer of the court” in an incorrect answer choice. B is incorrect. Although the murder remains unsolved, this does not relieve the attorney from the Rules’ prohibition. D is incorrect. Here, the attorney discovered that information during his representation of the client, so it is protected as confidential information. If the attorney had advised the client regarding how to commit the prior crime or how to hide the body, he would of course be subject to discipline and criminal prosecution.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not reveal any information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent. Model Rule 1.6(a). Here, the information was obtained during the course of the attorney’s representation, and consequently “relates” to that representation. Consequently, the attorney should not reveal it. A is incorrect. Although there are several exceptions to Model Rule 1.6’s confidentiality requirement, none of the exceptions requires an attorney to disclose a client’s past crimes of violence. Often, when testing this particular issue, the MPRE uses the idea of an attorney as “an officer of the court” in an incorrect answer choice. B is incorrect. Although the murder remains unsolved, this does not relieve the attorney from the Rules’ prohibition. D is incorrect. Here, the attorney discovered that information during his representation of the client, so it is protected as confidential information. If the attorney had advised the client regarding how to commit the prior crime or how to hide the body, he would of course be subject to discipline and criminal prosecution.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.6(a)","Model Rule 1.6"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[213,214],"sourceAnswerPages":[309],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理委托人处理人身伤害案件时，委托人告诉律师，自己先前曾杀死一名男子，并将尸体埋在附近沼泽中。谋杀与人身伤害案件完全无关。尸体尚未被发现，委托人也不是这宗仍未侦破案件的嫌疑人。律师是否必须主动向有关机关披露自己知道先前谋杀及被害人尸体位置？","choicesZh":{"A":"必须，因为律师是法院工作人员。","B":"必须，因为谋杀仍未侦破。","C":"不必须，因为律师在代理过程中取得信息。","D":"不必须，因为律师没有就先前谋杀代理委托人或向其提供意见。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。除非委托人作出知情同意，律师不得披露与委托人代理有关的任何信息。《职业行为示范规则》1.6(a)。本题中，信息在律师代理过程中取得，因而与该代理“有关”。所以，律师不应披露。A 不正确。规则 1.6 的保密要求虽有多项例外，但没有任何例外要求律师披露委托人过去实施的暴力犯罪。MPRE 考查此问题时，经常在错误选项中使用律师是“法院工作人员”的说法。B 不正确。谋杀虽未侦破，也不能解除规则对律师的禁止。D 不正确。本题中，律师是在代理该委托人期间获知信息，因此作为保密信息受到保护。当然，如果律师曾向委托人建议如何实施先前犯罪或如何藏匿尸体，会受到纪律处分和刑事起诉。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-020","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":20,"topic":"Model Rule 1.8(f)(1)","question":"An attorney represented a client in an automobile accident. The client’s father sent the attorney a check for $20,000 and asked the attorney to apply it to the client’s fee and “give it top priority.” The client’s father told the attorney not to tell the client about the payment. The attorney agreed not to tell his client. Is it improper for the attorney to accept the father’s check?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney agreed not to tell his client.","B":"Yes, because the client’s father is trying to influence the attorney’s actions in the case.","C":"No, because the client’s father is a family member of the client.","D":"No, because the client is allowed to pay the attorney’s fee in any reasonable manner."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not accept compensation for representing a client from one other than the client unless: (1) the client gives informed consent.” Model Rule 1.8(f)(1). Consequently, the attorney cannot accept the father’s check without informing the client. B is incorrect. Although it would clearly be improper for the client’s father to attempt to influence the attorney’s conduct, the primary issue is that the attorney cannot accept the check without getting the client’s consent. Additionally, simply asking the attorney to make the case his “top priority” is not really influencing the attorney’s conduct. C is incorrect. The familial relationship between the client and his father does not excuse the attorney from this Rule. D is incorrect. A close reading of the question shows that the client is not the one who asked his father to pay his bill.","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not accept compensation for representing a client from one other than the client unless: (1) the client gives informed consent.” Model Rule 1.8(f)(1). Consequently, the attorney cannot accept the father’s check without informing the client. B is incorrect. Although it would clearly be improper for the client’s father to attempt to influence the attorney’s conduct, the primary issue is that the attorney cannot accept the check without getting the client’s consent. Additionally, simply asking the attorney to make the case his “top priority” is not really influencing the attorney’s conduct. C is incorrect. The familial relationship between the client and his father does not excuse the attorney from this Rule. D is incorrect. A close reading of the question shows that the client is not the one who asked his father to pay his bill.","irac":{"issue":"Is it improper for the attorney to accept the father’s check?","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not accept compensation for representing a client from one other than the client unless: (1) the client gives informed consent.” Model Rule 1.8(f)(1). Consequently, the attorney cannot accept the father’s check without informing the client. B is incorrect. Although it would clearly be improper for the client’s father to attempt to influence the attorney’s conduct, the primary issue is that the attorney cannot accept the check without getting the client’s consent. Additionally, simply asking the attorney to make the case his “top priority” is not really influencing the attorney’s conduct. C is incorrect. The familial relationship between the client and his father does not excuse the attorney from this Rule. D is incorrect. A close reading of the question shows that the client is not the one who asked his father to pay his bill.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not accept compensation for representing a client from one other than the client unless: (1) the client gives informed consent.” Model Rule 1.8(f)(1). Consequently, the attorney cannot accept the father’s check without informing the client. B is incorrect. Although it would clearly be improper for the client’s father to attempt to influence the attorney’s conduct, the primary issue is that the attorney cannot accept the check without getting the client’s consent. Additionally, simply asking the attorney to make the case his “top priority” is not really influencing the attorney’s conduct. C is incorrect. The familial relationship between the client and his father does not excuse the attorney from this Rule. D is incorrect. A close reading of the question shows that the client is not the one who asked his father to pay his bill.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.8(f)(1)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[214],"sourceAnswerPages":[309,310],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理委托人处理汽车事故。委托人的父亲向律师寄送一张 20,000 美元支票，请求用于支付委托人的费用，并“给予最高优先级”。父亲告诉律师不要把付款告知委托人，律师也同意不说。律师接受父亲的支票是否不适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师同意不告知委托人。","B":"是，因为委托人的父亲试图影响律师在案件中的行为。","C":"否，因为委托人的父亲是委托人的家属。","D":"否，因为委托人可以采用任何合理方式支付律师费。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。“律师不得从委托人以外的人处接受代理报酬，除非：(1) 委托人作出知情同意。”《职业行为示范规则》1.8(f)(1)。因此，律师未经委托人知情，不能接受父亲的支票。B 不正确。委托人父亲试图影响律师办理案件显然会不适当，但首要问题是，律师未经委托人同意不能接受支票。此外，仅仅请求律师把案件列为“最高优先级”，并不真正构成影响律师行为。C 不正确。委托人与父亲的家庭关系不能使律师免于遵守该规则。D 不正确。仔细阅读题目可知，并非委托人请求父亲支付账单。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-021","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":21,"topic":"Rule 4.1(a)","question":"A seller was engaged in negotiations to sell a golf course to a buyer who was unrepresented in the transaction. The seller told the buyer to call his attorney if the buyer had any questions about the property. The buyer called the seller’s attorney and expressed hesitations concerning buying the seller’s interest in the golf course. The attorney responded that he thought the buyer was getting a wonderful deal. At the time the attorney spoke to the buyer, the attorney knew that there was a defect in the title and that the buyer’s attempt to purchase the seller’s interest in the golf course would be worthless. After speaking with the attorney, the buyer agreed to make the purchase. Shortly after the sale closed, the buyer discovered that his acquisition was worthless. Were the attorney’s actions proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the seller told the buyer to call his attorney if he had any questions.","B":"Yes, because the attorney merely expressed his opinion regarding the sale.","C":"No, because the buyer was not represented by another lawyer, and the attorney should not have implied he had the buyer’s best interests in mind.","D":"No, because the attorney made a false statement to the buyer."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. “In the course of representing a client a lawyer shall not knowingly: (a) make a false statement of material fact or law to a third person.” Rule 4.1(a). Here, the attorney knows of the title defect and is basically committing fraud by telling the buyer that it is a wonderful deal. A is incorrect. This is another variation of the “it was the client’s idea” wrong answer. B is incorrect. The attorney’s statement was not an opinion, considering the title defect made this statement an outright lie. C is incorrect. Here, the attorney didn’t imply anything—he flat-out lied to the buyer because he knew the attempt to purchase the property would be worthless.","rule":"D is the correct answer. “In the course of representing a client a lawyer shall not knowingly: (a) make a false statement of material fact or law to a third person.” Rule 4.1(a). Here, the attorney knows of the title defect and is basically committing fraud by telling the buyer that it is a wonderful deal. A is incorrect. This is another variation of the “it was the client’s idea” wrong answer. B is incorrect. The attorney’s statement was not an opinion, considering the title defect made this statement an outright lie. C is incorrect. Here, the attorney didn’t imply anything—he flat-out lied to the buyer because he knew the attempt to purchase the property would be worthless.","irac":{"issue":"Were the attorney’s actions proper?","rule":"D is the correct answer. “In the course of representing a client a lawyer shall not knowingly: (a) make a false statement of material fact or law to a third person.” Rule 4.1(a). Here, the attorney knows of the title defect and is basically committing fraud by telling the buyer that it is a wonderful deal. A is incorrect. This is another variation of the “it was the client’s idea” wrong answer. B is incorrect. The attorney’s statement was not an opinion, considering the title defect made this statement an outright lie. C is incorrect. Here, the attorney didn’t imply anything—he flat-out lied to the buyer because he knew the attempt to purchase the property would be worthless.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. “In the course of representing a client a lawyer shall not knowingly: (a) make a false statement of material fact or law to a third person.” Rule 4.1(a). Here, the attorney knows of the title defect and is basically committing fraud by telling the buyer that it is a wonderful deal. A is incorrect. This is another variation of the “it was the client’s idea” wrong answer. B is incorrect. The attorney’s statement was not an opinion, considering the title defect made this statement an outright lie. C is incorrect. Here, the attorney didn’t imply anything—he flat-out lied to the buyer because he knew the attempt to purchase the property would be worthless.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 4.1(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[214,215],"sourceAnswerPages":[310],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名卖方正与一名在交易中没有律师代理的买方谈判出售高尔夫球场。卖方告诉买方，如对房产有任何疑问，可以致电卖方律师。买方致电该律师，对购买卖方的高尔夫球场权益表示犹豫。律师回答说，他认为买方会得到一笔非常划算的交易。律师与买方交谈时，明知产权存在缺陷，买方购买卖方高尔夫球场权益的尝试不会取得任何价值。与律师交谈后，买方同意购买。交易完成后不久，买方发现所取得的权益一文不值。律师的行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为卖方告诉买方，如有任何疑问可以致电其律师。","B":"适当，因为律师只是表达自己对交易的意见。","C":"不适当，因为买方没有另一名律师代理，而该律师不应暗示自己考虑了买方的最佳利益。","D":"不适当，因为律师向买方作出虚假陈述。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。“律师在代理委托人过程中不得明知而：(a) 向第三人作出虚假的重大事实或法律陈述。”规则 4.1(a)。本题中，律师知道产权缺陷，却仍告诉买方这是一笔非常划算的交易，实质上是在实施欺诈。A 不正确。这又是“由委托人提出”型错误选项的一种变体。B 不正确。鉴于产权缺陷使陈述成为彻底谎言，律师所说并非意见。C 不正确。本题中，律师没有仅仅暗示什么，而是明知购买房产将一文不值，仍直接向买方说谎。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-022","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":22,"topic":"Model Rule 7.2(b)","question":"An attorney and a doctor entered into a reciprocal referral arrangement. The attorney agreed to prominently display ads for the doctor in her office, and to mention the doctor to all of her clients who requested a doctor recommendation. In return, the doctor agreed to prominently display ads for the attorney’s firm in his office and to recommend the attorney to any of his patients who indicated a need for legal services. The agreement was not exclusive, and the clients and patients were informed of the advertising agreement. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney advertised for the doctor.","B":"Yes, because the doctor gave recommendations regarding legal services.","C":"No, because the patients and clients were informed of the agreement.","D":"No, because the attorney did not pay the doctor for the recommendations."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2(b), an attorney “shall not give anything of value to a person for recommending the lawyer’s services.” Here, the agreement with the doctor clearly violates this Rule, since the doctor is receiving valuable advertising space and recommendations for his recommendations regarding the attorney. B is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney is giving the doctor something of value for recommending the attorney’s services, not that the attorney asked the doctor to put up some ads. C is incorrect. Remember that many wrong answers on the MPRE will set up a “no-harm, no-foul” situation. Even if the patients and clients knew of the arrangement, this would not cure the problem of the attorney giving the doctor something of value for recommending the attorney’s services. D is incorrect. As stated above, the Rule is concerned with giving someone “anything of value” for recommending the lawyer’s services, not simply whether the lawyer is giving that person “money.”","rule":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2(b), an attorney “shall not give anything of value to a person for recommending the lawyer’s services.” Here, the agreement with the doctor clearly violates this Rule, since the doctor is receiving valuable advertising space and recommendations for his recommendations regarding the attorney. B is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney is giving the doctor something of value for recommending the attorney’s services, not that the attorney asked the doctor to put up some ads. C is incorrect. Remember that many wrong answers on the MPRE will set up a “no-harm, no-foul” situation. Even if the patients and clients knew of the arrangement, this would not cure the problem of the attorney giving the doctor something of value for recommending the attorney’s services. D is incorrect. As stated above, the Rule is concerned with giving someone “anything of value” for recommending the lawyer’s services, not simply whether the lawyer is giving that person “money.”","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2(b), an attorney “shall not give anything of value to a person for recommending the lawyer’s services.” Here, the agreement with the doctor clearly violates this Rule, since the doctor is receiving valuable advertising space and recommendations for his recommendations regarding the attorney. B is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney is giving the doctor something of value for recommending the attorney’s services, not that the attorney asked the doctor to put up some ads. C is incorrect. Remember that many wrong answers on the MPRE will set up a “no-harm, no-foul” situation. Even if the patients and clients knew of the arrangement, this would not cure the problem of the attorney giving the doctor something of value for recommending the attorney’s services. D is incorrect. As stated above, the Rule is concerned with giving someone “anything of value” for recommending the lawyer’s services, not simply whether the lawyer is giving that person “money.”","analysis":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.2(b), an attorney “shall not give anything of value to a person for recommending the lawyer’s services.” Here, the agreement with the doctor clearly violates this Rule, since the doctor is receiving valuable advertising space and recommendations for his recommendations regarding the attorney. B is incorrect. The issue here is that the attorney is giving the doctor something of value for recommending the attorney’s services, not that the attorney asked the doctor to put up some ads. C is incorrect. Remember that many wrong answers on the MPRE will set up a “no-harm, no-foul” situation. Even if the patients and clients knew of the arrangement, this would not cure the problem of the attorney giving the doctor something of value for recommending the attorney’s services. D is incorrect. As stated above, the Rule is concerned with giving someone “anything of value” for recommending the lawyer’s services, not simply whether the lawyer is giving that person “money.”","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.2(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[215],"sourceAnswerPages":[310],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师与一名医生达成相互推荐安排。律师同意在其办公室显眼位置展示医生广告，并向所有请求推荐医生的委托人提及该医生。作为交换，医生同意在其诊所显眼位置展示该律师事务所的广告，并向任何表示需要法律服务的患者推荐律师。协议并非排他性协议，委托人与患者也都被告知广告安排。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师为医生做广告。","B":"是，因为医生对法律服务作出推荐。","C":"否，因为患者和委托人都被告知该协议。","D":"否，因为律师没有为推荐向医生支付金钱。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。根据《职业行为示范规则》7.2(b)，律师“不得为了他人推荐律师服务而向其给予任何有价值之物”。本题中，与医生达成的协议显然违反该规则，因为医生以推荐律师为交换，获得了有价值的广告空间和推荐机会。B 不正确。本题问题在于律师为了让医生推荐律师服务而给予其有价值之物，而不在于律师请求医生张贴广告。C 不正确。请记住，MPRE 的许多错误答案会设计成“没有损害，就没有违规”的情形。即使患者和委托人知道安排，也不能消除律师为了医生推荐律师服务而给予其有价值之物的问题。D 不正确。如上所述，规则关注的是为了推荐律师服务而给予他人“任何有价值之物”，而不只是律师是否给此人“金钱”。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-023","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":23,"topic":"Model Rule 1.2(d)","question":"An attorney was hired by a restaurant owner. The owner asked the attorney whether any restaurants or bars had been prosecuted for violating a local law that stated alcohol could not be served before noon on Sundays. He also asked whether the law’s $50 fine could be imposed for every sale on a Sunday before noon. The attorney accurately told the client that the fine could only be imposed once for each Sunday, not for each transaction, and that no one had been prosecuted under the law. Several weeks later, the attorney learned that the owner’s restaurant had begun to have “Sunday booze breakfasts” where alcohol was served starting at 9 a.m. on Sundays. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney reasonably should have known that the information she gave the client would encourage him to violate the law.","B":"Yes, because the attorney aided the owner in violating the law.","C":"No, because the attorney merely told the owner factual information regarding the law.","D":"No, because the attorney was not acting as an officer of the court."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. Although a lawyer is not allowed to counsel or assist a client in criminal or fraudulent conduct, a lawyer may help a client determine the validity, scope, meaning, or application of the law to a client’s particular situation. Model Rule 1.2(d). Here, the attorney is not helping the owner break the law; she is simply stating facts regarding the law’s fine and enforcement. A is incorrect. As long as a lawyer is not helping a client to violate the law, he or she is not required to make a guess as to how that client is going to use the lawyer’s advice, nor is the attorney subject to discipline if the client decides to use the lawyer’s advice to break the law. Here, the attorney is merely telling the owner facts regarding the law. B is incorrect. Here, all the attorney did was tell her client about the law and its history. She did nothing to aid him in violating the law. D is incorrect. The primary issue here is that the attorney was merely stating facts regarding the law. Remember that the MPRE often uses the idea of the attorney as an “officer of the court” in wrong answer choices.","rule":"C is the correct answer. Although a lawyer is not allowed to counsel or assist a client in criminal or fraudulent conduct, a lawyer may help a client determine the validity, scope, meaning, or application of the law to a client’s particular situation. Model Rule 1.2(d). Here, the attorney is not helping the owner break the law; she is simply stating facts regarding the law’s fine and enforcement. A is incorrect. As long as a lawyer is not helping a client to violate the law, he or she is not required to make a guess as to how that client is going to use the lawyer’s advice, nor is the attorney subject to discipline if the client decides to use the lawyer’s advice to break the law. Here, the attorney is merely telling the owner facts regarding the law. B is incorrect. Here, all the attorney did was tell her client about the law and its history. She did nothing to aid him in violating the law. D is incorrect. The primary issue here is that the attorney was merely stating facts regarding the law. Remember that the MPRE often uses the idea of the attorney as an “officer of the court” in wrong answer choices.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"C is the correct answer. Although a lawyer is not allowed to counsel or assist a client in criminal or fraudulent conduct, a lawyer may help a client determine the validity, scope, meaning, or application of the law to a client’s particular situation. Model Rule 1.2(d). Here, the attorney is not helping the owner break the law; she is simply stating facts regarding the law’s fine and enforcement. A is incorrect. As long as a lawyer is not helping a client to violate the law, he or she is not required to make a guess as to how that client is going to use the lawyer’s advice, nor is the attorney subject to discipline if the client decides to use the lawyer’s advice to break the law. Here, the attorney is merely telling the owner facts regarding the law. B is incorrect. Here, all the attorney did was tell her client about the law and its history. She did nothing to aid him in violating the law. D is incorrect. The primary issue here is that the attorney was merely stating facts regarding the law. Remember that the MPRE often uses the idea of the attorney as an “officer of the court” in wrong answer choices.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. Although a lawyer is not allowed to counsel or assist a client in criminal or fraudulent conduct, a lawyer may help a client determine the validity, scope, meaning, or application of the law to a client’s particular situation. Model Rule 1.2(d). Here, the attorney is not helping the owner break the law; she is simply stating facts regarding the law’s fine and enforcement. A is incorrect. As long as a lawyer is not helping a client to violate the law, he or she is not required to make a guess as to how that client is going to use the lawyer’s advice, nor is the attorney subject to discipline if the client decides to use the lawyer’s advice to break the law. Here, the attorney is merely telling the owner facts regarding the law. B is incorrect. Here, all the attorney did was tell her client about the law and its history. She did nothing to aid him in violating the law. D is incorrect. The primary issue here is that the attorney was merely stating facts regarding the law. Remember that the MPRE often uses the idea of the attorney as an “officer of the court” in wrong answer choices.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.2(d)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[215,216],"sourceAnswerPages":[311],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名餐厅所有者聘请律师。他询问律师，是否有餐厅或酒吧曾因违反一项禁止星期日中午前供应酒精饮料的当地法律而受到起诉。他还询问，该法规定的 50 美元罚款能否针对星期日中午前的每一笔销售分别施加。律师准确告知委托人，罚款只能按每一个星期日施加一次，不能按每笔交易施加，而且至今无人因该法律受到起诉。数周后，律师得知餐厅开始举行“星期日酒精早餐”，每个星期日上午 9 时开始供应酒精。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师理应知道，她提供的信息会鼓励委托人违法。","B":"是，因为律师协助所有者违法。","C":"否，因为律师只是向所有者说明有关法律的事实信息。","D":"否，因为律师当时不是以法院工作人员身份行事。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。律师虽然不得建议或协助委托人实施犯罪或欺诈行为，但可以帮助委托人判断某项法律对其具体情况而言是否有效、适用范围如何、含义为何以及如何适用。《职业行为示范规则》1.2(d)。本题中，律师没有帮助所有者违法，只是陈述罚款和执法历史的事实。A 不正确。只要律师没有帮助委托人违法，就不必猜测委托人会如何利用律师意见；即使委托人决定利用意见违法，律师也不因此应受纪律处分。本题中，律师只是告知所有者有关法律的事实。B 不正确。律师所做的只是说明法律及其历史，没有采取任何行动协助违法。D 不正确。首要问题是律师只是在陈述有关法律的事实。请记住，MPRE 经常在错误选项中使用律师是“法院工作人员”的说法。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-024","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":24,"topic":"Model Rule 5.5(a)","question":"An attorney and a financial planner decided to form a partnership for the purpose of providing clients with legal and financial services. The announcement of the proposed partnership, the firm stationery, and all public directory listings clearly state that the financial planner is a certified financial planner and that the attorney is a lawyer. The attorney is the only person in the partnership who gives legal advice. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because there is no indication that clients sign anything consenting to the partnership arrangement.","B":"Yes, because the partnership provides legal services.","C":"No, because the attorney is the only person in the partnership who gives advice on legal matters.","D":"No, because all public communications clearly state that the financial planner is a certified financial planner and the attorney is a lawyer."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not form a partnership with a nonlawyer if any of the activities of the partnership consist of the “practice of law.” A lawyer who does so is assisting another in the unauthorized practice of law, and is subject to discipline. Model Rule 5.5(a). While the attorney may be the only person giving legal advice, the partnership itself is in fact delivering legal services. A is incorrect. As always, a client’s consent does not allow an attorney to violate the Rules. C is incorrect. The important point is that the partnership is providing legal services. It makes no difference that the attorney is the only one in the partnership giving legal advice. D is incorrect. The fact that the firm’s materials clearly delineate each partner’s role does not change the overall analysis.","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not form a partnership with a nonlawyer if any of the activities of the partnership consist of the “practice of law.” A lawyer who does so is assisting another in the unauthorized practice of law, and is subject to discipline. Model Rule 5.5(a). While the attorney may be the only person giving legal advice, the partnership itself is in fact delivering legal services. A is incorrect. As always, a client’s consent does not allow an attorney to violate the Rules. C is incorrect. The important point is that the partnership is providing legal services. It makes no difference that the attorney is the only one in the partnership giving legal advice. D is incorrect. The fact that the firm’s materials clearly delineate each partner’s role does not change the overall analysis.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not form a partnership with a nonlawyer if any of the activities of the partnership consist of the “practice of law.” A lawyer who does so is assisting another in the unauthorized practice of law, and is subject to discipline. Model Rule 5.5(a). While the attorney may be the only person giving legal advice, the partnership itself is in fact delivering legal services. A is incorrect. As always, a client’s consent does not allow an attorney to violate the Rules. C is incorrect. The important point is that the partnership is providing legal services. It makes no difference that the attorney is the only one in the partnership giving legal advice. D is incorrect. The fact that the firm’s materials clearly delineate each partner’s role does not change the overall analysis.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer shall not form a partnership with a nonlawyer if any of the activities of the partnership consist of the “practice of law.” A lawyer who does so is assisting another in the unauthorized practice of law, and is subject to discipline. Model Rule 5.5(a). While the attorney may be the only person giving legal advice, the partnership itself is in fact delivering legal services. A is incorrect. As always, a client’s consent does not allow an attorney to violate the Rules. C is incorrect. The important point is that the partnership is providing legal services. It makes no difference that the attorney is the only one in the partnership giving legal advice. D is incorrect. The fact that the firm’s materials clearly delineate each partner’s role does not change the overall analysis.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.5(a)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[216],"sourceAnswerPages":[311],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师与一名理财规划师决定组成合伙组织，为委托人提供法律及财务服务。拟议合伙组织的公告、事务所信笺及所有公开名录均明确说明，理财规划师是认证理财规划师，律师是执业律师。该律师是合伙组织中唯一提供法律意见的人。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为没有迹象表明委托人签署了同意该合伙安排的文件。","B":"是，因为该合伙组织提供法律服务。","C":"否，因为该律师是合伙组织中唯一就法律事项提供意见的人。","D":"否，因为所有公开沟通均明确说明理财规划师是认证理财规划师，律师是执业律师。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。如果合伙组织的任何活动构成“法律执业活动”，律师就不得与非律师组成该合伙组织。这样做的律师是在协助他人擅自从事法律执业活动，并会受到纪律处分。《职业行为示范规则》5.5(a)。律师虽然可能是唯一提供法律意见的人，但合伙组织本身事实上正在提供法律服务。A 不正确。委托人同意始终不能使律师获得违反规则的权利。C 不正确。关键是合伙组织提供法律服务。律师是其中唯一提供法律意见的人并不重要。D 不正确。事务所材料明确区分每名合伙人的角色，不会改变整体分析。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-025","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":25,"topic":"Model Rule 3.9","question":"A bond-trading firm hired an attorney under a general retainer agreement. The firm told the attorney that a congressional committee was holding hearings concerning the extent of regulation in bond-trading industry. The firm wanted the attorney to testify that the industry was overregulated. The attorney agreed with the firm regarding the regulation issue. The attorney subsequently testified before the relevant congressional committee. The attorney registered his appearance under his own name and did not disclose that he was appearing on behalf of a client. Afterwards, he billed the firm for his appearance. Was the attorney’s conduct improper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the firm paid the attorney for his testimony.","B":"Yes, because the attorney failed to disclose that he was testifying in representative capacity.","C":"No, because the attorney is allowed to engage in political activity under his own name.","D":"No, because the attorney agreed with the firm."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer representing a client before a legislative body or administrative agency in a nonadjudicative proceeding shall disclose that the appearance is in a representative capacity.” Model Rule 3.9. Consequently, the attorney should have disclosed that he was appearing on behalf of the firm. A is incorrect. The issue here is the attorney’s failure to disclose that he was appearing in a representative capacity. The fact he was paid for the testimony is irrelevant to the primary issue. C is incorrect. While a lawyer may be allowed to engage in political activity under his or her own name as long as such activity does not violate the Rules, the important point here is that he is acting at the behest of the firm. D is incorrect. Although the attorney may agree with his client, he is still appearing at the committee on the firm’s behalf because they asked him to do so.","rule":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer representing a client before a legislative body or administrative agency in a nonadjudicative proceeding shall disclose that the appearance is in a representative capacity.” Model Rule 3.9. Consequently, the attorney should have disclosed that he was appearing on behalf of the firm. A is incorrect. The issue here is the attorney’s failure to disclose that he was appearing in a representative capacity. The fact he was paid for the testimony is irrelevant to the primary issue. C is incorrect. While a lawyer may be allowed to engage in political activity under his or her own name as long as such activity does not violate the Rules, the important point here is that he is acting at the behest of the firm. D is incorrect. Although the attorney may agree with his client, he is still appearing at the committee on the firm’s behalf because they asked him to do so.","irac":{"issue":"Was the attorney’s conduct improper?","rule":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer representing a client before a legislative body or administrative agency in a nonadjudicative proceeding shall disclose that the appearance is in a representative capacity.” Model Rule 3.9. Consequently, the attorney should have disclosed that he was appearing on behalf of the firm. A is incorrect. The issue here is the attorney’s failure to disclose that he was appearing in a representative capacity. The fact he was paid for the testimony is irrelevant to the primary issue. C is incorrect. While a lawyer may be allowed to engage in political activity under his or her own name as long as such activity does not violate the Rules, the important point here is that he is acting at the behest of the firm. D is incorrect. Although the attorney may agree with his client, he is still appearing at the committee on the firm’s behalf because they asked him to do so.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer representing a client before a legislative body or administrative agency in a nonadjudicative proceeding shall disclose that the appearance is in a representative capacity.” Model Rule 3.9. Consequently, the attorney should have disclosed that he was appearing on behalf of the firm. A is incorrect. The issue here is the attorney’s failure to disclose that he was appearing in a representative capacity. The fact he was paid for the testimony is irrelevant to the primary issue. C is incorrect. While a lawyer may be allowed to engage in political activity under his or her own name as long as such activity does not violate the Rules, the important point here is that he is acting at the behest of the firm. D is incorrect. Although the attorney may agree with his client, he is still appearing at the committee on the firm’s behalf because they asked him to do so.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.9"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[216,217],"sourceAnswerPages":[311,312],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家债券交易公司根据一般聘用协议聘请一名律师。公司告知律师，一个国会委员会正就债券交易行业的监管程度举行听证。公司希望律师作证说明行业受到过度监管。律师在监管问题上同意公司观点，随后在有关国会委员会前作证。他以个人名义登记出席，没有披露自己代表委托人出席。事后，他就出席向公司开具账单。律师的行为是否不适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为公司为律师的证言支付报酬。","B":"是，因为律师没有披露自己以代表他人的身份作证。","C":"否，因为律师可以用个人名义参加政治活动。","D":"否，因为律师同意公司观点。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。“律师代表委托人在立法机关或行政机构的非裁判程序中出席时，应披露其以代表他人的身份出席。”《职业行为示范规则》3.9。因此，律师本应披露自己代表公司出席。A 不正确。本题问题是律师没有披露其代表身份，为证言获得报酬与首要问题无关。C 不正确。律师虽然可以用自己名义参与不违反规则的政治活动，但本题关键是他依公司要求行事。D 不正确。律师即使认同委托人观点，仍是在公司请求下代表公司出席委员会。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-026","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":26,"topic":"Rule 2.5(A) / Rule 2.5","question":"The rules of the judicial district in which a judge sits state that parties are allowed two postponements as of right, but any additional postponements should only be approved if the requesting party convinces the judge that a postponement is appropriate. The judge routinely grants postponements because he believes it allows the parties a longer time to reach a settlement and ultimately does little to affect the outcome. Are the judge’s actions improper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the judge should expedite the matters that appear before him.","B":"Yes, because judges should not try to force parties to settle.","C":"No, because the judge has discretion in how to manage his courtroom schedule.","D":"No, because postponements do little to affect the ultimate outcome of the case."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. A judge is required to perform his or her duties diligently. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.5(A). Importantly, issues should be resolved without unnecessary cost or delay. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.5, Comment [4]. Here, the judge is granting postponements without any consideration of the individual cases, likely leading to unnecessary cost and delay for the parties involved. B is incorrect. The clear focus of the question is on the routine postponements. This question does not address that issue and should be discarded. Additionally, simply granting a postponement is not forcing a party to do anything, so don’t read some kind of unlawful pressure into this answer choice. C is incorrect. While a judge does have discretion in managing the schedule in his or her courtroom, he or she cannot manage it in such a way as to create unnecessary cost or delay. D is incorrect. While the postponements may not affect the ultimate outcome, they are still likely to cause unnecessary cost and delay.","rule":"A is the correct answer. A judge is required to perform his or her duties diligently. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.5(A). Importantly, issues should be resolved without unnecessary cost or delay. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.5, Comment [4]. Here, the judge is granting postponements without any consideration of the individual cases, likely leading to unnecessary cost and delay for the parties involved. B is incorrect. The clear focus of the question is on the routine postponements. This question does not address that issue and should be discarded. Additionally, simply granting a postponement is not forcing a party to do anything, so don’t read some kind of unlawful pressure into this answer choice. C is incorrect. While a judge does have discretion in managing the schedule in his or her courtroom, he or she cannot manage it in such a way as to create unnecessary cost or delay. D is incorrect. While the postponements may not affect the ultimate outcome, they are still likely to cause unnecessary cost and delay.","irac":{"issue":"Are the judge’s actions improper?","rule":"A is the correct answer. A judge is required to perform his or her duties diligently. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.5(A). Importantly, issues should be resolved without unnecessary cost or delay. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.5, Comment [4]. Here, the judge is granting postponements without any consideration of the individual cases, likely leading to unnecessary cost and delay for the parties involved. B is incorrect. The clear focus of the question is on the routine postponements. This question does not address that issue and should be discarded. Additionally, simply granting a postponement is not forcing a party to do anything, so don’t read some kind of unlawful pressure into this answer choice. C is incorrect. While a judge does have discretion in managing the schedule in his or her courtroom, he or she cannot manage it in such a way as to create unnecessary cost or delay. D is incorrect. While the postponements may not affect the ultimate outcome, they are still likely to cause unnecessary cost and delay.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. A judge is required to perform his or her duties diligently. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.5(A). Importantly, issues should be resolved without unnecessary cost or delay. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.5, Comment [4]. Here, the judge is granting postponements without any consideration of the individual cases, likely leading to unnecessary cost and delay for the parties involved. B is incorrect. The clear focus of the question is on the routine postponements. This question does not address that issue and should be discarded. Additionally, simply granting a postponement is not forcing a party to do anything, so don’t read some kind of unlawful pressure into this answer choice. C is incorrect. While a judge does have discretion in managing the schedule in his or her courtroom, he or she cannot manage it in such a way as to create unnecessary cost or delay. D is incorrect. While the postponements may not affect the ultimate outcome, they are still likely to cause unnecessary cost and delay.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 2.5(A)","Rule 2.5"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[217],"sourceAnswerPages":[312],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"某法官所在司法区的规则规定，当事人有权当然获得两次延期；任何额外延期只有在申请方说服法官延期适当时才应获准。该法官通常都会批准延期，因为他认为这会给予各方更多时间达成和解，最终对结果影响很小。该法官的行为是否不适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为法官应加快处理其面前的事项。","B":"是，因为法官不应试图迫使当事人和解。","C":"否，因为法官有权酌情管理法庭日程。","D":"否，因为延期对案件最终结果影响很小。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。法官必须勤勉履行职责。《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.5(A)。尤其是，应当在不产生不必要费用或延误的情况下解决问题。规则 2.5 评注 [4]。本题中，法官不考虑个案情况就批准延期，很可能给有关当事人造成不必要的费用和延误。B 不正确。题目明确关注例行延期。该选项没有处理这一问题，应予排除。此外，仅仅批准延期并不是迫使当事人做任何事情，不要在本选项中读入某种非法压力。C 不正确。法官虽然有权酌情管理法庭日程，却不能以造成不必要费用或延误的方式管理。D 不正确。延期虽然可能不影响最终结果，仍很可能造成不必要费用和延误。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-027","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":27,"topic":"Model Rule 8.3(b)","question":"An attorney regularly appears before a trial court judge who is running for reelection in six months. Over the past year, the attorney has noticed that the judge has become increasingly ill tempered and erratic on the bench. Several lawyers she knows are automatically filing requests for judicial substitution whenever a case in which they are to appear is assigned to the judge. The attorney’s law partners suggested that she also file requests for judicial substitution whenever one of her cases is assigned to the judge. Because of the judge’s respected standing, they believe reporting the judge might hurt their clients in the eyes of other members of the judiciary. Additionally, they believe the judge is unlikely to win reelection. Should the attorney report the judge to the authorities?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because of the judge’s courtroom conduct.","B":"Yes, because all the lawyers filing requests for judicial substitution causes unnecessary cost and delay.","C":"No, because reporting the judge may hurt her clients.","D":"No, because the judge is unlikely to be reelected."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer who knows that a judge has committed a violation of applicable rules of judicial conduct that raises a substantial question as to the judge’s fitness for office shall inform the appropriate authority.” Model Rule 8.3(b). Since the judge is behaving erratically, the attorney has a duty to report it. B is incorrect. The issue here is not unnecessary cost and delay, but the fact the judge may not be fit for his office. C is incorrect. While reporting the judge may hurt her clients in some abstract way, this is not enough of a reason to not report the judge’s conduct to the authorities. D is incorrect. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Additionally, even if he doesn’t get reelected, he’ll be hearing cases for at least another six months.","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer who knows that a judge has committed a violation of applicable rules of judicial conduct that raises a substantial question as to the judge’s fitness for office shall inform the appropriate authority.” Model Rule 8.3(b). Since the judge is behaving erratically, the attorney has a duty to report it. B is incorrect. The issue here is not unnecessary cost and delay, but the fact the judge may not be fit for his office. C is incorrect. While reporting the judge may hurt her clients in some abstract way, this is not enough of a reason to not report the judge’s conduct to the authorities. D is incorrect. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Additionally, even if he doesn’t get reelected, he’ll be hearing cases for at least another six months.","irac":{"issue":"Should the attorney report the judge to the authorities?","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer who knows that a judge has committed a violation of applicable rules of judicial conduct that raises a substantial question as to the judge’s fitness for office shall inform the appropriate authority.” Model Rule 8.3(b). Since the judge is behaving erratically, the attorney has a duty to report it. B is incorrect. The issue here is not unnecessary cost and delay, but the fact the judge may not be fit for his office. C is incorrect. While reporting the judge may hurt her clients in some abstract way, this is not enough of a reason to not report the judge’s conduct to the authorities. D is incorrect. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Additionally, even if he doesn’t get reelected, he’ll be hearing cases for at least another six months.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer who knows that a judge has committed a violation of applicable rules of judicial conduct that raises a substantial question as to the judge’s fitness for office shall inform the appropriate authority.” Model Rule 8.3(b). Since the judge is behaving erratically, the attorney has a duty to report it. B is incorrect. The issue here is not unnecessary cost and delay, but the fact the judge may not be fit for his office. C is incorrect. While reporting the judge may hurt her clients in some abstract way, this is not enough of a reason to not report the judge’s conduct to the authorities. D is incorrect. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Additionally, even if he doesn’t get reelected, he’ll be hearing cases for at least another six months.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 8.3(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[217,218],"sourceAnswerPages":[312,313],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师经常在一名将于六个月后竞选连任的初审法官面前出庭。过去一年中，律师注意到法官在法官席上的脾气越来越暴躁，行为也越来越反常。她认识的数名律师只要自己的案件被分配给该法官，就会自动申请更换法官。该律师的事务所合伙人建议她也这样做。考虑该法官备受尊重的地位，他们认为举报法官可能使其他司法人员对其委托人产生不利看法。此外，他们认为法官不太可能连任。该律师是否应向有关机关举报法官？","choicesZh":{"A":"应当，因为法官在法庭上的行为。","B":"应当，因为所有律师申请更换法官会造成不必要费用和延误。","C":"不应当，因为举报法官可能损害其委托人利益。","D":"不应当，因为法官不太可能连任。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。“律师知道法官违反适用司法行为规则，而且该违反会对法官是否适任产生重大疑问时，应通知适当机关。”《职业行为示范规则》8.3(b)。该法官行为反常，因此律师有义务举报。B 不正确。本题问题不是不必要费用和延误，而是法官可能不适任。C 不正确。举报法官虽然在抽象意义上可能损害委托人，但这不足以成为不向有关机关举报法官行为的理由。D 不正确。这又是“没有损害，就没有违规”型错误选项。此外，即使法官没有连任，未来至少六个月仍会继续审理案件。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-028","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":28,"topic":"Model Rule 1.7","question":"An attorney currently represents a plaintiff in a suit to recover for breach of contract. The plaintiff also has pending a personal injury lawsuit against his neighbor. However, the plaintiff is represented by another attorney in the personal injury matter. The neighbor has asked the attorney to represent him in the plaintiff’s personal injury claim. The neighbor knows that the attorney represents the plaintiff in the contract action. Is it improper for the attorney to represent the neighbor in the personal injury claim?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because both matters could be appealed to the same court and force the attorney to argue two conflicting causes of action.","B":"Yes, because the attorney is representing the plaintiff in the contract matter.","C":"No, because the neighbor knows that the attorney represents the plaintiff in the contract action.","D":"No, because one claim is in contract and the other is in tort."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer may not represent one client if it will be directly adverse to another client without the client’s informed consent. Model Rule 1.7. This is true even if the lawyer would be acting as an advocate against the client in a matter unrelated to the one in which the lawyer represents the client. Model Rule 1.7, Comment [6]. Here, representing the neighbor in the personal injury claim would be directly adverse to the plaintiff. A is incorrect. The important point is that this situation could cause a conflict between two current clients, not that it might affect the attorney’s arguments in the claims. C is incorrect. While the neighbor might know, there is no indication that the plaintiff knows anything about this. D is incorrect. The conflict still exists even if one action is in an area of the law that is different from the other.","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer may not represent one client if it will be directly adverse to another client without the client’s informed consent. Model Rule 1.7. This is true even if the lawyer would be acting as an advocate against the client in a matter unrelated to the one in which the lawyer represents the client. Model Rule 1.7, Comment [6]. Here, representing the neighbor in the personal injury claim would be directly adverse to the plaintiff. A is incorrect. The important point is that this situation could cause a conflict between two current clients, not that it might affect the attorney’s arguments in the claims. C is incorrect. While the neighbor might know, there is no indication that the plaintiff knows anything about this. D is incorrect. The conflict still exists even if one action is in an area of the law that is different from the other.","irac":{"issue":"Is it improper for the attorney to represent the neighbor in the personal injury claim?","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer may not represent one client if it will be directly adverse to another client without the client’s informed consent. Model Rule 1.7. This is true even if the lawyer would be acting as an advocate against the client in a matter unrelated to the one in which the lawyer represents the client. Model Rule 1.7, Comment [6]. Here, representing the neighbor in the personal injury claim would be directly adverse to the plaintiff. A is incorrect. The important point is that this situation could cause a conflict between two current clients, not that it might affect the attorney’s arguments in the claims. C is incorrect. While the neighbor might know, there is no indication that the plaintiff knows anything about this. D is incorrect. The conflict still exists even if one action is in an area of the law that is different from the other.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer may not represent one client if it will be directly adverse to another client without the client’s informed consent. Model Rule 1.7. This is true even if the lawyer would be acting as an advocate against the client in a matter unrelated to the one in which the lawyer represents the client. Model Rule 1.7, Comment [6]. Here, representing the neighbor in the personal injury claim would be directly adverse to the plaintiff. A is incorrect. The important point is that this situation could cause a conflict between two current clients, not that it might affect the attorney’s arguments in the claims. C is incorrect. While the neighbor might know, there is no indication that the plaintiff knows anything about this. D is incorrect. The conflict still exists even if one action is in an area of the law that is different from the other.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.7"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[218,219],"sourceAnswerPages":[313],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师目前代理原告提起违约诉讼。原告还针对邻居有一宗待决人身伤害诉讼，但在人身伤害事项中由另一名律师代理。邻居请求该律师在人身伤害索赔中代理自己。邻居知道该律师正在合同诉讼中代理原告。律师在人身伤害索赔中代理邻居是否不适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为两项事务都可能上诉至同一法院，迫使律师为两项相互冲突的诉因进行辩论。","B":"是，因为该律师正在合同事项中代理原告。","C":"否，因为邻居知道该律师正在合同诉讼中代理原告。","D":"否，因为一项是合同索赔，另一项是侵权索赔。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。未经委托人知情同意，律师不得代理一名委托人直接对抗另一名委托人。《职业行为示范规则》1.7。即使律师在与其代理现委托人之事项无关的另一事项中对该委托人担任辩护人，这一规则仍然适用。规则 1.7 评注 [6]。本题中，在人身伤害索赔中代理邻居会直接不利于原告。A 不正确。关键是该情形会在两名现委托人之间造成冲突，而不是可能影响律师在索赔中的论证。C 不正确。邻居虽然知情，但没有迹象表明原告知悉此事。D 不正确。即使两项诉讼属于不同法律领域，冲突仍然存在。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-029","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":29,"topic":"Model Rule 1.8(e)(1) / Model Rule 1.8","question":"An attorney is representing a plaintiff in a wrongful discharge case on a contingent fee basis. The plaintiff cannot afford the expenses of the investigation necessary to prepare for trial. The client asked the attorney to pay for these expenses. The attorney declined to pay, but offered to guarantee the client’s promissory note to a local bank in order to secure the necessary funds. May the attorney guarantee the promissory note?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the funds will be used for the investigation.","B":"Yes, because the attorney took the case on a contingent fee basis.","C":"No, because the attorney is lending her credit to the client.","D":"No, because the attorney is financing the plaintiff’s claim."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not provide financial assistance to a client in connection with pending or contemplated litigation, except that: (1) a lawyer may advance court costs and expenses of litigation, the repayment of which may be contingent on the outcome of the matter.” Model Rule 1.8(e)(1). Here, since the funds are being used for the investigation, they are proper under the Rules. B is incorrect. A lawyer may advance funds, but only if they are used for trial preparation. The contingent fee basis of the case does not make any and all fund advancements lawful, so A is a better answer. C is incorrect. “Lawyers may not subsidize lawsuits or administrative proceedings brought on behalf of their clients, including making or guaranteeing loans to their clients for living expenses, because to do so would encourage clients to pursue lawsuits that might not otherwise be brought, and because such assistance gives lawyers too great a financial stake in the litigation. These dangers do not warrant a prohibition on a lawyer lending a client court costs and litigation expenses, including the expenses of medical examination and the costs of obtaining and presenting evidence, because these advances are virtually indistinguishable from contingent fees and help ensure access to the courts.” Model Rule 1.8, Comment [10]. Here, the attorney is not financing the litigation, she is merely providing assistance with trial costs. D is incorrect. As long as the attorney’s financial assistance is used for court costs and expenses of litigation, the attorney may lend her credit to a client.","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not provide financial assistance to a client in connection with pending or contemplated litigation, except that: (1) a lawyer may advance court costs and expenses of litigation, the repayment of which may be contingent on the outcome of the matter.” Model Rule 1.8(e)(1). Here, since the funds are being used for the investigation, they are proper under the Rules. B is incorrect. A lawyer may advance funds, but only if they are used for trial preparation. The contingent fee basis of the case does not make any and all fund advancements lawful, so A is a better answer. C is incorrect. “Lawyers may not subsidize lawsuits or administrative proceedings brought on behalf of their clients, including making or guaranteeing loans to their clients for living expenses, because to do so would encourage clients to pursue lawsuits that might not otherwise be brought, and because such assistance gives lawyers too great a financial stake in the litigation. These dangers do not warrant a prohibition on a lawyer lending a client court costs and litigation expenses, including the expenses of medical examination and the costs of obtaining and presenting evidence, because these advances are virtually indistinguishable from contingent fees and help ensure access to the courts.” Model Rule 1.8, Comment [10]. Here, the attorney is not financing the litigation, she is merely providing assistance with trial costs. D is incorrect. As long as the attorney’s financial assistance is used for court costs and expenses of litigation, the attorney may lend her credit to a client.","irac":{"issue":"May the attorney guarantee the promissory note?","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not provide financial assistance to a client in connection with pending or contemplated litigation, except that: (1) a lawyer may advance court costs and expenses of litigation, the repayment of which may be contingent on the outcome of the matter.” Model Rule 1.8(e)(1). Here, since the funds are being used for the investigation, they are proper under the Rules. B is incorrect. A lawyer may advance funds, but only if they are used for trial preparation. The contingent fee basis of the case does not make any and all fund advancements lawful, so A is a better answer. C is incorrect. “Lawyers may not subsidize lawsuits or administrative proceedings brought on behalf of their clients, including making or guaranteeing loans to their clients for living expenses, because to do so would encourage clients to pursue lawsuits that might not otherwise be brought, and because such assistance gives lawyers too great a financial stake in the litigation. These dangers do not warrant a prohibition on a lawyer lending a client court costs and litigation expenses, including the expenses of medical examination and the costs of obtaining and presenting evidence, because these advances are virtually indistinguishable from contingent fees and help ensure access to the courts.” Model Rule 1.8, Comment [10]. Here, the attorney is not financing the litigation, she is merely providing assistance with trial costs. D is incorrect. As long as the attorney’s financial assistance is used for court costs and expenses of litigation, the attorney may lend her credit to a client.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not provide financial assistance to a client in connection with pending or contemplated litigation, except that: (1) a lawyer may advance court costs and expenses of litigation, the repayment of which may be contingent on the outcome of the matter.” Model Rule 1.8(e)(1). Here, since the funds are being used for the investigation, they are proper under the Rules. B is incorrect. A lawyer may advance funds, but only if they are used for trial preparation. The contingent fee basis of the case does not make any and all fund advancements lawful, so A is a better answer. C is incorrect. “Lawyers may not subsidize lawsuits or administrative proceedings brought on behalf of their clients, including making or guaranteeing loans to their clients for living expenses, because to do so would encourage clients to pursue lawsuits that might not otherwise be brought, and because such assistance gives lawyers too great a financial stake in the litigation. These dangers do not warrant a prohibition on a lawyer lending a client court costs and litigation expenses, including the expenses of medical examination and the costs of obtaining and presenting evidence, because these advances are virtually indistinguishable from contingent fees and help ensure access to the courts.” Model Rule 1.8, Comment [10]. Here, the attorney is not financing the litigation, she is merely providing assistance with trial costs. D is incorrect. As long as the attorney’s financial assistance is used for court costs and expenses of litigation, the attorney may lend her credit to a client.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.8(e)(1)","Model Rule 1.8"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[219],"sourceAnswerPages":[313,314],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师以风险代理费方式代理一宗违法解雇案件的原告。原告无力支付为准备审判所需的调查费用，请求律师支付。律师拒绝付款，但提出可以为委托人向当地银行开具的本票提供担保，以取得所需资金。律师能否为本票提供担保？","choicesZh":{"A":"能，因为资金将用于调查。","B":"能，因为律师以风险代理费方式接受案件。","C":"不能，因为律师正在把自己的信用借给委托人。","D":"不能，因为律师正在资助原告的主张。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。“律师不得就待决或拟议诉讼向委托人提供经济援助，但下列情形除外：(1) 律师可以垫付诉讼费和诉讼开支，而偿还可取决于事项结果。”《职业行为示范规则》1.8(e)(1)。本题中，资金用于调查，因此在规则下适当。B 不正确。律师可以垫付资金，但只能用于审判准备。采用风险代理费并不会使所有资金垫付都合法，因此 A 更好。C 不正确。“律师不得补贴代表委托人提起的诉讼或行政程序，包括向委托人提供生活费贷款或为此担保，因为这样会鼓励委托人提起原本可能不会提起的诉讼，也会使律师在诉讼中拥有过大的经济利益。不过，这些危险不足以禁止律师向委托人垫付诉讼费和诉讼开支，包括医学检查费用以及取得和提出证据的费用，因为这种垫付与风险代理费几乎无法区分，并有助于确保诉诸法院的机会。”规则 1.8 评注 [10]。本题中，律师并非资助诉讼，只是在审判费用方面提供协助。D 不正确。只要律师的经济援助用于诉讼费和诉讼开支，就可以把自己的信用借给委托人。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-030","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":30,"topic":"Model Rule 5.1 / Model Rule 1.1","question":"An attorney hired a recent law school graduate as an associate. For the first six months, the attorney carefully reviewed and revised the associate’s work. One day, the attorney told the associate that he would be going on vacation and was assigning her the representation of a plaintiff in a personal injury case that was going to trial while he was away. The associate had never conducted or observed a trial before and she was unfamiliar with the relevant law and procedure. She did not believe that she would have enough time to learn everything that she needed to know. Before the trial began, she met with the plaintiff and disclosed that this would be her first trial, but the plaintiff did not object. Although the associate prepared diligently, the plaintiff lost the trial. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the plaintiff lost the case.","B":"Yes, because the attorney did not ensure that the associate was competent to conduct the trial on her own.","C":"No, because the plaintiff did not object to the associate’s representation.","D":"No, because the attorney closely supervised the associate for six months."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. A newly admitted lawyer in a firm must have his or her work properly supervised by a more experienced lawyer to ensure that all lawyers in the firm conform to the Model Rules of Professional Conduct. Model Rule 5.1. Under Model Rule 1.1, a law firm owes a client a duty of competence, specifically the appropriate “legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness and preparation reasonably necessary for the representation.” Consequently, when a firm uses less-experienced lawyers to perform client work, the partners must supervise that work. In this case, the associate, who had never conducted or observed a trial, needed supervision. There was not enough time for the associate to become familiar with the relevant law and procedure, nor did she have the experience to competently handle the case. Importantly, the attorney did not take the proper precautions to make sure that the associate was adequately prepared to carry out the assignment. A is incorrect. The outcome of the trial is irrelevant to whether the associate was properly supervised. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. C is incorrect. The plaintiff’s apparent consent would not mitigate this Rule violation. As always, be wary of choices that imply a client can consent to a violation of the Rules. D is incorrect. While the attorney may have closely supervised the associate for six months, this did not change the fact she was not prepared to handle the trial.","rule":"B is the correct answer. A newly admitted lawyer in a firm must have his or her work properly supervised by a more experienced lawyer to ensure that all lawyers in the firm conform to the Model Rules of Professional Conduct. Model Rule 5.1. Under Model Rule 1.1, a law firm owes a client a duty of competence, specifically the appropriate “legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness and preparation reasonably necessary for the representation.” Consequently, when a firm uses less-experienced lawyers to perform client work, the partners must supervise that work. In this case, the associate, who had never conducted or observed a trial, needed supervision. There was not enough time for the associate to become familiar with the relevant law and procedure, nor did she have the experience to competently handle the case. Importantly, the attorney did not take the proper precautions to make sure that the associate was adequately prepared to carry out the assignment. A is incorrect. The outcome of the trial is irrelevant to whether the associate was properly supervised. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. C is incorrect. The plaintiff’s apparent consent would not mitigate this Rule violation. As always, be wary of choices that imply a client can consent to a violation of the Rules. D is incorrect. While the attorney may have closely supervised the associate for six months, this did not change the fact she was not prepared to handle the trial.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. A newly admitted lawyer in a firm must have his or her work properly supervised by a more experienced lawyer to ensure that all lawyers in the firm conform to the Model Rules of Professional Conduct. Model Rule 5.1. Under Model Rule 1.1, a law firm owes a client a duty of competence, specifically the appropriate “legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness and preparation reasonably necessary for the representation.” Consequently, when a firm uses less-experienced lawyers to perform client work, the partners must supervise that work. In this case, the associate, who had never conducted or observed a trial, needed supervision. There was not enough time for the associate to become familiar with the relevant law and procedure, nor did she have the experience to competently handle the case. Importantly, the attorney did not take the proper precautions to make sure that the associate was adequately prepared to carry out the assignment. A is incorrect. The outcome of the trial is irrelevant to whether the associate was properly supervised. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. C is incorrect. The plaintiff’s apparent consent would not mitigate this Rule violation. As always, be wary of choices that imply a client can consent to a violation of the Rules. D is incorrect. While the attorney may have closely supervised the associate for six months, this did not change the fact she was not prepared to handle the trial.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. A newly admitted lawyer in a firm must have his or her work properly supervised by a more experienced lawyer to ensure that all lawyers in the firm conform to the Model Rules of Professional Conduct. Model Rule 5.1. Under Model Rule 1.1, a law firm owes a client a duty of competence, specifically the appropriate “legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness and preparation reasonably necessary for the representation.” Consequently, when a firm uses less-experienced lawyers to perform client work, the partners must supervise that work. In this case, the associate, who had never conducted or observed a trial, needed supervision. There was not enough time for the associate to become familiar with the relevant law and procedure, nor did she have the experience to competently handle the case. Importantly, the attorney did not take the proper precautions to make sure that the associate was adequately prepared to carry out the assignment. A is incorrect. The outcome of the trial is irrelevant to whether the associate was properly supervised. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. C is incorrect. The plaintiff’s apparent consent would not mitigate this Rule violation. As always, be wary of choices that imply a client can consent to a violation of the Rules. D is incorrect. While the attorney may have closely supervised the associate for six months, this did not change the fact she was not prepared to handle the trial.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.1","Model Rule 1.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[219,220],"sourceAnswerPages":[314,315],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师聘用一位法学院应届毕业生担任助理律师。最初六个月，该律师仔细审阅并修改助理律师的工作。有一天，律师告诉助理，自己将去休假，并将一宗休假期间开庭的人身伤害案件原告代理任务交给她。助理律师此前从未进行或旁听过审判，也不熟悉相关法律和程序。她认为自己没有足够时间掌握所有必要知识。审判开始前，她与原告会面，披露这是自己第一次出庭审判，但原告没有反对。尽管助理律师勤勉准备，原告仍在审判中败诉。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为原告败诉。","B":"是，因为该律师没有确保助理律师具备独立进行审判的称职能力。","C":"否，因为原告没有反对由助理律师代理。","D":"否，因为该律师曾对助理律师进行六个月严密监督。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。为确保事务所所有律师均遵守《职业行为示范规则》，事务所中刚取得执业资格的律师必须由经验更丰富的律师对其工作进行适当监督。规则 5.1。根据规则 1.1，律师事务所对委托人负有称职代理义务，具体而言，必须具备该代理合理需要的“法律知识、技能、周全程度和准备”。因此，事务所安排经验较少的律师处理委托人事务时，合伙人必须监督其工作。本题中，该助理律师从未进行或旁听过审判，需要监督。她没有足够时间熟悉相关法律和程序，也缺少称职办理案件所需经验。关键是，该律师没有采取适当预防措施，确保助理律师已经充分准备好完成任务。A 不正确。审判结果与助理律师是否受到适当监督无关。这又是“没有损害，就没有违规”型错误选项。C 不正确。原告看似同意，不能减轻该项违反规则的行为。应始终警惕暗示委托人可以同意违反规则的选项。D 不正确。该律师虽曾严密监督助理六个月，却不能改变她尚未准备好独立进行审判这一事实。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-031","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":31,"topic":"Model Rule 2.1 / Model Rule 1.3","question":"An attorney represented a local amusement park in a personal injury claim brought by one of its customers. Although the park had a valid defense to the claim, the attorney advised the park to pay the damages requested by the customer in order to avoid bad publicity. Relying on the attorney’s advice, the park paid the claim. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney did not zealously advocate the interests of his client.","B":"Yes, because the park paid the claim although it had a valid defense.","C":"No, because the park accepted the attorney’s advice.","D":"No, because paying the claim avoided bad publicity."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. When a lawyer gives a client advice, he or she may rely on considerations other than those involved in giving purely technical legal advice. Model Rule 2.1. Consequently, it is proper for a lawyer to rely on moral, economic, and social factors in doing so, which is what the attorney did here when he took potential bad publicity into consideration. A is incorrect. This answer choice refers to the issue of a lawyer’s responsibility to “act with . . . zeal in advocacy.” Model Rule 1.3, Comment [1]. However, the issue is what is the proper scope for the advice a lawyer gives a client. Not only is it proper for a lawyer to refer to considerations other than the law in advising a client, he or she also has a responsibility to exercise independent professional judgment in advising a client and to advise the client in a straightforward, honest manner, in the best interests of the client. B is incorrect. Although the park may have had a valid defense, this did not mean the attorney violated the Rules by taking other factors into consideration when he gave the park advice. C is incorrect. While the park did accept the attorney’s advice, this would not excuse the attorney if his advice violated the Rules in some other manner. This is another example of a wrong answer choice based around the client’s actions.","rule":"D is the correct answer. When a lawyer gives a client advice, he or she may rely on considerations other than those involved in giving purely technical legal advice. Model Rule 2.1. Consequently, it is proper for a lawyer to rely on moral, economic, and social factors in doing so, which is what the attorney did here when he took potential bad publicity into consideration. A is incorrect. This answer choice refers to the issue of a lawyer’s responsibility to “act with . . . zeal in advocacy.” Model Rule 1.3, Comment [1]. However, the issue is what is the proper scope for the advice a lawyer gives a client. Not only is it proper for a lawyer to refer to considerations other than the law in advising a client, he or she also has a responsibility to exercise independent professional judgment in advising a client and to advise the client in a straightforward, honest manner, in the best interests of the client. B is incorrect. Although the park may have had a valid defense, this did not mean the attorney violated the Rules by taking other factors into consideration when he gave the park advice. C is incorrect. While the park did accept the attorney’s advice, this would not excuse the attorney if his advice violated the Rules in some other manner. This is another example of a wrong answer choice based around the client’s actions.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"D is the correct answer. When a lawyer gives a client advice, he or she may rely on considerations other than those involved in giving purely technical legal advice. Model Rule 2.1. Consequently, it is proper for a lawyer to rely on moral, economic, and social factors in doing so, which is what the attorney did here when he took potential bad publicity into consideration. A is incorrect. This answer choice refers to the issue of a lawyer’s responsibility to “act with . . . zeal in advocacy.” Model Rule 1.3, Comment [1]. However, the issue is what is the proper scope for the advice a lawyer gives a client. Not only is it proper for a lawyer to refer to considerations other than the law in advising a client, he or she also has a responsibility to exercise independent professional judgment in advising a client and to advise the client in a straightforward, honest manner, in the best interests of the client. B is incorrect. Although the park may have had a valid defense, this did not mean the attorney violated the Rules by taking other factors into consideration when he gave the park advice. C is incorrect. While the park did accept the attorney’s advice, this would not excuse the attorney if his advice violated the Rules in some other manner. This is another example of a wrong answer choice based around the client’s actions.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. When a lawyer gives a client advice, he or she may rely on considerations other than those involved in giving purely technical legal advice. Model Rule 2.1. Consequently, it is proper for a lawyer to rely on moral, economic, and social factors in doing so, which is what the attorney did here when he took potential bad publicity into consideration. A is incorrect. This answer choice refers to the issue of a lawyer’s responsibility to “act with . . . zeal in advocacy.” Model Rule 1.3, Comment [1]. However, the issue is what is the proper scope for the advice a lawyer gives a client. Not only is it proper for a lawyer to refer to considerations other than the law in advising a client, he or she also has a responsibility to exercise independent professional judgment in advising a client and to advise the client in a straightforward, honest manner, in the best interests of the client. B is incorrect. Although the park may have had a valid defense, this did not mean the attorney violated the Rules by taking other factors into consideration when he gave the park advice. C is incorrect. While the park did accept the attorney’s advice, this would not excuse the attorney if his advice violated the Rules in some other manner. This is another example of a wrong answer choice based around the client’s actions.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 2.1","Model Rule 1.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[220],"sourceAnswerPages":[315],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理当地一家游乐园处理一名顾客提出的人身伤害索赔。游乐园虽有有效抗辩，律师仍建议其支付顾客请求的损害赔偿，以避免负面报道。游乐园依律师建议支付了索赔。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师没有热忱主张委托人利益。","B":"是，因为游乐园虽有有效抗辩，仍支付了索赔。","C":"否，因为游乐园接受了律师建议。","D":"否，因为支付索赔避免了负面报道。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。律师向委托人提供意见时，可以考虑纯技术性法律意见以外的因素。《职业行为示范规则》2.1。因此，律师可以适当地考虑道德、经济和社会因素；本题律师考虑潜在负面报道正是如此。A 不正确。该选项涉及律师“在主张中……热忱行事”的责任。规则 1.3 评注 [1]。但本题问题是律师向委托人提供意见的适当范围。律师不仅可以在提供意见时提及法律以外的因素，也有责任行使独立专业判断，以直接、诚实且符合委托人最佳利益的方式提供意见。B 不正确。游乐园虽可能拥有有效抗辩，律师在提供意见时考虑其他因素并不因此违反规则。C 不正确。游乐园虽接受律师意见，但如果意见以其他方式违反规则，这不会使律师免责。这又是一个围绕委托人行为设计的错误选项。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-032","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":32,"topic":"Model Rule 8.4(c)","question":"An attorney, who is corporate counsel for a company, is investigating the possibility that an employee has been embezzling from the company. The attorney believes that if the employee were questioned, the employee would not answer truthfully if he knew the real purpose of the questions. The attorney plans to question the employee and falsely tell him that he is not a suspect and that his answers to the questions will be held in confidence. Are the attorney’s actions proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because legal proceedings are not yet pending.","B":"Yes, because the employee should reasonably understand that the attorney does not represent him.","C":"No, because the attorney’s conduct involves misrepresentation.","D":"No, because the attorney is corporate counsel for the company, and the employee is an employee of the company."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may not “engage in conduct involving dishonesty, fraud, deceit, or misrepresentation.” Model Rule 8.4(c). Here, the attorney is planning to falsely tell the employee he is not a suspect and that his answers will be held in confidence. A is incorrect. Even if no legal proceedings are pending, the attorney is still engaging in dishonest behavior. B is incorrect. The issue here is not which party the attorney represents, but the fact that the attorney is planning to misrepresent the nature of his questioning. D is incorrect. As stated above, the important issue is that the attorney is planning to lie.","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may not “engage in conduct involving dishonesty, fraud, deceit, or misrepresentation.” Model Rule 8.4(c). Here, the attorney is planning to falsely tell the employee he is not a suspect and that his answers will be held in confidence. A is incorrect. Even if no legal proceedings are pending, the attorney is still engaging in dishonest behavior. B is incorrect. The issue here is not which party the attorney represents, but the fact that the attorney is planning to misrepresent the nature of his questioning. D is incorrect. As stated above, the important issue is that the attorney is planning to lie.","irac":{"issue":"Are the attorney’s actions proper?","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may not “engage in conduct involving dishonesty, fraud, deceit, or misrepresentation.” Model Rule 8.4(c). Here, the attorney is planning to falsely tell the employee he is not a suspect and that his answers will be held in confidence. A is incorrect. Even if no legal proceedings are pending, the attorney is still engaging in dishonest behavior. B is incorrect. The issue here is not which party the attorney represents, but the fact that the attorney is planning to misrepresent the nature of his questioning. D is incorrect. As stated above, the important issue is that the attorney is planning to lie.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may not “engage in conduct involving dishonesty, fraud, deceit, or misrepresentation.” Model Rule 8.4(c). Here, the attorney is planning to falsely tell the employee he is not a suspect and that his answers will be held in confidence. A is incorrect. Even if no legal proceedings are pending, the attorney is still engaging in dishonest behavior. B is incorrect. The issue here is not which party the attorney represents, but the fact that the attorney is planning to misrepresent the nature of his questioning. D is incorrect. As stated above, the important issue is that the attorney is planning to lie.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 8.4(c)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[220],"sourceAnswerPages":[315,316],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名担任公司法律顾问的律师正在调查某雇员可能挪用公司资金。律师认为，如果雇员知道提问的真实目的，接受询问时就不会如实回答。律师计划询问雇员，并虚假地告诉他，他不是嫌疑人，而且其回答将予保密。律师的行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为法律程序尚未开始。","B":"适当，因为雇员理应理解该律师不代理自己。","C":"不适当，因为律师的行为涉及虚假陈述。","D":"不适当，因为律师是公司法律顾问，而雇员是公司雇员。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。律师不得“从事涉及不诚实、欺诈、欺骗或虚假陈述的行为”。《职业行为示范规则》8.4(c)。本题中，律师计划虚假地告诉雇员，他不是嫌疑人，而且其回答会予以保密。A 不正确。即使没有待决法律程序，律师仍在从事不诚实行为。B 不正确。本题问题不在于律师代理哪一方，而在于律师计划歪曲询问性质。D 不正确。如上所述，关键问题是律师计划说谎。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-033","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":33,"topic":"Model Rule 1.5(d) / Model Rule 1.8(i) / Model Rule 1.8(i)(2)","question":"A client asked an attorney for representation in a suit to establish the client’s ownership of certain intellectual property. The client did not have the necessary funds to pay the attorney’s usual rate. The client proposed instead to pay the attorney an amount in cash equal to 10 percent of the fees and royalties the client might recover as a result of the suit. The attorney accepted the proposal and took the case. The resulting payment is unlikely to exceed the fee the attorney would have earned from his normal fee agreement. Are the attorney’s actions proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the client proposed the fee arrangement.","B":"Yes, because the attorney may contract with the client for a reasonable contingent fee.","C":"No, because the resulting payment may exceed the attorney’s normal fee.","D":"No, because the attorney is acquiring a proprietary interest in the subject matter of the litigation."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. A fee may be contingent on the outcome of the matter for which the service is rendered, except in a matter in which a contingent fee involves a domestic matter or a criminal case. Model Rule 1.5(d). Here, there is nothing improper in the parties’ agreement, and it appears to be reasonable under the circumstances. A is incorrect. This is the classic “it was the client’s idea” wrong answer choice. Remember, simply because a client thinks something is a good idea does not make it proper under the Rules. C is incorrect. Although the overall fee may exceed the attorney’s normal fee, as long as the agreement is reasonable to the parties, there is nothing making such an agreement automatically improper. D is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.8(i), “a lawyer shall not acquire a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of litigation . . . except that the lawyer may . . . contract with a client for a reasonable contingent fee in a civil case.” Model Rule 1.8(i)(2). Consequently, there is no general ban against an attorney receiving a proprietary interest in a client’s cause of action, so long as it is part of a reasonable contingent fee in a civil case. Here, the agreement appears reasonable.","rule":"B is the correct answer. A fee may be contingent on the outcome of the matter for which the service is rendered, except in a matter in which a contingent fee involves a domestic matter or a criminal case. Model Rule 1.5(d). Here, there is nothing improper in the parties’ agreement, and it appears to be reasonable under the circumstances. A is incorrect. This is the classic “it was the client’s idea” wrong answer choice. Remember, simply because a client thinks something is a good idea does not make it proper under the Rules. C is incorrect. Although the overall fee may exceed the attorney’s normal fee, as long as the agreement is reasonable to the parties, there is nothing making such an agreement automatically improper. D is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.8(i), “a lawyer shall not acquire a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of litigation . . . except that the lawyer may . . . contract with a client for a reasonable contingent fee in a civil case.” Model Rule 1.8(i)(2). Consequently, there is no general ban against an attorney receiving a proprietary interest in a client’s cause of action, so long as it is part of a reasonable contingent fee in a civil case. Here, the agreement appears reasonable.","irac":{"issue":"Are the attorney’s actions proper?","rule":"B is the correct answer. A fee may be contingent on the outcome of the matter for which the service is rendered, except in a matter in which a contingent fee involves a domestic matter or a criminal case. Model Rule 1.5(d). Here, there is nothing improper in the parties’ agreement, and it appears to be reasonable under the circumstances. A is incorrect. This is the classic “it was the client’s idea” wrong answer choice. Remember, simply because a client thinks something is a good idea does not make it proper under the Rules. C is incorrect. Although the overall fee may exceed the attorney’s normal fee, as long as the agreement is reasonable to the parties, there is nothing making such an agreement automatically improper. D is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.8(i), “a lawyer shall not acquire a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of litigation . . . except that the lawyer may . . . contract with a client for a reasonable contingent fee in a civil case.” Model Rule 1.8(i)(2). Consequently, there is no general ban against an attorney receiving a proprietary interest in a client’s cause of action, so long as it is part of a reasonable contingent fee in a civil case. Here, the agreement appears reasonable.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. A fee may be contingent on the outcome of the matter for which the service is rendered, except in a matter in which a contingent fee involves a domestic matter or a criminal case. Model Rule 1.5(d). Here, there is nothing improper in the parties’ agreement, and it appears to be reasonable under the circumstances. A is incorrect. This is the classic “it was the client’s idea” wrong answer choice. Remember, simply because a client thinks something is a good idea does not make it proper under the Rules. C is incorrect. Although the overall fee may exceed the attorney’s normal fee, as long as the agreement is reasonable to the parties, there is nothing making such an agreement automatically improper. D is incorrect. Under Model Rule 1.8(i), “a lawyer shall not acquire a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of litigation . . . except that the lawyer may . . . contract with a client for a reasonable contingent fee in a civil case.” Model Rule 1.8(i)(2). Consequently, there is no general ban against an attorney receiving a proprietary interest in a client’s cause of action, so long as it is part of a reasonable contingent fee in a civil case. Here, the agreement appears reasonable.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.5(d)","Model Rule 1.8(i)","Model Rule 1.8(i)(2)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[220,221],"sourceAnswerPages":[316],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名委托人请求律师代理诉讼，以确认其对某些知识产权的所有权。委托人没有足够资金按律师惯常收费支付，因而提议改为以现金向律师支付一笔金额，等于他因诉讼可能追回的费用和特许权使用费的 10%。律师接受提议并承办案件。最终付款不太可能超过律师按正常收费协议可赚取的金额。律师的行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为收费安排由委托人提出。","B":"适当，因为律师可以与委托人约定合理的风险代理费。","C":"不适当，因为最终付款可能超过律师正常费用。","D":"不适当，因为律师正在取得诉讼标的中的所有权利益。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。除涉及家事事项或刑事案件外，费用可以取决于所提供服务之事项的结果。《职业行为示范规则》1.5(d)。本题中，双方协议没有不当之处，且在具体情形下显得合理。A 不正确。这是典型的“由委托人提出”型错误选项。请记住，仅仅因为委托人认为某事是好主意，并不会使其符合规则。C 不正确。即使总费用可能超过律师正常费用，只要协议对双方合理，就不会自动不当。D 不正确。根据规则 1.8(i)，“律师不得取得诉因或诉讼标的中的所有权利益……但律师可以……在民事案件中与委托人约定合理的风险代理费。”规则 1.8(i)(2)。因此，只要属于民事案件中的合理风险代理费安排，规则并不一般性禁止律师取得委托人诉因中的所有权利益。本题协议显得合理。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-034","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":34,"topic":"Model Rule 5.4(d)(2) / Model Rule 5.4","question":"An attorney joined a firm as a salaried associate. The firm engaged solely in the practice of law. The son-in-law of one of the partners, who is not a lawyer, is vice-president of the firm and office manager. He works under the direct supervision of his father-in-law, a named partner in the firm. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the partner’s son-in-law is an officer of the firm.","B":"Yes, because there is no indication client confidences are kept from the son-in-law.","C":"No, because the attorney is only a salaried employee.","D":"No, because the son-in-law is under the direct supervision of a named partner."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not practice with or in the form of a professional corporation or association authorized to practice law for a profit, if: . . . a nonlawyer is a corporate director or officer thereof or occupies the position of similar responsibility.” Model Rule 5.4(d)(2). Here, since the partner’s son-in-law is vice-president of the firm, his position in the firm violates the Rule. B is incorrect. The issue here has to do with the son-in-law’s leadership position in the firm, not the fact he may be privy to clients’ confidential information. C is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 prohibits lawyers from practicing with a corporation that is improperly sharing legal fees or decision-making responsibility with a nonlawyer. The Rule does not limit that prohibition to lawyers who only practice as members, shareholders, or partners. D is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 prohibits nonlawyers from being directors or officers. Consequently, it does not matter whether the son-in-law is being supervised by a named partner.","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not practice with or in the form of a professional corporation or association authorized to practice law for a profit, if: . . . a nonlawyer is a corporate director or officer thereof or occupies the position of similar responsibility.” Model Rule 5.4(d)(2). Here, since the partner’s son-in-law is vice-president of the firm, his position in the firm violates the Rule. B is incorrect. The issue here has to do with the son-in-law’s leadership position in the firm, not the fact he may be privy to clients’ confidential information. C is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 prohibits lawyers from practicing with a corporation that is improperly sharing legal fees or decision-making responsibility with a nonlawyer. The Rule does not limit that prohibition to lawyers who only practice as members, shareholders, or partners. D is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 prohibits nonlawyers from being directors or officers. Consequently, it does not matter whether the son-in-law is being supervised by a named partner.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not practice with or in the form of a professional corporation or association authorized to practice law for a profit, if: . . . a nonlawyer is a corporate director or officer thereof or occupies the position of similar responsibility.” Model Rule 5.4(d)(2). Here, since the partner’s son-in-law is vice-president of the firm, his position in the firm violates the Rule. B is incorrect. The issue here has to do with the son-in-law’s leadership position in the firm, not the fact he may be privy to clients’ confidential information. C is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 prohibits lawyers from practicing with a corporation that is improperly sharing legal fees or decision-making responsibility with a nonlawyer. The Rule does not limit that prohibition to lawyers who only practice as members, shareholders, or partners. D is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 prohibits nonlawyers from being directors or officers. Consequently, it does not matter whether the son-in-law is being supervised by a named partner.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not practice with or in the form of a professional corporation or association authorized to practice law for a profit, if: . . . a nonlawyer is a corporate director or officer thereof or occupies the position of similar responsibility.” Model Rule 5.4(d)(2). Here, since the partner’s son-in-law is vice-president of the firm, his position in the firm violates the Rule. B is incorrect. The issue here has to do with the son-in-law’s leadership position in the firm, not the fact he may be privy to clients’ confidential information. C is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 prohibits lawyers from practicing with a corporation that is improperly sharing legal fees or decision-making responsibility with a nonlawyer. The Rule does not limit that prohibition to lawyers who only practice as members, shareholders, or partners. D is incorrect. Model Rule 5.4 prohibits nonlawyers from being directors or officers. Consequently, it does not matter whether the son-in-law is being supervised by a named partner.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.4(d)(2)","Model Rule 5.4"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[221],"sourceAnswerPages":[316,317],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师加入一家事务所，担任领取固定薪资的助理律师。该事务所专门从事法律业务。一名合伙人的女婿不是律师，却担任事务所副总裁兼办公室经理。他在作为事务所具名合伙人的岳父直接监督下工作。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为合伙人的女婿是事务所管理人员。","B":"是，因为没有迹象表明委托人保密信息不会向女婿披露。","C":"否，因为该律师只是领取固定薪资的雇员。","D":"否，因为女婿受一名具名合伙人直接监督。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。“在下列情形下，律师不得在获准以营利为目的从事法律业务的专业公司或协会中执业，或以该形式执业：……非律师担任该公司的董事或管理人员，或者担任负有类似职责的职位。”《职业行为示范规则》5.4(d)(2)。本题中，合伙人的女婿担任事务所副总裁，其职位违反规则。B 不正确。本题涉及女婿在事务所的领导职位，而不是他可能知悉委托人保密信息。C 不正确。规则 5.4 禁止律师在不当地与非律师分享法律服务费或决策责任的公司中执业。该禁令并不只适用于以成员、股东或合伙人身份执业的律师。D 不正确。规则 5.4 禁止非律师担任董事或管理人员。因此，女婿是否受具名合伙人监督并不重要。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-035","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":35,"topic":"Rule 2.11(A)(3)","question":"A judge is one of three trustees of a trust for her grandchildren’s benefit. The trust owns 1,000 shares of stock in a large telecommunications company. The stock has been selling at $1,000 a share. The telecommunications company is suing another company for breach of contract, and the case is assigned to the judge for trial. The judge reasonably believes that she can be fair and impartial. Additionally, she does not personally own stock in either party to the litigation. May the judge hear the case?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because she doesn’t personally own any stock in either party.","B":"Yes, because the judge reasonably believes that she can remain impartial.","C":"No, because the outcome of the lawsuit is likely to affect the value of the stock.","D":"No, because the trust has a significant financial interest in the telecommunications company."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. If a judge personally or as a fiduciary (trustee of a trust) has more than a de minimis economic interest in the subject matter in controversy, disqualification is required. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(3). Consequently, the judge should not hear the case. A is incorrect. Even if the judge does not personally own stock in either party to the litigation, she is a trustee who owns a legal interest in the stock while acting as a fiduciary for others with an equitable interest. Under these circumstances, the judge’s impartiality can still be reasonably questioned by others. B is incorrect. Although the judge believes she can be fair and impartial (and she might, in fact, be correct in this belief), that impartiality could be reasonably questioned by others. C is incorrect. Even if the value of the stock is unlikely to be affected, disqualification is still necessary to prevent the appearance of judicial impropriety and the judge’s impartiality can reasonably be questioned under the circumstances. The overarching goal of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct is to make sure judges act in a manner that promotes public confidence in the integrity, fairness, and impartiality of the judiciary. Consequently, members of the judiciary must avoid creating even an appearance of bias.","rule":"D is the correct answer. If a judge personally or as a fiduciary (trustee of a trust) has more than a de minimis economic interest in the subject matter in controversy, disqualification is required. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(3). Consequently, the judge should not hear the case. A is incorrect. Even if the judge does not personally own stock in either party to the litigation, she is a trustee who owns a legal interest in the stock while acting as a fiduciary for others with an equitable interest. Under these circumstances, the judge’s impartiality can still be reasonably questioned by others. B is incorrect. Although the judge believes she can be fair and impartial (and she might, in fact, be correct in this belief), that impartiality could be reasonably questioned by others. C is incorrect. Even if the value of the stock is unlikely to be affected, disqualification is still necessary to prevent the appearance of judicial impropriety and the judge’s impartiality can reasonably be questioned under the circumstances. The overarching goal of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct is to make sure judges act in a manner that promotes public confidence in the integrity, fairness, and impartiality of the judiciary. Consequently, members of the judiciary must avoid creating even an appearance of bias.","irac":{"issue":"May the judge hear the case?","rule":"D is the correct answer. If a judge personally or as a fiduciary (trustee of a trust) has more than a de minimis economic interest in the subject matter in controversy, disqualification is required. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(3). Consequently, the judge should not hear the case. A is incorrect. Even if the judge does not personally own stock in either party to the litigation, she is a trustee who owns a legal interest in the stock while acting as a fiduciary for others with an equitable interest. Under these circumstances, the judge’s impartiality can still be reasonably questioned by others. B is incorrect. Although the judge believes she can be fair and impartial (and she might, in fact, be correct in this belief), that impartiality could be reasonably questioned by others. C is incorrect. Even if the value of the stock is unlikely to be affected, disqualification is still necessary to prevent the appearance of judicial impropriety and the judge’s impartiality can reasonably be questioned under the circumstances. The overarching goal of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct is to make sure judges act in a manner that promotes public confidence in the integrity, fairness, and impartiality of the judiciary. Consequently, members of the judiciary must avoid creating even an appearance of bias.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. If a judge personally or as a fiduciary (trustee of a trust) has more than a de minimis economic interest in the subject matter in controversy, disqualification is required. Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.11(A)(3). Consequently, the judge should not hear the case. A is incorrect. Even if the judge does not personally own stock in either party to the litigation, she is a trustee who owns a legal interest in the stock while acting as a fiduciary for others with an equitable interest. Under these circumstances, the judge’s impartiality can still be reasonably questioned by others. B is incorrect. Although the judge believes she can be fair and impartial (and she might, in fact, be correct in this belief), that impartiality could be reasonably questioned by others. C is incorrect. Even if the value of the stock is unlikely to be affected, disqualification is still necessary to prevent the appearance of judicial impropriety and the judge’s impartiality can reasonably be questioned under the circumstances. The overarching goal of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct is to make sure judges act in a manner that promotes public confidence in the integrity, fairness, and impartiality of the judiciary. Consequently, members of the judiciary must avoid creating even an appearance of bias.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 2.11(A)(3)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[221,222],"sourceAnswerPages":[317],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名法官是为其孙辈利益设立之信托的三名受托人之一。信托持有一家大型电信公司 1,000 股股票，股票交易价格为每股 1,000 美元。该电信公司正在起诉另一家公司违反合同，案件被分配给该法官审理。法官合理地相信自己能够公平公正。此外，她个人没有持有诉讼任何一方的股票。法官能否审理本案？","choicesZh":{"A":"能，因为她个人不持有任何一方的股票。","B":"能，因为法官合理地相信自己能够保持公正。","C":"不能，因为诉讼结果很可能影响股票价值。","D":"不能，因为信托在电信公司中拥有重大经济利益。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。如果法官本人或者以受信人身份（即作为信托受托人）在争议事项中拥有超过最低限度的经济利益，就必须回避。《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.11(A)(3)。因此，法官不应审理本案。A 不正确。即使法官个人不持有诉讼任何一方的股票，她仍是受托人，以受信人身份对股票拥有法律上的利益，而他人拥有衡平法上的利益。在这种情况下，其他人仍可合理地质疑法官公正性。B 不正确。法官虽然相信自己能够公平公正，而且这种判断事实上也可能正确，但其他人仍可合理质疑其公正性。C 不正确。即使诉讼不太可能影响股票价值，仍有必要回避，以避免造成司法行为不当的观感；在本题情形下，其他人可以合理地质疑法官公正性。《司法行为示范准则》的总体目标，是确保法官以能够提升公众对司法体系诚信、公平和公正之信心的方式行事。因此，司法人员甚至必须避免造成偏见的表象。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-036","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":36,"topic":"Model Rule 5.6(b)","question":"An attorney represented a local business in a contract dispute against an advertising agency. The attorney received a proposed settlement that required the attorney to agree not to represent the business in any subsequent proceeding involving the agency. The attorney thought the settlement was reasonable and that another attorney could protect the business in any future disputes with the advertising agency. However, the attorney thought he might be able to get more money for his client if he also agreed to not represent any other client against the agency. Is the attorney subject to discipline if he agrees to the settlement?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the proposed settlement restricts the attorney’s right to represent the business in the future.","B":"Yes, because the attorney believes it might help the client more to agree to not represent any client against the agency.","C":"No, because the attorney believes the settlement is reasonable.","D":"No, because the attorney believes another attorney could protect the business in future disputes."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 5.6(b), an attorney shall not participate in offering or making “an agreement in which a restriction on the lawyer’s right to practice is part of the settlement of a client controversy.” Consequently, attorney cannot agree to not represent the business as part of the settlement. B is incorrect. The issue here is not the potential of a better settlement offer. The issue here is that an attorney cannot be part of a settlement restricting his or her right to represent a client. C is incorrect. This is clearly a wrong answer choice, as it implies that as long as the attorney believes something is reasonable, it is proper under the Rules. D is incorrect. This answer choice also centers around the attorney’s beliefs, instead of addressing the issue of what the attorney is actually doing; specifically, restricting his right to represent the client in the future.","rule":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 5.6(b), an attorney shall not participate in offering or making “an agreement in which a restriction on the lawyer’s right to practice is part of the settlement of a client controversy.” Consequently, attorney cannot agree to not represent the business as part of the settlement. B is incorrect. The issue here is not the potential of a better settlement offer. The issue here is that an attorney cannot be part of a settlement restricting his or her right to represent a client. C is incorrect. This is clearly a wrong answer choice, as it implies that as long as the attorney believes something is reasonable, it is proper under the Rules. D is incorrect. This answer choice also centers around the attorney’s beliefs, instead of addressing the issue of what the attorney is actually doing; specifically, restricting his right to represent the client in the future.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline if he agrees to the settlement?","rule":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 5.6(b), an attorney shall not participate in offering or making “an agreement in which a restriction on the lawyer’s right to practice is part of the settlement of a client controversy.” Consequently, attorney cannot agree to not represent the business as part of the settlement. B is incorrect. The issue here is not the potential of a better settlement offer. The issue here is that an attorney cannot be part of a settlement restricting his or her right to represent a client. C is incorrect. This is clearly a wrong answer choice, as it implies that as long as the attorney believes something is reasonable, it is proper under the Rules. D is incorrect. This answer choice also centers around the attorney’s beliefs, instead of addressing the issue of what the attorney is actually doing; specifically, restricting his right to represent the client in the future.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 5.6(b), an attorney shall not participate in offering or making “an agreement in which a restriction on the lawyer’s right to practice is part of the settlement of a client controversy.” Consequently, attorney cannot agree to not represent the business as part of the settlement. B is incorrect. The issue here is not the potential of a better settlement offer. The issue here is that an attorney cannot be part of a settlement restricting his or her right to represent a client. C is incorrect. This is clearly a wrong answer choice, as it implies that as long as the attorney believes something is reasonable, it is proper under the Rules. D is incorrect. This answer choice also centers around the attorney’s beliefs, instead of addressing the issue of what the attorney is actually doing; specifically, restricting his right to represent the client in the future.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.6(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[222],"sourceAnswerPages":[317,318],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理当地一家企业处理与某广告公司的合同争议。律师收到一项拟议和解，要求他同意不在任何以后涉及广告公司的程序中代理该企业。律师认为和解合理，也认为另一名律师能够在以后与广告公司的争议中保护企业。不过，律师认为，如果他进一步同意不代理任何其他委托人对抗该广告公司，可能为委托人争取到更多款项。如果律师同意和解，是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为拟议和解限制律师将来代理该企业的权利。","B":"是，因为律师认为同意不代理任何委托人对抗广告公司，可能给本委托人带来更多利益。","C":"否，因为律师认为和解合理。","D":"否，因为律师认为另一名律师能够在未来争议中保护该企业。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。根据《职业行为示范规则》5.6(b)，律师不得参与提出或订立“把限制律师执业权作为委托人争议和解一部分的协议”。因此，律师不能在和解中同意不再代理该企业。B 不正确。本题问题不在于可能取得更好的和解要约，而在于律师不能成为限制自己代理委托人权利之和解协议的一方。C 不正确。这显然是错误选项，因为它暗示只要律师认为某事合理，该行为在规则下就适当。D 不正确。该选项同样围绕律师看法展开，而没有处理律师实际所做之事，即限制自己将来代理委托人的权利。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-037","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":37,"topic":"Model Rule 5.4(d)(3) / Model Rule 5.4(a)(3)","question":"A law firm has 1,000 lawyers in six states. It has placed the supervision of all routine administrative and financial matters in the hands of a nonlawyer. The nonlawyer is paid a regular monthly salary and a year-end bonus from the law firm’s net income from fees. Organizationally, the nonlawyer reports to an attorney who acts as the managing partner of the law firm. The attorney deals with all issues related to the law firm’s supervision of the practice of law. The nonlawyer does not have access to client files and does not control the professional judgment of lawyers in the firm. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the firm is sharing legal fees with a nonlawyer.","B":"Yes, because the nonlawyer is engaged in the unauthorized practice of law.","C":"No, because the nonlawyer does not control the professional judgment of lawyers in the firm.","D":"No, because the nonlawyer does not have access to confidential information."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not practice with or in the form of a professional corporation or association authorized to practice law for a profit, if: . . . a nonlawyer has the right to direct or control the professional judgment of a lawyer.” Model Rule 5.4(d)(3). Here, the nonlawyer only supervises routine administrative and financial matters and does not control the professional judgment of lawyers in the firm. A is incorrect. “A lawyer or law firm may include nonlawyer employees in a compensation or retirement plan, even though the plan is based in whole or in part on a profit-sharing arrangement.” Model Rule 5.4(a)(3). Consequently, as long as the nonlawyer’s position is not violating the Rules in some other way (namely, by controlling the judgment of lawyers), it is proper to share fees with the nonlawyer employee. B is incorrect. Here, since the nonlawyer is not giving legal advice or controlling others in the giving of any sort of legal advice, the nonlawyer is not engaged in the unauthorized practice of law. D is incorrect. The issue here is whether the nonlawyer can control the actions of lawyers. The issue of confidentiality is irrelevant.","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not practice with or in the form of a professional corporation or association authorized to practice law for a profit, if: . . . a nonlawyer has the right to direct or control the professional judgment of a lawyer.” Model Rule 5.4(d)(3). Here, the nonlawyer only supervises routine administrative and financial matters and does not control the professional judgment of lawyers in the firm. A is incorrect. “A lawyer or law firm may include nonlawyer employees in a compensation or retirement plan, even though the plan is based in whole or in part on a profit-sharing arrangement.” Model Rule 5.4(a)(3). Consequently, as long as the nonlawyer’s position is not violating the Rules in some other way (namely, by controlling the judgment of lawyers), it is proper to share fees with the nonlawyer employee. B is incorrect. Here, since the nonlawyer is not giving legal advice or controlling others in the giving of any sort of legal advice, the nonlawyer is not engaged in the unauthorized practice of law. D is incorrect. The issue here is whether the nonlawyer can control the actions of lawyers. The issue of confidentiality is irrelevant.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not practice with or in the form of a professional corporation or association authorized to practice law for a profit, if: . . . a nonlawyer has the right to direct or control the professional judgment of a lawyer.” Model Rule 5.4(d)(3). Here, the nonlawyer only supervises routine administrative and financial matters and does not control the professional judgment of lawyers in the firm. A is incorrect. “A lawyer or law firm may include nonlawyer employees in a compensation or retirement plan, even though the plan is based in whole or in part on a profit-sharing arrangement.” Model Rule 5.4(a)(3). Consequently, as long as the nonlawyer’s position is not violating the Rules in some other way (namely, by controlling the judgment of lawyers), it is proper to share fees with the nonlawyer employee. B is incorrect. Here, since the nonlawyer is not giving legal advice or controlling others in the giving of any sort of legal advice, the nonlawyer is not engaged in the unauthorized practice of law. D is incorrect. The issue here is whether the nonlawyer can control the actions of lawyers. The issue of confidentiality is irrelevant.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not practice with or in the form of a professional corporation or association authorized to practice law for a profit, if: . . . a nonlawyer has the right to direct or control the professional judgment of a lawyer.” Model Rule 5.4(d)(3). Here, the nonlawyer only supervises routine administrative and financial matters and does not control the professional judgment of lawyers in the firm. A is incorrect. “A lawyer or law firm may include nonlawyer employees in a compensation or retirement plan, even though the plan is based in whole or in part on a profit-sharing arrangement.” Model Rule 5.4(a)(3). Consequently, as long as the nonlawyer’s position is not violating the Rules in some other way (namely, by controlling the judgment of lawyers), it is proper to share fees with the nonlawyer employee. B is incorrect. Here, since the nonlawyer is not giving legal advice or controlling others in the giving of any sort of legal advice, the nonlawyer is not engaged in the unauthorized practice of law. D is incorrect. The issue here is whether the nonlawyer can control the actions of lawyers. The issue of confidentiality is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.4(d)(3)","Model Rule 5.4(a)(3)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[222,223],"sourceAnswerPages":[318],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家律师事务所在六个州拥有 1,000 名律师。事务所将所有日常行政和财务事务交由一名非律师监督。该非律师获得固定月薪及从事务所法律服务费净收入中支付的年终奖金。在组织关系上，非律师向一名担任事务所管理合伙人的律师汇报。该律师负责处理与事务所监督法律业务有关的全部问题。非律师无权查阅委托人案卷，也不控制事务所律师的专业判断。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为事务所正在与非律师分享法律服务费。","B":"是，因为非律师正在擅自从事法律执业活动。","C":"否，因为非律师不控制事务所律师的专业判断。","D":"否，因为非律师无权查阅保密信息。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。“在下列情形下，律师不得在获准以营利为目的从事法律业务的专业公司或协会中执业，或以该形式执业：……非律师有权指导或控制律师的专业判断。”《职业行为示范规则》5.4(d)(3)。本题中，非律师只监督日常行政和财务事务，不控制事务所律师的专业判断。A 不正确。“律师或律师事务所可以让非律师雇员参加报酬或退休计划，即使该计划全部或部分以利润分享安排为依据。”规则 5.4(a)(3)。因此，只要非律师职位没有以其他方式违反规则（尤其是控制律师判断），就可以与该非律师雇员分享收益。B 不正确。本题中，非律师既没有提供法律意见，也没有控制他人提供法律意见，因此没有擅自从事法律执业活动。D 不正确。本题问题是非律师能否控制律师行为，保密问题无关。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-038","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":38,"topic":"Model Rule 1.6(b)","question":"A plaintiff filed suit against both an attorney and her client, alleging a violation of state law as the result of a bad business deal. The attorney represented the client in the deal. The attorney and the client are each represented by separate counsel in the plaintiff’s suit. In responding to a deposition under subpoena, the attorney revealed, to the extent the attorney reasonably believes necessary to defend herself, confidential information imparted to the attorney by the client that will be favorable to the attorney but damaging to the client. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because disclosure is detrimental to the client.","B":"Yes, because the client revealed confidential information.","C":"No, because the attorney was defending herself.","D":"No, because the attorney and client are each represented by separate counsel."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may reveal confidential information “to establish a defense to a criminal charge or civil claim against the lawyer based upon conduct in which the client was involved.” Model Rule 1.6(b) (5). Here, since the plaintiff is suing both the client and the attorney over the same business deal, the attorney may reveal the information. A is incorrect. Although the disclosure may be detrimental to the client, the Rules allow the attorney to defend herself against civil and criminal actions. B is incorrect. As stated above, a lawyer may reveal confidential information to defend himself or herself. D is incorrect. The issue here does not turn on whether the attorney and client are represented by separate counsel. The important point is that the attorney can reveal confidential information to defend herself.","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may reveal confidential information “to establish a defense to a criminal charge or civil claim against the lawyer based upon conduct in which the client was involved.” Model Rule 1.6(b) (5). Here, since the plaintiff is suing both the client and the attorney over the same business deal, the attorney may reveal the information. A is incorrect. Although the disclosure may be detrimental to the client, the Rules allow the attorney to defend herself against civil and criminal actions. B is incorrect. As stated above, a lawyer may reveal confidential information to defend himself or herself. D is incorrect. The issue here does not turn on whether the attorney and client are represented by separate counsel. The important point is that the attorney can reveal confidential information to defend herself.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may reveal confidential information “to establish a defense to a criminal charge or civil claim against the lawyer based upon conduct in which the client was involved.” Model Rule 1.6(b) (5). Here, since the plaintiff is suing both the client and the attorney over the same business deal, the attorney may reveal the information. A is incorrect. Although the disclosure may be detrimental to the client, the Rules allow the attorney to defend herself against civil and criminal actions. B is incorrect. As stated above, a lawyer may reveal confidential information to defend himself or herself. D is incorrect. The issue here does not turn on whether the attorney and client are represented by separate counsel. The important point is that the attorney can reveal confidential information to defend herself.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer may reveal confidential information “to establish a defense to a criminal charge or civil claim against the lawyer based upon conduct in which the client was involved.” Model Rule 1.6(b) (5). Here, since the plaintiff is suing both the client and the attorney over the same business deal, the attorney may reveal the information. A is incorrect. Although the disclosure may be detrimental to the client, the Rules allow the attorney to defend herself against civil and criminal actions. B is incorrect. As stated above, a lawyer may reveal confidential information to defend himself or herself. D is incorrect. The issue here does not turn on whether the attorney and client are represented by separate counsel. The important point is that the attorney can reveal confidential information to defend herself.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.6(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[223],"sourceAnswerPages":[318,319],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告同时起诉一名律师及其委托人，指控二人在一宗糟糕商业交易中违反州法律。该律师曾在交易中代理委托人。原告诉讼中，律师与委托人各自由不同律师代理。在依传票参加证言录取并作答时，该律师在其合理认为为自身辩护所必需的范围内，披露了委托人曾向其提供的保密信息；该信息对律师有利，却会损害委托人。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为披露损害委托人。","B":"是，因为委托人披露的是保密信息。","C":"否，因为律师是在为自己辩护。","D":"否，因为律师与委托人各自由不同律师代理。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。律师可以披露保密信息，“以针对基于委托人参与之行为而对律师提出的刑事指控或民事主张确立抗辩”。《职业行为示范规则》1.6(b)(5)。本题中，原告因同一商业交易同时起诉委托人和律师，因此律师可以披露信息。A 不正确。披露虽然可能损害委托人，规则仍允许律师针对民事和刑事程序为自己辩护。B 不正确。如上所述，律师可以披露保密信息以进行自我辩护。D 不正确。本题不取决于律师和委托人是否各自由不同律师代理，关键是律师可以披露保密信息以保护自己。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-039","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":39,"topic":"Model Rule 1.6","question":"A stolen painting was found in the possession of an attorney, who was holding it as a favor for one of her clients. The authorities did not believe the attorney was involved in the theft in any way, but they did believe the attorney’s client knew important information regarding the theft. The attorney was subpoenaed to testify before a grand jury. The grand jury asked for the name of the client who had left the painting in the attorney’s possession, and the attorney provided the name to the grand jury. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the identity of the client is protected by the attorney-client privilege.","B":"Yes, because the theft does not involve an imminent threat of death or serious bodily harm.","C":"No, because the attorney can provide the information to defend herself.","D":"No, because the client’s name is not privileged under the circumstances."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Although Model Rule 1.6 protects a client’s confidential information under the attorney-client privilege, revealing the name of the client does not appear to invoke the Rule in these circumstances. The question notes that the attorney is simply holding the painting as a favor to a client—it does not appear to have anything to do with the attorney’s representation of the client or the client’s confidential information. A is incorrect. As stated above, this situation does not appear to have anything to do with the attorney-client privilege. B is incorrect. As stated above, the information is not confidential under Model Rule 1.6, so it is irrelevant whether it would fall under the exception that allows the disclosure of confidences to prevent imminent death or substantial bodily harm. C is incorrect. The question notes that the authorities do not believe the attorney was involved in any way, so revealing the information doesn’t have anything to do with the attorney defending herself against a criminal charge.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Although Model Rule 1.6 protects a client’s confidential information under the attorney-client privilege, revealing the name of the client does not appear to invoke the Rule in these circumstances. The question notes that the attorney is simply holding the painting as a favor to a client—it does not appear to have anything to do with the attorney’s representation of the client or the client’s confidential information. A is incorrect. As stated above, this situation does not appear to have anything to do with the attorney-client privilege. B is incorrect. As stated above, the information is not confidential under Model Rule 1.6, so it is irrelevant whether it would fall under the exception that allows the disclosure of confidences to prevent imminent death or substantial bodily harm. C is incorrect. The question notes that the authorities do not believe the attorney was involved in any way, so revealing the information doesn’t have anything to do with the attorney defending herself against a criminal charge.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Although Model Rule 1.6 protects a client’s confidential information under the attorney-client privilege, revealing the name of the client does not appear to invoke the Rule in these circumstances. The question notes that the attorney is simply holding the painting as a favor to a client—it does not appear to have anything to do with the attorney’s representation of the client or the client’s confidential information. A is incorrect. As stated above, this situation does not appear to have anything to do with the attorney-client privilege. B is incorrect. As stated above, the information is not confidential under Model Rule 1.6, so it is irrelevant whether it would fall under the exception that allows the disclosure of confidences to prevent imminent death or substantial bodily harm. C is incorrect. The question notes that the authorities do not believe the attorney was involved in any way, so revealing the information doesn’t have anything to do with the attorney defending herself against a criminal charge.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Although Model Rule 1.6 protects a client’s confidential information under the attorney-client privilege, revealing the name of the client does not appear to invoke the Rule in these circumstances. The question notes that the attorney is simply holding the painting as a favor to a client—it does not appear to have anything to do with the attorney’s representation of the client or the client’s confidential information. A is incorrect. As stated above, this situation does not appear to have anything to do with the attorney-client privilege. B is incorrect. As stated above, the information is not confidential under Model Rule 1.6, so it is irrelevant whether it would fall under the exception that allows the disclosure of confidences to prevent imminent death or substantial bodily harm. C is incorrect. The question notes that the authorities do not believe the attorney was involved in any way, so revealing the information doesn’t have anything to do with the attorney defending herself against a criminal charge.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.6"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[223,224],"sourceAnswerPages":[319],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一幅被盗画作在一名律师处被发现；律师受一名委托人之托，作为帮忙替其保管。有关机关不认为律师以任何方式参与盗窃，但认为其委托人知道有关盗窃的重要信息。律师收到传票，到大陪审团作证。大陪审团询问把画作留给律师保管之委托人的姓名，律师予以提供。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为委托人身份受律师—委托人特权保护。","B":"是，因为盗窃不涉及迫在眉睫的死亡或严重身体伤害威胁。","C":"否，因为律师可以提供信息为自己辩护。","D":"否，因为在本题情形下委托人姓名不受特权保护。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。虽然《职业行为示范规则》1.6 保护处于律师—委托人特权范围内的保密信息，但在本题情形下，披露委托人姓名显然不会触发该规则。题目指出，律师只是作为帮忙替委托人保管画作；这似乎与律师代理委托人或委托人保密信息毫无关系。A 不正确。如上所述，该情形似乎与律师—委托人特权无关。B 不正确。如上所述，该信息不属于规则 1.6 下的保密信息，因此其是否落入允许为防止迫在眉睫的死亡或重大身体伤害而披露秘密的例外并不重要。C 不正确。题目明确说有关机关不认为律师以任何方式参与，因此披露信息与律师针对刑事指控为自己辩护无关。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-040","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":40,"topic":"Model Rule 7.1 / Model Rule 8.4(e) / Model Rule 7.3","question":"An attorney served as lieutenant governor immediately prior to reopening his law office in the state. The attorney printed and mailed an announcement of his return to private practice to members of the bar, persons who had previously been his clients, and personal friends whom he had never represented. The printed announcement stated that the attorney had reopened his law office, gave his address and telephone number, and added that he had recently served as lieutenant governor. Are the attorney’s actions proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because all of the information is true.","B":"Yes, because all of the information is public.","C":"No, because the attorney is implying an ability to improperly influence the courts.","D":"No, because he mailed the announcement to people who had never been clients."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. In advertising his or her services, a lawyer may not make a false or misleading statement. Model Rule 7.1. Here, nothing in the mailing is false, and it appears that its main purpose is simply to alert members of the community that the lawyer is returning to private practice. B is incorrect. Although all the information is public, it could be presented or phrased in a way to create an improper misrepresentation under Model Rule 7.1 or an improper implication of influence under Model Rule 8.4(e). Consequently, the simple fact the information is in the public domain does not make the advertisement proper. C is incorrect. Although the lawyer states he was once lieutenant governor, the mailing does not appear to violate Model Rule 8.4(e)’s prohibition against implying an improper influence. D is incorrect. Although the mailing was sent to people who were not his clients, this does not violate Model Rule 7.3’s prohibitions regarding solicitation of clients. Importantly, written contact is prohibited when the lawyer knows the person does not want to be solicited or the solicitation involves coercion, duress, or harassment. Model Rule 7.3(b). Here, the mailing announcing the new law office appears to do none of these things.","rule":"A is the correct answer. In advertising his or her services, a lawyer may not make a false or misleading statement. Model Rule 7.1. Here, nothing in the mailing is false, and it appears that its main purpose is simply to alert members of the community that the lawyer is returning to private practice. B is incorrect. Although all the information is public, it could be presented or phrased in a way to create an improper misrepresentation under Model Rule 7.1 or an improper implication of influence under Model Rule 8.4(e). Consequently, the simple fact the information is in the public domain does not make the advertisement proper. C is incorrect. Although the lawyer states he was once lieutenant governor, the mailing does not appear to violate Model Rule 8.4(e)’s prohibition against implying an improper influence. D is incorrect. Although the mailing was sent to people who were not his clients, this does not violate Model Rule 7.3’s prohibitions regarding solicitation of clients. Importantly, written contact is prohibited when the lawyer knows the person does not want to be solicited or the solicitation involves coercion, duress, or harassment. Model Rule 7.3(b). Here, the mailing announcing the new law office appears to do none of these things.","irac":{"issue":"Are the attorney’s actions proper?","rule":"A is the correct answer. In advertising his or her services, a lawyer may not make a false or misleading statement. Model Rule 7.1. Here, nothing in the mailing is false, and it appears that its main purpose is simply to alert members of the community that the lawyer is returning to private practice. B is incorrect. Although all the information is public, it could be presented or phrased in a way to create an improper misrepresentation under Model Rule 7.1 or an improper implication of influence under Model Rule 8.4(e). Consequently, the simple fact the information is in the public domain does not make the advertisement proper. C is incorrect. Although the lawyer states he was once lieutenant governor, the mailing does not appear to violate Model Rule 8.4(e)’s prohibition against implying an improper influence. D is incorrect. Although the mailing was sent to people who were not his clients, this does not violate Model Rule 7.3’s prohibitions regarding solicitation of clients. Importantly, written contact is prohibited when the lawyer knows the person does not want to be solicited or the solicitation involves coercion, duress, or harassment. Model Rule 7.3(b). Here, the mailing announcing the new law office appears to do none of these things.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. In advertising his or her services, a lawyer may not make a false or misleading statement. Model Rule 7.1. Here, nothing in the mailing is false, and it appears that its main purpose is simply to alert members of the community that the lawyer is returning to private practice. B is incorrect. Although all the information is public, it could be presented or phrased in a way to create an improper misrepresentation under Model Rule 7.1 or an improper implication of influence under Model Rule 8.4(e). Consequently, the simple fact the information is in the public domain does not make the advertisement proper. C is incorrect. Although the lawyer states he was once lieutenant governor, the mailing does not appear to violate Model Rule 8.4(e)’s prohibition against implying an improper influence. D is incorrect. Although the mailing was sent to people who were not his clients, this does not violate Model Rule 7.3’s prohibitions regarding solicitation of clients. Importantly, written contact is prohibited when the lawyer knows the person does not want to be solicited or the solicitation involves coercion, duress, or harassment. Model Rule 7.3(b). Here, the mailing announcing the new law office appears to do none of these things.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.1","Model Rule 8.4(e)","Model Rule 7.3","Model Rule 7.3(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[224],"sourceAnswerPages":[319,320],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师在重新于本州开办律师事务所之前，刚刚结束副州长任期。律师印制了一份重返私人执业的公告，邮寄给律师协会成员、先前的委托人，以及从未由其代理过的私人朋友。印刷公告说明律师已经重新开办事务所，列出其地址和电话号码，并补充说明他最近曾担任副州长。律师的行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为全部信息真实。","B":"适当，因为全部信息都属于公开信息。","C":"不适当，因为律师暗示自己有能力不当地影响法院。","D":"不适当，因为他把公告邮寄给从未成为委托人的人。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。律师为自己的服务做广告时，不得作出虚假或具有误导性的陈述。《职业行为示范规则》7.1。本题中，邮件内容没有任何虚假，其主要目的似乎只是告知社区成员该律师将重返私人执业。B 不正确。即使全部信息都是公开的，仍可能以违反规则 7.1 的方式呈现或措辞，造成不当虚假陈述，也可能违反规则 8.4(e)，不当地暗示影响力。因此，仅仅因为信息处于公共领域，并不会使广告当然适当。C 不正确。律师虽然说明自己曾担任副州长，邮件看起来并未违反规则 8.4(e) 关于不得暗示不当影响力的规定。D 不正确。公告虽然寄给了一些从未由其代理过的人，但这并不违反规则 7.3 对招揽委托人的禁止。关键是，律师明知某人不愿被招揽，或者招揽涉及胁迫、强制或骚扰时，书面联系才受到禁止。规则 7.3(b)。本题的事务所开业公告显然不涉及这些情况。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-041","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":41,"topic":"Model Rule 1.6(a) / Model Rule 5.1","question":"A sole practitioner recently underwent chemotherapy and was advised that he could not return to work for six months. The attorney delivered all of his clients’ files to a friend of his, who is also a sole practitioner. The friend agreed to review each client’s file promptly, take any action necessary to protect each client’s interests, and treat the information in the files as confidential. The attorney then wrote to his clients, advising each client that the client’s file had been delivered to his friend for review and for any action necessary to protect the client’s interest, and that the client was free to select another lawyer. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney did not obtain prior consent from his clients.","B":"Yes, because the attorney did not plan to supervise his friend in providing representation to the clients.","C":"No, because the friend agreed to act diligently in representing the clients.","D":"No, because finding alternative representation was necessary due to the chemotherapy."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent.” Model Rule 1.6(a). Here, it would be improper for the attorney to turn over all the client files and reveal all the confidences therein before receiving consent from the clients. B is incorrect. The issue here is not supervision. Additionally, the attorneys do not work in the same firm or partnership, so Model Rule 5.1 doesn’t apply. C is incorrect. While the friend appears to be a diligent attorney, this would not excuse the violation of Model Rule 1.6(a). D is incorrect. While finding alternative representation may have been necessary, the attorney could not share client information without first gaining consent.","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent.” Model Rule 1.6(a). Here, it would be improper for the attorney to turn over all the client files and reveal all the confidences therein before receiving consent from the clients. B is incorrect. The issue here is not supervision. Additionally, the attorneys do not work in the same firm or partnership, so Model Rule 5.1 doesn’t apply. C is incorrect. While the friend appears to be a diligent attorney, this would not excuse the violation of Model Rule 1.6(a). D is incorrect. While finding alternative representation may have been necessary, the attorney could not share client information without first gaining consent.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent.” Model Rule 1.6(a). Here, it would be improper for the attorney to turn over all the client files and reveal all the confidences therein before receiving consent from the clients. B is incorrect. The issue here is not supervision. Additionally, the attorneys do not work in the same firm or partnership, so Model Rule 5.1 doesn’t apply. C is incorrect. While the friend appears to be a diligent attorney, this would not excuse the violation of Model Rule 1.6(a). D is incorrect. While finding alternative representation may have been necessary, the attorney could not share client information without first gaining consent.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall not reveal information relating to the representation of a client unless the client gives informed consent.” Model Rule 1.6(a). Here, it would be improper for the attorney to turn over all the client files and reveal all the confidences therein before receiving consent from the clients. B is incorrect. The issue here is not supervision. Additionally, the attorneys do not work in the same firm or partnership, so Model Rule 5.1 doesn’t apply. C is incorrect. While the friend appears to be a diligent attorney, this would not excuse the violation of Model Rule 1.6(a). D is incorrect. While finding alternative representation may have been necessary, the attorney could not share client information without first gaining consent.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.6(a)","Model Rule 5.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[224,225],"sourceAnswerPages":[320],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名独立执业律师最近接受了化学治疗，医生建议他六个月内不能复工。律师将所有委托人案卷交给一名同样独立执业的朋友。朋友同意迅速审查每名委托人的案卷，采取保护其利益所需的任何行动，并对案卷信息保密。随后，律师致函各委托人，告知其案卷已交给朋友审查并采取保护其利益所需的行动，同时告知委托人可自由选择另一名律师。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师没有事先取得委托人同意。","B":"是，因为律师没有计划监督朋友向委托人提供代理。","C":"否，因为朋友同意勤勉代理委托人。","D":"否，因为化学治疗使寻找替代代理成为必要。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。“除非委托人作出知情同意，律师不得披露与委托人代理有关的信息。”《职业行为示范规则》1.6(a)。本题中，律师在取得委托人同意前交出所有案卷并披露其中全部保密信息，是不适当的。B 不正确。本题问题不在于监督。此外，两名律师不在同一事务所或合伙组织中，规则 5.1 不适用。C 不正确。朋友虽然看起来是一名勤勉律师，也不能免除违反规则 1.6(a) 的行为。D 不正确。寻找替代代理虽然可能必要，但律师仍不能在未先取得同意时分享委托人信息。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-042","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":42,"topic":"Model Rule 1.15(a) / Model Rule 1.15(c) / Model Rule 1.15(b)","question":"An attorney and a client entered into a written retainer and hourly fee agreement that required the client to pay $10,000 in advance of any services rendered by the attorney and which required the attorney to return any portion that was not earned. The agreement further provided that the attorney would render monthly statements and withdraw her fees as billed. The attorney deposited the entire fund in her Clients’ Trust Account. Thereafter, the attorney rendered monthly progress reports and statements for services to the client after services were rendered, showing the balance of the client’s fee advance. However, the attorney did not withdraw any of the $10,000 advance until one year later when the matter was concluded to the client’s complete satisfaction. At that time, the attorney had billed the client reasonable legal fees of $5,000. The attorney wrote two checks on her Clients’ Trust Account: one to herself for $5,000, which she deposited in her general office account, and one for $5,000 to the client. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney required the client to pay in advance.","B":"Yes, because the attorney failed to withdraw her fees as billed.","C":"No, because the client’s payment was put in the Clients’ Trust Account.","D":"No, because the attorney’s fee was reasonable."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall hold property of clients or third persons that is in a lawyer’s possession in connection with a representation separate from the lawyer’s own property.” Model Rule 1.15(a). Additionally, “a lawyer shall deposit into a client trust account legal fees and expenses that have been paid in advance, to be withdrawn by the lawyer only as fees are earned or expenses incurred.” Model Rule 1.15(c). Importantly, once the funds were identified as belonging to the attorney (i.e., the funds were earned), they should have been removed from the Clients’ Trust Account. A is incorrect. It is proper for a lawyer to accept fees in advance, so long as those fees are kept in a client trust account until they are earned by the lawyer. Model Rule 1.15(c). C is incorrect. Before the fees were earned, it was absolutely proper to place them in the Clients’ Trust Account. However, once the fees were earned and became the attorney’s property, they had to be removed from the account. The only time a lawyer’s own funds should be in the client trust account is when those funds are used for the sole purpose of paying bank service charges on the account, and those funds should only be in the amount necessary for that purpose. Model Rule 1.15(b). D is incorrect. Although the attorney’s fee was reasonable, the issue here is that the attorney’s money remained impermissibly commingled in the account.","rule":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall hold property of clients or third persons that is in a lawyer’s possession in connection with a representation separate from the lawyer’s own property.” Model Rule 1.15(a). Additionally, “a lawyer shall deposit into a client trust account legal fees and expenses that have been paid in advance, to be withdrawn by the lawyer only as fees are earned or expenses incurred.” Model Rule 1.15(c). Importantly, once the funds were identified as belonging to the attorney (i.e., the funds were earned), they should have been removed from the Clients’ Trust Account. A is incorrect. It is proper for a lawyer to accept fees in advance, so long as those fees are kept in a client trust account until they are earned by the lawyer. Model Rule 1.15(c). C is incorrect. Before the fees were earned, it was absolutely proper to place them in the Clients’ Trust Account. However, once the fees were earned and became the attorney’s property, they had to be removed from the account. The only time a lawyer’s own funds should be in the client trust account is when those funds are used for the sole purpose of paying bank service charges on the account, and those funds should only be in the amount necessary for that purpose. Model Rule 1.15(b). D is incorrect. Although the attorney’s fee was reasonable, the issue here is that the attorney’s money remained impermissibly commingled in the account.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall hold property of clients or third persons that is in a lawyer’s possession in connection with a representation separate from the lawyer’s own property.” Model Rule 1.15(a). Additionally, “a lawyer shall deposit into a client trust account legal fees and expenses that have been paid in advance, to be withdrawn by the lawyer only as fees are earned or expenses incurred.” Model Rule 1.15(c). Importantly, once the funds were identified as belonging to the attorney (i.e., the funds were earned), they should have been removed from the Clients’ Trust Account. A is incorrect. It is proper for a lawyer to accept fees in advance, so long as those fees are kept in a client trust account until they are earned by the lawyer. Model Rule 1.15(c). C is incorrect. Before the fees were earned, it was absolutely proper to place them in the Clients’ Trust Account. However, once the fees were earned and became the attorney’s property, they had to be removed from the account. The only time a lawyer’s own funds should be in the client trust account is when those funds are used for the sole purpose of paying bank service charges on the account, and those funds should only be in the amount necessary for that purpose. Model Rule 1.15(b). D is incorrect. Although the attorney’s fee was reasonable, the issue here is that the attorney’s money remained impermissibly commingled in the account.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. “A lawyer shall hold property of clients or third persons that is in a lawyer’s possession in connection with a representation separate from the lawyer’s own property.” Model Rule 1.15(a). Additionally, “a lawyer shall deposit into a client trust account legal fees and expenses that have been paid in advance, to be withdrawn by the lawyer only as fees are earned or expenses incurred.” Model Rule 1.15(c). Importantly, once the funds were identified as belonging to the attorney (i.e., the funds were earned), they should have been removed from the Clients’ Trust Account. A is incorrect. It is proper for a lawyer to accept fees in advance, so long as those fees are kept in a client trust account until they are earned by the lawyer. Model Rule 1.15(c). C is incorrect. Before the fees were earned, it was absolutely proper to place them in the Clients’ Trust Account. However, once the fees were earned and became the attorney’s property, they had to be removed from the account. The only time a lawyer’s own funds should be in the client trust account is when those funds are used for the sole purpose of paying bank service charges on the account, and those funds should only be in the amount necessary for that purpose. Model Rule 1.15(b). D is incorrect. Although the attorney’s fee was reasonable, the issue here is that the attorney’s money remained impermissibly commingled in the account.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.15(a)","Model Rule 1.15(c)","Model Rule 1.15(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[225],"sourceAnswerPages":[320,321],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师与委托人订立书面聘用及小时收费协议，要求委托人在律师提供任何服务前预付 10,000 美元，并要求律师退还其中未赚取的部分。协议还规定律师每月提供对账单，并按账单取出费用。律师将全部款项存入委托人信托账户。此后，律师在提供服务后，定期向委托人提供进度报告及服务对账单，显示预付费用余额。不过，律师一直没有取出 10,000 美元预付款中的任何部分，直到一年后事项结束，委托人完全满意。此时，律师已向委托人开具合理法律服务费 5,000 美元。律师从委托人信托账户开出两张支票：一张向自己支付 5,000 美元并存入一般办公账户；另一张向委托人支付 5,000 美元。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师要求委托人预付费用。","B":"是，因为律师没有在费用赚取后按账单取出。","C":"否，因为委托人付款被存入委托人信托账户。","D":"否，因为律师费合理。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。“律师应将其在代理关系中占有的委托人或第三人财产，与律师自己的财产分开保管。”《职业行为示范规则》1.15(a)。此外，“律师应把预付法律服务费和开支存入委托人信托账户，只有在费用已经赚取或开支已经发生时才可取出。”规则 1.15(c)。关键是，一旦资金被确定属于律师（即费用已经赚取），就应从委托人信托账户取出。A 不正确。律师可以接受预付费用，只要在律师赚取前将其留在委托人信托账户中。规则 1.15(c)。C 不正确。在费用赚取前，将其存入委托人信托账户完全适当。但一旦费用已经赚取并成为律师财产，就必须从该账户取出。律师自己的资金只能为支付该账户银行服务费这一唯一目的而存入委托人信托账户，而且金额只能以该目的所需为限。规则 1.15(b)。D 不正确。律师费虽然合理，本题问题是律师自己的资金被不当地继续混同在账户中。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-043","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":43,"topic":"Model Rule 1.5(d)(2) / Model Rule 1.5","question":"An attorney entered into a written retainer agreement with a defendant in a criminal case. Because the defendant couldn’t afford the attorney’s fee, the defendant asked if they could agree to transfer ownership of certain stocks to the attorney if the attorney successfully prevented him from going to prison. The attorney agreed to the retainer agreement. Later, the charges against the defendant were dismissed. Is the retainer agreement proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the charges were dismissed.","B":"Yes, because the defendant couldn’t afford the attorney otherwise.","C":"No, because this is a criminal case.","D":"No, because a lawyer may not acquire a proprietary interest in a client’s property."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. An attorney cannot charge a contingent fee for representing a defendant in a criminal case. Model Rule 1.5(d)(2). A is incorrect. Even though he obtained a good result for his client, the attorney still violated the prohibition against contingency fees in criminal cases. B is incorrect. Although the defendant may not have been able to afford the attorney otherwise, this fact does not make the retainer agreement appropriate. D is incorrect. The issue here is that an attorney cannot charge a contingent fee in a criminal matter. Model Rule 1.5(d)(2). Importantly, “a lawyer may accept property in payment for services, such as an ownership interest in an enterprise, providing this does not involve acquisition of a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of the litigation.” Model Rule 1.5, Comment [4].","rule":"C is the correct answer. An attorney cannot charge a contingent fee for representing a defendant in a criminal case. Model Rule 1.5(d)(2). A is incorrect. Even though he obtained a good result for his client, the attorney still violated the prohibition against contingency fees in criminal cases. B is incorrect. Although the defendant may not have been able to afford the attorney otherwise, this fact does not make the retainer agreement appropriate. D is incorrect. The issue here is that an attorney cannot charge a contingent fee in a criminal matter. Model Rule 1.5(d)(2). Importantly, “a lawyer may accept property in payment for services, such as an ownership interest in an enterprise, providing this does not involve acquisition of a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of the litigation.” Model Rule 1.5, Comment [4].","irac":{"issue":"Is the retainer agreement proper?","rule":"C is the correct answer. An attorney cannot charge a contingent fee for representing a defendant in a criminal case. Model Rule 1.5(d)(2). A is incorrect. Even though he obtained a good result for his client, the attorney still violated the prohibition against contingency fees in criminal cases. B is incorrect. Although the defendant may not have been able to afford the attorney otherwise, this fact does not make the retainer agreement appropriate. D is incorrect. The issue here is that an attorney cannot charge a contingent fee in a criminal matter. Model Rule 1.5(d)(2). Importantly, “a lawyer may accept property in payment for services, such as an ownership interest in an enterprise, providing this does not involve acquisition of a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of the litigation.” Model Rule 1.5, Comment [4].","analysis":"C is the correct answer. An attorney cannot charge a contingent fee for representing a defendant in a criminal case. Model Rule 1.5(d)(2). A is incorrect. Even though he obtained a good result for his client, the attorney still violated the prohibition against contingency fees in criminal cases. B is incorrect. Although the defendant may not have been able to afford the attorney otherwise, this fact does not make the retainer agreement appropriate. D is incorrect. The issue here is that an attorney cannot charge a contingent fee in a criminal matter. Model Rule 1.5(d)(2). Importantly, “a lawyer may accept property in payment for services, such as an ownership interest in an enterprise, providing this does not involve acquisition of a proprietary interest in the cause of action or subject matter of the litigation.” Model Rule 1.5, Comment [4].","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.5(d)(2)","Model Rule 1.5"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[226],"sourceAnswerPages":[321],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师与一宗刑事案件的被告订立书面聘用协议。由于被告无力支付律师费，他询问双方能否约定，如果律师成功使其免于入狱，就把某些股票的所有权转让给律师。律师同意该聘用协议。后来，对被告的指控被撤销。该聘用协议是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为指控已被撤销。","B":"适当，因为否则被告无力聘请律师。","C":"不适当，因为这是刑事案件。","D":"不适当，因为律师不得取得委托人财产的所有权利益。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。律师代理刑事案件被告时不得收取风险代理费。《职业行为示范规则》1.5(d)(2)。A 不正确。律师虽然为委托人取得了良好结果，仍违反刑事案件禁止风险代理费的规定。B 不正确。即使被告否则无力聘请律师，也不会使聘用协议适当。D 不正确。本题问题是律师不能在刑事事项中收取风险代理费。规则 1.5(d)(2)。尤其要注意，“律师可以接受财产作为服务报酬，例如企业中的所有权利益，但不得因此取得诉因或诉讼标的的所有权利益。”规则 1.5 评注 [4]。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-044","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":44,"topic":"Model Rule 1.3","question":"An attorney represents a plaintiff in a civil action that was filed a year ago and is about to be set for trial. In discussing possible trial dates with opposing counsel and the court clerk, three possible trial dates were available. The three dates were all within 60 days of each other. Without first consulting the plaintiff, the attorney requested the latest possible date because she was also representing a defendant in a felony criminal trial and wanted as much time as possible to prepare the criminal case. The later trial date will not prejudice the plaintiff. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney is not acting diligently in her representation of the plaintiff.","B":"Yes, because the attorney is favoring the criminal defendant over the civil plaintiff.","C":"No, because the later date will not prejudice the plaintiff.","D":"No, because the attorney is the master of her own schedule."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer should act with reasonable diligence and promptness when representing a client. Model Rule 1.3. Here, the civil action was filed a year ago, and any “delay” is going to be 60 days at most. Since the question states that the client will not be prejudiced by the delay, it is absolutely reasonable for the attorney to take the latest trial date. A is incorrect. As stated above, there is no indication the attorney is not acting diligently. Importantly, diligence does not mean that an attorney has to act as quickly as possible. B is incorrect. Except for the fact the plaintiff could have had an earlier trial date (which might not have actually worked for the plaintiff’s schedule), there is no indication the attorney is favoring one client over another. D is incorrect. While a lawyer can make their own schedule, a lawyer cannot manage his or her schedule in a way that prejudices clients.","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer should act with reasonable diligence and promptness when representing a client. Model Rule 1.3. Here, the civil action was filed a year ago, and any “delay” is going to be 60 days at most. Since the question states that the client will not be prejudiced by the delay, it is absolutely reasonable for the attorney to take the latest trial date. A is incorrect. As stated above, there is no indication the attorney is not acting diligently. Importantly, diligence does not mean that an attorney has to act as quickly as possible. B is incorrect. Except for the fact the plaintiff could have had an earlier trial date (which might not have actually worked for the plaintiff’s schedule), there is no indication the attorney is favoring one client over another. D is incorrect. While a lawyer can make their own schedule, a lawyer cannot manage his or her schedule in a way that prejudices clients.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer should act with reasonable diligence and promptness when representing a client. Model Rule 1.3. Here, the civil action was filed a year ago, and any “delay” is going to be 60 days at most. Since the question states that the client will not be prejudiced by the delay, it is absolutely reasonable for the attorney to take the latest trial date. A is incorrect. As stated above, there is no indication the attorney is not acting diligently. Importantly, diligence does not mean that an attorney has to act as quickly as possible. B is incorrect. Except for the fact the plaintiff could have had an earlier trial date (which might not have actually worked for the plaintiff’s schedule), there is no indication the attorney is favoring one client over another. D is incorrect. While a lawyer can make their own schedule, a lawyer cannot manage his or her schedule in a way that prejudices clients.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer should act with reasonable diligence and promptness when representing a client. Model Rule 1.3. Here, the civil action was filed a year ago, and any “delay” is going to be 60 days at most. Since the question states that the client will not be prejudiced by the delay, it is absolutely reasonable for the attorney to take the latest trial date. A is incorrect. As stated above, there is no indication the attorney is not acting diligently. Importantly, diligence does not mean that an attorney has to act as quickly as possible. B is incorrect. Except for the fact the plaintiff could have had an earlier trial date (which might not have actually worked for the plaintiff’s schedule), there is no indication the attorney is favoring one client over another. D is incorrect. While a lawyer can make their own schedule, a lawyer cannot manage his or her schedule in a way that prejudices clients.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[226],"sourceAnswerPages":[321,322],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理一宗一年前提起、即将确定审判日期的民事诉讼原告。律师与对方律师和法院书记员讨论可能的审判日期时，有三个日期可供选择，彼此相差均在 60 日内。律师没有先咨询原告，便请求最晚日期，因为她还在一宗重罪刑事审判中代理被告，希望尽可能多留出时间准备刑事案件。较晚日期不会损害原告。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师在代理原告时没有勤勉行事。","B":"是，因为律师偏袒刑事被告而非民事原告。","C":"否，因为较晚日期不会损害原告。","D":"否，因为律师是自己日程的主人。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。律师代理委托人时，应以合理的勤勉和及时程度行事。《职业行为示范规则》1.3。本题中，民事诉讼虽已提起一年，但任何“延期”最长仅 60 日。题目明确说委托人不会因延期受损，因此律师选择最晚审判日期完全合理。A 不正确。如上所述，没有迹象表明律师未勤勉行事。尤其是，勤勉不意味着律师必须以最快可能速度行事。B 不正确。除原告本可取得较早审判日期（而该日期事实上可能也不适合原告日程）外，没有迹象表明律师偏袒一名委托人。D 不正确。律师虽然可以安排自己的日程，却不能以损害委托人的方式管理日程。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-045","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":45,"topic":"Model Rule 3.4","question":"A defendant was on trial for murder. During closing arguments, the prosecutor accurately stated the testimony in the case. He ended his closing argument by saying, “The defendant’s whole defense is based on the testimony of his friend. I don’t believe the defendant’s friend was telling the truth, and I don’t think you believe him either.” The prosecutor reasonably believed that the defendant’s friend didn’t tell the truth. Is the prosecutor subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the prosecutor asserted his personal opinion about the friend’s credibility.","B":"Yes, because the prosecutor alluded to the beliefs of the jurors.","C":"No, because the prosecutor accurately stated the testimony in the case.","D":"No, because the prosecutor reasonably believed that the defendant’s friend had lied."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. At trial, an attorney is not allowed to state his or her personal opinion about the credibility of a witness. Model Rule 3.4. B is incorrect. The issue here is that the prosecutor is stating his beliefs, not that he may be alluding to the beliefs of jurors. C is incorrect. Even if the prosecutor accurately stated the testimony in the case, an attorney is not allowed to state his or her personal opinion about the credibility of a witness. D is incorrect. Even if the prosecutor reasonably believed the witness was lying, he could not state his belief to the court.","rule":"A is the correct answer. At trial, an attorney is not allowed to state his or her personal opinion about the credibility of a witness. Model Rule 3.4. B is incorrect. The issue here is that the prosecutor is stating his beliefs, not that he may be alluding to the beliefs of jurors. C is incorrect. Even if the prosecutor accurately stated the testimony in the case, an attorney is not allowed to state his or her personal opinion about the credibility of a witness. D is incorrect. Even if the prosecutor reasonably believed the witness was lying, he could not state his belief to the court.","irac":{"issue":"Is the prosecutor subject to discipline?","rule":"A is the correct answer. At trial, an attorney is not allowed to state his or her personal opinion about the credibility of a witness. Model Rule 3.4. B is incorrect. The issue here is that the prosecutor is stating his beliefs, not that he may be alluding to the beliefs of jurors. C is incorrect. Even if the prosecutor accurately stated the testimony in the case, an attorney is not allowed to state his or her personal opinion about the credibility of a witness. D is incorrect. Even if the prosecutor reasonably believed the witness was lying, he could not state his belief to the court.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. At trial, an attorney is not allowed to state his or her personal opinion about the credibility of a witness. Model Rule 3.4. B is incorrect. The issue here is that the prosecutor is stating his beliefs, not that he may be alluding to the beliefs of jurors. C is incorrect. Even if the prosecutor accurately stated the testimony in the case, an attorney is not allowed to state his or her personal opinion about the credibility of a witness. D is incorrect. Even if the prosecutor reasonably believed the witness was lying, he could not state his belief to the court.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.4"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[226,227],"sourceAnswerPages":[322],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名被告因谋杀罪受审。检察官在结案陈词中准确陈述了案件证言，并在结尾说：“被告的全部抗辩都建立在他朋友的证言上。我不相信被告的朋友说了实话，我想你们也不相信他。”检察官合理地相信被告朋友没有说实话。该检察官是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为检察官就朋友的可信度表达个人意见。","B":"是，因为检察官提到了陪审员的看法。","C":"否，因为检察官准确陈述了案件证言。","D":"否，因为检察官合理地相信被告朋友说谎。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。在审判中，律师不得就证人的可信度陈述个人意见。《职业行为示范规则》3.4。B 不正确。本题问题在于检察官表达自己的看法，而不在于他可能提到陪审员的看法。C 不正确。即使检察官准确陈述案件证言，仍不得就证人可信度表达个人意见。D 不正确。即使检察官合理地相信证人在说谎，也不能在法院陈述其个人看法。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-046","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":46,"topic":"Model Rule 7.1","question":"A recently graduated attorney began a plaintiffs’ personal injury practice, but was having a difficult time attracting clients. The attorney filmed an advertisement where the attorney appeared to be arguing a case before the United States Supreme Court. In the commercial, the Court appears to rule unanimously in favor of the attorney’s client. The voice-over states that results would vary depending upon particular legal and factual circumstances, but that the attorney would “Fight for what’s yours!” The attorney had never appeared in court. As a result of airing the commercial, the attorney received several significant cases. May the attorney use this commercial to advertise his practice?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because it is merely a dramatization.","B":"Yes, because the commercial contained an express disclaimer.","C":"No, because the commercial created an unjustified expectation about the results that the attorney could achieve in court.","D":"No, because the commercial implied the attorney had successfully appeared in court."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.1, “[a] lawyer shall not make a false or misleading communication about the lawyer or the lawyer’s services. A communication is false or misleading if it contains a material misrepresentation of fact or law, or omits a fact necessary to make the statement considered as a whole not materially misleading.” Here, the commercial is misleading in that it implies the young attorney has argued a case in court, when he has never actually done so. A is incorrect. This answer choice should be immediately discarded because it implies an attorney can do whatever he or she wants in a commercial as long as it is “merely a dramatization.” B is incorrect. While the commercial did contain a disclaimer, it still falsely implied that the attorney had appeared in court. C is incorrect. Model Rule 7.1, Comment [3] states that it is improper for a lawyer to make an advertisement that would lead a reasonable person to form an unjustified expectation that the lawyer will reach the same results in his or her case. Here, however, the larger issue is that the commercial is misleading in that it leads a viewer to believe the lawyer has appeared in court, when in fact he has not done so.","rule":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.1, “[a] lawyer shall not make a false or misleading communication about the lawyer or the lawyer’s services. A communication is false or misleading if it contains a material misrepresentation of fact or law, or omits a fact necessary to make the statement considered as a whole not materially misleading.” Here, the commercial is misleading in that it implies the young attorney has argued a case in court, when he has never actually done so. A is incorrect. This answer choice should be immediately discarded because it implies an attorney can do whatever he or she wants in a commercial as long as it is “merely a dramatization.” B is incorrect. While the commercial did contain a disclaimer, it still falsely implied that the attorney had appeared in court. C is incorrect. Model Rule 7.1, Comment [3] states that it is improper for a lawyer to make an advertisement that would lead a reasonable person to form an unjustified expectation that the lawyer will reach the same results in his or her case. Here, however, the larger issue is that the commercial is misleading in that it leads a viewer to believe the lawyer has appeared in court, when in fact he has not done so.","irac":{"issue":"May the attorney use this commercial to advertise his practice?","rule":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.1, “[a] lawyer shall not make a false or misleading communication about the lawyer or the lawyer’s services. A communication is false or misleading if it contains a material misrepresentation of fact or law, or omits a fact necessary to make the statement considered as a whole not materially misleading.” Here, the commercial is misleading in that it implies the young attorney has argued a case in court, when he has never actually done so. A is incorrect. This answer choice should be immediately discarded because it implies an attorney can do whatever he or she wants in a commercial as long as it is “merely a dramatization.” B is incorrect. While the commercial did contain a disclaimer, it still falsely implied that the attorney had appeared in court. C is incorrect. Model Rule 7.1, Comment [3] states that it is improper for a lawyer to make an advertisement that would lead a reasonable person to form an unjustified expectation that the lawyer will reach the same results in his or her case. Here, however, the larger issue is that the commercial is misleading in that it leads a viewer to believe the lawyer has appeared in court, when in fact he has not done so.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 7.1, “[a] lawyer shall not make a false or misleading communication about the lawyer or the lawyer’s services. A communication is false or misleading if it contains a material misrepresentation of fact or law, or omits a fact necessary to make the statement considered as a whole not materially misleading.” Here, the commercial is misleading in that it implies the young attorney has argued a case in court, when he has never actually done so. A is incorrect. This answer choice should be immediately discarded because it implies an attorney can do whatever he or she wants in a commercial as long as it is “merely a dramatization.” B is incorrect. While the commercial did contain a disclaimer, it still falsely implied that the attorney had appeared in court. C is incorrect. Model Rule 7.1, Comment [3] states that it is improper for a lawyer to make an advertisement that would lead a reasonable person to form an unjustified expectation that the lawyer will reach the same results in his or her case. Here, however, the larger issue is that the commercial is misleading in that it leads a viewer to believe the lawyer has appeared in court, when in fact he has not done so.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 7.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[227],"sourceAnswerPages":[322,323],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名刚毕业的律师开始从事原告方人身伤害业务，但很难吸引委托人。他拍摄一则广告，广告中自己看似正在美国最高法院前为案件辩论，而最高法院看似全体一致裁定其委托人胜诉。旁白说明，具体结果会因不同法律和事实情形而异，但律师会“为属于您的权益而战！”该律师从未在法院出庭。广告播出后，他取得数宗重大案件。律师能否使用这则广告宣传业务？","choicesZh":{"A":"能，因为这只是一段戏剧化演示。","B":"能，因为广告明确包含免责声明。","C":"不能，因为广告使人对律师在法院能够取得的结果产生没有正当依据的期待。","D":"不能，因为广告暗示律师曾成功在法院出庭。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。根据《职业行为示范规则》7.1，“律师不得就律师本人或律师服务作出虚假或具有误导性的沟通。如果一项沟通包含对事实或法律的重大虚假陈述，或遗漏了为使整体陈述不产生重大误导所必需的事实，该沟通就是虚假或具有误导性的。”本题中，广告暗示年轻律师曾在法院为案件辩论，而他事实上从未出庭，因此具有误导性。A 不正确。该选项暗示律师只要称广告是“戏剧化演示”就可以随意表现，应立即排除。B 不正确。广告虽然包含免责声明，仍虚假暗示律师曾在法院出庭。C 不正确。规则 7.1 评注 [3] 指出，律师发布会使理性人产生没有正当依据之期待，认为律师将在其案件中取得相同结果的广告，是不适当的。不过，本题更严重的问题是，广告使观众相信律师曾在法院出庭，而事实并非如此。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-047","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":47,"topic":"Model Rule 3.4 / Model Rule 3.8 / Model Rule 3.8(d)","question":"A prosecutor met with police officers to review the evidence in a case he was working on. During the meeting, the prosecutor discovered that the police had found several social media posts that showed the defendant was out of state at the time the crime was committed. The defendant’s appointed counsel was busy handling a large caseload and neglected to seek access to the prosecution’s investigative file. The prosecutor was virtually certain that suspect’s counsel was unaware of the social media posts. Does the prosecutor have to reveal the existence of the posts to the defendant’s counsel?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the posts show the defendant was out of state at the time the crime was committed.","B":"Yes, because the evidence was obtained by the police.","C":"No, because under the adversary system of criminal justice, it is expected that each party will marshal the evidence best supporting his or her position.","D":"No, because the suspect’s counsel did not seek access to the prosecution’s investigative file."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. With regard to civil cases, the adversary system contemplates that the evidence in a case is to be marshaled competitively by the parties. Model Rule 3.4, Comment [1]. Likewise, a lawyer’s duty not to conceal evidence in a civil case does not carry with it a duty to report or volunteer all relevant information. Prosecutors, however, have special responsibilities. Model Rule 3.8. “A prosecutor has the responsibility of a minister of justice and not simply that of an advocate” (Model Rule 3.8, Comment [1]) and as such, must “make timely disclosure to the defense of all evidence or information known to the prosecutor that tends to negate the guilt of the accused.” Model Rule 3.8(d). The fact that the social media posts show that the defendant was out of state at the time the crime was committed “tends to negate the guilt” of the defendant. B is incorrect. The fact the information was obtained by the police does not affect the analysis. C is incorrect. This is a true statement with regard to civil cases. See Model Rule 3.4. However, as discussed above, a prosecutor has special responsibilities in a criminal case. Model Rule 3.8. D is incorrect. The prosecutor’s responsibilities under Model Rule 3.8 exist independently of a request by defense counsel.","rule":"A is the correct answer. With regard to civil cases, the adversary system contemplates that the evidence in a case is to be marshaled competitively by the parties. Model Rule 3.4, Comment [1]. Likewise, a lawyer’s duty not to conceal evidence in a civil case does not carry with it a duty to report or volunteer all relevant information. Prosecutors, however, have special responsibilities. Model Rule 3.8. “A prosecutor has the responsibility of a minister of justice and not simply that of an advocate” (Model Rule 3.8, Comment [1]) and as such, must “make timely disclosure to the defense of all evidence or information known to the prosecutor that tends to negate the guilt of the accused.” Model Rule 3.8(d). The fact that the social media posts show that the defendant was out of state at the time the crime was committed “tends to negate the guilt” of the defendant. B is incorrect. The fact the information was obtained by the police does not affect the analysis. C is incorrect. This is a true statement with regard to civil cases. See Model Rule 3.4. However, as discussed above, a prosecutor has special responsibilities in a criminal case. Model Rule 3.8. D is incorrect. The prosecutor’s responsibilities under Model Rule 3.8 exist independently of a request by defense counsel.","irac":{"issue":"Does the prosecutor have to reveal the existence of the posts to the defendant’s counsel?","rule":"A is the correct answer. With regard to civil cases, the adversary system contemplates that the evidence in a case is to be marshaled competitively by the parties. Model Rule 3.4, Comment [1]. Likewise, a lawyer’s duty not to conceal evidence in a civil case does not carry with it a duty to report or volunteer all relevant information. Prosecutors, however, have special responsibilities. Model Rule 3.8. “A prosecutor has the responsibility of a minister of justice and not simply that of an advocate” (Model Rule 3.8, Comment [1]) and as such, must “make timely disclosure to the defense of all evidence or information known to the prosecutor that tends to negate the guilt of the accused.” Model Rule 3.8(d). The fact that the social media posts show that the defendant was out of state at the time the crime was committed “tends to negate the guilt” of the defendant. B is incorrect. The fact the information was obtained by the police does not affect the analysis. C is incorrect. This is a true statement with regard to civil cases. See Model Rule 3.4. However, as discussed above, a prosecutor has special responsibilities in a criminal case. Model Rule 3.8. D is incorrect. The prosecutor’s responsibilities under Model Rule 3.8 exist independently of a request by defense counsel.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. With regard to civil cases, the adversary system contemplates that the evidence in a case is to be marshaled competitively by the parties. Model Rule 3.4, Comment [1]. Likewise, a lawyer’s duty not to conceal evidence in a civil case does not carry with it a duty to report or volunteer all relevant information. Prosecutors, however, have special responsibilities. Model Rule 3.8. “A prosecutor has the responsibility of a minister of justice and not simply that of an advocate” (Model Rule 3.8, Comment [1]) and as such, must “make timely disclosure to the defense of all evidence or information known to the prosecutor that tends to negate the guilt of the accused.” Model Rule 3.8(d). The fact that the social media posts show that the defendant was out of state at the time the crime was committed “tends to negate the guilt” of the defendant. B is incorrect. The fact the information was obtained by the police does not affect the analysis. C is incorrect. This is a true statement with regard to civil cases. See Model Rule 3.4. However, as discussed above, a prosecutor has special responsibilities in a criminal case. Model Rule 3.8. D is incorrect. The prosecutor’s responsibilities under Model Rule 3.8 exist independently of a request by defense counsel.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.4","Model Rule 3.8","Model Rule 3.8(d)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[227,228],"sourceAnswerPages":[323],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名检察官与警察会面，审查自己正在办理之案件的证据。会面期间，检察官发现警方找到数则社交媒体帖子，表明犯罪发生时被告身处州外。被告的指定律师正在忙于处理大量案件，疏于申请查阅控方调查档案。检察官几乎可以确定，嫌疑人的律师不知道这些帖子。检察官是否必须向被告律师披露帖子的存在？","choicesZh":{"A":"必须，因为帖子表明犯罪发生时被告身处州外。","B":"必须，因为证据由警方取得。","C":"不必须，因为刑事司法采用对抗制，各方理应分别汇集最有利于自身立场的证据。","D":"不必须，因为嫌疑人的律师没有申请查阅控方调查档案。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。就民事案件而言，对抗制预期由双方以竞争方式汇集案件证据。《职业行为示范规则》3.4 评注 [1]。同样，律师在民事案件中不得隐匿证据的义务，并不附带要求其报告或主动提供全部相关信息。检察官则负有特殊责任。规则 3.8。“检察官承担的是司法维护者的责任，而不仅仅是辩护人的责任”（规则 3.8 评注 [1]），因此必须“及时向辩方披露检察官所知悉的、倾向于否定被告有罪的全部证据或信息”。规则 3.8(d)。社交媒体帖子显示犯罪发生时被告身处州外，因而“倾向于否定被告有罪”。B 不正确。信息由警方取得这一事实不影响分析。C 不正确。这一说法对民事案件是正确的。参见规则 3.4。但如上所述，检察官在刑事案件中负有特殊责任。规则 3.8。D 不正确。检察官在规则 3.8 下的责任独立于辩护律师是否提出请求。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-048","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":48,"topic":"Model Rule 3.4(f)","question":"Two weeks before the date set for trial, the attorney for the plaintiff discovered that there was an eyewitness to the accident that formed the basis of the plaintiff’s claim. The attorney interviewed the witness. The witness’s version of the accident was contrary to that of the plaintiff and, if believed by the trier of fact, would establish that the plaintiff was at fault. The witness told the attorney that she had been thinking about taking a vacation to Japan the following week and didn’t want to testify because she hated speaking in front of other people. The attorney told the witness that, since no one had subpoenaed her yet, she had no obligation to appear. She told the witness that if she didn’t want to testify, the attorney would feel a lot better if the witness took the vacation and avoided the trial. Are the attorney’s actions proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the witness didn’t want to testify.","B":"Yes, because the attorney didn’t offer the witness any inducement not to testify.","C":"No, because the attorney asked the witness to leave the jurisdiction.","D":"No, because no one had subpoenaed the witness."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from asking a person other than the client to refrain from voluntarily giving relevant information to another party unless that person is a relative, employee, or agent of the client and the lawyer reasonably believes that the person’s interests will not be adversely affected by refraining to give that information. Model Rule 3.4(f). In that case, the attorney may instruct the person to refrain from giving information unless subpoenaed. Here, the eyewitness does not appear to fall under any of the exceptions to the Rule. A is incorrect. While the witness may not want to testify, the attorney should not have asked her to leave the country. B is incorrect. Even though the attorney did not offer the witness any inducement not to appear at trial, Model Rule 3.4(f) prohibits the attorney from making any “request” to refrain from voluntarily giving relevant information. D is incorrect. Even if a witness has not yet been subpoenaed, Model Rule 3.4(f) prohibits an attorney from asking that witness to refrain from giving relevant information to another party, unless that witness falls within one of the three exceptions.","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from asking a person other than the client to refrain from voluntarily giving relevant information to another party unless that person is a relative, employee, or agent of the client and the lawyer reasonably believes that the person’s interests will not be adversely affected by refraining to give that information. Model Rule 3.4(f). In that case, the attorney may instruct the person to refrain from giving information unless subpoenaed. Here, the eyewitness does not appear to fall under any of the exceptions to the Rule. A is incorrect. While the witness may not want to testify, the attorney should not have asked her to leave the country. B is incorrect. Even though the attorney did not offer the witness any inducement not to appear at trial, Model Rule 3.4(f) prohibits the attorney from making any “request” to refrain from voluntarily giving relevant information. D is incorrect. Even if a witness has not yet been subpoenaed, Model Rule 3.4(f) prohibits an attorney from asking that witness to refrain from giving relevant information to another party, unless that witness falls within one of the three exceptions.","irac":{"issue":"Are the attorney’s actions proper?","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from asking a person other than the client to refrain from voluntarily giving relevant information to another party unless that person is a relative, employee, or agent of the client and the lawyer reasonably believes that the person’s interests will not be adversely affected by refraining to give that information. Model Rule 3.4(f). In that case, the attorney may instruct the person to refrain from giving information unless subpoenaed. Here, the eyewitness does not appear to fall under any of the exceptions to the Rule. A is incorrect. While the witness may not want to testify, the attorney should not have asked her to leave the country. B is incorrect. Even though the attorney did not offer the witness any inducement not to appear at trial, Model Rule 3.4(f) prohibits the attorney from making any “request” to refrain from voluntarily giving relevant information. D is incorrect. Even if a witness has not yet been subpoenaed, Model Rule 3.4(f) prohibits an attorney from asking that witness to refrain from giving relevant information to another party, unless that witness falls within one of the three exceptions.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer is prohibited from asking a person other than the client to refrain from voluntarily giving relevant information to another party unless that person is a relative, employee, or agent of the client and the lawyer reasonably believes that the person’s interests will not be adversely affected by refraining to give that information. Model Rule 3.4(f). In that case, the attorney may instruct the person to refrain from giving information unless subpoenaed. Here, the eyewitness does not appear to fall under any of the exceptions to the Rule. A is incorrect. While the witness may not want to testify, the attorney should not have asked her to leave the country. B is incorrect. Even though the attorney did not offer the witness any inducement not to appear at trial, Model Rule 3.4(f) prohibits the attorney from making any “request” to refrain from voluntarily giving relevant information. D is incorrect. Even if a witness has not yet been subpoenaed, Model Rule 3.4(f) prohibits an attorney from asking that witness to refrain from giving relevant information to another party, unless that witness falls within one of the three exceptions.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.4(f)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[228,229],"sourceAnswerPages":[323,324],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"距离预定审判日期还有两周时，原告律师发现事故有一名目击证人，而该事故构成原告主张的基础。律师询问证人。证人对事故经过的说法与原告相反；如果事实裁判者相信她的证言，就会认定原告对事故负有过错。证人告诉律师，她一直考虑下一周前往日本度假，而且因为讨厌在他人面前讲话，不愿作证。律师告诉证人，由于尚无人向她发出传票，她没有出庭义务。律师又说，如果她不愿作证，若证人去度假并避开审判，律师会安心得多。律师的行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为证人不愿作证。","B":"适当，因为律师没有向证人提供任何不作证的利益诱因。","C":"不适当，因为律师要求证人离开该司法管辖区。","D":"不适当，因为尚无人向证人发出传票。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。除非相关人员是委托人的亲属、雇员或代理人，且律师合理地认为该人员因不提供信息不会受到不利影响，否则律师不得要求委托人以外的人不要自愿向另一方提供相关信息。《职业行为示范规则》3.4(f)。在符合该例外时，律师可以指示该人员在未收到传票前不要提供信息。本题中，该目击证人显然不属于规则的任何例外。A 不正确。证人虽不愿作证，律师仍不应要求她离开本国。B 不正确。即使律师没有向证人提供不出庭的任何利益诱因，规则 3.4(f) 仍禁止律师作出任何要求其不要自愿提供相关信息的“请求”。D 不正确。即使证人尚未收到传票，规则 3.4(f) 仍禁止律师要求该证人不要向另一方提供相关信息，除非该证人属于三项例外之一。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-049","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":49,"topic":"Model Rule 1.11(a)(2) / Model Rule 1.11","question":"An attorney was employed in the state’s environmental protection division during the time that an investigation of a suspected polluter was being conducted by that office. The attorney took no part in the investigation and had no knowledge of the facts other than those disclosed in the press. Two months ago, the attorney left the environmental protection division and entered private practice. Last week, the suspected polluter was charged with offenses allegedly disclosed by the prior investigation. The suspected polluter asked the attorney to represent him, and the attorney agreed to do so. The environmental protection division was promptly notified and consented to the representation. May the attorney represent the suspected polluter in this case?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the environmental protection division consented to the representation.","B":"Yes, because the attorney had no responsibility for or knowledge of the facts of the investigation of the suspect.","C":"No, because the attorney was employed by the environmental protection division during the time of the investigation.","D":"No, because the charges arose from the prior investigation."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.11(a)(2), “a lawyer who has formerly served as a public officer or employee of the government . . . shall not otherwise represent a client in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee” unless authorized to do so by the government agency. Here, the attorney had nothing to do with the investigation. Remember, Model Rule 1.11 is concerned with former government employees exploiting knowledge about a party unfairly. Here, there is no knowledge to exploit. A is incorrect. Although the environmental protection division consented, the big issue here is that the attorney had nothing to do with the earlier investigation. Thus, the consent was unnecessary. C is incorrect. As stated above, for Model Rule 1.11 to apply, the lawyer must have “participated personally and substantially” in the matter. Although the attorney worked for the environmental protection division, he had nothing to do with the earlier investigation. D is incorrect. As stated above, for Model Rule 1.11 to apply, the lawyer must have “participated personally and substantially” in the matter. Consequently, the fact this case arose from the earlier investigation doesn’t change the analysis.","rule":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.11(a)(2), “a lawyer who has formerly served as a public officer or employee of the government . . . shall not otherwise represent a client in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee” unless authorized to do so by the government agency. Here, the attorney had nothing to do with the investigation. Remember, Model Rule 1.11 is concerned with former government employees exploiting knowledge about a party unfairly. Here, there is no knowledge to exploit. A is incorrect. Although the environmental protection division consented, the big issue here is that the attorney had nothing to do with the earlier investigation. Thus, the consent was unnecessary. C is incorrect. As stated above, for Model Rule 1.11 to apply, the lawyer must have “participated personally and substantially” in the matter. Although the attorney worked for the environmental protection division, he had nothing to do with the earlier investigation. D is incorrect. As stated above, for Model Rule 1.11 to apply, the lawyer must have “participated personally and substantially” in the matter. Consequently, the fact this case arose from the earlier investigation doesn’t change the analysis.","irac":{"issue":"May the attorney represent the suspected polluter in this case?","rule":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.11(a)(2), “a lawyer who has formerly served as a public officer or employee of the government . . . shall not otherwise represent a client in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee” unless authorized to do so by the government agency. Here, the attorney had nothing to do with the investigation. Remember, Model Rule 1.11 is concerned with former government employees exploiting knowledge about a party unfairly. Here, there is no knowledge to exploit. A is incorrect. Although the environmental protection division consented, the big issue here is that the attorney had nothing to do with the earlier investigation. Thus, the consent was unnecessary. C is incorrect. As stated above, for Model Rule 1.11 to apply, the lawyer must have “participated personally and substantially” in the matter. Although the attorney worked for the environmental protection division, he had nothing to do with the earlier investigation. D is incorrect. As stated above, for Model Rule 1.11 to apply, the lawyer must have “participated personally and substantially” in the matter. Consequently, the fact this case arose from the earlier investigation doesn’t change the analysis.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. Under Model Rule 1.11(a)(2), “a lawyer who has formerly served as a public officer or employee of the government . . . shall not otherwise represent a client in connection with a matter in which the lawyer participated personally and substantially as a public officer or employee” unless authorized to do so by the government agency. Here, the attorney had nothing to do with the investigation. Remember, Model Rule 1.11 is concerned with former government employees exploiting knowledge about a party unfairly. Here, there is no knowledge to exploit. A is incorrect. Although the environmental protection division consented, the big issue here is that the attorney had nothing to do with the earlier investigation. Thus, the consent was unnecessary. C is incorrect. As stated above, for Model Rule 1.11 to apply, the lawyer must have “participated personally and substantially” in the matter. Although the attorney worked for the environmental protection division, he had nothing to do with the earlier investigation. D is incorrect. As stated above, for Model Rule 1.11 to apply, the lawyer must have “participated personally and substantially” in the matter. Consequently, the fact this case arose from the earlier investigation doesn’t change the analysis.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.11(a)(2)","Model Rule 1.11"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[229],"sourceAnswerPages":[324],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师曾在州环境保护部门任职，当时该部门正在调查一名涉嫌污染者。该律师没有参与调查，对事实的了解也仅限于媒体报道。两个月前，律师离开环境保护部门，开始私人执业。上周，涉嫌污染者因先前调查据称揭示的犯罪受到指控。他请求该律师代理，律师同意。环境保护部门很快收到通知，并同意该代理。律师能否在本案中代理涉嫌污染者？","choicesZh":{"A":"能，因为环境保护部门同意代理。","B":"能，因为律师对嫌疑人的调查既不负有职责，也不了解相关事实。","C":"不能，因为调查期间律师受雇于环境保护部门。","D":"不能，因为指控源于先前调查。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。根据《职业行为示范规则》1.11(a)(2)，“先前曾担任政府公职人员或政府雇员的律师……不得以其他方式代理委托人处理其作为公职人员或雇员时曾亲自且实质性参与的事项”，除非取得政府机构授权。本题中，该律师与调查毫无关系。请记住，规则 1.11 关注前政府雇员不公平地利用有关某一方的知识。本题中没有可供利用的知识。A 不正确。环境保护部门虽然同意，但核心问题是该律师未参与先前调查，因此同意没有必要。C 不正确。如上所述，规则 1.11 适用要求律师曾“亲自且实质性参与”有关事项。该律师虽然在环境保护部门工作，却与先前调查毫无关系。D 不正确。如上所述，规则 1.11 适用要求律师曾“亲自且实质性参与”有关事项。因此，本案源于先前调查不会改变分析。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-050","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":50,"topic":"Model Rule 1.15","question":"When one of the attorney’s regular clients left on a year abroad, the client delivered to the attorney more than enough money to pay the client’s taxes when they became due. The attorney placed the money in her Clients’ Trust Account. Soon after the client left, the attorney was in need of a temporary loan. Although she was reasonably certain the client would not object, she still tried to call the client before taking any money out of the client’s account. However, the attorney could not reach the client. The attorney borrowed the money and replaced it 10 days later, resulting in no fees or other damages to the client. After the client returned, the attorney told the client what she had done, and the client approved the attorney’s conduct. Was it proper for the attorney to take the loan?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the client approved the attorney’s conduct.","B":"Yes, because the attorney was reasonably certain the client would not object.","C":"No, because the attorney used the client’s funds for a personal purpose.","D":"No, because the attorney could not reach her client before taking the funds."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer is required to keep a client’s funds separate from his or her own. Model Rule 1.15. Importantly, the attorney could not use the funds in her Clients’ Trust Account to give herself a loan, as those funds belonged to her client. A is incorrect. This is an example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Even though the client may have approved the attorney’s conduct, the attorney had already violated the Rules before she told her client and received the client’s approval. B is incorrect. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Whether or not the client is harmed, an attorney may not use a client’s funds for his or her personal use. D is incorrect. While this answer choice implies some sort of issue of consent, the choice simply states that the attorney’s actions would have been proper had she reached the client before taking the funds. It doesn’t actually say anything regarding consent.","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer is required to keep a client’s funds separate from his or her own. Model Rule 1.15. Importantly, the attorney could not use the funds in her Clients’ Trust Account to give herself a loan, as those funds belonged to her client. A is incorrect. This is an example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Even though the client may have approved the attorney’s conduct, the attorney had already violated the Rules before she told her client and received the client’s approval. B is incorrect. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Whether or not the client is harmed, an attorney may not use a client’s funds for his or her personal use. D is incorrect. While this answer choice implies some sort of issue of consent, the choice simply states that the attorney’s actions would have been proper had she reached the client before taking the funds. It doesn’t actually say anything regarding consent.","irac":{"issue":"Was it proper for the attorney to take the loan?","rule":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer is required to keep a client’s funds separate from his or her own. Model Rule 1.15. Importantly, the attorney could not use the funds in her Clients’ Trust Account to give herself a loan, as those funds belonged to her client. A is incorrect. This is an example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Even though the client may have approved the attorney’s conduct, the attorney had already violated the Rules before she told her client and received the client’s approval. B is incorrect. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Whether or not the client is harmed, an attorney may not use a client’s funds for his or her personal use. D is incorrect. While this answer choice implies some sort of issue of consent, the choice simply states that the attorney’s actions would have been proper had she reached the client before taking the funds. It doesn’t actually say anything regarding consent.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. A lawyer is required to keep a client’s funds separate from his or her own. Model Rule 1.15. Importantly, the attorney could not use the funds in her Clients’ Trust Account to give herself a loan, as those funds belonged to her client. A is incorrect. This is an example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Even though the client may have approved the attorney’s conduct, the attorney had already violated the Rules before she told her client and received the client’s approval. B is incorrect. This is another example of the “no-harm, no-foul” wrong answer choice. Whether or not the client is harmed, an attorney may not use a client’s funds for his or her personal use. D is incorrect. While this answer choice implies some sort of issue of consent, the choice simply states that the attorney’s actions would have been proper had she reached the client before taking the funds. It doesn’t actually say anything regarding consent.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.15"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[229,230],"sourceAnswerPages":[324,325],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师的常年委托人出国一年时，向律师交付了远超到期税款所需的资金。律师将资金存入委托人信托账户。委托人离开后不久，律师需要一笔短期贷款。她虽合理地确信委托人不会反对，仍在从委托人账户取款前尝试联系，但未能联系到。律师借用该款，并在 10 日后归还，没有给委托人造成任何费用或其他损害。委托人返回后，律师告知自己所做之事，委托人认可了律师行为。律师借用该款是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"适当，因为委托人认可了律师行为。","B":"适当，因为律师合理地确信委托人不会反对。","C":"不适当，因为律师将委托人资金用于个人目的。","D":"不适当，因为律师取款前未能联系委托人。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。律师必须将委托人资金与自己的资金分开保管。《职业行为示范规则》1.15。尤其重要的是，委托人信托账户中的资金属于委托人，律师不得用它向自己提供贷款。A 不正确。这是“没有损害，就没有违规”型错误选项。即使委托人后来认可，律师在告知并取得认可前已经违反规则。B 不正确。这又是“没有损害，就没有违规”型错误选项。无论委托人是否受到损害，律师都不得把委托人资金用于个人用途。D 不正确。该选项似乎提出某种同意问题，却只说如果律师取款前联系到委托人，行为就会适当，并没有真正说明是否取得同意。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-051","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":51,"topic":"Rule 2.9 / Rule 2.9(A)(1) / Rule 2.9(A)(1)(b)","question":"One day, a trial judge was in his office when the telephone rang at his clerk’s desk. Since everyone else had already left for the day, the judge answered it. The call was from a lawyer in a case presently pending before the judge. The lawyer was calling to attempt to reschedule a pretrial conference because of a family emergency. The lawyer had tried to call opposing counsel in the case, but she was not answering his calls. The judge agreed to reschedule the pretrial conference for the following week. The next day, the judge instructed the clerk to contact opposing counsel to inform her of the telephone call and the fact that the pretrial conference had been rescheduled. Did the judge act properly?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the communication only dealt with scheduling matters.","B":"Yes, because no one else was in the office to take the call.","C":"No, because the judge engaged in ex parte communication.","D":"No, because the judge waited until the next day to contact opposing counsel."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. Generally, a judge is not allowed to engage in ex parte communications. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9. However, “when circumstances require it, ex parte communication for scheduling, administrative, or emergency purposes, which does not address substantive matters, is permitted.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1). If a judge does engage in such communication, he or she should make provision to promptly notify all the parties of the substance of the communication. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1)(b). Here, the circumstances of the situation made the judge’s actions proper, as no one else was there to answer the phone, the lawyer had an emergency and had tried to call opposing counsel, the matter merely involved rescheduling, and the judge promptly informed the opposing party of the rescheduling. B is incorrect. Although there was no one else to take the call, the communication could have been improper if it had involved some other matter besides scheduling. C is incorrect. As stated above, in certain circumstances, it is proper for a judge to engage in ex parte communications. D is incorrect. There is no time limit for informing the parties, so long as the judge promptly notifies all the parties of the substance of the communication. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1)(b).","rule":"A is the correct answer. Generally, a judge is not allowed to engage in ex parte communications. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9. However, “when circumstances require it, ex parte communication for scheduling, administrative, or emergency purposes, which does not address substantive matters, is permitted.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1). If a judge does engage in such communication, he or she should make provision to promptly notify all the parties of the substance of the communication. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1)(b). Here, the circumstances of the situation made the judge’s actions proper, as no one else was there to answer the phone, the lawyer had an emergency and had tried to call opposing counsel, the matter merely involved rescheduling, and the judge promptly informed the opposing party of the rescheduling. B is incorrect. Although there was no one else to take the call, the communication could have been improper if it had involved some other matter besides scheduling. C is incorrect. As stated above, in certain circumstances, it is proper for a judge to engage in ex parte communications. D is incorrect. There is no time limit for informing the parties, so long as the judge promptly notifies all the parties of the substance of the communication. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1)(b).","irac":{"issue":"Did the judge act properly?","rule":"A is the correct answer. Generally, a judge is not allowed to engage in ex parte communications. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9. However, “when circumstances require it, ex parte communication for scheduling, administrative, or emergency purposes, which does not address substantive matters, is permitted.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1). If a judge does engage in such communication, he or she should make provision to promptly notify all the parties of the substance of the communication. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1)(b). Here, the circumstances of the situation made the judge’s actions proper, as no one else was there to answer the phone, the lawyer had an emergency and had tried to call opposing counsel, the matter merely involved rescheduling, and the judge promptly informed the opposing party of the rescheduling. B is incorrect. Although there was no one else to take the call, the communication could have been improper if it had involved some other matter besides scheduling. C is incorrect. As stated above, in certain circumstances, it is proper for a judge to engage in ex parte communications. D is incorrect. There is no time limit for informing the parties, so long as the judge promptly notifies all the parties of the substance of the communication. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1)(b).","analysis":"A is the correct answer. Generally, a judge is not allowed to engage in ex parte communications. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9. However, “when circumstances require it, ex parte communication for scheduling, administrative, or emergency purposes, which does not address substantive matters, is permitted.” Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1). If a judge does engage in such communication, he or she should make provision to promptly notify all the parties of the substance of the communication. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1)(b). Here, the circumstances of the situation made the judge’s actions proper, as no one else was there to answer the phone, the lawyer had an emergency and had tried to call opposing counsel, the matter merely involved rescheduling, and the judge promptly informed the opposing party of the rescheduling. B is incorrect. Although there was no one else to take the call, the communication could have been improper if it had involved some other matter besides scheduling. C is incorrect. As stated above, in certain circumstances, it is proper for a judge to engage in ex parte communications. D is incorrect. There is no time limit for informing the parties, so long as the judge promptly notifies all the parties of the substance of the communication. Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(1)(b).","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 2.9","Rule 2.9(A)(1)","Rule 2.9(A)(1)(b)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[230],"sourceAnswerPages":[325],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一天，一名初审法官正在办公室里，这时书记员桌上的电话响了。由于其他人当天都已经下班，法官接了电话。来电者是一名律师，其代理的案件当时正在该法官面前审理。该律师因家庭紧急情况，想尝试重新安排一次审前会议。他曾试图致电该案的对方律师，但对方一直没有接听。法官同意将审前会议改到下一周。第二天，法官指示书记员联系对方律师，告知她这通电话以及审前会议已经改期的事实。法官的行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该沟通只涉及日程安排事项。","B":"是，因为办公室里没有其他人可以接听电话。","C":"否，因为法官进行了单方沟通。","D":"否，因为法官等到第二天才联系对方律师。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。一般而言，法官不得进行单方沟通。《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.9。然而，“在情况确有需要时，为安排日程、处理行政事务或应对紧急情况而进行、且不涉及实体事项的单方沟通，是允许的。”《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.9(A)(1)。如果法官确实进行了此类沟通，应当作出安排，及时将沟通的实质内容通知所有当事人。《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.9(A)(1)(b)。在本题中，当时的具体情况使法官的行为适当：没有其他人在场接听电话；律师遇到紧急情况并已尝试联系对方律师；所涉事项仅为重新安排日程；而且法官及时将改期一事通知了对方。因此，A 正确。B 不正确。尽管当时没有其他人可以接听电话，但如果沟通涉及日程安排以外的事项，该沟通仍可能不适当。C 不正确。如上所述，在某些情况下，法官进行单方沟通是适当的。D 不正确。只要法官及时将沟通的实质内容通知所有当事人，规则并未规定一个固定的通知时限。《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.9(A)(1)(b)。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-052","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":52,"topic":"Professional Responsibility","question":"When an attorney was elected to the state legislature, he closed his law office. Soon thereafter, a tech startup, one of the legislator’s former private clients and a constituent in his legislative district, asked him to try to persuade a state agency to rule in its favor in a copyright dispute. The legislator wrote a letter on his legislative letterhead to the agency’s chair, urging that the agency rule in the startup’s favor. Eventually the agency ruled in the startup’s favor, in part because of the legislator’s efforts. Was it proper for the legislator to write the letter to the agency?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the legislator acted on behalf of the startup as a constituent and not as a client.","B":"Yes, because the legislator used his legislative letterhead.","C":"No, because the agency ruled in the startup’s favor in part due to the legislator’s efforts.","D":"No, because the legislator used his public position to influence the agency."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. A practicing lawyer who is also a legislator may not use his or her official authority for the benefit of a private client. However, a legislator who is a lawyer but who is no longer practicing law may assist a constituent in his or her dealings with a state agency. Here, the facts show that the legislator is helping the startup as a constituent, not a client. Consequently, the legislator’s action would not subject him to discipline. B is incorrect. The paper the letter was written on does not change the analysis. C is incorrect. The legislator’s efforts on behalf of a constituent were proper because he was no longer practicing law and was not seeking to assist the startup as a private client. Therefore, it does not matter that the legislator in fact influenced the agency in his former client’s favor. D is incorrect. Because the legislator is acting on the startup’s behalf as a constituent, his actions are proper.","rule":"A is the correct answer. A practicing lawyer who is also a legislator may not use his or her official authority for the benefit of a private client. However, a legislator who is a lawyer but who is no longer practicing law may assist a constituent in his or her dealings with a state agency. Here, the facts show that the legislator is helping the startup as a constituent, not a client. Consequently, the legislator’s action would not subject him to discipline. B is incorrect. The paper the letter was written on does not change the analysis. C is incorrect. The legislator’s efforts on behalf of a constituent were proper because he was no longer practicing law and was not seeking to assist the startup as a private client. Therefore, it does not matter that the legislator in fact influenced the agency in his former client’s favor. D is incorrect. Because the legislator is acting on the startup’s behalf as a constituent, his actions are proper.","irac":{"issue":"Was it proper for the legislator to write the letter to the agency?","rule":"A is the correct answer. A practicing lawyer who is also a legislator may not use his or her official authority for the benefit of a private client. However, a legislator who is a lawyer but who is no longer practicing law may assist a constituent in his or her dealings with a state agency. Here, the facts show that the legislator is helping the startup as a constituent, not a client. Consequently, the legislator’s action would not subject him to discipline. B is incorrect. The paper the letter was written on does not change the analysis. C is incorrect. The legislator’s efforts on behalf of a constituent were proper because he was no longer practicing law and was not seeking to assist the startup as a private client. Therefore, it does not matter that the legislator in fact influenced the agency in his former client’s favor. D is incorrect. Because the legislator is acting on the startup’s behalf as a constituent, his actions are proper.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. A practicing lawyer who is also a legislator may not use his or her official authority for the benefit of a private client. However, a legislator who is a lawyer but who is no longer practicing law may assist a constituent in his or her dealings with a state agency. Here, the facts show that the legislator is helping the startup as a constituent, not a client. Consequently, the legislator’s action would not subject him to discipline. B is incorrect. The paper the letter was written on does not change the analysis. C is incorrect. The legislator’s efforts on behalf of a constituent were proper because he was no longer practicing law and was not seeking to assist the startup as a private client. Therefore, it does not matter that the legislator in fact influenced the agency in his former client’s favor. D is incorrect. Because the legislator is acting on the startup’s behalf as a constituent, his actions are proper.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":[],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[230,231],"sourceAnswerPages":[325,326],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师当选州议会议员后，关闭了自己的律师事务所。不久之后，一家科技初创公司——既是该议员以前执业时的委托人，也是其选区内的选民——请求他设法说服一个州行政机构在一项版权争议中作出对该公司有利的裁决。该议员使用印有其议员身份的信笺，致信该机构负责人，敦促该机构作出有利于该公司的裁决。最终，该机构裁决该公司胜诉，其中部分原因是该议员所作的努力。该议员向该机构写这封信是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为该议员是以该公司选民代表的身份，而不是以其律师的身份行事。","B":"是，因为该议员使用了印有其议员身份的信笺。","C":"否，因为该机构之所以作出有利于该公司的裁决，部分是由于该议员的努力。","D":"否，因为该议员利用公职身份影响了该机构。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。一名同时担任议员的执业律师，不得利用其官方权力为私人委托人谋取利益。但是，一名具有律师资格但已不再执业的议员，可以协助其选民处理与州行政机构之间的事务。本题事实表明，该议员是在帮助作为其选民的初创公司，而不是在帮助其委托人。因此，该议员的行为不会使其应受纪律处分。B 不正确。书信使用何种纸张并不改变分析结果。C 不正确。该议员已不再执业，也不是以私人委托关系为该公司提供帮助，因此他代表选民所作的努力是适当的。所以，即使该议员事实上影响了行政机构，使其作出有利于其前委托人的决定，也不影响结论。D 不正确。由于该议员是在以选民代表的身份为该公司行事，其行为是适当的。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-053","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":53,"topic":"Model Rule 3.3","question":"When called by the attorney as witnesses in a personal injury claim, both the client and his wife testified that they had never seen a warning label on the product. The existence of a warning label was the manufacturer’s primary defense. After their testimony, but while the trial was still in progress, the attorney learned from the wife’s sister that there indeed had been a warning label on the box, but that the wife had removed and destroyed it. When the attorney confronted the wife with her sister’s statement, the wife admitted destroying the label but insisted that her husband knew nothing about it. The attorney continued the trial, but made no reference to the absence of a warning label in his summation to the jury. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney failed to take reasonable remedial action after he realized that the wife had given perjured testimony.","B":"Yes, because the wife committed perjury.","C":"No, because he did not reference the warning label in his summation.","D":"No, because the attorney did not rely on the wife’s testimony once he discovered the perjury."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. “If a lawyer, the lawyer’s client, or a witness called by the lawyer, has offered material evidence and the lawyer comes to know of its falsity, the lawyer shall take reasonable remedial measures, including, if necessary, disclosure to the tribunal.” Model Rule 3.3. Simply not referring to the absence of the warning label in his summation is not a valid remedial measure. B is incorrect. While the attorney called the wife as a witness, the issue is that the attorney came to know of the falsity of the testimony and took no reasonable remedial measures to correct it. If the attorney never discovered the lie, he could not reasonably be expected to do something about it. C is incorrect. As stated above, simply not referring to the absence of the warning label in his summation is not a valid remedial measure. D is incorrect. While he may not have relied on the testimony, the attorney still knew of its falsity and did nothing to remediate matters.","rule":"A is the correct answer. “If a lawyer, the lawyer’s client, or a witness called by the lawyer, has offered material evidence and the lawyer comes to know of its falsity, the lawyer shall take reasonable remedial measures, including, if necessary, disclosure to the tribunal.” Model Rule 3.3. Simply not referring to the absence of the warning label in his summation is not a valid remedial measure. B is incorrect. While the attorney called the wife as a witness, the issue is that the attorney came to know of the falsity of the testimony and took no reasonable remedial measures to correct it. If the attorney never discovered the lie, he could not reasonably be expected to do something about it. C is incorrect. As stated above, simply not referring to the absence of the warning label in his summation is not a valid remedial measure. D is incorrect. While he may not have relied on the testimony, the attorney still knew of its falsity and did nothing to remediate matters.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"A is the correct answer. “If a lawyer, the lawyer’s client, or a witness called by the lawyer, has offered material evidence and the lawyer comes to know of its falsity, the lawyer shall take reasonable remedial measures, including, if necessary, disclosure to the tribunal.” Model Rule 3.3. Simply not referring to the absence of the warning label in his summation is not a valid remedial measure. B is incorrect. While the attorney called the wife as a witness, the issue is that the attorney came to know of the falsity of the testimony and took no reasonable remedial measures to correct it. If the attorney never discovered the lie, he could not reasonably be expected to do something about it. C is incorrect. As stated above, simply not referring to the absence of the warning label in his summation is not a valid remedial measure. D is incorrect. While he may not have relied on the testimony, the attorney still knew of its falsity and did nothing to remediate matters.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. “If a lawyer, the lawyer’s client, or a witness called by the lawyer, has offered material evidence and the lawyer comes to know of its falsity, the lawyer shall take reasonable remedial measures, including, if necessary, disclosure to the tribunal.” Model Rule 3.3. Simply not referring to the absence of the warning label in his summation is not a valid remedial measure. B is incorrect. While the attorney called the wife as a witness, the issue is that the attorney came to know of the falsity of the testimony and took no reasonable remedial measures to correct it. If the attorney never discovered the lie, he could not reasonably be expected to do something about it. C is incorrect. As stated above, simply not referring to the absence of the warning label in his summation is not a valid remedial measure. D is incorrect. While he may not have relied on the testimony, the attorney still knew of its falsity and did nothing to remediate matters.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.3"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[231],"sourceAnswerPages":[326],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"在一宗人身伤害索赔案件中，委托人及其妻子被律师传唤作证，两人均作证称从未在该产品上看到过警示标签。制造商的主要抗辩正是该警示标签的存在。两人作证之后、但审判仍在进行期间，律师从妻子的姐妹处得知，包装盒上确实曾有一个警示标签，但妻子已将其撕下并销毁。律师以她姐妹的陈述质问妻子时，妻子承认销毁了标签，但坚持称其丈夫对此毫不知情。律师继续进行审判，但在向陪审团作总结陈词时，没有提及不存在警示标签一事。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师在意识到妻子作了伪证后，未采取合理的补救措施。","B":"是，因为妻子作了伪证。","C":"否，因为律师在总结陈词中没有提及警示标签。","D":"否，因为律师发现伪证后没有再依赖妻子的证言。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。“如果律师、律师的委托人或律师传唤的证人已经提出重要证据，而律师后来确知该证据为虚假，律师应当采取合理的补救措施，必要时包括向审裁机构披露。”《职业行为示范规则》规则 3.3。律师仅仅不在总结陈词中提及不存在警示标签，并不是有效的补救措施。B 不正确。虽然律师传唤了妻子作证，但关键问题在于，律师后来确知证言虚假，却没有采取合理的补救措施予以纠正。如果律师从未发现谎言，就不能合理期待他对此采取行动。C 不正确。如上所述，仅仅不在总结陈词中提及不存在警示标签，并不是有效的补救措施。D 不正确。即使律师后来没有依赖该证言，他仍然知道证言虚假，却没有采取任何措施补救。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-054","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":54,"topic":"Model Rule 1.6","question":"The partners in a firm gave all lawyers in the firm, on a monthly basis, detailed descriptions of the work they were doing for their different clients. One of the partners mentioned this practice to a new client, and the client complained that his confidences had been violated. Are the partners subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because sharing the information increased the risk that it might be improperly disclosed to third parties outside the firm.","B":"Yes, because lawyers may not disclose client information, even to other firm lawyers, unless the disclosure is in furtherance of the representation.","C":"No, because, absent client instructions to the contrary, lawyers may discuss client information with other lawyers in the firm.","D":"No, because lawyers may discuss client information with other lawyers in the firm, regardless of client instructions to the contrary, as long as the disclosure does not disadvantage the client."},"answer":"C","explanation":"C is the correct answer. “Except to the extent that the client’s instructions or special circumstances limit that authority, a lawyer is impliedly authorized to make disclosures about a client when appropriate in carrying out the representation . . . . Lawyers in a firm may, in the course of the firm’s practice, disclose to each other information relating to a client of the firm, unless the client has instructed that particular information be confined to specified lawyers.” Model Rule 1.6, Comment [5]. Consequently, it was proper for the partners to give detailed descriptions of the work being done. A is incorrect. Although more people knowing about something could theoretically increase the risk of that information getting out, the Rules allow lawyers to share information within a firm. B is incorrect. The Rules allow lawyers to disclose information to other lawyers within a firm. This makes sense, considering the desire for competent and efficient representation. D is incorrect. Lawyers may discuss cases within a firm so long as the client has not given instructions to the contrary. This answer choice should have been crossed out immediately since it implies that lawyers can willfully ignore client instructions regarding confidences.","rule":"C is the correct answer. “Except to the extent that the client’s instructions or special circumstances limit that authority, a lawyer is impliedly authorized to make disclosures about a client when appropriate in carrying out the representation . . . . Lawyers in a firm may, in the course of the firm’s practice, disclose to each other information relating to a client of the firm, unless the client has instructed that particular information be confined to specified lawyers.” Model Rule 1.6, Comment [5]. Consequently, it was proper for the partners to give detailed descriptions of the work being done. A is incorrect. Although more people knowing about something could theoretically increase the risk of that information getting out, the Rules allow lawyers to share information within a firm. B is incorrect. The Rules allow lawyers to disclose information to other lawyers within a firm. This makes sense, considering the desire for competent and efficient representation. D is incorrect. Lawyers may discuss cases within a firm so long as the client has not given instructions to the contrary. This answer choice should have been crossed out immediately since it implies that lawyers can willfully ignore client instructions regarding confidences.","irac":{"issue":"Are the partners subject to discipline?","rule":"C is the correct answer. “Except to the extent that the client’s instructions or special circumstances limit that authority, a lawyer is impliedly authorized to make disclosures about a client when appropriate in carrying out the representation . . . . Lawyers in a firm may, in the course of the firm’s practice, disclose to each other information relating to a client of the firm, unless the client has instructed that particular information be confined to specified lawyers.” Model Rule 1.6, Comment [5]. Consequently, it was proper for the partners to give detailed descriptions of the work being done. A is incorrect. Although more people knowing about something could theoretically increase the risk of that information getting out, the Rules allow lawyers to share information within a firm. B is incorrect. The Rules allow lawyers to disclose information to other lawyers within a firm. This makes sense, considering the desire for competent and efficient representation. D is incorrect. Lawyers may discuss cases within a firm so long as the client has not given instructions to the contrary. This answer choice should have been crossed out immediately since it implies that lawyers can willfully ignore client instructions regarding confidences.","analysis":"C is the correct answer. “Except to the extent that the client’s instructions or special circumstances limit that authority, a lawyer is impliedly authorized to make disclosures about a client when appropriate in carrying out the representation . . . . Lawyers in a firm may, in the course of the firm’s practice, disclose to each other information relating to a client of the firm, unless the client has instructed that particular information be confined to specified lawyers.” Model Rule 1.6, Comment [5]. Consequently, it was proper for the partners to give detailed descriptions of the work being done. A is incorrect. Although more people knowing about something could theoretically increase the risk of that information getting out, the Rules allow lawyers to share information within a firm. B is incorrect. The Rules allow lawyers to disclose information to other lawyers within a firm. This makes sense, considering the desire for competent and efficient representation. D is incorrect. Lawyers may discuss cases within a firm so long as the client has not given instructions to the contrary. This answer choice should have been crossed out immediately since it implies that lawyers can willfully ignore client instructions regarding confidences.","conclusion":"Answer C is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.6"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[231,232],"sourceAnswerPages":[326,327],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家律师事务所的合伙人每月都会让所内所有律师详细说明他们正在为不同委托人进行的工作。一名合伙人向一位新委托人提到这种做法，该委托人抱怨说，他的保密信息受到了侵犯。这些合伙人是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为共享这些信息增加了其被不当披露给事务所以外第三方的风险。","B":"是，因为除非披露是为了推进代理事项，否则律师不得披露委托人信息，即使向事务所内其他律师披露也不可以。","C":"否，因为在委托人没有作出相反指示的情况下，律师可以与事务所内其他律师讨论委托人信息。","D":"否，因为只要披露不会使委托人处于不利地位，无论委托人是否作出相反指示，律师都可以与事务所内其他律师讨论委托人信息。"},"explanationZh":"C 是正确答案。“除非委托人的指示或特殊情况限制了这种权限，律师在适当履行代理工作时，被默示授权披露与委托人有关的信息……一家律师事务所的律师在事务所执业过程中，可以相互披露与该所委托人有关的信息，除非委托人已经指示特定信息仅限指定律师知悉。”《职业行为示范规则》规则 1.6，评注 [5]。因此，合伙人让律师详细说明正在进行的工作是适当的。A 不正确。虽然从理论上说，知道某项信息的人越多，该信息外泄的风险可能越大，但《规则》允许律师在事务所内部共享信息。B 不正确。《规则》允许律师向同一事务所内的其他律师披露信息。考虑到对胜任且高效代理的需要，这一规定是合理的。D 不正确。只要委托人没有作出相反指示，律师就可以在事务所内部讨论案件。本选项暗示律师可以故意无视委托人关于保密信息的指示，因此应当立即排除。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-055","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":55,"topic":"Model Rule 1.1","question":"An attorney accepted the representation of a plaintiff in a products liability case on a contingent fee basis. While preparing the case for trial, the attorney realized that the direct examination and cross-examination of the experts would involve engineering issues with which the attorney was not familiar and, as a consequence, the attorney might not be able to represent the plaintiff competently. Without informing the plaintiff, the attorney consulted another attorney, who is also an engineer and a recognized specialist in products liability. The two attorneys agreed that the other attorney would participate in the trial to the limited extent of conducting the direct examination and cross-examination of the experts and that the attorneys would divide the fee in proportion to the services performed and the responsibility assumed by each. Upon conclusion of the matter, the attorney provided the plaintiff with a written statement explaining the relevant fees. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorneys’ agreement limited the scope of each one’s representation.","B":"Yes, because the plaintiff was not advised of the association of the other attorney.","C":"No, because the fee would be divided in proportion to the services performed and the responsibility assumed by each.","D":"No, because the fee to be paid by the plaintiff was not increased by reason of the other attorney’s association."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. Although an attorney can retain or contract with other lawyers outside the lawyer’s own firm to assist in the provision of legal service, “the lawyer should ordinarily obtain informed consent from the client.” Model Rule 1.1, Comment [6]. While an attorney has the right to make decisions in many aspects of a representation, in the case of bringing in another attorney, the attorney must first ask for client consent. A is incorrect. The agreement between the attorneys is not an example of a prohibited limitation. C is incorrect. The division of the fee does not change the fact that the attorney brought in another attorney without asking the client first. D is incorrect. While this implies a “no-harm, no-foul” situation, the client would still be harmed by having his representation undertaken by an attorney he has no knowledge about.","rule":"B is the correct answer. Although an attorney can retain or contract with other lawyers outside the lawyer’s own firm to assist in the provision of legal service, “the lawyer should ordinarily obtain informed consent from the client.” Model Rule 1.1, Comment [6]. While an attorney has the right to make decisions in many aspects of a representation, in the case of bringing in another attorney, the attorney must first ask for client consent. A is incorrect. The agreement between the attorneys is not an example of a prohibited limitation. C is incorrect. The division of the fee does not change the fact that the attorney brought in another attorney without asking the client first. D is incorrect. While this implies a “no-harm, no-foul” situation, the client would still be harmed by having his representation undertaken by an attorney he has no knowledge about.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. Although an attorney can retain or contract with other lawyers outside the lawyer’s own firm to assist in the provision of legal service, “the lawyer should ordinarily obtain informed consent from the client.” Model Rule 1.1, Comment [6]. While an attorney has the right to make decisions in many aspects of a representation, in the case of bringing in another attorney, the attorney must first ask for client consent. A is incorrect. The agreement between the attorneys is not an example of a prohibited limitation. C is incorrect. The division of the fee does not change the fact that the attorney brought in another attorney without asking the client first. D is incorrect. While this implies a “no-harm, no-foul” situation, the client would still be harmed by having his representation undertaken by an attorney he has no knowledge about.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. Although an attorney can retain or contract with other lawyers outside the lawyer’s own firm to assist in the provision of legal service, “the lawyer should ordinarily obtain informed consent from the client.” Model Rule 1.1, Comment [6]. While an attorney has the right to make decisions in many aspects of a representation, in the case of bringing in another attorney, the attorney must first ask for client consent. A is incorrect. The agreement between the attorneys is not an example of a prohibited limitation. C is incorrect. The division of the fee does not change the fact that the attorney brought in another attorney without asking the client first. D is incorrect. While this implies a “no-harm, no-foul” situation, the client would still be harmed by having his representation undertaken by an attorney he has no knowledge about.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.1"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[232],"sourceAnswerPages":[327],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师以风险代理收费方式，接受一名原告的委托，代理一宗产品责任案件。在准备开庭时，该律师意识到，对专家证人进行直接询问和交叉询问将涉及他不熟悉的工程问题，因此他可能无法胜任地代理原告。该律师没有告知原告，便咨询了另一名律师；后者同时也是工程师，并且是公认的产品责任专家。两名律师约定，由另一名律师在庭审中仅负责对专家证人进行直接询问和交叉询问，并且两名律师按照各自提供的服务以及承担的责任比例分配律师费。案件结束后，该律师向原告提供了一份说明相关费用的书面文件。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为两名律师之间的约定限制了各自代理的范围。","B":"是，因为没有告知原告另一名律师参与代理一事。","C":"否，因为律师费将按照各自提供的服务以及承担的责任比例进行分配。","D":"否，因为另一名律师的参与没有增加原告应支付的律师费。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。虽然律师可以聘请事务所以外的其他律师或与其订立合同，协助提供法律服务，但“律师通常应当取得委托人的知情同意。”《职业行为示范规则》规则 1.1，评注 [6]。律师有权就代理工作的许多方面作出决定，但在引入另一名律师参与代理时，必须先征得委托人的同意。A 不正确。两名律师之间的约定并不是一种被禁止的代理范围限制。C 不正确。律师费的分配方式并不能改变这样一个事实：该律师在没有事先征得委托人同意的情况下引入了另一名律师。D 不正确。尽管本选项暗示这是一种“无损害即无过错”的情形，但让一名委托人毫不知情的律师参与其代理，仍会损害委托人的利益。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-056","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":56,"topic":"Model Rule 1.1 / Model Rule 1.2","question":"During a legislative committee hearing, an attorney, reasonably believing that it was in the witness’s best interest not to answer, advised the witness not to answer certain questions on the grounds that she had a constitutional right not to answer. The committee chairperson directed the witness to answer and cautioned her that refusal to answer was a misdemeanor and that criminal prosecution would be instituted if she did not answer. Upon the attorney’s advice, the witness persisted in her refusal to answer. The witness was subsequently convicted for her refusal to answer. Were the attorney’s actions proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney reasonably believed the witness had a legal right to refuse to answer the questions.","B":"Yes, because refusing to answer was only a misdemeanor.","C":"No, because the witness committed a crime by refusing to answer.","D":"No, because the witness was successfully convicted for refusing to answer."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A is the correct answer. In making decisions and exercising options, a lawyer is required to act with competence. Model Rule 1.1. This means that he or she must employ “the legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness and preparation reasonably necessary for the representation.” In other words, the lawyer is not a guarantor of a successful outcome or even of a consequence-free result. He or she is required, however, to exercise his or her judgment in a manner that would be considered reasonable by other lawyers under all the circumstances. There are circumstances in which a lawyer may reasonably advise a client not to respond to questions by a legislative fact-finding committee, even when the client is faced by the risk of imprisonment. These facts do not tell us what the underlying facts were, but they do state clearly that the attorney “reasonably believed” that it was in the best interests of the witness not to answer. Ultimately, the attorney is not subject to discipline so long as he acted reasonably. B is incorrect. The level of potential criminal punishment does not change the analysis. C is incorrect. The facts do not disclose the constitutional basis for the refusal by the witness to answer the committee’s questions. Obviously, however, the consequences to the witness in failing to exercise her constitutional rights were deemed by the attorney to be more serious and severe than the refusal to answer. Although the general rule is that an attorney may not counsel a client to engage, or assist a client, in conduct that the lawyer knows is criminal (Model Rule 1.2), most courts would support the witness’ right to refuse to answer if her constitutional rights were in jeopardy. D is incorrect. For the reasons stated above, the fact that the witness was successfully convicted does not change the analysis.","rule":"A is the correct answer. In making decisions and exercising options, a lawyer is required to act with competence. Model Rule 1.1. This means that he or she must employ “the legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness and preparation reasonably necessary for the representation.” In other words, the lawyer is not a guarantor of a successful outcome or even of a consequence-free result. He or she is required, however, to exercise his or her judgment in a manner that would be considered reasonable by other lawyers under all the circumstances. There are circumstances in which a lawyer may reasonably advise a client not to respond to questions by a legislative fact-finding committee, even when the client is faced by the risk of imprisonment. These facts do not tell us what the underlying facts were, but they do state clearly that the attorney “reasonably believed” that it was in the best interests of the witness not to answer. Ultimately, the attorney is not subject to discipline so long as he acted reasonably. B is incorrect. The level of potential criminal punishment does not change the analysis. C is incorrect. The facts do not disclose the constitutional basis for the refusal by the witness to answer the committee’s questions. Obviously, however, the consequences to the witness in failing to exercise her constitutional rights were deemed by the attorney to be more serious and severe than the refusal to answer. Although the general rule is that an attorney may not counsel a client to engage, or assist a client, in conduct that the lawyer knows is criminal (Model Rule 1.2), most courts would support the witness’ right to refuse to answer if her constitutional rights were in jeopardy. D is incorrect. For the reasons stated above, the fact that the witness was successfully convicted does not change the analysis.","irac":{"issue":"Were the attorney’s actions proper?","rule":"A is the correct answer. In making decisions and exercising options, a lawyer is required to act with competence. Model Rule 1.1. This means that he or she must employ “the legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness and preparation reasonably necessary for the representation.” In other words, the lawyer is not a guarantor of a successful outcome or even of a consequence-free result. He or she is required, however, to exercise his or her judgment in a manner that would be considered reasonable by other lawyers under all the circumstances. There are circumstances in which a lawyer may reasonably advise a client not to respond to questions by a legislative fact-finding committee, even when the client is faced by the risk of imprisonment. These facts do not tell us what the underlying facts were, but they do state clearly that the attorney “reasonably believed” that it was in the best interests of the witness not to answer. Ultimately, the attorney is not subject to discipline so long as he acted reasonably. B is incorrect. The level of potential criminal punishment does not change the analysis. C is incorrect. The facts do not disclose the constitutional basis for the refusal by the witness to answer the committee’s questions. Obviously, however, the consequences to the witness in failing to exercise her constitutional rights were deemed by the attorney to be more serious and severe than the refusal to answer. Although the general rule is that an attorney may not counsel a client to engage, or assist a client, in conduct that the lawyer knows is criminal (Model Rule 1.2), most courts would support the witness’ right to refuse to answer if her constitutional rights were in jeopardy. D is incorrect. For the reasons stated above, the fact that the witness was successfully convicted does not change the analysis.","analysis":"A is the correct answer. In making decisions and exercising options, a lawyer is required to act with competence. Model Rule 1.1. This means that he or she must employ “the legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness and preparation reasonably necessary for the representation.” In other words, the lawyer is not a guarantor of a successful outcome or even of a consequence-free result. He or she is required, however, to exercise his or her judgment in a manner that would be considered reasonable by other lawyers under all the circumstances. There are circumstances in which a lawyer may reasonably advise a client not to respond to questions by a legislative fact-finding committee, even when the client is faced by the risk of imprisonment. These facts do not tell us what the underlying facts were, but they do state clearly that the attorney “reasonably believed” that it was in the best interests of the witness not to answer. Ultimately, the attorney is not subject to discipline so long as he acted reasonably. B is incorrect. The level of potential criminal punishment does not change the analysis. C is incorrect. The facts do not disclose the constitutional basis for the refusal by the witness to answer the committee’s questions. Obviously, however, the consequences to the witness in failing to exercise her constitutional rights were deemed by the attorney to be more serious and severe than the refusal to answer. Although the general rule is that an attorney may not counsel a client to engage, or assist a client, in conduct that the lawyer knows is criminal (Model Rule 1.2), most courts would support the witness’ right to refuse to answer if her constitutional rights were in jeopardy. D is incorrect. For the reasons stated above, the fact that the witness was successfully convicted does not change the analysis.","conclusion":"Answer A is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 1.1","Model Rule 1.2"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[233],"sourceAnswerPages":[327,328],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"在一次立法委员会听证会上，一名律师合理地认为，证人不回答某些问题符合其最佳利益，因此以该证人享有不予回答的宪法权利为由，建议她不要回答这些问题。委员会主席指示证人作答，并警告她，拒绝回答构成轻罪；如果她仍不回答，将对其提起刑事诉讼。证人依照律师的建议，坚持拒绝回答。此后，证人因拒绝回答而被定罪。该律师的行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师合理地认为证人享有拒绝回答这些问题的法律权利。","B":"是，因为拒绝回答只构成轻罪。","C":"否，因为证人以拒绝回答的方式实施了犯罪。","D":"否，因为证人最终因拒绝回答而被定罪。"},"explanationZh":"A 是正确答案。律师在作出决定和行使选择权时，必须以胜任的方式行事。《职业行为示范规则》规则 1.1。这意味着律师必须具备并运用“代理事项所合理必要的法律知识、技能、周全程度和准备”。换言之，律师并不保证一定获得成功结果，也不保证其建议不会产生任何不利后果。但是，律师必须以在所有具体情况下会被其他律师认为合理的方式运用专业判断。在某些情况下，即使委托人面临被监禁的风险，律师仍可合理地建议其不要回答立法事实调查委员会的问题。本题并未交代作为这一建议基础的具体事实，但明确说明律师“合理地认为”，不回答符合证人的最佳利益。归根结底，只要律师的行为是合理的，他就不应受纪律处分。B 不正确。可能受到的刑事处罚属于何种程度，并不改变分析结果。C 不正确。题目没有披露证人拒绝回答委员会问题所依据的具体宪法理由。不过，律师显然判断，如果证人不行使宪法权利，其后果会比拒绝回答更为严重。一般规则确实是，律师不得建议委托人实施其明知属于犯罪的行为，也不得协助委托人实施该行为（《职业行为示范规则》规则 1.2）；但如果证人的宪法权利处于危险之中，多数法院会支持其拒绝回答的权利。D 不正确。基于上述理由，证人最终被定罪这一事实并不改变分析结果。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-057","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":57,"topic":"Rule 2.9(A)(2)","question":"While presiding over the trial of a highly publicized case, a judge received a lengthy letter from a local attorney. The letter discussed the law applicable to the case. The judge knew that the attorney did not represent either party or any client whose interests could be affected by the outcome. After reading the trial briefs in the case, the judge concluded that the attorney’s letter better explained the law applicable to the case pending before him than either of the trial briefs. The judge followed the attorney’s reasoning in formulating his decision. Prior to rendering his decision, the judge did not tell the attorneys arguing the case anything about the letter. Is the judge subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the attorney is not of record as counsel in the case.","B":"Yes, because the judge, prior to rendering his decision, did not communicate its contents to all counsel and give them an opportunity to respond","C":"No, because the judge did not initiate the communication with the attorney.","D":"No, because the attorney did not represent any client whose interests could be affected by the outcome."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. Each side is entitled to know and to respond to every fact and every argument presented by the other side. If a judge could consult any legal source he or she wished without identifying it and giving each side the right to respond, the adversarial nature of the judicial system would collapse. At the same time, there are instances in which a judge will need to get advice on the law, especially when, as in these facts, the lawyers have not stated the situation clearly. To enable the judge to do this without subverting the adversarial process, Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(2) permits a judge to “obtain the written advice of a disinterested expert on the law applicable to a proceeding before the judge, if the judge gives advance notice to the parties of the person to be consulted and the subject matter of the advice to be solicited, and affords the parties a reasonable opportunity to object and respond to the notice and to the advice received.” A is incorrect. The issue here is that the judge based his decision on the attorney’s letter without informing the attorneys arguing the case. C is incorrect. The fact that the judge didn’t initiate the communication does not solve the primary problem. D is incorrect. It’s not enough that the expert consulted by the judge be impartial. The parties are entitled to know what the expert has been asked and what he or she has advised.","rule":"B is the correct answer. Each side is entitled to know and to respond to every fact and every argument presented by the other side. If a judge could consult any legal source he or she wished without identifying it and giving each side the right to respond, the adversarial nature of the judicial system would collapse. At the same time, there are instances in which a judge will need to get advice on the law, especially when, as in these facts, the lawyers have not stated the situation clearly. To enable the judge to do this without subverting the adversarial process, Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(2) permits a judge to “obtain the written advice of a disinterested expert on the law applicable to a proceeding before the judge, if the judge gives advance notice to the parties of the person to be consulted and the subject matter of the advice to be solicited, and affords the parties a reasonable opportunity to object and respond to the notice and to the advice received.” A is incorrect. The issue here is that the judge based his decision on the attorney’s letter without informing the attorneys arguing the case. C is incorrect. The fact that the judge didn’t initiate the communication does not solve the primary problem. D is incorrect. It’s not enough that the expert consulted by the judge be impartial. The parties are entitled to know what the expert has been asked and what he or she has advised.","irac":{"issue":"Is the judge subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. Each side is entitled to know and to respond to every fact and every argument presented by the other side. If a judge could consult any legal source he or she wished without identifying it and giving each side the right to respond, the adversarial nature of the judicial system would collapse. At the same time, there are instances in which a judge will need to get advice on the law, especially when, as in these facts, the lawyers have not stated the situation clearly. To enable the judge to do this without subverting the adversarial process, Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(2) permits a judge to “obtain the written advice of a disinterested expert on the law applicable to a proceeding before the judge, if the judge gives advance notice to the parties of the person to be consulted and the subject matter of the advice to be solicited, and affords the parties a reasonable opportunity to object and respond to the notice and to the advice received.” A is incorrect. The issue here is that the judge based his decision on the attorney’s letter without informing the attorneys arguing the case. C is incorrect. The fact that the judge didn’t initiate the communication does not solve the primary problem. D is incorrect. It’s not enough that the expert consulted by the judge be impartial. The parties are entitled to know what the expert has been asked and what he or she has advised.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. Each side is entitled to know and to respond to every fact and every argument presented by the other side. If a judge could consult any legal source he or she wished without identifying it and giving each side the right to respond, the adversarial nature of the judicial system would collapse. At the same time, there are instances in which a judge will need to get advice on the law, especially when, as in these facts, the lawyers have not stated the situation clearly. To enable the judge to do this without subverting the adversarial process, Model Code of Judicial Conduct, Rule 2.9(A)(2) permits a judge to “obtain the written advice of a disinterested expert on the law applicable to a proceeding before the judge, if the judge gives advance notice to the parties of the person to be consulted and the subject matter of the advice to be solicited, and affords the parties a reasonable opportunity to object and respond to the notice and to the advice received.” A is incorrect. The issue here is that the judge based his decision on the attorney’s letter without informing the attorneys arguing the case. C is incorrect. The fact that the judge didn’t initiate the communication does not solve the primary problem. D is incorrect. It’s not enough that the expert consulted by the judge be impartial. The parties are entitled to know what the expert has been asked and what he or she has advised.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Rule 2.9(A)(2)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[233,234],"sourceAnswerPages":[328,329],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名法官在主持审理一宗受到高度公众关注的案件期间，收到当地一名律师写来的一封长信。信中讨论了适用于该案的法律。法官知道，该律师既不代理本案任何一方，也不代理任何利益可能受到判决结果影响的委托人。法官阅读本案双方提交的审判书状后，认为该律师的来信对本案适用法律的说明，比任何一方的书状都更加清楚。法官在形成判决时采纳了该律师的论证。在作出判决之前，法官没有向本案出庭律师透露与该信有关的任何情况。该法官是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为写信的律师并非本案登记在案的代理律师。","B":"是，因为法官在作出判决前，没有把信件内容告知所有代理律师并给予他们回应的机会。","C":"否，因为法官并未主动与该律师进行沟通。","D":"否，因为该律师不代理任何利益可能受判决结果影响的委托人。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。诉讼每一方都有权知悉并回应另一方提出的每一项事实和每一个论点。如果法官可以任意咨询任何法律信息来源，却无需说明来源，也无需给予各方回应的权利，司法制度的对抗性质就会瓦解。与此同时，法官有时确实需要就法律问题取得意见，尤其是在像本题这样，双方律师都未能清楚阐明问题的情况下。为了使法官能够在不破坏对抗程序的前提下取得此类意见，《司法行为示范准则》规则 2.9(A)(2) 允许法官“就法官面前某一程序所适用的法律，取得无利害关系专家的书面意见，但法官须事先将拟咨询人员及拟征求意见的主题通知各方，并给予各方合理机会，对该通知以及收到的意见提出异议和作出回应。”A 不正确。本题的问题在于，法官以该律师的来信作为判决依据，却没有告知正在代理本案的律师。C 不正确。沟通并非由法官主动发起，并不能解决核心问题。D 不正确。法官所咨询的专家保持公正还不够；各方还有权知道法官向专家询问了什么，以及专家给出了什么意见。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-058","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":58,"topic":"Model Rule 5.4","question":"An attorney started a new law firm and moved into offices in a building owned by a bank. The attorney borrowed heavily from the bank to finance the firm. At the bank’s suggestion, an employee of the bank, who is not a lawyer, serves as a part-time office manager for the law firm without compensation from the firm. The duties of the office manager are to advise the firm generally on fees and time charges, program matters for the computer services, and consult with the attorney on accounting and billing practices to ensure solvency. The attorney and the bank do not discuss client confidences. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the bank will be involved in the practice of law.","B":"Yes, because a nonlawyer will be advising the law firm on fees and time charges.","C":"No, because the office manager is paid by the bank.","D":"No, because secrets or confidences of clients will not be disclosed to the bank."},"answer":"D","explanation":"D is the correct answer. So long as client confidences are not exposed, the arrangement is proper under the Rules. Importantly, no fees are being shared with the bank employee, and the bank employee is not serving as a director or in a similar position in the firm. Model Rule 5.4. Here, the bank employee is merely acting as a part-time office manager, giving advice on how to keep the firm solvent. A is incorrect. There is no indication that this setup will result in the bank improperly practicing law. The bank employee is merely a part-time office manager, giving advice regarding the running of the office. B is incorrect. It is improper for a nonlawyer third party to direct the lawyer’s professional judgment in rendering legal services. Model Rule 5.4, Comment [1]. Here, the bank employee is not giving advice on representation, but merely on office billing and solvency matters. C is incorrect. Although there is no sharing of fees, the arrangement could cause a problem if confidences were being shared with the bank.","rule":"D is the correct answer. So long as client confidences are not exposed, the arrangement is proper under the Rules. Importantly, no fees are being shared with the bank employee, and the bank employee is not serving as a director or in a similar position in the firm. Model Rule 5.4. Here, the bank employee is merely acting as a part-time office manager, giving advice on how to keep the firm solvent. A is incorrect. There is no indication that this setup will result in the bank improperly practicing law. The bank employee is merely a part-time office manager, giving advice regarding the running of the office. B is incorrect. It is improper for a nonlawyer third party to direct the lawyer’s professional judgment in rendering legal services. Model Rule 5.4, Comment [1]. Here, the bank employee is not giving advice on representation, but merely on office billing and solvency matters. C is incorrect. Although there is no sharing of fees, the arrangement could cause a problem if confidences were being shared with the bank.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"D is the correct answer. So long as client confidences are not exposed, the arrangement is proper under the Rules. Importantly, no fees are being shared with the bank employee, and the bank employee is not serving as a director or in a similar position in the firm. Model Rule 5.4. Here, the bank employee is merely acting as a part-time office manager, giving advice on how to keep the firm solvent. A is incorrect. There is no indication that this setup will result in the bank improperly practicing law. The bank employee is merely a part-time office manager, giving advice regarding the running of the office. B is incorrect. It is improper for a nonlawyer third party to direct the lawyer’s professional judgment in rendering legal services. Model Rule 5.4, Comment [1]. Here, the bank employee is not giving advice on representation, but merely on office billing and solvency matters. C is incorrect. Although there is no sharing of fees, the arrangement could cause a problem if confidences were being shared with the bank.","analysis":"D is the correct answer. So long as client confidences are not exposed, the arrangement is proper under the Rules. Importantly, no fees are being shared with the bank employee, and the bank employee is not serving as a director or in a similar position in the firm. Model Rule 5.4. Here, the bank employee is merely acting as a part-time office manager, giving advice on how to keep the firm solvent. A is incorrect. There is no indication that this setup will result in the bank improperly practicing law. The bank employee is merely a part-time office manager, giving advice regarding the running of the office. B is incorrect. It is improper for a nonlawyer third party to direct the lawyer’s professional judgment in rendering legal services. Model Rule 5.4, Comment [1]. Here, the bank employee is not giving advice on representation, but merely on office billing and solvency matters. C is incorrect. Although there is no sharing of fees, the arrangement could cause a problem if confidences were being shared with the bank.","conclusion":"Answer D is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 5.4"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[234],"sourceAnswerPages":[329],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师创办了一家新律师事务所，并将办公地点设在一栋由某银行所有的大楼内。为给事务所筹措资金，该律师向银行借入了大笔款项。在银行建议下，银行的一名非律师雇员兼任该律师事务所的兼职办公室经理，事务所不向其支付报酬。办公室经理的职责是就收费和计时收费事项向事务所提供一般性建议，为计算机服务编排相关事项，并就会计和账单管理做法与律师商议，以确保事务所保持偿付能力。律师与银行之间不讨论委托人的保密信息。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为银行将会参与从事法律执业活动。","B":"是，因为一名非律师将就收费和计时收费事项向律师事务所提供建议。","C":"否，因为该办公室经理由银行支付报酬。","D":"否，因为不会向银行披露委托人的秘密或保密信息。"},"explanationZh":"D 是正确答案。只要委托人的保密信息没有被泄露，该安排在《规则》下就是适当的。重要的是，律师费并未与该银行雇员分享，而且该银行雇员也没有担任事务所的董事或类似职位。《职业行为示范规则》规则 5.4。本题中，银行雇员只是在担任兼职办公室经理，就如何维持事务所的偿付能力提供建议。A 不正确。没有任何迹象表明该安排会导致银行不当地从事法律执业活动。银行雇员只是一名兼职办公室经理，就办公室运营提供建议。B 不正确。非律师第三方指挥律师在提供法律服务时的专业判断，是不适当的。《职业行为示范规则》规则 5.4，评注 [1]。本题中，银行雇员并未就如何代理委托人提供意见，而只是就办公室账单管理和偿付能力事项提供建议。C 不正确。尽管不存在律师费分成，但如果委托人的保密信息被分享给银行，这种安排仍可能产生问题。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-059","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":59,"topic":"Model Rule 3.6(a) / Model Rule 3.6(b)(2)","question":"An attorney represents a famous actor in an action against a newspaper for libel. The case has attracted much publicity, and a jury trial has been demanded. After one of the pretrial hearings, as the attorney left the courthouse, news reporters interviewed the attorney. In responding to questions, the attorney revealed the identity of the reporter who made the statements that were the subject of the claim. Were the attorney’s actions proper?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the trial has not commenced.","B":"Yes, because the statement relates to a matter of public record.","C":"No, because prospective jurors might learn of the attorney’s remarks.","D":"No, because the attorney identified a prospective witness in the case."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. During representation, a lawyer “shall not make an extrajudicial statement that the lawyer knows or reasonably should know will be disseminated by means of public communication and will have a substantial likelihood of materially prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding in the matter.” Model Rule 3.6(a). However, there is an exception for “information contained in a public record.” Model Rule 3.6(b)(2). Here, since the actor has sued the newspaper for libel, the identity of the reporter is a matter of public record. A is incorrect. Although the trial has not yet commenced, the Rule is concerned with “prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding.” Model Rule 3.6(a). Clearly, prejudice can be created before a trial has actually commenced. C is incorrect. While prospective jurors may learn of the attorney’s remarks, this does not automatically make any remarks subject to discipline. D is incorrect. While the reporter may be a prospective witness, the important point is that lawyers may make statements regarding matters of public record.","rule":"B is the correct answer. During representation, a lawyer “shall not make an extrajudicial statement that the lawyer knows or reasonably should know will be disseminated by means of public communication and will have a substantial likelihood of materially prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding in the matter.” Model Rule 3.6(a). However, there is an exception for “information contained in a public record.” Model Rule 3.6(b)(2). Here, since the actor has sued the newspaper for libel, the identity of the reporter is a matter of public record. A is incorrect. Although the trial has not yet commenced, the Rule is concerned with “prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding.” Model Rule 3.6(a). Clearly, prejudice can be created before a trial has actually commenced. C is incorrect. While prospective jurors may learn of the attorney’s remarks, this does not automatically make any remarks subject to discipline. D is incorrect. While the reporter may be a prospective witness, the important point is that lawyers may make statements regarding matters of public record.","irac":{"issue":"Were the attorney’s actions proper?","rule":"B is the correct answer. During representation, a lawyer “shall not make an extrajudicial statement that the lawyer knows or reasonably should know will be disseminated by means of public communication and will have a substantial likelihood of materially prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding in the matter.” Model Rule 3.6(a). However, there is an exception for “information contained in a public record.” Model Rule 3.6(b)(2). Here, since the actor has sued the newspaper for libel, the identity of the reporter is a matter of public record. A is incorrect. Although the trial has not yet commenced, the Rule is concerned with “prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding.” Model Rule 3.6(a). Clearly, prejudice can be created before a trial has actually commenced. C is incorrect. While prospective jurors may learn of the attorney’s remarks, this does not automatically make any remarks subject to discipline. D is incorrect. While the reporter may be a prospective witness, the important point is that lawyers may make statements regarding matters of public record.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. During representation, a lawyer “shall not make an extrajudicial statement that the lawyer knows or reasonably should know will be disseminated by means of public communication and will have a substantial likelihood of materially prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding in the matter.” Model Rule 3.6(a). However, there is an exception for “information contained in a public record.” Model Rule 3.6(b)(2). Here, since the actor has sued the newspaper for libel, the identity of the reporter is a matter of public record. A is incorrect. Although the trial has not yet commenced, the Rule is concerned with “prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding.” Model Rule 3.6(a). Clearly, prejudice can be created before a trial has actually commenced. C is incorrect. While prospective jurors may learn of the attorney’s remarks, this does not automatically make any remarks subject to discipline. D is incorrect. While the reporter may be a prospective witness, the important point is that lawyers may make statements regarding matters of public record.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.6(a)","Model Rule 3.6(b)(2)"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[234],"sourceAnswerPages":[329,330],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代理一位著名演员，起诉一家报纸诽谤。该案受到广泛关注，并已要求进行陪审团审判。一次审前听证结束后，该律师离开法院时接受了新闻记者采访。在回答问题时，律师披露了作出本案被诉言论的记者身份。该律师的行为是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为审判尚未开始。","B":"是，因为该陈述涉及公共记录中的事项。","C":"否，因为候选陪审员可能会得知该律师的言论。","D":"否，因为该律师指明了一名本案潜在证人的身份。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。在代理期间，律师“不得在庭外发表其明知或理应知道将通过公共传播方式散布、并且极有可能对该事项的裁判程序造成实质性不利影响的言论。”《职业行为示范规则》规则 3.6(a)。不过，“公共记录中所包含的信息”属于例外。《职业行为示范规则》规则 3.6(b)(2)。本题中，由于该演员已经起诉报纸诽谤，该记者的身份属于公共记录事项。A 不正确。尽管审判尚未开始，该规则关注的是“对裁判程序造成不利影响”。《职业行为示范规则》规则 3.6(a)。显然，在审判正式开始之前也可能形成这种不利影响。C 不正确。潜在陪审员虽然可能得知律师的言论，但这并不自动意味着任何此类言论都会使律师应受纪律处分。D 不正确。该记者虽然可能是潜在证人，但关键在于，律师可以就公共记录事项发表言论。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}},{"id":"mpre-practice3-060","exam":"MPRE","subject":"Professional Responsibility","examSet":"Practice MPRE 3","questionNumber":60,"topic":"Model Rule 3.9","question":"An attorney is a well-known healthcare expert. During congressional hearings on healthcare, the attorney testified to her personal belief and expert opinion on the pending healthcare package. She failed to disclose in her testimony that she was being compensated by a private client for her appearance. In her testimony, the attorney took the position favored by her client, but the position was also one that the attorney believed was in the public interest. Is the attorney subject to discipline?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because a lawyer may not accept a fee for trying to influence legislative action.","B":"Yes, because a lawyer who appears in a legislative hearing should identify the capacity in which the lawyer appears.","C":"No, because Congress is interested in the content of the testimony and not who is paying the witness.","D":"No, because the attorney believed that the position she advocated was in the public interest."},"answer":"B","explanation":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer appearing before a legislative body must disclose that the appearance is in a representative capacity. Model Rule 3.9. Requiring a lawyer to disclose the client relationship allows the legislative body to assess any potential bias of the lawyer. A is incorrect. Lawyers frequently serve as paid lobbyists. This activity is permissible so long as the lawyer-lobbyist complies with applicable rules, such as the requirement to disclose his or her representative capacity when appearing before a legislative body. C is incorrect. As stated above, the congressional members at the hearing would be very interested in knowing whether a witness is possibly biased in his or her testimony; therefore, Model Rule 3.9 requires a lawyer appearing in a representative capacity to disclose that fact to the legislative body. D is incorrect. The fact that the attorney believes her client’s position is in the public interest does not cure the Rule violation. Always be wary of answer choices that imply a Rule was not violated because the attorney would have said or done the same thing despite the Rule violation.","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer appearing before a legislative body must disclose that the appearance is in a representative capacity. Model Rule 3.9. Requiring a lawyer to disclose the client relationship allows the legislative body to assess any potential bias of the lawyer. A is incorrect. Lawyers frequently serve as paid lobbyists. This activity is permissible so long as the lawyer-lobbyist complies with applicable rules, such as the requirement to disclose his or her representative capacity when appearing before a legislative body. C is incorrect. As stated above, the congressional members at the hearing would be very interested in knowing whether a witness is possibly biased in his or her testimony; therefore, Model Rule 3.9 requires a lawyer appearing in a representative capacity to disclose that fact to the legislative body. D is incorrect. The fact that the attorney believes her client’s position is in the public interest does not cure the Rule violation. Always be wary of answer choices that imply a Rule was not violated because the attorney would have said or done the same thing despite the Rule violation.","irac":{"issue":"Is the attorney subject to discipline?","rule":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer appearing before a legislative body must disclose that the appearance is in a representative capacity. Model Rule 3.9. Requiring a lawyer to disclose the client relationship allows the legislative body to assess any potential bias of the lawyer. A is incorrect. Lawyers frequently serve as paid lobbyists. This activity is permissible so long as the lawyer-lobbyist complies with applicable rules, such as the requirement to disclose his or her representative capacity when appearing before a legislative body. C is incorrect. As stated above, the congressional members at the hearing would be very interested in knowing whether a witness is possibly biased in his or her testimony; therefore, Model Rule 3.9 requires a lawyer appearing in a representative capacity to disclose that fact to the legislative body. D is incorrect. The fact that the attorney believes her client’s position is in the public interest does not cure the Rule violation. Always be wary of answer choices that imply a Rule was not violated because the attorney would have said or done the same thing despite the Rule violation.","analysis":"B is the correct answer. A lawyer appearing before a legislative body must disclose that the appearance is in a representative capacity. Model Rule 3.9. Requiring a lawyer to disclose the client relationship allows the legislative body to assess any potential bias of the lawyer. A is incorrect. Lawyers frequently serve as paid lobbyists. This activity is permissible so long as the lawyer-lobbyist complies with applicable rules, such as the requirement to disclose his or her representative capacity when appearing before a legislative body. C is incorrect. As stated above, the congressional members at the hearing would be very interested in knowing whether a witness is possibly biased in his or her testimony; therefore, Model Rule 3.9 requires a lawyer appearing in a representative capacity to disclose that fact to the legislative body. D is incorrect. The fact that the attorney believes her client’s position is in the public interest does not cure the Rule violation. Always be wary of answer choices that imply a Rule was not violated because the attorney would have said or done the same thing despite the Rule violation.","conclusion":"Answer B is correct."},"ruleReferences":["Model Rule 3.9"],"sourceTitle":"Strategies & Tactics for the MPRE","sourceEdition":"2018","sourceQuestionPages":[235],"sourceAnswerPages":[330],"sourceStatus":"MPRE-style practice question; not an actual MPRE question according to the book","createdAt":"2026-07-27T00:00:00.000Z","attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师是知名的医疗保健专家。在国会举行的医疗保健听证会上，该律师就一项待审议的医疗保健方案陈述了自己的个人看法和专家意见。她在作证时没有披露，自己此次出席是由一名私人委托人付费的。该律师在证言中采取了其委托人支持的立场，而她本人也认为该立场符合公共利益。该律师是否应受纪律处分？","choicesZh":{"A":"是，因为律师不得为试图影响立法行动而收取费用。","B":"是，因为律师出席立法听证会时，应当说明自己以何种身份出席。","C":"否，因为国会关心的是证言内容，而不是由谁向证人付费。","D":"否，因为该律师认为自己所主张的立场符合公共利益。"},"explanationZh":"B 是正确答案。律师代表他人出席立法机构时，必须披露其是以代理身份出席。《职业行为示范规则》规则 3.9。要求律师披露委托关系，可以使立法机构评估律师是否存在潜在偏见。A 不正确。律师经常担任有偿游说者。只要作为游说者的律师遵守适用规则，例如在立法机构面前出席时披露其代理身份的要求，这种活动就是允许的。C 不正确。如上所述，参加听证会的国会议员会非常关心某位证人的证言是否可能带有偏向；因此，《职业行为示范规则》规则 3.9 要求以代理身份出席的律师向立法机构披露这一事实。D 不正确。该律师相信其委托人的立场符合公共利益，并不能弥补对《规则》的违反。凡是暗示律师即使没有违反规则也会说出或做出同样事情、因而规则并未受到违反的选项，都应保持警惕。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-27T09:46:18-07:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"}}];
